inl note_of_stable_entry_points_3 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 3u32, "2026-10-14T12:54Z", "lane-b", "investigated: origin user 10-07 00:23 'emitted code more stable entry point wise, rust has hacks vs C'; Rust wraps spiral_main in a 1 GB thread + wasm cfg + exit(101) and Kino patch_rust text-replaces that main (7 sites); proposed DoD: spiral_main everywhere incl. C, --entry exit-code|print-value|none option, 0 text-patching consumers; evidence lane-b/stable-entry-points.txt")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_4 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: unchanged. Related progress: Rust/C closures got readable stable names ({stem}_c{tag}) on 10-11, so entry stability now mostly concerns methods. Lowered P2 -> P3.")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_5 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: Rust uncaught failwith exits 1 like C, Kotlin and Swift (pack-c/rust-failwith-exit.ps1, in PACK-NIGHT-1017): failwith_exit goes agree-fail -> agree; failwith stays a panic because lib try/with and tests use catch_unwind. Naming measurement over sample main.rs: 260 named methods vs 226 numbered methodN (46 percent of methods still unstable), 87 named vs 97 anonymous closures. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_6 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:37 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured the proposed DoD (spiral_main everywhere, an entry option, 0 text-patching consumers) on today's outputs (samples/closure_apply): Rust, Kotlin and Swift emit spiral_main plus a 1 GB-stack thread wrapper, Zig emits spiralMain (camelCase, its own naming) plus spiralMainThread/spiralMainThreadReserved, while C still has only int32_t main() at line 43 with the body inline, so C is the one backend without a separately callable entry. Consumers still text-patch: Cells calls Cell.patch_rust at cells.ex:379 and :436, implemented in apps/cells/spi/document domain (patch_rust rewrites lines, collapses newlines and replaces std::process::exit(main.join().unwrap()); with show/unit exit text). So the two concrete gaps are: C spiral_main split, and an emit option that makes patch_rust unnecessary (show_value vs exit code).")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_7 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:23 helper-lane lap 2: read what Cells' patch_rust actually rewrites (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi:145-326), which splits the 0-text-patching goal in two: (1) snippet expansion, not entry: snippets/snippet_of collect named Rust code literals from the output, and rewrite_lines/emit_of/expand replace each __spiral_emit_rust <args> <name> ; line with the snippet's payload, substituting ,  ... with the call's arguments (split at depth 0, a trailing .clone() on a vN argument stripped) - a textual macro system layered on the residual; (2) entry: show_exit/unit_exit/show_join/unit_join and patch_join_exit replace std::process::exit(main.join().unwrap()) with printing the value or exiting 0, keeping panic exit codes. So --entry exit-code|print-value covers only (2); (1) needs the compiler to expand emit-rust snippets itself (or the feature retired), otherwise Cells keeps a text pass.")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_8 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:53Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:53 helper-lane lap 3: counted and settled the snippet half (retire vs compile-in): users are many (the !\\\\\\\\ operator / rust.emit_expr appear 599 times in 35 files: lib/spiral about 380 incl. runtime 49, rust 39, leptos 34, file_system 32, sm' 31; dice ui 72, polyglot 14, dir-tree-html 12, apps/spiral 8, plus the old tictactoe repo 167), so retiring is a corpus migration. But compile-in already exists: CodegenRust's inlineRustEmits (spiral_compiler.fs 163273, applied to every Rust output at 164124) expands each __spiral_emit_rust args vN line using the vN string binding, so the committed Rust outputs carry no markers (0 in rust_emit_tuple_args, rust_macro_reuse_clone, dir_tree_html.rs, apps/spiral spiral.rs and build.rs). The host's rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs 398, used at 572 when the core writes Rust) and Cells' cell.spi snippets/expand (145-326, also matching the old Fable emitRustExpr marker) are now second and third copies of the same expansion. So the snippet half needs no new compiler feature: next is checking whether any Cells-compiled cell still reaches Cells' pass with a marker (if none, the text pass and the host copy can go), leaving only the entry half (--entry exit-code|print-value).")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_9 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:35 helper-lane lap 4: checked who still feeds markers to Cells' snippet pass: Cells calls Cell.patch_rust on every Rust cell (cells.ex 379 and 436, via domain.ex 22 into the Gleam-compiled spi/cell.spi), but the input is always compiler output, which inlineRustEmits has already expanded, and cells.ex 977 turns any surviving __spiral_emit_rust or emitRustExpr marker into a ProcessError (generated Rust still holds an emit marker). The only inputs that still carry markers are hand-written fixtures in cells_test.exs (12, 91) and cell_test.exs (6, 11, 21, 64, 83), which test the pass itself. So the snippet half of cell.spi (snippet_of 145, snippets 192, the emit_of/expand rewrite through ~326) and the host's rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs 398/572) are removable without behaviour change, keeping the entry half (show_exit/unit_join/patch_join_exit) until --entry exists; the 977 guard stays as the tripwire. Removal is a Cells + host edit: proposed for lane 2's Cells work and a host pack, not applied.")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_10 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:55Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:55 helper-lane lap 5: one caveat before removing Cells' snippet half, from reading cell.spi 192-326: rewrite_lines does two things - it expands marker lines (dead, see lap 4) and it DROPS every line of the form let mut vN: Rc<str> = literal; (snippet_of, 145-169) whether or not a marker uses it, and patch_rust then collapses the blank lines. So removing the half is behaviour-preserving only if compiler output never carries such string-literal binding lines; if inlineRustEmits leaves them behind after inlining (unused bindings), Cells currently hides them and removal would surface them as rustc unused-variable warnings, not errors. The removal patch should therefore first be checked against a few real Cells Rust outputs (grep the cached cell main.rs files for let mut v..: Rc<str> = quote lines), then either drop the half entirely or keep only the binding filter.")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_11 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T22:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:16 helper-lane lap 6: checked the lap-5 caveat against real compiler output. Across the 58 Rust programs the installed compilers emitted today under spiral-bin/runs/try (pre-Cells, the text Cells would receive), there are 1,503 string-literal bindings of the form let mut vN: Rc<str> = literal; and none is unused: every vN appears again after its binding line. So compiler output does not leave dead literal bindings behind, and removing Cells' drop-and-collapse half of rewrite_lines (cell.spi snippet_of) would not surface rustc unused-variable warnings on this corpus. The removal patch can go ahead, coordinated with lane 2 since it is apps/cells.")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_12 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T23:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:44 helper-lane lap 7: the whole Cells rewrite_lines half is dead on real output, not only the marker part. Across today's 58 compiler Rust outputs there are 0 emit markers (__spiral_emit_rust or emitRustExpr; the compiler's inlineRustEmits already expands them) and 0 lines in snippet_of's exact form (let mut vN: Rc<str> = followed by a bare string literal and ;, which is not valid Rust and so never emitted). Staged lab/STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS/snippet_half_patch.py (apps/cells, --apply gated, dry run OK). It removes apps/cells/spi/cell.spi lines 128-270 (digits_end, var_end, last_var, snippet_of, unescape, pairs, snippets, strip_clone, split_args, subst, expand, emit_of, rewrite_lines; the script checks none is used elsewhere), and patch_rust keeps join_lines (read_lines textn) plus collapse_newlines, so its output is unchanged on marker-free input. It must ship with test edits: cell_test.exs (inlines __spiral_emit_rust, the emitRustExpr case, the timing case) and cells_test.exs line 12 feed hand-written markers, so they become cases asserting marker-free input passes through. Applied together with the other Cells-window patches when no Cells run is live (lane 2 has stopped, so Cells is with helper lane 1).")
inl note_of_stable_entry_points_13 () = Note ("STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS", 13u32, "2026-10-20T01:09Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 21:09 helper-lane: Cells' snippet half is removed (apps/cells, uncommitted, for the user's commit). lab/STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS/window.ps1 applied snippet_half_patch.py (cell.spi loses digits_end through rewrite_lines, about 140 lines, and patch_rust keeps join_lines plus collapse_newlines) and tests_patch.py in a Cells idle window. tests_patch.py turns the marker-fed tests into tests on already-inlined compiler output: the cells_test.exs @rust stub, the cell_test.exs value test and the linear-time big_rust test (same assertions, inlined emits); the former-marker-spelling test, which only exercised the dead path, was dropped. mix test (cell_test, cells_test, notebook_report_test; mix-20261019-2048.log) gives 80 passed, so the change was kept. Cells' Rust patching is now only the entry/exit rewrite (show_exit, join patching), which is the stable-entry-point part the item is about.")
inl kino_fsi_cleanup () = Task ("KINO-FSI-CLEANUP", "Kino fsi_chain/fsi_session still mention dotnet-repl and .dib: confirm what F# cells need natively and drop the rest; confirmed 2026-10-11 18:3x, nothing turned out to be droppable without a design call: F# cells need the dotnet fsi session and its ;; submissions, #r nuget resolution, the ///- --timeout and ///- --ignore cell directives (ignored?/1 is applied by the livemd runner as well), and the prelude that opens Microsoft.DotNet.Interactive.Formatting, which polyglot's live notebooks really use (Notebooks.livemd sets Formatter.ListExpansionLimit, NotebooksHtml.livemd and Testing.livemd call Formatter.Register, actual.ToDisplayString () and AssertExceptionFormatter.Display (), and a small Formatter shim stands in when the dll is absent); the only dotnet-repl-specific piece is the .dib document route, which is still the fallback format for any non-.livemd notebook and for a #!import of one, so it is a live input path even though polyglot migrated all five of its .dib notebooks to .livemd and no .dib file exists in either repo today; dropping that route would remove a user-facing format, which is the user's call", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,fsharp", "current", "current")
inl sample_readmes_decision () = Task ("SAMPLE-READMES", "119 older per-sample README.md files describe code in prose; delete as drift or keep (user decision); 2026-10-12 user position: no prose in repos; decide per README: fold the fact into the sample name or a test-block name, else delete (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS); 2026-10-12 classification (keyword heuristic, list in job scratch metrics/sample-readmes.tsv): 120 READMEs = 58 descriptions (4,247 words, mostly restate the sample name) + 46 bug histories (4,435 words, \"used to\"/regressions; the commits already hold them) + 12 expectations (1,084 words: exit codes/oracle notes -> inline expectations, TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST) + 3 manual checks (2,955 words, mega_brzozowski_derivatives alone 2,677; manual steps -> automation); proposal for the user: delete the 58 descriptions now, the 46 histories once SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS gives tests descriptive names, fold the 12 expectations, automate the 3 manual checks; 2026-10-13 23:41 user decision: move every fact a README carries into its sample's names/test expectations, then delete all 119 (this is the user's OK for the tracked-file deletion); 2026-10-14 02:30 lane 3 facts-to-code mapping for all 119 READMEs (read only; C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\sample-readmes\\mapping.md, one row per sample: facts, what code/oracle/outputs already carry, the change; applied by the main thread after the bless): 20 READMEs are contradicted by the blessed oracle (9 abi_external* with Rust error and 3 Delphi 0xC0000139 crashes, dynamic_array_tuple_managed Rust exit 2, lua_while_union_pair Wasm exit 1, the closure diamond/external/transitive packages and 4 package/prototype samples all error, two Cube delay samples C build-fail on Windows); 47 describe the retired Spiral-bin C-to-Rust/Delphi lowerer (drop; their invalid.c/source.c/negative-*.c/c-shim.h inputs are dead, deletion is the main thread's call); 15 samples are 42-returning shells whose only content was the README (11 native_package_fixed_array/owned, 3 native_package_closure, rust_global_item_macro); 15 need an expectation (mostly the expected compile-error text the oracle does not store, incl. one per mega_brzozowski negative_* package, which exposes the two that fail for the wrong reason); 16 renames carry one-line intents; 10 are pure restatements; 5 files are lower-case readme.md; 2026-10-14 02:34 lane 3 note: the mapping.md summary categories overlap (one row can be contradicted, retired-lowerer and rename at once), so 20/47/15/15/16/10/2 do not sum to the 119 rows; 2026-10-14 02:57 lane 3 root causes of the 20 contradictions (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\sample-readmes\\root-causes.md): no active silent wrong answer and no compiler bug; H1 harness: test.ps1:619 agree-fail treats any two nonzero exits as a shared failure, so a value-returning sample's wrong answer is not DISAGREE (90 C rows exit nonzero, 460 non-C rows exposed; hides lua_while_union_pair Wasm 1 vs 235); fix: only exits above 125, NTSTATUS or timeout (or a declared failure fixture) count as failures; H2 harness: bless (test.ps1:691) never drops rows for backends a sample no longer schedules: 55 stale rows (22 Rust, 22 Delphi, 11 Zig), 22 of them the abi_external* and dynamic_array_tuple_managed Rust/Delphi rows left by 20f6af8d (C-only since 2026-09-28; Delphi 0xC0000139 = old 32-bit FPC ABI, Rust exit 2 = old lowerer's refcount); H3 harness: no CFlags rule for abi_external_buffer_length_negative, so its C row is a link build-fail, not the runtime rejection (add the mutable-buffer shim rule); F1 fixtures: 9 package samples (closure diamond/external/transitive, 6 package prototype callbacks) use |core-, which resolves to deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core (spiral_compiler.fs:180647-180657) without the samples' +./-./=. (tmp/samples/core/operators.spi), so all rows error with Unbound variable and their negatives fail for the wrong reason; K1: cube delay C build-fail is the intended Linux-only poll.h scope (spiral_compiler.fs:177150); 2026-10-14 03:01 lane 3 addendum: all 12 oracle rows that differ from C are dispositioned (6 Known, failwith_exit shared failure, lua Wasm C2, 4 stale H2); all three latest-*.tsv show the same 8 masked rows and no c-only row that ran differently; H4 harness: test.ps1:632-635 can relabel a row that ran with a different answer as c-only (masks nothing today; restrict c-only to build-fail rows); 2026-10-14 03:14 lane 3 after-bless patch scripts (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\after-bless, each -DryRun, exact anchors, LF; stacked on scratch copies of test.ps1, parse clean): h1_agree_fail (failure-class exits 1, 3, 134, Rust 101, Delphi FPC 200-219/227/229/231/232, negative/NTSTATUS, timeout only with timeout; C value exits 4..235 stay values; flips in today's oracle: lua_while_union_pair Wasm and the stale abi_external_struct_argument Delphi row), h2_drop_stale_rows (bless drops rows the harness no longer schedules for a sample in the run: 55 today), h3_abi_negative_flags, h4_c_only_build_fail, wasm_fixture (runner splits an exit of 126 or more into two runs, high and low nibble: lua Wasm reads 235, no residual changes; checked on scratch copies), package_samples_core (8 samples, 12 files, 28 dotted operators to upstream +,-,=), expected_errors_check (ExpectedErrors in matrix.psd1 enforced as WRONG-ERROR), readme_apply (15 renames + one named literal + 6 ExpectedErrors entries, deletes 117 of 119 READMEs, keeps recursive_union_multi_case: no core so it fails on +. not on its boundary, and mega_brzozowski_derivatives: at least 6 negatives report unbound names before their intended rejection); correction: native_source_package_prototype_record_callback_missing_instance already fails for its own reason (ETY-InstanceNotFound); native_layout_stack_mutable_string_negative fails because no backend supports stack mutable layouts, not on the managed string the README names; 2026-10-14 03:21 lane 3 correction: Rust and Delphi do run stack mutable layouts (native_layout_stack_mutable* exit 15), so their rejection of native_layout_stack_mutable_string_negative is the managed-string check behind a generic 'doesn't support stack mutable layout types' message; only C and F# lack stack_mut; after-bless scripts revised: wasm_fixture accepts the split exit only when both split runs exit cleanly, h2 drops only on a full bless (-Suite all, no -Filter), readme_apply writes a sample's edits only if all apply and keeps READMEs whose carrier is not live yet (needs h3 for abi_external_buffer_length_negative, expected_errors_check for the 6 ExpectedErrors samples); stacked on scratch copies: 117 READMEs deleted, harness parses, test.ps1 parses; 2026-10-14 03:40 lane 3: recursive_union_core.ps1 (recursive_union_multi_case gains packages: core-), brzozowski_negatives.ps1 (5 siblings re-export root names under root_*; 8 negatives reach their intended rejection: provisional texts; 30 ExpectedErrors entries; negative_antimirov_missing_finite_authority blocked: it needs the root prototype symbol_compare and the sibling at once), runbook C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\after-bless\\RUNBOOK.md (11 ordered steps with dry-run, apply, verify filter and expected verdicts); all scripts stacked on a scratch tree: test.ps1 and matrix.psd1 parse, 36 ExpectedErrors entries; 2026-10-14 03:48 lane 3 revision: bend_arms and wasm_fixture now throw on a second apply (all after-bless scripts applied twice on a scratch tree: every second apply throws before writing or does nothing); readme_apply and brzozowski_negatives both add to an existing ExpectedErrors block, in either order (34 entries); brzozowski texts: 22 observed, skip_normalization and seal_unverified observed today, 4 provisional, the 2 negative_forge_* recorded after the first run; finding: antimirov_typed_slot_bound passes as its own row but lists 4 type errors in indexed_assignment.spi/injective_bound.spi when it is loaded as