inl note_of_calyx_backend_2 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 2u32, "2026-10-14T13:09Z", "main-thread", "09:09 compile probe of the 42 Ebpf-list samples missing from the Calyx list (installed single-flight DLL): 15 compile (native_tail_recursion ts_tail_loop ebpf_arith ebpf_shift ebpf_bool ebpf_div ebpf_bits ebpf_umul ebpf_uacc ebpf_bacc ebpf_char ebpf_sh31 ebpf_uadd ebpf_tworec ebpf_snot). The others stop on f32 (12), tuples (6), macros (1), or a while/map shape whose codegen error names no feature (7: while_loop, while_control, ts_while_loop, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit, ebpf_key, ebpf_minkey). Next: add the 15 to the Calyx list after the bless and keep those that agree with C under cider; then name the missing feature in the empty errors.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_3 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 3u32, "2026-10-14T15:34Z", "main-thread", "11:34 widened the Calyx list 9 -> 21 rows that agree with C (single-flight-20261014-112558). Dropped: native_tail_recursion (cider timed out at 120 s on the deep loop), ebpf_arith and ebpf_div (DISAGREE). Root cause, measured with cider on minimal programs: std_sdiv_pipe's quotient truncates like C, but its out_remainder is a floored modulo with the divisor's sign (7 % -2 = -1 and -7 % 2 = 1; C gives 1 and -1). Fixed in source: signed % = left - (left / right) * right via std_sdiv_pipe, std_mult_pipe, std_sub. Both samples rejoin the list with pack-1c.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_4 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 4u32, "2026-10-14T18:25Z", "main-thread", "14:24 signed remainder fix installed with pack-1d; ebpf_arith and ebpf_div are back on the Calyx list and agree with C in single-flight-20261014-140125; the list is 23 (was 9 this morning).")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_5 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: Calyx 23/23 agree (pack-1h oracle). Next grain: f32 or tuples (the largest unsupported classes).")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_6 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 6u32, "2026-10-15T12:51Z", "lane-a", "08:51 lane-a: tuple support patch ready (lane-a/patches/calyx-tuples.ps1, shared section): tuple locals, tuple returns as out0..outN component ports, tuple Dyn. Copied-tree run single-flight-20261015-085030 (Debug candidate, -Parallel 1): Calyx 28/28 rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0; +5 rows tuple_pair, nested_tuple, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_t2, ebpf_t3 (harness patch lane-a/patches/harness-openqasm-calyx.ps1); ebpf_nest3 stays off (f32). gpt5parallel build pending.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_7 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-10-15T22:10Z", "lane-a", "18:10 lane-a: the lane-A patch for this item shipped with main's pack-1j/1k (pack-1k review: 4,484 rows, all added rows agree, DISAGREE 0, per main).")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_8 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:33 lap 1 grain: today's pack 5 review: Calyx 28 rows agree with C, 0 disagree (9 at v1 on 10-10, tuples added 10-15); subsets alongside: OpenQasm 31, Ebpf 52, Lean 446 + 1 known Next: list C-list samples rejected only for i64/u64 on Calyx (the widest remaining v1 gap after tuples)")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_9 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:28 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1's premise: a static scan of the 455 single-file samples that run natively on C but are not on the Calyx list (28 entries) finds 0 samples blocked only by i64/u64; every i64/u64 sample also uses floats, strings, arrays, unions, macros or closures, so 64-bit ints are not the widest gap. Instead the scan found integer-only samples within the documented v1 subset that were never added: frontier_hello (x - 42), zig_exit_wide (exit 305), native_tail_recursion (self-tail countdown from 1,000,000: watch cider's speed), record_value and wgsl_record_loop (records, like the supported tuple locals), method_i8 (i8, sub-word: unknown on Calyx), typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 chained i32 lets: cell count stress). Out of subset: recur_sum (non-tail recursion), method_return and the closure_* samples (closures), native_package_* and mega runtime_native (packages, wider features). HEAVY QUEUE (main, since it needs matrix list edits): add those 7 to the Calyx list in tests/matrix.psd1 and run test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Backend C,Calyx -Suite examples -Native (no accept); keep the ones that agree with C, record why the rest fail. Next: after that run, take the first real failing construct (likely records or i8) as the next codegenCalyx slice")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_10 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:05Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:05 lap 3 grain: predicted the queued run from codegenCalyx's own refusals (spiral_compiler.fs 169807-170250 list every unsupported construct: f32, f64, 64-bit ints, non-primitive ops, tuple values in scalar position, mixed operand widths, non-self-tail recursion, non-bool/int conditions, unit methods or while loops in value position, closures, unsupported conversions, failwith, macros, globals). Records are not on that list because partial evaluation flattens them into per-field variables, and a join point that returns a record becomes a multi-output method, the out0..outN path that tuples already use; so record_value and wgsl_record_loop should compile. method_i8 hits the width check only if an i8 meets a wider operand (its comparison is i8 against an i8 literal, so it should pass); typecheck_long_let_body is 2,000 std_reg cells and groups, a size stress for cider rather than a codegen gap; frontier_hello, zig_exit_wide and native_tail_recursion are plain i32. Expected: all 7 compile; the risk is cider time on the 1,000,000-step countdown. Next: read the run; if countdown times out, keep it off the list with a Timeouts entry instead")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_11 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:20 lap 5 grain: the coverage run no longer needs a matrix edit. test.ps1 has -Probe <backend> (line 18; line 97 adds the probed backend to every sample that has a C row; line 161 writes its outputs to the run scratch; line 41 refuses -Accept), so the 7-candidate check is one probe row: test.ps1 -Suite examples -Backend C -Probe Calyx -Native -Filter on the 7 ids. Queued that row (after the switch window) to replace the matrix-edit row, so main can run it at any time instead of between runs. Same done for OPENQASM3-BACKEND and LEAN-BACKEND.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_12 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:37Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:37 lap 6 grain: checked the probe row against the accepted pack 5 results before it runs. All 7 candidates have a native C row, but frontier_hello is in the frontier suite, so the -Suite examples probe would have silently skipped it. The CALYX and OPENQASM probe rows now say -Suite examples,frontier (test.ps1:23 splits commas, so pwsh -File passes the list fine). C exits for the comparison: frontier_hello 0, zig_exit_wide 305, native_tail_recursion 0, record_value 0, wgsl_record_loop 8, method_i8 0, typecheck_long_let_body 236. Calyx returns main's i32 through spiral_io, so 305 is the one row that also checks a wide exit code through cider and auxin.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_13 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:00 lap 7, correcting lap 3's prediction from the code: codegenCalyx's width function (spiral_compiler.fs 169813-169820) accepts only i32/u32/char (32) and bool (1); every other primitive is unsupported, so samples/method_i8 (pick over i8) fails at compile with 'the primitive Int8T', not at the operand-width check. Expected probe outcome: 6 of 7 compile (frontier_hello, zig_exit_wide, native_tail_recursion, record_value, wgsl_record_loop, typecheck_long_let_body) and method_i8 is a codegen refusal. If sub-word ints are wanted on Calyx, the fix is small: width 8/16 for Int8T/UInt8T/Int16T/UInt16T, with sign extension at comparisons, since Calyx std primitives are width-generic.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_14 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 14u32, "2026-10-19T16:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:34 lap 8 grain: staged the sub-word integer support lap 7 called small: lab/CALYX-BACKEND/subword.py (3 edits in codegenCalyx: width 16 for Int16T/UInt16T and 8 for Int8T/UInt8T, the four literal forms as 8'd/16'd with the two's-complement bit pattern, signed_of covers Int8T/Int16T so comparisons pick the signed std cells). Dry run on today's spiral_compiler.fs: 3 anchors match once. method_i8 uses only an i8 register, equality and literals, so no width conversion is needed (ConvOp stays unsupported). HEAVY QUEUE row added: a Debug candidate with subword.py, then the Calyx probe filtered to method_i8 (or fold it into the next pack)")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_15 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:00 lap 9 grain: checked subword.py's premise against the cached Calyx sources (toolchains/calyx-src/primitives): std_reg (compile.futil:29), std_slt (binary_operators.futil:140, comb, one-bit out) and std_sdiv_pipe are declared with a WIDTH parameter, so an 8- or 16-bit register, signed comparison and division instantiate with no new primitive; the only fixed width is the @external spiral_io memory (32), which main's i32 already fits. So the patch's 3 edits are complete for method_i8; conversions between widths (std_pad/std_slice) remain the next gap if a sample needs them")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_16 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:35Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:35 lap 10 grain: subword.py still dry-runs on the pack-7 tree (3 anchors once); payoff sized by a static scan of samples not on the Calyx list (28 entries): only 2 samples use i8/i16/u8/u16 without floats, strings, arrays, unions, macros or failwith, closure_pair_i8 (needs closures, outside Calyx v1) and method_i8, so subword buys exactly +1 row (method_i8) today; its value is the width-generic path for later samples, not coverage. Next: the -Probe Calyx row, then the subword candidate row (method_i8)")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_17 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:52 lap 11 grain, latest-of-everything check of the native tier: the cached toolchains/calyx-src (cider + auxin build) is at calyxir/calyx main d6bcdc8 (2026-08-18); upstream main is b912213 (2026-10-13), exactly 1 commit ahead and documentation only (installation instructions, #2729), and the last tagged release is still v0.7.1 (2024-02). So the Calyx runner is current; no rebuild needed. Next: the -Probe Calyx row, then the subword candidate row")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_18 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 18u32, "2026-10-19T21:20Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:20 main: pack 9 ACCEPTED 17:20 (single-flight-20261019-161549: 7,057 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 155055); subword.py (helper lane 2) is in: no existing row changed; its own proof is the method_i8 probe queued by helper lane 2, which can now run on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_19 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 19u32, "2026-10-19T22:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 18:42 lap 5 grain: probe rung on the pack 10 compiler (single-flight-20261019-181123, -Probe Calyx): 38 C samples without a Calyx row agree with C, method_i8 among them, so subword.py is proven end to end; 463 compile errors are the documented gaps (floats, strings, unions, arrays); staged Calyx-next.txt for pack 11 Next: pack 11 review and accept")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_20 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 20u32, "2026-10-19T23:04Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:04 drive grain, next feature staged: lab/CALYX-BACKEND/int_widths.py (7 anchored edits to codegenCalyx, dry run by default, writes only after every anchor matched, refuses a file that already has the 64-bit width): i64/u64 become 64-bit registers with 64'd literals and signed ops for Int64T; integer TyConv between any widths (same width = copy, narrowing = std_slice, unsigned widening = std_pad, signed widening = std_pad then std_lsh and std_srsh by the width difference, since cider has no std_signext); bool to and from every int width; if and while conditions on any int width (they hard-coded std_neq(32), which would mismatch an i8 or i64 condition); shift amounts resized to the shifted value's width (i64 >> 1i32 hit operands of different widths). Dry run on today's spiral_compiler.fs: 7 anchors once; applied to a scratch copy, a second apply refuses. The emitted shapes are proven in cider directly: lab int_widths_probe.futil (i8 -5 sign-extended to 64, times 3000000000, sliced to 32, plus u64 max squared sliced) dumps 2179869185 = -2115098111 as i32, the value C computes. F# not compiled yet (no compiler build beside pack 11's review). Expected first rows: samples/i64_scalar and ts_int_wrap (C rows, scalar i64/u64/u8 only). Next: main applies it to a pack candidate after pack 11's accept and runs -Probe Calyx")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_21 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:06Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:06 census of main's 18:42 probe (single-flight-20261019-181123, 463 Calyx compile errors) by cause: strings 148, no Calyx key in a backend switch 44, int arrays 37, f32 25, 64-bit integers 17, f64 10, macros 10, heap 12, closures 12; plus 2 samples on operands of different widths (small_int_ops, small_int_scalar: an i8 shifted by 1i32). int_widths.py addresses those 19, of which 5 use nothing else the backend lacks (i64_scalar, ts_int_wrap, small_int_ops, small_int_scalar, package_default_int_i64); the other 14 also need heap, closures, text or ABI shims. So the first pack row should add about 5 Calyx rows")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_22 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 22u32, "2026-10-19T23:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:25 more evidence for int_widths.py without a compiler build: lab/CALYX-BACKEND/i64_scalar_probe.futil computes every check of samples/i64_scalar with the cells and wiring int_widths.py emits (std_sdiv_pipe(64) 5000000000 / -4, std_mult_pipe(64) 1e6 squared, std_srsh(64) of -8 by a 32-bit count padded to 64, signed i32 to i64 and to u64 through pad + lsh + srsh by 32, std_slice(64, 32), u64 add and logical shift) and cider returns 0, as C does; a negative control with one wrong expected constant returns 1")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_23 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 23u32, "2026-10-20T00:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:00 lap 22 grain, the gap after integer widths, from the long tail of the 18:42 probe: 39 failures stop at a union type, 30 of them non-recursive (tag-only enums like North/East/South/West, Zero/One, and payload unions like Hit : i32 | Miss : i32, Circle | Rect | Empty) and 9 recursive or GADT-indexed. Non-recursive unions fit Calyx the way WGSL does them (SpiralUnionN: a tag plus one field per case payload value), as one tag register plus payload registers per local, a tag compare chain for unbox, and flattened ports for parameters and returns, which the tuple support already has. Strings (148) and f32/f64 (35, no IEEE floats in cider) stay out. Next after main's width pack: draft the non-recursive union lowering")
inl note_of_calyx_backend_24 () = Note ("CALYX-BACKEND", 24u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); Calyx gained 38 rows including method_i8 (subword.py proven).")
inl kotlin_backend () = Task ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", "Kotlin as a full Spiral backend: codegenKotlin in spiral_compiler.fs, the harness Kotlin list, native tier Kotlin 2.4.21 on Temurin JDK 25. 2026-10-11: all 246 listed samples agree with C on both cores (single-flight-20261011-034849, hopac-20261011-042553, restore single-flight-20261011-045745; the 3774-row ladder single-flight-20261011-204644 has Kotlin 246/246, verified 2026-10-12). Representation, semantics and proof details live in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Left: recursive unions, array elements of more than one value, a closure that calls itself, function types other than a plain arrow, recursive/stack/ref layouts, globals, and BackendSwitch records without a Kotlin key; 2026-10-12 closed: 246 rows agree with C on both cores, warm compile server (median 285 ms/row); the Not supported list (recursive unions, multi-value array elements, self-calling closures, stack/ref layouts, globals) moves to BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,compiler", "current", "current")
inl note_of_kotlin_backend_1 () = Note ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", 1u32, "2026-10-16T08:47Z", "main-thread", "04:47 main: pack-1q installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-041645, 6628 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-032411 + Swift probe single-flight-20261016-035425). Kotlin list -> 425 rows. New: main on a 1 GB Thread (1,000,000-deep mutual tail recursion of join points; the JVM has no tail calls), unit main calls spiral_main, recursive unions (UHn_Zero sentinel object emitted on demand), records returned from join points (ty_to_data flattening), kotlin.math float ops + Math.pow, arrays of tuples (SpiralTupleN), multi-scrutinee union unbox (shared tag or -1, like C). Left: a closure that calls itself, non-arrow function types, recursive/stack/ref layouts, globals, Printf (staged tmp/pack-r/printf.ps1).")
inl note_of_kotlin_backend_2 () = Note ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", 2u32, "2026-10-16T10:03Z", "main-thread", "06:03 main: pack-1r installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-053218, 6660 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-050109, Swift 050650, Bend 051447 against the pack-1q bless: 0 changes on listed rows). Printf (System.out.write of the printf_pieces text, flushed) and unit tails inside self-tail loops return; Kotlin 425 -> 429 (printf_portable, op_args_deref_if, wgsl_unit_self_loop, mega runtime_bench). Open: lua_wide_integers needs a conversion Kotlin lacks.")
inl note_of_kotlin_backend_3 () = Note ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", 3u32, "2026-10-16T10:08Z", "main-thread", "06:08 main: diagnosed lua_wide_integers on Kotlin: codegenKotlin TyConv has no 64-bit <-> float pairs (spiral_compiler.fs ~170284-170296 covers f32/f64 -> i32/u32, i64 <-> u64, 64 -> 32); missing Float64/Float32 -> Int64/UInt64 (toLong()/toULong()) and Int64/UInt64 -> Float64/Float32 (toDouble()/toFloat()). Small next-pack item; the sample also needs Printf (in since pack-1r).")
inl note_of_kotlin_backend_4 () = Note ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", 4u32, "2026-10-16T11:57Z", "main-thread", "07:57 main: pack-1s installed both modes (07:23/07:31) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-073131, 6,685 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1r bless 053218 overridden by probes 062511 (Kotlin), 061551 (Swift), 064814 (Python); the one extra row, Bend typecheck_long_let_body, exits 236 like C. ts_int_wrap Python Known -> agree.")
inl note_of_kotlin_backend_5 () = Note ("KOTLIN-BACKEND", 5u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl swift_backend () = Task ("SWIFT-BACKEND", "Swift as a full Spiral backend: codegenSwift in spiral_compiler.fs, the harness Swift list, native tier Windows swiftc 6.4. 2026-10-11: all 246 listed samples agree with C on both cores (the same runs as KOTLIN-BACKEND; the 3774-row ladder single-flight-20261011-204644 has Swift 246/246, verified 2026-10-12). Representation, semantics and proof details live in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Left: recursive unions, array elements of more than one value, a closure that calls itself, function types other than a plain arrow, recursive/stack/ref layouts, globals, and BackendSwitch records without a Swift key; 2026-10-12 closed: 246 rows agree with C; build speed (swiftc ~3 s/row) moves to SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE, unsupported shapes to BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,compiler", "current", "current")
inl note_of_swift_backend_1 () = Note ("SWIFT-BACKEND", 1u32, "2026-10-16T08:47Z", "main-thread", "04:47 main: pack-1q installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-041645, 6628 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-032411 + Swift probe single-flight-20261016-035425). Swift list -> 424 rows. New: main on a WinSDK CreateThread with a 1 GB reserved stack (deep recursive-union frees), harness batch exe linked /STACK:1073741824 instead of importing WinSDK per chunk, unit main calls spiral_main, recursive unions (UHn_Zero() sentinel struct), @escaping function parameters/captures/closure params/function-type domains (~25 native_closure_* build-fails), records returned from join points, CRT f/f64 math, arrays of tuples, multi-scrutinee union unbox. Left: a closure that calls itself, non-arrow function types, recursive/stack/ref layouts, globals, Printf (staged).")
