inl note_of_self_checking_samples_5 () = Note ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:17 lap 4 grain: the harness --test-blocks step named in the lap 1 note does not exist: no script in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts mentions test blocks, so the matrix cannot read a test-block sample yet; the lab slice (lab/SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS/ebpf_family.spi) passes 3/3 through Cells since the mounts fix; design for the missing step: a matrix row kind Cells whose job runs the .spi through spiral cells --gate --metrics and turns each (test, target) pass/fail into one row (backend = target, exit 0 or 1, stdout = the test name), so test blocks get oracle columns like samples Next: write the row-kind as a lab patch for test.ps1 (pack material), proven on the ebpf slice; SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS consumes it")
inl test_signal_metrics () = Task ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", "Test signal and cost metrics drive sample cleanup (2026-10-12, mined from 926 single-flight + 710 hopac runs since 09-26, 362,684 rows): silent rows (no text or outcome change in 20+ runs) are only 3.5% of median cost, so pruning by silence alone saves little; Kotlin is 41% of the total cost (about 92 min native for 253 rows, ~22 s each, JVM kotlinc startup) with 13 rows that ever flipped: batch all Kotlin samples into one kotlinc run (one package per sample) and run each with java -cp; F# is 21% (compile only, mega_omniledger 180 s); the nested-combination families heap_/closure_/union_/array_/case_/method_/mut_ hold ~1,600 rows and about half the cost with at most 50 ever-flipped rows (case_: 323 rows, 0 flips): compact them into composite programs that cover many combinations per compile, keeping every ever-flipped row standalone; highest-signal families: native_ 391 flipped rows, dynamic_ 169, managed_ 108, ts_ 76, frontier_ 52; text-change co-change twins (1,965) are driven by global renames, so redundancy must be measured on outcome flips; next: Kotlin batching in test.ps1, a fast tier (all ever-flipped rows + one per family) for the edit loop, full matrix for bless; Kotlin done 2026-10-12: tests/native-shims/kotlin-server/KotlinServer.java compiles every Kotlin row of a run in one warm JVM (K2JVMCompiler in process, -no-stdlib against kotlin-stdlib.jar), a pre-pass in test.ps1 before the native lanes, lanes only run java -cp; Kotlin row median 285 ms vs ~22 s (single-flight-20261012-050734, all 246 rows agree, bless + reproduction 0 differences); a full single-flight suite 50 -> 26 min; next: same treatment for Swift (swiftc ~3 s/row), compaction of the low-signal families, the fast tier; 2026-10-12 12:00 recomputed over all single-flight runs (6,079 rows, 4,011 with >=5 runs, summed row time 8,162 s): signal (output changes + 2x status flips) concentrates in native_ (3,546), dynamic_ (1,218), managed_ (1,084), frontier_ (779), ts_ (631); silent cost concentrates in array_ (200 of 270 rows silent, 1,106 s), heap_ (205/276, 978 s), union_ (116/192), method_ (91/126), mut_ (65/90); negative_/contract_ rows are silent by design (stable rejections); coverage-first fast tiers (every backend and family, then signal per second): 300 s = 734 rows / 3.7% of the time / 51% of historical signal, 600 s = 1,098 / 7.4% / 73%, 1,200 s = 1,657 / 14.7% / 88%; lists in the job scratch (metrics/fast-tier-*.tsv, compaction.py); next: a harness fast suite from the 600 s list (matrix.psd1, compiler lane) and folding the silent combinatorial families into fewer test blocks (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS); caveat: signal = changed during development, a proxy, not proof a row catches bugs; 2026-10-14 00:43 fast suite built (uncommitted): pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast; tmp/scripts/fast-tier.ps1 mines this mode's <cache>/runs/*/results.tsv at run time (~21 s, examples/ ids folded into samples/, runs >20% timeouts skipped) and writes <run dir>/fast-tier.tsv, so no generated list goes into the tree (matrix.psd1 keeps only the hand-written policy Fast = budget 600 s, mature 5 runs, timeout share 0.2, cost = median of the last 7 runs); passes: cheapest row per backend and family, every row with <5 runs, then signal per second; a non-C row brings its C oracle row inside the budget; rows absent from the latest full run always run; -Bless/-Record refuse it; recomputed over 916 single-flight runs (reference single-flight-20261013-225859, 3,872 rows, summed median cost 5,866 s, signal 9,908): 300 s = 455 rows / 5.1% / 29.5% of signal, 600 s = 725 / 10.2% / 55.9%, 1,200 s = 1,297 / 20.5% / 79.2%, every backend and family covered (the 10-12 figures were lower-cost because examples/ and samples/ halves counted as separate rows and C oracle rows were not budgeted); gpt5parallel over 716 hopac runs: 462 rows / 6.0% of 9,950 s / 60.8%; static checks: both scripts parse, all 725 fast keys map to -Suite all jobs (4,094 jobs; the 222 new Bend rows have no history yet and run as unseen); not run (main thread owns test.ps1 runs); next: one pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native on the main thread, compare wall time to the budget and check for no false REGRESSED, then the Swift warm-compile treatment and SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS; 2026-10-14 01:57 cost by backend now (normalized history, summed median row cost 5,866 s): Gleam 1,261 s for 129 rows (~9.8 s/row, 21%, signal 544), Rust 911 s (signal 3,081), Swift 781 s for 248 rows (13%, signal 157), Lean 590 s, C 531 s, Delphi 413 s, F# 302 s, Zig 297 s, Bend 211 s, Kotlin 132 s after the warm server; so the next warm-compile batching targets are Gleam (one gleam build per row) and Swift; silent rows (no residual change or outcome flip in >=5 runs): 1,708 of 3,868 mature rows (44%) holding 1,459 s (25%), led by array_ 190 rows/270 s, heap_ 202/217 s, mega 252/200 s, union_ 120/119 s, ebpf_ 114/92 s, method_ 90/92 s, mut_ 65/74 s; contract_ 330 rows are silent by design (stable rejections, 47 s); 2026-10-14 02:22 redundancy measured on outcome flips (job scratch metrics/coflip.ps1, 916 single-flight runs, examples/ ids folded): 5,613 rows ever seen, 992 ever flipped an outcome (compile status or native status/exit/oracle), but those 992 rows have only 157 distinct flip histories (the run indices where they flipped): 835 rows are co-flip twins of another row and only 76 rows have a unique history; the largest groups are whole backend lanes flipping in the same runs (146 Wasm rows, 125 Zig, 72 Bend, 51 Rust, 44 Bend, 42 Gleam, 35 Lua, 33 Rust array_), i.e. most outcome flips are backend-wide events (toolchain, prelude, codegen switch) that one row per backend would have caught; next for the tier: keep one cheapest row per flip-history group and spend the freed budget on rows with unique histories; caveat: no-toolchain/ran transitions count as flips, so environment changes inflate the groups; 2026-10-14 02:28 fast tier made redundancy-aware (decided after measuring both): a pass now takes the cheapest row of every distinct outcome-flip history before the signal-per-second pass, and that pass skips rows that only repeat a covered history with no residual change; 600 s: 671 rows, 10.2% of cost, all 130 flip histories of the current rows, 49.3% of the raw signal (before: 725 rows, 85 of 130 histories, 55.9% raw signal, the difference being co-flip twins counted again); 300 s: 316 rows, 129/130, 19.8%; 1,200 s: 1,237 rows, 130/130, 78.1%; fixed in review: test.ps1 added keys through a subexpression that enumerates the HashSet (fails on the first row), now plain if/else, its loop checked standalone against the list (671 fast, 3,201 rest); a signal-pass break that could stop early is now continue; tmp/AGENTS.md documents -Suite fast with these numbers and notes that unseen rows run outside the budget (222 new Bend rows, ~947 jobs until a full run); 2026-10-14 03:14 Gleam lane measured (a copy of the warm <cache>/scratch/single-flight/gleam-native-0 project, 5 samples): gleam build 2.2-3.8 s, then gleam run -m spiral_run 3.1-4.4 s, but erl -noshell -pa build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval 'spiral_run:main().' runs the same build in 1.1-1.4 s with the same exit codes (0, 42) and byte-identical stdout (printf_portable, portable_console_lines, static_string); so replacing gleam run with a direct erl call in test.ps1's Gleam lane (around line 482) saves about 2 s per row (~260 s of Gleam's 1,261 s), and compiling every Gleam row of a run as modules of one project with one gleam build (like the Kotlin warm server) would remove most of the remaining ~2.5 s build per row; not changed tonight because the main thread is running and blessing suites with this harness; 2026-10-14 03:42 Gleam batching prototyped outside the harness: 40 sample residuals copied as modules prog_k with one runner module run_k each into a copy of the warm gleam-native-0 project: one gleam build for all 40 took 5.5 s (vs about 2.7 s per row today, ~108 s), then erl -noshell -pa build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval 'run_k:main().' per program 1.07 s each (42.8 s); all 40 exit codes and stdout hashes equal single-flight-20261013-225859's Gleam rows; so a Gleam pre-pass in test.ps1 (like the Kotlin warm server: write every Gleam row as a module of one project, one build, lanes only run erl) cuts the lane about 5x (40 rows: ~48 s vs ~240 s); running all programs in one VM with captured group leaders would cut the 1 s erl start too; left for the main thread since it is running and blessing suites with this harness; 2026-10-14 03:59 budget curve with the flip-history pass (single-flight, 3,872 rows, 5,866 s summed): 300 s 316 rows 19.8% of raw signal 129/130 histories; 450 s 539 rows 35.0% 130/130; 600 s 671 rows 49.3%; 900 s 962 rows 68.7%; 1,200 s 1,237 rows 78.1%; 1,800 s 1,632 rows 86.6%; gpt5parallel at 600 s: 346 rows, 6.0% of 9,950 s, 50.7% of raw signal, 197 of 200 histories; 2026-10-14 04:14 fast runs no longer feed the signal: a run directory holding fast-tier.tsv updates last-seen state and costs but adds no changes or flips, so picked rows do not gain signal by being picked (600 s tier unchanged: 671 rows, 130/130 histories, no fast runs exist yet); documented in tmp/AGENTS.md; stopped 04:14: the next step is the main thread's first pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native (compare wall time with the 600 s budget, expect no false REGRESSED), then the Gleam pre-pass and Swift batching in test.ps1 and SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "harness,metrics,samples,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_signal_metrics () = Planning ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_test_signal_metrics_1 () = Note ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:50Z", "main-thread", "08:50 cost shares re-measured on single-flight-20261014-073517 (4,094 jobs, 3.77 h of summed compile+native): Gleam 27.0% (129 rows, ~28 s each, a cold gleam build + run per row), Rust 13.4%, Swift 10.5%, Bend 10.2%, C 8.6%, Lean 8.0%, Delphi 5.5%, Zig 5.0%, F# 2.9% (compile only), Wgsl 2.4%. Kotlin left the top 10 since the warm Kotlin server (was 41%). Next levers: Gleam pre-pass (lane B), Swift batching (SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE, main), then the composite-program compaction of the nested families.")
