inl note_of_ml_labs_4 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:40 helper-lane lap 1: scoped the Leduc Lab's real dependencies (read-only greps of C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library, still 80d6a1a of 2026-02-05; git fetch shows no upstream commits since): game/leduc opens corebase, corecuda, coreext, corepython, serializerm, tensorm, game, model_cfr, ml.cem_models and ml.cfr_models, and its qualified calls lean on refm 32, arraym 20, random 20, rangem 11, deckm 9, optionm 8, sa_listm 8, tensorm 8, plistm 5, jsonm 4. So the Leduc Lab is not CPU-standalone: it reaches corecuda and corepython (CUDA kernels through Python), and the CPU reference needs those opens split out first. corebase itself is what CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is adopting as the slim core, so that migration is the natural first step for both labs.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_5 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:26 helper-lane lap 2: per-file split of game/leduc for a CPU-only Leduc Lab: corebase-only today are cardm (7 lines) and base (2); game (73 lines) needs corecuda only for corecuda.random.shuffle (one call), and deckm (24) opens corecuda for its random module; model_cfr/model_cem (78/80) add tensorm plus ml.*_models; types, full, play_*, train_* (63-190 lines each) pull corepython and serializerm (the Python/CUDA runtime). So the CPU rules core (cardm, deckm, game) is 104 lines that become CPU-only by giving them a CPU random shuffle (a seeded Fisher-Yates over corebase arrays) in place of corecuda.random; the CFR/CEM models are the second slice.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_6 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:00 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the CPU replacement for the one GPU dependency of the Leduc rules core: deckm.draw_card uses corecuda random (u32_ubound over intrinsic.popc_u32, then sample_masked_bit_u32); lab/ML-LABS/cpu_deck.spi does the same draw on a CPU - a seeded xorshift32 step, popcount by clearing the lowest bit, and the k-th set bit of the 6-bit deck mask (k = rng mod popcount) - with cardm's u32_to_card mapping (0-1 King, 2-3 Queen, 4-5 Jack) and draw_all emptying the deck. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded): exit 111 = every one of the 6 cards drawn exactly once, the same seed gives the same sequence, the next seed a different one. Next: drop it into a lab copy of deckm.spi in place of corecuda.random/intrinsic and compile cardm+deckm+game with the ML library's own corebase (default int i32, f32).")
inl note_of_ml_labs_7 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:40 helper-lane lap 4: before porting deckm/game, checked which backends corebase itself can reach, and it narrows the Leduc Lab's CPU target: corebase's static arrays and lists are backend_switch macros over a C++ static_array<el,dim> (sam.spi 4-7: CppHost, CudaHost, CudaDevice, plus a Python static_array) and sa_listm's push/pop/unsafe_set_length are C++ method macros (sa_listm.spi), so game.spi's table (sa 2 int pots, sa 2 card) and random_action's sa_list 3 compile only for CppHost/Cuda/Python, never for C, Rust or Zig. corecuda.random.shuffle (random.spi) is a plain Knuth loop over index/set/length with int_range on u32_ubound, so only the bound draw is GPU-specific. Plan for the CPU slice: target CppHost (C++ host build, no CUDA) with a CPU rng (the xorshift draw from cpu_deck.spi, already exit 111 on C) behind u32_ubound and popc; a C/Rust/Zig Leduc would need sam/sa_listm arms for those backends first, which is a corebase port, not a Leduc one.")
inl package_registry_design () = Task ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", "Package manager design: exact caller-pinned locks (the prototype's plan format), declared capabilities checked at install, index separate from blob storage (crates.io model: index + object storage/CDN; Cloudflare R2 candidate), signatures; social platforms (X/Bluesky/Mastodon) only as optional mirrors and discovery, never the source of truth; 2026-10-14 02:20 lane 3 typed model (not compiled; C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\registry\\model.spi, 290 lines of Spiral nominals/unions/records + operation types), built from the prototype's planner (geandra_v0_4.py validate_lock/plan_install: schema, caller-pinned root, digest-keyed nodes, iterative DFS order, cycle/unreachable rejection, grants vs declared capabilities, offline/budget): exact locks (exact_version, exact_requirement carries the dependency's package_id, RootDiffersFromCallerPin), declared capabilities as a union (files by path scope, network host, process, env, foreign library per backend, GPU, clock) checked against granted ones at install (CapabilitiesNotGranted lists package*capability), index separate from blobs (signed append-only index_checkpoint chain of Released/Yanked entries vs content-addressed blob_origin ObjectStore bucket/key e.g. R2, social platforms only as MirrorOnly with a required authoritative digest), detached Ed25519 signatures for releases and checkpoints; PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS: ModuleDirectory packages live as immutable directories in a content-addressed store and are linked into the workspace by DirectoryJunction (files by hard link or copy; errors JunctionTargetNotDirectory, StoreEntryMutable); editions and default numerics are per release (edition UpstreamEdition2|ForkEdition3, numeric_defaults) and travel to the compiler as one compile_unit per package, which needs the compiler to hold DefaultEnv per package instead of per process; correction: model.spi is 284 lines, not 290", Story, P3, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "packages,registry", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_package_registry_design () = Planning ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Pair)
