inl note_of_package_registry_design_5 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user direction 2026-10-17: the Rust/cargo ecosystem (rayon, many small crates under the 1k-line gate, compiled in parallel) is the end-product target to the end, not Zig and its package manager, and no dynamic languages for end products. So the registry design builds on cargo: Spiral packages map to crates, locks pin crate versions and Spiral package digests together, and the 1k-line crate split is a design input the registry must make cheap (many tiny crates, one lock).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_6 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:40 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix: the 10-14 typed model lived only in job tmp (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\registry\\model.spi, 285 lines, comment-free); copied into spiral/lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi (original left). Drift found against note 5 (user 10-17: build on cargo, Spiral packages map to crates): the model has 0 mentions of cargo or crate; its versioning is exact_version + exact_requirement over sha256 package_id/blob_id with its own index_checkpoint and blob_origin (R2 bucket/object_key) and social mirrors. Under the cargo direction, release_manifest needs a crate mapping (crate name, crate version, features, Cargo.lock pin) and the own index/blob store becomes optional (crates.io or a cargo alternative registry index).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_7 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:27 helper-lane lap 2: brought the lab model in line with the cargo direction (note 5): lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi gains nominal crate_name and cargo_feature, type cargo_crate {crate; crate_version : exact_version; features}, union lock_pin = SpiralSource : exact_requirement | CargoLock : cargo_crate * sha256_hex (the Cargo.lock checksum), and release_manifest gets rust_crates : list cargo_crate and pins : list lock_pin next to its Spiral requires. Spiral sources keep sha256 path identity (exact_requirement with package_id); Rust dependencies are pinned the way Cargo.lock pins them. Both the 10-14 model and this revision type-check on today's compiler (try.ps1 C with a trivial probe main, exit 0).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_8 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:00 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the index decision into the model (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi, type-checks via try.ps1 C, exit 0): union index_backend = OwnContentAddressedIndex url | OwnIndexWithStaticMirror url * url | CargoSparseRegistry url, union rust_resolution = CratesIoWithLock | AlternativeRegistry url, a sparse_index_path union mirroring cargo's 1/2/3/prefixed name layout, and registry_choice. Decision recorded in chosen_registry: Spiral packages keep the own sha256 content-addressed index served as static files with a mirror (a GitHub Pages copy plus a raw-git copy of the same tree), because their identity is the source hash (exact_requirement, package_id) and cargo's sparse index keys by name+semver, which cannot express that; Rust crates resolve the normal cargo way (crates.io plus the Cargo.lock checksum pins already in lock_pin), so no alternative registry is needed. CargoSparseRegistry stays a case only for the day Spiral packages ship as Rust crates by name.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_9 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:40 helper-lane lap 4: added read_index's path function to the model, index_path name (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi): the own index uses cargo's sparse layout so the same static-file tooling and caching rules apply - 1/name, 2/name, 3/first-char/name, otherwise first-two/next-two/name - over sm.slice (inclusive end) and sm.length from the core. Probe path_probe.spi checks a, ab, abc and spiral (expected exit 15: 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc, sp/ir/spiral); unrun until main's freeze all-clear. Cargo lowercases names for the path; Spiral package names are already lowercase identifiers, so no case folding is modelled.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_10 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: path_probe.spi exits 15 as predicted - index_path gives 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc and sp/ir/spiral.")
inl social_transport_sim () = Task ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", "X-like simulator as Kino's battle test (user 2026-10-12: deferring it was a bad call; it truly battle-tests Kino): a deterministic, seeded event simulator of many humans/agents publishing Spiral cells as posts (post, reply = downstream cell, quote/link = upstream dependency, edit = new revision, delete, like = demand/interest, unlike = withdrawn demand), with delayed, duplicate, reordered and stale deliveries and cancelled consumers, driving Kino's real compile/run path (warm server, concurrent cells over shared prefixes); invariants checked on every schedule: each visible result equals the sequential re-run of its exact revision, stale revisions never surface, shared prefixes compile once (cache hits counted), withdrawn demand cancels work and frees memory, no cross-request contamination (hopac fix 57 class), equivalent reorderings commute; phase 1: event log format + replay runner + 5 invariants + a 100-cell scenario from lib notebooks; phase 2: scale (10^4 cells), memory ceilings, agent personas; later: transport frames (280-char, twitter-text v3 weighting, NFC) and optional real X/Bluesky/Mastodon mirrors; 2026-10-12 phase 1 built (apps/xsim, untracked, Elixir, path dep on apps/kino): Scenario (cells of any .spi via Kino parse_spi -> posts: definitions as a reply chain, tests replying to the latest definition; seeded likes/unlikes/edits; deliveries shuffled in a window with duplicates), World (idempotent per event index, causal per post+user, replies/edits/likes before their post buffered), Runner (demand-driven: wanted posts -> prefix chain key -> one task per key, cancel on withdrawn demand or edit, results in completion order), Invariants (sequential equivalence vs a fresh baseline, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand hidden, commute across schedules), fault knobs (naive_visibility, cancel: false, rerun_completed, leaking executor) each proven to trip its invariant; mix test 8/8 x5 seeds; mix xsim.run on lib math/date_time/sm' with the fake executor: up to 195 posts, 1,068 deliveries, 104 duplicates, 80 buffered, all invariants ok, commute ok; a leaking executor fails sequential equivalence and commute; next: --executor kino on Kino's new in-memory Notebook.evaluate (agent B), then scale and memory ceilings; 11:55 first runs on real Kino (Notebook.evaluate): two genuine findings, KINO-SOURCE-DIAGNOSTICS (temp paths make outputs nondeterministic) and TEST-PRELUDE-SEMANTICS (tests depend on an earlier static test's open); xsim now models Kino's linear prefixes (tests on the trunk, --branch tests optional) and normalizes temp paths; 12:15 simulator made incremental (prefix key = H(parent key, source), edits re-key only their subtree, the runner only touches posts each delivery changed, refcounted prefix keys, lazy prefix sources): full lib corpus (36 notebooks with tests as interleaved author threads, 1,651 posts, 5,916 deliveries, 529 duplicates, 4 edits + 2 breaking edits per thread) 626.5 s -> 17.5 s simulator time with the fake executor, ~linear (228/535/1,651 posts: 2.3/5.1/17.5 s), every invariant and commute ok; tests 10/10 (new: root edit re-keys the subtree); multi-notebook corpus (--spi a,b,...) and --breaking edits added; 12:25 transport frames: Xsim.Frames splits a cell into numbered frames within 280 weighted characters (twitter-text v3 ranges: Latin weight 1, CJK/Hangul 2; NFC; split at grapheme boundaries; header \"<post> k/n\"), World buffers frames per post and applies the rebuilt post at its earliest frame index once complete (parent missing -> pending), --frames in mix xsim.run; tests 12/12 (Hangul cell -> several frames, each <= 280, shuffled rebuild exact; framed corpus with 30% duplicates and a 20-wide shuffle window keeps every invariant and commutes with the unframed run); 12:20 real-Kino finding KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE; 12:40 --churn N (N edits cycling the root of each thread, woven through the stream: agent-rewrite storms re-key every prefix and cancel in-flight work) exposed an over-strict invariant, not a runner bug: cancelled-then-restarted prefixes counted as double compiles (14 cancels, 5 keys restarted); one_compile_per_prefix is now starts - cancellations <= 1 per key (the rerun_completed fault still trips it); --save/--replay delivery logs (JSON lines) for exact bug reproduction; full lib corpus with frames: 1,651 posts -> 6,643 events, ~8,000 deliveries incl. ~1,330 duplicates, all invariants + commute ok in 20-24 s per schedule; tests 14/14; 12:50 phase-2 scale reached with the fake executor: lib corpus x6 as 216 author threads (--spi-file, since mix.bat caps the command line at 8,191 chars), 9,906 posts -> 40,585 events -> 46,604 deliveries incl. 6,019 duplicates, all framed, with edits, breaking edits and root churn: every invariant + commute ok, 118.9 s simulator time, peak BEAM 110 MB, 300 s wall incl. the sequential baseline; next: real-Kino scenarios at growing size, memory ceilings of the daemon side, agent personas; 13:20 in CI (uncommitted): apps/xsim/build.ps1 (mix test; the framed lib corpus with edits, breaking edits and churn over 2 schedules + commute; unless -fast a cold 2-concurrent real-Kino scenario that guards KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE, then the daemon is stopped) runs from spiral scripts/build.ps1 after the lib notebooks; local -fast run 190 s wall incl. deps and compile; 14:20 semantic oracle added after the KINO-C-FALSE-PASS report: breaking edits are known-wrong revisions, so on the real Kino path any visible ok result for a post whose current revision is a breaking edit is a violation (breaking_edits_fail); a lenient executor that passes everything is caught, an honest one is not (test); the concurrent-vs-sequential invariants alone cannot see a false pass because both sides are wrong the same way; tests 15/15; 18:53: CI run 38312827285 (spiral 17:47 commit) failed in apps/xsim mix test: the churn test asserted map_size(results) > 6, which depends on timing (a fast runner cancels every stale revision, leaving exactly 6); replaced with a deterministic check (the root ends at the last churn edit); 15/15 pass on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42, 1234; uncommitted; 2026-10-14 01:14 agent personas (uncommitted): Xsim.Personas, mix xsim.run --personas lurker=N,fickle=N,editor=N,breaker=N; lurkers like about half the posts, fickle users toggle like/unlike 5 times on one post (withdrawn demand, cancel and restart), editors rewrite one definition 3 times and then revert to the original source (ABA: the prefix key returns to an earlier value), breakers push a breaking test edit and then fix it back (the broken source joins scenario.broken); persona streams interleave round-robin and weave with churn; fixed an off-by-one: --edits 0 produced one edit; tests 25/25 on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42 (new: 4 schedules with 30% duplicates and a 10-wide window, every invariant, breaking_edits_fail with an honest executor, every post ends at its original source, the schedules commute; an unknown persona raises); full lib corpus (36 notebooks) with personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: 1,662 posts, 8,467 events, about 9,730 deliveries, 5,690-5,745 prefixes, 47-54 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 17.5-21.6 s per schedule, peak BEAM 70 MB; next: the real-Kino size ladder with daemon memory ceilings (script ready at the job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: isolated port 13977, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP), waiting for the heavy gate; 2026-10-14 02:04 real-Kino size ladder (job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: its own daemon on port 13977 with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN=3, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP, users 2, 2 schedules, concurrency 2, through the heavy gate): math.spi 3 cells: 3 prefixes, 52 s cold + 10 s, daemon tree peak 1,464 MB; 6 cells: 5 prefixes, 69-101 s + 22-34 s, 1,735-1,858 MB; 12 cells: 10 prefixes, 201 s + 103 s, 2,353 MB (one pool compiler reached 1,116 MB); 8 cells with --memo and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1: 8 prefixes, 43.8 s then 0.8 s (memo hits 15 then 43 cumulative, misses 13), 2,331 MB, every invariant and commute ok; daemon tree = erl 58 MB + 3 pool compilers (dotnet 374-1,116 MB) + 3 pwsh compiler_bridge relays at 101-114 MB each (about 320 MB, filed as KINO-BRIDGE-OVERHEAD); genuine finding: the 6-cell rung failed sequential equivalence and commute only because a failing Rust cell prints the OS thread id of its panic (thread '<unnamed>' (52388) panicked), so two runs of one revision differ; xsim's Invariants.comparable now normalizes the id as it did temp paths (test added, 26/26), rerun passes; Kino-side normalization filed as KINO-PANIC-THREAD-ID; apps/xsim/build.ps1's CI run now passes --personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 (that exact command: pass, 102 s wall); 2026-10-14 02:17 phase-2 scale with personas (fake executor): lib corpus x6 (216 author threads) with frames, edits, breaking edits, churn and personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 per thread: 9,972 posts -> 50,647 events -> about 58,160 deliveries incl. about 7,510 duplicates, 22,638-22,888 prefixes, 63-64 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 89-93 s per schedule, peak BEAM 147 MB, 467 s wall; xsim tests 26/26 on seeds 0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026; daemon-side ceiling observed on the real path: each pool compiler recycles past about 1.2 GB (logged at 1,228-1,318 MB, KINO-DAEMON-MEMORY policy), so with 3 compilers plus 3 pwsh relays and erl the daemon tree is bounded near 4.3 GB; 2026-10-14 02:21 full math.spi on real Kino with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 22 posts, 80 events, about 92 deliveries, 21 prefixes, 2-3 cancels, schedule 1 291 s then schedule 2 5.2 s (memo hits 179 then 390 cumulative, misses 32), daemon tree peak 3,115 MB (compilers 788/803/1,087 MB, 3 recycles at 1,228-1,318 MB), 32.7 min wall including the sequential baselines; sequential equivalence, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand and commute all ok, but breaking_edits_fail flagged f0p4: an xsim oracle bug, not a Kino false pass: the breaking edit bumped the last digit run, turning 7.3890560989306495 into 7.3890560989306496 (the same f64, and inside _assert_approx_eq's tolerance), so the edit was not breaking; Scenario.break_source now bumps the whole literal's integer part (7.389... -> 8.389..., 9 -> 10), shared by --breaking and the breaker persona, with a test (27/27 on seeds 0, 1, 42); 2026-10-14 02:48 fix verified on real Kino: math.spi 5 cells with --breaking 1 (the edit now turns the f0p4 test's 7.3890560989306495 into 8.3890560989306495) on its own daemon: 5 prefixes, 53 s + 18 s, every invariant ok including breaking_edits_fail (the cell now fails honestly), commute ok, daemon tree 1,796 MB; xsim tests 27/27 on 69 seeds (0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026 and 100-159); 2026-10-14 03:00 the --edits 0 fix changes build.ps1's cold real-Kino race guard (math.spi 3 cells, users 2, edits 0, 1 schedule, concurrency 2) from 8 to 7 events; that exact scenario on a cold isolated daemon still passes with peak_inflight=2 (3 prefixes, 32.7 s, every invariant ok, daemon tree 1,226 MB, 47.8 s wall); churn-heavy persona stress over math+date_time+sm' (319 posts, 2,000+ events, 40-50% duplicates, churn 6-12, fickle 6-8, breaker 4-6) passes on seeds 1-16 at concurrency 1, 3 and 8; 2026-10-14 03:26 simulator-side memory ceiling: Runner evict: true / mix xsim.run --evict frees a prefix's result when its last demand goes (withdrawn like, edit, re-key), recomputes on renewed demand, counts evictions in one_compile_per_prefix (starts - cancels - evictions <= 1) and adds the invariant unwanted_results_freed (every held result belongs to a wanted post); summary gains peak_results and evictions; full lib corpus with personas, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: results held 1,608 instead of about 5,700 (-72%), about 4,000 evictions per schedule, starts 5,657-5,689 vs 5,744-5,792 kept (no extra compiles at this scale), every invariant and commute ok; on math+date_time+sm' with churn 6: 316 held instead of 1,548-1,599 at +1.4-4% starts; tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 (new: eviction commutes with keeping, never holds more results, evictions happen); the default stays off so earlier numbers compare; 2026-10-14 03:37 second notebook on real Kino: date_time.spi 20 cells with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 20 posts, 70 events, about 80 deliveries, 20 prefixes, schedule 1 94.5 s then 3.1 s (memo hits 131 then 306 cumulative, misses 44), every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail and commute ok, daemon tree peak 2,181 MB (one compiler at 1,190 MB, 5 recycles in the daemon log), 11 min wall; my daemon port 13977 is free again; next: multi-notebook real-Kino corpora (math+date_time interleaved threads) and --evict against Kino's memo; 2026-10-14 03:39 eviction at phase-2 scale (lib corpus x6, same options as the 9,972-post run): results held 8,232 instead of about 22,700 (-64%), but 24,250-24,798 starts instead of 22,701-22,952 (+7-8% recomputes from renewed demand after churn and fickle toggles), 148-188 s per schedule instead of 89-93 s, peak BEAM 133 vs 147 MB; every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and commute ok; so dropping results on withdrawn demand trades about 8% more compiles for a bounded result store, which is the policy question for Kino's memo (keep, evict, or evict with a grace window); 2026-10-14 03:44 correction: the 4.3 GB daemon-tree bound above is an estimate from the recycle threshold (3 compilers at about 1.3 GB, relays, erl), not a measurement; the highest measured peak is 3,115 MB (full math.spi); 2026-10-14 04:09 first multi-notebook real-Kino run (math.spi + date_time.spi, 10 cells each, as two interleaved author threads via --spi-file; a comma list through mix.bat had silently kept only math): 20 posts, 91 events, about 105 deliveries, 16 prefixes, with --memo, --evict and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: schedule 1 224 s then 21 s (memo hits 52 then 121 cumulative, misses 18-19), 0 evictions needed, every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and breaking_edits_fail ok, commute ok; daemon tree peak 3,398 MB (compilers 877/981/1,104 MB), the highest measured so far; 16.6 min wall; evict stress on 3 notebooks passes on seeds 20-35 at concurrency 1 and 6; xsim tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 and 200-259; next: a daemon-memory sampler in xsim itself (today the ladder script reads the tree after the run), larger multi-notebook corpora on real Kino when the gate is quiet, and Kino's memo policy under eviction; 2026-10-14 04:14 final code checked as CI runs it: pwsh apps/xsim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (mix test, then the personas corpus run) exit 0 in 117 s: 1,662 posts, about 9,730 deliveries, every invariant and commute ok; 2026-10-14 04:26 review fixes: --breaking and the breaker persona now only pick test cells whose source Scenario.break_source actually changes (a tail like pair.1 was picked, left unchanged and then flagged as a false breaking_edits_fail violation), and the fickle persona returns no events on an empty thread instead of crashing; test added, 29/29 on seeds 0-19", Epic, P1, D7, 82u32, Active, "", "social,simulator,cells,stress,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_social_transport_sim () = Planning ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_1 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:36Z", "lane-b", "daemon-memory sampler written in a private copy (apps/xsim lib/xsim/daemon_memory.ex + mix xsim.run --daemon-memory): finds the daemon pid from Kino's hello banner, samples its process tree's working set every 1 s from one pwsh sampler, prints 'daemon pid= peak_mb= procs= samples=' per schedule; test queued behind the heavy gate")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_2 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 2u32, "2026-10-14T20:17Z", "lane-b", "sampler proven on real Kino (private copy, apps/xsim daemon_memory.ex + xsim.run --daemon-memory; the pwsh port needs :hide on Windows or it exits silently): math.spi 4 cells, 2 schedules, every invariant + commute ok, daemon tree peak 1,150 MB over 7 processes, 36 samples; xsim tests 30/30")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_3 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 3u32, "2026-10-15T09:07Z", "lane-b", "05:07 lane B: scale step (fake executor, whole lib/spiral: 76 .spi, 200 users, 5 edits, 20% duplicates, concurrency 16, xsim on the tree Kino with tonight's landed changes): 1,960 posts, 217,004 events, ~260k deliveries (43k duplicates), ~6,000 starts over ~5,900 prefixes, 120-133 cancels per schedule; every invariant ok on both schedules and commute ok; 158 s and 200 s per schedule, peak BEAM 171/258 MB; xsim tests 30/30; 10^4 cells not reached yet (the lib yields ~2k posts; --churn or a second corpus would scale it)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_4 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: scale run: whole lib/spiral, 1,960 posts, 217k events, invariants ok, peak BEAM 258 MB (lane B, 10-15). Next grain: run it against the pack-1h compiler with concurrency raised.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_5 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 5u32, "2026-10-16T03:11Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:11 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) apps/xsim moved to the i574n/x repo as apps/sim (user request: everything X-related in x). It depends on the sibling spiral/apps/kino by path, so it still battle-tests Kino from downstream: from x, mix test 30/30 and the lib-corpus run (2 schedules, every invariant ok, commute ok). spiral's scripts/build.ps1 and CI no longer run it; its kino-executor run now uses a private daemon port (13950) and stops only that daemon.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_6 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 6u32, "2026-10-18T07:25Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 03:25 new mix xsim.world: writes the X state a scenario leaves (framed posts as reply chains, edits as new versions with edit_history_post_ids, like and reply counts, optional lost frames) as an e2e stub world plus the expected program; x client e2e uses it, apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates and compares the fixtures. Elixir until SIM-IN-SPIRAL")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_7 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 7u32, "2026-10-18T08:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 04:34 found while adding the xsim.world drift guard: (1) on Windows mix.bat drops everything after a comma in a bare argument, so build.ps1's corpus run '--personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2' ran lurker=2 only; Personas.parse now also accepts '+' and the scripts use it (test added); (2) JSON.encode! writes map keys in atom-creation order (OTP 26+), which differs across builds; xsim.world writes key-sorted JSON (Xsim.XWorld.json). Guard now passes across MIX_BUILD_PATHs")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_8 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 8u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 x/apps/sim follow-ups to KINO-RENAME: --executor kino is now --executor cells (Xsim.Executors.cells; DaemonMemory takes daemon_port), verified on real Cells (math.spi 3 cells, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail, commute ok, daemon tree 934 MB via --daemon-memory, which landed from lane B's private copy); --spi lists accept '+' like --personas (mix.bat comma bug). With the persona fix the CI corpus run grew from 1,662 to 1,714 posts (10,002 events, ~11,500 deliveries), every invariant and commute ok. Left in this epic: real-Cells corpora beyond 2 notebooks, Cells' memo policy under eviction, 10^4 cells on the real path")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_9 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 9u32, "2026-10-18T20:46Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:46 edits travel as frames too: Frames.explode_posts turns {:edit, id, source} into {:edit_frame, id, rev, k, n, body} (rev counts a post's edits, so frames of different edits never mix; an empty source still gets one frame), the World gathers each revision's frames and applies the edit at its first frame's index, waiting for the post if needed; summary gains framed_edits. Test: 3 edits + 1 breaking edit framed at 20 weight, 30% duplicates, 12-wide shuffle: every invariant ok, framed_edits = edits, commutes with the unframed run; sim tests 37/37")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_10 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 10u32, "2026-10-18T21:18Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 17:18 grace-window eviction built: Runner evict: {:grace, n} (mix xsim.run --evict-grace N) marks a result for eviction n deliveries after its last demand goes, drops the mark when demand returns (counted as rescues when the result was held), flushes all marks at the end so unwanted_results_freed still holds; test: fickle+editor+churn scenario keeps every invariant, commutes with keep, ends with no marks (timing-dependent counts left out of the test). First measurement, lib corpus 1,714 posts, frames, churn, personas, 1 schedule each: keep starts 9,900 peak 9,809; evict 10,100 (+2.0%) peak 1,665; grace25 10,344 peak 1,705; grace100 10,769 peak 1,853 rescues 7; grace400 10,032 (+1.3%) peak 2,138 rescues 116; all invariants ok. Starts vary run to run with cancel timing, so repeats decide (preliminary)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_11 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 11u32, "2026-10-18T22:27Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:27 decision on the result-store policy (memo under eviction), measured: total starts are dominated by cancel/restart timing (keep 11,092 vs evict 9,822 mean over 3 runs under machine contention), so the runner now counts recomputes (a key queued again after its result was evicted). Lib corpus, 1,714 posts, 2-3 runs each: evict 261 recomputes (2.6% of ~10k compiles), peak 1,664 results (-85% vs keep ~11,000); grace100 220 (-16%), peak 1,790; grace400 152 (-42%), peak 2,202 (-80% vs keep), 104 rescues; every invariant ok in every run. Chosen for Cells' memo: evict with a grace window (~400 deliveries): on the real path a recompute is a compile of seconds while a held result is a small text, so -42% recomputes for +32% held results (still -80% vs keeping all) is the better trade; immediate eviction only when memory is the binding constraint. Most evictions come from re-keys (edits, churn), which no grace window can save")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_12 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 12u32, "2026-10-19T00:48Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:48 correction to note 11, measured on the real path: the fake executor has no persistent memo, real Cells does. Unpaced real runs never evict (deliveries arrive in ms, compiles take seconds, so lapses and re-keys cancel in-flight work instead), so the runner gained --pace-ms N (waits up to N ms for finished tasks between deliveries). math.spi 6 cells, fickle=6+editor=1, 3 users, paced 3 s, fresh SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO_DIR per run: keep 25 real compiles (memo misses), peak 21 results; evict 26 misses, peak 6, 17 evictions, 2 recomputes all served by the memo (hits 37); grace10 26 misses, peak 16, 0 rescues. Decision revised: with Cells' disk memo behind the result store, evict immediately (same real compiles, smallest store); a grace window only pays without a persistent memo. CI corpus run now uses --evict; the grace path stays covered by its unit test")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_13 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 13u32, "2026-10-19T00:59Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:59 first three-notebook real-Cells run: math + date_time + sm' (8 cells each, interleaved author threads via --spi-file), --memo --evict, personas fickle=2+editor=1+breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3, fresh memo dir, own daemon port: 24 posts, 121 events, 137-139 deliveries, 21 prefixes, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail on both schedules, commute ok; schedule 1 122 s cold, schedule 2 32 s (memo hits 33 then 99, misses 34-35); daemon tree peak 3,445 MB over 8 processes (--daemon-memory), the highest measured so far (previous 3,398 MB with two notebooks); not scaled further on a shared 16 GB machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_14 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: located: xsim lives in the x repo (x/apps/sim, x lane's tree), so main records only; the 10-18 notes show the next steps already moved: --pace-ms for real Cells, the three-notebook real run (math + date_time + sm', all invariants, daemon peak 3,445 MB via --daemon-memory), so the sampler grain is done Next: ask nothing; next lap read x lane's SIM-IN-SPIRAL progress and decide whether this epic's remaining 18% is scale runs on a quiet machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_15 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: x lane state (read only): X-CELLS-SPLIT at 35 percent, its prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter and events modules pass Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) after today's byte-unit lib flip; those are the modules xsim's frames reuse (SIM-IN-SPIRAL note 3), so the sim's Spiral port has its shared base; this epic's remaining 18 percent stays scale runs of the real-Cells three-notebook scenario on a quiet machine Next: when a quiet window opens (evening), rerun the three-notebook real-Cells scenario at concurrency 3 with --daemon-memory to confirm the 3,445 MB peak holds after today's lib changes")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_16 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:22 lap 4 grain: core v1 holds for the sim: the x lane rebuilt x on the fixed core at 13:0x (view 1005 -> 987 lines, 16 crates under 1000, no _Lt order enum) and its e2e passes, 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases with all 4 sim threads, post-thread 34 posts read back, delete-thread 34; helper lane 2 proposes splitting SIM-IN-SPIRAL into a bulk story owned by the x lane with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep Next: decide the SIM-IN-SPIRAL split with the x lane (main proposes yes: the Spiral port does not need Kino without Elixir)")
inl language_name () = Task ("LANGUAGE-NAME", "Pick the edition-3 language name once SPIRAL3-EDITIONS parses real programs (votes 2026-10-12: Espira 835, Spile 790, Geandra 610 - Geandra as a release codename); prefer a name that keeps .spi/package.spiproj; run a real clearance search before announcing; user decision 2026-10-12 18:53: decide later; keep 'Spiral 3' / 'edition 3' until it parses real programs and a clearance search is done", TaskKind, P3, D2, 0u32, Planned, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "naming,edition", "current", "current")
