inl note_of_spiral_bignum_modexp_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-BIGNUM-MODEXP", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: still paused per the user (10-13); verified there is still no bignum, limb or modpow code in lib/spiral (0 hits). Recorded the backend constraint the design must meet, from apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: 32 x u32 limbs need a u32 x u32 -> u64 product, which C, Rust, Zig and Delphi do natively and Lua 5.1 does through the spiral_w64 helper ({hi, lo} pairs above 2^53, exact since 10-15), but the WGSL backend has no i64/u64 at all, so a WGSL arm must split each limb into 16-bit halves (four u32 partial products per limb pair) or use 16-bit limbs (64 x u16 in u32 lanes). Choosing 16-bit limbs everywhere keeps one Spiral implementation for every backend at about 4x the multiplies; u32 limbs with a WGSL-only mul-hi helper keeps C/Rust fast.")
inl tibia_tcp_bytes () = Task ("TIBIA-TCP-BYTES", "Raw TCP byte streams for the headless client: lib/spiral/networking.spi has an HTTP client (reqwest) and a tcp_client nominal, but no tested path to connect to 127.0.0.1:7172, write length-prefixed byte buffers and read exact byte counts; add that (Rust target first), with a test against a local echo server and then against Canary's game port, and byte-buffer helpers (u8/u16/u32 little-endian put/get, strings with u16 length) shared with apps/tibia/wire.spi; 2026-10-13 02:44 user: keep harnessing OTClient to the end (the baseline bots run as Lua inside it, and Spiral's Lua backend can run our policy there too); deferred until many parallel sessions are actually needed", TaskKind, P4, D4, 0u32, Paused, "WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB", "lib,networking,tibia", "current", "current")
