inl note_of_eoie_zed_sweep_1 () = Note ("EOIE-ZED-SWEEP", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:57Z", "lane-b", "shape found: threads.db (zstd JSON) messages[].Agent.content[] items {ToolUse: {id, name, raw_input, input: {type: json, value}}}; 8 threads hold 897 tool uses (terminal 445, read_file 199, grep 101, list_directory 51, find_path 30, write_file 25, edit_file 19); main's job tmp/sweep/intents.py now reads that shape (+ list_directory as search)")
inl note_of_eoie_zed_sweep_2 () = Note ("EOIE-ZED-SWEEP", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: Zed threads.db is unchanged since 10-12 17:42 (no new Zed agent threads to sweep). Lowered P3 -> P4 until Zed is used again.")
inl eoie_one_call_spec () = Task ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", "TDD spec for one-call eoie (user direction 2026-10-13 22:23): the hairiest commands invented from mined agent history are both the tutorial and the tests; eoie/spec/one-call.md holds cases (provenance, habit, fixture, command, exact expected output) ordered by recorded calls replaced; all start red; first slice: a runner that extracts the fenced blocks, builds the fixture in a temp root, runs eoie once and diffs the whole output, then implement eoie do <root> '<program>' (typed statement language, one receipt with clock) in the idiomatic Spiral core until cases 1-6 are green; extend cases from tmp/sweep/hairy.md clusters (2036 compound signatures, 11.5k calls); 2026-10-14 02:04 lane 3: spec/one-call.md extended from 6 to 33 cases (27 new, 25 distinct clusters + 2 failure companions), mined again from agent history (tmp/one-call/mine_clusters.py: 21,239 shell calls with 2+ intents): read+search 1,832, read+walk 841, read+clock 716, search+walk 420, read+replace 279 (dry-run), search+git 240, read+wait 226 (+timeout), read+process 222, read+build 209 / search+build 163, walk+aggregate 207, read+search+clock 178, read+walk+clock 170, read+json 156, read+write 140 (append with clock), read+aggregate 124, walk+git 119 (grouped status), edit+build 116, multi-file edit with expected counts 106 (+mismatch), table count by 105, hash 669, copy except 94, job timeout+notify 57, git log by day 86, search with git status 87, run then markers 89; every case has fixture (new setup block for git), one eoie do command and exact expected output with receipt counts, bytes, exit codes; placeholders <hash> <commit> <pid> <HH:mm> documented; all red until the runner and eoie do exist", Story, P1, D5, 25u32, Active, "EOIE-ONE-CALL", "eoie,one-call,tdd", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_1 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:22Z", "lane-a", "08:22 lane-a: runner first slice (prototype, pwsh; the real one belongs in Spiral): lane-a\\one-call\\run-spec.ps1 extracts 33 cases, 62 fixture files, 4 setups (not run yet), builds roots, runs eoie do, matches placeholders: 0 green, 33 red (unknown verb: do).")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_2 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: eoie help on 10-15: 6 verbs (status, patch, agile, bundle, proxy, help), 17 distinct agile subverbs, 70 proxy capabilities (unchanged since 10-14 08:23). Spec runner still a pwsh prototype (lane-a one-call/run-spec.ps1); 0 of the red cases turned green tonight.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_3 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 3u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "Runner lab/EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC/one-call-runner.ps1 on build 1 (19:15): 12/34 green, exactly the target set 1 2 3 7 8 9 10 11 13 14 25 26. Next slice in build 3: crate eoie_do_facts (682 generated lines; sha2, regex, serde_json; globs via a new eoie_do_read_matching export) for hash (28), env + exists (19), nearest (18), json get (20), lines count by (22), files count by extension (17); fact union + grammar tests; eoie_do decode routes any fact verb to FactProgram; facts and read queries cannot share one program yet. Still red after it: run/stop/errors (6 15 16 24 33), git (12 23 31 32), dirs (4), diff (5), append (21), table (27), copy (29), job (30), lab audit (34).")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_4 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 4u32, "2026-10-18T23:57Z", "claude", "Build 3 (19:50): runner 17/34 green, adding 17 18 19 20 28 (facts crate). 22 was red because the program tokenizer dropped the backslash of unknown escapes (\\w became w); build 4 keeps \\x unless x is a quote or backslash (regression test regex_escapes_survive_the_tokenizer). Build 4 also adds crate eoie_do_process (823 lines; sysinfo, regex; per-run job object so stops take the tree; clears NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath so cmd /c finds root scripts) for run/until/background/stop, tail output, search in output, errors in output by, processes where command ~; a scratch probe matched cases 6 15 16 33 byte for byte (15 depends on 5-digit pids: its header pads pid to the widest pid). table count by (27) joined the facts crate, probe matches. Left after build 4: 4 dirs, 5 diff, 12 23 31 32 git (need a setup grammar: git init/commit at/write/append line), 21 append, 24 edit+run in one program, 29 copy, 30 job in do, 34 lab audit.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_5 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 5u32, "2026-10-19T00:33Z", "claude", "Builds 4-6 (20:32): runner 24/34 green. Build 4: process crate live (6 15 16 33), table count by (27), tokenizer escape fix (22). Build 5b: pid column fixed at width 7 so the processes table no longer depends on pid length (spec case 15 header updated to it); runner rule: the spaces after a placeholder cell are flexible, since <HH:mm> and <pid> are padded at placeholder width in the spec; quoted run words keep their quotes. Build 6: edits then runs in one program (24): eoie_do validates and commits edits, then eoie_do_process_rejection (checked before any write) and eoie_do_process_after_edits (report, counts, failed) give one combined receipt; a failed edit runs nothing. eoie_do sits at 991 generated lines after part_at/printed became let; the next eoie_do feature needs a split. Still red: 4 dirs, 5 diff, 12 23 31 32 git (setup grammar), 21 append, 29 copy, 30 job, 34 lab audit.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_6 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 6u32, "2026-10-19T01:01Z", "claude", "Build 8b (21:01): runner 25/34 green; case 4 dirs/tag/count by landed in the facts crate (930 generated lines). A dirs line owns the tag and count lines below it; tag expressions combine <file> ~ \"re\" with and, or, not (or of ands). Build 7 had a panic: real program records carry empty fields, which the hand-encoded probe never had (lesson: probe with trailing empty fields). The test that nested a nullary constructor in a pattern (FactParsed DirsDetail) failed to parse; it now asserts through rejection_at. Still red (9): 5 diff, 12 23 31 32 git (setup grammar), 21 append, 29 copy, 30 job in do, 34 lab audit.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_7 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 7u32, "2026-10-19T01:05Z", "claude", "Case 5 diff (21:20) deferred to its own crate: written into eoie_do_facts it pushed the crate to 1,047 generated lines (gate 1,000). Profile: outcome_merged 160 and receipt_counts 92 lines, because each { state with ... } arm rebuilds a 30-field tally. A typed counter union over an Rc<RefCell<[u64; 30]>> barely helped (1,052): per-arm payload rebinding and one let per literal argument eat the savings, and inl->let on small helpers made it worse (1,065). Facts sources restored to the build 8b set (regenerates byte-identical). Plan: eoie_do_tables crate (diff by keys on columns; maybe absorb table count by) routed like facts. The primitive and grammar draft were tested to compile; the spec case 5 key column is one space wider than the spec's own widest-cell rule and needs the same whitespace correction as case 1.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_8 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 8u32, "2026-10-19T01:07Z", "claude", "Case 5 draft parked at lab/EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC/eoie_do_tables (crate eoie-do-tables, 330 generated lines, no extra deps; diff grammar test; scratch probe 21:07 printed the case 5 table and receipt rows a 3  b 4  changed 2  added 1  removed 0). To land: move it to src, add the workspace member and eoie_do dep, add diff to the program verbs and a TableProgram route in eoie_do decode/run (eoie_do is at 991 lines, so trim first, e.g. fold the per-crate *_run wrappers into one export call), refresh Cargo.lock offline, add the grammar notebook to the registry, and fix the spec case 5 key column (width 7, widest-cell rule).")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_9 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 9u32, "2026-10-19T01:18Z", "claude", "Build 9 (21:18): diff landed as crate eoie_do_tables (330 lines), promoted from the lab draft (lab folder removed). Facts delegates any program containing diff to it, so eoie_do stays at 991 lines (one routing word added). Spec case 5 key column corrected to the widest-cell rule. Runner 26/34 green; read 5/5, ps 5/5, inherit-pipe PASS, timeout-tree 2/2. Red (8): 12 23 31 32 git (need a setup grammar), 21 append, 29 copy, 30 job in do, 34 lab audit. The next eoie_do-routed family must trim eoie_do first.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_10 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 10u32, "2026-10-19T07:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:31 eoie lane: 34/34 on a probe build of the regenerated eoie_do chain (was 26). New families, each its own crate under 1000 lines: eoie_do_write 684 (append with {clock:FMT}, copy <glob> to <dir> except ...; reads after writes go through new eoie_do_read rejection/report exports), eoie_do_git 880 (init, commit all at <iso>, history, status group by state, log count by day) plus search ... with git status in eoie_do_read, eoie_do_jobs 727 (job start/wait on real supervised eoie jobs through new eoie_jobs exports; wait resolves the newest job by label), eoie_do_lab 538 (lab audit against a backlog via agile list --json). eoie_do became a router (991 -> 270 lines) over a program_family union, with the edit engine moved to eoie_do_edit (862). The runner now runs setup blocks (eoie do programs grouped by family, or plain CLI lines); setups 12, 23 and 31 use append instead of the missing write/append-line verbs; cases 29 and 34 had one extra pad space against the +2 column convention of every other table, fixed in the spec. Release build with these is job eoie-release-install-4.")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_11 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 11u32, "2026-10-19T09:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 05:40 eoie lane: 34/34 confirmed on the real debug build and the installed release eoie.exe (05:39). The spec cases now all run their setups through the runner. Next for the families: stale = last note older than 14 days (EOIE-LAB-AUDIT wording), gitignored files present in lab (not done yet), job notify beyond the printed line (not done yet).")
inl note_of_eoie_one_call_spec_12 () = Note ("EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC", 12u32, "2026-10-19T10:09Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:09 eoie lane: eoie.exe 06:08 has 34/34 on the installed binary. Fixed a clock-dependent bug the 06:00 release exposed: git log since <date> passed a bare date to git, which reads it as that date at the current time of day, so after 06:00 local a 10:00Z commit on the since day dropped out (case 31). A bare date now means midnight.")
inl hopacsota () = Task ("HOPACSOTA", "hopacsota: parallel core restarted from single-flight's evaluator, the Hopac way (fill-once cells, server-owned tables, local scheduler), determinism by construction, no record/replay; contract = spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md (decisions D1-D15, gates G1-G6, phases 0-7, scoreboard, decision-change log); gpt5parallel stays reference until Phase 6; user decision 2026-10-13 22:47, plan 2026-10-13 23:15", Epic, P0, D8, 25u32, Active, "", "compiler,hopacsota,parallel", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:39Z", "user", "2026-10-14 06:39 user decision: gpt5parallel no longer gates blesses (rungs dropped; build only to keep it compiling); pack 2 (Bend small ints + A4) waits for hopacsota")
inl note_of_hopacsota_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-14T11:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:38 touch: Phase 0 runs today (R3 instrumented build queued in the night cache; R4 after); gpt5parallel no longer gates blesses")
inl note_of_hopacsota_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: phase 0 nearly done (HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH 90%: R3 measured, R4 orders proven byte-identical with the keyed D6 merge). Phase 1 (HOPACSOTA-P1) is next once the phase-1 design note is written.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:56Z", "main-thread", "06:56 groom: status corrected from Planned 0% to Active 15%: phase 0 (HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH) is at 90% with R3 and R4 measured and D6 proven; phases 1-7 not started.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 5u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: user 21:05: hopacsota is carried until F# is gone; HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD (later) rereads the full Hopac docs against the plan.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the gpt5parallel-era bootstrap (Downloads\\files\\code\\bootstrap_llm_compiler_v60..v101.00.md, 2026-01-03..05), as lessons, not designs to copy: (1) join-point waits that blocked (ManualResetEventSlim.Wait) starved the workers that should signal them; v101 made every wait a helping wait (the waiter runs queued work) and stopped the soft-abort that switched parallelism off; Hopac jobs avoid this by construction, so a phase gate could assert that no blocking OS wait sits on the evaluator path. (2) Rerunning a failed build deterministically was used only to tell a race (passes serially) from a real error, as a diagnostic, never as a fallback. (3) Default DOP = ProcessorCount, eager worker start, and draining workers at the end of peval avoided leaking spinning threads between builds. (4) Depth guards gave false positives on finite long stdlib loops (listm); limits sized to the stack regime (BigStack floors) plus re-entry guards keyed by reference identity, not hash, separated cycles from long recursions.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:17 lap 1 grain: epic state from PLAN.md scoreboard: Phase 0 (R1-R4) and Phase 1 (Gate 1, seams, 0 cells on 6,758 rows) done, Phase 2 Active at 30%, Phases 3-7 Planned; research child closed this lap; progress 15 -> 25 Next: read HOPACSOTA-P2's last gate attempt and name its next measurable step")
inl note_of_hopacsota_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: epic step named: P2's next measurable step is the engine slice (BodyContext + body-thread bridge in a new PartEvalEngineHopacsota section, 350-500 lines est.) gated by hopacsota-g3.ps1 against today's accepted pack 5 run single-flight-20261019-080524 at 1 worker; research and LF children closed this morning Next: -")
inl note_of_hopacsota_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA", 9u32, "2026-10-19T18:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:41 lap 4 grain: epic state: research and LF scheduling Done; P2 (engine slice) is the critical path, P3 (scheduling policy) and P4/P6 wait on it; the gpt5parallel pairs that P6 retires are PartEval, SignalRSupervisor and GraphIdentity (the 4 adapters are hooks and vanish with them) Next: P2 engine slice as a candidate pack (pair-drift -Tracked plus a private Debug build make it safe to iterate)")
inl hopacsota_research () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", "hopacsota Phase 0 (no core code): R1 rest of Bend 2 (BendTT 6+, term_check/term_infer, emit_match/emit_task); R2 shared-state inventory with every access line; R3 per-specialization work/span from an instrumented single-flight copy on the mega roots + a scaling corpus (apps/spiral, all lib/spiral, generated N=10/100/1000 modules) -> the chains that make the span long and the D16 technique for each (parallel evaluation is required, R3 never decides whether); R4 Deferred DFS/BFS/random-seed worklist on single-flight, -Suite all residual hashes identical; gate numbers into spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md scoreboard; 2026-10-14 00:58 R1 done (BendTT pp. 7-10, 12 bend2 functions + checker bench: D12 +12 applied, 5 not applied) and R2 done (31 inventory rows, 0 without a line, 3/3 R2-decides resolved, 34/34 PartEval write lines covered, 5 proposed decision changes P1-P5 in PLAN.md); R3, R4 open; 2026-10-14 01:50 P1-P5 folded into the PLAN.md decision text (technical core facts 3-4, D3, D4, D5, D6, D7, D16; the Decision changes log kept as history); every PLAN.md line now <= 120 columns (inventory turned into a list); 2026-10-14 02:58 lane 3 R3/R4 patch scripts (not built; anchors checked once each against the current spiral_compiler.fs into scratch copies): C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\hopacsota-p0\\r3_instrument.ps1 (-Target copy; SPIRAL_R3_DUMP=<path> TSV per specialization: run, id, kind, annotated, awaited, parent, offset_us, self_us, inclusive_us, outcome, note; awaited cache hits too), r3_simulate.py (W, S, greedy list scheduling at 1/2/4/8 workers, critical path, top 10 awaited chains with a D16 technique each; checked on a synthetic dump), r4_deferred.ps1 (SPIRAL_R4_POLICY=dfs|bfs|random:<seed>: closures and annotated methods go through a worklist, dfs runs them at once in the caller's context = single-flight, bfs/random drain after the root with D3 relative context unless SPIRAL_R4_CONTEXT=canonical; SPIRAL_R4_REPORT=<path> counts inline-depth and dynamic-if restarts, validating callers, relative-trace bodies, stack/inline/if stops)", Story, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "HOPACSOTA", "compiler,hopacsota,research", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:50Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:50 R3 mega roots: outputs identical with instrumentation; W/S 1.00 on all 5; peval only 28-672 ms of 2.5-8.2 s compiles, so parse/typecheck/codegen dominate (D16.4); apps/spiral R3 running")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 2u32, "2026-10-14T11:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:52 R3 apps/spiral: W 58.0 s, S 20.7 s, W/S 2.80, simulated 2.76x at 8 workers; span = root body 8.6 s + method#639 7.3 s + method#283 4.7 s, so D1 caps near 2.8x; intra-body parallelism is the proposed next decision (after R4)")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 3u32, "2026-10-14T13:34Z", "main-thread", "09:34 R3 scaling corpus generated (job tmp/hopacsota-p0/r3_scaling_corpus.ps1: N independent modules x 6 join-point levels, main calls each). Plain single-flight Release compile: N=10 3.0 s, N=100 4.8 s (701 C functions), N=1000 stack overflow. Cause: the typechecker recurses term' -> match_clause once per sequential let and runs on a Hopac worker stack, overflowing between 1000 and 2000 lets in one body (2000 dies about 1540 frames deep). Fixed in source (inferOnLargeStack, shared WDiff section, next pack). The W/S scaling measurement for N=1000 waits for that build.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 4u32, "2026-10-14T16:06Z", "main-thread", "12:06 R4 suite gate (R4 DLL in the night cache, run from a copied apps/compiler/tmp tree so it cannot touch the shared samples): -Suite all compile-only under dfs, bfs, random:11, random:23 and random:37. All 4098 rows have identical compile status and residual hash across the 5 policies (0 differ). Each policy's run has the same 3582 ok and 516 error; 53 rows fail only in the copied tree (package paths), identically under every policy. Caveat: the suite runs did not set SPIRAL_R4_REPORT, so deferral counts come from targeted runs. On the 5 mega roots every policy deferred 24 methods (max queue 24; 0 restarts, 0 validating callers, 0 stack stops) with identical output, but 3 mega roots compile to a trivial program, so the strong check is apps/spiral (1656 specializations), now running under dfs/bfs/random:11 with the report on.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 5u32, "2026-10-14T16:09Z", "main-thread", "12:09 R4 decided on apps/spiral, with SPIRAL_R4_REPORT on. Every policy defers 636 methods and 579 closures (max queue 1215 under bfs/random; 0 restarts, 0 stack stops). The three outputs differ only in lines 3-60, the global type declaration header (55/58 lines, same multiset of lines); all 18,100 lines of bodies and names after it are byte-identical. So the only order dependence is the order of globals, which is D6 (depth-first merge of globals), and no other cause appears. The PLAN.md scoreboard has the R3/R4 rows and notes. Next: D6 merge in the R4 copy, then 0 differing lines on apps/spiral.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 6u32, "2026-10-14T16:35Z", "main-thread", "12:35 D6 merge in the R4 copy (each deferred body collects its globals in its own list, a marker records the call position, depth-first flatten after the drain keeps first occurrences): apps/spiral peval globals now match DFS, but the outputs still differ (dfs 18,180 lines vs bfs/random 18,181). The rest is codegen-side. (a) A second globals block that codegen emits (after the UTF-8 helpers) comes out in a different order, so codegen adds its own globals while walking residual bodies in an order that depends on evaluation order (likely join-point dictionary insertion order). (b) type Box<'T> appears in both blocks under bfs, so codegen's dedup set misses the merged entry. Next: find codegen's global' sources and its body walk order, and order codegen by canonical method key (a D7-style rule) before claiming 0 lines.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 7u32, "2026-10-14T16:35Z", "main-thread", "12:35 hypothesis for the remaining diff (unverified): a memoized join-point body's globals go to whichever globals list was current when it was first evaluated. BackendSwitch validation evaluates the other backends' arms into a throwaway statement list (and possibly a throwaway globals list), and the policies reach validation from different first callers (validating callers dfs 899, bfs 604). So which bodies first run inside validation, and lose their globals to the throwaway or emit them later through codegen, depends on order. If confirmed, the rule is to give each body its globals once, independent of the first caller's context (record per body and merge at its canonical position), and it would also be a latent single-flight dependence. Check: count globals added while RustGapTrace.validating > 0, per policy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 8u32, "2026-10-14T18:26Z", "main-thread", "14:26 refuted the 13:0x hypothesis: single-flight's BackendSwitch validation env (spiral_compiler.fs ~163482) replaces seq, cse, backend and the tag counter but keeps s.globals, so validation-time globals reach the real list under every policy. The remaining R4+D6 difference on apps/spiral is narrower: the header still places clap_Command and std_string_String later under bfs, and type Box appears twice. Candidates: a globals path that bypasses peval's global' (codegen's TyOp(Global) arm appends to the same env.globals list, so its position depends on codegen walk order), or a body whose marker lands in a list that is not the one its first DFS caller used. Next: tag each global with its source (peval global', codegen) in the R4 copy and diff per source.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 9u32, "2026-10-14T19:59Z", "main-thread", "15:59 pinned with the peval-globals dump (R4 + D6 copy, apps/spiral): dfs and bfs hold the SAME 60 globals in a different order (e.g. clap_Command at 3 under dfs and 10 under bfs), so the remaining difference starts in peval, not codegen. Mechanism: a memoized join-point body runs at its first EVALUATION-time caller. Under dfs that is the first caller in depth-first order; under bfs a non-deferred caller can reach the key first, and the body's globals land at that caller's position. Refined D6 rule for the plan: globals belong to the body (its join-point key), not to the caller that happened to evaluate it. Every call site, cache hits included, records a reference marker; the merge expands a key's globals at its first reference in depth-first order and skips later references. The R4 copy needs per-key global lists plus reference markers on memo hits; that is the next experiment.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 10u32, "2026-10-14T21:21Z", "main-thread", "17:21 key-level D6 tried in the R4 copy: per-key global lists, a marker at the first evaluation and at every memo hit (closure true and method Some ret_ty), expansion at the first depth-first reference. Result on apps/spiral: dfs peval globals unchanged (60 lines, single-flight order), but bfs/random still order them differently in the same places (clap_Command at 3 vs 10), and the dfs OUTPUT changed: type Box now appears twice (line 8 from peval, line 73 from codegen). So (1) the misplaced globals do not come from join-point memo order alone; the next suspect is globals emitted by inline (inl) code inside deferred bodies, which carry no key; (2) the flattened list somehow defeats codegen's dedup (codegen re-adds Box), possibly a marker or near-identical string left behind. Both are open; scripts in job tmp/hopacsota-p0 (r4_deferred.ps1 has the key-level edits; the dump is SPIRAL_R4_GLOBALS_DUMP). Stopping R4 for today.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 11u32, "2026-10-14T21:52Z", "main-thread", "17:52 mechanism for the duplicate 'type Box': peval's EOp Global arm (~164536) calls global' (adds to s.globals for the matching backend) AND always leaves TyOp(Global) in the residual; codegen's TyOp(Global) arm re-adds it unless its dedup HashSet (seeded from env.globals at codegen start) already holds the string. Under key-level D6 the flattened list shows Box yet codegen re-adds it, so codegen's seed and the flattened list diverge. Next session: dump codegen's seed set and the residual TyOp(Global) strings to find the mismatch.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 12u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #3 (P0): close D6. Dump codegen's dedup seed set against the residual TyOp(Global) strings, and add globals from inlined code to the per-key merge (scripts in job tmp/hopacsota-p0). Gate: apps/spiral byte-identical under dfs/bfs/random. Then R3 W/S for N=1000 (now compiles) and close Phase 0.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 13u32, "2026-10-15T03:25Z", "main-thread", "23:25 D6: the extra 'type Box' in R4's dfs output (key-level merge) is a stale GlobalsIndex, not a codegen dedup bug. Flatten clears and refills the root globals list, but the per-list membership set (ConditionalWeakTable) keeps the old contents, so codegen-time peval (ty_to_data/nominal_apply on the root env) does not see the key-list globals and re-appends them after the codegen helpers. Installed DLL: one Box at line 8. Fix in the scratch r4_deferred.ps1: Flatten refreshes the index. R4b build queued to re-measure dfs/bfs/random:11 on apps/spiral. Rule for the real D6: any merge that rewrites a globals list must rebuild its GlobalsIndex.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_14 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 14u32, "2026-10-15T03:37Z", "main-thread", "23:37 D6 stale-index fix measured (R4b, apps/spiral F#): dfs is now line-for-line identical to the installed compiler (18,180 lines, 0 differ; the duplicate 'type Box' is gone). bfs differs in 44 lines and random:11 in 55 (were 55 and 58), all in the globals header (lines 4-54, same multiset). Example: clap_Command is 4th under dfs and 11th under bfs. Next: a pre-merge tree dump (SPIRAL_R4_TREE_DUMP, list/index/item per globals list; staged in r4_deferred.ps1 as core-r4c.fs) to see which list owns each moved global under each policy. Builds after the pack-1f bless.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_15 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 15u32, "2026-10-15T04:06Z", "main-thread", "00:06 D6 tree dump (R4c, SPIRAL_R4_TREE_DUMP; scripts tmp/hopacsota-p0/tree_paths.ps1): under dfs, 'type clap_Command = class end' sits in the body list of the first app method (key-1), between Ref and clap_Arg. Under bfs the same method's deferred body (key-2 > globals-5) does not contain it. Instead the caller's own list (key-0 body, globals-1) emits it right after the key-2 marker, i.e. after the deferred call returned. These stubs come from nominal bodies (lib backend.spi rust_type -> fsharp_global inside a TTerm), evaluated when the nominal type is resolved and memoized per type. So the emission goes to whichever list resolves the type first in time: under bfs that is the caller, which uses the returned annotated type before the body runs. D6 rule extension: type-level memoized evaluation (nominal resolution / TTerm) must be referenced like a join-point key: a per-type list on first resolution and a marker on every later resolution, expanded at its first depth-first reference. Next: find the nominal resolution memo in single-flight peval (term_real_nominal 162683 is called from ty's TTerm arm 162845) and add type-key markers in the R4 copy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_16 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 16u32, "2026-10-15T04:07Z", "main-thread", "00:07 correction to the previous note: single-flight's nominal_type_apply (162714) has no memo; it re-evaluates the nominal body (and so its TTerm/fsharp_global) on every resolution, and global' dedups per list. So 'memoized per type' is unverified. What is verified: under bfs the deferred body's list lacks the stub while the caller's list has it after the call. Remaining candidates: the body never resolves that nominal itself (under dfs it got it through a nested call whose annotation is resolved by the caller), or a join-point memo hit without a recorded marker. Next step: an R4 hook that logs (policy, current list, R4 frame stack) whenever global' adds a watched string, instead of more reading.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_17 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 17u32, "2026-10-15T04:36Z", "main-thread", "00:36 D6 found the memo: the type join point cache (join_point_type, single-flight peval ~162759). It evaluates a type join point's body once, so nominal stubs from rust_type/fsharp_global inside it go into the first evaluator's list in time, and a memo hit records nothing (the watch hook confirmed one global' call per run for clap_Command). R4e gives type join points a key list on first evaluation and a marker on every hit, and key markers are ignored once the session closes (codegen-time peval). apps/spiral F# vs installed: dfs 0 lines differ, bfs 13 (was 44; one moved line, std_borrow_Cow), random:11 20 (was 55; Ref, Vec, std_borrow_Cow). Watching std_borrow_Cow next. D6 rule: every memo whose miss can emit globals (method, closure, type join point, and any other found this way) needs key lists plus hit markers.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_18 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 18u32, "2026-10-15T05:41Z", "main-thread", "01:41 D6 lead (R4g, type keys labeled by argument types). The last bfs move is std_borrow_Cow. dfs: its stub comes from type join point [str] (Cow<str>) under run > normalize_path > replace_regex. bfs: the same [str] key under replace_regex has an EMPTY list, and the stub is emitted by [slice u8] under execute_with_options. Hypothesis: the join_point_type memo key has no backend in it. If the [str] body was first evaluated while validating another backend's BackendSwitch arm (bfs validating callers 604 vs dfs 899), fsharp_global's Fsharp arm is not current there, so the memoized evaluation emits no stub. If true, single-flight can drop a stub entirely when a type join point is only ever evaluated first under validation. R4h (labels add backend and validating depth) staged as core-r4h.fs; runs after the pack-1g bless.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_19 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 19u32, "2026-10-15T06:25Z", "main-thread", "02:25 R4h result (type keys labeled with backend and validating depth): the stub-bearing type join point is first evaluated during BackendSwitch validation of another backend's arm. dfs: type@Rust/v1[str] under replace_regex; bfs: type@Gleam/v1[slice u8] under execute_with_options. Validating a non-current arm runs fsharp_global's Fsharp arm, whose global goes into the real globals list. The (backend, closure) validation cache backend_switch_validated is a fourth memo with no hit markers, so in bfs the later [str] evaluation under replace_regex emits nothing. R4i gives backend_switch_validated key lists and hit markers; building. Side finding (lib, not D6): apps/spiral's F# output starts with two Rust lines (thread_local!/#[test]) from lib/spiral/rust/testing.spi run_tests', whose global calls are not behind a Rust backend switch. Same in the installed compiler's output.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_20 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 20u32, "2026-10-15T06:53Z", "main-thread", "02:53 D6 CLOSED for the R4 deferred evaluator. R4i keys the four memos that can emit globals: method, closure and type join points, plus backend_switch_validated, each with a list per key and hit markers. The merge rebuilds the GlobalsIndex, and markers are ignored after the session closes. Results: apps/spiral F# is byte-identical under dfs, bfs and random:11 (EBE4D894C6FB, 18,180 lines) and equal to the installed compiler. Full suite (4,143 rows, copied tree): dfs = bfs = random:11 on every row (compile + residual). dfs vs the installed pack-1g bless differs only in the 53 known copied-tree package-path rows (ok->error); every other row matches. Scoreboard in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md (2026-10-15 entry). Scripts: tmp/hopacsota-p0/r4_deferred.ps1 (core-r4i.fs), tree_paths.ps1. Next for hopacsota: carry the D6 keyed merge (four memo kinds) into the real parallel design (D16), and file the lib run_tests' Rust-globals leak separately.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_21 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 21u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #2: D6 is proven in the R4 copy. Write the hopacsota phase-1 design that carries the keyed merge (four memo kinds, index rebuild, inert markers after close) into D16's parallel evaluator, and check that the R4 counters for D3's relative context (1,203 relative-trace bodies, 0 restarts) still hold.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_22 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 22u32, "2026-10-15T11:22Z", "main-thread", "07:22 phase 0 gate recorded in PLAN.md (Phase 1 starting point). R1/R2 remain the open parts of this item.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_research_23 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH", 23u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: R3 instrumentation patch no longer applies (its PartEval anchor moved behind PartEvalSeams in pack-1v); re-home R3 into PartEvalSeamsHopacsota, which already sees every deferred body.")
