inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:09Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:09 helper-lane lap 1: inventory of lib/spiral against the D15 engine table (spawn, fill-once cell, request/reply, biased choice): threading.spi has spawn (Rust std::thread::spawn through a comment-splice macro, lines 22-23) and std::sync::mpsc channels (lines 255-279) on Rust only; there is no Gleam arm for either, no fill-once cell (OnceLock appears only as trace.spi's private static slot), and no biased choice (no select/crossbeam). So a D15 module signature today has 2 of 4 operations, Rust-only, and the spawn path is the drift-era macro hack. Still gated on P6 and SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (0%, Planned).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:02 helper-lane lap 2: worked the D15 operations through the two target runtimes, recording the one that does not map directly. Rust: spawn = std::thread::scope / rayon::spawn; fill-once cell = std::sync::OnceLock (std, no crate); request/reply = std::sync::mpsc request carrying a one-shot reply sender; biased choice = crossbeam_channel::select_biased! (std has no select). Gleam (gleam_erlang 1.x): spawn = process.spawn; fill-once cell = a Subject that receives one value, read by process.receive; request/reply = process.call (subject, timeout, request builder). Biased choice is the gap: an Erlang/Gleam selector takes messages in mailbox arrival order, not in the order the alternatives were listed, so left-bias needs a two-step read (poll the left subject with timeout 0, then the right with 0, then block on a selector over both) - that is an extra rule the D15 signature must state (choice is left-biased only among values already available), or Hopac's semantics differ by backend.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:58Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:58 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the D15 interface as executable Spiral rather than prose: lab/HOPACSOTA-P7/engine.spi declares type cell t = {fill : t -> bool; read}, type server request reply = {call}, union choice_side = Left | Right, and type engine = {spawn; new_cell_i64; biased_ready}, where biased_ready takes the left and right readiness tests and returns Left whenever left is ready - the left-bias rule stated in the type's contract (choice only among values already available, the Gleam poll-left-then-select shape). A sequential_engine instance (spawn runs inline, fill refuses a second write like Hopac's IVar.fill) makes it runnable: probe main fills once (41), sees the second fill refused, and gets Left from two ready sides; C exit 42 via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy. A Rust (OnceLock, crossbeam select_biased) and a Gleam (Subject) instance are the next two implementations of the same record.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:29 helper-lane lap 4: checked the planned Rust-arm instance against codegenRust before writing it, and it cannot work as planned: Spiral closures compile to Rc<dyn Fn(...) -> ...> (the closure factory at spiral_compiler.fs ~164059 returns Rc<dyn Fn({domain}) -> {range}>, captures cloned into it), and Rc is neither Send nor Sync, so engine.spawn cannot hand a Spiral closure to std::thread::spawn (or to rayon/scoped threads) - rustc would reject the residual with E0277. A real parallel instance of the D15 engine record on Rust therefore needs a codegen mode where closures reachable from spawn are Arc<dyn Fn + Send + Sync> with Send captures (a closure-flavour switch, like FT_Closure vs FT_Pointer), or an engine whose jobs are function pointers plus plain data instead of closures. The cell half is fine single-threaded (std::cell::OnceCell, or std::sync::OnceLock once Send exists). Recorded as the prerequisite; no lab code this lap.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:49 helper-lane lap 5: checked the fn-pointer alternative from lap 4 and it is not portable either: to_fptr (core v1 core.spi 123, !!!!ToFunPtr) produces FT_Pointer functions, which only the C++ codegen (CodegenCpp, 174763-175229: FunPointerMethod, typedef ... (* Fun)) emits; C, F# and Lua reject any non-vanilla function (Non-standard functions are not supported, 174028 / 162978 / 168345) and Rust has no FT_Pointer arm. So a parallel D15 engine instance today exists only for C++ (fptr jobs) or needs a new closure flavour (Send + Sync, Arc-based) in codegenRust; the second is the useful one for hopacsota on Rust. Recorded as a design input for the Rust-target decision; the sequential engine record (C exit 42) stays the shared interface.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p7_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P7", 9u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: update to the lap-5 portability table: F# no longer rejects FT_Pointer functions once pack 11 lands. lab/FSX-FROM-SPI/pointer_closure_patch.py makes the F# closure join point and F# tyv accept (FT_Vanilla | FT_Pointer), proven by an exported pointer function running under dotnet fsi. C and Lua still reject them, so to_fptr remains non-portable for the engine interface; the cell/server signature should stay on vanilla closures.")
