inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_1 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the premise is outdated: Kino passes per-test crates to the Rust builder (///> rust -d <crate>); a test block parsing JSON through serde_json::Value passes (job tmp/night/civil/crates.spi). Left: package-level dependency declaration and the x_api decoder checks as test blocks.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_2 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x/apps/client/wire.spi has 4 decoder test blocks on ///> rust -d serde_json (posts with metrics and references, users found/unknown, next_token, first_error), 17/17 with the zig ones; left: package-level dependency declaration")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_3 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:44 helper-lane lap 1: read Cells for the remaining package-level part: per-test crates work (builders.ex parses -d/--deps for rust, gleam, python, cuda builders; x/apps/client/wire.spi uses ///> rust -d serde_json). A notebook-level declaration also exists already: notebook.ex default_builders (lines 608-627) takes the ///> lines above the test-static prelude as the builders for every test without its own ///> lines, so ///> rust -d serde_json above test name static = covers all untargeted tests of that file. What is still missing: (1) a test with its own ///> rust line does not inherit the prelude's -d crates (the prelude is a default, not a merge), and (2) nothing at package.spiproj level, so each notebook of a package repeats its crates. Smallest useful change: merge prelude deps into same-tool explicit builders.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_4 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:29 helper-lane lap 2: closed gap (1) in code (uncommitted): Cells notebook.ex with_builders now passes a targeted test's own builders through inherit_deps, which appends the -d crates of the default builder with the same tool (the static prelude's ///> lines or the declared default), skipping crates the test already names; untargeted tests are unchanged (they get the defaults as before). Memo keys stay correct because the plan is keyed on the whole source, which contains the prelude's ///> lines. New ExUnit case in notebook_targets_test.exs (own rust -d serde_json + prelude rust -d serde_json -d chrono gives serde_json, chrono; a zig test inherits nothing); both files parse; README documents the inheritance. HEAVY QUEUE: mix test notebook_targets_test.exs (~4 min). Gap (2), package-level deps in package.spiproj, remains.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_5 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:57 helper-lane lap 3: gap (1) verified: the same bounded mix test run (37 passed) includes notebook_targets_test.exs's new case a test's own target inherits the static prelude's crates for the same tool, so inherit_deps works (own rust -d serde_json plus prelude rust -d serde_json -d chrono gives serde_json, chrono; zig inherits nothing). Left: gap (2), package-level crates declared once in package.spiproj for every notebook of the package; progress 60 -> 80.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_6 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: checked the design gap (2) prerequisite in the compiler: package.spiproj is parsed by a fixed field table (spiral_compiler.fs config, 177418-177428: version, name, moduleDir, modules, packageDir, packages, default-int, default-float, edition) through record fields, so a new crates: section would be rejected as an unknown field by the compiler, the editor and every consumer build, not only by Cells. The design therefore needs two parts: (a) the compiler accepts and ignores a crates field (one fields-table row parsing indented lines, like packages, stored nowhere or in RawSchema for tools), and (b) Cells' mount step (mounts.ex reads package.spiproj already, package_entries at 324) collects those lines as -d crate tokens for rust targets of cells in that package, merged like the prelude defaults (inherit_deps). The token syntax stays the one apps/spiral already copies verbatim into Cargo.toml (name=version or name={version=...,features=[...]}, spiral.spi 304-310). Order: (a) first, in a compiler pack.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_7 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:47Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:47 helper-lane: part (a) staged as lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/crates_field_patch.py (2 anchors, --check OK): a crate_lines parser (one whitespace-free token per line at the first line's indentation or deeper, the same indentation rule as packages) and a fields-table row crates whose value the compiler parses and drops (fun _ s -> s), so package.spiproj may carry crates: without the compiler, editor or consumer builds caring. Probe package lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/probe (packages |core-, crates: near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} and borsh=1.5, modules main); run.ps1 compiles it with the installed compiler (expected: rejected) and with a Debug candidate (expected: exit 0). HEAVY QUEUE row added; part (b), Cells reading the section, follows once (a) is in a pack.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_8 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T20:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:57 helper-lane: part (a) is in the installed compiler (pack 8b, accepted 15:46): lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/probe (package.spiproj with a crates: section listing near-sdk with features and borsh) compiles with the installed compiler (exit 0, main.c written), where the field table used to reject unknown keys. Part (b), Cells' mount step reading crates: into -d tokens, is still open, so not Done.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_9 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 9u32, "2026-10-19T23:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:45 helper-lane lap 7: part (b) is staged, now that Cells is with helper lane 1. lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/cells_crates_patch.py (apps/cells, --apply gated, dry run OK) adds Mounts.declared_crates(dir), which reads the indented lines of a package.spiproj crates: section; Builders.with_crates(builders, crates), which appends them as deps of rust builders only, with the cell's own -d winning by crate name; and a call in Cells.run that collects the crates of every --package the cell mounts. Specs keep their quoting through dep_arg's raw path (the 18:55 NEAR finding), so a crates: line can be pasted from Cargo.toml syntax. ExUnit case staged as lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/crates_section_test.exs (section parse, override by name, gleam untouched). Applied and tested in the next Cells window together with test_opens and the STABLE-ENTRY-POINTS removal.")
inl note_of_kino_rust_crates_10 () = Note ("KINO-RUST-CRATES", 10u32, "2026-10-20T00:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 20:42 helper-lane: Done. Part (b) is in apps/cells (uncommitted, for the user's commit). From lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/cells_crates_patch.py: Mounts.declared_crates, Builders.with_crates, and the Cells.run hook that adds a mounted package's crates: lines to its rust builders, with the cell's own -d winning by name. A follow-up fix in Mounts.package_entries ends the packages: section at any unindented line, so crates: entries are never taken as package imports; the first end-to-end run had put itoa=1 into the generated packages: list and failed with PackageErrors. End to end: lab/KINO-RUST-CRATES/e2e has a package with crates: itoa=1, module digits calling itoa::Buffer through a macro, and a notebook with ///- --package . and a ///> rust test that has no -d. It passes 1 of 1 (run-2036.log), so the crate reached the cargo build from the package file alone. mix test with the change: 39 passed (notebook_report, notebook_targets, mounts_name and the new crates case, mix-*.log). Part (a), the compiler accepting the crates: field, shipped in pack 8b.")
