inl kino_without_elixir () = Task ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", "Remove the Elixir dependency from the BEAM setup (user 2026-10-15 21:05): port Kino's Elixir shell to Spiral compiling to Gleam so CI needs only OTP + Gleam + rebar3", Epic, P1, D7, 10u32, Active, "SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", "kino,gleam,ci", "current", "current")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_1 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:20Z", "main-thread", "22:20 main: spike (job tmp/gleamspike/kino_spike): Gleam 1.19.1 on OTP 29 with Elixir removed from PATH runs Kino's runner primitives through a 30-line Erlang FFI (open_port spawn_executable with exit_status, binary, stderr_to_stdout, deadline + port_close): git/zig/pwsh exit codes and output captured, a 5 s process killed at 1.5 s, file read; gleam export erlang-shipment starts cold with OTP alone. So Gleam does not need to simulate Elixir: Mix tasks become gleam run -m, ExUnit becomes Spiral test blocks, Jason becomes gleam_json. Size of the port: 9,490 lines of Elixir in apps/kino/lib (notebook 1,222, kino 1,133, compiler_daemon 1,055, targets 787, compiler_client 639, memo 526, builders 469 ...) plus 5,311 lines of exs tests; the domain is already 1,906 lines of Spiral compiled to Gleam (spiral_kino@domain). The only Elixir-only dependency is Livebook's kino 0.19 (smart_cell.ex, display.ex): drop or keep optional. Order: targets/builders/runner first (process layer, the spike's FFI), then memo/mounts, notebook, compiler_daemon/client; CI drops elixir-version from setup-beam when mix is gone.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_2 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: toolchain.ex sized (14 of 18 resolvers are option, SPIRAL_<TOOL> env, first PATH candidate) and modeled as typed Spiral with an injected probe (union fallback OnPath | FileAt, found ByOption | ByEnvironment | ByFallback), 3 tests pass (job tmp/night/civil/toolchain.spi); then the real probe in plain Spiral (lib env.get_environment_variable, PATH split, file_system.file_exists) resolves the machine python.exe and rustc.exe on rust and fsharp, 4/4. PATH separator and .exe follow platform.is_windows (4/4 still pass). Next: the full table and file_exists arms beyond Zig/F#/Rust.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_3 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 design 1/5, BEAM shell (Spiral to Gleam on OTP). application: one_for_one root + Task.Supervisor, registers the Livebook smart cell: static_supervisor root. runner: watcher process per run, Port.open spawn_executable with exit_status/stderr_to_stdout, caller monitor kills the tree, deadline + drain, 16 MB tail, Windows cmd capture-file workaround, taskkill/tasklist/pgrep shell-outs: factory_supervisor Temporary child + selector. slots: GenServer semaphore with monitors: actor. compiler_pool: GenServer pool, fairness, free_mb admission: actor + factory_supervisor. compiler_daemon: GenServer, gen_tcp acceptor with the line protocol (hello, compile, notebook, retire) on 13905, Port to dotnet --server-tcp, shadow DLLs, persistent_term stamps, idle retire, working-set recycle: supervised actors, TCP via gen_tcp externals. compiler_client: gen_tcp client, ETS busy counters, FileLock-guarded spawn of elixir -S mix: spawn the gleam erlang-shipment instead. notebook: Task.async_stream bounded by cell_concurrency, ipynb/html/report: processes + slots. targets: per-backend build/run, :global.trans gleam lock, 4 warm gleam packages: named lock actor. kino.ex: run pipeline, persistent_term + :global.trans rust init: init actor. file_lock, mounts, source_blocks, lisp, accept, notebook_cli, 5 mix tasks: System.cmd/File: lib Gleam arms, CLI via shipment entry.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_4 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 design 2/5, pure parts and Rust. Already Spiral (spi/*.spi, 1,906 lines, Gleam spiral_kino@domain): directives, cell, text, document, notebook plan, targets table; the Elixir boundary is a stringly wire codec (domain.ex encode_cells/decode_plan) that goes away once callers are Spiral. Move into the domain: builders (builder lines, dispatch, argv), memo key composition + reached_program/package index, source_map, x_compat, display parse_debug + plot svg, accept rewrite, toolchain (night job typed it). Drop: smart_cell, result to_livebook, display_kino (Livebook kino 0.19 is the only Elixir-only dep), fsi_session/fsi_chain (warm dotnet fsi, against the no-interactive direction). Rust, only after KINO-HOT-PATHS profiling: Windows process-tree control (Job Objects instead of taskkill/tasklist shell-outs and the cmd capture workaround), hashing and walking (compiler DLL sha256 per context, package prints, mounts.newest) with rayon par_map from async.spi, the reach tokenizer if it shows. Integrate as one long-lived Spiral-to-Rust helper over a port with a framed protocol, not a NIF (a NIF crash takes the VM). Cell fan-out stays on BEAM: it waits on external tools. Memo keys use :erlang.term_to_binary today: the port needs a canonical Spiral encoding (one cold run, sound).")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_5 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 design 3/5, Gleam side. gleam_otp 1.3: actor (new, on_message, start, continue, call/send over process.Subject), static_supervisor (OneForOne/OneForAll/RestForOne, restart_tolerance, nesting), factory_supervisor (dynamic children from a template, Transient default, named, shutdown timeout). gleam_erlang 1.3 (OTP 27+): monitors incl PortDown, trap_exits, links, named subjects, selectors with timeout and select_other, send_after, kill. No port opening (gleam/erlang/port is a type only, gleam/otp/port deprecated) and no TCP: open_port, port_close, port_info, gen_tcp go through externals (the 10-16 spike used a 30-line .erl FFI). gleam/erlang/os is gone in 1.x and lib platform.is_windows imports it: needs an os:type external before the bump. Pins: apps/kino gleam.toml has gleam_stdlib only (1.0.5 resolved); cell targets pin stdlib 0.57 and gleam_erlang below 1.0: two manifests, bump apart. Spiral Gleam backend: closures, unions with exhaustive match, global import and @external work; gaps: base.try_unit/try_finally emit only comments on Gleam (give them a spawn+monitor arm), arrays and refs live in the process dictionary (never share across actors, keep actor state immutable), Subject/Selector need $ type literals with params, and a Spiral package cannot carry .erl, so prefer BIF-only externals + gleam/dynamic/decode for port messages.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_6 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 design 4/5, lib/spiral reuse and growth. Gleam arms today: env.get_environment_variable (envoy), file_system.file_exists (filelib external, with a ///> gleam test), platform.is_windows, guid, date_time, console, base/backend plumbing. Missing on Gleam, to add as granular lib modules with ///> gleam tests (not kino-private code): runtime process (spawn_executable port, exit status, env, cd, deadline, kill tree: today runtime.spi is Rust std::process + clap only), networking tcp listen/accept/connect/recv line (Rust reqwest/tcp only), crypto sha256 (crypto:hash external; crypto.spi has Rust/F#/Python), file_system read/write/rename/mkdir/delete/list/stat/exclusive create (file: externals; only file_exists exists), threading/async arms that mean actors and monitors on BEAM (threading.spi channel/mutex are Rust/F#). Rust side is already rich: runtime process_command, threading arc/mutex/channel, async par_map/into_par_iter (rayon), crypto sha256, file_system walk_dir: the Rust helper reuses these as is. New lib module worth adding: a typed supervision vocabulary (actor, subject, selector, monitor, supervisor child spec) with a Gleam arm now and room for a Rust/Zig arm later, so the core is not Gleam-shaped.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_7 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 design 5/5, migration order. Pattern: strangler on the existing domain seam. Each slice writes the Spiral module, exports it through the Gleam build, turns the Elixir module into a delegate (as domain.ex is now), keeps its ExUnit file unchanged as the oracle, then moves those cases into Spiral test blocks (///> gleam + test) and deletes the ExUnit file in the same change. First slice: Runner end to end: lib process/port Gleam arms + runner.spi (watcher, caller monitor, deadline, drain, tail, tree kill), proven by runner_test.exs 8 tests (timeout kills the grandchild, killing the caller kills the tree, Windows short-lived output), then slots (3 tests). Then builders + file_lock + toolchain, targets (warm gleam packages), memo + mounts (memo_test 23), notebook + report, compiler pool/daemon/client keeping the 13905 line protocol byte-identical so run_notebook.ps1 and lib/spiral/build.ps1 keep working, then kino.ex pipeline and the CLI entry (gleam export erlang-shipment, started by erl -pa). Stays until the end: mix.exs + spi/domain_compiler.ex as build glue, the mix spiral.notebook fallback in run_notebook.ps1, the elixir-spawned daemon, the Livebook smart cell (then dropped). Last change: delete mix.exs, deps/kino, deps/table, the mix tasks, elixir-version in gh-pages.yml. Rust helper only where profiling shows it.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_8 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: first slice prerequisite measured: lib runtime.spi has 2 Gleam mentions and no process/port Gleam arm yet (runtime is Rust std::process + clap); the design (notes 6-7) orders runner.spi first, proven by runner_test.exs 8 tests; Gleam string units are now bytes lib-wide (LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM Done today), which the runner's line handling relies on Next: write the lib process Gleam arm (spawn_executable port + exit status) with a ///> gleam test, in a lab copy of runtime.spi")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_9 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:03 lap 2 grain: precise unblock for the first strangler slice (lib process Gleam arm): lib runtime.spi's process API is Rust-shaped (process_command builders over am'.vec u8, sm'.std_string, threading.mutex_guard stdin), so the slice first needs a backend-neutral function (run file args -> exit code + stdout/stderr bytes, deadline, kill); on BEAM, erlang:open_port/2 is reachable through @external but collecting port messages needs receive, which no plain function offers; Cells' Gleam project (apps/cells gleam.toml) depends only on gleam_stdlib, so either add gleam_erlang (process selectors) to the cell Gleam build or let a lib Gleam arm ship a small .erl FFI module beside the generated program Next: decide gleam_erlang vs a bundled .erl: check how Cells assembles a Gleam cell project (does it copy extra src files?) in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells (gleam runner)")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_10 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:04 answered: every cell Gleam project already depends on gleam_erlang (apps/cells targets.ex:341-348 @gleam_deps: gleam_stdlib 0.57.0, gleam_time, gleam_erlang >=0.34, envoy, gary, gtempo), so the process arm can open the port with @external(erlang, erlang, open_port) [spawn_executable, exit_status, binary, args] and collect {Port, {data, Bin}} and {Port, {exit_status, N}} through a gleam_erlang selector (select_other + gleam/dynamic/decode); no bundled .erl needed. Next: the backend-neutral run function in a lab copy of runtime.spi with Rust (std::process::Command) and Gleam (port + selector) arms and a ///> rust ///> gleam test running a tiny command.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_11 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:07 lap 2 grain: first code of the strangler slice, as a lab draft: spiral/lab/KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR/process_capture.spi, capture_stdout program args : string with a Rust arm (std::process::Command, exit 3 on a nonzero status) and a Gleam arm (os:find_executable + erlang:open_port spawn_executable [binary, exit_status, args] + a gleam_erlang 0.34 selector collecting data and exit_status messages via erlang:element and gleam_stdlib identity), test git_version_prints_its_name on rust and gleam; args are one space-separated string for now (lists across the Gleam boundary come next) Next: HEAVY QUEUE: gate the draft; on green, add deadline + kill and a list-of-args signature, then move it into lib runtime.spi")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_12 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:16 first gate of the process_capture draft: compiles on both targets (0 type errors) but git_version_prints_its_name fails on rust (so gleam was not run); queued a rerun with the notebook output to read the failure.")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_13 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 13u32, "2026-10-19T14:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:49 lap 3 grain: the process_capture draft's 0/1 was the uppercase-mount PackageErrors bug (fixed); compiling process_capture.spi directly with the installed compiler for Rust succeeds (31,287 bytes, exit 0), so the Rust and Gleam arms at least type-check Next: re-gate after the core switch (queue 3) to see the runtime result of git --version on rust and gleam")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_14 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 11:03 lap 3 grain: first green slice: lab/KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR/process_capture.spi capture_stdout passes its Cells gate on rust AND gleam (1/1, 11:03): the Gleam arm runs erlang:open_port spawn_executable [binary, exit_status, args] and collects data + exit_status through a gleam_erlang 0.34 selector. Three fixes on the way: one global per Gleam import (a multi-line import block duplicated lib's single-line imports), parenthesize a comparison before |> (|> binds tighter than >), and test with a real executable: a scoop shim (scoop/shims/git.exe) exits 0 but forwards nothing through an Erlang port pipe (probe: no data message, while scoop/apps/git/current/cmd/git.exe sends data then exit_status), so the test runs hostname Next: add a deadline + kill (port_close + os:cmd taskkill/kill by os_pid) and a list-of-args signature, then move capture into lib runtime.spi with a ///> gleam test")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_15 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 15u32, "2026-10-19T18:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:32 lap 4 grain: deadline + kill landed in the lab primitive: capture_status_within program args ms returns the exit code, or -1 after killing a child that outlives the deadline (Rust: spawn + try_wait poll + kill; Gleam: open_port + process.select with the remaining ms, then os_pid from port_info, port_close while the port is open, taskkill /F /T or kill -9); the Gleam externs moved into one shared process_gleam_prelude so either function alone emits them; Cells gate 3/3 on rust AND gleam (hostname capture, quick exit 0, ping -n 30 past a 400 ms deadline returns -1), no PING.EXE left after the run; one fix on the way: port_close after taskkill raised badarg (the port had already closed), so close first, then kill Next: list-of-args signature (args as a Spiral list instead of a space-split string, so arguments with spaces survive), then stderr capture")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_16 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 16u32, "2026-10-19T19:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:17 lap 5 grain: list-of-args signature landed: capture_stdout_list program (args : list string) joins the list with a U+001F separator made by a target macro (lib console.spi style, no Spiral string escape needed) and splits it in the target (Rust split + filter, Gleam string.split into spawn_executable args), so an argument with spaces stays one argument (empty-string arguments are dropped, the one limit); the Gleam prelude now opens ports through one spiral_process_open_argv and collects through one spiral_process_captured; gate 4/4 on rust and gleam: python -c counts its argv as 2 with argv[1] = a b Next: stderr capture (stderr_to_stdout on the port, Stdio::piped for Rust) as a separate function returning both streams, then promote the module from lab to lib/spiral as process.spi (needs the lib gate and a usage owner)")
inl note_of_kino_without_elixir_17 () = Note ("KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", 17u32, "2026-10-19T21:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:46 lap 5 grain: stderr capture: capture_combined_list returns stdout and stderr together (Gleam opens the port with stderr_to_stdout, so the streams interleave as written; Rust appends stderr after stdout, which only differs when a program writes both), and the Gleam prelude now has one spiral_process_open_with taking extra port options; gate 5/5 on rust and gleam (python writing only to stderr is captured) Next: promote the module to lib/spiral as process.spi with its 5 tests (lib gate + one consumer), then wire the first Kino-free consumer: the Cells tool lookup that shells out today")
inl lean_kernel_notebooks () = Task ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", "Write the Lean kernel bottom-up as notebooks (user 2026-10-15 21:05): finish the CIC kernel begun in samples/mega_lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel as a Tibia-style package of modules with test blocks", Epic, P1, D8, 5u32, Active, "", "lean,spiral", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_1 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:21Z", "main-thread", "22:21 main: read of the mega sample (gpt5 browser-tab era): one 9,296-line main.spi (499 inl, 104 union, 74 nominal, 6 type), no comments or headings, 179 lines over 120 chars (longest 5,205: a kebab-case certificate string returned by main), names that encode iteration history rather than meaning (221 corrected_cic_*, 86 cic_corrected_*, assert_cic_*/assert_corrected_*), and 40 negative_* sub-packages, each a package.spiproj + main.spi that must fail to type-check. The valuable core is intrinsic typing: contexts and sorts as phantom nominals (corrected_cic_term (ctx_extend empty_ctx identity_sort) bool_sort), so ill-typed terms are compile errors. Conversion is feasible: (1) keep the mega sample frozen as the compiler regression oracle (its residual hashes and cross-core checks depend on it) and build the kernel as a new package; (2) one module per bottom-up layer (sorts/universes, contexts, terms, substitution, conversion/defeq, inductives/iota, metas), ///- --package . at the top, ///> c|zig|rust test blocks that assert behaviour instead of a certificate string; (3) each negative becomes a test block using Kino's existing ///- --fails ETY-code directive (code plus quoted substring) next to the code it guards: 40 packages and 80 files become 40 blocks; (4) names without the corrected_ prefixes, the module namespace carries cic; (5) X-compat line limit from day one. First grain: sorts + contexts module with its negatives, measured against the sample's matching rows.")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_2 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: first draft (job tmp/night/cic/kernel.spi): sorts and contexts as nominal indices, an intrinsically typed term union with an existential Apply domain, structural size; 4 test blocks incl. 2 negatives pinned to their types; location in the tree is still to decide.")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_3 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: the location question is settled for now: the first CIC kernel draft (sorts and contexts as nominal indices, intrinsically typed term union with an existential Apply domain, 4 test blocks passing 4/4 on 10-17, plus a neg package with its expected unbound_variable and wrong_domain texts) moved from job tmp/night/cic into spiral/lab/LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS; its final home is a Tibia-style package (its own repo or lib) once it grows past the draft Next: HEAVY QUEUE: Cells gate of the lab copy (4 tests) to re-prove it, then the next kernel grain: beta conversion on the typed terms")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_4 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:16 lap 2 grain: lab gate of kernel.spi: 2 of 4 pass (identity_is_a_lambda_over_its_bound_variable and applying_identity_to_a_constant fail, 0 type errors); on 10-17 all 4 passed in job tmp with the same |core- package, so a core or lib change since then changed a runtime result Next: HEAVY QUEUE 2 rerun with -NotebookOut to see actual vs expected")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_5 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:49 lap 3 grain: raw rerun explained the 2 failures: identity_is_a_lambda_over_its_bound_variable and applying_identity_to_a_constant had no ///> target, which Cells has rejected since CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS (the 10-17 draft predates it); both now carry ///> c and ///> zig; the two fails-negatives already pass Next: re-gate after the core switch (queue 3), then beta conversion")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_6 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:33 lap 4 grain: first half of beta: is_beta_redex detects Apply whose existential operator is a Lambda (the match on the unpacked operator refines its GADT index, so the check is total without a cast); lab/LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS/kernel.spi gate 6/6 after the core switch (4 run tests on c and zig, including a constant-headed application that is not a redex, plus the 2 fails negatives) Next: beta step proper: substitute the argument for Here in the body, which needs a typed substitution over the context index (weakening for There); start with the closed case (body context = extend empty_context domain)")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_7 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T19:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:28 lap 5 grain: first real beta step: substitute_top replaces the top variable of a closed body (Here gives the argument, There unwraps, Sort/Constant rebuild, Apply recurses through the existential), and beta_top contracts a root redex; the GADT refinement carries the typing (matching Here equates sort with domain, so returning the argument typechecks) and a negative proves it is checked: returning the argument for There is rejected at compile time; gate 8/8 (5 run tests on c and zig, 3 fails negatives); Lambda inside the body still fails at run time, it needs weakening Next: weakening (shift a term into an extended context: term c s -> term (extend c h) s), which unlocks substitution under binders and full normalization")
inl note_of_lean_kernel_notebooks_8 () = Note ("LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T19:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:30 main: substitute_top and beta_top are now context-polymorphic (forall context), so beta reduces root redexes in any context, not just closed terms; gate still 8/8, 0 type errors. Weakening needs no function: There already takes any term (term c s -> term (extend c h) s), the explicit-lift representation. Left for full normalization: substitution under a Lambda, where the replaced variable sits below the binder (needs an exchange/lift-under step), then a normalize loop.")
inl test_fails_specific () = Task ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", "Negative test blocks matching only 'Unification failure' pass on any unrelated type error in the package (apps/spindle mutation: the module broke and the negative still passed); pin each to its own Got/Expected text, or have Kino require the diagnostic inside the test body. apps/tibia has 6 such tests", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,testing,negative-tests", "current", "current")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_1 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: Kino fixes in the tree (uncommitted): fails text matched with space runs collapsed (the compiler pads Got:), and the memo key includes the parsed fails expectation. ExUnit case added (notebook_report_test.exs); mutants fail it; full Kino suite 240 tests, 1 failure = the known private MIX_BUILD_PATH artifact. Ready to commit.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_2 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:10 helper-lane lap 1: verified: the Kino side is committed (59b477ba: notebook.ex collapse_spaces on both sides, expectation code plus substring in the memo key, notebook_report_test.exs). Census of fails clauses still pinned only to Unification failure, all repos: tibia/src 18 (vocations 2, vitality 1, readiness 3, quantity 2, policy 1, pack_sim 5, network 2, catalog 1; tibia lane owns them, not touched) and x/apps/client/wire.spi:534 an_app_token_cannot_be_signed (x lane). The 4 other x/tibia negatives already pin Expected or key text. Remaining gap in Cells: expected_compile_error accepts any matching diagnostic anywhere in the package; nothing checks that the error span lies inside the test body. Side find: notebook.ex defp unsupported has a glued line (do    text = ...), harmless Elixir but a bad-edit scar.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_3 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:03 helper-lane lap 2: design for the span-in-body check, from reading Cells: (1) a test cell's source is ///- options, its ///> target rows, an empty row, then the body rows (document.spi test_cell_source, line 496), and the cell's program ends with that body, so the body is the program's last k lines with k = rows after the cell source's first empty row; (2) SourceMap.remap rewrites main.spi:L:C to source:line:col but keeps the original in parentheses (source:line:col (main.spi:L:C)), and leaves it unchanged when the mapping is ambiguous, so program line numbers survive in every message. Rule: in notebook.ex expected_compile_error, scan the message's diagnostics (each starts at a main.spi:L:C location); pass only if a diagnostic whose text contains the expected substring has L greater than program_lines - k; otherwise fail with the expected text appears only outside the test body (line L). That keeps tibia's 18 bare Unification failure negatives honest without rewriting them, as long as their errors point into the body.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_4 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:05 helper-lane lap 3: DONE with evidence (implemented, uncommitted): Cells notebook.ex now requires a fails expectation's matching diagnostic to sit in the test's own body. body_head takes the cell's first body line (after its ///- and ///> lines), body_floor finds the last program line equal to it in the SpiralError's program text (details), and in_body? splits the message at each main.spi:L:C location (kept even after source remapping) and accepts only a segment with L past that floor that contains the expected text; with no locations or no program the old rule applies. A failure located before the body gives: the expected error ... is reported only before the test body (which starts at program line N). Tests: new ExUnit case (an error at main.spi:1 from a broken definition fails, the same error at line 4 in the body passes), and the 4 stub errors of the two older fails tests moved from main.spi:3:5 (the wrap header line) to 4:5 (the body); mix test notebook_report_test.exs + notebook_test.exs: 42 passed (bounded eoie job). Real compiler check (lab/TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC/inside.spi and outside.spi through run_notebook, process compiler): the body-located Unification failure passes, the definition-located one fails. README documents the rule. tibia's 18 bare negatives keep passing as long as their errors are in their bodies.")
inl self_hosted_spiral () = Task ("SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", "Spiral rewritten in Spiral (user 2026-10-15 22:45): compiler targets Wasm (xsim comes for free), one set of core data structures shared by compiler and formatter; until then F# (formatter, hopacsota) and Elixir (Kino) stay interim code to be regenerated, and upstream-bound work is split from our own (some as PR candidates, some ours)", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,self-hosting,spiral", "current", "current")
inl note_of_self_hosted_spiral_1 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: first concrete anchor: the compiler already exposes its tokenizer as a CLI (TokensCli: tokens <file> | --stdin, one row per token with line, from, nearTo, class), and the formatter, Zed and edition work all sit on that tokenizer, so a Spiral tokenizer checked token-for-token against TokensCli output on the lib corpus is the natural first self-hosted slice (an oracle exists) Next: size the F# tokenizer (tokenize_line and its tables) in lines and list its data structures")
inl note_of_self_hosted_spiral_2 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: sized the first slice: the /// ## Tokenize section runs from spiral_compiler.fs:42787 to the next section header at 45631 (2,845 lines, which also holds the parser's shared types: RawMacro, ParserErrors, BlockParsingEnv, ParseResult); the tokenizer's own data model is TokenKeyword, ParenthesisState, Parenthesis, MacroEnum, Literal, SemanticTokenLegend, SpiralToken, LexState (lex_state_init at 43109: triple_quotes, comment_depth, macro_close), TokenizerMacro, LineToken, LineComment, LineTokenErrors, LineTokens; module Tokenize starts at 43520; the oracle for a Spiral port is TokensCli (one row per token) Next: measure the module Tokenize body alone (43520 to its end) and map SpiralToken's cases to a Spiral union draft in lab/SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL")
inl note_of_self_hosted_spiral_3 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T18:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:40 lap 4 grain: corrected slice size: the /// ## Tokenize section proper is spiral_compiler.fs 42787-43523, 737 lines (the lap 2 figure of 2,845 ran to the next column-0 header, but section headers are indented, so it swallowed BlockSplitting and the parser types); it holds 12 types (TokenKeyword, ParenthesisState, Parenthesis, MacroEnum, Literal, SemanticTokenLegend, SpiralToken, LexState, TokenizerMacro, LineToken, LineComment, LineTokenErrors) and 152 let bindings, a realistic first self-hosting target Next: port the 12 types as Spiral unions/records in a lab package (no logic yet), with test blocks that build each case")
inl note_of_self_hosted_spiral_4 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T20:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:46 lap 5 grain: first self-hosting slice in Spiral: lab/SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL/tokens.spi ports the tokenizer data model of spiral_compiler.fs 42793-43106 (token_keyword 31 cases, parenthesis and its state, macro_kind, literal with 13 typed payloads, semantic_token_legend as a union plus legend_code giving the F# enum values 0-13, spiral_token 22 cases, lex_state with in_triple) and the first logic, word_token, the keyword table of the F# var parser (42991-43009, including true/false as literals and the variable fallback); Cells gate 2/2 on c and rust, 0 type errors Next: next tokenizer function: the operator/symbol classifier (the ?: and ?| keyword operators at 43181) and a char-class predicate set (is_var_char_starting, is_var_char), each with test blocks mirroring the F# behavior")
inl fsx_from_spi () = Task ("FSX-FROM-SPI", "Generate F# (.fsx) from .spi notebooks instead of hand-writing it, for the formatter and hopacsota layers, with GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection simulated by phantom data (user 2026-10-15 22:45)", Story, P2, D6, 15u32, Active, "SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL", "fsharp,spiral,self-hosting", "current", "current")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_1 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:11 helper-lane lap 1: measured the two targets in spiral_compiler.fs today: the Formatter section is lines 177553-180934 (about 3,380 F# lines, reads tokens and parse_block results only) and the hopacsota layer PartEvalSeamsHopacsota is lines 53397-53540 (about 143 lines, G1 142). Both are written against core F# types (tokens, RawExpr, PartEval dictionaries), so generated F# must name those types through macros or nominal stand-ins; the 143-line seams layer is the realistic pilot, the formatter the payoff. Still gated on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (0%).")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_2 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:04 helper-lane lap 2: found the concrete compiler gap: generated F# can only be a script today. Every samples/*/main.fsx is top-level lets ending in the program's value (closure_apply ends in if v2 then 0 else 1), with no module or namespace in any sample output, and the library entry op !!!!Export (Op union line 43683: a name and a function without runtime captures) is implemented only in CodegenRust (~163802), CodegenGleam (~167185) and CodegenTypescript (~172954); codegenFsharp has no Export arm. So generating the formatter or the hopacsota seams as F# that the compiler can link needs, first, F# Export (a module with named functions instead of a script body), the same contract the Rust/Gleam/TS arms already enforce (duplicate export check, closure without captures).")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_3 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:20 helper-lane lap 3: drafted F# Export as lab/FSX-FROM-SPI/patch.py (4 anchors, --check mode: anchors OK on today's spiral_compiler.fs, LF). It mirrors CodegenRust's arm: codegenFsharp gains exports/export_names/closure_of_var; TyJoinPoint(JPClosure) with no args records the var's closure tag; TyOp(Export, name, closure var) emits let name (v1 : t, ...) : range = f_cN () args (struct tuple for several params, the shape F# closures already take: closure_apply's let rec f_c0 () (v0 : int32)); exports land after the let rec group, before the script body. Probe lab/FSX-FROM-SPI/probe (add_one, add_pair) + use.fsx (#load main.fsx, open Main, expects FSX-EXPORT-OK). Heavy-queued to ride the next compiler pack; Fsharp residual-only changes expected: none (no sample uses Export on F#).")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_4 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 4u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_5 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 5u32, "2026-10-19T21:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:45 helper-lane lap 6: the pack 7 F# Export patch never worked end to end. On the installed compiler, lab/FSX-FROM-SPI/probe fails with Non-standard functions are not supported in the F# backend. The cause: Export's peval retypes the exported function to FT_Pointer (single-flight 160392), and both the F# closure join point (163091) and F# tyv (162738) accepted only FT_Vanilla. Fix: lab/FSX-FROM-SPI/pointer_closure_patch.py, two arms that accept (FT_Vanilla | FT_Pointer), --apply gated. On a private-cache Debug candidate (pointer-run.ps1, 0 errors) the probe emits let add_one (v0 : int32) : int32 = closure0 () v0 and add_pair over a struct tuple, and dotnet fsi use.fsx prints FSX-EXPORT-OK, exit 0. The probe was also refreshed for core v1 (+ instead of +., packages |core-). Offered to main for pack 11. The item's bigger scope, generating the formatter and hopacsota layers as .fsx, is still ahead.")
inl note_of_fsx_from_spi_6 () = Note ("FSX-FROM-SPI", 6u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); pointer_closure_patch.py is in: F# Export accepts FT_Pointer closures (helper lane 1 probe prints FSX-EXPORT-OK).")
