inl note_of_pipelines_green_7 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 7u32, "2026-10-18T13:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:32 main-thread: push run 39129835185 on 80b16019 (user commit 09:02) failed: build ubuntu, apps/spiral compile, lib/spiral/rust/near.spi:44:19 Expected: } (git-0e's 08:54 block-form conversion put ') : () -> ()' after an indented block closer inside a record, which the installed parser also rejects). Isolated (a lib copy with only near.spi reverted compiles) and reverted by git-0e at 09:31 to its e1eef3bc content; a lib-backed probe compiles again. Needs the user's next commit; the other 11 converted lib files compile. My bless now aborts first if a lib-backed sample does not compile.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_8 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 8u32, "2026-10-18T13:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:42 main-thread: spiral push 39127751148 on e1eef3bc is fully green (build ubuntu + windows, push to gh-pages): the first green spiral push of the day, carrying the macro lookahead fix, the cells warm-slot fix and today's backend work. i574n/.github rerun 39103223121 failed again, as expected: it cloned spiral main at 80b16019 (broken near.spi); goes green after the user commits git-0e's near.spi revert, then one more rerun.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_9 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: diagnosed the 06:48 push runs: spiral, dice, polyglot fail on Linux only, rustix 0.37.27/0.37.28 (near-workspaces 0.11 -> cargo-near -> near-cli-rs -> keyring -> zbus 3 -> async-io 1) uses rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range attributes that nightly-2026-10-18 rejects (the Windows nightly verification never compiles rustix); alphabet fails in dir-tree-html exit 3 (UTF-8 slice of its Korean README); x fails in apps/client/protocol.spi:80 and eoie in eoie-do-edit (their lanes); dice/polyglot/alphabet also lose html at mix spiral.html before mix deps.get Next: check the next push runs of spiral, dice, polyglot, alphabet after the user commits the fixes")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_10 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:53 lap 2 grain: push checklist (uncommitted fixes the user's next push must carry, git status at 09:5x): spiral 30 modified + 18 new (gh-pages.yml nightly pin, scripts/core.ps1 mix deps.get, workspace/.cargo/config.toml rustix cfg, scripts/publish-tree.ps1 failed-render guard from helper 2, test.ps1 per-backend native keys, sm'.spi fixes, Cells edits); dice 6 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, .cargo/ rustix cfg); polyglot 3 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, apps/chat/.cargo/); alphabet 3 + 2 (gh-pages.yml); dir-tree-html 3 (gh-pages.yml, dir_tree_html.spi UTF-8 runs + README test); blog 6 + 4; x 24 + 2 (ci.yml, protocol.spi fix); eoie 32 + 8 (gh-pages.yml nightly, missing generated crates per the eoie lane); i574n.github 3 Next: after the user pushes, read each repo's run; the first new failure per repo becomes this item's next grain")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_11 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 11u32, "2026-10-19T18:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:13 lap 4 grain: push checklist refreshed at 14:1x: uncommitted spiral 229 (22 new; includes the core v1 consumer migration, pack 7 compiler, 147 regenerated sample outputs from the accepted reviews), dice 12, alphabet 5, polyglot 6, dir-tree-html 3, i574n.github 4, plus the fork clone deps/The-Spiral-Language (core v1); the 10-17/10-18 gh-pages runs were green in dice, alphabet, polyglot and spiral, so the 06:48 reds are only the causes already fixed in these trees Next: after the user pushes: watch spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, then i574n.github (push-only workflow, last) with ci-runs.ps1")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_12 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 12u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 lap 5 grain: compiler install race fixed in build.ps1: other lanes compile continuously with the shared Release DLL (a Rust compile from another session started mid-build at 16:08), so the Release copy step failed twice with MSB3021/MSB3027 (FParsecCS.dll and others locked by .NET Host); Unlock-SpiralOutputs already ran before the 3-minute compile, which left the window open; now a build that fails only on locked-file copies (no error FS) unlocks again and reruns the incremental build up to 3 times, so the copy follows the unlock within seconds; the pack 9 install then succeeded and hashes equal the candidate Next: none; watch the next installs for the retry line")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_13 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 13u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 correction to note 12: the pack 9 install at 16:10 built in 67 s without contention, so the new unlock-and-recopy retry did not fire; it is parsed and in place but not yet exercised by a real lock race.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_14 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 14u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:45 lap 6 grain: second face of the shared-DLL lock race: the pack 12 install (20:33) built fine, but Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun could not swap 5 DLLs (FSharp.Core, FSharpx.Collections, Hopac.Core, Hopac.Platform, the host SpiralCompiler.dll: Move access denied while other lanes ran compilers), so they stay IL; same code, slower startup, and the host no longer hash-equals the R2R candidate (the core DLL does); the unlock-and-recopy retry covers only the msbuild copy Next: make the ReadyToRun swap use the same move-aside trick (rename the locked IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp>, then move the R2R one in), so a busy machine still gets precompiled outputs")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_15 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 15u32, "2026-10-20T00:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:46 main: R2R swap fixed in build.ps1 (Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun): when Move(r2r, dll, overwrite) is refused because a running compiler holds the dll, it renames the IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp> (Unlock-SpiralOutputs already deletes those later) and moves the R2R image in, moving the IL file back if that fails; proven with a simulated open-with-delete-share handle (overwrite refused, aside + move-in works); parse clean; first real exercise is the next Release build.")
inl ci_single_pipeline () = Task ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", "One workflow file per repo delegating to scripts/workflow.ps1 (user 2026-10-15 21:05): spiral compiler.yml folded into gh-pages.yml, eoie.yml replaced by gh-pages.yml + scripts/init.ps1 + scripts/workflow.ps1", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,pipelines,eoie", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_1 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: done in the trees, not committed: spiral gh-pages.yml gains schedule (17 6 * * *) and workflow_dispatch (suite input) with a compiler job (ubuntu, 360 min, zig 0.17.0 added, oracle artifact) running scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler -Suite; the build job skips schedule; compiler.yml removed. eoie: eoie.yml removed, gh-pages.yml (ubuntu + windows, rust 1.88.0 + rustfmt, setup-beam, zig) runs scripts/workflow.ps1, whose init.ps1 clones spiral as a sibling the way dice clones polyglot and runs spiral init-app-build.ps1 + mix deps.get; the eoie.yml steps became Invoke-Block calls. polyglot gh-pages.yml gained setup-zig. Next: the user's commit, then the first runs.")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_2 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night audit of the committed HEADs: one workflow file per repo, every build job runs pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1. The last deviation (x ci.yml xc-windows building xc and checking --help inline) moved into x/scripts/workflow.ps1 -Xc, tested locally; uncommitted in x. Every job of every repo now delegates to scripts/workflow.ps1; done once pushed and green.")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_3 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:08 main-thread: Done. Audit of the committed HEADs: spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, polyglot, i574n.github and tibia each have only gh-pages.yml, and x has only ci.yml; every run step is pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1 (x's Windows job calls workflow.ps1 -Xc, committed in a85e58a). Green status is tracked in PIPELINES-GREEN.")
inl spiral_to_yaml () = Task ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", "Idea (user 2026-10-15 21:50): a Spiral-to-YAML backend for workflow files so CI definitions are typed and cannot drift", TaskKind, P4, D5, 0u32, Planned, "CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", "ci,backend,idea", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night input: with every job delegating to workflow.ps1, the 7 workflows are 42-117 lines whose bulk is toolchain setup (4-17 uses steps each), per-repo copies of version pins that drift (CI zig 0.17 vs local 0.18-dev, OTP 27 vs 29, old Rust nightlies). The first useful output is one typed toolchain-pin source shared by all repos.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured the drift note 1 described: today the 9 workflows agree on every shared pin (zig 0.17.0, OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2, Python 3.12.10, dotnet 9.0.202, rust nightly-2026-10-18; blog, x, eoie and dir-tree-html use subsets), i.e. the 10-17 drift (OTP 27 vs 29, old nightlies) was fixed by hand-syncing 9 copies, which is exactly what a single typed pin source would make unnecessary. One pin worth a look before typing it: setup-dotnet 9.0.202 in CI while the local compiler builds target net11.0 (bin/.../net11.0), so either the CI compiler job installs its own SDK through the scripts or the pin is stale. Gate CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: answered the dotnet question from lap 1: the compiler does not use the workflows' setup-dotnet 9.0.202 at all. Resolve-SpiralDotnet (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1) only accepts an SDK that has .NET 11 (Test-DotnetHasSdk11), looking in SPIRAL_DOTNET, the cache's toolchains/dotnet, the shared cache, then PATH; when none qualifies, scripts/install-dotnet.ps1 installs channel 11.0 preview into the cache with dotnet-install (called from spiral scripts/workflow.ps1:19, lib/spiral/lib.ps1:19 and Cells run_notebook.ps1:90). So the 9.0.202 pin in 6 workflows serves only older F# paths (lib/fsharp builds, polyglot), and a typed pin source has two .NET entries with different owners: workflow 9.0.202 and the compiler's 11.0 preview from install-dotnet.ps1 - a second, script-level pin that YAML generation alone would not see.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:23 helper-lane lap 3: full pin census as the typed pin source's input (lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.tsv: repo, file, line, pin over the tracked .ps1/.yml/.toml/.psd1/.json/.nix/Dockerfile files of the 9 repos, deps and samples excluded; 199 hits). YAML side: 100 action refs (uses: @vN, already locked by actions.lock), plus setup pins repeated per workflow - otp 29, elixir 1.20, gleam 1.19.2 and zig 0.17.0 in 9 workflows each, rebar3 3.27.3 in 9 (written with two quote styles, 6 single and 3 double: drift a typed source would remove), python 3.12.10 in 7, dotnet 9.0.202 in 6, rust nightly-2026-10-18 in 10. Script side, invisible to YAML generation: nightly-2026-10-18 hard-coded in 22 places across 14 scripts (test.ps1, five spiral app build.ps1, init.ps1 x2, polyglot math, dice lib and contract, alphabet documents, dir-tree-html), nightly-2024-07-14 in 7 (the NEAR contract pin: spiral and polyglot init, polyglot chat contract, dice contract), x's rust-toolchain.toml channel, and install-dotnet.ps1's Channel 11.0 Quality preview. So one typed pin record needs about 10 entries (rust main, rust near, dotnet workflow, dotnet compiler, otp, elixir, gleam, rebar3, zig, python), emitted both into YAML and into a generated pins.ps1 the scripts dot-source.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:41 helper-lane lap 4: the typed pin source as code, lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): union tool with the 10 entries from the lap-3 census (RustMain, RustNear, DotnetWorkflow, DotnetCompiler, Otp, Elixir, Gleam, Rebar3, Zig, Python), one pin list, and two exhaustive renderers - render_ps1 writes a pins.ps1 the scripts can dot-source (one line per pin, for example RustToolchain = nightly-2026-10-18), render_yaml writes the with: lines of the setup steps with one quote style (the rebar3 drift disappears), skipping the two script-only pins (RustNear, DotnetCompiler) by an empty key. Adding a tool without both renderings is a missing-case error. Probe main compares both outputs with the exact expected text (expect exit 11).")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: pins.spi exits 11 as predicted - both renderers (pins.ps1 lines and setup-step with: lines) are byte-equal to the expected text from the typed pin list.")
inl toolchain_latest () = Task ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", "Move every pinned toolchain to its newest release, rc, beta or nightly (user 2026-10-15 21:20: we are far behind, the dib era pinned 2024 Rust): Rust channels, .NET, OTP/Elixir/Gleam, Python, Zig, Lean, Swift, Kotlin, Node-free bun, actions versions", Epic, P1, D5, 20u32, Planned, "", "toolchains,ci", "current", "current")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_1 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: pins in our workflows today: Rust nightly-2025-05-09 (gh-pages builds), nightly-2024-07-14 (NEAR contracts), nightly-2025-11-01 (app builds), 1.88.0 (eoie); setup-dotnet 9.0.202 while the compiler builds on .NET 11 rc; OTP 27, Elixir 1.18, Gleam 1.19.1, rebar3 3.24.0 (local OTP is 29); Python 3.12.10; zig 0.17.0 in CI vs 0.18-dev locally; Kotlin 2.4.21, Swift 6.4, Lean v4.35.0-rc4, playwright 1.44.0, trunk 0.21.14. Next grain: check release dates for each family, then bump one family per pack with a full suite.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_2 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: version matrix taken; .NET 11 rc.2 (11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105) installs in 63 s into a private cache and builds the compiler (145 s); a full review on that build (single-flight-20261017-034133) has 0 differing cells against bless 145428 on all 6,906 rows. install-dotnet.ps1 already takes the newest 11.0 preview, so only the shared local cache is behind (rc.1); move it as its own step after PACK-NIGHT-1017 is blessed, with a Tibia toolchain notice. Order: .NET rc.2, CI Rust nightlies to stable 1.99, CI OTP 27 to 29, scoop erl before choco on PATH.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_3 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (brzozowski alpha54, samples alpha705, spiral-bin alpha417; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 11): when moving toolchains, pin compiler identity as content fingerprints plus a mandatory capability probe, not one wrapper hash (identical spiral-compile wrappers sat on top of a good and a bad companion). Choose a new release by capability superset: an apparently latest lane was a functional subset of the stable one. Keep health per capability and history-aware (SDK ok / compiler attested / restore smoke failing as separate states). Use typed env profiles validated before launch (a malformed export produced SPIRAL_DOTNET=/dotnet). Preserve executable bits on rehydrate.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_4 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 4u32, "2026-10-18T09:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 05:05 main-thread local scoop status (manifests as of today): gleam 1.19.1 -> 1.19.2, elixir 1.19.4 -> 1.20.4, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0 (Kotlin native tier runs KotlinServer on JDK 25), pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.6, rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, uv 0.9.21 -> 0.13.0, go 1.25.4 -> 1.27.2. Web: OTP 29.0 shipped 2026-05-13 (CI still pins 27). Not installed mid-run: every tool version is part of the native-memo key (a bump replays nothing) and peers share the binaries, so bump one family per quiet window with a full review.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_5 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:26 lap 1 grain: Rust family done today (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE: nightly-2026-10-18 in every build script, the matrix, Cells and 8 CI workflows, every build verified, Linux rustix fixed by cfg); remaining families from the 10-18 scoop list: gleam 1.19.2, elixir 1.20.4, temurin 27, pwsh 7.6.6, rustup 1.29.1, uv 0.13, go 1.27.2, and OTP 29 in CI (still 27 in some workflows) Next: check which workflows still pin otp-version 27 vs 29 and gleam 1.19.1 vs 1.19.2; bump one family per quiet window with a full review")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_6 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: CI pins re-read across 10 repos: OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2 everywhere except tibia (OTP 27, Elixir 1.18, Gleam 1.19.1, tibia lane's tree, told); setup-dotnet pins 9.0.202 in spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, dir-tree-html, i574n.github while the compiler builds net11.0 (run_notebook installs .NET 11 itself), so the 9.0.202 pin is the next family to move Next: check what still needs the .NET 9 SDK in CI (fsi notebooks? dotnet tools) before bumping setup-dotnet to 11")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_7 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:03 lap 2 grain: found why CI still pins setup-dotnet 9.0.202: lib/spiral/lib.ps1 generates F# projects with TargetFramework net9.0 and runs the global dotnet publish (used by dice lib/build.ps1 and lib/fsharp/build.ps1), while the compiler, matrix and Cells already use their own .NET 11 SDK through Resolve-SpiralDotnet (env.ps1, install-dotnet.ps1); other direct dotnet calls are bench scripts (brzozowski bench.ps1, lib bench/parsing) and polyglot's temp cube; the only fsproj files left with net9.0 are vendored fable-library sources in polyglot Next: lib.ps1: generate net11.0 and publish with Resolve-SpiralDotnet, prove with dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 (HEAVY QUEUE), then drop setup-dotnet from the gh-pages workflows (alphabet mirrors dice)")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_8 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:03 lap 2 grain: .NET step applied in the tree: lib/spiral/lib.ps1 PublishFsharp now generates net11.0 and publishes with the .NET 11 SDK from Ensure-SpiralCompiler (Resolve-SpiralDotnet) instead of the global dotnet, so dice lib/build.ps1 and lib/fsharp/build.ps1 no longer need CI's setup-dotnet 9.0.202 Next: HEAVY QUEUE 2: dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 on net11; on green, remove setup-dotnet from the dice and alphabet workflows (and the other repos that only pin it for this)")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_9 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 9u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:51 proven: dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 with the patched lib.ps1 (net11.0, .NET 11 SDK from Resolve-SpiralDotnet) publishes and runs: FSHARP-OK dice/lib/fsharp / result 72096518 (main heavy queue 2, 10:50-10:51). Remaining: setup-dotnet 9.0.202 in the gh-pages workflows is now unused by our builds; left in place until the next push shows no other step needs a global .NET 9 (removing it is then a one-line workflow edit in each repo, alphabet mirroring dice).")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_10 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 10u32, "2026-10-19T18:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:36 lap 4 grain: scoop status 14:3x, matrix-relevant tools behind latest: zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 -> dev.349 (Zig is now the first oracle), wasmtime 49.0.2 -> 49.0.3, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0 (the Kotlin server runs on JDK 25), pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.7 (every script), rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, cmake 4.2.1 -> 4.4.4, uv 0.9.21 -> 0.13.1, git 2.52 -> 2.56, zed 1.23.2 -> 1.24.2; current: gleam 1.19.2, bun 1.4.3, erlang 29.1.2, elixir 1.20.4, .NET 11 rc.1; node 23.5.0 and python 3.12.10 are not scoop-managed and old (node 23 is an EOL odd line). Bumps change toolchain.tsv stamps, so they need one dedicated -AcceptToolchain pack, never mid-review Next: toolchain pack after pack 8b accepts: scoop update zig-dev wasmtime temurin-jdk pwsh rustup, rerun -Native (memo replays only the rows whose tool stamps changed), accept with -AcceptToolchain if DISAGREE 0")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_11 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 11u32, "2026-10-19T23:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:21 lap 6 grain: toolchain pack staged but moved to 10-20: scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1 updates wasmtime 49.0.2, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 and rustup 1.28.2 (scoop keeps the old versions, so scoop reset app@old rolls back) and reviews against the latest accept without accepting; zig-dev stays out of it (Zig is the first oracle, a dev-snapshot bump gets its own pack); tonight's matrix window went to packs 11 and 12; NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS needs the native Cells rustc (1.88.0-nightly) itself bumped to >= 1.93 for near-sys 0.2.14, a rustup self-update does not do that Next: run toolchain-job.ps1 first thing on 10-20, then a separate zig-dev pack, then the native rustc bump with the NEAR rerun")
inl ci_nix () = Task ("CI-NIX", "Nix on GitHub Actions (user 2026-10-15 21:20): a flake that pins the toolchains and generates the files the workflows need, built and checked on the Linux runners only (no WSL on the dev machine)", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN,TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", "ci,nix", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_nix_1 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:12 helper-lane lap 1: pin inventory a flake must reproduce (read from the 8 repos' single workflows today): spiral, polyglot, dice, i574n.github use setup-dotnet 9.0.202, setup-python 3.12.10, erlef/setup-beam OTP 29 + Elixir 1.20 + Gleam 1.19.2 + rebar3 3.27.3, mlugg/setup-zig 0.17.0, moonrepo/setup-rust nightly-2026-10-18, oven-sh/setup-bun; alphabet adds setup-go, setup-tectonic and cache-apt-pkgs; polyglot adds snok/install-poetry; blog, x and dir-tree-html use subsets; all publish with peaceiris/actions-gh-pages and pass artifacts with upload/download-artifact. So the flake's devShell is about 9 toolchains; nixpkgs carries every one except a dated rust nightly (needs oxalica rust-overlay or fenix) and a pinned bun/zig minor (overlay or fetchurl). Still gated on PIPELINES-GREEN and TOOLCHAIN-LATEST.")
inl note_of_ci_nix_2 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:04 helper-lane lap 2: drafted lab/CI-NIX/flake.nix (unrun; Linux-only by design): a devShell for x86_64-linux with the 10-19 pin set - oxalica rust-overlay nightly 2026-10-18 plus wasm32-unknown-unknown, mitchellh zig-overlay 0.17.0, beam.packages.erlang_29 (erlang, elixir_1_20, rebar3), gleam, dotnet-sdk_9, python312, bun and powershell (every workflow is pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1). Unverified points, to settle on the first CI run: whether nixpkgs already has an elixir_1_20 attribute under erlang_29, and that gleam/bun/python minor versions match the workflow pins (nixpkgs follows its own versions, so exact Gleam 1.19.2 and Python 3.12.10 may need overrides). The flake does not yet generate workflow files (the item's second half).")
inl note_of_ci_nix_3 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:21 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the CI job sketch lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml (non-blocking: continue-on-error, ubuntu-24.04 only, checkout then cachix/install-nix-action@v31.11.2 with flakes enabled, then nix develop ./lab/CI-NIX --command pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1). Version check: cachix v31.11.2 tagged 2026-10-14 is the newest Nix installer action (DeterminateSystems v23 is 2026-09-09). Fixed a flake gap against gh-pages.yml: setup-rust installs clippy, rust-src and rustfmt, so the rust-overlay toolchain now lists those extensions. Adding the job needs gh actions-lock in spiral (actions.lock lists the 10 current uses; a new uses fails at startup until regenerated). Still gated on PIPELINES-GREEN.")
inl note_of_ci_nix_4 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:25 helper-lane lap 6: version recheck of the sketch's actions (gh api releases, read-only). cachix/install-nix-action v31.11.2 (2026-10-14) is still the newest, and DeterminateSystems/nix-installer-action is still at v23. actions/checkout has v7.0.1 (2026-07-20) above the sketch's v6.1.0, so lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml now pins checkout@v7.0.1. Adopting the job into a workflow needs gh actions-lock regenerated in that repo (actions.lock rule).")
inl ci_k3s () = Task ("CI-K3S", "Kubernetes compliance tested with k3s on GitHub Actions (user 2026-10-15 21:20): manifests for the services that can run headless, applied to a throwaway k3s cluster in CI", Story, P3, D5, 0u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,kubernetes", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_1 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:44 helper-lane lap 1: inventory for the manifests (git ls-files over spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, eoie, blog, i574n.github, dir-tree-html): no Kubernetes manifests, compose files or service Dockerfiles exist; the only container files are five .devcontainer/Dockerfile (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, i574n.github), which are dev images, not services. Headless long-running candidates that a k3s smoke could host: the Spiral compiler host's --server mode (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host) and the Cells compiler daemon (apps/cells compiler_daemon.ex); everything else is a CLI or a static site. So step 1 is a service image (compiler --server with a readiness probe), and only then manifests + a k3s job. Gate PIPELINES-GREEN unchanged (Active 35%).")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_2 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: checked whether the compiler server can be the first k3s service: SpiralCompiler --server <socket> <stall> (host Program.fs:1183) and its TCP variant runTcpServer (:981-992) listen on 127.0.0.1 with port 0 (OS-picked), write the port and pid to <socket>.bridge / pid files, and print spiral-session-ready port=N pid=N. So in a container it is not reachable as is: a Kubernetes Service needs a fixed port on 0.0.0.0 and a readiness probe; the ready line is a good exec/log probe, but the bind address and port must become configurable first (e.g. an env var for address and port, defaulting to today's loopback/0 so local Cells clients are unaffected). That small host change is the actual first step of this item.")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_3 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:10 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the host change as lab/CI-K3S/server_bind_patch.py (one anchor in apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs runTcpServer line 984, --check OK): SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND=address:port picks the listener's endpoint, unset keeps today's 127.0.0.1:0, so local Cells clients and the .bridge port file are unchanged; an invalid value fails at startup with the expected form. Probed the parser first (lab/CI-K3S/bind_parse.fsx, bounded fsi): IPEndPoint.TryParse reads 0.0.0.0:7470, 127.0.0.1:0 and [::]:7470 as expected, rejects localhost:7470, but silently reads a bare 7470 as the IPv4 address 0.0.29.46 with port 0, so the patch only calls TryParse when the value has exactly one colon (or ]: for IPv6) and otherwise fails. Rides the next host build; a k3s Service then targets the fixed port with spiral-session-ready as the readiness signal.")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_4 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CI-K3S/server_bind_patch.py dry run matches apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs today. It rides the next host build: with SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND unset nothing changes, so it is offered to main alongside pack 11.")
inl near_windows_native () = Task ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", "NEAR builds still need WSL (user 2026-10-15 21:20: avoid WSL on this machine, patching near would cost patches and compile times): track whether native Windows builds become possible; until then NEAR parts stay last and off the local machine", TaskKind, P3, D3, 0u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "near,windows,wsl", "current", "current")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_1 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: no WSL3 exists as far as known; WSL2's memory is bounded only by .wslconfig (memory=, autoMemoryReclaim). Decision kept: no WSL on the dev machine, NEAR and other Linux-only parts run on CI Linux runners.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_2 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T02:56Z", "main-thread", "22:56 main: correction of my previous note: WSL 3.0.3.0 exists and is installed (kernel 6.18.54, user updated it 10-15). Benchmark (job tmp/wslbench/bench.ps1, Ubuntu): cold boot 12.7 s, warm call 0.9 s, idle VM ~2.0-2.3 GB of host memory, a 2 GB allocation inside raised the VM to 4.3 GB and it returned to 2.3 GB within 20 s (reclaim works, unlike WSL2), ext4 write 643 MB/s vs /mnt/c 74 MB/s, listing 9,006 files over /mnt/c 5.9 s, shutdown releases everything. Verdict: NEAR tests can come back as bounded WSL jobs: clone into ext4, run through Invoke-Heavy -Wsl, wsl --shutdown afterwards, never during a Tibia live block. Risk: .wslconfig memory=12GB on a 16 GB machine lets one cargo build take the host down; recommended memory=6GB (user's file, not edited). NixOS and nixos2 distros are installed too (CI-NIX can be tried locally in WSL).")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_3 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:44 helper-lane lap 1: narrowed what actually needs WSL, from dice/contract's scripts: the contract build is already Windows-native (BuildSpiral to Rust, then cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown, export check, dist/dice.wasm); only tests/build.ps1 goes to Linux (Invoke-Block -Linux -> spiral scripts/core.ps1 Invoke-Linux -> wsl --exec bash, cargo run of the near-workspaces sandbox tests, which need the Linux/mac-only neard sandbox binary). So the WSL-free route on this machine is: keep the native wasm build, run the contract logic as mocked unit tests on Windows (NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS: testing_env!/VMContextBuilder), and leave the sandbox tests to CI Linux. Side find: the contract pins nightly-2024-07-14 while the workspace moved to nightly-2026-10-18 (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE).")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_4 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: confirmed the split already in dice's scripts: scripts/build.ps1 sets nearSandboxRunsHere = IsLinux and only then builds the contract (contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1) and runs the sandbox tests (contract/tests/build.ps1, 3 retries); the gh-pages workflow runs on ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025, so CI Linux covers the sandbox and Windows (local and CI) skips the whole contract, including the wasm build that needs no WSL. Concrete improvement within the no-WSL rule: on Windows run contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1 too, so the wasm build and its export check (new, contribute_seed, roll_within_bounds ...) are proven natively on every Windows run; only tests/build.ps1 needs Linux. Not edited: dice and alphabet must change together (mirror rule) and dice CI is red on other causes right now.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_5 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:12 helper-lane lap 3: staged the change as lab/NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE/dice_build_patch.py (anchor --check OK on dice scripts/build.ps1, LF): contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1 moves out of the IsLinux block, so the wasm build and its export check run on every Windows run, while contract/tests/build.ps1 (the NEAR sandbox) stays Linux-only. Checked the Windows prerequisites by reading: contract/build.ps1 is platform-neutral (Spiral compile, cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build for wasm32, a pure-PowerShell wasm export reader), and polyglot scripts/init.ps1 installs nightly-2024-07-14 with the wasm32 target on any OS; this machine has that toolchain. Mirror rule: alphabet has no NEAR contract (its scripts never mention contract or near), so the change has no alphabet counterpart. Not applied: dice CI is still red from the 06:48 run; apply after the user's push turns it green.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_6 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE/dice_build_patch.py dry run matches dice/scripts/build.ps1 today. It is applied together with the alphabet mirror.")
inl sefaz_xsd_app () = Task ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", "New app (user 2026-10-15 21:50): a Spiral generator from all SEFAZ XSDs, Tibia style with test blocks and X-compatible from day one, Delphi backend, ACBr and Zeus as the benchmarks the way Tibia uses open-source bots, generating GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes able to carry ERP engines; open choice: generate at build time in Rust (build.rs + OUT_DIR caching, crate reuse) or in Spiral itself, since Zig comptime would re-parse the XSDs on every build", Epic, P2, D8, 0u32, Planned, "", "app,sefaz,delphi,spiral", "current", "current")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_1 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:22Z", "main-thread", "10:22 main-thread fork: decision input (user question 2026-10-17: keep advancing Tibia for dogfood, or start the XSD tasks?). Measured from the backlog: the Tibia lane surfaced ~20 tooling items on 2026-10-12..14 (compiler GADT-ONCE fixes, C self-tail/incref loops, WGSL tail-call/union/dispatch bugs, Kino memo scope and Rust/C flag parity, hopac Debug crash, private-cache leak, macro-argument limits, eoie note author), 2 tooling notes on 10-14, and 0 since: 10-15 had 68 tibia-lane notes all on TIBIA items (potion fidelity, hunting ground, retreat, policy), 10-16/17 none. Remaining TIBIA work is game fidelity and policy, i.e. business logic; its compiled modules stay valuable as a regression corpus (already in the pre-pack corpus check). SEFAZ-XSD would exercise surfaces Tibia never touched: generating thousands of types from XSDs (compile-time scale, a real load for hopacsota's parallel evaluation), reflection/prototype codegen (the CONVO-IN-SPIRAL schema machinery, both directions), XML parse/serialize, the Delphi backend (least used), X-compat from day one. Recommendation: make SEFAZ-XSD-APP the next dogfood once reprioritized; Tibia stays maintenance + corpus. Open choice in the title (Rust build.rs vs Spiral-side generation) is the first experiment, in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/ per LAB-IN-REPO.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_2 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:46Z", "main-thread", "10:46 main-thread fork: XSD census (research only, not a start; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/census.py, input = sparse XSD-only clone of the Zeus benchmark ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET, kept outside the repo as data): 580 .xsd (500 distinct contents, 5.1 MB, 1 parse error; NF-e/NFC-e, CT-e and its modals, MDF-e, events, DF-e distribution, several versions each), 12,076 elements, 2,238 complexTypes (572 named, 151 distinct names; the rest anonymous inline), 5,389 simpleTypes (1,445 named, 179 distinct names; TCOrgaoIBGE x35, TVerEvento x33 across versions), 11,360 enumeration facets, 2,055 regex patterns, 1,875 maxLength / 1,759 minLength, 393 choices, 819 attributes, 590 includes + 83 imports, max nesting depth 23. Mapping for the generator: enumerations -> unions (11k cases), choices -> unions, minOccurs/maxOccurs -> option/array, length/pattern facets -> validated nominal types whose regexes compile through the brzozowski derivatives work (lib dogfood), shared tiposBasico -> one module per (family, version) with content-hash dedupe (80 duplicate files). Build.rs-vs-Spiral question: parsing 5 MB is ~1 s, so the real cost is compiling the generated Spiral (12k elements of types and validators) - first experiment: generate nfe v4.00 + its tiposBasico only, measure single-flight and hopacsota compile time, which doubles as a large hopacsota load test.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_3 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:54Z", "main-thread", "10:54 main-thread fork: enumeration mapping measured (research, not a start; scripts spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/enum_gen.py and enums_gen.py, generated .spi written to the git-ignored lab/.runs/sefaz-generated/, XSD data = the DFe.NET sparse clone): TCodUfIBGE (tiposBasico_v1.03, 27 values) -> union + parse (option) + code_of: 94 lines, compiles and round-trips '35' (exit 27), 3 s with gcc. All 58 distinct named enumerated simpleTypes of the corpus = 2,179 cases -> 4,653 lines in one module: compiles in 5 s total (only one parse reachable from main, so this is front end + type check cost, peval of the rest is lazy), 1.25 MB of C for that one used parser chain. Most of the 11,360 enumeration facets are anonymous inline simpleTypes inside the leiaute files, so the generator must name them by element path. Next for the decision: generate the full NF-e v4.00 type set (named + inline) and compile it with every type reachable, single-flight vs hopacsota.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_4 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: inline enumerations measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/inline_enums.py): 1,405 enumerated simpleTypes (313 named, 1,092 inline: leiauteNFe 325, cteTiposBasico 186, prod 81, ...) collapse to only 266 DISTINCT value sets holding 3,312 values (vs 11,360 enumeration facets): dedupe by value set is a ~3.4x cut. Shape: 473 single-value enums (fixed constants such as versao 1.00 -> a literal/singleton type, not a union), 306 two-value (mostly 0/1 or 1/2 flags), 130 three, 132 four; the largest has 792 values. So the generator emits ~266 shared enumeration unions plus typed singleton constants, keyed by value set and referenced by element path; that is far below the 4,653-line / 2,179-case module that already compiles in 5 s. Still to measure for the decision: complexTypes (2,238, mostly anonymous) and the 2,055 patterns.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_5 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 5u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: patterns measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/patterns.py): 2,055 pattern facets are only 196 DISTINCT regexes (median length 20, max 650: the NFC-e QR-code URL); features over the distinct ones: char classes 182, bounded repeats {n,m} 166, alternation 98, groups 64, optional 51, star/plus 16, \\\\d\\\\s\\\\w 12, XSD class subtraction -[ 4, negated class 1, \\\\p properties 0; most reused [0-9]{8} x73, [0-9]{1,4} x67, [0-9]{6} x67, [0-9]{1,15} x64. Reading: a small regex dialect (classes, bounded repeats, alternation, groups) compiled once per distinct pattern into DFAs - exactly what the brzozowski-derivatives work does; only XSD class subtraction needs a dedicated rule. Decision inputs now complete on enumerations (266 value sets) and patterns (196); complexTypes are the remaining unmeasured part.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_6 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: complexTypes measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/complex_types.py, structure = element names/types/occurs and sequence/choice nesting): 2,238 complexTypes are 826 distinct structures (2.7x dedupe); 539 contain a choice (-> unions), 719 carry attributes; most are small records (1-10 descendant elements for ~1,650 of them) with a long tail (84 with 40+ descendants: the NF-e/CT-e document roots). Generator size estimate after dedupe: ~826 records/unions + ~266 enumeration unions + ~196 compiled patterns + typed singleton constants, organized per (family, version) with shared basics. All research scripts (census, enum_gen, enums_gen, inline_enums, patterns, complex_types) live in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app and read a DFe.NET sparse XSD clone (git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --no-checkout https://github.com/ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET.git; git sparse-checkout set --no-cone '*.xsd'). Still a research item: start only on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_7 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 7u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the six measurement .py scripts deleted; every number they produced (1,405 enums -> 266 value sets, 2,055 patterns -> 196 regexes, 2,238 complexTypes -> 826 structures) and the DFe.NET sparse clone command are in notes 1-6. The generator itself is to be written in Spiral on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_8 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:12 helper-lane lap 1: verified the lab folder spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app is gone (cleanup 10-17 18:05; numbers live in notes 1-6). Prerequisite found for the Spiral-side generator that note 7 settles on: lib/spiral has no XML reader at all (the only xml hits are xmlns attributes in 10 leptos svg files), so the first slice needs either a Rust-arm primitive over a crate (roxmltree, read-only DOM, fits XSD reading) or an XSD-subset tokenizer in Spiral; roxmltree keeps it to one $ primitive plus typed Spiral walking, but Kino test blocks cannot use crates yet (KINO-RUST-CRATES), so tests of that slice would be e2e only. Still research: start only on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_9 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:05 helper-lane lap 2: sized the first slice's input so the reader decision is concrete (research only, no generator started): the DFe.NET sparse clone still exists as data at C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\xsd-data (580 .xsd). tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd is 23 KB / 608 lines with only 13 element kinds (enumeration 116, annotation/documentation/restriction/simpleType 53 each, whiteSpace 49, pattern 43, maxLength 16, minLength 3, element 2, complexType 1, sequence 1, schema 1), 7 attribute names (name, type, base, value, targetNamespace, elementFormDefault, attributeFormDefault), 2 comments, no CDATA and no entity references. So a Spiral XSD-subset tokenizer (start/end/self-closing tags, double-quoted attributes, text, comments) covers this file without a crate, which favours the no-crate route over roxmltree for slice 1 and keeps Kino test blocks usable.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_10 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:21 helper-lane lap 3: sized the second input the same way (research only; lab/SEFAZ-XSD-APP/xsd_shape.py, python -I over the job-tmp DFe.NET clone): leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd is 352 KB / 7,718 lines, 21 element kinds (documentation 915, annotation 914, element 900, enumeration 424, simpleType/restriction 295, whiteSpace 176, sequence 175, complexType 121, maxLength 117, minLength 116, pattern 77, choice 22, attribute 15, length 5, include 2, unique/selector/field 2 each, import 1), 20 attribute names (incl. minOccurs 333, maxOccurs 26, use 15, ref 2, xpath 4, schemaLocation 3, xmlns:ds), indentation depth 24, 6 comments, no CDATA, only double-quoted attributes, 23 entity references of exactly two kinds (&quot; 14, &amp; 9) plus the xml declaration. So the slice-1 tokenizer from lap 2 grows by: a processing-instruction skip, the 5 predefined entities in attribute values and text, and identity constraints (unique/selector/field) parsed but ignored by the generator. Still no crate needed. Research only; the generator starts on an explicit go.")
inl suite_map () = Task ("SUITE-MAP", "Map the whole test suite (user 2026-10-15 21:50): harness rows, lib and app test blocks, Kino suites, eoie tests, Tibia tests, by area, cost and overlap, like the TODO sweep does for comments, so it can be split and run in parallel deliberately", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "tests,harness,map", "current", "current")
inl note_of_suite_map_1 () = Note ("SUITE-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night map: harness 6,906 rows (examples 6,073, contracts 724, frontier 104, mega 5); rows and native-ran per backend: F# 967/0, C 519/493, Zig 457, TypeScript 447, Python 444, Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Lean 443, Gleam 443, Wasm 441, Lua 439, Rust 427/415, Delphi 414/402, Bend 401, Ebpf 52, Wgsl 50, OpenQasm 31, Calyx 28, Cpp 16/12. Spiral test blocks: spiral/lib 375, apps/tibia 185, x 48, dice 32, polyglot 24, blog 21, eoie 11, alphabet 2, kino 0. ExUnit: Kino 240, x sim 30. eoie hand-written Rust tests: 25 files, 85 fns (vendor excluded).")
inl spiral_path_syntax () = Task ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", "Module and field access with '.' (posts.post, p.author, listm.map) reads as a domain to X, which rewrites it into t.co links when sources are posted; the symbol-only syntax work could switch member access to '::' (Rust-like) or another separator. Decide with the edition-3 syntax lane; check conflicts with existing '::' uses (GADT constructor signatures)", Story, P3, D3, 5u32, Planned, "", "spiral,syntax,x-compat", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night evidence (candidate --x-compat autolinks = member access X would link): lib/spiral 272 (.map 154, .run 22, .to 22, .box 16), apps/tibia 155 (.at 28, .ms 25, .target 18), blog 5, x client 0 after hand renames. Renaming cannot scale, so postable edition-3 sources need a separator change.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: measured the '::' conflict the title asks about: GADT case signatures (| Case :: ...) occur on 4,412 lines in 467 .spi/.spir files across the repos (eoie state alone: core 47, coverage 32, pipeline 24, differential 18; tibia 23; dice 2), so '::' as member access would only be separable by spacing (a::b vs Case :: t), a whitespace-sensitive lexer rule that the formatter and every reader must keep. Also sharpened the problem: X only autolinks when the field name after the dot is a real TLD (the 10-17 census hits are .map, .run, .to, .box, .at, .ms - all delegated TLDs), so members whose name is not a TLD (p.author) are safe while TLD names (.post is a sponsored TLD, hence posts.post) link; the separator change matters only for TLD-named members.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: sized the rename-only alternative against IANA's current TLD list (1,437 delegated TLDs, data.iana.org): across lib/spiral .spi, dotted member names that are TLDs number 17 distinct names in about 397 uses (regex count, macro text included): map 150, box 94, show 83, to 20, run 15, org 13, now 5, data 3, storage 3, stream 2, pin 2, day 2, open/do/city/select/zip 1 each. The top three (map, box, show) are 82 percent of all autolink-prone uses in lib, so renaming three functions (e.g. map -> each / map_to, box -> boxed, show -> shown) would remove most of lib's X links without a separator change; tibia's .at .ms .target and the long tail would still need the separator or per-repo renames. Input for the edition-3 decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES' renames could absorb the three.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: extended the rename-only sizing from lib to every repo with a reusable counter (lab/SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX/tld_members.py + tlds.txt, IANA list fetched today: 1,437 TLDs; regex over .spi, so file names inside strings such as eoie's .rs/.md also count). TLD-named members after a dot, distinct/uses/top-3 share: lib/spiral 16/381/81% (map 135, box 94, show 83); tibia/src 16/156/48% (at 34, ms 26, health 15, gold 13, band 13); eoie/src 19/257/55% (rs 58, zip 54, report 31, target 30); dice 10/195/69% (map 56, box 41, th/td 38 each: html tables); x 3/33 (id 31); blog/engine 3/7; apps/cells/spi 1/5 (one). So renaming three lib functions fixes lib and most of dice, but tibia and eoie spread over domain words (at, ms, health, report, target, id), and x's .id is its own record field; a rename-only route would mean per-repo vocabulary bans, which favours a separator change for edition 3. Input for the edition-3 lane's decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES carries the lib part.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:45 helper-lane lap 4: sized the other side of the '::' separator option: tight a::b already occurs 5,137 times in 437 .spi files under C:\\home\\git, but nearly all of it is Rust path text inside macros (std::fs::..., std::env::... in eoie, x, dice, lib), which the Spiral lexer never reads as Spiral tokens, while every Spiral use of '::' is the spaced GADT signature (Case :: type, 4,412 lines per lap 1). So '::' member access is lexically separable today by the rule tight = member access, spaced = GADT signature, with macro bodies unaffected; the cost is readability, since Spiral member paths would look like the Rust paths in the macros next to them, and the formatter must never insert spaces around a member '::'. X does not autolink a::b (no dot), which is the goal. Input for the edition-3 lane: '::' works mechanically; a non-Rust-looking separator avoids the visual collision.")
inl x_compat_parameterize () = Task ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", "X specifics are hardcoded in shared tooling: compiler section XCompat + host --x-compat, Kino Spiral.Kino.XCompat (+ priv/tlds.txt, edition-3 warnings). Turn them into a postability profile (weights, link rules, limits) that the x repo supplies, so spiral core stays platform-neutral", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,kino,compiler,x-compat", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_1 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: XC005 adds more X constants to the shared section (280, weight ranges, 23 per link); they belong in the postability profile with the TLD list.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:15 P3 -> P2: user 2026-10-16 asked that X-specific things hardcoded in shared tooling move to or be parameterized from the x repo; the compiler section belongs to the compiler lane")
inl x_near_provenance () = Task ("X-NEAR-PROVENANCE", "Hypothesis: anchor (post id, content hash) of program threads on NEAR so readers can tell an executed thread from an edited or forged one (X edits/deletes; apps/sim models them). dice already compiles Spiral to a NEAR wasm contract tested with near-workspaces, but the sandbox needs WSL on Windows (slow); only worth it once X-CLIENT-THREADS runs programs fetched from X", Story, P5, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "x,near,provenance", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_near_provenance_1 () = Note ("X-NEAR-PROVENANCE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:59Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:59 folded into X-ON-NEAR (2026-10-18): anchoring post hashes becomes a byproduct once posts themselves live in a contract")
inl spiral_dispatch_unify () = Task ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", "One backend dispatch pattern in lib/spiral and apps (user 2026-10-15 23:00): run_target and its family date from the Fable era, is_zig/is_rust guards are drift; unify on the compiler-native BackendSwitch, measured", Feature, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,backends", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: review. History: run_target (polyglot e8e47636, 2023-10) emitted both arms into one F# file under #if FABLE_COMPILER_RUST / #else, so its API is a union target plus a function of it; when the compiler gained BackendSwitch it was re-implemented on lib backend_switch but kept that API and a 7-case union (Gleam Lua Fsharp Cuda=Python Cpp Rust TypeScript). Consequence: a run_target '| _ =>' arm never reaches Zig, C, Lean, Wasm, Bend or Delphi (they hit backend_switch's per-backend no-arm placeholder), which is why 33 is_zig () guards appeared (14 lib files) next to 45 is_rust (); usage 10-15: run_target family ~150 uses in 21 files, backend_switch records 217 in 30, direct BackendSwitch 4. A Zig case in the union would break every exhaustive run_target match, and GADTs or prototypes do not help because the backend is a compile-time constant, not a value to refine.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: benchmark (job tmp/dispatchbench: generate.ps1 + run-batch.ps1, 400 generated functions per style, one warm --batch process, first-compile ms, medians over 4 rotated style orders; Rust column = parse + typecheck + peval, Zig/C = peval + codegen): run_target 1,955 / 1,135 / 851; is_zig guard 2,899 / 618 / 1,294; record listing all 14 keys 3,263 / 1,734 / 1,437; record with only the needed keys + a default (backend_switch_or) 1,920 / 941 / 838. Decision: backend_switch_or (fallback) { needed keys } everywhere: cost-neutral or better than run_target, its default really covers every backend, every arm still validated, no union, no guards; listing every key is the slowest form and is avoided. Grain 1 done: backend_switch_or added to lib/spiral/backend.spi, platform.is_windows migrated (is_zig guard + run_target -> one record with Rust/Fsharp/Gleam/Zig + failwith default): Rust/F#/Zig/Gleam outputs byte-identical, C changes from the uncompilable backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder to a runtime failwith, platform.spi Kino test 1/1. Next: the 33 is_zig guard sites file by file with the lib sweep (lane-b lib-sweep.ps1) before/after, then the standalone run_target sites; run_target and its union are deleted when the last caller is gone.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 3u32, "2026-10-16T04:20Z", "main-thread", "00:20 main: grain 2 done in the tree: all 32 is_zig () guard chains in lib/spiral (am', base, common, console x9, crypto, env, file_system x5, guid x2, listm', sm' x4, testing, trace x3) rewritten by tmp/dispatchbench/guards-to-records.ps1 into inl otherwise () = <old else branch, unchanged> + backend_switch_or otherwise { Rust/Zig keys from the old branches }; is_zig () now has 0 callers outside backend.spi. Needed by hand: type annotations on 4 sites (record arms are separate fields, so a bare macro arm is a value-restriction error; a typed_arm helper does not work because Spiral generalizes the local otherwise) and Kino's cell console.spi gained CppHost/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend keys (lib records validate all 14 keys, and inside a Kino cell console.write_line is Kino's 9-key switch). Proof: probe programs for 15 migrated functions x 8 backends (tmp/dispatchbench/probes.ps1): 118 of 120 outputs byte-identical, same 91 compile; the 2 differences are substring on C (old: is_rust () evaluated to the backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder as a C bool; new: the real default) and file_delete on Zig (extra unused trace globals, BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK). Lib sweep of the 13 touched notebooks 126/126 before and after; harness rows of the 7 lib-using samples 53/53 parity with the oracle, DISAGREE 0; Kino mix test 239 (1 known env-only failure); Tibia combat.spi 7/7. Semantic lesson: a record arm is evaluated (for its type) under every backend's context, an if-guard branch never was, so code moved into a default must be valid under every key. Not done: apps/spiral regeneration; the standalone run_target sites (~100) and the 45 is_rust () uses (step 3).")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 4u32, "2026-10-16T04:48Z", "main-thread", "00:48 main: design revised with evidence. Converting the ~100 run_target matches to records would need a type annotation on every arm (records make each arm a separate field; matches unify their arms), so the scalable unification is the other way round: complete union backend (+ Zig, C, Lean, Delphi, Wasm, Bend; run_target_args' maps every BackendSwitch key), so every | _ => really reaches every backend and Zig gets | Zig => arms; the 24 step-2 records whose default is a run_target fold back into it (tmp/dispatchbench/fold-zig-arms.ps1). Proof in a lib copy: probes 120/120 identical to the unfolded union version, 113/120 identical to the current tree, the 7 differences all C rows moving from backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholders to the _ default; only base.spi's test-only exhaustive match needed a | _ arm. Cost (400 generated functions, 4 rotations, machine loaded by a concurrent review): the complete-union run_target is +28% (Rust), +16% (Zig), +15% (C) over today's 8-key run_target, and +28/+24/+38% over a record with a default. Decision: complete union for value-level dispatch (correctness on the Zig default and every backend outweighs the cost); records stay for type-level switches and keyed defaults; is_rust/is_zig go. Applying to the tree after the pack-1p review frees the lib, with a sweep of every run_target notebook.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 5u32, "2026-10-16T04:49Z", "main-thread", "00:49 main: is_rust () decision: the ~30 standalone uses in lib (async, resultm, threading, date_time, am', parsing, runtime, convert, wasm, testing, file_system) stay. They are plain two-way if splits, not guards in front of a run_target; a static if evaluates only the taken branch, so it compiles cheaper and avoids the every-backend evaluation trap that the Kino console hit. Rule: a guard in front of a dispatch is drift and becomes an arm (done for all is_zig sites); a standalone binary predicate is allowed.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 6u32, "2026-10-16T07:18Z", "main-thread", "03:18 main: final design applied to the tree lib (14 files from the proven copy tmp/dispatchbench/libunion; backups tmp/rotation/lib-before-union): union backend has every key, run_target_args' maps each, lib dispatch is run_target matches, backend_switch_or removed (no code references left). AGENTS.md lib dispatch paragraph rewritten (guard chain in front of a dispatch = drift; standalone is_rust () predicate = fine). Lib sweep on the installed pack-1p DLL: 14 of 38 notebooks pass (am', crypto, guid, parsing, physics, runtime, sm', testing, trace, rust/testing, ...) before a memory reap; the remaining 24 run after the pack-1q probes.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 7u32, "2026-10-16T08:58Z", "main-thread", "04:58 main: lib sweep of the tree lib (complete-union design) on the installed compiler: 38/38 notebooks pass, 329 tests, 0 failed (02:41-03:08 first 19 before a memory reap, 177 tests; 04:47-04:58 the other 19 on pack-1q, 152 tests: date_time 32, seq 26, listm' 22, stream 16, file_system 15, math 14, ...). Harness: the pack-1q review shows 0 residual changes on every non-Kotlin/Swift backend, so the rewrite is output-neutral on all harness samples. Left: a Kino mix test on the tree lib, and the kino console.spi switch (records with every key) is fine as is.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 8u32, "2026-10-16T10:07Z", "main-thread", "06:07 main: Kino mix test on the tree (union-design lib, installed pack-1r compiler): 239 tests, 0 failures, 1 excluded (06:04-06:07). All proofs in: probes 120/120 vs the fold, harness 0 residual changes on non-Kotlin/Swift backends (pack-1q review), lib sweep 38/38 notebooks 329 tests, Kino 239/0. Done; the 14 lib files + backend.spi + AGENTS.md paragraph are ready to commit.")
inl bend2_in_spiral () = Task ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite the Bend 2 checker and compiler (TypeScript today) in Spiral with GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection from day one, as a benchmark against the TS version and Bend 2 self-hosted later (user 2026-10-15 23:00)", Epic, P2, D8, 0u32, Planned, "LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", "bend,spiral,kernel,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: assessment of the local clone (spiral-bin toolchains/bend, bendlang/bend d3cacb7 2026-10-08): core ~12.8k TS lines (bend2/comp.ts 6,385, bend.ts 3,882, safe.ts 1,567, main.ts 924) plus 1,693 .bend test files (tests/check 265, flatten 245, io 157, parse 153, compile 130, reg 111, run 96, proof 94); the repo ships its own flake.nix. Worth it: the test corpus is a ready differential oracle (TS implementation as reference, the Tibia pattern), a native Spiral checker gives the speed benchmark against bun/TS and later Bend-in-Bend, and its term/substitution/normalization/conversion core is the same layer as LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS, so both kernels can share bottom modules. Cost: fast upstream churn (#1337 on 10-08). Start after the Lean kernel's first layers: parser + checker against the 418 parse/check tests, measured against bun bend2.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:13 helper-lane lap 1: churn measured (git fetch into the toolchain clone only, worktree still d3cacb7): upstream bendlang/bend moved 105 commits in 11 days, d3cacb7 (10-08, #1337) -> 8f86f73 (10-19 09:05 -0300, #1653), and bend2/ alone changed 21 files, +2,292/-1,526 lines. Today's local bend2 sizes (non-blank lines): comp.ts 5,756, bend.ts 3,674, safe.ts 1,457, main.ts 853. So a Spiral port chasing HEAD would rewrite ~30% of the checker per fortnight: the port must target one pinned commit and use the 1,693 .bend tests of that commit as the oracle, rebasing deliberately. Still gated on LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:05 helper-lane lap 2: listed the kernel layer a shared bottom module must cover, from the local clone (d3cacb7) bend2/bend.ts (156 top-level functions) and safe.ts (78): evaluation term_wnf, term_snf, term_force, term_apply/term_unapply; conversion term_compare, term_descend, term_key; binding ctx_bind, tele_bind, tele_unbind, term_higher/term_lower (de Bruijn level shifts); checking Infer, Check, term_infer, term_check, term_check_kind, tele_check, def_check; and safe.ts's subst, scope_bind, kernel_check, safe_check, inferable. That is the same layer as LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS' whnf/defeq/infer, so the shared Spiral module boundary is: terms with levels + shift (higher/lower) + subst, then wnf/snf, then compare, then infer/check; the parser (parse_term* , ~11 functions) stays per-language.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:22 helper-lane lap 3: answered the term-representation question by reading bend2/bend.ts (d3cacb7): Bend 2 keeps TWO forms, LTerm = TermOf<[LTerm]> whose Var.i is a de Bruijn LEVEL (term_lower line 782 binds Var(k, d + j) at depth d) and HTerm = TermOf<HBody> where every binder body is a host closure (x: HTerm) => HTerm (HOAS); term_higher (693) evaluates LTerm into HTerm by indexing an env list by level, i.e. normalization by evaluation with levels for quoting. Lean 4's kernel is locally nameless instead (bvar indices + fvar ids, instantiate/abstract). Decision for the shared bottom module: levels plus NbE values whose binders are DEFUNCTIONALIZED closures (env list, body term), not host lambdas: levels need no shifting under binders (Bend's choice), Lean's fvars map onto levels at check time, and defunctionalized closures stay comparable and printable on every Spiral backend (C/Zig/Lua have no cheap first-class closures; HOAS bodies cannot be hashed for term_key). Lean's instantiate becomes eval in an extended env. Still gated on LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS (Active 5%).")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:30 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the lap-3 representation decision as code, lab/BEND2-IN-SPIRAL/nbe.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): terms with de Bruijn LEVELS (Var level, Lam body, App), values with defunctionalized closures (VLam of env list * body, plus neutral VVar level and VApp), eval extends a closure's env by one value per binder (the binder's level is the env length), apply beta-reduces or builds a neutral, and quote depth reads a VLam back by evaluating its body against VVar depth, so no index shifting appears anywhere (the property that made Bend choose levels). Check: the normal form of Church two applied to two is lambda lambda with 4 applications, and (lambda x. x) (lambda y. y) quotes to Lam (Var 0); expected exit 142. This is the bottom layer the Lean kernel notebooks and a Bend 2 checker would share.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T16:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:00 helper-lane lap 5: extended nbe.spi with the next kernel layer (unrun until the freeze all-clear): Pi of domain * codomain (codomain under one binder) and Typ in terms, VPi carrying the domain value plus a defunctionalized codomain closure and VTyp in values, eval/quote for both, and conversion as equality of normal forms (conv a b = quote 0 a = quote 0 b). New checks: (lambda A. A) Typ converts to Typ, (lambda A. Pi A A) Typ converts to Pi Typ Typ (the codomain's level-0 reference resolves through the closure env), and Typ does not convert to Pi Typ Typ; expected exit 111142 together with the earlier 142.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: nbe.spi failed to parse at a one-line nested pattern (match identity with | Lam (Var 0i32) => ...); replaced by an is_identity helper with nested multi-line matches, rerun pending.")
inl crate_gate_harden () = Task ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", "Harden the 1,000-line generated-crate gate (user 2026-10-15 23:00: nice seeing the 1k loc paying off): it already counts .rs + .spi per crate dir in eoie source-topology; make it a gate in every repo that emits Rust crates and decide whether .spi stays inside the count", TaskKind, P3, D3, 10u32, Planned, "", "eoie,gate,rust", "current", "current")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_1 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: evidence 10-15: the gate stopped the strict renewal when agile_todo_scan reached 1,184 (922 .rs + 167 .spi + 95 notebook .spi) and forced a clean split into sources/scan crates; the largest crate after the split is command_spec_domain at 998, so it must be split before the next agile verb.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_2 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory: gated: eoie (crate max 949-998), x (report-only, view.rs 1,885 over); ungated monoliths: apps/spiral spiral.rs 24,281 lines, dice ui main.rs 16,878, dice lib dice.rs 6,639, apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,464.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_3 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 3u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x: all 12 generated crates under 1,000 after the view split (view 999, digest 989, jsonl 929, args 889, xc 862); the margin on view is 1 line, so the gate stays report-only until RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION or a further split gives room")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_4 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: re-inventory 10-19 (lines of the generated .rs): ungated monoliths apps/spiral spiral.rs 23,418 (24,281 on 10-17), dice ui src/main.rs 16,878 (same), dice lib dice.rs 7,777 (6,639 on 10-17, grew 17 percent), apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, dice contract dice_contract.rs 3,396 and polyglot chat_contract.rs 1,374 (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES); x's largest crates are now digest 991, view 991, args 960, so view gained 8 lines of margin since 10-18 but the report-only mode should stay until there are ~5 percent of headroom. The gate (eoie source-topology) still exists only in eoie; dice lib growing past 7.7k is the strongest case for adding report-only counts to dice's build first (it mirrors to alphabet).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_5 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:31 helper-lane lap 2: wrote the report-only step as lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1 (a generated crate = an .rs with a sibling .spi; prints those over the limit, never fails) and ran it on dice, alphabet, spiral/apps and polyglot/apps: 448 generated crates, 12 over 1,000 lines - spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet apps/documents documents.rs 15,574 (missing from the 10-17 inventory), dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374, and 6 compiler samples' main.rs (mega roots and typecheck_long_let_body, which are matrix fixtures and should be excluded from any gate). dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is not caught because its source .spi is not beside it, so the rule needs the [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output convention (x's) to find every generated crate. Call it in-process (& script -Root a, b): pwsh -File collapses the -Root array.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_6 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: switched the report to the planned detection (lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1, still report-only): generated crates = outputs declared by a Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] output = path, plus the sibling rule (an .rs beside its .spi); apps/compiler/tmp/samples (matrix fixtures) and target/deps/dist are excluded. Run over dice, alphabet, spiral/apps, polyglot/apps, x and blog: 26 generated crates (17 declared by metadata, all of them x's, every one under 1,000 lines), 6 over the limit, all sibling-rule: spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet documents.rs 15,574, dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374. dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is still invisible because neither rule reaches it; giving dice ui's Cargo.toml the x-style [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output is the one-line fix that makes it count. Next: propose the step as non-failing in dice and alphabet build.ps1 (mirror rule) after their CI is green.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_7 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: staged the one-line fix that makes dice ui visible to the gate: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py adds [package.metadata.spiral] entry = src/dice_ui.spi, output = src/main.rs to dice/ui/Cargo.toml (anchor --check OK; values read from ui/build.ps1, which compiles src/dice_ui.spi to src/main.rs). Cargo ignores package.metadata, so builds are unaffected; with it the report finds main.rs (16,878 lines) as the seventh over-limit crate. The report step for dice and alphabet build.ps1 waits for their CI to be green (mirror rule; alphabet has no ui crate, its counterpart is apps/documents).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_8 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py dry run matches dice/ui/Cargo.toml today. Per the dice/alphabet mirror rule it gets applied to both repos at once.")
inl x_repo_template () = Task ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", "i574n/x as the from-scratch Spiral repo template (create-react-app style): find the shape every new Spiral repo should start from; record each decision with its reason and measured cost", Story, P2, D3, 70u32, Active, "", "template,spiral,x,ci", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_1 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:27Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:27 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Decisions so far: (1) spiral cloned beside the repo (eoie model), no symlinks (dice's deps/polyglot link is the older, slower model) and no SPIRAL_ROOT override, because package.spiproj fixes packageDir ../../../spiral/lib; scripts/common.ps1 resolves XRoot/SpiralRoot and fails with the init hint. (2) scripts/init.ps1 clones spiral from the same owner and runs spiral's init-app-build; scripts/build.ps1 builds every app; scripts/workflow.ps1 = init + build; one CI file .github/workflows/ci.yml (ubuntu + windows) runs workflow.ps1, nothing else standardized (yml standardization stays with the main lane). (3) Root Cargo workspace, stable Rust (generated Rust builds on cargo 1.99.0, no nightly pin), release profile lto+cg1. (4) Kino test blocks run from the sibling Kino with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER=process (no daemon left), outputs under target/. (5) .gitattributes LF + generated client.rs marked. Measured locally (spiral already beside, 16 GB box, heavy gate): full scripts/build.ps1 517 s (client: cargo cold 130 s, e2e ~60 s, 4 zig test files ~180 s; sim: mix test 30/30, corpus run ~60 s, kino executor run ~30 s); client-only rebuild 345 s. Not measured yet: init from scratch, Linux CI.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_2 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:31Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:31 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Open decision: the sibling clone tracks spiral's branch tip with no pin. Everything in x was proven against spiral's uncommitted working tree (tonight's compiler packs, other lanes' Kino edits), so CI (init.ps1 clones spiral HEAD) can differ until spiral is committed and pushed. Options: a ref in init.ps1, or a recorded validated spiral commit per x commit. Also measured: stable Rust via rust-toolchain.toml; the generated client builds on cargo 1.99.0.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_3 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 3u32, "2026-10-16T05:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-16 01:40 template shape now includes a Cargo workspace of apps/<app>/<crate>/ entries, each Cargo.toml carrying [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, a metadata-driven build loop, and scripts/gate.ps1 (report-only, -Enforce throws) for the 1000-line generated-file rule")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_4 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory of x/scripts (4.5 KB): generic as-is init.ps1 (clone spiral beside from the remote owner and domain, spiral init-app-build), gate.ps1 (1,000-line generated Rust gate over package.metadata.spiral crates) and workflow.ps1; common.ps1 is generic except the XRoot name; only build.ps1 lists the apps. The blog repo is the second consumer to prove the template on.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_5 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T05:20Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 01:20 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) User decision, supersedes the open pin question in note 2: no spiral pin. Consumer repos keep cloning spiral main for dogfooding; a red downstream run after a spiral bug is the intended signal (fix spiral, rerun). Stop suggesting a pin unless a concrete new friction outweighs dogfooding.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_6 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T06:41Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 02:41 closed, not open: the template clones spiral main with no pin (user 2026-10-18, memory slide-with-spiral-main). A spiral regression turning x red is the dogfooding signal: fix spiral and rerun x CI. The 2026-10-15 23:31 'open decision' note is superseded")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_7 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 measured since note 1: Linux CI runs (ci.yml: xc-windows 1.4-2.1 min, build ubuntu-24.04 7.8 min, build windows-2025 11.4 min on the green run 39075003305); CI pins OTP 27 / Elixir 1.18 while the box runs OTP 28. Still unmeasured: init.ps1 from an empty folder")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_8 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:47Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:47 CI toolchain bumped to the latest releases in x, spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, blog, polyglot, i574n.github, tapas2, vault: OTP 27 -> 29 (29.1.2, 2026-10-14), Elixir 1.18 -> 1.20 (1.20.4 supports OTP 27-29), Gleam -> 1.19.2, rebar3 3.24.0 -> 3.27.3, setup-beam@v1 -> v1.24.1 where floating. Checked locally on official Elixir 1.20.4/OTP 29: x/apps/sim compiles with --warnings-as-errors, 30/30; Cells had 4 new 1.20 type-checker warnings (3 unpinned size() variables in domain.ex, 1 unreachable warm/1 clause), fixed; Cells still builds on 1.19 and the sim drift guard plus a real-Cells run pass. fc1943s backups, mechahaze and tibia untouched")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_9 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 9u32, "2026-10-18T18:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:17 template gotcha: .github/workflows/actions.lock (gh actions-lock) is enforced by GitHub; a commit that adds an action to a workflow without regenerating the lock fails at startup in 0 s with no log (x run 39140732842: the gh-pages publish job's download-artifact and actions-gh-pages were missing from the committed lock). The template should regenerate the lock in the same commit as any workflow change")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_10 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T00:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:17 init from an empty folder, measured on clean CI runners instead of locally (run 39137498346, the last green ci run): ubuntu-24.04 job 8.4 min, of which scripts/workflow.ps1 (init.ps1 clones spiral beside + init-app-build, then the whole build: client crates, cargo, e2e, sim) 7.6 min; windows-2025 job 19.1 min: moonrepo/setup-rust 6.5 min (toolchain install dominates), setup-beam 0.6, workflow.ps1 11.5; xc-windows (xc only) 1.4 min. Locally the same takes 30-60 min on this shared machine, so CI is the reference for template cost. Left: fold the gh-pages publish job and the actions.lock rule into the template, and the yml standardization the main lane owns")
