inl note_of_pack_night_1017_4 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:45Z", "main-thread", "06:45 main-thread night: corpus check repeated on the exact final DLL (with rust-lazy-default): lib parsing 38, sm 37, runtime 7, testing 6, physics 39, Tibia hunt_sim 11, gpu_sim 6 = 144 passed, 0 failed, 0 missing. Every pre-install gate is met for the day run.")
inl note_of_pack_night_1017_5 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 5u32, "2026-10-17T11:28Z", "main-thread", "07:28 main-thread day: pack applied to the tree (byte-identical to the reviewed candidate) and installed in single-flight (113 s), hopacsota (150 s), gpt5parallel (773 s); export contracts pass. The Kino suite caught a consumer the corpus gate missed: Kino cell.spi patch_rust literal-matched 'Err(_) => 101 });', so after rust-failwith-exit Kino cells returned their i32 as the process exit code (KinoLiveTest x2). Kino now reads the panic code from the join; eoie test-attestation.ps1 likewise. Full review on the installed pack running; bless follows if it reproduces 053315.")
inl note_of_pack_night_1017_6 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 6u32, "2026-10-17T11:51Z", "main-thread", "07:51 main-thread day: review on the installed pack single-flight-20261017-072102 equals the candidate review 053315 on all 6,906 rows (0 cells differ), DISAGREE 0; vs bless 145428 only Zig 457 + Rust 415 residual-only and the 2 intended Rust exit cells 101 -> 1 (failwith_exit now agrees with C). Bless running.")
inl note_of_pack_night_1017_7 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 7u32, "2026-10-17T12:40Z", "main-thread", "08:40 main-thread day: BLESSED from single-flight-20261017-081502 (6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, native tier 1,298 s with 4 lanes) after a first bless attempt was reaped for low memory (it ran beside two fsc builds; retried alone). It reproduces the review single-flight-20261017-072102 on every compile/residual/native/exit/stdout/oracle cell (0 differ), which equals the candidate review 053315. EXPECTED.tsv 6,956 rows. Pack landed: tree + installed in all 3 modes + oracle.")
inl note_of_pack_night_1017_8 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 8u32, "2026-10-17T17:56Z", "main-thread", "13:56 main-thread: eoie integration check of today's compiler (night pack + Zig wide exit + GADT nesting + grain 2a, rc.1-compatible build). build.ps1 -Test -SkipRelease -CompilerContracts -SpiralCompiler <installed DLL> ran cargo test --workspace --include-ignored: 276 test results, 0 failed, and the ignored compiler-contract tests compiled fixtures with the installed compiler. Two environment findings: (1) eoie's agile_note_receipts tests fail inside an agent session because AI_AGENT/CLAUDECODE leak into the eoie child (eoie then demands --as); they pass with both cleared, so the tests should clear them (eoie test isolation). (2) The rc.2 build couldn't run under the system dotnet, now fixed (see MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL).")
inl note_of_pack_night_1017_9 () = Note ("PACK-NIGHT-1017", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:08Z", "main-thread", "14:08 main-thread: the eoie test isolation fix is in the tree. eoie_contracts/tests/agile_note_receipts.rs command() now also env_removes AI_AGENT and CLAUDECODE, so the agent-session --as rule can't leak into tests run from an agent. Measured: the full eoie build -Test -SkipRelease inside a live agent session (CLAUDECODE=1, nothing cleared) gives 276 test results with 0 failed; before the fix, 3 of 6 agile_note_receipts tests failed and stopped the workflow.")
inl fsharp_native_tier () = Task ("FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER", "Run F# rows natively in the matrix: dotnet fsi --exec on each main.fsx with an exit wrap (the generated fsx ends in a bare value, so fsi always exits 0), oracle C, F#-only rows scored by exit; first full run 2026-10-17: of 329 rows with a C row, 327 agree, 2 fail to compile in fsi (FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION), none differ", Feature, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,harness,fsharp", "current", "current")
inl note_of_fsharp_native_tier_1 () = Note ("FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night prototype tmp/night/fsharp-tier/run.ps1: bundled dotnet fsi --exec on each committed main.fsx with |> fun x -> match box x with :? int32 as code -> exit code | _ -> exit 0 appended, stdout hashed like test.ps1, compared with the C row; 543 compile-ok rows in 37 min at 2 lanes (median 7.2 s under load). Harness integration point: test.ps1 excludes Fsharp from the native jobs (the nativeJobs filter); an opt-in switch or a budgeted lane, F#-only rows scored by exit.")
inl note_of_fsharp_native_tier_2 () = Note ("FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:22 main-thread: integrated in the tree (verification in the next review): test.ps1 Build-And-Run has an Fsharp branch (copies main.fsx into the row's bin dir with the exit wrap appended, runs Get-SpiralNativeTools Dotnet fsi --quiet --exec, 60 s; an error FS in its output is build-fail, else exit and stdout hashed like the other backends), the nativeJobs filter no longer drops Fsharp, and env.ps1's tools table gained Dotnet (Resolve-SpiralDotnet). On by default: the native memo replays unchanged F# rows after the first run (prototype: 543 rows, 37 min at 2 lanes). AGENTS.md paragraph added.")
inl note_of_fsharp_native_tier_3 () = Note ("FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER", 3u32, "2026-10-18T14:04Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:04 main-thread first harness run with the tier on: review single-flight-20261018-084525 ran 6,485 native rows (5,941 plus 544 F#), 10 build-fail (the 8 known plus the 2 expected F# while_loop and while_control, FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION), DISAGREE 0. The F# rows got no oracle verdict because test.ps1's oracle loop lists its backends and lacked Fsharp; fixed in the tree (Fsharp added). Scored after the fact from that run: 330 F# rows with a C row agree with C on exit and stdout, 0 differ; 214 are F#-only samples (scored by exit). Not blessed today (the review had 544 behavior rows from the new tier and the bless would have ended after 11:00); the next review plus bless records them.")
inl note_of_fsharp_native_tier_4 () = Note ("FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER", 4u32, "2026-10-18T17:50Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:50 main-thread: pack 2 accepted. Run single-flight-20261018-123705 (installed 10:28 single-flight, SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2): compiled 228.9 s, native 6,485 ran, 10 build-fail, oracle agree 5,766, DISAGREE 0; 6,994 rows written to baseline/EXPECTED.tsv (was 061211). Verification per cell: vs git-0e's review 110213 there are 30 behavior cells and 0 residual differences. 3 are C exit cells (UCRT under mingw 16.2 there, msvcrt here), 22 are oracle cells that follow from them, 4 are the new multiline_triple_string_under_indented rows, and 1 is mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals on F#: a timeout at 60 s in the review, exit 1 in 43.8 s here. A hand run gives exit 1 in 53 s: its residual starts /usr/bin/sync, which is POSIX-only, so it fails deterministically on Windows and runs near the 60 s fsi budget; a Known entry follows. All 530 C rows equal my same-toolchain run 084525. F# native rows (976, incl. 330 that agree with C) are now in the baseline.")
inl fsharp_macro_mutation () = Task ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", "samples/while_loop and while_control fail in dotnet fsi (FS0027 value is not mutable): they assign dyn locals through a BackendSwitch macro and F# binds locals with let; known rows now, a portable assignment primitive later", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,fsharp", "current", "current")
inl note_of_fsharp_macro_mutation_1 () = Note ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: found by the first F# native run (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER): while_loop and while_control set dyn locals through an F# macro arm (!a <- !b) while F# codegen binds them with let (FS0027). Options: let mutable for dyn locals (every F# residual changes), known rows, or a portable assignment primitive (recommended long-term).")
inl note_of_fsharp_macro_mutation_2 () = Note ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", 2u32, "2026-10-18T13:09Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:09 main-thread design for the portable primitive: an !!!!Assign(target, value) op checked like Conv (target must be a dyn local of the value's type), lowered per backend as each already writes assignment (C/Rust/Zig/Kotlin/Swift/TS/Python/Lua a = b, Delphi a := b, Lean v := b in its let mut block, Gleam/Bend rejected as they have no mutation), and on F# the codegen marks every assigned local let mutable (one prepass over the residual collecting Assign targets), so only programs that assign change F# text. The two samples then replace their BackendSwitch set' with !!!!Assign and their F# rows stop failing in fsi (FS0027); until then they are the 2 expected F# build-fails of the native tier (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER).")
inl note_of_fsharp_macro_mutation_3 () = Note ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:23Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:23 main-thread: F# side fixed in the tree, unbuilt. Shared CodegenFsharp gets macro_assigned_binds/_op, a walker that collects every variable a macro assigns (a CMTerm followed by text starting with <-), descending into While, Do, Indent, If, UnionUnbox and IntSwitch bodies. with_macro_assigned sets it per emitted body (methods, closures, main; saved and restored, because nested printers run inside an outer body), and op's let emission prints let mutable v<n> : t for a single-variable let in that set. Only programs whose macros assign change F# text. F# closures take captures as arguments, so no mutable is captured (FS0407). Hand check, mutable applied to the committed .fsx: while_loop F# exit 0 = C exit 0 (was FS0027). while_control gets past FS0027 but then hits FS0039 (continue is not defined). Its break'/continue' are raw break/continue macros, and F# has no such statements, so that row needs a Known entry (F# has no break/continue statements), added after today's accept because matrix.psd1 is frozen until then. The longer-term !!!!Assign primitive from note 2 stays the cleaner design; the macro-target walker covers today's samples.")
inl note_of_fsharp_macro_mutation_4 () = Note ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", 4u32, "2026-10-18T17:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:55 main-thread: verified on the Debug candidate. while_loop F# emits 2 let mutable and exits 0 under fsi (was FS0027). while_control emits 2 let mutable and stops only at FS0039 (no continue in F#), now a Known matrix entry.")
inl note_of_fsharp_macro_mutation_5 () = Note ("FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:14 helper-lane lap 1: DONE with evidence: the macro-target walker (macro_assigned_binds) is committed (2fda5e52); samples/while_loop/main.fsx carries 2 let mutable; the accepted oracle (baseline EXPECTED.tsv, 2026-10-19 00:28) has samples/while_loop Fsharp ok, native ran, exit 0 (was FS0027) and samples/while_control Fsharp build-fail covered by its matrix.psd1 Known entry (F# has no break or continue statements). The title's later part, the portable !!!!Assign primitive, moved to its own item SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE (P3) so this one can close.")
inl samples_stale_outputs () = Task ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", "Stale tracked sample outputs: 44 main.rs/main.pas of the C-shim samples (abi_external_*, dynamic_array_*, native_managed_array_scc*_tail_recursion) have had no Rust or Delphi row since 09-28, plus oracle rows of deleted samples that -Accept never drops (accept-prune.py staged); deleting tracked files is the user's call", TaskKind, P3, D1, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,samples,cleanup", "current", "current")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_1 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: found while counting Rust tag impls: the 4 main.rs files date from 10-07 and their samples have no Rust row in matrix.psd1, so nothing regenerates them.")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_2 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:48 helper-lane lap 1: verified the 4 stale main.rs are still tracked with no Rust row (dynamic_array_growth_union_managed/_nested, dynamic_array_resize_union_managed, dynamic_array_string_union; deletion stays the user's call). Found a second, larger class and its cause: the accepted oracle keeps 19 rows of samples whose folders no longer exist (native_layout_* x16, native_fptr_basic, native_fptr_reuse, native_closure_managed_branch), because test.ps1's -Accept (lines 1065-1070) drops only rows whose sample is still scheduled with a different backend; a sample that vanished is never scheduled, so its rows are kept forever. Fix prepared, not applied (test.ps1 is the main lane's accept path): lab/SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS/accept-prune.py (3 anchors, dry-applied to a copy, parses, +6/-3) also drops rows whose id is unscheduled when the run is a full -Suite all without -Filter, and names them in the dropped list. Also removed the empty untracked dir samples/contract_fsi_adversarial (created 09-26, stem referenced nowhere).")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_3 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:33 helper-lane lap 2: generalized the census (every samples/**/main.<backend ext> checked against the accepted oracle's id|backend rows): 48 outputs have no oracle row, not 4. 44 are Rust and Delphi outputs of the C-shim samples - abi_external_* (Rust and Delphi), dynamic_array_{geometric_growth, growth_union_managed, growth_union_nested, reserve_zero, resize_alias, resize_nested, resize_union_managed, string_resize, string_union, tuple_managed}, native_managed_array_scc(3)_tail_recursion - last written by dc991bb9 on 09-28, i.e. three weeks without a row; the other 4 are mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned main.c, native_closure_capture main.fsx, native_tail_recursion main.futil and recur_sum main.wgsl. The title's 4 main.rs are among them (the tag-impl count found only the ones with union impls). Deletion stays the user's call; the list is reproducible with the same oracle join.")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_4 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:16 helper-lane lap 3: retitled to the real class and prepared the user's deletion list: lab/SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS/delete-44.txt holds the 44 tracked main.rs/main.pas (Rust and Delphi outputs of samples whose matrix entry lists only C, so no run ever rewrites or checks them), recomputed now against the accepted oracle (baseline EXPECTED.tsv of 08:55). The other 4 outputs without an oracle row stay OFF the list after a stem check: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned main.c is written and read by that family's bench.ps1, and native_closure_capture main.fsx, native_tail_recursion main.futil and recur_sum main.wgsl belong to samples the matrix still lists for other backends. For the user (one command, from the spiral root, after the core-switch accept since the switch rewrites samples): Get-Content lab/SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS/delete-44.txt | ForEach-Object { git rm -q -- $_ }.")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_5 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_6 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T21:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:39 helper-lane lap 6: half verified done. The accept-prune edit is in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (lines 1106-1112; accept-prune.py's anchors are gone because it is applied). The accepted baseline (spiral-bin/baseline/EXPECTED.tsv, written 17:19 by the pack 9 accept) has no row left for the 19 deleted samples. Only 3 native_layout ids remain, and all 3 folders exist. Still open, and the user's call because it deletes tracked files: lab/SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS/delete-44.txt, all 44 entries still tracked today (main.rs/main.pas of the C-shim samples, no Rust or Delphi row).")
inl date_time_portable () = Task ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", "Finish portable date_time guids: the timestamp pair is portable since 2026-10-17; date_time_guid_from_date_time and date_time_from_guid still use backend chains; the drafted civil_of_days and guid_prefix_of_micros (6 backends pass) replace them once ticks vs micros for the 7th fraction digit is decided", TaskKind, P2, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "lib,portable,zig", "current", "current")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_1 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: timestamp_guid_from_timestamp and timestamp_from_guid are portable in the tree (tests on 6 backends). Drafts for the rest in job tmp/night/civil/civil.spi (civil_of_days, guid_prefix_of_micros; 36 + 30 asserts on fsharp/rust/zig/python/gleam/lua). Decision needed: keep 100 ns ticks (F# today) or micros everywhere (then date_time_guid_from_date_time_3 expects ...9999-0).")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_2 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:12 main-thread: reviewed before applying the drafts. Recommendation on the open question: micros everywhere (timestamp is already micros, Lua to_timestamp is seconds * 1e6, chrono/Python/Erlang are micro or finer; only F#'s 7th fraction digit becomes 0). Not applied yet because it buys no coverage: the date_time_guid tests run only on fsharp and rust -d chrono, as max_value, unix_epoch, specify_date_kind and add_days exist only on the .NET, chrono, Python, JS and Erlang arms (Lua's are stubs, get_year returns 0, and Zig has none). The real blocker for Zig, now the default backend, is a portable date_time: i64 micros since the Unix epoch, now/utc_now from the platform clock, and calendar fields and format in plain Spiral through civil_of_days (draft passes on 6 backends). With that, the guid functions become portable for free.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_3 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T14:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:07 main-thread: a first Zig date_time draft (date_time_switch Zig : i64 micros UTC, arms for get_year, format, format_iso8601, min/max_value, unix_epoch, to_calendar_utc, specify_date_kind, to_universal_time, to_timestamp, add_days, now, utc_now, civil helpers) failed its first Cells run: lib programs that reach date_time stop with 'TracedError: Expected a function, closure, record or a layout type possibly inside a nominal. Got: backend_switch {Gleam : ...; Lua : i64; Fsharp : System.DateTime; ...}', i.e. one of the new arms applies or indexes a date_time value during validation on other backends (suspects: convert.unbox on the backend_switch nominal outside Lua's own arm, or the format arm's argument order). Reverted at 10:06 to HEAD (the TODO tag stays); the draft is in the session scratchpad (zig-date-time-patch.ps1 and its test). Next try: one arm at a time with a compile probe per arm.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_4 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:16 helper-lane lap 1: root cause of the 10-18 failure found by reading lib: date_time is nominal date_time = macro type backend_switch of date_time_switch, so convert.unbox on it inspects that record type (hence Expected a function, closure, record or a layout type ... Got: backend_switch {Gleam ...; Lua : i64; ...}). The Lua arms, also i64, never unbox: they read with a macro ($'!date_time * 1000000' : i64, line 577) and build with a macro ($'math.floor(!micros / 1000000)' : date_time, line 289). The lab draft (lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/zig-date-time-patch.ps1) now bridges the same way through two helpers, zig_micros_of = $'!date_time' : i64 and zig_date_time_of = $'!micros' : date_time, and no arm calls convert.unbox. Open risk: std.time.microTimestamp may be gone in Zig 0.17's std.Io rework (now/utc_now arms only). HEAVY QUEUE: C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab\\DATE-TIME-PORTABLE\\run.ps1 (applies the patch to lib date_time.spi, runs its Cells notebook incl. the new zig test, restores lib on failure; ~5-10 min; patches the shared lib while it runs).")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_5 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:07Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:07 helper-lane lap 2: settled the open risk: std.time.microTimestamp is gone in both installed Zigs (scoop zig 0.17 and zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146: lib/std/time.zig is now only the ns_per_* constants and epoch; the clock moved to std.Io, which needs an Io instance the Spiral Zig prelude does not have), and the compiler has no Zig clock either (MonotonicDelayMs has no Zig arm). The lab patch now builds now/utc_now on a zig_micros_now helper: a block-form global fn spiralMicrosNow() that calls std.os.windows.ntdll.RtlGetSystemTimePrecise (100 ns since 1601, minus 116444736000000000, divided by 10) on Windows and std.os.linux.clock_gettime(.REALTIME) elsewhere - both present in zig 0.17's std, no libc - with the Zig arm reading it through $'spiralMicrosNow()' : i64; other backends' validation copies of the global are dropped by UnusedGlobals.prune. Same heavy-queue row (lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/run.ps1) now tests the full arm set.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_6 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 main heavy queue ran lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/run.ps1 (10:14-10:21): it failed and restored lib/spiral/date_time.spi (git diff clean). Cause: error[EJP0040] partial evaluation nested more than 1000 dynamic if branches at the Zig format arm (main.spi:235: zig_micros_of |> zig_civil_of_micros |> zig_format_from (...) 0i32), so zig_format_from is a recursive inl that unrolls over the format string; make it a let rec (join point) like lib's other per-character loops. The gate also reported fsharp failed targets on date_time_guid_from_date_time and _2 (check whether those fail on the unpatched tree too). Requeue after the fix.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_7 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:24 helper-lane lap 3: applied main's 10:22 diagnosis to the lab patch: zig_format_from is now a let rec (join point) and its inner run loop a let rec run (j : i32) : i32, so neither unrolls over the format string (the EJP0040 at the Zig format arm). From the 10:14 log: 27 passed, 6 failed - the 5 guid tests on fsharp hit EJP0040 at main.spi:847 (sm'.range in date_time_guid_from_date_time) and the zig epoch test. Whether the fsharp guid failures predate the patch is unknown, so run.ps1 now takes -Baseline: it runs the unpatched notebook first and prints both failed_targets maps (baseline vs patched) before restoring lib on failure. Requeued as run.ps1 -Baseline (~12 min, patches the shared lib while it runs; not during the 11:40 core-switch freeze).")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_8 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T18:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:36 helper-lane: triaged main's queue-3b run (run.ps1 -Baseline, 14:10-14:27, switched core): the baseline notebook is fully green now (32 passed, 0 failed, so the 5 fsharp guid EJP0040 failures of 10:14 were pre-existing and are gone after the core switch), and the patched notebook fails only zig_date_time_is_micros_since_the_unix_epoch on zig: the generated main.zig assigns an i64 to an i32 (main.zig:1452 v187 = v180: expected type i32, found i64), i.e. the get_year arm's convert.convert from i64 to i32 emitted a plain assignment on Zig. The let rec format loops compiled (no EJP0040 any more). Fix applied to lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/zig-date-time-patch.ps1: the get_year arm narrows with conv (a TyConv, which codegenZig lowers with an explicit cast) instead of convert.convert. Rerun run.ps1 alone when the gate is free (slot 1 is busy until about 15:20).")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_9 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T21:04Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:04 helper-lane: the 16:55 rerun is invalid: lib/spiral/date_time.spi already held the Zig patch when it started (left in the tree after the 14:10 run), so the script patched it a second time and every test failed (passed 0). Restored the file to HEAD plus an unrelated 3-line TODO reflow (now the only diff), and hardened the lab scripts: zig-date-time-patch.ps1 refuses a file that already has the zig_civil section, and run.ps1 checks that its restore reproduced the original bytes (prints RESTORE FAILED otherwise). The get_year conv fix is still the next thing to verify, in a clean rerun.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_10 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T21:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:21 helper-lane: clean rerun (lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/patched-20261019-1711.log, accepted pack 8b compiler): date_time.spi with the Zig arms passes 33 of 33, failed_targets empty, including zig_date_time_is_micros_since_the_unix_epoch (epoch format, min/max years, add_days both ways, the ffffff fraction, format_iso8601) - the get_year conv fix worked. run.ps1 keeps the patch on a pass, so lib/spiral/date_time.spi now has a Zig date_time (i64 micros since the epoch, now/utc_now from RtlGetSystemTimePrecise or clock_gettime, civil fields and format in plain Spiral), uncommitted (+83/-2 incl. the earlier TODO reflow). Still open for the title: switching date_time_guid_from_date_time / date_time_from_guid to civil_of_days and guid_prefix_of_micros, which waits on the ticks-vs-micros decision for the 7th fraction digit.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_11 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T23:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:01 helper-lane lap 7: the Zig guid writer is in lib. lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/zig-guid-patch.ps1, run through guid-run.ps1 (guid-20261019-1848.log), adds a Zig arm to date_time_guid_from_date_time and the test zig_date_time_guid_writes_seven_contiguous_fraction_digits. The arm builds the prefix from zig_civil fields, keeps the 7-digit fraction field contiguous (2-4-1 split), writes the 7th digit as 0 since Zig holds micros, and uses time zone 00000 (UTC). The gate passes 34 of 34 and the change is kept. DECISION on ticks vs micros: keep the existing 7-digit layout so stored guids still parse, with micros backends writing a trailing 0. FOUND a latent F# bug: that arm formats yyyyMMdd-HHmm-ssff-ffff-f with .NET custom specifiers, where ff, ffff and f each repeat the leading fraction digits, so .1234567 is written 12-1234-1. Rust writes contiguous 12-3456-7, and date_time_from_guid reads 7 contiguous digits, so an F# guid with a non-zero fraction does not round-trip. The tests only use 0 and all-9 fractions, which look the same either way. Next: fix the F# format (ss + fffffff split by position) and add a non-zero-fraction test on fsharp and rust.")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_12 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T23:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:57 helper-lane: the F# guid fraction bug is fixed in lib. lab/DATE-TIME-PORTABLE/fraction-patch.ps1, run by fraction-run.ps1 (fraction-20261019-1942.log, which now applies inside the gate slot), changes the shared target arm of date_time_guid_from_date_time. Instead of the .NET specifiers ssff-ffff-f, it formats ss plus fffffff and splits the 7 digits by position (2-4-1). It also adds date_time_guid_fraction_digits_are_contiguous on fsharp and rust: date_time_milliseconds 1970 1 1 0 0 1 123 gives the prefix 19700101-0000-0112-3000-0 on both. Gate: 35 of 35, failed_targets empty, kept. The F# writer now agrees with Rust, Zig and date_time_from_guid. Left for the title: replacing the per-backend chains with the drafted civil_of_days and guid_prefix_of_micros, now that the 7-digit layout is decided.")
inl matrix_run_only_useful () = Task ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", "Run only what can change (user 2026-10-17: benchmark everything, run only what is useful): (1) every heavy/timed run in the ledger %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv + cost-report.ps1 (ledger, matrix runs, per backend, signal over full runs, Kino per-target ms); (2) matrix native memo: test.ps1 -ReuseNative replays native outcomes keyed by output bytes + toolchain/matrix signature (refused for -Accept/-Record); (3) deterministic compiler builds across cache folders (PathMap + content-addressed core staging in build.ps1) so a candidate DLL can be compared to the installed one by hash and Kino's memo survives a rebuild elsewhere; (4) Kino warm Gleam packages.", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "harness,kino,cost", "current", "current")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_1 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T12:45Z", "main-thread", "08:45 main-thread: native memo proven. Full -ReuseNative run on the blessed binaries replayed 5,892 rows (59.9 summed native minutes) with 0 to run: native tier 9.3 s vs 1,298 s, whole run about 3.5 min vs 25, and 0 of 6,906 rows differ from the bless single-flight-20261017-081502. Invalidation: changing SPIRAL_CXX (part of the signature) replays 0 of 15 rows, against 15 of 15 unchanged. Rule: review and iteration runs use -ReuseNative; a bless always runs every native row (refused otherwise), and the bless must reproduce the review.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_2 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:28Z", "main-thread", "09:28 main-thread: compiler builds are byte-reproducible across cache folders. Isolated det-f and the shared cache both give SpiralCompiler.dll 53878A40C43B7A94 and SpiralCompilerCore.dll CAC5AC4544446A7D on SDK rc.2. Four fixes got there: build.ps1 passes -p:PathMap=<cache>=<shared> for the pdb path; the core is staged content-addressed under <shared>/core-src/<mode>/<sha16>/; Directory.Build.props sets TargetFrameworkMonikerAssemblyAttributesPath to <shared>/obj-shared/<project>/ (F#'s deflated signature data embedded the obj path); and env.ps1 resolves the shared SDK for private caches (it silently fell back to C:\\Program Files\\dotnet rc.1). With this, a candidate DLL can be proven equal to the installed one by sha256, and Kino's content-keyed memo survives rebuilds elsewhere. SDK moved to 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105. The -ReuseNative review single-flight-20261017-092327 equals the bless on all 6,906 rows in about 4 min.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_3 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:29Z", "main-thread", "10:29 main-thread: tooling moved out of job tmp into spiral/lab/harness-run-only-useful/: the heavy gate with the run ledger (heavy.ps1; the job-tmp path is now a forwarder, the queue lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\heavy-queue, comments stripped, internal names made distinctive), compare-runs, rebuild-determinism, cross-folder-determinism, bindiff.py, fsharp-resources (inflates F#'s signature data to find embedded paths), kino-tests and kino-suite (now with --slowest). Known entries in tests/matrix.psd1 can be scoped by Oracle. Under -Oracle Zig the whole suite has DISAGREE 0, with Zig agreeing on 5,354 rows against 5,383 under C; the only divergent sample is dynamic_array_bounds_negative (checked vs unchecked index). The memo signature now hashes only test.ps1's native code, found through its AST.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_4 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T16:28Z", "main-thread", "12:28 main-thread: Kino runs now benchmark themselves, whoever calls them. apps/kino/spi/run_notebook.ps1 appends a 'notebook' row (label: notebook file and warm/mix route, wall seconds, outcome) and run_suite.ps1 a 'kino-suite' row to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv, both through scripts/heavy.ps1's Write-RunLedger; run_suite now also passes --slowest 25. Found while testing: a notebook path with an apostrophe (lib/spiral/sm'.spi) broke the command scriptblock, and the swallowed error dropped the row, so the text is now an escaped string literal and failures print a warning. Harness runs keep per-row times in runs/*/results.tsv and builds in results/builds.tsv. cost-report.ps1 covers ledger, harness and Kino per-target times.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_5 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T16:42Z", "main-thread", "12:42 main-thread: compile memo landed (test.ps1 -ReuseCompile), the compile-side twin of -ReuseNative. Key: sha256 of every compiler DLL (sound now that builds are reproducible), every .spi/.spir/.spiproj under the bundle, lib/ and the core package dir (271 samples reach packages through packageDir ../..), SPIRAL_* settings, mode, and the job's backend, input, output and timeout. ok/error compiles are stored with their output bytes. A replayed ok row restores the output when it differs and is tagged compile-reused; timeouts, crashes and emitted rows are never stored; -Bless/-Record refuse the flag. Proof: on the same compiler, run 1 (fill) compiled in 226 s and run 2 replayed all 6,915 compiles in 2.3 s. The whole -Suite all -Native run took 73 s, and both equal single-flight-20261017-121042 (0 cells). A SPIRAL_IF_NESTING_LIMIT change replays 0 of 1. Measured costs today: a full cold run with natives is about 30 min; a compiler-change review with -ReuseNative about 5 min; an unchanged-compiler rerun about 1 min. cost-report counts compile-reused rows apart.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_6 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:44Z", "main-thread", "13:44 main-thread: SDK incident and fix. After the rc.2 SDK move, the rebuilt compiler's runtimeconfig required the 11.0.0-rc.2 runtime, and eoie's regeneration (which runs the compiler through C:\\Program Files\\dotnet, rc.1 only) failed with 'You must install or update .NET'. A tree global.json pin vanished within minutes (cause unknown), so build.ps1 now chooses the newest installed SDK whose bundled runtime (sdk/<v>/Microsoft.NETCoreSdk.BundledVersions.props) the system dotnet also has, writes the global.json into <cache>/build-cwd/<sdk>/ and builds from there, with crossgen2 targeting the same runtime. It moves to rc.2 by itself once the system has it. MSBuild did not regenerate runtimeconfig.json on an SDK change (it isn't a target input), so the stale bin/*.runtimeconfig.json and obj/*.genruntimeconfig.cache had to be deleted once. Rebuilt Debug plus 3 modes; single-flight-20261017-133144 and hopacsota-20261017-133648 equal the grain 2a review on all 6,915 rows.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_7 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:00Z", "main-thread", "14:00 main-thread: fast-tier mining is incremental. -Suite fast used to re-mine every run history before its first compile (995 single-flight runs: 54 s). The fold state is now cached in <cache>/fast-tier-fold/<mode>-<policy>.json (2.6 MB) with a stamp per run (name, results.tsv size and mtime, fast-tier.tsv marker), and only new runs are mined; any changed earlier stamp means a full recompute. Proofs: the selection list (-ListPath) is byte-identical to the uncached baseline both on build (29 s) and on resume (10 s), and after appending a run the resumed fold (10 s) equals a full recompute (31 s). The remaining ~10 s is pwsh startup, statting 1,096 run folders and JSON load: the PWSH-FREE native-verb target. Also today: lib/spiral/crypto.spi lost get_file_hash' (it shelled out to pwsh for a sha256 that the native get_file_hash already computes); crypto.spi now has 4 of 4 tests passing.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_8 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (spiral-samples alpha705 + brzozowski alpha54, Jun-Sep 2026; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 6): a rule worth adding to the memo: if the generated residual hash equals the control's, a wall-time delta is lifecycle noise, not a regression. The exact-graph cache once returned a false fresh hit after a source edit until the key bound normalized path + source SHA (schema 3); check that the native memo signature covers every input the same way (it already covers SPIRAL_CXX). Cache hits are identity evidence only: performance claims need fresh runs with session teardown before each measured compile.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_9 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 9u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: rebuild-determinism promoted to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/rebuild-determinism.ps1 (now touches the content-addressed staged core so the rebuild is real; log in <cache>/runs); the one-off proofs (compile-memo-proof, cross-folder-determinism, fsharp-resources, bindiff.py, kino-tests) deleted, their results are in this item's notes and AGENTS.md; lab/harness-run-only-useful removed. The bounded runner lab/try.ps1 moved to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/try.ps1 (runs in <cache>/runs/try).")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_10 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 10u32, "2026-10-18T13:04Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:04 main-thread: parts (1)-(4) are in place (the warm Gleam slots also no longer run stale programs, CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE). Refinement found today: Get-NativeSignature hashes every file under tests/native-shims into every row's key, so the tibia lane's 2-line wgsl-run edit made review 1 rerun all 5,931 native rows (37 min instead of about 10). Drafted: a per-backend shim key (TypeScript run_main.mjs, Lua run_main.lua, Python run_main.py, Cpp cpp_native.py, Gleam gleam/, Kotlin kotlin-server/, OpenQasm openqasm-run/, Wgsl wgsl-run/, C and Ebpf the root headers, the other ten backends none, unknown backends all), applied after today's bless.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_11 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 11u32, "2026-10-18T14:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:08 main-thread: per-backend shim key applied to test.ps1 (Get-ShimKey, part of the native-code signature; a backend's native memo key now hashes only its own shims: TypeScript run_main.mjs, Lua run_main.lua, Python run_main.py, Cpp cpp_native.py, Gleam gleam/, Kotlin kotlin-server/, OpenQasm openqasm-run/, Wgsl wgsl-run/, C and Ebpf the root headers, ten backends none, unknown ones all). Tested on a copy: distinct keys per backend, Zig and Rust the empty-input hash. The next review reruns natives once (the native code changed), after that a shim edit reruns only its backend's rows.")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_12 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 12u32, "2026-10-19T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:26 lap 1 grain: measured gap: pack 5 changed only the Rust compile line in test.ps1 and the wgsl runner, yet native memo replayed 0 of 6,508 rows, because Get-NativeSignature hashes the whole Build-And-Run text (every backend's switch clause) plus every tool stamp into every row's key. Design: per-backend native key = that backend's switch clause in Build-And-Run + the shared remainder (clauses removed) + only the tool stamps that backend uses (Rustc for Rust, WgslRun for Wgsl, ...); today that would have rerun about 490 rows (Rust 438 + Wgsl 50) instead of 6,508 Next: implement Get-NativeBackendKey in a lab copy of test.ps1, prove distinct keys per backend and that editing the Rust clause changes only Rust's key")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_13 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:38 picked as this cycle's take-to-the-end task. Built in spiral/lab/MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL: Get-NativeSignature.ps1 (per-backend native key: the backend's own if-block or switch clause in Build-And-Run + the shared remainder with every backend block cut out + Invoke-Native, Get-CFlags, Get-ShimKey and the native tier block; tool stamps only for the tools that code references; '*' key = all blocks, for backends without their own), apply-backend-keys.ps1 (AST-located swap + Get-NativeMemoKey picks its backend key; patched file parses), prove-backend-keys.ps1 on copies of test.ps1: 19 backends get distinct keys; editing the Rust clause changes only Rust (and '*'); bumping WgslRun changes only Wgsl; editing shared Build-And-Run code changes all 20. Today's pack 5 would have rerun about 490 native rows instead of 6,508. Apply after the pack 5 accept ends (the running accept parses test.ps1); the first -Native run afterwards reruns every row once (new keys).")
inl note_of_matrix_run_only_useful_14 () = Note ("MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 14u32, "2026-10-19T13:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:51 DONE, proven end to end: per-backend native keys applied to test.ps1 (and documented in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md); review-keys round A (single-flight-20261019-085820, -Oracle Zig,C) reran all 6,508 native rows once on the new keys with 0 differences from the accepted baseline in compile, exit, stdout, native or residual (the 1,189 differing cells are all oracle-column relabels from Zig as first oracle); round B (single-flight-20261019-094419) replayed 6,508 of 6,508 native rows from the memo and ran only the 13 new F# rows that helper 2 added to matrix.psd1. From now on an edit to one backend's Build-And-Run block or one tool bump reruns only that backend.")
inl hopacsota_hopac_depth () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", "hopacsota uses Hopac's real API, not gpt5parallel's subset (user 2026-10-17). Measured: gpt5parallel's side of spiral_compiler.fs calls about 20 entry points (IVar.fill 165, Job.bind 70, Promise.Now 50, Ch.send 41, start 28; withNack/Latch/MVar/Proc/Scheduler 0) of Hopac 0.5.1's 424 documented members. Adopt where PLAN D1-D16 and gate G2 allow (choice stays left-biased and deterministic: no Alt.random/chooser/<~>): Scheduler.create per build + Scheduler.kill at build end (the fix-57 cross-build leak class becomes impossible); Alt.withNackJob/wrapAbortJob for D16.2 speculation losers; Latch (holding/within) for fork-join of a body's children instead of IVar counting; Job.conCollect / <*> for independent argument evaluation; MVar.modifyFun or Job.foreverServer/iterateServer for owner state instead of locks; Stream.mapPipelinedJob for bounded codegen-per-method pipelining (D16.4); Job.isolate/switchToWorker/onThreadPool for blocking file IO off workers; Job.tryFinallyJob/useIn for scoped cleanup; Scheduler startWithActions top-level handler feeding the typed Diagnostic (D13); Alt.paranoid in Debug builds to catch double commits; Promise.queue/Hopac.memo for demand-driven keys. Each adoption is a measured A/B against single-flight with the hopacsota gates.", Story, P1, D5, 30u32, Paused, "", "compiler,hopacsota,hopac", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:14Z", "main-thread", "09:14 main-thread fork: first measurement (fsi --optimize+, Hopac 0.5.1, 64 children x 2000-iteration work, 2000 rounds, machine loaded by parallel compiler builds; script job-tmp study/hopac-bench/bench.fsx): fork-join via IVar + Interlocked counting (gpt5parallel's pattern) 540 us, Latch 272 us, Job.conCollect 275 us, Promise.start+read 386 us -> Latch/conCollect ~2x. Scheduler.create (NumWorkers = cores, MaxStackSize 1.5 GB) + one fork-join + Scheduler.kill = 2.2 ms per build, so a scheduler per build (kills the fix-57 leak class) is free next to any compile. Alt.withNackJob as speculation loser: 71 us per race, nack delivered 2000/2000 (the loser must be the left alternative or it is never instantiated). Job.Scheduler.isolate vs thunk: inconclusive (warm-up artifact). Next: repeat on an idle machine, then the same A/B inside the hopacsota engine (P2).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:36Z", "main-thread", "09:36 main-thread fork: D3 fill-once key table, cost per lookup+tryFill (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/table.fsx; 8 concurrent jobs x 20,000 lookups over 4,096 keys, loaded machine): ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd of an IVar 447 ns, MVar.modifyFun over a Dictionary 931 ns, an owner server (Job.foreverServer + request/reply IVar) 1,221 ns. The Hopac-native forms add 0.5-0.8 us per lookup, negligible next to a join-point specialization (ms) unless a build does >100k table lookups. Decision input for P2: keep the table inside the engine module (G2 allowlists one structure there) and pick by measured lookups per build; the evaluator only sees fill/read either way (D15).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:54Z", "main-thread", "09:54 main-thread fork: D16.4 codegen-per-method shape (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/pipeline.fsx; 400 methods of uneven cost, 8 cores, loaded): sequential 87.2 ms, Job.conCollect 34.5 ms (2.5x) with results in the original order (conCollect returns by position, so D6's deterministic merge order comes for free); Stream.ofSeq |> Stream.mapPipelinedJob d |> Stream.foldFun did not finish within 240 s (killed) for d=2 in Hopac 0.5.1 - treat mapPipelinedJob as suspect until a minimal repro shows whether it is this usage or the library. For bounded parallelism use conCollect over fixed-size chunks or a Latch-limited pool instead.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:57Z", "main-thread", "09:57 main-thread fork: mapPipelinedJob minimal probe (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/pipelined-min.fsx, each probe raced against a 5 s timeout): mapPipelinedJob 2/1 and mapPipelinedFun 2 over 10 items all return 55 correctly (0-47 ms), so the library function works; the earlier non-finishing run is specific to that benchmark's shape (400 CPU-heavy Job.thunk bodies, list-consing fold) and stays unexplained - repeat it with a timeout race before ruling on mapPipelinedJob for codegen.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed, resume in main lane after reprioritization): every job-tmp study/hopac-bench path in earlier notes is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\hopac-bench (bench.fsx fork-join/scheduler/nack, table.fsx key table, pipeline.fsx + pipelined-min.fsx). Proven: Latch/conCollect 2x over IVar counting; Scheduler per build 2.2 ms; withNackJob reliable (loser left); D3 table ConcurrentDictionary 447 ns vs MVar 931 vs server 1,221; conCollect keeps order. Open: rerun all on an idle machine; pipeline.fsx mapPipelinedJob non-finish (raced 240 s) vs minimal OK; then A/B inside the hopacsota engine at P2. Run with: dotnet fsi --optimize+ <file> (refs Hopac 0.5.1 from ~/.nuget).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes the job-tmp and spiral-bin\\study paths in earlier notes): spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/ (fork-join.fsx, key-table.fsx, codegen-pipeline.fsx, pipelined-min.fsx; each starts with #r nuget: Hopac, 0.5.1 so any machine restores it; run dotnet fsi --optimize+ <file>). CORRECTION: rerunning pipelined-min.fsx from the repo, mapPipelinedJob 2 over 10 items timed out (None after 5 s) while degree 1 and mapPipelinedFun 2 returned 55; the earlier run of the same file returned 55 - an intermittent hang at degree >= 2 in Hopac 0.5.1, not this benchmark's shape. Do not use mapPipelinedJob for codegen; conCollect is the order-preserving form.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 7u32, "2026-10-17T14:38Z", "main-thread", "10:38 main-thread fork: mapPipelined hang RATE (spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/pipelined-rate.fsx, 30 trials each, 10 items, 2 s race): mapPipelinedJob degree 1 finished 30/30, degree 2 18/30, 4 17/30, 8 22/30; mapPipelinedFun 2 18/30; mapJob control 30/30. So Hopac 0.5.1's pipelined stream maps lose ~25-45% of runs at any degree > 1 (the stream never closes), independent of the mapper. Rule for hopacsota: no Stream.mapPipelined*; bounded parallel map = Job.conCollect over chunks or a Latch-limited pool; if the D14 HUD uses Stream.Src taps, verify them under the same race harness first. A minimal repro for the library is this file.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 8u32, "2026-10-17T14:58Z", "main-thread", "10:58 main-thread fork: ordered bounded maps measured as the mapPipelined replacement (spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/ordered-pool.fsx, 400 uneven methods, 30 raced trials each, machine loaded by a suite): unbounded Job.conCollect 39.6 ms; chunked conCollect d=2/4/8 100/107/74 ms; token pool (Ch of d tokens, Promise per item, read in order) 113/134/135 ms; ALL correct, finished 30/30 and in sequential order. Reading: bounding costs 2-3x here (chunk barriers wait for their slowest member; the token pool pays a channel round trip per item), so for D16.4 use unbounded conCollect and bound only when memory per item demands it (then chunking, sized so chunks are long relative to the slowest item). The Hopac primitives that hang are only the Stream.mapPipelined family.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused until main-lane reprioritization): lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth now holds fork-join, key-table, codegen-pipeline, pipelined-min, pipelined-rate, ordered-pool (.fsx, #r nuget Hopac 0.5.1). Settled: Latch/conCollect over IVar counting (2x); Scheduler per build (2.2 ms); withNackJob for speculation; Stream.mapPipelined* hang 25-45% at degree > 1 - banned; unbounded conCollect is the ordered parallel map (bounded forms cost 2-3x). Open: idle-machine reruns; A/B inside the hopacsota engine at P2.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 10u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the six Hopac probes (.fsx, nuget Hopac 0.5.1, portable) moved to apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/hopac-probes/ next to PLAN.md, which cites their numbers and asks for an idle-machine rerun before Gate 3.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:29 lap 1 grain: the six probes sit in apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/hopac-probes (codegen-pipeline, fork-join, key-table, ordered-pool, pipelined-min, pipelined-rate .fsx); PLAN.md lines 887-900 cite their numbers as measured under load and ask for an idle-machine rerun before Gate 3; queued that rerun Next: HEAVY QUEUE (idle machine only): dotnet fsi each probe 3x, compare with the PLAN numbers")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 lap 2 grain: the idle-machine rerun cannot happen during the day (four lanes busy, heavy queue plus the core switch until about 13:30); scheduled for the evening quiet window, in the heavy queue as idle-only Next: -")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 13u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: still idle-only; tonight the machine is quiet only after pack 8b accepts and the toolchain pack runs, and only if no lane holds a heavy slot Next: run at 21:xx if both heavy slots are free for 30 minutes, else defer to the next night")
inl hopacsota_lf_scheduling () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", "Lingua Franca ideas for hopacsota, where they do not conflict with Hopac (user 2026-10-17, like the Bend study). LF (lf-lang/lingua-franca core/.../generator/ReactionInstanceGraph.java, graph/PrecedenceGraph.java; lf-lang/reactor-c core/threaded/scheduler_*.c) gets deterministic multicore execution from a static precedence graph: reactions get levels (longest upstream path), getBreadth = max reactions per level bounds useful workers, causality cycles are found by SCC and reported with the cycle, deadlines propagate upstream to give an EDF priority, chains of single-dominated reactions run inline without sync, and the adaptive scheduler measures per-level cost each tag to choose the worker count for the next. For hopacsota: (1) level/breadth of the D3 key graph as the frontier report and the worker-count cap (P2/P3); (2) critical-path-first worklist order (longest remaining chain), still merged in single-flight order by D6 so output is unchanged; (3) inline-run single-dominated chains below the P3 granularity cutoff; (4) a cycle report for a key graph deadlock in rustc style (like EJP0040), from the D4 wait-for graph; (5) measured per-level cost feeding the cutoff; (6) LF tracepoints with Chrome-trace export as the D13/P4 trace format.", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,hopacsota,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:15Z", "main-thread", "09:15 main-thread fork: first LF-style measurement on real graphs (job-tmp study/lf-levels/levels.py: method call graph of committed C residuals, runtime helpers excluded, Tarjan SCC then longest-path levels on the condensation; the output method graph is a proxy for the evaluation key graph, since each method is one join-point specialization). mega_brzozowski_derivatives: 15-39 methods, 7-9 levels, breadth 4-17, average parallelism (SCCs/levels) 1.7-5.0, 9-17 leaf methods, 11-22 self-recursive, 0 multi-node cycles; rust_static_closure_chain 81 independent closures in 1 level (breadth 81); mega_omniledger_erp_kernel 0 methods: all 15,757 lines are inlined into main, so method-level forking finds nothing there and only D16.2 speculation / inline-body splitting can shorten it. So LF's breadth bound says ~2-5 workers per level are useful on the brzozowski family, more on closure-heavy code. Next: the same metrics on hopacsota's own key graph (dfs/bfs worklist trace) instead of the output proxy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:36Z", "main-thread", "09:36 main-thread fork: corpus distribution (job-tmp study/lf-levels/corpus.py over every samples/**/main.c; 214 residuals with >= 2 methods): median 2 methods, 2 levels, breadth 2, average parallelism 2.0; buckets <=1: 64, 1-2: 99, 2-4: 41, >4: 10; deepest are the mega_brzozowski family (7-9 levels); widest rust_static_closure_chain (81 independent closures, 1 level) and the dynamic_array_* samples (7-13 wide); 5 residuals have multi-method cycles (largest 3 methods: mutual recursion). Reading: method-level forking alone gives ~2x on a typical sample and up to 4-5x on the mega family, so the hopacsota win on large programs has to come from D16.1 typed bodies at finer grain (closures, type join points) and D16.4 parse/typecheck/codegen pipelining - consistent with PLAN D16; a breadth-based worker cap (LF getBreadth) would keep most samples at 2 workers and avoid oversubscription. Caveat: residual methods are post-inlining, so the evaluation-time key graph is wider than this proxy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: weighted critical path (job-tmp study/lf-levels/critical.py; work = body lines of each residual method, SCC-condensed call DAG, bound = total work / heaviest path = the most any scheduler can gain at method grain): mega_brzozowski runtime_bench 4,006 lines over 39 methods, critical 825 -> bound 4.86; inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 4.5; indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 4.28; runtime_native 3.58; the native_cube_* frames ~2.8; across 103 residuals with >= 3 methods the median bound is 1.74 (max 22.7, the closure chain). So critical-path-first ordering matters exactly on the mega family: with 8 workers and a ~4.5x bound, a scheduler that starts the 825-line chain first finishes near the bound, while one that starts leaves first can lose most of it. This is the number to beat on hopacsota's real key graph (next: the same metric from a dfs worklist trace with measured per-key time instead of residual lines).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 4u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/lf-levels is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\lf-levels (levels.py per-file, corpus.py distribution, critical.py weighted bound; python -I <script> apps/compiler/tmp/samples). Proven on residual C call graphs: median 2 levels/breadth 2; mega_brzozowski 7-9 levels, breadth 4-17, weighted bound 3.6-4.9x; omniledger fully inlined (0 methods). Open: the same metrics on hopacsota's real key graph from a dfs worklist trace with measured per-key time (needs a trace hook in the hopacsota layer), then critical-path-first ordering A/B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/ (levels.py, corpus.py, critical.py; python -I lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/corpus.py apps/compiler/tmp/samples).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:36Z", "main-thread", "10:36 main-thread fork: policy simulation (spiral/lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/schedule.py: list scheduling on the SCC-condensed residual call DAG, a method runs once its callees are done, cost = body lines; python -I schedule.py apps/compiler/tmp/samples <P>). Critical-path-first (HLFET upward rank) reaches the lower bound max(critical, work/P) within 0-4% on every graph at P=2/4/8. Leaves-first is the bad case: at P=4 runtime_bench 1,338 vs 1,006 (33% slower), inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 1,128 vs 872 (29%), indexed_dfa 1,047 vs 851 (23%); random order sits between (up to 24% slower). At P=8 workers are abundant and order mostly stops mattering (leaves-first still 13-20% slower on 3 graphs). Decision input for P2/P3: order the hopacsota worklist by upward rank (known after one build, or estimated by key depth), keep the D6 merge in single-flight order so output is unchanged; the gain is largest when workers < breadth, i.e. under the suite's parallel load.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: the first build has no weights, so schedule.py now also tries a structural proxy: hop-rank = number of methods on the longest path to the root (graph shape only), ties broken by the node's own size. At P=2 it is within 0-5% of weighted critical-first everywhere; at P=4 within 0-6% on 4 of 6 graphs (runtime_bench 1,012 vs 1,006, cube equal) and 6-9% behind on the other 3 dfa graphs (906 vs 851, 918 vs 872, 786 vs 723) - still well ahead of leaves-first (+23-33%) and random. Decision input: first build orders by hop-rank (available as soon as the key graph is discovered), later builds by the weighted upward rank measured in the previous build (the D16.3 hint store already keeps per-key data).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 8u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: correction to the hop-rank P=4 numbers: equal or within 1% of weighted critical-first on 3 of 6 graphs (bench_interned, runtime_bench 1,012 vs 1,006, cube) and 5-9% behind on the other 3 (inventory 918 vs 872 = 5.3%, indexed_dfa 906 vs 851 = 6.5%, runtime_native 786 vs 723 = 8.7%).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling has levels.py, corpus.py, critical.py, schedule.py. Settled: critical-path-first reaches the bound within 0-4%; leaves-first up to 33% slower at P=4; hop-rank (structure only) is the first-build proxy. Open: the same on hopacsota's real key graph (needs a worklist trace hook), then the ordering A/B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 10u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the four LF simulation .py files deleted; their results (worklist order: longest remaining chain first within 4 percent) are absorbed in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md 2026-10-17. A rerun, if wanted, should be written in Spiral.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:29 lap 1 grain: closed: the study's results live in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md (2026-10-17 absorbed measurements, line 901: longest-remaining-chain first within 4 percent of optimum, leaves first up to 33 percent slower, level breadth caps useful workers; measured breadth per sample family); the only open part, the same ordering on hopacsota's real key graph, needs a worklist trace hook and is Phase 3 work (HOPACSOTA-P3), not this study Next: -")
inl lib_reactors () = Task ("LIB-REACTORS", "A reactor library in Spiral after Lingua Franca's model (user 2026-10-17): reactors with typed input/output ports, state, timers and logical/physical actions, reactions with declared triggers/sources/effects, logical time tags (time, microstep) and after-delays, banks/multiports, modal reactors, deterministic execution by precedence levels, deadlines; modeled GADT-first (port direction and payload type in the index, existential reactor packing for heterogeneous graphs, HKT over the scheduler) so the graph is checked at compile time and partial evaluation flattens it to straight-line code per backend (Zig/Rust/C/Lean). Steal LF's semantics and test corpus shapes (test/C/src: concurrent, generics, multiport, modal_models; examples-lingua-franca patterns Chain/Loop/FullyConnected/ForkJoin), not its runtimes. Stretch: LF's @property MTL specs (verifier/ CToUclidVisitor + statespace explorer) as properties proved on the Lean backend. Generic library work; consumers decide their own use.", Feature, P2, D6, 60u32, Active, "", "lib,spiral,prior-art,lean", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_1 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:18Z", "main-thread", "09:18 main-thread fork: first prototype compiles and runs (scratch, job-tmp study/reactors/main.spi, 60 lines; not in lib/ yet): union signal t = Present | Absent (LF's absent port), type reactor s i o = {init : s; react : s -> i -> s * o}, timer/scale/after (one-tag logical delay)/sink reactors, chain composes at inl level so the precedence order is static, run is one let rec over logical time. Partial evaluation erases the records and closures: the whole timer->scale->after->sink graph becomes one C loop0(count, held signal, sum, seen) - the fused straight-line form the item asks for. 20 ticks, timer every 4: C exit 305, Lua 305, Zig 49 (= 305 mod 256: Zig exits u8; keep test results < 256). Spiral facts learned: record field access needs a named record type (no row polymorphism on inl params); inl patterns take one tuple level (inl a, b = ...), not nested parens; conversion is !!!!Conv(i32, x); untyped integer literals are i32. Next: ports as GADT indices (direction + payload in the type), banks/multiports, microsteps, an LF pattern test (Chain/Loop/ForkJoin) as Spiral test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_2 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:35Z", "main-thread", "09:35 main-thread fork: LF ForkJoin pattern works (job-tmp study/reactors/forkjoin.spi): both a b (same input to two reactors, paired outputs) builds a compile-time bank of 4 scale reactors, join4 merges with LF's absent semantics (Present+Absent = the present value), all chained after a timer and into a sink. 12 ticks, timer every 3 (values 1..4), bank x1..x4, join sum -> sink 100 + 4 seen = exit 104 as computed by hand. Still one loop0 with the bank unrolled inline (v*2, v*3, v*4 statements): the static graph is fused as intended. Next: a banks combinator over n (inl rec over a compile-time count instead of nested both), microstep tags for zero-delay cycles, and multiports typed by a GADT index.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_3 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:49Z", "main-thread", "09:49 main-thread fork: banks of a compile-time size n (LF new[n]): a real-dialect helper (job-tmp study/reactors/bank_real.spir: open real_core; inl rec bank n mk = if n = 1i32 then mk 1i32 else both (mk n) (bank (n - 1i32) mk), plus join_signals over the nested output pairs) builds the nested both tree, but the typed .spi side cannot receive it: annotating the result as reactor _ (signal i64) _ hits ETY-ValueRestriction (holes are not allowed for a non-value), and the state type differs per n. Design consequence for the lib: (a) composition (chain/both/bank/run) lives in the real dialect and only the program's edge types (inputs, outputs, final result) are typed .spi; or (b) banks of identical members keep their states in an array (one loop over members, parallel-friendly); or (c) a type-level natural index, blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG. Plan: (a) for heterogeneous graphs, (b) for LF multiports/banks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_4 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:56Z", "main-thread", "09:56 main-thread fork: option (b) works - homogeneous bank with array state (job-tmp study/reactors/arraybank.spi): member states in one array_base i64 created once (!!!!ArrayCreate), each tag a let rec over members reads/updates its slot (!!!!ArrayIndex / !!!!ArrayIndexSet), member count is a RUNTIME value (inl ~members = 4i32) and the types stay fixed. 4 members x 5 ticks (member i adds input*(i+1)): C exit 94 = 350 mod 256, matching an independent Python computation. This is the LF bank/multiport form, and its per-member loop is the natural place for parallel execution later (members of one level are independent). Heterogeneous graphs keep the inl-composed form (fused loop0), banks of identical members use this array form.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_5 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/reactors is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\reactors (main.spi chain -> 305, forkjoin.spi -> 104, arraybank.spi -> 94, bank_real.spir sketch; compile any with ..\\convo-spiral\\try.ps1 -Name X -Source file -Root <dir>, it kills after 60 s and uses the shared single-flight DLL). Proven: inl-composed reactors fuse into one loop; ForkJoin with absent semantics; array-state banks with a runtime member count. Decided: heterogeneous graphs = inl composition, identical-member banks = array state (n-indexed typed banks hit the value restriction). Open: microsteps/zero-delay cycles, GADT-typed ports, LF pattern tests as Spiral test blocks, then move into lib/spiral via a scratch copy (never half-edited lib files).")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_6 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/lib-reactors/ - chain.spi (305), forkjoin.spi (104), array-bank.spi (94), bank-value-restriction.spi + bank_real.spir (the ValueRestriction evidence); all reverified 2026-10-17 from the repo with pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source <file> [-Modules <x_real.spir>] [-Backend C|Zig|Lua|Rust] (60 s kill, shared single-flight DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs).")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_7 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T14:43Z", "main-thread", "10:43 main-thread fork: (1) port typing for free: lab/lib-reactors/negative-port-type-mismatch.spi wires a signal f64 reactor after a signal i64 one and fails at compile time (ETY-TermError: react : f64 -> signal f64 ... vs signal i64), i.e. LF's connection type check is just chain's type; keep it as a negative test block when this moves to lib. (2) microsteps (lab/lib-reactors/microsteps.spi): tag = {time; micro}, a reaction that emits Present re-schedules itself at the same time and micro+1 (LF zero-delay logical action), Absent advances logical time, a cap bounds zero-delay cycles; countdown bursts 3,2,1 at t=0 and t=5 -> sum 12 over 6 microsteps, C exit 126 = Python check. Next: microsteps across a chained graph (each reactor sees the same tag), deadlines, then the lib move with test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_8 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T14:54Z", "main-thread", "10:54 main-thread fork: microsteps through a chained graph (lab/lib-reactors/microsteps-chain.spi): reactor s i o now takes the tag as an argument (react : s -> tag -> i -> s * signal o) and chain passes the SAME tag to every member, so a downstream reactor (stamp) sees each microstep's exact {time; micro}; countdown -> stamp (time*1000 + micro*10 + value) -> total, 10 ticks, bursts at t=0 and 5: total 15,072 over 6 microsteps, exit 230 = Python model. This is LF's superdense time with a fused chain (still one loop). Next: deadlines (a reaction whose physical lag exceeds its deadline runs a handler) and the lib move with test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_9 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:08Z", "main-thread", "11:08 main-thread fork: deadlines (lab/lib-reactors/deadlines.spi): clock = {logical; physical}, with_deadline d body on_violation wraps any reactor and runs the handler when physical - logical > d (LF deadline semantics); physical time simulated by a deterministic lag sequence (lag 9 every 4th tick, else t mod 3) so the result is checkable: 12 ticks, deadline 5 -> 3 violations handled, 9 on-time samples summing 450, exit 3450 = Python model. Prototype set now: chain (fused loop), forkjoin, array banks, port typing (negative test), microsteps alone and through a chain, deadlines. Next: move into lib/spiral as reactors.spi with these as test blocks via a scratch lib copy, then a real physical clock on the native backends.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_10 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 10u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/lib-reactors has chain, forkjoin, array-bank, microsteps, microsteps-chain, deadlines (+ negative-port-type-mismatch, bank-value-restriction evidence); every lab experiment re-verified 11:09 from the repo (25 of 26 pass; the 26th, gadt repro, now compiles because the bug is fixed). Open: lib move (reactors.spi with test blocks via a scratch lib copy), real physical clock on native backends.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_11 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 11u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): lib/spiral/reactors.spi (module registered after async) unifies the prototypes into one model: signal, tag {time; micro}, reactor s i o = {init; react : s -> tag -> i -> s * o}, chain, both, with_deadline (input = physical lag), run_ticks, run_micro (fold over present outputs, microstep cap). Six test blocks on C and Rust, 6/6 green through cells: chain_delays_one_tick (45, 5; the unified timer emits its ordinal), both_fans_out_and_joins (100, 4), microsteps_run_until_absent (12, 6), microsteps_share_the_tag_through_a_chain (15072, 6), deadlines_run_the_violation_handler (450, 3), array_bank_keeps_member_state (350, via core a arrays, no raw ops). Prototype oracle rerun first (exits 305/104/94/126/230/3450 matched). The port-typing negative became harness sample samples/reactors_negative_port_type (|core-, ExpectedErrors Contains the Expected: ... signal i64 record; 4 backends error as intended, new rows until the next bless). bank-value-restriction is a language limitation, not a bug: a bank built in real mode cannot be bound in top-down code (ETY-ValueRestriction on the unannotated reactor record); banks stay explicit (both) or runtime (array). lab/lib-reactors deleted. Open: modal reactors, real physical clock on native backends, GADT port direction in the index.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_12 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 12u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread: samples/reactors_negative_port_type now uses lib reactors through packageDir (timer -> a gauge reactor taking signal f64: Expected ... signal i64), no private copy of the definitions.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_13 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:17 helper-lane lap 1: verified lib/spiral/reactors.spi is committed (59b477ba) and samples/reactors_negative_port_type is in the accepted oracle (C, Delphi, Fsharp, Rust rows = error as intended). First open point, modal reactors, prototyped: lab/LIB-REACTORS/modal.spi defines union reactor_mode and modal first second leave : reactor (reactor_mode * sa * sb) i o (LF reset transition: the entered mode restarts from its init, the left mode keeps its state); probe (try.ps1 with lib reactors.spi as module, C, via Invoke-Heavy): timer 1 -> modal switches to a hold-100 mode when the output reaches 3, 6 ticks into sink: compile exit 0, run exit 306 = expected 1+2+3+3x100 with 6 samples seen.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_14 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:08 helper-lane lap 2: modal reactors done in code: history transitions added (union mode_entry = Reset | History; Reset restarts the entered mode from its init, History resumes its saved state - LF's reset vs history transitions), probed first in lab/LIB-REACTORS/modal.spi on C (Invoke-Heavy): timer -> modal entry (timer) (timer then scale 10) with leave on multiples of 3, 9 ticks into sink gives exit 72081 = reset sum 72 and history sum 81, both predicted by an independent PowerShell model. Then moved into lib/spiral/reactors.spi (uncommitted, additive: union mode_entry, union reactor_mode, inl modal before run_ticks) with test modal_reset_restarts_history_resumes asserting (72i64, 81i64) on the file's c and rust targets. HEAVY QUEUE: Cells gate on lib/spiral/reactors.spi (~5 min). Open after this: a real physical clock on native backends, GADT port direction.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_15 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:52 helper-lane lap 3: verified the lib move myself instead of waiting for the queue: Cells on lib/spiral/reactors.spi (run_notebook.ps1, process compiler, no warm) as a bounded eoie heavy job finished in 64 s with 7 of 7 tests passed, including the new modal_reset_restarts_history_resumes (72, 81) on c and rust. Queue row marked done; the lab prototype lab/LIB-REACTORS/modal.spi (untracked, superseded by the lib code) was removed. Remaining open points of the item: a real physical clock for with_deadline on native backends, and GADT port direction in the reactor index.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_16 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T22:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:00 helper-lane lap 6: a real physical clock is in lib. lib/spiral/reactors.spi gains physical_micros () : i64, microseconds since the Unix epoch, uncommitted and additive (staged as lab/LIB-REACTORS/physical-addition.spi). On C it uses GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime under _WIN32, declared by hand so windows.h never enters generated C, and clock_gettime REALTIME elsewhere. On Rust it uses SystemTime since UNIX_EPOCH. On Zig it reuses date_time.zig_micros_now. Its test physical_micros_reads_a_real_clock_that_moves_forward (first > 1.7e15 and second >= first) passes on c, rust and zig. lab/LIB-REACTORS/physical-run.ps1 (physical-20261019-1756.log) shows 8 of 8 tests passed, failed_targets empty, and the change kept. Next: drive with_deadline's lag from physical_micros minus the logical tag's time in run, and GADT port direction.")
inl convo_in_spiral () = Task ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite what is useful from Convo-Lang (convo-lang/convo-lang, GPL-3.0, TypeScript; nothing vendored) in Spiral itself with GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection (user 2026-10-17). Useful parts (packages/convo-lang/src/lib, examples/convo 290 files): the transcript is both program and record (> system/user/assistant/define blocks, completions appended back with @tokenUsage/@format tags); functions with doc-commented typed params become tool schemas; struct types drive @json structured output validated against the type; @condition'd messages; graph-routed agents (convo-node-graph); a completion cache keyed by the sorted hash of the messages (ConvoHashCacheBase) = deterministic replay without network; a global concurrency lock; ConvoModelTester runs a convo across models asserting expected roles, format, function called and return value with duration. Spiral design: role-indexed GADT messages, tool registry as an existential list with reflection-derived JSON schema (prototypes per type), HKT over the completion provider, type-directed decoders for structured output, transcripts as livemd/.spi cells whose recorded completions are inline expectations, hash-keyed record/replay as the default test mode, assertions as Spiral test blocks. First consumers: eoie one-call TDD cases (spec/one-call.md), xsim, the X client.", Epic, P2, D6, 75u32, Active, "", "spiral,lib,eoie,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:20Z", "main-thread", "09:20 main-thread fork: (1) scope mined from Convo's 290 .convo files (job-tmp study/convo-mine/mine.py): files using > user 270, > assistant 215, @import 176 (composition of convos), @tokenUsage 152 / @model 147 / @time 140 (each completion recorded inline), > define 94, > system 75, typed functions 47 with recorded > call 30 / > result 29, struct 34, @json 24, @enableVision 15; rare: @edge graph routing 8, @on 8, @condition 7, components/RAG/tailwind 1-3. MVP = transcript + import + recorded completion metadata + typed tools with call/result records + struct-typed @json output; skip vision/components/RAG. (2) First Spiral sketch compiles and runs (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/main.spi): role-indexed GADT union message role (System/User/Assistant :: string * recorded/Call/Result), transcript = union rec of (exists role. message role * transcript) built with say, walked by inl rec with inl exists unpacking; a call/result balance check and a weight fold run entirely in partial evaluation - the C residual is only the arithmetic over the one runtime value (exit 110 = expected). Spiral facts: an existential must be the whole case field (Say :: (exists role. message role * transcript)), packed as Say (exists (m, rest)); runtime-loaded transcripts need a plain (non-GADT) union, the GADT index is for statically built flows. Next: make an unanswered Call a type error (index transcript by a pending-call phantom), reflection-derived tool schema from a function's record type, and a hash-keyed record/replay completion store.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:33Z", "main-thread", "09:33 main-thread fork: type-level tool-call protocol designed and half-proven (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/protocol.spi): union rec transcript pending indexed by the count of tail Results not yet claimed by a Call (Done : transcript zero; Result : n -> succ n; Call : succ n -> n; others keep n). An unanswered Call and an orphan Result are both rejected at compile time with clear errors (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero). The valid transcript hits compiler bug GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (any value of such a union hangs compilation), so the protocol waits on that fix; the existential-per-message sketch (main.spi, exit 110) remains the working form meanwhile.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:43Z", "main-thread", "09:43 main-thread fork: reflection-derived tool schema is WIP (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi; bounded runner try.ps1). Working pieces: a .spir module listed as 'schema_real*' in package.spiproj; .spi -> real via a forall of the calling .spi function (schema_of forall t. (name) (_ : t) = real schema_real.tool_schema name `t); real functions take term args first and return a forall (inl tool_schema name = forall params. => ...), type-only ones as 'inl json_type forall t. : string' with typecase; a type application binds LOOSER than + (name + json_type `(i32) parses as (name + json_type) `(i32) -> 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'), so parenthesize every type application inside an expression. Blocked: any real_core.record_type_fold over the parameter record (even with a constant folder body, in a .spir module or in a .spi real block like lib reflection.spi) fails with 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'; the compiler's evaluation (single-flight EOp RecordTypeFold: apply f state, apply DSymbol key, then type_apply the field type) matches lib's folder shape, so the suspect is how the record TYPE argument reaches record_type_fold's returned forall from a top-down forall variable (lib only calls it on a type bound by real's own forall inside union_to_record's callback). Next: bind the record type with a real-side forall (a real helper taking `t and calling the fold inside its own forall), or fold with real_core.record_type_try_find over known keys as a fallback.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main-thread fork: schema bisect state (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/p-*/schema_real.spir + try.ps1, each variant compiled under a 60 s kill): OK = tool_schema name = forall params. => name (exit 10); json_type `(i32) (7); json_type `params (6 = 'object', i.e. params falls to typecase's _ branch). FAIL 'Cannot apply a forall with a term' = every record op on params: real_core.record_type_fold (multi-line, one-line, bound folder, .spi real block, literal record type), real_core.record_type_length `params (plain, parenthesized, aliased) and even the raw !!!!RecordTypeLength(`params). Lead: params arriving through schema_of forall t. (_ : t) from inl args : get_weather is not a bare record type at that point (alias/nominal or an unresolved forall), and the working lib path (arg_union -> reflection.get_union_fields_untag, passed in last night's corpus) only folds records produced by union_to_record's callback. Next session: print the type with !!!!PrintStatic inside tool_schema, then try schema_of with an explicit record type annotation instead of the type alias, or pass the record type with `({location : string; ...}) from .spi.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T13:48Z", "main-thread", "09:48 main-thread fork: SOLVED - reflection-derived tool schema works (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi): type get_weather = {location : string; days : i32; metric : bool} compiles to ONE C string literal, the JSON schema {name: getWeather, parameters: {type: object, properties: {location: string, days: integer, metric: boolean}}}, via real_core.record_type_fold + typecase + !!!!SymbolToString, zero runtime cost. Root cause of the earlier 'Cannot apply a forall with a term': a .spir module must start with 'open real_core' (core's own iam_real.spir does), else + and = resolve to forall-typed definitions; real + is numeric !!!!Add, so string joins need !!!!StaticStringConcat (defined here as inl (++) a b). Also: list the module as schema_real* in package.spiproj; parenthesize type applications inside expressions. Next: nested record/array/union fields (recursive typecase), optional fields, descriptions from a parallel record, then decode a model's JSON reply back into the same record type.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: nested schemas work (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/nested_real.spir + nested-main.spi): one recursive inl rec schema_of forall t. with typecase over primitives, array_base ~el (items schema) and {} (object: record_type_fold over the fields, recursing per value type). type get_forecast = {where : place; days : i32; hours : array_base i32} with place = {city : string; lat : f64} compiles to one C string literal: an object whose where is a nested object (city string, lat number), days integer, hours array of integer. Remaining for the tool layer: unions as oneOf/enum (union_to_record), optional fields, descriptions, field order (record_type_fold walks the map order, here location/days/metric came out in declaration order but that should be checked against Map key order), and the reverse direction: a type-directed JSON decoder into the same record.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: field order checked: record_type_fold walks a map keyed by (field index, name), so schemas list fields in declaration order (where, days, hours here; alphabetical would be days, hours, where). No sort needed.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-17T13:58Z", "main-thread", "09:58 main-thread fork: unions as enums work (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/p-enum/schema_real.spir + enum-main.spi): the _ branch checks union_type_is and folds union_to_record's case record keys; union unit_system = Metric | Imperial inside type set_units = {system : unit_system; precision : i32} compiles to one literal whose system property is a string enum [Metric, Imperial] (declaration order) and precision an integer. Covered now: primitives, nested records, arrays, payload-less unions. Next: unions with payloads as oneOf, option as optional field, the reverse JSON decoder.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/convo-mine and study/convo-spiral are now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-mine and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral (main.spi existential transcript -> 110; protocol.spi type-level call/result protocol, rejects bad transcripts, valid one blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG; schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi flat schema; nested_real.spir + nested-main.spi nested/array; p-enum unions as enum; try.ps1 bounded runner). Proven: compile-time JSON schema by reflection for primitives, nested records, arrays, payload-less unions (one C string literal, declaration order). Open: payload unions as oneOf, option as optional, descriptions, type-directed JSON decoder, hash-keyed record/replay store, then lib placement and first consumer (eoie one-call cases).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/ - transcript.spi (110), protocol.spi (type-level protocol, valid case blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG), schema.spi + schema_real.spir (150), nested.spi + nested_real.spir (236), enum.spi + enum_real.spir (152), mine.py (python -I mine.py <a convo-lang clone>); all reverified from the repo with pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source <file> [-Modules <x_real.spir>] [-Backend C|Zig|Lua|Rust] (60 s kill, shared single-flight DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-17T14:42Z", "main-thread", "10:42 main-thread fork: schema coverage complete for tool parameters (spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/full_real.spir + full.spi, run: pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source lab/convo-in-spiral/full.spi -Modules lab/convo-in-spiral/full_real.spir): option fields are left out of required, payload unions become oneOf (payload case = object keyed by the case name, nullary case = const), all-nullary unions a string oneOf of consts, tuples arrays with prefixItems, plus primitives, nested records and arrays; one compile-time literal (452 bytes for a 4-field forecast record), exit 5 = 453 mod 256 with the tuple added. Spiral facts: join is a keyword (a helper named join gave 'Expected a function... Got:' far from the cause), and type-only functions in an inl rec ... and inl group need a unit argument (forall t. () : string). Next: descriptions from a parallel record, then the reverse direction (type-directed JSON decoder), which is the larger piece.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-17T14:42Z", "main-thread", "10:42 main-thread fork: correction: the full forecast schema is 451 characters before tuples (exit 195) and 517 with tuple prefixItems (exit 5 = 517 mod 256), not 453.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-17T14:51Z", "main-thread", "10:51 main-thread fork: reverse direction started - type-directed JSON decoder works (spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/decode.spi + json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir; in PowerShell call the runner in-process: & lab/try.ps1 -Source lab/convo-in-spiral/decode.spi -Modules @('lab/convo-in-spiral/json_scan.spi','lab/convo-in-spiral/decode_real.spir')). decode_real.decode s t uses record_type_map over the target record, each field's parser picked by typecase on its type (i32, string, bool) from typed .spi scanners (value_at finds the key, parse_i32/parse_string/parse_bool); a runtime reply string {location: Cincinnati, days: 3, metric: true} decodes into get_weather: exit 113 = 3 + 100 + 10 as computed. Learned: in .spi, + on strings is a constraint error (use !!!!StaticStringConcat, which also works on runtime strings), char has no ordering and no conversion to i32 (digit lookup by equality). Limits now: flat records, no escapes inside strings, no nested objects/arrays/unions/option - next is recursion through the same typecase arms the schema uses, then the record/replay store keyed by message hash.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-17T14:52Z", "main-thread", "10:52 main-thread fork: decoder handles nested records (decode-nested.spi; runner now takes -Modules a,b through pwsh -File too): field and decode are one inl rec ... and inl group, a record-typed field slices its object by brace matching (object_end skips string contents and escapes) and decodes it recursively; {days: 3, where: {city: Cincinnati, days: 40}, metric: true} -> exit 153 = 3 + 100 + 40 + 10, flat decode.spi still 113. Known flaw: value_at finds the first occurrence of a key at any depth, so a nested key that comes BEFORE the same top-level key would be read as the top-level one - next fix: scan only depth-1 keys of the current object (reuse object_end's depth walk), then arrays, option (missing key = None) and unions (oneOf by case key), mirroring full_real.spir's schema arms.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-17T14:55Z", "main-thread", "10:55 main-thread fork: decoder key lookup fixed: json_scan.value_at now walks the object tracking depth and string state (escapes skipped) and matches a key only at depth 1 outside strings. Regression input decode-key-depth.spi puts the nested where.days (40) BEFORE the top-level days and adds a string value whose text is an escaped days:9 key; top-level days still reads 3 (exit 153), decode.spi 113 and decode-nested.spi 153 unchanged. Next: arrays (items between brackets at depth), option (missing key = None), unions (case key), and string escapes in parse_string.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-17T15:00Z", "main-thread", "11:00 main-thread fork: option fields decode (decode-option.spi): a missing key is None, a present one Some of the decoded value; {city: Recife, days: 4} into {city : string; days : option i32; units : option string} -> exit 60 = 4 + 50 (units None) + 6; decode/decode-nested/decode-key-depth unchanged (113/153/153). Real-dialect fact: the core option constructors take their type first there (None `inner, Some `inner x), else 'Cannot dyn a forall into a runtime var' / 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'. Decoder now covers primitives, nested records, option, depth-correct keys; left: arrays, unions, string escapes.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-17T15:07Z", "main-thread", "11:07 main-thread fork: arrays decode (decode-arrays.spi): json_scan.item_positions walks the array at depth 1 (strings and escapes skipped, so commas/brackets inside strings do not split), counts items, then allocates and records each item start; parse_i32_array / parse_string_array fill typed arrays; decode_real dispatches array_base by element type. {customer: ana, quantities: [3, 40, 200], tags: [a,b / ]x]} -> 233243 = 3*1000 + 243 + 3*10000 + 2*100000 exactly. Spiral facts reapplied: bind values before passing them to !!!! ops, and use block form for if-then sequences (a one-line 'if c then a; f x' sequences as (if c then a); f x). Decoder now: primitives, nested records, option, arrays of i32/string, depth-correct keys. Left: arrays of records/other element types (generic fill), unions, string escapes in values.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-17T15:10Z", "main-thread", "11:10 main-thread fork: type-level tool-call protocol UNBLOCKED by the GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG fix: lab/convo-in-spiral/protocol.spi (transcript pending indexed by unclaimed tail Results) now compiles and runs (exit 110, same weights as the existential form), and negative-protocol-unanswered-call.spi / negative-protocol-orphan-result.spi are compile errors (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero). So an unanswered tool call is a TYPE error now.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/convo-in-spiral has transcript, protocol (+2 negatives), schema/nested/enum/full (_real.spir) for schemas, decode* + json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir for decoding, mine.py; every lab experiment re-verified 11:09 from the repo (25 of 26 pass; the 26th, gadt repro, now compiles because the bug is fixed). Covered: compile-time JSON schema (primitives, records, arrays, tuples, option, unions as oneOf/enum); runtime decoder (primitives, nested records, option, i32/string arrays, depth-correct keys); type-level call/result protocol. Open: arrays of records, union decoding, string escapes, record/replay store, lib placement, first consumer (eoie one-call cases).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-17T15:14Z", "main-thread", "11:14 main-thread fork: arrays of records decode (decode-record-array.spi): json_scan.fill_array forall el. (count) (item : i32 -> el) is a generic typed .spi filler, and decode_real passes a closure that decodes each item's object slice recursively (real side applies the type first: fill_array `el ...); {customer: ana, lines: [{sku: p1, qty: 2}, {sku: p22, qty: 30}]} -> 32232 exactly. Decoder suite 6 of 6 (113, 153, 153, 60, 233243, 32232). Note for tests: values read with !!!!ArrayIndex need a type annotation before field access. Left: union decoding, string escapes, record/replay store.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-17T15:16Z", "main-thread", "11:16 main-thread fork: unions decode (decode-union.spi), mirroring the schema's oneOf: a nullary case is its name as a string, a payload case a one-key object {Case: payload}; decode_real folds union_to_record's cases at compile time into a runtime if-chain on the case name and builds the value with nominal_create (key, payload) - the same constructor lib reflection uses; {units: Imperial, where: {City: Recife}, other: {Grid: {row: 4, col: 7}}} -> 4762 = 2 + 60 + 4700. The decoder is now one positional decode_at (field lookup only in decode), and the suite is 7 of 7 (113, 153, 153, 60, 233243, 32232, 4762). Coverage now matches the schema side except tuples and string escapes. Paused here.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-17T15:17Z", "main-thread", "11:17 main-thread fork: tuples decode (decode-tuple.spi) as 2-item arrays, matching the schema's prefixItems: {span: [12, days], where: {Coordinates: [3, 9]}} into {span : i32 * string; where : location} -> 43912 = 12 + 40000 + 3900, including a tuple union payload. Decoder suite 8 of 8 (lab/convo-in-spiral/decode*.spi with json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir). Decoder and schema now cover the same types; open: string escapes in values, tuples longer than 2, record/replay store, lib placement. Paused.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-10-17T22:11Z", "main-thread", "18:11 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): the typed JSON codec is lib/spiral json_scan.spi (runtime scanner) + json_real.spir (full schema: records with required, options, tuples via prefixItems, unions as const/oneOf, arrays; plus the typecase decoder) + json.spi (public decode and tool_schema, value witness for the type), registered after reactors; 9 test blocks on C and Rust, 9/9 through cells (the 8-case decoder suite 113/60/153/153/233243/32232/4762/43912 plus the exact tool schema JSON). The call/result protocol is lib/spiral/conversation.spi: a GADT transcript indexed by pending calls (no_pending, pending_call n), so an unanswered call or an orphan result is a type error; test an_answered_call_closes_the_transcript (weight 11118 = prototype exit 110 mod 256). Its two negatives became harness samples conversation_negative_orphan_result and conversation_negative_unanswered_call (ExpectedErrors Expected: transcript zero, 4 backends, new rows until the next bless). Superseded and deleted: the flat/enum/nested schema iterations, the runtime transcript checker, mine.py (its corpus counts are in note 1). lab/convo-in-spiral removed. Open: string escapes in decoded values, tuples longer than 2, record/replay store, the .convo surface syntax.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_24 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-10-19T12:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:18 helper-lane lap 1: first open point (string escapes) fixed in a lab copy: lib json_scan.closing_quote stopped at the first quote even after a backslash, and parse_string returned raw escape text. lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/json_scan.spi skips escaped characters in closing_quote and unescapes \\n \\t \\r \\quote \\backslash \\/ (others, incl. \\u, kept raw) through text_between + unescape_from with runtime !!!!StaticStringConcat. Probe (try.ps1, C, Invoke-Heavy): lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/escapes.spi exits 3097 as predicted with the patched scanner and -1 with today's lib scanner. HEAVY QUEUE: C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab\\CONVO-IN-SPIRAL\\run.ps1 (copies the scanner into lib, appends test decode_unescapes_strings = 13097 to json.spi, runs Cells on json.spi for C and Rust, restores both lib files on failure; ~5-10 min).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_25 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-10-19T13:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:10 helper-lane lap 2: second open point (tuples longer than 2) fixed in the lab copy lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/json_real.spir: a Spiral 3-tuple is a * (b * c), so the old ~a * ~b arm decoded item 1 as a nested pair and the schema nested prefixItems; new schema_items and decode_items (typecase recursion with an item index) flatten the tail, so (i32 * string * i32) reads the JSON array [12, days, 7] and the schema lists three prefixItems. Probe via try.ps1 C (Invoke-Heavy) with both lab modules: triple.spi exit 1207 = 12 * 100 + 7, with the string and bool checked. Pairs take the same path as before (their tail is not a pair). A JSON [x, [y, z]] now reads as a flat triple, matching how Spiral itself cannot tell a * (b * c) from a triple. The queued run.ps1 now installs json_real.spir too and adds test decode_reads_tuples_longer_than_two.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_26 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-10-19T14:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:25 main heavy queue ran lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/run.ps1 (10:21): json.spi 11/11 passed incl. decode_unescapes_strings, so the json_scan string-escape patch stays in lib (json.spi +16, json_scan.spi +32/-5, uncommitted).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_27 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-10-19T14:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:26 helper-lane lap 3: (1) the queued run.ps1 ran at 10:21 (main heavy queue): json.spi 11/11 passed on C and Rust incl. decode_unescapes_strings and decode_reads_tuples_longer_than_two, so lib json_scan.spi, json_real.spir and json.spi now carry both fixes (uncommitted, 55+/7-); open points 1 and 2 closed. (2) third open point, record/replay store, first slice in lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/replay.spi: record walks a typed transcript with a stack of open calls (Call pushes name+arguments, Result pops the innermost, matching the GADT's LIFO pending_call nesting, User/System/Assistant pass through) into an exchanges list; replay name arguments looks a recorded result up (empty on a miss). Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded job, 6 s): a transcript with two calls separated by a user turn exits 2111 as predicted (2 exchanges, both replays exact, the miss empty), with dynamic strings so the lookups run at runtime. Remaining: persisting exchanges (JSON lines through the lib codec) and the .convo surface syntax.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_28 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:31 helper-lane lap 4: persistence format decided and drafted: lib json has a decoder and tool_schema but no encoder (json.spi/json_real.spir list only decode, tool_schema, schema_of, decode_at), and a general typecase encoder would need runtime number formatting inside real code; the replay store only stores strings, so exchanges persist as JSON Lines {name, arguments, result} with a three-replace string escaper (backslash first, then quote, newline) built on sm'.replace in the .spi layer. Staged as lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/persist-addition.spi (exchanges union, json_string, exchange_line, exchange_lines, and test exchanges_persist_as_json_lines on c and rust with the exact expected two-line text), to append to lib conversation.spi after the core-switch freeze and run through its Cells gate. Reading the lines back reuses json.decode on a record {name; arguments; result}.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_29 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 29u32, "2026-10-19T22:15Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:15 helper-lane lap 6: transcript persistence is in lib. lib/spiral/conversation.spi gains the persist section (uncommitted, additive): an exchanges union, json_string, exchange_line and exchange_lines, so a tool-call log persists as JSON Lines {name, arguments, result}. Its test exchanges_persist_as_json_lines passes on c and rust with the exact two-line text. lab/CONVO-IN-SPIRAL/persist-run.ps1 (persist-20261019-1815.log) shows 2 of 2 tests passed and the change kept. The first try failed on c: sm'.replace has no C arm (backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral). The escaper is now plain Spiral: it scans bytes for the three specials (backslash, quote, newline), compared through sm.index of a constant string so Rust compares bytes with bytes, and copies the spans between them with sm.slice. Specials are ASCII, so each cut lands on a UTF-8 boundary, and the C slice guard holds for non-ASCII text too. Next: read the lines back through json.decode into the exchanges union (replay from disk).")
inl gadt_growing_index_hang () = Task ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", "Compiler hang (both builds tested: candidate single-flight DLL of pack night-1017): a value of a recursive GADT whose case FIELD mentions the type at a grown index never finishes compiling (CPU-bound, ~230 MB, killed after 45-250 s). 8-line repro: nominal zero = (); nominal succ n = (); union rec transcript pending = | Done :: transcript zero | Call :: forall n. i32 * transcript (succ n) -> transcript n; inl main () : i32 = (inl t : transcript zero = Done) then 7i32. Same union defined but unused: compiles. Index kept the same (chain n -> chain n) or a non-recursive index change (box (succ n)): compile and run. Wrong programs over the full Call/Result transcript are rejected fast and correctly (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero), so type checking is fine; the suspect is the representation/reachable-cases closure of a GADT-indexed union (pack-1x: 'a GADT-indexed union holds only its reachable cases'), which expands transcript zero -> transcript (succ zero) -> ... without bound. Fix: compute reachable cases over the index modulo the case's quantified variables (a case whose field index grows is reachable for every instance, no expansion), or bound the closure and fall back to all cases; add the repro as the regression sample. Found while prototyping CONVO-IN-SPIRAL's type-level tool-call protocol (unanswered Call = type error).", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,gadt,bug", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_1 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:34Z", "main-thread", "09:34 main-thread fork: correction to the title: only the single-flight candidate DLL (job-tmp night/cand-a, same source as the installed pack) was tested; gpt5parallel and hopacsota are untested. Repro files in job-tmp study/convo-spiral/v-grow-field.spi and v-min.spi; bounded runner try.ps1 (kills after N s).")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_2 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF: repro files moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral (v-grow-field.spi = 8-line repro, v-min.spi, v-unused.spi compiles, v-rec-same-index.spi and v-nonrec-index.spi compile); run with %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral\\try.ps1 -Name x -Source <file> (60 s kill). Not started; fix belongs to the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_3 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION: spiral/lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/ - repro.spi (hangs: TIMEOUT 30 s), call-result.spi (hangs), defined-unused-compiles.spi / same-index-compiles.spi / nonrecursive-index-compiles.spi (exit 7). Reverified on the REBUILT installed single-flight DLL (after the rc.2 rebuild), so the bug is in the current tree. When fixed: repro.spi becomes the regression sample under apps/compiler/tmp/samples and this lab dir is deleted.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_4 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 4u32, "2026-10-17T14:39Z", "main-thread", "10:39 main-thread fork: ROOT CAUSE located (read-only, single-flight PartEval ~159191-159290 of spiral_compiler.fs): ty (TUnion ...) computes each case payload type and drops it when ty_is_uninhabited holds (the pruning added for lane F's compiler_probe_gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime 'Pattern miss'). ty_is_uninhabited has a depth cap (loop 32) but follows YNominal/YApply through nominal_type_apply, which evaluates the applied union's TUnion again, which calls ty_is_uninhabited with a FRESH cap of 32. With a growing index (transcript (succ n) -> transcript (succ (succ n)) -> ...) every nested instance is new, so neither the cap nor any memo stops it: unbounded recursion, CPU-bound with slowly growing memory, triggered only when a value forces the union's representation (matches every bisect result). Fix sketch: make the inhabitation check coinductive per nominal head - carry a set of nominal global ids currently being expanded (in s or a [<ThreadStatic>]-free parameter) and answer 'inhabited' (false) on re-entry of the same head, or pass the remaining depth into nominal_type_apply's union evaluation instead of restarting at 32. Pruning is only an optimization, so assuming inhabited on re-entry is safe. Check the gpt5parallel pair for the same pattern; regression sample = lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/repro.spi plus the 3 *-compiles.spi controls and lane F's probe.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_5 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:58Z", "main-thread", "10:58 main-thread: root cause found and fixed in the tree (both cores). PartEval's TUnion evaluation drops cases whose payload is uninhabited with ty_is_uninhabited, which caps its own walk at depth 32. But it reaches a payload's union body through nominal_type_apply, and evaluating that TUnion calls ty_is_uninhabited again with a fresh depth of 32. Regular recursion (chain n -> chain n) stops when the second visit hits the type join point's pending entry, which raises and counts as inhabited. A growing index (transcript (succ n)) makes a new type join point key at every level, so the forcing never ends. Fix: one nesting budget of 32 shared across those re-entries (single-flight a ref cell, gpt5parallel a ThreadLocal); when it's exhausted the answer is 'inhabited', the conservative direction the existing fallbacks already take. Patch: lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/uninhabited-nesting.ps1 (-DryRun). Regression sample samples/frontier_gadt_growing_index (the repro): before, timeout at 20 s on F#/C/Rust/Delphi (hopacsota-20261017-104913); after, compiled in 34-835 ms, exit 7. The study's full Call/Result transcript (lab call-result.spi) compiles and runs too. Pack review with the Zig exit fix is running.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_6 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:10Z", "main-thread", "11:10 main-thread fork: VERIFIED FIXED by the main lane's change in the tree (ty_is_uninhabited now counts nesting across re-entries with uninhabited_nesting, capped at 32 overall, instead of restarting its depth per nominal_type_apply; single-flight/gpt5parallel/hopacsota rebuilt 10:41-11:05, installed single-flight DLL 10:57): lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/repro.spi now compiles and runs (exit 7) instead of hanging, the 3 *-compiles.spi controls still exit 7, and the full Convo protocol transcript (previously hanging) compiles. Left per the lab lifecycle: add repro.spi as the regression sample under apps/compiler/tmp/samples with the review/bless, then delete lab/gadt-growing-index-hang. Note for hopacsota review: uninhabited_nesting is shared mutable state (gate G2 bans ThreadLocal and shared counters outside the engine) - check how it is declared before the hopacsota layer evaluates types in parallel.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_7 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:47Z", "main-thread", "11:47 main-thread: DONE. Blessed from single-flight-20261017-111908 (6,915 rows, DISAGREE 0), which reproduces the review 105810 on every cell; hopacsota-20261017-111317 equals it too. The only changes from the 08:40 bless are Zig's 457 residuals (ZIG-HUB-SPEED's wide exit, same pack) and the new rows of the two regression samples. The repro is promoted to samples/frontier_gadt_growing_index and lab/gadt-growing-index-hang is deleted (lab lifecycle).")
inl lab_in_repo () = Task ("LAB-IN-REPO", "Experiments live in the repo, not in job tmp (user 2026-10-17: tmp overuse is drift; it also blocks continuing on another machine or in a cloud agent). Convention started in spiral/lab: one directory per backlog item named by its id lowercased (lab/convo-in-spiral, lab/lib-reactors, lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth, lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling, lab/gadt-growing-index-hang), git-tracked sources only, one shared bounded runner lab/try.ps1 (60 s kill, shared DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL), run outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs; portable references only (nuget refs, repo-relative paths, no user-profile paths). Lifecycle: when the item is Done, its lab dir is promoted (lib/, apps/, samples/) or deleted in the same change; an item Paused/Planned keeps it. Measured drift it replaces: this job's tmp holds ~850 scripts in ~120 topic folders (packs, lanes, probes, benches) plus spiral-bin\\packs, all lost with the job and invisible to other machines; the heavy gate itself (tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 with the run ledger) lives there. Next: (1) EOIE-LAB-AUDIT; (2) move the reusable main-lane tools (heavy gate + ledger, compare-runs, kino-suite runner, pack scripts) into repo homes (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts or lab/<item>); (3) per-repo lab/ in eoie, i574n.github, polyglot as they need it.", Story, P1, D4, 45u32, Paused, "", "process,lab,drift", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_1 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:47Z", "main-thread", "10:47 main-thread fork: first main-lane tools moved into the repo: (1) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 = the heavy gate + run ledger, comment-free and split into named functions (Wait-HeavySlot, Get-HeavyTicketPrefix, Wait-WslReady, Stop-IdleWsl); the FIFO queue moved from the job tmp to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\heavy-queue (machine state, outlives jobs); exclusion still comes from the machine-global mutexes, so the job-tmp copy and this one exclude each other (smoke: repo copy took slot 2 while the parent held slot 1; both ledger rows written); until every lane sources the repo copy, FIFO order between the two queue dirs is not shared. (2) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/compare-runs.ps1 = cell-by-cell diff of two harness runs (run name or dir), DISAGREE count; verified on bless 081502 vs reuse run 084050 (0 cells). Next: lanes switch to . apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1, then the Kino suite runner and the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs) get repo homes.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_2 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: (3) apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1 = the Kino suite runner from the job-tmp lane-b script merged with the targeted-tests runner (-Label, -Tests a,b, -Dll, -Port, -TimeoutMin): repo-relative paths, MIX_BUILD_PATH and logs under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin (kino-builds/<label>, kino-suite/<label>.log), retires its daemon by TCP afterwards, and defers while a live Tibia client/server process runs (the old lane-specific arena flag file is gone). Smoke through the repo heavy gate: cell_test 12 tests, 0 failures. Moved so far: heavy.ps1, compare-runs.ps1, run_suite.ps1; still in job tmp: the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs), after-bless / determinism scripts, kino-tests.ps1 (superseded by run_suite -Tests).")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_3 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): lab convention live in spiral/lab (7 item dirs, runner lab/try.ps1); main-lane tools moved: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1, apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1. Next: lanes source the repo heavy.ps1 (shared FIFO queue), move the pack scripts, per-repo lab/ elsewhere.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_4 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:30Z", "main-thread", "11:30 main-thread: reconciled with the study lane. Canonical tools are apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 (gate + run ledger), scripts/compare-runs.ps1 and apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1, and the job-tmp heavy.ps1 is a one-line forwarder to scripts/heavy.ps1. I removed my duplicates from lab/harness-run-only-useful; it keeps only the experiment probes (rebuild/cross-folder determinism, bindiff.py, fsharp-resources, kino-tests). Patches live in lab/zig-hub-speed and lab/gadt-growing-index-hang.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_5 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:29 lap 1 grain: today's moves out of job tmp into spiral/lab: HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH (9 R3/R4 scripts), WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB (toolchain.spi), SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS (ebpf_family.spi), UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC (upstream_let_rec_annotation draft), LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS (CIC kernel + neg package); each rewritten LF with repo-relative packageDir; job tmp outputs (ipynb, html, logs) stay behind Next: count what job tmp still holds that a backlog item cites, and move the next batch")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_6 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:03 lap 2 grain: backlog notes cite 47 distinct job-tmp paths; the most cited, sweep/ (intent-report.txt 8x, intents.py), serves eoie-lane items (EOIE-ZED-SWEEP and others) and holds a copy of the opencode database, so it must never be moved into a git-tracked lab (credential tables, possible provider keys); rotation/* (CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION 7 notes) are applied one-shot patches, which the lab lifecycle deletes rather than promotes; night/civil, night/ebpf-blocks, night/cic and hopacsota-p0 were moved into spiral/lab this morning; 9 cited paths no longer exist (into, todo-source, caches, outputs, ...), mostly prose words matched after 'job tmp' Next: rule for the move: only source files (spi, spiproj, ps1, py, fsx) of main-lane items, never databases or logs; next batch: night/bend64 and rotation/coverage-probe if any open item still needs them")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_7 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:35 lap 4 grain: resolved Done folders: of 99 spiral lab folders, 18 belong to Done items; 10 were removed after a zip into the session scratchpad (CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES, DIR-TREE-README, FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS, FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER, MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL, MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY, TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC, UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC, WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30, SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE: none tracked, nothing written in the last 2 h, references only in ledger and finished queue rows); kept: CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (probe-lib.ps1 is the live lab gate tool, to be promoted), HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH (P3 inputs), CELLS-GATE + CELLS-REPIN (cross-referenced scripts), SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ (after-freeze probes), ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE and SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS (touched within 2 h) Next: promote probe-lib.ps1 into a tracked gate script (it hardcodes scratchpad MIX_BUILD_PATH, which must become a parameter), then resolve the remaining 8")
inl eoie_lab_audit () = Task ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", "eoie verb for lab lifecycle (eoie-first; one call): eoie lab audit <repo lab dir> <backlog root> lists every lab/<id> directory with its backlog item status and last note date, and flags: item missing (orphan lab), item Done (promote or delete now), no note for >14 days (stale), files ignored by .gitignore but present (run outputs outside .runs), absolute user-profile paths in sources (non-portable). Exit non-zero on orphan/Done so CI and the end-of-run check can gate on it. Written as granular Spiral in eoie, tests as Spiral test blocks. Consumer: LAB-IN-REPO.", Story, P1, D4, 10u32, Paused, "", "eoie,lab,process", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:48Z", "main-thread", "10:48 main-thread fork: TDD case written first (eoie/spec/one-call.md case 34, 'Audit lab directories against the backlog'): fixture with 3 lab dirs (one Paused item, one orphan, one Done item) and a source holding a C:\\Users nuget path; setup creates the 2 items with agile add/set as eoie do statements; command eoie do . 'lab audit lab against backlog'; exact expected table (lab, item, status, progress, verdict: keep / orphan: no backlog item OLD-PROBE / done: promote or delete), one non-portable line with file:line, receipt 'failed labs 3 keep 1 orphan 1 done 1 stale 0 non-portable 1'. Item id = lab dir name uppercased. Open in the spec: agile statements inside eoie do (the setup needs them) and the stale rule (last note older than 14 days at <clock>). Implementation is granular Spiral in eoie, not started (compiler-heavy builds wait for a free gate).")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): spec case 34 in eoie/spec/one-call.md is the TDD target; implementation not started.")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-10-17T22:21Z", "main-thread", "18:21 main-thread: spiral/lab was emptied 2026-10-17 (every folder promoted to lib/samples/scripts/lanes or deleted, with a note on its item; lab/ is now created on demand). eoie/spec/one-call.md lines ~919-943 still cite lab/convo-in-spiral paths as audit fixtures; refresh those expected outputs when the audit verb is built.")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_4 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T09:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 05:40 eoie lane: implemented as an eoie do statement, 'lab audit <dir> against <backlog>' (crate eoie_do_lab, 540 lines): per lab folder the backlog item, status, progress and a verdict (keep, orphan: no backlog item X, done: promote or delete, stale: no note for N days when the last note is older than 14 days), plus non-portable C:\\Users\\... and /home/<user>/ paths with file:line; exits 1 on any finding. Spec case 34 green on the installed eoie.exe. Not yet: gitignored files present in a lab folder.")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_5 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T10:09Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:09 eoie lane: added the remaining finding: files ignored by .gitignore inside a lab folder (git status --ignored=matching), except under .runs, print as '<path>  ignored by .gitignore (run output outside .runs)' and add 'ignored N' to the receipt. Stale now means the item's last note is older than 14 days. The /home/<user>/ rule only matches at a token start, so C:/home/git paths are not flagged. On the real eoie repo: 5 labs, all keep, 0 findings. Installed in eoie.exe 06:08. Remaining from the item text: a top-level 'eoie lab audit' verb outside do (the do statement covers one-call use).")
inl spiral_realworld () = Task ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", "RealWorld (Conduit) in Spiral, after leangineer.com/blog/realworld-in-lean (2026-10): Lean 4 app passes the official API suite (154/154) and website suite (74 run), 1,033 backend lines (TS 1,267, Go 1,371, C# 2,396) + 616 site, typed route table (handler types from routes, copied by hand from OpenAPI), proofs only for slug validity (~40 proof lines) and HTML escaping, Postgres + scrypt through C FFI, 1,170-1,650 req/s (bare Std.Http 1,800 vs Go >100k; 8,640 with an unreleased runtime fix). Assessment: Spiral can beat it NOW on speed (Rust/Zig/C residuals instead of the Lean runtime), on the spec gap (derive the route table and request/response types from the OpenAPI JSON at compile time - the reflection schema work in CONVO-IN-SPIRAL is the same machinery, run in reverse), and on escaping/SQL safety by construction (nominal SafeHtml/parameterized SQL types, typestate GADTs); it can NOT match the machine-checked theorems until the bottom-up CIC kernel is real (LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS, 5%; its mega sample checks at compile time only), so the near-term path to proofs is the Lean backend: emit the domain (slug, escape) as Lean and prove it there, while the server runs native. Prerequisites: an HTTP server lib (lib/spiral networking has only a client), a Postgres client, JSON decode (CONVO-IN-SPIRAL), an HTML builder. Measure against the same suites and the article's numbers.", Epic, P3, D7, 10u32, Paused, "", "spiral,app,lean,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:49Z", "main-thread", "10:49 main-thread fork: first step on the article's Proposition 1 gap (route table copied by hand): spiral/lab/spiral-realworld/route_real.spir checks AT COMPILE TIME that a route string names exactly the handler's parameter record (record_type_fold over the fields, each must appear as :name followed by / or the end, and the number of ':' segments must equal the field count; error_type otherwise). route-ok.spi ('/articles/:slug/comments/:id' with {slug : string; id : i32}) compiles, the route string is a constant; negative-route-missing-param, -extra-param and -wrong-name are compile errors naming the route. Learned: literal string ops fold during partial evaluation (!!!!StringLength/StringIndex/StringSlice; the slice end index is inclusive and bounds are checked statically). Next: generate the routes and parameter records from the RealWorld OpenAPI JSON instead of writing them (closes the gap fully), typed handlers per method, then the HTTP server lib question.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:56Z", "main-thread", "10:56 main-thread fork: spec gap closed for routes: lab/spiral-realworld/routes_gen.py reads the official RealWorld OpenAPI file (gothinkster/realworld specs/api/openapi.yml, 922 lines, kept outside the repo as data) and emits, per parameterized operation, a parameter record typed from the spec (e.g. DELETE /articles/{slug}/comments/{id} -> {slug : string; id : i32}) and a route_real.route check; generated module in the git-ignored lab/.runs/realworld-generated/routes.spi. Result: 19 operations over 12 paths, all 11 parameterized operations compile through the compile-time route checker (exit 240) - where the Lean app copied its route table by hand, here spec drift is a compile error. Next: request/response bodies from components/schemas through the CONVO schema/decoder machinery (decode_real + full_real), then the handler signatures.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): lab/spiral-realworld has route_real.spir (compile-time route/params check), route-ok + 3 negatives, routes_gen.py (OpenAPI -> routes; 11 parameterized operations checked). Open: request/response bodies from components/schemas via the CONVO schema/decoder, handler signatures, the HTTP server lib question.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 4u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): the compile-time route checker is lib/spiral routes_real.spir + routes.spi (route path witness: the path must name exactly the handler record's fields as :name segments, else a compile error naming the route); test route_names_exactly_the_handler_fields on C and Rust, 1/1. Its three negatives became harness samples routes_negative_missing_param / _extra_param / _wrong_name that use lib through packageDir (no copies), ExpectedErrors match the route-specific message on 4 backends. routes_gen.py (OpenAPI yml -> parameter records) deleted: redo it in Spiral on the new lib json (decode the spec's JSON form into typed records, emit routes and records), which also covers the request/response bodies step. lab/spiral-realworld removed.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:49 helper-lane lap 1: state verified: lib/spiral routes.spi + routes_real.spir are committed (59b477ba) and the three routes_negative_* samples have 12 accepted oracle rows (4 backends each). Answered part of the open HTTP-server question by reading lib: lib/spiral/networking.spi has a client side only (reqwest request builder/get/post/send/body/header/json, response_text, a TCP client, ip_address), and no server binding exists anywhere in lib or the spiral workspace Cargo.toml (no hyper, axum, tokio, actix or tiny_http). So the Conduit backend needs a new server binding; the smallest Rust option is a synchronous one (tiny_http-style accept loop) to keep the crate count low, with axum/tokio only if the request rate matters (the Lean article measured 1,170-1,650 req/s). Bodies: the JSON decoder (CONVO-IN-SPIRAL) gets string-escape support in its queued run, a prerequisite for real request bodies.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:34 helper-lane lap 2: sized a no-crate server: lab/SPIRAL-REALWORLD/tcp_server.spi is one Rust-arm macro (std::net::TcpListener on 127.0.0.1:0, a server thread that reads the request line and answers GET /api/tags with an HTTP/1.1 200 JSON body tags: [], and an in-process client that sends the request and checks the body); compiled through the installed compiler and rustc via try.ps1 (Invoke-Heavy): exit 0. So a Conduit backend can start on std alone (no hyper, axum or tokio). Macro lessons on the way: format! must be written with an escaped bang in single-quote macro text (the bang starts a splice), and a JSON double quote inside a Rust string literal needs backslash-backslash-backslash-quote in the macro text, because backslash-quote is unescaped to a bare quote.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:16 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the typed route table the Lean version copies by hand, as lab/SPIRAL-REALWORLD/routes.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): union route with all 19 Conduit API endpoints, path parameters as nominal payloads (slug, username, comment_id; DeleteComment carries slug * comment_id), and one exhaustive endpoint function giving method, OpenAPI path template and auth class (Public, OptionalAuth, RequiredAuth), so adding a route without its method/path/auth is a missing-case error. Probe main walks all 19 and expects exit 1912 (19 routes, 12 needing a token). Next: the reverse direction (method + path -> option route) over the same table, then generate this file from the OpenAPI JSON instead of writing it.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 8u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:01 helper-lane lap 5: added the reverse direction to lab/SPIRAL-REALWORLD/routes.spi (unrun until the freeze all-clear): segments_from splits a path on slashes, and route_of method path matches method plus segment list against the same 19 routes (literal segments as string patterns, path parameters bound into the nominal slug/username, the feed route ordered before the slug route so /articles/feed is not read as a slug), returning NoRoute otherwise. The probe now routes /articles/feed (RequiredAuth), POST /articles/<slug>/favorite (RequiredAuth), /tags (Public) and an unknown path; expected exit 1111. Comment ids (DeleteComment) still need an i32 parse of the segment.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: routes.spi failed to parse at a one-line match with a nested constructor pattern (match x with | Route Feed => ...); replaced by a multi-line is_feed helper, rerun pending.")
inl ci_actions_lockfile () = Task ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", "Adopt GitHub Actions workflow lockfiles (public preview 2026-10-14; .github/workflows/actions.lock via the gh actions-lock CLI extension; pins actions and reusable workflows by commit, transitively; check mode for CI; Dependabot regenerates entries in its update PRs; Require-lockfile policy at repo/org; available on Free/Team). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 of 84 uses: lines across our 8 workflow repos (spiral 17, alphabet 14, polyglot 12, dice 11, i574n.github 11, x 8, dir-tree-html 7, eoie 4) are SHA-pinned; third-party actions by tag include awalsh128/cache-apt-pkgs-action, erlef/setup-beam, mlugg/setup-zig, moonrepo/setup-rust, oven-sh/setup-bun, snok/install-poetry, wtfjoke/setup-tectonic and peaceiris/actions-gh-pages (deploys with a write token). Gain: supply-chain pinning without editing YAML plus reviewable bumps; no effect on the local reference run (i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 runs steps natively, never resolves uses:). Plan: lock one repo first (i574n.github), add the check step, then the rest; dice and alphabet in the same change (mirror rule).", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,security,workflow", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_1 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:45Z", "main-thread", "10:45 main-thread fork: first repo done locally, UNCOMMITTED for review (i574n.github): installed gh extension github/gh-actions-lock (gh 2.83.2, logged in). gh actions-lock --no-fix (read-only) reported 11 unpinned actions in gh-pages.yml; gh actions-lock (fix mode) then (a) rewrote each floating major in the YAML to the exact current release (checkout v6 -> v6.1.0, setup-rust v1 -> v1.3.0, setup-beam v1 -> v1.24.1, setup-dotnet v5 -> v5.4.0, ... 11 lines) and (b) wrote .github/workflows/actions.lock (67 lines, version v0.0.3: per workflow the exact refs, per dependency ref + sha1 commit + owner_id + repo_id); --no-fix afterwards: All 1 workflow valid. Note the YAML rewrite: fix mode stops floating majors (bumps then come from Dependabot or a rerun), so review that before committing. Next: add 'gh actions-lock --no-fix' as a CI step (needs the extension in the runner), then the other 7 repos (dice + alphabet together); gh itself is from 2025-12, update per latest-of-everything.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_2 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:57Z", "main-thread", "10:57 main-thread fork: dice and alphabet locked together (mirror rule), UNCOMMITTED: fix mode rewrote 11 (dice) / 13 (alphabet) uses: lines to exact releases and wrote actions.lock (dice 67 lines, 10 locked dependencies; alphabet 98 lines, 15); --no-fix: All 1 workflow valid in both. The dependency counts differ because the two workflows already diverged before this change (dice 11 uses:, alphabet 14) - a pre-existing mirror gap to reconcile separately. Locked so far: i574n.github, dice, alphabet (3 of 8); left: spiral, polyglot, x, dir-tree-html, eoie.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_3 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:05Z", "main-thread", "11:05 main-thread fork: all 8 repos locked locally, UNCOMMITTED: spiral (10 locked deps, 17 uses: lines rewritten), polyglot (11, 12), x (6, 8), dir-tree-html (6, 7), eoie (4, 4), plus the earlier i574n.github (10, 11), dice (10, 11), alphabet (15, 13); gh actions-lock --no-fix = All 1 workflow valid in every repo. Each repo has .github/workflows/actions.lock (new) and its workflow YAML with exact release tags. Left: (1) review + commit per repo (user), (2) a CI step running gh actions-lock --no-fix so drift fails the run, (3) optionally the Require-lockfile repo setting, (4) update gh (2.83.2 is from 2025-12).")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_4 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): all 8 repos locked locally and UNCOMMITTED (actions.lock + exact tags in each workflow); review and commit per repo, then the --no-fix CI step and gh update.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_5 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:20 helper-lane lap 1: verified: actions.lock is committed in all 8 original repos (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, dir-tree-html, x, i574n.github, eoie) and gh actions-lock --no-fix says All 1 workflow valid in each, also with today's uncommitted rust-nightly YAML edits. The new blog repo had no lockfile (--no-fix asked for fixes): ran gh actions-lock there, which wrote .github/workflows/actions.lock (LF, untracked, needs the user's commit; tags were already exact so the YAML is unchanged) and --no-fix is now valid. Plan item 2 (a CI step running --no-fix) is unnecessary: GitHub already refuses a run at startup when a uses: is missing from the lock (eoie and x pushes failed that way on 10-18). Left: commit blog's lock (user), update gh (2.83.2 of 2025-12; extension v0.2.0), optional Require-lockfile repo setting (user).")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_6 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:10 helper-lane lap 2: version check for the gh update: installed gh 2.83.2 (scoop main bucket, 2025-12) vs latest v2.102.0 (2026-09-30), scoop info confirms the update is available; the extension github/gh-actions-lock is already at its latest v0.2.0 (2026-10-12). Not updated this lap: a gh process (pid 5856, started 09:10, not mine) was running, and swapping scoop's current junction under another lane's gh run watch is avoidable risk. Plan: run scoop update gh when Get-Process gh is empty, then gh actions-lock --no-fix in all 9 repos to confirm the newer gh still validates.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_7 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:28 helper-lane lap 3: no gh process was running, so scoop update gh ran: 2.83.2 -> 2.102.0 (2026-09-30, the latest), extension github/gh-actions-lock v0.2.0 (latest). Re-validated with the new gh: gh actions-lock --no-fix prints All 1 workflow valid in all 9 repos (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, dir-tree-html, x, i574n.github, eoie, blog). Done: every repo has a valid lock and GitHub itself enforces it at run startup. User actions left (not agent work): commit blog/.github/workflows/actions.lock (untracked), and optionally the repo setting that requires a lockfile.")
inl pwsh_free () = Task ("PWSH-FREE", "Get rid of pwsh with a better product (user 2026-10-17): tracked PowerShell measured 2026-10-17 is ~10.9k non-blank lines in our repos (spiral 7,385: matrix.psd1 3,405 of matrix DATA, test.ps1 946, scripts/core.ps1 356, fast-tier 190, env 148, build 137, probe/bench/lint/gate/cost ~600, Kino runners ~250, lib/app build scripts ~400; eoie compiler-contracts 1,794; polyglot 678, dice 431, x 266, alphabet 212, i574n.github 75). A pwsh -NoProfile cold start costs ~500 ms per call vs ~20 ms for a native Spiral binary (eoie.exe), so the matrix, the gate and the Kino runners pay that on every invocation. Rewrite them as granular Spiral (eoie-style one-call verbs where they belong), matrix data as Spiral test blocks (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS), keeping today's measured behaviour (native/compile memo, run ledger, reproducible builds) as tests. Children: SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR, KINO-HOT-PATHS, EOIE-SCRUM-XP", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR,KINO-HOT-PATHS,EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "pwsh-free,dogfood,epic", "current", "current")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_1 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[1/11 inventory, 2026-10-17 13:45] git ls-files + non-blank counts: 160 tracked .ps1/.psd1 (0 .psm1) in 15 of our repos. spiral 46 files/7,385 lines; eoie 19/1,794; polyglot 23/678; dice 13/431; x 8/266; alphabet 8/212; i574n.github 5/75; dir-tree-html 4/52; legacy: tapas2 6/187, fc1943s.github 5/135, vault 4/110, tictactoe 10/59, singularity 4/37, futuresales 4/9, calc-parser 1/2. Not counted above: 29 pwsh cells in 3 .livemd run by core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook (polyglot init 17, build 8, i574n.github workflow 4), untracked spiral/lab/*.ps1, and agent job-tmp scripts (most ledger rows today wrap one). Classes (non-blank lines): data tables 3,409 (matrix.psd1 3,405); harness/test orchestration ~1,700 (test.ps1 950, fast-tier 190, zed test-at-row+test 154, cost-report 109, test-rust-exports 89, probe 83, bench-native 52, spiral-processes 48, compare-runs 22); eoie contracts/attestation 1,794; build/bootstrap/toolchain ~2,900 (core.ps1 356, env.ps1 149, lib.ps1 140, compiler build.ps1 137, apps/*/build ~290, per-repo init/build/outdated/publish); core-source tooling ~280 (pair-drift, projection-hash, hopacsota-lint, formatter/*); process supervision 157 (heavy.ps1, spiral-processes) + the mutex/worker code in test.ps1; Kino runners 157 + core.ps1 notebook helpers ~70; one-off benches ~380; CI: every workflow yml in 11 repos only runs pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_2 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[2/11 test.ps1 950] Loads matrix.psd1, expands suites into (sample, backend) jobs, holds a Global named mutex per cache dir, builds a compile signature (sha256 of every compiler DLL + every .spi/.spir/.spiproj under bundle, lib and core package + SPIRAL_* env), replays compile-memo JSON, spreads jobs over ForEach-Object -Parallel workers that drive dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --batch jobs.tsv results.tsv with deadline kill and the exit-4 warm-recycle retry protocol. Native tier: 17 backend runners (~360 lines of near-identical Invoke-Native + sha16-of-stdout branches), Kotlin warm JVM server, Swift 16-program chunk batches, Gleam one-project batch with failure attribution, native-memo keyed by an AST hash of its own functions, SetErrorMode 0x8003. Then oracle/baseline verdicts, results.tsv, summary, -Bless merge into EXPECTED.tsv, -Record. Primitives: named mutex; spawn with timeout + tree kill + capture + PATH prefix; throttled parallel lanes; sha256 of files and normalized text; TSV I/O; JSON memo store (40,079 compile-memo + 11,389 native-memo files today); toolchain table. Covered: proxy run/command-capture, eoie_process_streaming Job Object kill, batch-plan parallel chains, proxy hash. Missing: mutex, TSV, memo store, toolchain table. Estimate ~2,000-2,500 Spiral lines in ~6 modules (jobs, compile driver, backend runner table as a union, memo, verdicts, report), each under the 1,000-line gate; the self-AST native signature becomes an explicit runner version.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_3 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[3/11 fast-tier, compare, cost, probe] fast-tier.ps1 (190) mines every <cache>/runs/<mode>-*/results.tsv: today 1,841 run dirs (1,096 single-flight), latest results.tsv 6,916 rows/9.4 MB, so one -Suite fast selection parses up to ~7M TSV rows in PowerShell before the first compile, then runs 4 budget passes (cheapest per backend/family, young rows, flip histories, signal per second). That, not the 480 ms start, is the largest per-call cost in the harness and the natural first native verb: TSV in, TSV out, deterministic, testable byte for byte against the pwsh output. compare-runs (22, run diff), cost-report (109, ledger + medians), bench-native (52), spiral-processes (48, list/stop compiler daemons and workers by command line) are agent tools: each one verb over the same results-row + process-list primitives (~80-200 Spiral lines each). probe.ps1 (83) needs spawn with timeout, dotnet-stack/trace attach and per-sample log dirs: port after the compile driver. Estimate: fast-tier ~350 Spiral lines (row history, median, passes as a union of pass kinds) on a shared results-row module also used by compare, verdicts and bless.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_4 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[4/11 matrix.psd1 -> test blocks] ~3,150 of its 3,405 lines are backend membership lists, an inverted index backend -> samples: Zig ~455, Wasm ~442, Lua ~440, Bend ~402, Swift ~351, Kotlin ~305, Backends map ~247 (backend set -> samples), Lean ~169, Gleam ~162, AlsoTypeScript ~113, Ebpf ~53, Wgsl ~51, OpenQasm ~32, Calyx ~29. The rest are per-sample attributes: ExpectedErrors 36 (diagnostic substring), Timeouts 22, CFlags 21 (glob -> gcc flags / shim include), Known 14 (backend, optional oracle scope, reason), plus Smoke 15, Mega 5, Fast policy 4 numbers. In the SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS shape each becomes source: the block target line names its backends (replaces every membership list and AlsoTypeScript), expected exit/stdout inline (replaces cache-only EXPECTED.tsv for converted families), expected diagnostic, timeout, c flags/shim and known divergence with oracle scope as block attributes. Only Smoke/Mega/Fast stay, as a ~40-line Spiral policy record. Convert family by family (ebpf_* first: 31 samples, 166 rows), the harness reads both sources until a list is empty, delete each list at zero. Do not write a psd1 reader for the native harness (Import-PowerShellDataFile is the only parser today): migrate the data, or dump it once to TSV as an interim.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_5 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[5/11 heavy gate + Kino runners] heavy.ps1 (109): Invoke-Heavy = two Global named mutex slots (spiral-heavy-job, -2), FIFO ticket files <ticks>-<pid>.t in %LOCALAPPDATA%/spiral-bin/heavy-queue with dead-pid pruning, slot 2 only above 3,072 MB free, WSL tickets with a 45-min starvation prefix, BelowNormal priority, wsl --shutdown when idle, one runs.tsv ledger row; Invoke-Timed = ledger only. Ledger today 07:02-13:15: 77 rows (51 heavy, 19 timed, 6 notebook, 1 kino-suite), wrapped runs 5-1,832 s, so the 0.5 s start is noise there: the win is one native gate binary every caller (pwsh, mix, eoie, Zed) can use. Keep mutex names, ticket format and ledger columns so pwsh and native callers coexist during the switch. run_notebook.ps1 (115): rebases path args, tries the warm daemon on TCP 13905 (lines: notebook request, then line/error/end), else resolves dotnet + compiler (may build it), mix deps, mix spiral.notebook, ledger row. The warm route is the hot path: pwsh start + heavy.ps1 dot-source to send one request. lib/spiral/build.ps1 runs 38 notebooks this way; zed test-at-row starts pwsh twice per click (itself + run_notebook) plus a dotnet block-rows call. run_suite.ps1 (42): MIX_BUILD_PATH per label, env, mix test with timeout + taskkill /T, daemon retire, ledger. No eoie verb covers these yet. Estimate: gate ~300 Spiral lines, notebook client ~200, suite runner ~150.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_6 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[6/11 build, bootstrap, toolchain] env.ps1 (149) is the hub everyone dot-sources (test, build, Kino runners, lib.ps1, eoie spiral.ps1/workspace.ps1/contracts, x sim): cache dir, mode names, core projection (#if pair stripper), Resolve-SpiralDotnet (spawns dotnet --list-sdks per call), a 19-tool native toolchain table with env overrides and scoop/current fallbacks, compiler DLL path, package dir, normalized text sha256. Compiler build.ps1 (137): pair-drift + hopacsota-lint gates, core staged by sha16, SDK chosen by what the system dotnet can run (temp global.json), PathMap, crossgen2 ReadyToRun with skip stamps, unlock-by-rename, builds.tsv receipt. lib.ps1 (140): BuildSpiral (compile with timeout, output-freshness check), PublishFsharp (fsproj generation). core.ps1 (356): Invoke-Block (retries, env scoping, WSL), symlink helpers, Invoke-Notebook, Invoke-PwshNotebook; shared by polyglot/dice/alphabet/tapas2/vault/dir-tree-html through polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 -> spiral/scripts/core.ps1. Per-repo init (clone siblings, rustup, symlinks), build (notebook export + BuildSpiral + cargo), outdated, publish are 5-40 line glue. Covered: proxy toolchain/portable-toolchain, fs-symlink, fs-copy-tree, binary-install, run; lib runtime execute_with_options. Estimate: env + toolchain table ~400 Spiral lines (one record per tool), compiler build driver ~300, BuildSpiral/app builds ~300; per-repo glue becomes a repo manifest read by one workflow verb.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_7 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[7/11 eoie compiler-contracts 1,794] eoie already has the native driver: build.ps1 (39) only cargo-builds eoie-dev, runs it, then Publish-EoieBinary (stage, schema + topology smoke with timeout, hash re-checks, atomic move), which proxy binary-install already models. workspace.ps1 (222, shared): bundle resolve, compiler snapshot with hash re-verification, sidecar guards, atomic generated-file write, Get-EoieOwners (cargo metadata -> [package.metadata.spiral] owners). test-regeneration (225) copies the workspace and compiles each owner in its own dotnet process, sequentially, not --batch: porting it to one --batch process also removes a .NET start per owner. renew-cold-rebuild (165) wraps the existing cold-rebuild-matrix verb; renew-strict-evidence (107), test-native-coverage (185), test-native-probes (94), test-source-package (105), test-strict-preflight (75), test-attestation (129, rustc fixtures), test-binary-publication (96, publisher mutation tests), test-dev-workflow (195, fixture workspaces with spaces), test-selected-build (17, cargo test); scripts/workflow.ps1 is the CI order. Primitives: cargo metadata JSON, spawn with timeout, sha256 snapshot, atomic replace, temp fixtures; nearly all exist as proxy verbs or in eoie_dev. Contracts become Spiral test blocks (eoie_dev_spiral_tests style). Estimate ~1,500 Spiral lines in ~5 packages; dev.ps1, eoie.ps1, spiral.ps1 (93) become eoie-dev subcommands.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_8 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[8/11 15 largest files] matrix.psd1 3,405 -> test-block attributes, then delete (note 4). test.ps1 950 -> native harness verb ~2,200 Spiral (note 2). core.ps1 356 -> Get-LastSortedItem and Update-Json have no callers (delete); Invoke-Block becomes the workflow step runner; symlinks -> proxy fs-symlink; Invoke-PwshNotebook goes with the 29 livemd pwsh cells; ~250. test-regeneration 225 + workspace.ps1 222 -> eoie-dev regenerate with batch compile ~500. lib/spiral/bench/parsing/bench.ps1 214 -> one-off bench: record its numbers, then a Spiral bench block. test-dev-workflow 195, test-native-coverage 185, renew-cold-rebuild 165 -> eoie test blocks over existing verbs ~600. fast-tier 190 -> first port ~350. env.ps1 149 -> workspace toolchain module ~400. lib.ps1 140 -> BuildSpiral as a spiral CLI verb ~150; PublishFsharp leaves with the no-Fable goal. compiler build.ps1 137 -> build verb ~300 that keeps the dotnet/crossgen calls identical so builds stay byte-reproducible (PathMap, obj-shared). apps/spiral/build.ps1 136 -> CLI self-check as test blocks (help, export round trip vs the previous CLI, rust/cuda/lua/gleam cases) ~250. apps/zed/tasks/test-at-row.ps1 133 -> spiral-zed/CLI subcommand (block rows, reach, impacted tests) talking to the warm daemon directly, saving 2 pwsh starts per click ~250. Total ~5,500 Spiral lines for ~6,800 pwsh lines, 3,405 of them data that moves into sources.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_9 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[9/11 hidden pwsh + primitive gaps] Outside .ps1: lib/spiral/crypto.spi get_file_hash' (Rust side) shells out to pwsh Get-FileHash although sha2 is already in its deps; lib/spiral/runtime.spi test blocks use pwsh -c as the child process (ReadAllText, a 5-line writer), so lib tests need pwsh; apps/spiral/spiral.spi process_rust/gleam/cuda build pwsh -c strings only for error traces (not run), but the wasm contract path really runs pwsh scripts/invoke-block.ps1 -Linux; Kino targets.ex has a pwsh cell kernel; .zed/tasks.json starts test-at-row through pwsh. WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB status: present: spawn + timeout + Job Object tree kill (eoie_process_streaming/windows.rs), run/command-capture/batch-plan with parallel chains, hash/hash-tree, fs-*, symlink, toolchain, binary-install; workspace_primitives: outcome, numbers, bytes, clock, console, files, rooted. Missing (0 hits in eoie/src and lib/spiral): named OS mutex (Global\\ on Windows, flock on Linux), header-indexed TSV codec, JSON memo store (file per key, atomic), normalized text sha256 (Get-TextSha256 defines residual identity in EXPECTED.tsv: must match bit for bit), toolchain table resolving scoop .shim path= lines and current junctions, free-memory query, process priority, git read (ls-remote url, ls-files), SetErrorMode for native children, line-protocol TCP client (Kino daemon).")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_10 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 10u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[11/11 risks] Bootstrap: a Spiral tool needs dotnet 11 SDK + SpiralCompiler build + The-Spiral-Language core clone + rustup/cargo. Generated Rust is committed (eoie src, apps/spiral/spiral.rs), so cargo alone builds the tools: keep the chain cargo build -> native tool -> (it installs dotnet, builds the compiler), never the reverse. On Windows install-dotnet.ps1 runs Microsoft dotnet-install.ps1, so download the SDK zip directly or keep that one pwsh call. Windows specifics: Global\\ named mutexes are machine-wide on Windows but file-backed and per-user on Linux; Job Objects are Windows-only (Linux needs process groups/killpg); SetErrorMode 0x8003 stops abort() waiting on WerFault (else 30 s false timeouts); scoop shims and current junctions; Swift batch links with /STACK; paths with spaces and non-ASCII (test-binary-publication fixture). CI: windows-2025 and ubuntu-24.04 runners ship pwsh and only run workflow.ps1, so CI is the last place it matters. Keep byte-identical: Get-TextSha256 residual hashes (EXPECTED.tsv), memo keys (a key change only replays nothing; record it once), TSV quoting (-UseQuotes Never), ledger columns other agents read. Coexistence: mix, Zed, agents and job-tmp scripts call these .ps1 paths today, so each path stays a one-line forwarder until its callers move. Measurement gap: no per-call timing of pwsh start vs script body was taken here (CPU-light lane); measure before ranking steps 2-3 against step 1.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_11 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 11u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[10/11 migration order] 0 primitives (WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB): normalized text sha256, TSV codec, named mutex, memo store, toolchain table, free memory, each with Spiral test blocks pinning todays outputs. 1 fast-tier verb (pure TSV, ~7M rows parsed per fast run) with compare-runs on the same results-row module; test.ps1 calls the binary. 2 Kino warm notebook client + zed test-at-row as native commands (2 pwsh starts per Zed click, 38 per lib build; 4-kind line protocol). 3 heavy gate as a native wrapper with the same mutex names, ticket files and ledger columns; heavy.ps1 becomes a forwarder, then goes. 4 harness: native compile driver (batch workers, memo, warm-recycle) behind test.ps1 first, then the native tier as one backend-runner table, verdicts/bless last; each step must reproduce a run row for row (verdicts, residual hashes) before its pwsh part is deleted. Meanwhile SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS empties matrix.psd1 family by family. 5 eoie: test-regeneration -> eoie-dev regenerate with --batch, contracts -> test blocks, Publish-EoieBinary -> binary-install. 6 env + compiler build, then per-repo init/build/workflow as data for one workflow verb; CI yml lines change last. Delete now or soon: core.ps1 Get-LastSortedItem/Update-Json (no callers), the pwsh call in crypto get_file_hash' (use sha2), PublishFsharp (no-Fable goal), tictactoe/singularity/futuresales/calc-parser scripts (~107 lines) if those repos are retired.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_12 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 12u32, "2026-10-17T17:50Z", "main-thread", "13:50 main-thread: absorbed the pwsh-inventory lane. Done now: memo stores no longer grow without bound. test.ps1 deletes compile-memo entries unused for 2 days (176 MB / 53k files after one day: each compiler build adds ~13k) and native-memo entries unused for 14 days (1 MB), and replays refresh their timestamps; the edits sit outside the AST-hashed native region, so the native memo stays valid. Queued facts for the migration: fast-tier scans ~1,096 run folders (~7M TSV rows) per -Suite fast run, the biggest per-call cost and the natural first native verb; the Kino warm-notebook path starts pwsh just to send one TCP request (38 times per lib/spiral build); lib/spiral/crypto.spi get_file_hash' shells out to pwsh even though sha2 is a dependency; and matrix.psd1 is ~3,150 lines of per-backend sample lists that become test-block attributes (don't write a psd1 reader).")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_13 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 13u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:13 styles-survey: CLI argument style for the pwsh replacements is now CLI-ARGS-STYLE (notes 1-3): clap is used only by apps/spiral, apps/wasm and alphabet documents via lib/spiral/runtime.spi (~310 lines, builder API, stringly ids); eoie and x hand-roll. Recommendation (b): one lib parser derived from unions (subcommands) and records (operands) by a compile-time reflection fold, generating usage/help/schema/errors; ~112 of the 124 tracked .ps1 in our 7 main repos have a param() block, so each becomes a typed record instead of new parsing code.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_14 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:36 lap 1 grain: tracked PowerShell re-measured 2026-10-19 (non-blank lines): spiral 8,028 in 53 files (7,385 on 10-17: grew 643), eoie 1,806, polyglot 678, dice 431, x 358 (266 on 10-17), alphabet 212, blog 64, dir-tree-html 53, about 11.6k total; growth comes from new lab scripts and today's CI fixes, so the count needs a lab vs product split Next: split the spiral count into lab/ vs product scripts so lab experiments do not read as pwsh debt")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_15 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: split done: spiral has 0 tracked lab/ scripts (lab is uncommitted), so all 8,066 tracked pwsh lines are product: matrix.psd1 3,468 (3,405 on 10-17, data), test.ps1 1,098 (946 on 10-17: +152 from the F# native tier, warm runner and cold fallback, toolchain identity, per-backend native keys), scripts/core.ps1 376, fast-tier.ps1 230, lib bench.ps1 214, env.ps1 150; test.ps1 is the growth to watch, matrix.psd1 moves out through SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS Next: pick the first test.ps1 piece with a clean seam for a native verb (fast-tier mining, already a separate script, 230 lines)")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_16 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: tracked pwsh grew today by harness fixes: test.ps1 +1 (warm F# exit mask) and pair-drift.ps1 +20 (-Tracked candidate mode); both are process + git-read glue that the Spiral workspace primitives (the eoie lane started them with toolchain) would replace Next: recount after the next push")
inl spiral_cli_granular () = Task ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", "Dogfood: rewrite the spiral/apps/spiral CLI under the 1,000-line generated-crate gate. Today it is one 1,918-line spiral.spi that generates one 24,280-line spiral.rs (24x the gate). Target: granular, domain-modeled Spiral modules, each generating a crate of at most 1k lines, with the CLI composed from them; it is the first pwsh replacement host (PWSH-FREE)", Story, P1, D7, 70u32, Active, "", "pwsh-free,dogfood,cli", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 1/9 map: apps/spiral/spiral.spi (1,918 lines) = 6 clap subcommands; each prints a JSON envelope {command_result: json(extension, code, code_path, output)} on stdout when SPIRAL_JSON=1. rust (--rs-path, --deps*, --wasm=, --contract=, --cleanup): writes target/spiral/<ws>/packages/Rust/<sha>/Cargo.toml + spiral.rs, cargo fmt with missing-manifest repair, cargo run, or a nightly wasm build then pwsh scripts/invoke-block.ps1 -Linux spiral_wasm. cuda (--py-path, --env Pip|Poetry, --deps): pip/poetry install, python. cpp: g++ -std=c++20, run. lua: in-process mlua, print/io.write capture. gleam (--target Erlang|JavaScript): gleam.toml, gleam run, or esbuild + trunk + index.html. export, alias dib-export (PATH EXT pairs): parses .cells/.dib magic blocks with parsing combinators, writes .spi/.spir/.fs/.md, async par_map. Dead code: persist_code_project/publish_* (fsproj, dotnet publish). Types: package_target, rust_runtime Wasm|Contract, export_output Fs|Md|Spi|Spir, magic, block, gleam_target, cuda_env, lua nominals. Spiral deps: lib/spiral core + spiral (file_system, runtime, parsing, sm', am', listm, mapm, crypto, date_time, env, reflection, async, trace, console). Cargo deps: mimalloc chrono regex sha2 serde_json clap futures encoding_rs(_io) async-walkdir mlua (vendored lua51). Callers: Kino builders.ex argv (rust/cuda/cpp/gleam-js/lua), notebook.ex dib-export PATH KIND, build.ps1 run check, test_json_protocol.py.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 2u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 2/9 where the 24,280 lines come from (spiral.rs sampled by fn spans): ~9,000 = runtime.execute_with_options, which re-parses the command STRING with lib parsing combinators: split_args ~5,000 (split_args_86 + method96 + method103, ~1,650 each: the p_segment alternatives are inlined 3x for the many1/sep_by continuations), split_command ~780 + closures, on_stdout/on_stderr ~430, execute body ~450, stdio/encoding helpers. ~3,850 = export parser (magic_command/content/blocks via p_string/many_chars_till/sep_end_by, + format_block/indent_block), +1,155 export loop (loop_144: trace + union_to_string inlined). ~3,380 = run_15: every process_* is inl, so all five runners are inlined into one dispatch fn. ~1,170 = 65 monomorphised unions/structs (each option/result payload is a new USn enum, ~18 lines). ~3,100 = trace state, clap get_command (290), file_system (read_link, find_parent, get_full_path ~115, create_dir ~200, normalize_path), sha2 hash. trace: 41 inline sites, 248 spiral_trace_hold calls, ~50-150 lines each including reflection.union_to_string (a nested Option chain, ~16 lines per union case per call site). Ratio 12.7x rs/spi, vs eoie ~4x (task_listing 189 spi -> 742 rs).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 3u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 3/9 eoie conventions that keep crates under 1k: one Cargo crate per dir with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, built by the same dotnet compiler (--batch in eoie spiral.ps1). package.spiproj packages = SOURCE-only Spiral packages inlined into each crate (workspace_primitives, rust_std_string/fs/env: one-line $'...' prims with ~dyn args; no lib/spiral sm'/file_system/runtime/parsing/trace). Cross-crate: !!!!Export(name, (fun ... : sig)) in main -> pub fn name (string params become &str, returns Rc<str>; i32/u64/bool prims; tuple params flatten; no usize/records/unions/lists). The consumer calls it through an inl $'crate::fn(&*!x)' : t wrapper plus a Cargo path dep. Verbs export () -> i32 entries that read std::env::args themselves (eoie_agile_list_run); the router matches argv[1]. let = emitted once (join point), inl = inlined; recursion is let rec, not combinators. Gate: eoie_source_topology topology.rs limit=1000 for the largest .rs file AND per crate dir (.rs + .spi counted, see CRATE-GATE-HARDEN); x/scripts/gate.ps1 counts generated .rs per package.metadata.spiral crate (report-only, -Enforce). Size follows what packages reach: planning_fields.spi is 26 lines and generates 762 rs. Do not copy: eoie_cli routing_terminal / eoie_process rust_global blobs (that era is rust_global, not the target).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 4u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 4/9 proposed split, shared source packages (no crate, inlined). Home = a lib/spiral workspace layer: the WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB slice that eoie later repoints its packageDir to, since spiral must not depend on eoie. cli_args: typed reader over std::env::args (flag_value --rs-path, flag_values --deps repeatable, flag_equals --wasm= with an empty default, flag_present --cleanup; union arg_problem Missing|Invalid; ~60-100 rs per crate). command_report: type report = extension code code_path output; union outcome Reported : report | Failed : diagnostic; JSON envelope with sorted keys code, code_path, extension, output and hand escaping; stdout/stderr + exit code; ~100. process_run: type process_request = program, args list, env pairs, cwd; one let run_process over std::process::Command with an argv vector, merged line capture, elapsed, optional timeout budget. It replaces execute_with_options and with it split_command/split_args entirely (~9k -> ~150 per crate). dep_spec: name | name=ver | name[features] -> toml line, shared by rust/gleam/cuda, ~40. text/fs/env prims in the eoie rust_std_* style. Rule for all of them: no lib/spiral trace, reflection.union_to_string, sm' record formatting, async or parsing; name unions with explicit let functions.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 5u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 5/9 proposed crates (apps/spiral/<crate>/, workspace members; estimated generated rs each): spiral_cli bin router: argv[1] match, usage text, dib-export alias, calls the exported entries (~200). cuda_runner: Pip|Poetry manifest, install, python, report (~300). cpp_runner: g++/SPIRAL_CXX build + run (~200). lua_runner: the only mlua dep, print/io.write capture (~300). gleam_runner Erlang: gleam.toml, owned src vs gleam_package, gleam build + run, output line filter (~400). gleam_js_bundle JavaScript: build, #app_ hash patch, bunx esbuild, index.html, trunk (~350). rust_manifest, pure text: workspace Cargo.toml (resolver 2|3, profiles), package Cargo.toml bin|cdylib, near main_state, near-sdk/indexmap defaults (~300, exports &str -> Rc<str>). rust_runner: sha2 package dir, write, cargo fmt + missing-manifest repair (str find, no regex), cargo run, output after the profile line, cleanup, exit 1 on error (~700). rust_wasm_runner: nightly wasm build + spiral_wasm called directly, no pwsh invoke-block (~250). notebook_export: line scanner over #! magic lines (no combinators) + Spi/Spir/Md rendering, sequential (~600). notebook_export_fs: F# module/namespace indent_block, or drop it after a caller grep (bold-native: no F#) (~450). Total ~4k vs 24k. Cargo deps left: sha2, mlua, mimalloc (chrono, regex, clap, futures, serde_json, encoding_rs, async-walkdir dropped).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 6/9 interfaces: each verb crate exports one () -> i32 entry (spiral_cuda_run, spiral_rust_run, spiral_export_run ...) that reads its own argv tail via cli_args, prints the envelope via command_report and returns the exit code. Only prims cross the ABI; domain types (report, cuda_env, gleam_target, export_output, rust_runtime) stay crate-local. Pure helpers another crate needs (rust_manifest) export &str -> Rc<str> renderers. Router side: inl cuda_run () : i32 = $'cuda_runner::spiral_cuda_run()' : i32, plus a Cargo path dep. Build: each crate Cargo.toml carries [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output; one compiler --batch run over all crates (the X-REPO-TEMPLATE build loop), then cargo build -p spiral. Gate: the x gate.ps1 shape for spiral (CRATE-GATE-HARDEN decides .rs-only vs .rs+.spi). Migration keeps ONE entry (no _native wrapper), strangler inside the monolith: spiral.spi keeps clap for help/parsing and routes an extracted verb to its crate, so spiral.rs shrinks with each slice and the build.ps1 run check keeps working. When the last runner leaves, clap/trace/execute_with_options go with it and spiral.spi becomes the ~200-line router.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 7u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 7/9 first slice: the cuda verb = cuda_runner crate + the cli_args, command_report and process_run packages, routed from the monolith. Why cuda: it is the smallest verb that has every concern (repeated flags, an enum flag, a deps manifest, a child process, the JSON envelope, failure exit), and it already has a non-pwsh acceptance test: test_json_protocol.py (one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL; a child exception gives a non-zero exit without command_result; a CUDA driver failure is not reported as success), plus the build.ps1 cuda check (spiral-check-cuda 42). It needs only python, no compiler or GPU. By contrast cpp needs g++, lua needs a vendored mlua build, rust needs cargo inside the test. Success = cuda_runner.rs under 1k, spiral.rs shrinks, the protocol and run checks pass, Kino cuda cells unchanged. Then: 2 cpp + lua; 3 notebook_export (largest independent win, ~5k lines; build.ps1 already diffs the export of every notebook against the previous CLI byte for byte); 4 gleam erlang + js; 5 rust_manifest + rust_runner; 6 rust_wasm_runner (WSL/Linux contract path, tested last per the no-WSL rule); 7 the router replaces clap and the dead fsproj code goes.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 8u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 8/9 risks: (a) ABI: !!!!Export maps only Spiral prims (no usize, records, unions or lists; strings &str in, Rc<str> out), so multi-value results need () -> i32 entries or text receipts. (b) Every crate re-emits its own copies of shared package types/helpers (~100-200 lines overhead each). (c) Explosion sources to block: inl functions with loops or several call sites must be let (join point); parser combinators duplicate continuation bodies (p_segment 3x = ~5k); literal specialisation, so use ~dyn args; reflection.union_to_string and #?{} record formatting expand per call site; trace inlines ~100 lines per site. (d) Behaviour to pin with tests: envelope key order/escaping (today serde_json arbitrary_precision BTreeMap); rust output = merged stream after the cargo profile line (+2); exit codes (rust 1, cuda/cpp failwith panic 101); --cleanup is SetFalse (flag present = keep files); the --help Usage: text; export PATH EXT pairs; the sha of a debug-formatted record names the package dir (changing it only costs cache misses). (e) pwsh/WSL is still inside the wasm/contract path. (f) Compile cost: 11 crates = 11 compiler jobs, so use --batch; the vendored mlua build stays isolated. (g) Parallel export moves from futures par_map to sequential (or std::thread::scope); measure it.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 9u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 9/9 tests: Spiral test blocks only (///> rust, test name =), no tests/*.rs. Per verb crate: a fixture in a temp dir, run the real built CLI as a child, check shows/hides on stdout/stderr (the eoie todo_cli.spi pattern). cuda ports the 4 test_json_protocol.py cases (then the .py is retired); cpp/lua/gleam/rust port the build.ps1 run-check cases (spiral-check-* 42); export ports the help/check.cells case (exact /// # check + inl main) plus a byte-identity test against a committed expected .spi for a few notebooks. Pure packages (cli_args, dep_spec, command_report JSON escaping, rust_manifest text) get unit test blocks with exact expected output. Open: which runner executes Spiral test blocks in the spiral repo (eoie has eoie_dev_spiral_tests), and whether test blocks inside a crate .spi count against the gate (eoie counts .spi; if so, keep tests in a sibling test package). Not verified here: no build or compile was run (CPU-light lane); every line estimate comes from spiral.rs fn spans and eoie ratios.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 10u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: baseline re-measured: apps/spiral is still one spiral.spi of 1,918 lines generating a 23,417-line spiral.rs (was 24,280 on 10-17: 863 fewer from compiler packs, still 23x the 1k gate) Next: pick the first verb to split out (smallest self-contained: version/help) and size its generated crate")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:55 lap 2 grain (first slice = cuda per note 7, not version/help: help has no runner to extract): read process_cuda (spiral.spi:1279-1394) for behaviour to pin. Finding: the deps transform renders Poetry TOML lines for BOTH envs, so Pip mode writes numpy='*' or name={version='*',features=[..]} into requirements.txt, which pip rejects; Cells builders.ex dep_arg also sends name='ver'. No caller passes cuda deps today (grep of ///! and ///> cuda/python lines with -d/--deps over every repo: 0 hits; test_json_protocol.py uses none), so cuda_runner should model dep_spec per env (pip: name==ver, name[features]; poetry: TOML line) instead of porting the bug. Other pins: TRACE_LEVEL=Verbose is injected into the child env; the try/split/concat output step is an identity; external_command is only used in the trace; failures are failwith (panic exit 101). Next: write the cli_args + command_report package signatures as a lab draft (lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/cuda_runner.spi) without compiling")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:40 lap 3 grain: drafted lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/cuda_runner.spi (not compiled; eoie crate conventions read from eoie/src workspace_primitives and agile_todo_paths): typed domain cuda_env Pip|Poetry, dep_spec AnyVersion|PinnedVersion|WithFeatures, cuda_problem MissingPyPath|UnknownEnv|InstallFailed|ChildFailed, records cuda_request and command_report; cli_args as argument_count/argument_at over std::env::args with let rec flag_index/flag_values_from (join points, no combinators); dep_line renders per env (pip name==ver and name[extras]; poetry TOML lines), which fixes the Pip bug from note 11 by construction; report_envelope builds the sorted-key JSON envelope {command_result: json(code, code_path, extension, output)}; spiral_cuda_run is the exported () -> i32 entry (!!!!Export), using workspace_primitives.console for stdout/stderr. Macro escapes follow the gotchas: Rust quotes inside macro text are written as backslash-backslash-quote (a single backslash is consumed as a Spiral escape). Open in the draft: process_run (std::process::Command with argv vector, merged capture) and manifest writing; json_text still uses serde_json, to be replaced by hand escaping so the crate drops that dependency. Next: draft process_run and manifest write, then queue the first compile of the crate (--batch) with a line count against the 1k gate")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 13u32, "2026-10-19T14:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:15 lap 4 grain: cuda_runner.spi draft gained the process half (138 lines, still uncompiled): nominal process_output over std::process::Output; union python_step PipInstall | PoetryInstall | PythonScript path | PoetryScript path, chosen per env by install_step/script_step, so the four command lines of process_cuda are data, not strings; command_output runs one program with up to 3 arguments (empty ones filtered), current_dir and TRACE_LEVEL=Verbose (the pin from note 11), no shell and no split_args parser, which is where ~9k of today's 23k generated lines come from; step_result_of maps exit code (code or 1) and stdout + stderr; manifest_name and write_text cover the manifest write. Macro text checked against the gotchas (no !=, no #, @ or backticks; splices are !name or &*!name; Rust quotes as plain double quotes inside single-quoted macros). Left before the first compile: wire spiral_cuda_run to manifest -> install -> script -> envelope with exit codes (failwith 101 parity), and drop serde_json from json_text. Next: write that wiring, then queue the first --batch compile with a line count")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 14u32, "2026-10-19T14:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:23 drive slice: THE FIRST SLICE COMPILES AND RUNS. lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/cuda_runner.spi (~180 lines) compiles with the installed compiler (try.ps1 -Backend Rust, heavy gate) to a 491-line main.rs, under the 1k gate (the monolith spiral.rs is 23,417), and rustc accepts it apart from the missing fn main of a lib entry (E0601, expected: the entry is the exported pub fn spiral_cuda_run). A harness (main.rs + fn main calling spiral_cuda_run) exercised the protocol: --py-path hello.py exits 0 and prints one JSON envelope whose command_result decodes (python json, twice) to code, code_path, extension, output in sorted order with quotes, tabs and CRLF escaped; no --py-path exits 1 with spiral cuda: --py-path is required; --env Conda exits 1 with unknown --env Conda; a raising script exits 1 with child failed plus the traceback. Lessons, now in the draft: a Rust macro call such as format!( inside macro text is parsed as a !( splice, so it must be written format!!( (the tokenizer error points at the first :: inside); Spiral string is Rc<str> on Rust, so every String-producing macro ends in .into() and slicing needs (&*!text)[..]. Dependencies used: std only (serde_json, clap, regex dropped for this verb). Next: make it a workspace crate (apps/spiral/cuda_runner with Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral]) and route the monolith's cuda verb to it, then port test_json_protocol.py's 4 cases to Spiral test blocks")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:24 drive slice cont.: acceptance proven on the existing oracle. apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py's three cuda cases (one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL Verbose..Critical in a temp folder named spiral json ü with child with spaces.py; child exception is a failure with the original diagnostic and no command_result; CUDA driver failure not reported as success) run against the cuda_runner harness: Ran 3 tests, OK (the harness ignores the leading cuda word, since flags are found by name). The fourth case is the rust verb, not this slice. Crate shell prepared for promotion: lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/crate/Cargo.toml (package cuda-runner, [lib] cuda_runner at cuda_runner.rs, [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, workspace version/edition/license/repository) and package.spiproj (|core-, module cuda_runner). Promotion (apps/spiral/cuda_runner + workspace member + routing the monolith's cuda arm to cuda_runner::spiral_cuda_run) waits until main's core switch window (11:40-13:30, apps/spiral frozen) has closed, so the crate is generated against core v1 once")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 16u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:38 drive slice cont.: second verb done and the shared package exists. lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR now holds cli_common.spi (20 blocks: command_report, step_result, process_output, join5/quote/newline, stdout_line/stderr_failure, argv reader flag_value/flag_values, json_text, report_envelope, step_result_of, read/write_text, parent_dir), cuda_runner.spi (open cli_common; 19 cuda blocks) and cpp_runner.spi (cxx from SPIRAL_CXX or g++, exe path .out(.exe via cfg!!(windows)), build -std=c++20 -O2 -w -lm, run, envelope extension cpp). Generated Rust with try.ps1 -Modules cli_common.spi: cuda_runner 491 lines, cpp_runner 161 lines (both under the 1k gate; shared helpers are re-emitted per crate only as far as each verb reaches them). Harnesses: cuda re-passes test_json_protocol.py's 3 cuda cases after the split (OK); cpp: hello.cpp exits 0 with an envelope decoding to extension cpp and output cpp ok, missing flag exits 1, a compile error exits 1 with build failed plus the g++ diagnostic, a program returning 3 exits 1 with child failed. Next after the switch window: promote both as apps/spiral/{cuda_runner,cpp_runner} crates with the shared package, route the monolith's cuda and cpp arms to them, and measure the monolith's spiral.rs shrink")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 17u32, "2026-10-19T14:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:47 lap 5 grain, the promotion plan read off apps/spiral/build.ps1 (so the post-switch step is mechanical): (1) the monolith's source of truth is spiral.livemd, exported to spiral.spi by build.ps1's pre-build (run_notebook --export-only), so the routing edit goes into the livemd through mix spiral.edit (and the regenerated spiral.spi), not into spiral.spi by hand; (2) routing at the top of main: if argv[1] is cuda or cpp, std::process::exit(cuda_runner::spiral_cuda_run()) / cpp_runner::spiral_cpp_run(), before clap parses, so the old arms become dead and can be deleted in the shrink step; (3) crates apps/spiral/{cli_common (Spiral source package only), cuda_runner, cpp_runner} with Cargo.toml [lib] + [package.metadata.spiral], added to spiral/workspace/Cargo.toml members, and path deps cuda-runner/cpp-runner in apps/spiral/Cargo.toml; (4) build.ps1 runs BuildSpiral for each crate entry before cargo build (like spiral.spi -> spiral.rs); (5) build.ps1's own run check already covers cuda (expects spiral-check-cuda 42 in the command_result output), and Cells' targets.ex parser tolerates a trace line before the envelope, which the crate never prints. Envelope is printed unconditionally by the monolith today too (no SPIRAL_JSON gate left in spiral.spi), so the crate matches it. Next: after the switch all-clear, write route-monolith as a lab script (livemd edit + crates + workspace + build.ps1) and queue apps/spiral/build.ps1 as the heavy proof")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 18u32, "2026-10-19T14:57Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:57 lap 5 grain: promotion script ready, plus a correction. lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/promote.ps1 (dry run by default, anchors checked at run time) writes apps/spiral/cli_common (source-only Spiral package), apps/spiral/cuda_runner and cpp_runner (each .spi with open cli_common.cli_common, package.spiproj with |core- + cli_common, Cargo.toml [lib] + [package.metadata.spiral]), adds both crates to spiral/workspace members and as path deps of apps/spiral, adds a BuildSpiral step per crate in apps/spiral/build.ps1 before the monolith's, and routes the monolith through mix spiral.edit on spiral.livemd (the main cell's source occurs exactly once): if argv[1] is cuda or cpp, std::process::exit(<crate>::spiral_<verb>_run()) before clap. Dry run lists the 11 writes and the edit. Correction to note 17: the monolith does gate printing: run_main prints the result only when SPIRAL_JSON=1 or the trace level is Info (my earlier grep was cut at 20 lines); the crates print the envelope always, which is a superset (build.ps1's run check and Cells' parser read it the same way; a silent run without SPIRAL_JSON gains an envelope line). HEAVY QUEUE (after main's switch all-clear, since it writes under apps/spiral): promote.ps1 -Apply, then pwsh apps/spiral/build.ps1 (its run check covers cuda; ~15 min); then measure spiral.rs and delete the dead cuda/cpp arms as the shrink step")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 19u32, "2026-10-19T15:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:26 lap 6 grain: picked the slice after cuda/cpp from a section map of apps/spiral/spiral.spi (1,918 lines -> spiral.rs 23,417): run 317, process_rust 298, process_gleam 264, process_lua 155, get_command 127, process_cuda 120 (split), process_cpp 46 (split). Next = lua_runner: process_lua (spiral.spi 1118-1269) is self-contained (5 mlua nominals, lua_values_debug/text, custom print and io.write capture, returns extension/code/code_path/output/stdio) and it is the monolith's ONLY user of mlua (Cargo.toml: mlua lua51 + vendored, which compiles the Lua C sources in every monolith build). So the split also takes a C build out of the monolith's dependency graph, the biggest build-time win per line among the remaining verbs. Protocol gap: test_json_protocol.py has no lua case (cuda x3, rust x1), so the slice starts with a lua protocol case (success prints and io.write capture, Lua runtime error = failure). Not started: apps/spiral .spi frozen until the all-clear; the lab crates also need a recompile on the post-switch core first.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 20u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:44 lap 7 grain: the lua_runner slice now has its oracle. apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py gains a lua helper plus 2 cases, and both pass against today's monolith (workspace/target/release/spiral.exe, 11:31 build): (1) print plus io.write under SPIRAL_JSON gives command_result with extension lua, code equal to the source byte for byte, and output = lua-print<TAB>1, newline, lua-write. The chunk's return value is NOT part of the protocol (process_lua computes it, but run drops it), and this case pins that. (2) error(...) exits non-zero with the message and no command_result. Lesson: Python write_text on Windows turns \\n into CRLF; the case writes with newline='' so code compares exactly. The split crate must keep both cases green.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 21u32, "2026-10-19T16:03Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:03 lap 8 grain, staged only (window): lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/lua_runner.spi plus the lua-crate shell (Cargo.toml with mlua lua51+vendored, package.spiproj cli_common + lua_runner). Design: one Rust primitive lua_run_of runs the chunk under mlua with print and io.write captured exactly as process_lua does (print joins its args with a tab and starts a new line after any pending io.write text; io.write appends to the pending line; output = lines joined with newlines plus the pending tail), and returns Result<String, String> as a nominal lua_run. lua_succeeded and lua_text read it, and run_lua writes the command_result envelope (extension lua, code read raw, code_path, output) or fails on stderr with the Lua error. The macro text was grepped for splice traps: only !code and !run splice, format!! is the escaped call, and tab/newline come from char::from to avoid backslashes. Not compiled yet (freeze); after the all-clear: BuildSpiral, check the 1k gate, run the 2 lua protocol cases against the crate through a small harness, then route.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 22u32, "2026-10-19T16:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:45 lap 9 grain: post-switch scan of lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (cli_common, cpp_runner, cuda_runner, lua_runner): none uses an array literal, am., loopw, dictm, rm or the old bind order, so the core switch needs no edit there. Version check: apps/spiral/Cargo.toml and the staged lua-crate both ask mlua >=0.11.4 and workspace/Cargo.lock holds 0.11.4, while crates.io's latest is 0.12.2. Decision: compile lua_runner.spi first on the locked 0.11.4 so the protocol cases compare like with like against the monolith; the 0.12 bump (latest of everything) follows as its own step, rerunning test_json_protocol.py's two lua cases on both crates")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 23u32, "2026-10-19T17:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:21 lap 10 grain: mlua 0.12 bump risk read off the changelog and docs.rs (no build): lua_runner.spi uses Lua::new_with(StdLib::ALL_SAFE, LuaOptions::default()), create_function, MultiValue iter, Value::to_string, globals().get::<Table>, load().eval; 0.12.0-0.12.2 list no breaking entry for any of them, and the 0.12.0 root re-export refactor keeps StdLib, LuaOptions, MultiValue, Table, Lua, Value, Error at the crate root (docs.rs 0.12.2). The only 0.12 changes touching this code are behavioural (callback panic handling, fractional-to-integer rejection on 5.3+, not lua51). So the bump should be the Cargo spec >=0.12.2 plus a lock update, no Spiral text change. Next: after all-clear compile lua_runner on 0.11.4, then on 0.12.2 with the protocol cases")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_24 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 24u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:43 lap 11 grain: the 0.12 bump's lock delta measured with cargo update -p mlua --precise 0.12.2 --dry-run in workspace/ (lockfile untouched): exactly 4 crates move, mlua 0.11.4 -> 0.12.2, mlua-sys 0.8.3 -> 0.13.0, lua-src 548.1.2 -> 551.0.2, luajit-src 210.6.2 -> 210.7.4; nothing else in the 249-crate graph changes, so the bump is isolated to the Lua path (lua51 + vendored builds from lua-src's bundled 5.1). Next: after all-clear, compile lua_runner on 0.11.4, then apply this bump and rerun the protocol cases")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_25 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 25u32, "2026-10-19T18:06Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:06 lap 12: lua_runner.spi compiles on core v1 with the installed compiler (125-line main.rs, under the 1k gate); as a lib it fails only on the missing mlua crate (8 x E0433). Next: scratch cargo crate on mlua 0.11.4 plus a harness for the 2 lua protocol cases")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_26 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 26u32, "2026-10-19T18:37Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:37 lap 13: third verb proven. The generated lua_runner.rs (125 lines) plus a 4-line fn main in a scratch crate on mlua =0.11.4 (lua51, vendored; lock seeded from workspace/Cargo.lock) builds in 17 s, and apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py -k lua passes both lua cases against it (print + io.write output exact, a runtime error fails with its message and no command_result). Next: the same on mlua 0.12.2 (all 4 crates already in the cargo cache), then promote lua with cuda/cpp")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_27 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 27u32, "2026-10-19T18:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:47 drive: the lua slice is ready to promote. The generated lua_runner.rs builds unchanged on mlua 0.12.2 (mlua-sys 0.13.0, lua-src 551.0.2) and passes both lua protocol cases, as on 0.11.4, so the bump needs no Spiral change. lab promote.ps1 now covers cuda, cpp and lua: crate dirs (lua_runner with the mlua lua51+vendored dependency), workspace members, path deps, a BuildSpiral step per crate and a router line per verb, and it checks that the main-cell anchor occurs exactly once in spiral.livemd's cell source; dry run lists 14 writes. Keep the crate on the monolith's mlua spec (>=0.11.4) and move both with one cargo update -p mlua --precise 0.12.2, since two mlua-sys versions in one workspace would both claim links = lua")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_28 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 28u32, "2026-10-19T18:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:52 drive, scope of the notebook_export slice from its callers: Cells renders .spi and .spir itself (Document.to_spi/to_spir) and calls the CLI export only for F# (notebook.ex:336, write_fs behind --fs-path), and the --fs-path users are dice lib/fsharp, polyglot lib/fsharp and apps/parser, alphabet apps/hangul and mechahaze-album1. The CLI's spi/spir export only feeds build.ps1 (check.cells and the previous-vs-new byte comparison). So the live format of this slice is Fs (namespace/module header from a '# Name (Namespace)' heading plus indent_block), Spi comes second for the build check, and Md has no caller. Oracle plan: render those 5 consumers' livemds to .cells and record the release CLI's fs export bytes as the expected output")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_29 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 29u32, "2026-10-19T19:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 15:29 drive: the export slice works and fits the gate. lab notebook_export.spi replaces the parser-combinator export (~5k generated lines in the monolith) with a line scanner over \\n#! blocks plus the F# namespace/module header and indent rules; it generates a 971-line crate (first build was 1147: union equality and single-use let helpers expanded, fixed by an output_code compare and inl), and its fs and spi exports of the 10 --fs-path notebooks are byte-identical to the release CLI's (20 of 20, lab export-check.ps1 against export-oracle.ps1's recorded bytes). promote.ps1 now routes export and dib-export too (5 verbs, 4 crates); after main's go, build.ps1's previous-vs-new export comparison checks it over every lib/apps livemd")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_30 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 30u32, "2026-10-19T20:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 16:43 drive: every verb now has a crate in lab that passes its checks. gleam_runner (564 generated lines) runs build.ps1's gleam case exactly and writes a gleam.toml byte-identical to the release CLI's; rust_runner (837 lines; Native, --wasm[=cmd] and --contract[=cmd], the wasm/contract path untested since it runs pwsh invoke-block -Linux) passes the rust run check and test_json_protocol.py's rust case. Both kept the child's final newline in output (release CLI: none), now stripped. promote.ps1 routes all 7 verbs; lab router.spi is the clap-free replacement for spiral.spi (usage text with Usage:, unrecognized subcommand text kept for Cells' dib-export fallback, exit 2 on usage errors where the monolith exits 0). Crate sizes: cuda 491, cpp 161, lua 125, export 971, gleam 564, rust 837 vs the monolith's 23,417")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_31 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 31u32, "2026-10-19T21:17Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 17:17 corrections before promote: (1) the router line now sends rust to rust_runner only when no --wasm/--contract argument is present, because the crate's contract/wasm path (cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 wasm build, pwsh invoke-block -Linux) has never run; that path stays on the monolith, and router_swap.py (which deletes the monolith) refuses until it is proven or the user accepts it unproven. (2) The crates' new test blocks are unrun until a Cells gate after promotion. (3) cuda, cpp, lua and export are re-proven from the current lab files (cli_common changed since their proofs) before the promote row is valid")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_32 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 32u32, "2026-10-19T22:04Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:04 ready for main's go: all six crates were re-proven from the exact lab files promote.ps1 copies (cuda 3 protocol cases, cpp 4 cases, lua 2 cases on mlua 0.11.4, export 20/20 byte-identical, gleam and rust equal to the release CLI on build.ps1's run-check sources plus the rust protocol case). lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR/proven.tsv pins each file's sha256 with its proving job, and promote.ps1 refuses if any file changed since. Queue row superseded accordingly")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_33 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 33u32, "2026-10-19T23:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:45 first crate test blocks RUN: lab test-blocks-run.ps1 (bounded job job-1792451601559: 33 s of work after a 31-minute gate wait) gated cli_common.spi from a scratch package (|core- + cli_common): discovered 2, executed 2, passed 2, failed 0 on rust (json_text escaping; the sorted report envelope inside command_result), so the hand-derived expected strings of cli_common are right. The other crates' blocks need cli_common as a package next to them (promote.ps1's layout), so they run after the promote. Next: main's go for the promote row")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_34 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 34u32, "2026-10-20T00:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:24 crate test blocks, run 2 (job-1792455111337, cuda_runner and notebook_export from scratch packages with cli_common as a module): all 3 test blocks passed (pip_and_poetry_dependency_lines; spi_export_matches_the_build_check and fsharp_heading_becomes_namespace_and_module), but the gate still exited 1: Cells also builds each file's trailing inl main () (the !!!!Export entry) as a cell on the default target, and Zig refuses Export with 2 args. So for a Cells gate the crate files need ///> _ (skip) above their Export main. The lab files stay as proven (proven.tsv pins their hashes); test-blocks-run.ps1 inserts the skip line into its scratch copies only, and job 3 reruns all four crates that way. For the promote: add ///> _ above each crate's main when the files move, then re-prove (hash change)")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_35 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 35u32, "2026-10-20T00:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:27 ALL CRATE TEST BLOCKS PASS: job-1792455850269 (2 min, slot 2 at once) gated cuda_runner, gleam_runner, rust_runner and notebook_export with ///> _ above each Export main: discovered 5, executed 5, passed 5, failed 0, gate exit 0; with cli_common's 2 from 19:44 that is every test block of the crates (cpp_runner and lua_runner have none). promote.ps1 now inserts that ///> _ line when it writes the crate files into apps/spiral (a directive line the compiler reads as a comment, so the hash-pinned proofs stay valid; dry run still passes), which makes the promoted crates gate-clean in Cells. Next: main's go for the promote row")
inl kino_hot_paths () = Task ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", "Implement Kino's performance-sensitive parts in Spiral (PWSH-FREE, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR): measure first. Today's numbers: per-cell time is dominated by external tools (gleam build ~2-9 s, dotnet fsi ~5 s floor, rustc); Kino's own overhead shows in warm runs (38 tests, 38 memo hits, 7-9 s = ~200 ms per test: memo key hashing (sha256 of compiler binaries, 20-40 ms per context), the compiler-daemon protocol, notebook parse/mounts). Profile a warm run, move the measured hot paths (memo keys, document/notebook parsing beyond the existing Spiral domain, runner/targets dispatch) into Spiral, and keep Elixir only as glue until KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR removes it", Story, P1, D6, 50u32, Active, "", "pwsh-free,kino,perf", "current", "current")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_1 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: first measurement: the per-context memo key hashes the compiler folder: 23 DLLs, 25.8 MB, sha256 of all of them takes 463 ms cold on this laptop (the item's 20-40 ms estimate assumed a warm cache); a key from file size + mtime + a one-time hash cached per path would make it near zero Next: check whether Cells hashes the DLLs once per run or once per context (grep memo.ex for the dll hash)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_2 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:58Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:58 main checked a suspected memo soundness bug and ruled it out: Path.wildcard on a backslashed base matches nothing, but memo.ex reaches the folder through Path.dirname, which returns forward slashes on Windows, so both SpiralCompiler.dll and SpiralCompilerCore.dll are hashed (probe: dirname -> C:/..., wildcard finds 2). memo.ex unchanged; memo_test.exs gained a guard test (a core change re-keys with the backslash spelling run_notebook.ps1 passes), 24/24 pass.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_3 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain (corrects lap 1's 463 ms): memo.ex compiler_binaries_print hashes only SpiralCompiler*.dll next to the dll, i.e. 2 files, 11.8 MB (SpiralCompiler.dll 0.28 MB + SpiralCompilerCore.dll 11.5 MB), not the 23 DLLs/25.8 MB of lap 1; sha256 of the two takes 83-135 ms warm (Get-FileHash, 3 runs). Frequency: Memo.context runs once per notebook execute (notebook.ex:523), so a 40-file gate rehashes the same 2 DLLs 40 times (~3-5 s per gate), with no cache, unlike kino_identity (memo.ex:235) which keeps its print in persistent_term. Cheapest fix: persistent_term cache of compiler_binaries_print keyed by {path, size, mtime} of each DLL, the kino_identity pattern; soundness holds because a rebuilt DLL changes mtime and size. Next: write that cache in memo.ex plus a memo_test case (touch the dll, expect a re-key) and queue the mix test run in the HEAVY QUEUE; then profile the rest of a warm run (daemon protocol, notebook parse)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_4 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:42 lap 3 grain: the binaries-print cache is prepared as lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo_print_cache.py (dry run clean; patched copies parse with Code.string_to_quoted!). compiler_binaries_print keeps its print in persistent_term under {Memo, :compiler_binaries, dir, [{name, {size, mtime, sha256 of the first 64 KB}}]}, so a notebook execute pays two stats and two 64 KB reads instead of hashing 11.8 MB. Why the head hash and not size+mtime alone (lap 2's plan): the existing test a change to the compiler core re-keys writes core one then core two, same size and the same second, so size+mtime would serve a stale print; the PE header in the first 64 KB carries the deterministic build's content-derived TimeDateStamp, so a real rebuild changes it too, and small fixtures are hashed whole. New ExUnit case: the print is reused while stamps hold and recomputed for a same-size core change. The TODO block at the site now names only the remaining step (memo keys to Spiral after profiling). HEAVY QUEUE: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo-cache-run.ps1 (applies, runs memo_test.exs in the heavy gate with its own MIX_BUILD_PATH, restores both files unless it passes; ~3 min). Next: after it passes, profile one warm 40-file gate for the next per-notebook cost (daemon protocol vs notebook parse)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_5 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:26 lap 4 grain, the next per-notebook cost found by reading memo.ex: Memo.context also runs source_bounds (memo.ex:167-195) on every notebook execute. For a lib notebook it lists the core package (21 files, 53 KB) and the lib package (84 .spi/.spir, 774 KB), reads every file, builds a sha256 key per text from {binaries print, text}, and does one memo fetch per text: a file read plus File.touch on a hit, so ~105 extra file opens and ~105 mtime writes per notebook, and the source-blocks compiler call only for unknown texts. Proxy timing in pwsh (same files, same counts, warm): reading the sources 6-19 ms, 105 memo reads with touches 18-71 ms, i.e. about 25-90 ms per notebook, the same order as the binaries hash being cached now. Cheapest fix, same pattern: keep the bounds of a {binaries print, text sha256} in persistent_term or a named ETS table for the BEAM's lifetime (a gate run reuses the same lib texts across its 40 notebooks), and stop touching memo entries that were read in this BEAM already (prune only needs one touch per run). Next: write that as a second memo patch with an ExUnit case, after the print-cache run lands (same files)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_6 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:43 lap 5 grain: second memo patch written and stacked: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/memo_bounds_cache.py (3 edits to memo.ex + 1 ExUnit case). source_bounds now asks remembered_bounds first: a named public ETS table (:spiral_cells_memo_bounds, created on first use, recreated if its owner died) answers known texts without touching disk; on a miss it falls back to the memo fetch and caches the hit, and freshly computed bounds are inserted next to the store. So within one BEAM (a 40-file gate) each lib text costs one memo read once instead of once per notebook, and the ~105 File.touch calls per notebook drop to the first notebook. Keys stay the existing bounds_key (binaries print + text), so a compiler change still misses. New case: bounds read once survive a cleared memo dir, and a compiler copy that cannot answer still gets them. Stacks after memo_print_cache.py on a copy of today's memo.ex/memo_test.exs (both parse); memo-cache-run.ps1 applies both, runs memo_test.exs, restores unless it passes")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_7 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:27 lap 6 grain: both memo patches LANDED in main's 10:56 run (memo-cache-run, memo_test 26 passed, kept): memo.ex now caches the compiler-binaries print in persistent_term by size+mtime+head hash (memo.ex:158-161) and source bounds in the named ETS table (memo.ex:212-227), which addresses the measured 20-40 ms per context of key hashing. Measure-first for what is left: lab/KINO-HOT-PATHS/warm-profile.ps1 + warm-profile.exs time a cold and a warm run of one lib notebook, then run mix profile.eprof over a warm run (top 60 functions into spiral-bin/runs/kino-hot-paths, not the repo); queued after the switch accept. Progress 30: two Elixir hot paths fixed; the move into Spiral comes after the profile shows the remaining split.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_8 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:45 lap 7 grain: fixed the profiler choice before the run: mix profile.eprof traces only the process that calls it, and Cells does its per-cell work (compile requests, memo lookups, target runs) in spawned tasks, so eprof would have shown mostly waiting. warm-profile.ps1 now uses mix profile.tprof --type time --report total (Elixir's tprof follows spawned processes by default, set_on_spawn, and totals them; checked in mix help on the scoop Elixir). The compiler daemon is a separate OS process and stays out of the profile; its share shows as time spent waiting in CompilerClient. Queue row updated.")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_9 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T16:18Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:18 lap 8 grain: warm-profile.ps1 warm run now passes --metrics (NotebookCli switch, writes tests.tsv with per-target Ms and memo column); warm wall minus the sum of Ms is Kino's own overhead, which the tprof top 60 then splits. Checked the switches (path keep, no-html via html boolean, output-dir) and that optionm'.spi still exists post-switch. Next: read both after the queue row runs")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_10 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:45 lap 9 grain: mapped which notebook parsing is still Elixir: Document.parse_spi and parse_dib already call the Spiral domain (Domain.from_spi, from_spi_bounded with the memoized source bounds, parse_dib, decode_cells), so .spi/.dib parsing is Spiral today; only .livemd goes through Elixir: parse_livemd (document.ex:22, one multiline Regex.split over the whole text plus livemd_part per piece) and to_livemd (render). So the item's notebook parse beyond the existing Spiral domain is exactly parse_livemd/to_livemd. The warm profile row decides whether it is hot (lib notebooks are .spi, so the lib gate does not even reach it; apps/spiral's spiral.livemd export does)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_11 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T17:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:21 lap 10 grain: memo-key binary hashing re-read: compiler_binaries_print (memo.ex:147-167) already caches the full sha256 in :persistent_term keyed by each DLL's size, mtime and a 64 KB head hash, and dir comes from Path.dirname so the wildcard is slash-safe; so the 20-40 ms per context measured on 10-14 is now paid once per BEAM process (installed SpiralCompiler*.dll = 5737984 bytes hashed in full on the first context). A cross-process cache (same stamp key, print stored in the memo dir) would remove even that; the warm profile's tprof total for content_hash decides whether it is worth it. Next: warm-profile.ps1 run when CPU frees up (queued)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_12 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:43 lap 11 grain: sized the one Elixir-side parse (document.ex:22-26, a single multiline Regex.split): Cells sees 4 .livemd files in spiral/apps, 215 KB total (apps/spiral/spiral.livemd 193 KB, the rest under 18 KB), none in lib; one regex pass over 193 KB is a few ms per process, well under the ~200 ms per test overhead, so livemd parsing is dropped as a port candidate (it moves with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, not for speed). Remaining candidates in measured order: the compiler-daemon round trip and mounts/memo keys per context; the warm profile decides")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_13 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 13u32, "2026-10-20T00:11Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:11 first warm profile, measured (bounded job job-1792453609736: lab warm-probe-run.ps1, 10 C-only test cells, reused Cells build): cold 29.8 s, warm 6.5 s with 10 of 10 memo hits; tprof over a third warm run inside the VM puts all of Kino's own CPU at about 0.22 s for the 10 cells (about 22 ms per cell), and about 62% of that is the Gleam-compiled Spiral domain scanning text one character at a time (kino_text_at 30.7 ms, has_char 28.6, spiral_wrap_signed 25.9 over 84k calls, binary.part 25.1, find_char 13.7, find_from 13.0); memo hashing is small (crypto.hash 1.1 ms, hash_update 0.8 ms). So the 200 ms-per-test premise of this item no longer holds: a warm run is dominated by the fixed per-invocation cost (mix and BEAM start, app boot, about 6 s of the 6.5), not per-test work. Two findings: (1) the hottest Kino code is now Spiral itself, i.e. the Gleam backend's per-character binary.part plus an i32 wrap on every index step, so the next hot-path win is codegen (no wrap for loop indices that cannot overflow, or slicing in bulk), not more porting; (2) metrics tests.tsv reports a memo hit's Ms from the stored cold result (sum 26.5 s here, larger than the 6.5 s wall), so Ms is not the hit's cost. Report: spiral-bin/runs/kino-hot-paths/warm-profile-20261019-194652.txt. Next: time a bare mix run in apps/cells to split the 6 s fixed cost, and a Gleam codegen note for index wrapping")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_14 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 14u32, "2026-10-20T00:37Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:37 fixed-cost split (bounded job job-1792456439743, lab fixed-cost.ps1, 3 rounds each, measured while main's pack 12 ran, so noisy): mix run --no-compile --no-start (BEAM and mix only) 2.6 to 6.8 s, median 5.8 s; with the Cells app started 2.3 to 3.9 s, median 3.3 s. So the BEAM plus mix boot alone accounts for the roughly 6 s of the 6.5 s warm notebook run, which confirms the 20:1x reading that per-invocation start, not per-test work, is the warm cost (a long-lived Cells process or the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR direction removes it; porting more per-test code would not). The 3 notebook rounds (19 to 42 s) are inconclusive: this script did not set SPIRAL_CC as warm-profile.ps1 does, and cc is part of the memo key, so they were probably misses; its output was discarded, so the hit count is unknown. Next: rerun only the notebook step with SPIRAL_CC set and memo_hits printed, on an idle machine")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_15 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 15u32, "2026-10-20T01:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:09 fixed-cost rerun on a quieter machine with SPIRAL_CC set (bounded job job-1792457639855, medians of 3): BEAM plus mix 2.2 s, with the Cells app started 2.2 s, mix spiral.notebook on the 10-cell probe 6.0 s with memo_hits 10 of 10 (round 1 was 16.3 s, a cold first round). So a warm notebook run costs about 3.8 s more than booting Cells, about 380 ms per cell, while tprof puts Kino's CPU at about 22 ms per cell: roughly 90% of the warm overhead is waiting, not BEAM CPU (candidates: mount building, memo-entry file reads and touches under Defender, the compiler handshake). Next: print the existing add_phase timings (cells.ex phases) per cell in the metrics TSV so the wait has a name before anything is ported")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_16 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 16u32, "2026-10-20T01:11Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:11 decisive experiment (bounded job job-1792458663308, lab in-vm-rounds.ps1/.exs: three runs of the 10-cell probe inside ONE BEAM via Mix.Tasks.Spiral.Notebook.run, all 10 memo hits each): round 1 3,484 ms, round 2 960 ms, round 3 1,059 ms. With boot at about 2.2 s, a fresh warm run (6.0 s) is about 2.2 s of BEAM and mix start, about 2.5 s of first-run initialisation inside the process (per-process caches: compiler-binary prints in persistent_term, the bounds table, mounts and the like), and about 1 s of real work for 10 cells (about 100 ms per cell, of which about 22 ms is BEAM CPU). So the hot path to remove is per-invocation start, not per-cell code: a long-lived Cells process (or a cross-process cache of the first-run state) would take a warm 10-cell gate from about 6 s to about 1 s. That changes this item's plan from porting memo keys and parsing to Spiral toward a resident Cells server, which fits KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's daemon direction. Next: name the 2.5 s first-run part (time Memo.context and the mounts in round 1 against round 2)")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_17 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 17u32, "2026-10-20T01:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:21 the 2.5 s first-run part is named: lazy code loading. lab preload-rounds.exs (bounded job job-1792459259608) loads the modules of the 20 started applications before the first notebook run: 543 not-yet-loaded modules (of 1,169) take 2,018 ms to load, and the first notebook round after that is 888 ms, the same as a warm second round (970 ms). So a fresh warm 10-cell gate is about 2.2 s BEAM and mix start + 2.0 s loading about 540 modules from disk on first use + about 0.9 s of real work. Remedies, in order of effect: a resident Cells process (pays both once), loading code in embedded mode from a release or archive so the 540 loads become one, then per-cell work last (about 90 ms per cell, roughly a quarter of it BEAM CPU). Progress stays 50; next: decide with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR whether the resident process is a Cells server or the compiler daemon's host")
inl note_of_kino_hot_paths_18 () = Note ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", 18u32, "2026-10-20T01:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 21:31 the 2 s code-loading cost split (bounded job job-1792459860394, lab preload-split.exs): reading the 545 pending .beam files (10.3 MiB) takes 251 ms (201 ms again), loading them takes 2,147 ms. So it is not disk or Defender: it is the VM loading and JIT-compiling (BeamAsm) about 10 MiB of code on first use in every fresh process. Embedded-mode or archive loading would not remove it, only a resident process that pays it once (or a smaller loaded set, for example not loading unused apps). That leaves the resident Cells process as the clear next step for warm gates")
inl eoie_scrum_xp () = Task ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "eoie agile beyond Kanban-style flow (user 2026-10-17: could we also be scrum or xp?). It has Epic|Feature|Story|TaskKind, priority/difficulty, deps, estimate (minutes), load, assignee agent|pair|user, leases/handoff and plan; missing: time-boxed sprints/iterations (start/end, committed items, a sprint goal), velocity from completed estimates, an explicit epic->child relation (today deps/tags), sprint review/retro records, and XP practices as data (pairing already exists as assignee=pair; TDD is the EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC direction; small releases = receipts). Each as a one-call verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile", "current", "current")
inl eoie_memory_links () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "One-call memory/backlog link check (user 2026-10-17: harden the memory+eoie symbiosis): eoie agile links <backlog root> --memory <dir> reports every backlog ID cited in memory files (frontmatter + body), dangling IDs, project/feedback memories citing Done items whose rule may have changed, and backlog items whose notes cite a missing memory slug; --json for agents. Done by hand on 2026-10-17 with a pwsh one-off: 55 memories, 0 dangling, ~12 Done references (mostly historical lessons). Spiral test blocks with fixture memory dirs.", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", "eoie,agile,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_links_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at eoie/src/eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi:140 (above agile_entry, with the dispatch arm to add); eoie agile todos harvests it with id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS.")
inl eoie_memory () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY", "eoie embodies the agents' memory (user 2026-10-17: the name comes from embodiment; absorb ~/.claude/projects/*/memory and the Codex, Grok, Zed, OpenCode and Antigravity equivalents in a typed way, keep only a bootstrap in each client pointing to the eoie tutorial, sync through eoie in git instead of zipping the profile). Typed model in Spiral, natively what Rust fakes with PhantomData: a memory item is a GADT indexed by kind (user | feedback | project | reference), and feedback and project require Why and HowToApply at the type level; the heterogeneous store is an existential pack; client adapters are an HKT-style interface (one per client format); per-kind prototypes give defaults; reflection derives the renderers (markdown frontmatter, JSON, client bootstraps) and the schema, like the Convo tool-schema reflection the study lane built. Found on this laptop 2026-10-17: Claude .claude/projects/C--home-git/memory (56 typed md files plus the MEMORY.md index); Zed AppData/Roaming/Zed/AGENTS.md (rules); Antigravity .gemini/antigravity (brain/, annotations/); OpenCode .local/share/opencode/opencode.db (read a copy; never the credential/account tables); Codex only config.toml (no memory store yet); Grok .grok (no memory store found). Children: EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL,EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS,EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS,EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP,EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "eoie,memory,epic,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread: absorbed the memory-formats lane (9 notes on EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS). New fact for the epic: Grok has a memory store, memory-v2 (topics/*.md: 11 curated topics; observations/_inbox: 19 notes with provenance frontmatter; generated MEMORY.md, never write it), and it also loads ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md through its Claude compatibility setting. Zed, OpenCode, Antigravity and Codex have rules/instructions but no memory store. Claude: 44 feedback, 8 project, 4 reference; every feedback/project item has Why and How; a byte-identical render needs quote style and body segments stored. Privacy: exclude ~/.config/opencode/service.json (plaintext password) and every auth/credential file by name; transcripts (threads, brain, sessions) are never imported. Related DeltaDB idea (Zed, 2026): history that links changes to the agent conversations behind them.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:23 from the bundles (tritcritnet alpha716 docs\\vision\\readme_eoie.md Feb 2026; core alpha9237 readmes Jun; samples alpha705; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): the old vision put memory in receipts, not in the model ('EOIE is the body that makes the brain leave fingerprints'). Concrete pieces missing from the memory epic: a resume compiler that builds the next-turn agenda from the last ledger (passed -> skip/cheap verify, failed dep -> block downstream, expected failure -> tolerated, stale report -> regenerate, partial patch -> repair plan); an incident-resume summary in one call (active lease, newest savepoint, files changed since, newest receipts, toolchain identity); a claim boundary per report (passed/blocked/synthetic/proxy/tolerated); reports projected from one typed receipt so prose cannot overwrite a metric.")
inl eoie_memory_model () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", "Typed memory model + lossless import of Claude's 56 memories: GADT memory<kind>, existential store, reflection-derived markdown renderer; stored as Spiral data in the eoie repo (like state/agile.spi). Proof: import, then render back, must be byte-identical to every source file (frontmatter, body, [[links]]); links resolved and dangling ones reported; Spiral test blocks only", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:44Z", "main-thread", "13:44 main-thread design sketch (not started; needs the user's go): kinds are nominals: nominal user = (), nominal feedback = (), nominal project = (), nominal reference = (). The item is a GADT: union memory kind = | User :: {slug; description; body} -> memory user | Feedback :: {slug; description; rule; why; how} -> memory feedback | Project :: {slug; description; fact; why; how} -> memory project | Reference :: {slug; description; pointer} -> memory reference, so a feedback without why/how doesn't typecheck. The store is existential: union stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, a list or map by slug. Links: [[slug]] parsed to a typed link list resolved against the store; dangling links and backlog IDs reported by check. Prototypes: per-kind defaults by typecase on the index. Reflection: renderers iterate a record's keys at compile time (markdown frontmatter name/description/metadata.type, JSON), the same technique as the Convo tool schemas (lab/convo-in-spiral). Adapters (HKT): a client is a type-level function f with import : path -> f stored and render : stored -> f text. Round-trip proof: the 56 Claude files plus MEMORY.md imported and rendered back byte-identical. Prerequisite checked today: recursive GADTs with grown indices compile since GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG's fix (blessed 111908).")
inl eoie_memory_verbs () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", "One-call verbs: eoie memory recall <query|ids> [--client <c>] (relevant memories for this task, ranked, with related links), eoie memory save <kind> <slug> <description> <body> (type-checked: feedback needs Why/How), eoie memory edit/forget, eoie memory check (subsumes EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS: backlog IDs, dangling links, Done items). Each verb ~20 ms native vs reading 56 files; Spiral test blocks", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl eoie_memory_adapters () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", "Client adapters (HKT-style interface, one instance per client): Claude (projects/*/memory md + MEMORY.md), Zed (AGENTS.md rules; threads db read-only), Codex (AGENTS.md), OpenCode (AGENTS.md/config; db read on a copy, never credential/account tables), Antigravity (.gemini/antigravity brain/annotations: format to study first), Grok (to find). Import (absorb once) and export (render each client's bootstrap or projection). No secrets ever enter the store (x-api-credentials stays a path reference)", Story, P2, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "14:10 Claude Code items (measured): C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\projects\\C--home-git\\memory = 56 item files + MEMORY.md; no other project dir has memory. Bytes: LF, no BOM, one final LF, no trailing spaces, 11 files non-ASCII (UTF-8), 783..11,214 B (sum 113,785; largest spiral-macro-gotchas). Frontmatter, fixed key order: name, description, metadata: {node_type: memory, type, originSessionId: uuid, modified: ISO-8601 ms Z} in 55 files (7 lines); inner-jev-autonomy has metadata: {type} only (4 lines). Then one blank line, then body. name equals filename stem in all 56. description max 206 chars: 48 YAML double-quoted (4 with backslash-escaped quotes), 8 plain (agent-sync-daily plain with raw Windows backslashes): keep a quote-style tag, never re-derive. Kinds: feedback 44, project 8, reference 4, user 0. All 52 feedback/project bodies hold **Why:** and **How to apply:** (51 line-initial, 1 inline); the 4 references hold neither. So the GADT why/how fields are sound, but bodies have prose around them: store body as ordered segments (text | why | how) so render is byte-exact. Links: 111 [[slug]] in 53 files, 39 distinct targets, 2 dangling: delete-dangling-freely (from dangling-check-by-stem) and corpus-check-before-packs (from kino-copy-lib-resolution; that is the MEMORY.md title of lib-smoke-before-syntax.md, a renamed slug: check should suggest it).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Claude index and loading: MEMORY.md = 56 lines + final LF, 9,151 B, no heading/frontmatter; every line is: - [Title](file.md) — hook (U+2014 em dash, single spaces), max 245 chars; all 56 items indexed, none missing. Title is free text, not the slug (Agent count -> max-three-agents.md, Corpus check before packs -> lib-smoke-before-syntax.md): a separate field, and index order is a list. Loading: the harness injects MEMORY.md into every session (seen verbatim in this agent's own context; documented cap first 200 lines), item files are read on demand; the agent writes all of it with file tools. Instructions: no ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, no ~/.claude/rules, no CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md at C:\\home\\git or any repo root; only in-repo docs (spiral/apps/compiler/AGENTS.md 931 B, its tmp/AGENTS.md 68,825 B) and upstream zed (.rules 12,651 B; CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md are 6-byte symlink-placeholder files reading .rules). In-repo AGENTS.md already sync through git: out of scope for import. ~/.claude/settings.json has no memory keys. Bootstrap: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (global, always on), 2-3 lines: memory lives in eoie, read the eoie memory tutorial first, record memories with eoie, not in this folder; and MEMORY.md shrunk to one pointer line so auto-memory stops growing a parallel store. Caution: Grok (compat.claude) and OpenCode (docs: fallback when no global AGENTS.md) also read ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, so keep that bootstrap client-neutral.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Zed: global rules = %APPDATA%\\Zed\\AGENTS.md (paths::agents_file = config_dir/AGENTS.md, zed/crates/paths/src/paths.rs:319), 908 B LF markdown, no frontmatter, one H2 section: Commit message (the customized built-in commit prompt, moved there by prompt_store/rules_to_skills_migration.rs, which appends Default Rules under ## title H2s and turns non-default Rules into ~/.agents/skills/slug/SKILL.md). Loaded into the native agent system prompt of every conversation (always on); no size cap found in crates/agent. Project rules: only the FIRST match of RULES_FILE_NAMES at a worktree root (.rules, .cursorrules, .windsurfrules, .clinerules, .github/copilot-instructions.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md; prompts.rs:22). settings.json (2,575 B JSONC, // header) has no rules or profiles fields; its agent block is model/permissions/dock only: adapter never touches it. Legacy Rules LMDB AppData/Local/Zed/prompts/prompts-library-db.0.mdb is kept by the migration (not memory). ~/.agents absent. threads.db (copy, mode=ro, query_only): one table threads(id TEXT PK, summary, updated_at, data_type, data BLOB, parent_id, folder_paths, folder_paths_order, created_at), 8 rows, all zstd = transcripts. Zed has no memory store. Bootstrap: upsert an H2 section ## Memory with one pointer line into the global AGENTS.md; the adapter is a section-preserving edit keyed by H2 title so the Commit message section stays byte-identical.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "OpenCode: ~/.config/opencode holds only cli.json (v2 schema, UI prefs) and service.json. PRIVACY: service.json is a plaintext service password: never read or import it (deny by name). No opencode.json(c), no global AGENTS.md, no .opencode dir at C:\\home\\git. opencode.db (db+wal+shm copied; sqlite_master and non-sensitive schemas only): account, account_state, control_account, credential (never read), event, event_sequence, instruction_blob(hash PK, value), instruction_entry(session_id, key, value, removed, time_created, time_updated; PK session_id+key), instruction_state(session_id PK, epoch_start, through_seq, initial_values JSON, current_values JSON), kv, migration, permission, project, project_directory, session_inbox, session_message, session_pending, session_v2, workspace, worktree. instruction_* is a per-session content-addressed snapshot of system-prompt parts: the one state row maps core/codemode, core/skill-guidance, core/date, core/environment to 64-hex hashes into instruction_blob (5 blobs, 17..11,778 B); instruction_entry is empty. No memory feature: nothing to import. Loading (OpenCode docs, not verified on this v2 build): ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md plus project AGENTS.md found walking up from cwd, falling back to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md when no global file exists, plus opencode.json instructions globs; all always on. Bootstrap: create ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer (this also stops the CLAUDE.md fallback double-load).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Antigravity (.gemini\\antigravity; antigravity-ide is a same-shape twin, plus antigravity-backup): no memory/knowledge store and no user rules on this machine. brain/conversation-uuid/ is per-conversation working state, not memory: .system_generated/{logs/transcript*.jsonl, messages/uuid.json, steps/n/*.txt tool outputs, tasks/*.log}, .user_uploaded/, scratch/ = conversation content, never import. annotations/uuid.pbtxt is protobuf text (title, last_user_view_time {seconds nanos}); antigravity_state.pbtxt holds onboarding/migration enums and installation_uuid; conversations/uuid.db and conversation_summaries.db are SQLite; agyhub_summaries_proto.pb binary. Rules per the bundled builtin/skills/agy-customizations docs: GEMINI.md and AGENTS.md (no frontmatter, always on) walked up from cwd or edited file to repo root; .agents/rules/*.md with frontmatter trigger: always_on or model_decision; workspace roots .agents/ .agent/ _agents/ _agent/; global ~/.gemini/config/ (here only config.json userSettings, projects/uuid.json, empty plugins and sidecars; no rule file; ~/.gemini/GEMINI.md absent). Limits: 24,000 B per rule file after @[label](path) include expansion, truncated on a line boundary; 20,000-token always-on rules budget, overflow demoted to path pointers. Bootstrap: one global rule under ~/.gemini/config/ (confirm the exact filename in the Customizations UI before the first write) with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: ~/.gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Codex (.codex): config.toml (283 B: approval/sandbox/model, projects c:\\home\\git trust, windows sandbox) has no instruction keys; no ~/.codex/AGENTS.md or AGENTS.override.md; rules/default.rules is exec-policy Starlark prefix_rule(pattern=[...], decision=...) = command permissions, not memory. A memory feature exists but is empty: memories_1.sqlite (copy) has stage1_outputs(thread_id PK, source_updated_at, raw_memory, rollout_summary, rollout_slug, generated_at, usage_count, last_usage, selected_for_phase2, selected_for_phase2_source_updated_at) 0 rows, jobs 0, consolidation_progress 1, _sqlx_migrations 2; no ~/.codex/memories dir (the phase-2 output folder). skills/ holds only .system vendor skills. Loading (Codex docs): ~/.codex/AGENTS.md (AGENTS.override.md wins) plus AGENTS.md from git root down to cwd, always on, combined cap project_doc_max_bytes (default 32 KiB). Import: nothing today; when memories appear, stage1 raw_memory rows are per-thread drafts and the memories folder is the curated output (read that, never the db). Bootstrap: ~/.codex/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: auth.json, .sandbox-secrets/, history.jsonl, sessions/: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Grok (.grok) HAS a memory store, memory-v2 (no memory key in config.toml: on via env or managed setting). Per scope (global/, workspaces/git-437d9324/): topics/*.md curated (11 workspace topics 722..16,945 B, 0 global; LF, no frontmatter: # Title, one-line summary, ## sections, - bullets); observations/_inbox/*.md pending (19; YAML frontmatter schema_version: 2, type, topic_hint, keywords, aliases, session_id, from_turn, through_turn, extraction_model, prompt_version, created_at epoch, job_id, observation_ordinal); archive/dream_hex/ consumed observations named session__tFROM-TO__nK.md (30-day retention); MEMORY.md generated, never write it; index.sqlite and memory_state.sqlite: leave alone. Loading (docs/user-guide/13-memory.md): both MEMORY.md indexes injected once per session, 8 KiB per scope; topics read on demand; capture after each turn into the inbox; /dream or auto-dream folds into topics; the model may edit topics/ and _inbox/ with file tools. Rules (12-project-rules.md, always on): ~/.grok/rules/*.md (absent), ~/.claude/rules and ~/.claude/CLAUDE*.md by compat.claude, [paths] extra_rule_dirs, then Agents.md, Claude.md, CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md from repo root to cwd (cwd only outside git; needs folder trust). Import: topics as a topic kind (title, summary, sections), observations as an observation kind with provenance. Bootstrap: ~/.grok/rules/eoie-memory.md, and either set memory_v2.enabled = false or keep one global topic whose body is the eoie pointer.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Adapter interface proposal: a client is a record over a type-level carrier f (result with a per-client error union, later an io effect): import : root -> f (list stored); render : list stored -> f (list {path; text}); bootstrap : tutorial_ref -> f (list {path; edit}) with union edit = Replace text | UpsertSection {heading; text} | AppendLine text, so foreign content in Zed/Codex/Antigravity AGENTS.md files survives. stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, and beside the kind GADT a source layout record, not part of the kind: {client; scope = Global | Workspace slug; path; quote = Plain | Double; meta = {node_type option; origin_session option; modified option}; body = list segment}. Renderers derive frontmatter from layout so bytes round-trip; normalization is a separate explicit verb. Kinds beyond user/feedback/project/reference: topic (Grok topics), observation (Grok inbox, with session/turn provenance), rule (an H2 section of an AGENTS.md). Indexes are derived: Claude MEMORY.md = ordered list of {title; file; hook}; Grok MEMORY.md is never rendered. Deny-list is a type: import walks only an allow-list of paths per client (Claude memory dir, Zed AGENTS.md, Grok topics and _inbox, Codex memories dir, OpenCode and Antigravity rule files); everything else is unrepresentable rather than filtered.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Round-trip test blocks: (1) 56 Claude items render byte-identical; (2) MEMORY.md byte-identical, em dash and order kept; (3) inner-jev-autonomy keeps its 4-line frontmatter (no node_type/session/modified); (4) double-quoted descriptions with escaped quotes and the plain one with raw backslashes keep their quoting; (5) the 11 non-ASCII files keep UTF-8; (6) the one inline How to apply body survives; (7) a feedback without why fails to typecheck (negative), references without Why/How typecheck; (8) links: 111 parsed, 39 targets, exactly 2 dangling reported, corpus-check-before-packs gets lib-smoke-before-syntax suggested via its index title; (9) Zed AGENTS.md: UpsertSection Memory twice is idempotent and Commit message stays byte-identical; (10) the 11 Grok topics and 19 observations re-render byte-identical; (11) import, render, import is a fixed point; (12) an allow-list test: a tree seeded with service.json, auth.json, .credentials.json, oauth_creds.json yields zero items. Privacy flags: OpenCode service.json plaintext password; .claude/.credentials.json; .codex/auth.json and .sandbox-secrets; .grok/auth.json; .gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json; OpenCode credential/account tables; Antigravity brain/ and every threads/sessions store are transcripts; Grok observations carry session ids and the agent-sync topic a scheduler id; Claude items carry originSessionId (fine in a private repo, strip before anything public); x-api-credentials stays a path pointer.")
inl eoie_memory_bootstrap () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", "Swap to bootstrap (needs the user's go): each client keeps one bootstrap entry ('memory lives in eoie: run eoie memory recall; tutorial at eoie/spec/...') and eoie in git becomes the synced source for the laptop and the work machine; a pull plus 'eoie memory export --client <c>' sets up a new machine. Rollback: export renders the full Claude memory dir again", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 prior art in bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\bundle_eoie_v20260723-alpha70462.zip state\\bootstrap.spi (Sep 2026): eoie's own bootstrap as typed Spiral. bootstrap_transition Boot -> FallbackSelected -> CandidateBuilt -> CandidateVerified -> Promoted; an execution_route chain Candidate -> RecentFallback -> ArchiveFallback -> HostEscapeRejected (host escape is a typed refusal, not an option); promotion only with typecheck AND e2e AND cold differential; rules such as CompilerPinnedByIdentity, FallbackBeforeHostEscape, GeneratedRustIsMirror, CrateUnderBudget. A ready shape for the bootstrap entry and for 'eoie memory export' rollback.")
inl eoie_union_reflection () = Task ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", "Drift from the GPT era (user 2026-10-17: eoie maps unions to numbers; with reflection and the GADT/prototype feature set we could do it in fewer lines): measured 2026-10-17 across 493 eoie .spi files, 16 modules use the pattern: 67 pipe_index/pipe_member/pipe_lookup calls, 79 '| Nu64 =>' arms and 22 literal 'a|b|c|...' name tables. Example: agile subcommands live in 5 hand-synced tables (the union in agile_subcommand_domain, its name string, the u64 index match, agile_full_usage_error's usage text, agile_operand_shapes in eoie_agile_mutation, plus the pipe list in agile_entry); adding a verb means editing all of them. Target: one union per concept; case names by reflection (lib reflection, with case enumeration added to the lib/compiler if it can't enumerate nullary cases at compile time yet); parsing by a fold over the cases; usage and operand shapes in one exhaustive spec : case -> {name; shape; summary} prototype match the compiler checks for completeness. The numeric layer goes away. Proof: eoie tests unchanged, generated crates stay under the 1k gate, LOC before/after per module. Blocks new verbs such as agile links (EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS)", Story, P1, D5, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,drift,reflection,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_1 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 related audits found in the bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 9, compiler alpha3006 agile_eoie.md, Sep 2026): a DU or option flattened into bools/strings and recombined in a later match; one boolean gating two authority domains; a hint field vetoing semantic closure; comparisons by rendered type name where an interned identity exists. Same family as union-to-reflection drift; one lint could cover all of them.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_2 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at eoie/src/agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi (above union agile_subcommand, with the target spec/derivation sketched in Spiral); eoie agile todos harvests it with id EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_3 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:05Z", "main-thread", "15:05 main-thread slice 1 done (unstaged, eoie src): new library package union_reflection/cases.spi (16 lines): union_cases_fold over a union's cases with raw UnionToRecord + RecordTypeFold + NominalCreate inside real (eoie has no core), in declaration order, and union_cases_joined for static text. agile_subcommand_domain now holds ONE exhaustive agile_subcommand_spec match (name + shape per case); names, the usage shape lookup, the full usage error in agile_mutation and the agile line of command_spec help are derived from it. Removed: the u64 index match, the names pipe string, agile_subcommands, agile_operand_shapes and the hand copy inside command_spec usage (4 hand-synced copies of the verb list). Gotchas: no backtick type application in top-down code, annotate lambda params used only inside !!!! ops, and a lookup fold must be let (as inl at 7 sites agile_mutation.rs hit 1567 lines; as let 712 vs 614 before). Publish + build -Test -SkipRelease: 197 Rust + 9 Spiral tests, 0 failed (slice 1a). Next: slice 2 replaces the 3 arity pipe_index dispatch tables in agile_entry with a typed parse (AgileKnown | AgileUnknownName) + exhaustive route match.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_4 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:14 styles-survey [1/2 what eoie re-implemented vs lib/spiral] 0 of 324 eoie package.spiproj list core or lib (x and alphabet use packageDir ../spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-). Re-implementations: (1) reflection.spi (374 lines: get_union_fields(_untag), union_try_pick, union_to_string, nameof, get_record_fields) -> pipe tables: rust_std_string pipe_member/pipe_index/pipe_lookup, 62 call sites in 14 files, 61 numeric u64 match arms in 5 files, 26 literal name tables of 4+ members; 145 lines of that numeric layer inside those 15 files (2,527 lines). (2) sm-prime string module (1,868 lines) -> rust_std_string/model.spi 34 lines (concat5, equals, starts_with, ends_with, contains, replace, trim, trim_end_matches), referenced from 119 files, concat5 called 323 times where lib has ++ and interpolation. (3) core operators -> workspace_primitives/numbers.spi (i32_eq, i32_le, i32_next, i32_prev, i32_text, u64_text): 350 call sites. (4) optionm-prime/resultm (310 + 514 lines) -> generic clones such as validation_result a = Valid | Invalid, quoted_result, separator_result among 31 own outcome/result unions (most are real domain unions and should stay). (5) listm-prime/am-prime -> none: 0 Cons in eoie, collections live in pipe strings and rust_global (390 occurrences; 362 raw macro lines). Rough drop with lib: ~145 numeric-layer lines + ~90 primitive lines + the 5-table sync per concept; the bigger gain is readability (~670 helper calls become operators), not size.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_5 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:15 styles-survey [2/2 blockers and path] (a) No-core packages: without core there is no |>, not, Some/None or bool =, so every eoie package rebuilds them; lib/spiral itself needs |core-. (b) 1k crate gate: lib code is written for inlining, e.g. spiral.rs is 24,280 lines because lib parsing split_args inlines to ~5,000 and union_to_string/trace inline into loops; lib union_try_pick builds a runtime list (fields ++ lowercased fields) and an array per call, while eoie union_cases_fold unrolls at compile time (an inl lookup at 7 sites took agile_mutation.rs 614 -> 1567 lines, a let fixed it). Importing lib wholesale would break the gate unless lib entry points become let. (c) Export boundary: 431 !!!!Export sites pass only i32/string/tuples, so unions are flattened to codes at every crate edge; x does the same (command_fields kind i32 + 13 scalars). Reflection inside a crate cannot remove the codes that cross crates. Path: 1. move union_cases_fold into lib reflection as the compile-time primitive and rebuild union_try_pick/union_to_string on it (fewer generated lines for lib users too); 2. a core-only eoie probe package (|core- + reflection) measuring crate lines before/after on agile_subcommand_domain; 3. compiler: Export of a nominal union as a pub Rust enum (or tag + payload pair) so crate edges stay typed. Without 3, steps 1-2 still remove the 5 hand-synced tables inside each crate.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_6 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 6u32, "2026-10-17T19:22Z", "main-thread", "15:22 main-thread slices 2+3 green (publish of 26 owners + build -Test -SkipRelease: 197 Rust + 11 Spiral tests, 0 failed, 583 s). Slice 2: agile_entry dispatch is a typed parse agile_subcommand_of (reflection fold to AgileKnown subcommand | AgileUnknownName) + one exhaustive agile_route match with per-verb arity; the 3 arity pipe_index tables are gone, and a new verb is a compile error until its spec row and route arm exist. Slice 3: union_case_suffix (Unbox + SymbolToString + StringSlice, inside real) derives case text from the case name: agile_case_text replaces the kind/priority/difficulty text matches, parse_agile_case replaces the 3 pipe_index parsers, validate_status and the task census statuses come from agile_status. Generated Rust over the 26 owners 14,556 -> 13,496 lines (-7 percent); pipe call sites 64 -> 57. Regression caught: agile_mutation.rs 1,166 lines because the inl usage error was copied into 19 cold error arms; now a let (slice 4 run checks the 1k gate with a release build).")
inl dib_modernize () = Task ("DIB-MODERNIZE", "Retire every .dib remnant straight to .spi test blocks, skipping the .livemd stage (user 2026-10-17: the livemd phase that lasted until test blocks was never enough). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 .dib files remain in our repos, 14 files mention them. Kino's F# route (fsi_chain.ex and fsi_session.ex resolve #!import x.dib from the dotnet-repl era; ~15 tests build .dib notebook fixtures); polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd imports lib/fsharp/Notebooks.dib and Testing.dib, which no longer exist (dead); history mentions in apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md and eoie ingest_frontier's port-now classifier. Delete the route and fixtures, or port what's still live", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kino,polyglot,migration", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_1 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at spiral/apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/fsi_chain.ex:184 (at the .dib import route); eoie agile todos harvests it with id DIB-MODERNIZE.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_2 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:41Z", "main-thread", "14:41 main-thread scope measured. Kino .dib surface: Document.parse_dib is the default parser for any unknown notebook extension (notebook.ex parse), cli_export falls back to the old dib-export CLI subcommand, the domain exports parse_dib (spi/document.spi and main.spi), directives.spi has a dib error kind, and fsi_chain.ex resolves #!import x.dib (TODO block placed there). Tests: 21 use .dib fixtures (notebook_test 13, display 3, fsi_session 3, notebook_report 1, document 1); 15 are Spiral notebook-runner behaviours and 6 exercise the F# FSI route. Many test the notebook-to-.spi export feature itself (spi is written only when the cells pass). So the modernization is architectural: .spi is the only source (test blocks are the cells), notebooks are output only (ipynb/html), and the .dib/.livemd parsers and the interactive F# kernel retire together (also the bold-native-goals direction). Order: (1) port the 15 Spiral runner tests to .spi fixtures with test blocks; (2) drop parse_dib and dib-export; (3) decide the FSI kernel (polyglot Perf.livemd and the i574n.github workflow livemd still have cells: port them to .spi or scripts); (4) drop parse_livemd. Each step is proven by the Kino suite (~2.5 min).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_3 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured against today's tree: Kino now lives at spiral/apps/cells (apps/kino is gone; the TODO block is in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/fsi_chain.ex). .dib surface: notebook.ex:464 still falls back to Document.parse_dib for any unknown extension; mentions per file document.ex 3, domain.ex 2, fsi_chain.ex 7, fsi_session.ex 2, notebook.ex 2, spi/document.spi 1, spi/main.spi 1. Tests touching .dib: notebook_test 12 (spiral cells accumulate..., a failing cell still writes the ipynb..., an fsharp notebook runs through the host callback, host cells share the job slots, rust contract flags reach the target, a package missing beside the notebook ..., the spiral lib package is mounted once, a //> _ cell is not compiled..., cells compile together, a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, --real cells become the main_real module, at most four cells run at a time), fsi_session 7 lines, display 1, document 1, notebook_report 1. Step 1 (port the Spiral-runner tests to .spi fixtures) therefore covers 10 of the 12 notebook_test cases; the fsharp host-callback and host-slots tests belong to step 3 (FSI kernel).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_4 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: first port drafted: lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/accumulate_test_port.exs is the .spi version of notebook_test.exs's first case (spiral cells accumulate, tests keep opens, and spi is written only when they pass). Mapping: the #!spiral cells become a .spi with a test demo static prelude (open testing), a plain definition (inl value) and a targeted test block value_is_one; the assertions that survive are the semantic ones (the prelude alone is never compiled; the test program carries both the prelude's open and the earlier definition; an .ipynb report is written), while the spi-export assertions (demo.spi written only when cells pass, without opens or calls) disappear because the .spi is now the source. It reuses the test module's own helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). Not wired into the suite yet.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_5 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:28 helper-lane lap 3: second case mapped: notebook_test.exs:80 (a failing cell still writes the ipynb and does not write spi) needs no new .spi test, because notebook_report_test.exs:30 (a .spi run reports each test block and a failing block fails the run) already asserts the same semantics on a .spi: error message =~ exited 1, the .ipynb is written, its metadata counts failed 1. The spi-not-written half disappears with .dib (the .spi is the source). The only assertion it lacks is the failing cell's output ename SpiralError; that 4-line assertion is staged as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/failing_case_assertion.exs, to append after notebook_report_test.exs line 60 when the ports are wired (unverified whether .spi runs set ename, so it rides the same queued Cells-suite run as the accumulate port). Ports needed so far: 1 new test (accumulate) + 1 assertion; 8 of the 10 Spiral-runner .dib cases remain to map.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_6 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:32 helper-lane lap 4: ported cases 3 and 4 of notebook_test.exs as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/flags_package_ports.exs (not wired, same helpers as the file: run_value, rustc_ok): (3) rust contract flags reach the target (notebook_test 218) becomes a .spi whose ///> rust -cd near-token line sits on a test block; the target callback must see tool rust, contract empty, deps near-token at *, and a source without ///> lines that carries both definitions; the old two-cell split (the flagless prior cell compiled alone) has no .spi counterpart, so that assertion drops. (4) a package missing beside the notebook (notebook_test 272) is the same test on note.spi with ///- --package vendor/pkg as its header: the mounted package.spiproj must list pkg-. Both keep only behaviour the .spi path has. Mapped so far: 4 of the 10 Spiral-runner cases (1 new test + 1 assertion + these 2).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_7 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane lap 5: mapped the remaining six Spiral-runner .dib cases, so step 1's plan is complete: (5) the spiral lib package is mounted once (308) - same test on a .spi with ///- --package <lib>, the mounted spiproj must list spiral- exactly once; (6) a //> _ cell is not compiled and still exported (336) - .spi form is a block with ///> _ above it; keeps the not-compiled assertion, drops the export assertion (the .spi is the source); (7) cells compile together (370) - .spi with two test blocks, asserting their compiles overlap; (8) a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, a --test cell's do not (404) - already .spi-native as test x static = versus test x =, port 1:1; (9) --real cells become main_real (443) - .spi with a .spir sibling or ///- --real blocks, keeping the main_real*- spiproj and the no-real-in-top-down assertions; (10) at most four cells run at a time (508) - ten test blocks in one .spi, same 2..4 concurrency bound. Totals: 2 cases need no port (2 is covered, 6 loses its export half), 8 become .spi tests (1, 3, 4 drafted in lab; 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 mechanical). The F# host-callback and host-slots cases stay for step 3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_8 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane: wrote ports 5, 6, 7, 8 and 10 as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/remaining_ports.exs (lib mounted once, ///> _ block not compiled, blocks compile together with overlapping compiles, static block definitions reach later blocks while plain ones do not, at most four blocks at a time), using only the notebook_test.exs helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). With the accumulate port, the ename assertion and the flags/package ports, everything but the --real case (9, needs a .spir sibling fixture) is drafted. Unrun: they wait for one coordinated Cells change with lane 2 and a mix test.")
inl kino_rename () = Task ("KINO-RENAME", "Rename the Kino project folder and code to something generic (user 2026-10-17 dislikes the kino name), timed with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR: the supervision part rewritten in Spiral compiling to Gleam 1.19 (the new compiler) removes Elixir from the repo, and that native core should give the name. Parallel or performance-heavy parts (rayon-style) compile to Rust. Precedent: the X client started as apps/spindle and became x/apps/client (xc); no memory recorded that rename, and backlog items X-CLIENT-THREADS and X-CLIENT-AGENT-JSON still say spindle", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,naming", "current", "current")
inl note_of_kino_rename_1 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 kino-native-design naming. The native core runs cells (notebook cells and test blocks, which document.spi already models as cells) through a supervised compile/run tree for many backends. Candidates, all free under spiral/apps, spiral/lib, lib/spiral (modules and bench/leptos/near/rust/target) and the C:\\home\\git repo names: cells, foreman, loom, kiln, workbench, crucible, proving. Avoid: lab (spiral/lab exists), bench (lib/spiral/bench), harness (compiler harness), relay (the removed Kino relay), runner (clashes with the Runner module). Recommendation: cells, i.e. apps/cells, Gleam package spiral_cells, Spiral.Kino.X becoming cells modules, CLI cells run path, env SPIRAL_CELLS_*: a plain role name like the spindle to x/apps/client rename, and it names the unit the core schedules, not the BEAM. Runner-up: foreman, if a name for the supervision core itself is wanted. Rename surface found: 13 non-source files (AGENTS.md, compiler test.ps1, apps/kino/spi run_suite/run_notebook, lab/harness-run-only-useful, formatter PLAN, README, apps/spiral/build.ps1, lib/spiral/build.ps1, near wallet build.ps1, tibia WORKFLOW, scripts/init.ps1, core.ps1) plus SPIRAL_KINO_* env vars, kino-PORT.lock, kino-gleam-warm cache dirs and the memo identity (a rename re-keys the memo once). Time it with the delegate-removal end of KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR so files move once.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_2 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:43Z", "main-thread", "15:43 main-thread, user 2026-10-17 decision: the new name is cells (apps/cells, Gleam package spiral_cells, SPIRAL_CELLS_* env), the kino-native-design pick. Timing stays as designed: the folder and code move once, when the last Elixir delegates are removed (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), so files are not moved twice; the rename itself is backlog work to plan now (13 files besides source, env vars, lock and cache names, memo identity = one cold run).")
inl note_of_kino_rename_3 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 3u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decisions 2026-10-17 (later round): rename to cells NOW, before the Elixir migration, and hard-cut every old name (SPIRAL_KINO_* env vars, cache and lock dirs) with no aliases; all consumers move in the same change, old caches go cold once.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_4 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: do the cells rename now, in today's run until 18:00; if it is not green by 17:50, restore from the backup and report.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_5 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 5u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done in the tree (unstaged; hard cut, no aliases): spiral/apps/kino -> apps/cells; Elixir Spiral.Kino.* -> Spiral.Cells.* (lib/spiral/cells, test/spiral/cells, cells.ex, cells_test.exs, cells_live_test.exs), mix app spiral_cells, Gleam package spiral_cells (src/spiral_cells), env SPIRAL_KINO_* -> SPIRAL_CELLS_* and EOIE_SPIRAL_KINO -> EOIE_SPIRAL_CELLS, cache names kino-* -> cells-*, daemon banner and socket spiral-kino -> spiral-cells, lock/log names, internal identifiers. 112 files in 7 repos (spiral, eoie, x, i574n.github, polyglot, dice, alphabet; the consumer repos reach spiral through symlinked deps) + 8 in a second pass. Livebook's own kino library (Kino.*, the kino dependency, mix.lock) keeps its name; the backlog state and eoie state/ history are untouched. Proof: mix compile ok; mix test 245 tests, 1 failure from a missed escaped literal (\\tKino), fixed and rerun ok; eoie publish + release build with its Spiral tests through apps/cells: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Old spiral-bin kino-* cache dirs are now stale (cold run once); DISK-CLEANUP can drop them.")
inl lib_usage_diagnostics () = Task ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", "Tooling to diagnose lib/spiral usage across our repos: which lib functions and test cells are never reached from any app, sample or repo; duplicated and generated code (the CLI lane found that ~9k of spiral.rs's 24k lines come from inlining the same parser 3 times); and expansion hot spots per generated crate, so the 1k-line packaging limit stays useful. Part of the branded tool suite (with the formatter); reuse and grow lib instead of per-app copies", Feature, P1, D5, 40u32, Active, "", "lib,tooling,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:39Z", "main-thread", "14:39 main-thread first census (text-based, job tmp lib-usage.ps1 and lib-usage.tsv, 96 s over every tracked .spi/.spir in spiral/eoie/polyglot/dice/alphabet/x): lib/spiral has 1,361 top-level inl/let definitions in 70 modules. 41 are never referenced anywhere, 20 of them FParsec-style trailing-underscore variants in fparsec.spi (p_string_, many1_satisfy_, sep_by1_, not_followed_by_ ...: strong deletion candidates), plus seq 5, testing 4 and listm' 3. 671 are used only inside their own file (candidates for private helpers or inlining). Limit of the text method: backend.run_target shows as unused although it is used everywhere, because it comes into scope by a route a grep can't see. So the real tool must ask the compiler's name resolution (e.g. a --references mode next to --source-blocks that emits definition -> use sites per package) and report per repo, per package and per lib cell; later, generated-code duplication per crate for the 1k gate.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: inlining sprawl is wanted where it buys speed, so the tool must not just count expanded lines. Classify each expansion as hot or cold and measure it: an A/B per backend (inl vs let for the same definition) through the harness benchmark rows. Measured today: eoie agile_subcommand_shape, a reflection fold over 18 cases, was an inl called from 7 usage-error sites, and agile_mutation.rs went 614 -> 1567 lines. That is cold code (error paths), so the copies gain nothing at run time and cost rustc time and the 1k gate; as a let it is emitted once. Hot loops are the opposite case: specialization removes dispatch and allocation. Report per crate: expansion factor, hot or cold by benchmark evidence, and a suggested inl/let flip. Longer term the compiler could outline cold paths itself (like Rust's cold attribute).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_3 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:19 main-thread: (1) text census v2 in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1: resolves package.spiproj flat modules (backend-, base-, common-, trace-), open lines and qualified module.name uses, separates test-block uses, verdicts unused/tests-only/file-local/lib-only/used-outside (running). (2) compiler-backed references, in the tree, not built: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT=<file> makes prepass (shared section) record def rows at FInl/FRecInl and use rows whenever a RawV resolves outside local scope, linked by E object identity, flushed at process exit; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 aggregates them. Plan: a compile-only harness run with the variable set (no -ReuseCompile) gives every sample's real resolution, then diff the two censuses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_4 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:16 main-thread text census v2 (lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1, 781 s at BelowNormal over 1,985 tracked .spi/.spir in spiral, eoie, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, tibia): 1,408 lib definitions in 80 modules: used-outside 404, lib-only 254, file-local 401, tests-only 118, unused 231. Flat modules, open lines and qualified uses resolved, so run_target is used-outside (95 lib uses, 2 outside), which v1 called unused. Unused by module: async 25, rust 22, threading 19, runtime 19, leptos 18, sm' 17, file_system 10, listm' 8. Spot check of 5 async ones (base_let', block_on_futures_lite, block_on_async_std, block_on_tokio_send, stream_collect_tokio): each occurs only in its own header and definition across all 7 repos, so they are real. Fixes made on the way: ordinal (case-sensitive) indexes, operator counts from a per-file token table. Next: the compiler-backed census (qualified uses recorded since the 08:5x source) to cross-check, then deletion candidates per module.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_5 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T14:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:21 SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT census over the full compile-only suite (668 s): 1,422 lib defs; unused 639, lib-only 400, file-local 376, used-outside 7. The used-outside count of 7 conflicts with the text census v2 (404). Suspected cause: use rows carry tree-copy sample paths that the aggregator classifies as lib or file-local. Check the path normalization in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 before trusting the unused column.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_6 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T14:27Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:31 used-outside=7 explained, and it's not a path bug. Of 38,282 use rows, only 19 come from outside lib and target lib/spiral. The compile-only samples import their own samples/core (10.7k rows) and the VS Code Plugin core in deps (7.8k), not lib/spiral. So this suite is the wrong corpus for outside use. Next run: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over the consumers (Cells gate on lib, blog engine, eoie, apps/*), then aggregate. Second gap: 7,016 outside use rows have no target. Those are names reached through open (create/set/index after open am) and nominal constructors (cell_array, f64_array), which PrepassReferences does not attribute yet. Until it covers open-resolved names and nominal defs, the unused column overcounts.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_7 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T14:28Z", "main-thread", "Correction: note 6 was written at 10:27, not 10:31 (the eoie stamp 14:27Z is the right one).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_8 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:32Z", "main-thread", "10:32 patch plan for the reference recorder's open/nominal gaps (spiral_compiler.fs; not applied, no build today). (1) Local open: prepassModule_open (~52893) folds the opened module's members into env.term.env. RawV (~53148) records a use only when the name is absent from env.term.env, so every name reached through a local open is dropped as if it were a local. Fix: record whenever definitions.TryGetValue e succeeds, and keep the env.term.env guard only for unresolved rows (locals are EV values and are never in definitions). (2) Unattributed defs: FNominal/FNominalRec (~53314) and FPrototype (~53337) add terms without PrepassReferences.define, so constructors such as cell_array/f64_array and prototype names stay unresolved; call define on each nominal constructor's term and on the prototype's EForall. (3) Still unexplained: top-level open am uses of create/set in samples main.spi, unresolved although 226 other rows resolve to deps core/am. Check whether package re-prepass rebuilds E (identity loss) by dumping both sides on one sample. Verify with: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over one sample that uses a local open plus a nominal, expect 0 unresolved rows for those names; then rerun the consumer corpus census.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_9 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 9u32, "2026-10-18T14:34Z", "main-thread", "10:34 plan steps (1) and (2) applied in the tree, unbuilt: PrepassReferences.used_unless_local (RawV records a use when its E is a known def, even if the name is in env.term.env, which covers local open), and nominal_rec plus FPrototype call PrepassReferences.define (constructors at the nominal's name range). Changes only the recorder, gated on SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT. Verify in the next pack: one sample with a local open and a nominal, then the consumer corpus census. Step (3), identity loss on top-level open am, is still open.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_10 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 10u32, "2026-10-18T14:35Z", "main-thread", "10:35 step (3) resolved, and it's not identity loss. The unresolved create/set/index uses in samples main.spi (package core-) are prototypes: samples/core/operators.spi defines them as prototype plus instance (instance create array = ...). Before today's edit FPrototype never called define, so the in-tree FPrototype define covers them. Expected after the next build: the unresolved outside rows (7,016) drop to roughly the builtin and type-level names.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_11 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 11u32, "2026-10-18T14:39Z", "main-thread", "10:39 lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 now prints the unresolved use rows grouped by name. Today's run: 11,966 rows over 1,886 names. The top names are module heads (sm' 520, optionm' 292, leptos 284, resultm 264, am' 216, sm 181, listm 172) and union constructors (Nil 474, Cons 375, None 211, Some 180, Ok 144). Constructors are covered by the in-tree nominal define. Module heads get a new in-tree recorder rule: used_unless_local skips EModule values, since the member is already recorded through module_member. This is also unbuilt. Next build check: rerun references.ps1 and expect the top unresolved names to be builtins and type-level names only.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_12 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 12u32, "2026-10-18T15:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:42 main-thread: the recorder edits from note 9 onwards (used_unless_local, define on nominal constructors and prototypes, skipping EModule heads) were pushed by the user at 15:29Z in f5e487e0; spiral CI compiles them now, the first build of them anywhere. New in the tree since then, unbuilt: the ProcessExit flush retries File.AppendAllLines up to 100 times at 20 ms on IOException, because parallel compiler processes appending to one SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT file could lose a process's rows. New lab script lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 runs the consumer corpus: the Cells --gate over the 40 lib/spiral .spi files that have test blocks plus the 9 blog engine modules, in chunks of 20 paths (mix.bat command-line limit), with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process so every compiler inherits the variable, memo off, a temp MIX_BUILD_PATH. It throws if no rows were recorded (compiler without the recorder), then runs references.ps1. Run it after the next install that contains the recorder.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_13 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: the recorder edits (used_unless_local, nominal constructors and prototypes, module heads skipped, and the ProcessExit flush retry) were built and installed with compiler pack 4 (2026-10-18 22:55 build, accepted 10-18 single-flight-20261018-234436) and ride pack 5; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 turns SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT into the census Next: HEAVY QUEUE: one census over the Cells lib build with SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT set, to list never-reached lib definitions")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_14 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:58 lap 2 grain: verified the installed compiler carries the recorder (UTF-16 literal SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT found by byte search in spiral-bin/bin/single-flight/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0/SpiralCompilerCore.dll, built 10-18 22:57; note: a naive Unicode.GetString search misses it at an odd byte offset). The HEAVY QUEUE row for this item is concretely pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 (lib files with tests + blog engine + x test files, chunks of 20, process compiler, memo off). Edited the script to throw right after the first chunk when no reference rows were recorded, instead of after the full corpus, so a recorder-less compiler costs one chunk of the heavy slot, not the whole run. Next: after the run, read consumer-census.tsv and list never-reached lib definitions per file")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_15 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:43 lap 3 grain: references.ps1 now prints the census goal directly: never-reached lib definitions grouped per module, most first, with their names in line order (cut at 160 chars), right after the verdict tally, so the queued consumer-census run ends with the list this item asks for. Checked on the 10-18 census file (pre-fix recorder, lib-only corpus, so only indicative): unused 639 of 1,422 definitions, top modules leptos 46, runtime 45, parsing 41, async 40, sm' 36, physics 30, rust 30, am' 30; real-dialect defs are recorded (sm'_real show_real lib-only 3, format_real 2 + 1), so .spir modules are not falsely unused. Next: after the heavy census, compare unused per module against the 10-18 numbers and turn the top modules' unused names into deletion candidates (dangling check by stem across repos first)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_16 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:09 lap 5 grain: first deletion candidates checked by stem across every repo under C:\\home\\git (spi, spir, livemd, dib): of the 17 sm' names the 10-18 text census called unused, the definitions index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode occur only at their own header and definition in sm'.spi (plus the matching /// headers in sm'_real.spir); format_custom' and encoding_1252' are already gone; the rest are operator names (~#, ~##, ++\\\\#...) that need the compiler-backed census. Not deleted: lib is frozen in the core switch window, and the switch may move sm' itself. The queued census row is made concrete: pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 after the switch accept (counts on the old core would be stale at once).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_17 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:28 lap 6 grain: stem check extended to runtime and file_system (the 10-18 census's unused lists): all 29 names occur only in their own lib module across every repo under C:\\home\\git; the only outside hits are look-alikes with their own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy nominal process_exit_code, tictactoe 2023 lib_spi copy of path_display). Candidates: runtime 19 = the clap builder leftovers (arg_overrides_with, arg_num_args, arg_value_name, value_parser_path_buf, value_parser_expr, matches_get_occurrences, matches_values_of) and the .NET Process wrapper family (new_process, process_start, process_exit_code, process_has_exited, process_kill, process_begin_error_read_line, process_begin_output_read_line, process_add_output_data_received, process_add_error_data_received, process_wait_for_exit_async, event_data) plus execute_retry; file_system 10 = file_stream, directory_info_exists, directory_info_creation_time, file_move, path_buf_from, path_buf_strip_prefix, path_display, path_buf_is_symlink, get_directory_name, init_trace_file. With lap 5's 7 sm' names: 36 verified deletion candidates, all waiting on the switch all-clear (lib frozen) and the census to confirm no route the text scan misses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_18 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: the recorder is built and installed, the consumer census and references.ps1 are ready, and 36 deletion candidates are verified by stem; left: the compiler-backed census after the switch, then the deletions. Progress 40.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_19 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain: stem check on async + threading (40 plain names from the 10-18 unused list). 38 occur only in their own module across every repo; the futuresales livemd hit is Elixir code, not Spiral. Two are false positives of the text census: cancellation_token (runtime.spi 198, 210, 835, 1115; networking.spi 150) and mutex_guard_ref_mut (runtime.spi 918-1005) are used, most likely in type position, where a term and a type share one name and the text scan counts only one of the two. That is one more case for the compiler-backed census. Running candidate total: 7 sm' + 29 runtime/file_system + 38 async/threading = 74 definitions with no use anywhere, the two false positives removed.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_20 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:19 lap 8 grain: post-switch re-check of the 36 sm', runtime and file_system candidates on today's migrated lib: each still occurs exactly at its header and definition (sm' also its _real.spir header), and no .spi/.spir/.livemd under C:\\home\\git gained a use; the only outside hits are own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy and proxy_failure_classification process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi copies) and backlog text. So the switch did not change the candidate list. Next: stage lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default) for the 36, applied after the compiler-backed census confirms")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_21 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 21u32, "2026-10-19T16:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:44 lap drive8 grain: deletion staging for the 36 verified candidates: lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default). Per name it finds the single /// ### header, requires the next line to be that definition, takes the indented body plus trailing blank lines, and re-checks that the name occurs exactly twice in its file; --apply writes only if every name passes (all or nothing, LF kept). Dry run on today's lib: 36 definitions, 209 lines (sm' 57, runtime 95, file_system 57), 0 problems; spot-checked builder_append_line 775-794 ends before builder_replace. Apply order: compiler-backed census confirms, evidence note, --apply, then lib Cells --gate over sm', runtime, file_system plus an apps/spiral compile (heavy queue)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_22 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 22u32, "2026-10-19T16:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:46 lap 9 grain: delete-unused.py now covers all 74 verified candidates: added the 38 async and threading names (census.tsv verdict unused, minus the false positives cancellation_token and mutex_guard_ref_mut and the 4 primed names the stem check did not cover). One had no /// ### header (async.spi default_cancellation_token right after cancellation_token), so the script now also takes a lone bare definition and expects 1 occurrence instead of 2. Dry run: 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_23 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 23u32, "2026-10-19T17:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:23 lap 10 grain: post-switch recheck plus a by-stem dangling check across every sibling repo (dangling-check-by-stem rule): delete-unused.py dry run still 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems; a word-bounded search for all 74 names over C:\\home\\git\\*\\**\\*.spi,spir,livemd finds hits outside lib in only 4 files, all local homonyms, not lib consumers: eoie bootstrap_policy/outcome.spi and proxy_failure_classification define their own process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi/rust/util.spi:527 defines its own path_display (its own lib copy), and a futuresales livemd output line mentions an Elixir start_child. So the text census and the cross-repo stem check agree. Next: compiler-backed confirmation (consumer-census.ps1, heavy, queued), evidence note, apply, lib Cells --gate + apps/spiral compile")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_24 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 24u32, "2026-10-19T17:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:44 lap 11 grain: joined delete-unused.py's 74 (module, name) pairs with the compiler-backed references-census.tsv of 10-18 (SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT rows through references.ps1): all 74 are present and all 74 have verdict unused with SameFile 0, Lib 0, Outside 0. With today's text dry run and the cross-repo stem search (0 consumers) that is three independent views agreeing; the one gap is that the compiler census predates the core switch and covers the builds it was fed, so consumer-census.ps1 (lib test files + blog engine + apps, heavy, queued) remains the post-switch confirmation before --apply")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_25 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 25u32, "2026-10-19T22:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:48 lap 19 grain: consumer-census.ps1's corpus (lib tests, blog/engine, x) leaves out dice, alphabet and polyglot, which main migrated to core v1 at 18:3x; a whole-word text scan of their 43 .spi/.spir/.livemd files (deps/target/dist skipped) finds 0 uses of any of delete-unused.py's 74 candidates, bare or qualified, so those three repos add no keeper. Next: the compiler-backed consumer-census run (a full lib gate, main's queue), then delete-unused.py --apply")
inl eoie_state_scale () = Task ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", "eoie backlog state bloat is intended (user 2026-10-17): state/agile.spi is now 1.15 MB and grows with every note; keep it as a realistic append-heavy log to simulate an Apache Iggy-style stream and measure how the compiler scales on real log data (parse/typecheck time of state/agile.spi per MB). Track the growth curve; refactor only if a measured threshold breaks a workflow", Story, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,scale,iggy", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_1 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (1/3) state/agile.spi measured read-only at 14:41 (before the IGGY-EVAL notes): 1,203,199 bytes, 1,588 lines, longest line 18,177 chars (the EOIE-DOGFOOD title). Rows: 337 Task (556,734 bytes, avg 1.65 KB), 1,040 Note (623,301 bytes, avg 600 B), 170 Planning; the rest is ~40 lines of union schema. Notes per UTC day: 10-14 306, 10-15 363, 10-16 118, 10-17 253 so far. Size over git commits: 10-08 43 KB, 10-09 53 KB, 10-12 163-180 KB, 10-13 313-416 KB, 10-14 686 KB, 10-15 791 KB, 10-17 06:53 996 KB, 13:00 1,091 KB, 14:45 1,213 KB: about 150-270 KB/day lately, so ~6-8 MB in a month if linear. Write path (eoie src/agile_note_model/model.spi): a note is not appended at EOF but spliced after the task's last note (notes_insertion), the whole text is re-read, compared to the original and replaced atomically (state_written; StateChangedSinceRead = optimistic concurrency, nothing written, caller retries); note counts and insertion points come from full-text scans. So every write is O(file) read + scan + rewrite. Receipts today: note writes 8-10 ms at 1.2 MB, show 4 ms: cheap now. Already broken threshold: the file is ~66k tokens, over the agent Read tool's 25k-token limit, so agents cannot read it whole (eoie agile show is the only path). typecheck_receipts.spi last attested agile.spi at 994,972 bytes.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_2 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (2/3) What to measure (no runs done by this lane). M1 compiler: wall time and peak RSS of the installed compiler's parse + typecheck of agile.spi (eoie agile check --compiler and the compiler's own phase timings), cold (session torn down, per BENCH-FRESH-PAIR) and warm, over the 11 git snapshots 43 KB..1.09 MB plus the live file, plus synthetic 2x/4x/8x copies (bindings renamed, same row mix). Fit time = a * MB^b: b > 1.2 flags superlinear behaviour; split the cost by driver (top-level binding count ~1,550, longest line 18 K chars, total string literal bytes) with single-variable synthetic files. M2 eoie verbs: elapsed_ms of note, set, show, list --json per snapshot (all receipts already print it), p50/p95 over a day of real use. M3 growth: bytes/day and notes/day from note timestamps (a one-line census; the ledger could log it daily). M4 contention: count StateChangedSinceRead rejections; today they are not recorded anywhere, so the note/set receipt should log retries.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_3 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (3/3) Thresholds that would warrant an Iggy-style segment layout (any one, measured twice): compiler check of state over 5 s warm or 15 s cold, or b over 1.3 at 4x size; peak RSS over 1.5 GB for the check; an agile write verb p95 over 100 ms; file over ~8 MB (about a month at the current rate) or a line over 64 KB; more than 1 StateChangedSinceRead retry per hour across lanes. Layout then: state/agile/ with an active segment for current task rows (projection) and append-only note segments sealed at ~1 MB or per month (notes appended at EOF, never spliced), each sealed segment immutable with its own typecheck receipt (typecheck_receipts.spi already keys modules by bytes + hash, so sealed segments are typechecked once and skipped), a small offset index per segment (item id -> note seqs) so show reads one index instead of scanning, and readers keeping their own offsets. To keep the scale signal after a split, freeze the monolithic file at each threshold crossing as a benchmark corpus, so compiler scaling stays measurable on real log data, which is the point of this item.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_4 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: measure first (11 git snapshots + 2x/4x/8x copies, warm/cold typecheck time and memory) and switch to the segment layout only when a threshold breaks (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, worse-than-linear growth); the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus.")
inl iggy_eval () = Task ("IGGY-EVAL", "Evaluate integrating Apache Iggy (persistent message streaming) in our projects: typed GADT/existential messages with reflection-derived codecs could make multidimensional sagas (compensations indexed by state at the type level); candidates: eoie job registry and receipts (EOIE-JOBS), the matrix run ledger, Kino cell events, xsim. Local workaround and evaluation only (no upstream contact)", Story, P3, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "iggy,messaging,research", "current", "current")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_1 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (1/7) Apache Iggy state 2026-10-17. Top-level ASF project since 2026-08-19. Latest server 0.9.0 (blog 2026-09-21; Rust SDK crate iggy 0.11.0, CLI 0.14.0); earlier 0.8.0 2026-04-22, 0.7.0 2026-02-24. 0.9.0: VSR (Viewstamped Replication) clustering inside the standard iggy-server binary, opt-in via cluster.enabled, single node default; per-topic durability (replicated default, persisted = quorum on stable storage), enforce_fsync removed; breaking: 0.8 data dirs not migrated, 0.8 clients cannot talk to 0.9 (until 1.0 every upgrade = fresh data dir). Model: stream > topic > partition, segmented log on disk under streams/s/topics/t/partitions/p, retention via per-topic message_expiry and segment_size, server-stored consumer offsets, consumer groups, optional per-message headers, no server-side compression. Transports TCP (binary, port 8090), QUIC 8080, HTTP REST 3000, WebSocket 8092, TLS on all. Server is thread-per-core shared-nothing on compio + io_uring since 0.6. License Apache-2.0. SDKs: Rust, Python, Node, Java, C#, Go, PHP (no Elixir). Benchmarks (v0.7 blog): ~1,000 MB/s writes at 8-32 producers, P95 1.5-1.8 ms, reads 3,361 MB/s. Sources: github.com/apache/iggy, iggy.apache.org/blogs/2026/09/21/release-0.9.0/, iggy.apache.org/blogs/2026/02/27/thread-per-core-io_uring/, iggy.apache.org/docs/server/configuration/, release-catalog.apache.org/iggy/")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_2 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (2/7) Cost on our 16 GB laptop (doc-based estimates, nothing measured). Defaults are server-class: memory_pool size 4 GiB budget, transfer_served_cache_bytes_max 2176 MiB per shard, cpu_allocation numa:auto with pin_cores=true (one shard per core, pinned) - would fight the heavy gate's 2 slots and memory admission. A laptop config must cap it: one shard, pin_cores false, pool ~128-256 MiB, segment_size ~16-64 MiB, durability replicated (single node), BelowNormal priority; idle RSS undocumented, guess tens of MB. Windows: the server's io_uring design is Linux-first; no doc states native Windows server support (compio has an IOCP driver, unverified for iggy-server), so realistically WSL or Docker, which collides with the no-WSL-until-workflow-passes rule. Client side is fine: the Rust SDK and the HTTP API work anywhere. Pre-1.0 churn: wire and disk formats broke at 0.9, so anything we store must be re-derivable. An always-on daemon is also a new liveness problem for EOIE-JOBS to watch. Net: a server process is not justified today; the value is in the log model (segments, offsets, consumer groups, headers), which we can adopt without the server.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_3 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (3/7) Integration map A (gain vs plain append file). (1) Run ledger %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv: 102 rows, 30 KB today, written by heavy.ps1 Write-RunLedger with AppendAllText + 5 retries from many lanes. Iggy would give atomic multi-writer append, a live tail for watchers and a stored offset per reader (cost-report). Gain low: a per-reader offset file next to the TSV gives the same. (2) Harness results.tsv per run (~6,900 rows/run, ~1,100 run folders): the fast-tier fold cache stamping each run by size+mtime is a hand-made consumer offset, and its remaining ~10 s is statting 1,096 folders. A run-index topic (one event per finished run: id, suite, mode, results sha) consumed with a durable offset removes the walk. Gain medium; keep the folders as the content-addressed artifact, log only index events. (3) eoie job receipts (EOIE-JOBS): best fit. A job is a saga (recorded > queued > admitted > running > exited|timed_out|semantic_failure > reaped); completion notification = consumer wake-up, stop-by-id = command topic, restart = replay from the last offset; write the record before spawn = produce before act. Gain high in design, but eoie must stay one-call and serverless, so an embedded segmented log is the fit, not an iggy-server.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_4 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (4/7) Integration map B. (4) eoie agile notes: notes are events (id, seq, at, author, text) stored as a projection; an append log + projection would remove the whole-file rewrite and StateChangedSinceRead retries between lanes (see EOIE-STATE-SCALE); keep agile.spi as the compiler stress corpus. Gain medium. (5) Kino cell/test events: verdicts, memo hit/miss and per-target ms live in ipynb metadata.spiral_test and ledger notebook rows; a cell-event topic would fan out to cost-report and xsim with replay. Gain low-medium. (6) agent-sync: each client re-parses other clients' transcripts (jsonl, SQLite copies); a sync topic with one consumer group per client replaces the read-last-150-rows rule, HTTP transport works from any client. Gain low, volume tiny. (7) xsim (x/apps/sim) already simulates an at-least-once, out-of-order stream: World dedups by delivery index (seen set), buffers events whose parent is missing (pending/drain), reassembles k-of-n frames; Log writes JSON lines. Iggy as a real transport would test those invariants against real redelivery: best testbed; no Elixir SDK, so HTTP API or a Rustler NIF over the Rust crate. (8) CI artifacts (CI-OFFLOAD): runners are ephemeral Linux (iggy native there) but results return as files anyway; ship segment files as artifacts, replay locally. Gain low.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_5 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (5/7) Typed saga sketch, part 1 (Spiral-like pseudo-syntax). Axes as phantom indices: nominal unbuilt = () ; nominal built = () ; nominal untested = () ; nominal tested = () ; nominal undeployed = () ; nominal deployed = (). One GADT, each case moves exactly one axis and states its guards on the others: union rec step b t d = | Start :: step unbuilt untested undeployed | Build :: forall t. step unbuilt t undeployed -> dll_sha -> step built untested undeployed | Test :: step built untested undeployed -> results_sha -> step built tested undeployed | Deploy :: step built tested undeployed -> release_id -> step built tested deployed. Illegal moves do not typecheck: Deploy on step built untested d has no case; rebuilding resets the test axis by its result type. Compensations are typed per step as inverse cases: | Rollback :: step built tested deployed -> step built tested undeployed | Untest :: step built tested undeployed -> step built untested undeployed; there is no Unbuild from deployed, so undo must go through Rollback first. Undo order: a journal indexed by a type-level stack, union rec journal s = | Empty :: journal nil | Push :: forall s x. journal s -> x -> journal (cons x s), and undo only accepts journal (cons x s) with x's inverse in scope, so compensations run strictly LIFO.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_6 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (6/7) Typed saga sketch, part 2: wire and codecs. Existential envelope: union envelope = | Envelope :: forall b t d. saga_id * u64 offset * step b t d -> envelope; producers erase the indices, a consumer recovers them once at the decode boundary: the Iggy header carries axis tags (build=built, test=untested, deploy=undeployed) plus a schema hash, and a typecase over the tag triple picks the payload decoder; an unknown triple is the only runtime rejection, everything downstream is total. Reflection codecs: payloads are records, and reflection over record keys derives the JSON (or fixed binary) codec and the schema hash from key names and field types, so a renamed field changes the hash and old segments are decoded by the old codec (keep codecs per hash, never migrate in place). Multidimensional: N axes = N indices; a step that touches two axes (Deploy needs tested) states it in its argument type; orthogonal axes (lint, docs) advance concurrently because their cases are polymorphic in the other indices (forall t d). Partitioning: saga_id is the partition key so one saga stays ordered; the consumer group per axis lets build, test and deploy workers scale separately while the GADT keeps the cross-axis order. Prototype/HKT use: one generic saga runner over any step family f b t d with a per-family compensation prototype instead of hand-written runners.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_7 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (7/7) Recommendation. Do not install iggy-server now (Windows server support unverified, WSL rule, pre-1.0 format churn, server-class defaults on a 16 GB laptop). Adopt the model instead, in order: (a) EOIE-JOBS: an embedded Iggy-shaped log inside eoie: stream/topic/partition folders, fixed-size segment files with an offset index, one offset file per consumer, message headers (kind, axis tags, schema hash), written before spawn; the job saga typed as in notes 5-6. (b) Harness run-index topic so the fast-tier fold reads from its offset instead of statting ~1,100 folders. (c) xsim gets a transport seam (file log today, Iggy HTTP later) so its dedup/out-of-order invariants can run against a real broker on a Linux CI runner where iggy is native, as a CI-OFFLOAD job, no laptop daemon. Keep the on-disk layout compatible with Iggy's concepts (not its bytes, which break pre-1.0), so swapping in iggy-server later is a transport change. Revisit a real server when one of these holds: a second machine or Linux box consumes our events live, more than ~3 concurrent writers contend on one log, or Iggy ships 1.0 with documented Windows support. Sources in note 1.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_8 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:50Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:50 helper-lane lap 1: checked the revisit triggers of note 7 on 10-19: Apache Iggy's latest release is still server-0.9.0 (2026-09-18; repo pushed today, no 1.0, Windows server support still undocumented), no second machine consumes our events, so do not install stays right. Sized adoption (b), the harness run-index topic: spiral-bin/runs now holds 1,908 run folders (1,764 with results.tsv), created at 17-48 per day this week (48 on 10-17 and 10-18), so the fast-tier fold's per-run stamp scan grows by ~50 folders a day; the fold cache already makes reruns incremental (54 s -> 10 s, AGENTS.md), so a run-index log would save the remaining stat pass, not the mining. Adoption (a) lives in EOIE-JOBS (eoie lane, Active 60%), (c) in xsim/CI-OFFLOAD.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_9 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:35 helper-lane lap 2: settled adoption (b) with a measurement: the fast-tier fold's per-run stamp pass (list the single-flight run folders, stat results.tsv size and mtime, probe fast-tier.tsv) over today's 1,153 single-flight runs (1,041 with results.tsv) takes 1.2 s cold-ish in PowerShell, against a fold that takes ~10 s incrementally (AGENTS.md). At ~50 new runs a day that grows by ~0.05 s per day, so a run-index log (Iggy-shaped or not) would save about a second per fast-tier call - not worth a new storage format. Drop (b) from the adoption list; (a) EOIE-JOBS' embedded log and (c) the xsim transport seam remain the only candidates, and no revisit trigger has fired.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_10 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:39 helper-lane lap 2: DONE as an evaluation (the item asked to evaluate integrating Iggy, not to integrate): decision recorded with evidence - do not install iggy-server (pre-1.0 format churn, server-class defaults on a 16 GB laptop, Windows server support undocumented; latest still server-0.9.0 of 2026-09-18), adopt the model only where it pays: (a) an Iggy-shaped embedded log inside EOIE-JOBS (eoie lane's item) and (c) a transport seam in xsim for a Linux CI broker run (CI-OFFLOAD); (b) the harness run index was measured and dropped (stamp pass 1.2 s over 1,153 runs). Revisit triggers stay as in note 7 (a second machine consuming events live, more than about 3 concurrent writers on one log, or Iggy 1.0 with Windows support); any of them reopens this as a new item.")
inl ci_offload () = Task ("CI-OFFLOAD", "Use the GitHub Actions minutes freed by dropping Fable (builds no longer need them, even on the NEAR projects) for heavy processing that spares the laptop: full matrix suites with cached compile-memo/native-memo, benchmarks, Kino lib suites, bundle mining. Results come back as artifacts the local tools read (run ledger rows, results.tsv). Learn first from the recent Anthropic Scion case and the DeltaDB release (research notes on this item)", Story, P1, D5, 40u32, Active, "", "ci,benchmark,offload", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_offload_1 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread research 2026-10-17. Anthropic Scion (engineering post 2026-10-13; thenewstack.io/anthropic-scion-git-replacement): Anthropic moved its monorepo off Git to an in-house system after Claude pushed merges from ~2,500/day (May) to 30,000+/day (Sept); its CPU and memory needs are about 200x below the Git mirroring it replaced; a team of ~7 built it in ~5 months, and it is not released. Zed DeltaDB (zed.dev/blog/delta-public-beta, Delta public beta 2026-09-16): extends Git's content-based versioning with incremental deltas and stores the agent conversations and instructions behind each change, while a commit stays the checkpoint you push, pull and build. Lessons for us: (1) agent-scale work needs content-addressed, incremental state; our compile/native memos and reproducible builds are the same idea applied to the harness, and CI can run them cheaply on cached memos; (2) link work history to the conversations behind it (DeltaDB): eoie notes with --as lanes already do this per item, and EOIE-MEMORY plus commit links would make it per change; (3) offload heavy runs to CI only where the result is content-keyed and replayable locally.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_2 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:26 main-thread first slice: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ci-runs.ps1 [-RunId N] downloads the compiler job's compiler-oracle artifact (EXPECTED.tsv + runs) of the newest completed schedule/workflow_dispatch run into <cache>/runs/ci-<id>, where compare-runs.ps1 can diff a Linux CI run against a local one. Tried on 39121783429: it fetched the formatter report and the per-worker compile results; that run died before results.tsv (SetErrorMode, fixed in the tree), so the first full comparison waits on the next green nightly.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_3 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: first slice ci-runs.ps1 is in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts; blocked on a scheduled run that reaches the oracle upload: none fired on 10-19 by 12:2xZ and the 10-18 one died at SetErrorMode (fixed in HEAD 668568ac) Next: after the 10-20 scheduled run, ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against today's pack 5 run (Linux vs Windows diff)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_4 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:00 lap 2 grain: schedule reliability measured from gh run list -R i574n/spiral --workflow gh-pages.yml: cron is 17 6 * * * (06:17Z) and the compiler job runs only on schedule or workflow_dispatch; the 10-18 schedule run started 11:04Z (4h47m late) and the 10-19 one has not started at 12:59Z (6h42m late, maybe dropped: GitHub sheds cron runs under load). The last 12 runs hold exactly one schedule run (39121783429, failed at SetErrorMode, artifact compiler-oracle 239 KB without results.tsv). So the offload path cannot rely on cron alone: a workflow_dispatch after each push the user wants scored (user-triggered; agents never trigger runs) or a second cron hour would make the Linux comparison predictable. Decision recorded for the user: none needed from agents; ci-runs.ps1 already accepts workflow_dispatch runs. Next: when any schedule or dispatch run with results.tsv exists, run ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against single-flight-20261019-072348")
inl note_of_ci_offload_5 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:45 lap 3: FIRST LINUX-VS-WINDOWS COMPARISON DONE. The 10-19 schedule run fired at 13:15Z (6h58m late), compiler job 39258592236 ran -Suite all -Native on HEAD 668568ac and uploaded compiler-oracle with results.tsv (7,007 rows); ci-runs.ps1 -RunId 39258592236 fetched it into spiral-bin/runs/ci-39258592236, and compare-runs.ps1 against the accepted pack 5 run single-flight-20261019-080524 gives 12,720 differing cells. Classified: (1) 12,4xx are no-toolchain on the runner (Kotlin 452, Swift 451, Lua 448, Lean 447, Wasm 443, Delphi 406, Bend 401, Wgsl 50, Calyx 28): the CI job installs only BEAM, bun, zig and python. (2) Linux exit-code semantics: zig_exit_wide exits 49 (305 mod 256) on C/Rust/Zig/Gleam/Python while F# reports 305, so the only DISAGREE is F#; C abort() is 134 (SIGABRT) where Windows gives 3, so managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and abi_external_buffer_length_negative turn agree-fail on 5 backends. (3) Rust build-fail on 5 array_* rows: rustup raced installing +1.99.0 under -Parallel 2 (could not rename downloaded component). (4) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# (root and all_terminals) exits 0 on Linux but 1 on Windows. (5) RESIDUAL differences between platforms: Swift 75 and Kotlin 75 (closure_pair*, c_self_tail_loop_heap, c_deep_list_decref ...), Ebpf 17 (ebpf_bacc, facc, nest3, nested, t2, t3, tacc, tworec ...), OpenQasm 6 (ebpf_t3, tacc, native_tail_recursion, tail_loop, ts_tail_loop, python_self_tail_call_depth): the same compiler source emits different text on Linux, which contradicts byte-reproducible output and needs its own item. Fix applied for (3): scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler now reads the matrix rustc toolchain from test.ps1 (regex on the Rust Invoke-Native line; finds 1.99.0 at HEAD and nightly-2026-10-18 in the tree) and runs rustup toolchain install once before test.ps1 (parse-checked; needs the user's push). (2) and (5) are test.ps1 / compiler matters for main. Next: open the residual-divergence question by diffing one Ebpf output (ebpf_tacc) between platforms once the artifact carries outputs, or regenerate it on WSL when WSL is allowed")
inl note_of_ci_offload_6 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:14 lap 4 grain: made the residual divergence diagnosable. The compiler job writes every output next to its sample (and accepts), so the runner's checkout already holds the Linux outputs; the artifact never carried them. gh-pages.yml compiler job: new always() step git diff --no-color -- apps/compiler/tmp/samples > \\/samples-output.diff, and the compiler-oracle artifact now uploads that file (YAML parse-checked; a run: step, so actions.lock is unaffected). ci-runs.ps1 prints the number of committed outputs that differ on the runner, grouped by extension, when the diff is present. Together they show the exact Swift/Kotlin/Ebpf/OpenQasm text that differs between Linux and Windows (75/75/17/6 residual hashes on run 39258592236). Needs the user's push; the next schedule or dispatch run carries it. Next: read the first samples-output.diff and classify each divergence (platform-dependent codegen vs path or newline leaks)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_7 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:12 lap 5 grain: narrowed the Linux-vs-Windows residual divergence by joining CI run 39258592236 against single-flight-20261019-080524 on rows that compile ok on both sides: Ebpf 17, Kotlin 75, OpenQasm 6, Swift 75, and the Swift and Kotlin sets are IDENTICAL (closure_pair*, method_pair*, tuple_pair, nested_tuple, record_value, tail loops, managed_string_*, native_string_utf8_*_source, wgsl_record_loop ...), while C, Rust, Zig, F#, Gleam, Python agree on the same samples. Ruled out: the compiler source (tree spiral_compiler.fs equals 668568ac, the commit CI built; sample .spi inputs unmodified) and CRLF (spiral_compiler.fs and the committed outputs hold 0 CR bytes, so multi-line literals cannot carry a CR). Suspects: the four divergent codegens are the newest, adjacent ones (codegenKotlin 170288, codegenSwift 171050, codegenOpenQasm 171797, codegenEbpf 172097) and share local name tables, so the cause is in shared helpers used only there (e.g. culture-dependent text or ordering; ICU on Linux vs NLS on Windows). The uncommitted samples-output.diff artifact step (lap 4, needs the user's push) shows the exact text. Next: read that diff and check the shared helpers of the four codegens.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_8 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:29 lap 6 grain: the Linux-vs-Windows triage is one call now: lab/CI-OFFLOAD/classify.ps1 -Local <run> -Ci <ci-run dir> joins results.tsv rows and classifies each differing row (no-toolchain-on-runner, toolchain-install-race, residual-only, oracle-only, exit-mod-256, abort-exit-134-vs-3, behavior) with per-backend counts and lists the unclassified ones. On run 39258592236 vs single-flight-20261019-080524: 3,171 differing rows = no-toolchain 3,126 (the Kotlin/Swift residual divergences hide inside these rows because their compile still runs), residual-only 23 (Ebpf 17, OpenQasm 6), oracle-only 6, exit-mod-256 5, abort 134-vs-3 2, toolchain race 1, behavior 8. The 8: 4 Rust array_* build-fail (the same rustup race, detail truncated before could not rename), mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# exit 1 local vs 0 Linux (x2), and NEW: native_cube_flush_delay_direct and native_cube_monotonic_delay_direct are C build-fail (Known) on Windows but build and exit 42 on Linux, so their Known entries are Windows-only facts and should carry that scope. Promote classify.ps1 next to ci-runs.ps1 once the user's push lands the diff artifact.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_9 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain, refines lap 6's NEW item: the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C Known entries already say why (POSIX-only residual, poll.h and clock_gettime; native C runs only on Linux), and the Linux run confirms it: both build and exit 42 there. So the matrix is right, and the gap is that a Known entry cannot name a platform: on Linux these rows ran and agreed, yet the Known entry still matches by id and backend whenever such a row is DISAGREE or build-fail, so a Linux regression on them would be masked. Proposal for main (test.ps1/matrix are main's): an optional Platform key on Known entries (Windows or Linux, compared with IsWindows) next to the existing Oracle scope, and Platform = 'Windows' on these two. classify.ps1 then reports them under their real verdict on Linux.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_10 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:20 lap 8 grain: lap 5 suspects checked by reading codegenKotlin and codegenSwift (spiral_compiler.fs 170352-171849): every float and int literal goes through ToString with InvariantCulture (R for floats), generated method and closure names are ASCII-only (generatedMethodName/generatedClosureName 161549), method and closure tags come from Dictionary insertion order (dict.Count), and there is no sort, culture compare or Environment.NewLine in those codegens; the only StartsWith calls take ASCII prefixes. So culture-dependent text is ruled out for the Kotlin/Swift residual divergence. Next: the samples-output.diff artifact after the user's push; if it shows name or order changes, look at closureBindingNames and the structural method/closure keys")
inl note_of_ci_offload_11 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:47 lap 9 grain: the Platform proposal from lap 7 is staged as lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform.py for main (test.ps1 and matrix.psd1 are main's files): Get-ScopedKnown (test.ps1:1002-1006) is the only place Known entries are filtered (both oracles go through it), so one Where-Object clause adds an optional Platform key (Windows or Linux, from IsWindows) next to the Oracle scope, and the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C entries (matrix.psd1:29-30) get Platform = 'Windows'. Dry run: 3 anchors match once, LF. Also checked: no new spiral CI run since 39258592236 (13:15Z schedule on 668568ac), so classify.ps1's split stands")
inl note_of_ci_offload_12 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:24 lap 10 grain: classify.ps1 rerun of CI 39258592236 against the pack-7 review single-flight-20261019-124451: 4 of the 9 behavior rows were the Rust toolchain race in another spelling (rustup: missing manifest in toolchain 1.99.0 ... installation was interrupted, array_cell and 3 more array samples), so the toolchain-install-race class now matches that text too (5 Rust rows). Behavior left: 5 rows = the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows (known-platform.py, dry run still 3 anchors once), plus mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and its all_terminals on Fsharp (local exit 1, CI exit 0; no C or Zig row, so no oracle catches it) and zig_lib_console Fsharp (residual and stdout differ). No newer CI run since 39258592236. Next: main applies known-platform.py with a pack; someone checks why the local F# mega row exits 1 (fsi stderr in the review's row detail)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_13 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: explained the 2 F# mega behavior rows (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and all_terminals, local exit 1 vs CI exit 0): ran the committed main.fsx with the matrix's exit wrapper in dotnet fsi (one process, scratch copy): Win32Exception, An error occurred trying to start process /bin/sh, at main.fsx line 14, i.e. the sample's own F# macros (main.spi:8042, 8202, 8389: external-recovery, fenced-lock and cross-process programs) spawn /bin/sh, which exists only on the Linux runner. So these rows are platform-bound like the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows: a Platform = Linux scope (known-platform.py's mechanism) or a Windows-scoped Known entry for the 2 F# rows would make local and CI agree; no compiler bug. Behavior left unexplained: zig_lib_console F# only")
inl note_of_ci_offload_14 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:07 lap 12: known-platform.py is applied (test.ps1 Platform filter, 2 cube C rows Windows-scoped). Staged lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform-fsharp.py: the existing all_terminals Fsharp Known entry gets Platform = Windows (its F# macros spawn POSIX programs); dry run matches 1 anchor; queue row for main")
inl note_of_ci_offload_15 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 15u32, "2026-10-19T18:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:32 lap 13: all 5 behaviour rows of CI 39258592236 against the pack 7 accept are now explained: 2 cube C rows (Windows-scoped in the tree), 2 F# mega rows (POSIX programs; known-platform-fsharp.py staged) and zig_lib_console Fsharp, whose committed main.fsx printed a bool through _.ToString() (True) while the uncommitted compiler emits an explicit true/false (git diff main.fsx line 9), so that row resolves with the push. Next: classify the first CI run after the push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_16 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:44 correction to the 14:07 and 14:32 notes: test.ps1 applies a Known entry only to a DISAGREE or build-fail row (test.ps1:1033-1036), and the two F# mega rows have no oracle row, so a Platform scope on all_terminals would change nothing; known-platform-fsharp.py is withdrawn (queue row marked) and deleted. And the cube C and F# mega rows keep differing field by field after the push, so behaviour 0 was wrong. Fix in the lab tool instead: classify.ps1 reads lab/CI-OFFLOAD/platform-bound.tsv (4 id/backend rows with reasons) and classes them platform-bound; against the pack 7 accept it now reports platform-bound 4 and behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console, which the push resolves)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_17 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 17u32, "2026-10-19T22:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:50 lap 19 grain: no push since 02:48 (latest CI is still the 09:15 schedule run 39258592236), so classify.ps1 was rerun against the newest accepted baseline, pack 9 (single-flight-20261019-161549), instead of pack 7: 4,433 of 7,007 CI rows differ and all are classed: no-toolchain-on-runner 3,126, oracle-only 1,168, residual-only 122, exit-mod-256 5, toolchain-install-race 5, platform-bound 4, abort-exit-134-vs-3 2, behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console Fsharp, which the push resolves). So packs 8b and 9 add no CI-visible behaviour change. Next: classify the first CI run after the user's push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_18 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 18u32, "2026-10-19T23:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:28 lap 21 grain, what to offload first, measured from today's heavy-gate ledger (spiral-bin/ledger/runs.tsv, 584 jobs, 22.3 h of run time, 3.2 h of queue wait): Kino/Cells notebook runs 6.8 h (297 jobs; pack_sim.spi alone 41 min in 20 runs, date_time.spi 41 min in 7), eoie release pipelines 5.2 h (106 jobs, several 26-45 min installs), suite accepts 4.2 h (7 runs of 43-64 min), reviews 1.7 h, censuses and sweeps 1.7 h, probes and candidate builds 0.9 h. An accept is the best first candidate on paper (deterministic, must reproduce a review row for row) but CI lacks 7 native toolchains (3,126 no-toolchain rows in the last classify), so a CI accept cannot replace a local one; Kino notebook gates are the larger and more portable share (C, Rust, Gleam, F#, Zig all exist on the runner), so the first offload should be a Cells lib gate job whose metrics TSV comes back as an artifact. Next: draft that job's inputs and artifact shape (no workflow edits or triggers from this lane)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_19 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 19u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:45 cells-gate-ci.ps1 checked locally (bounded job job-1792457040116 through lab cells-gate-ci-local.ps1, which only adds the heavy gate, a private MIX_BUILD_PATH and SPIRAL_CC): -Match routes.spi gated 1 file in 57 s and wrote the artifact folder as designed, cells-gate.tsv (routes.spi 1 discovered, 1 executed, 1 passed, 0 failed), metrics-0/tests.tsv and gate.log. So the CI step script works end to end on one file; a CI job only needs the compiler build that workflow.ps1 -Compiler already does, then this script over all 40 files and an upload of the folder. Next: the user wires the job (workflow edits and actions.lock are theirs)")
inl todo_as_source () = Task ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", "Destructive migration, to try autonomously with backups (user 2026-10-17): spread // TODO: blocks through the code on purpose as the source of truth for work, multi-line and holding Spiral code (the 120-column formatter allows dense blocks), harvested by eoie agile todos instead of a separate backlog file. Iterate on copies with periodic backups in tmp until it's state of the art. Open design points: how a TODO block links to an eoie item ID, how status and progress live in the block, the formatter's handling of them, and the no-comments rule (TODO blocks become an allowed marker kind)", Epic, P1, D7, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,todo,migration", "current", "current")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_1 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "main-thread", "14:20 main-thread dry run (non-destructive; backlog state backed up to job tmp todo-source/agile.spi.20261017-142038.bak; mapping in todo-source/mapping.tsv): of 332 items (162 open), only 32 open items cite a source file that exists, spread over 22 files, and 8 of those point at lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, which is prose. The other 130 open items (epics, research, cross-cutting work) have no code home. A full move into code TODO blocks would orphan ~80% of the open backlog, so the measured design is a hybrid. Code-anchored tasks become typed top-level todo blocks, parsed like test blocks (the compiler already skips top-level 'test' blocks in both BlockParsing sections, so a 'todo ID P1 D3 = <multi-line Spiral>' block can hold typecheckable Spiral and costs nothing in a build); everything else stays in eoie state. eoie agile todos then harvests both into one view, and the block's ID keys its notes. Next step, autonomous with backups: the compiler/formatter accepts todo blocks (one regression sample), then the 32 code-anchored items move into their files, rolled back by restoring the backup.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_2 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:31Z", "main-thread", "14:31 main-thread: step 1 landed in eoie and is published. eoie agile todos now harvests TODO blocks. A full-line TODO comment absorbs the following comment lines whose leader starts at the same column, keeping their indentation, so multi-line Spiral survives; a blank line, code, a directive or another TODO ends the block. TODO(ID): yields an id field in --json (only when present), and json_escaped now also escapes CR and LF. Lives in src/agile_todo_model/model.spi (new test todo_blocks_keep_their_spiral_lines_and_ids; 6/6 model and 3/3 todo_cli tests pass). The first build broke the 1k crate gate (todo_lines.rs 685 -> 1,024 lines: json_escaped was inlined 3x in finding_json and comment_starts_line twice). Making json_escaped, todo_id, finding_json, comment_starts_line and todo_free let join points brought it to 846. Release built, topology checks passed, eoie.exe published. End to end on a fixture: the block prints with its Spiral lines, and JSON carries id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS. Next: join harvested IDs with backlog status in agile todos, then move the 32 code-anchored open items into blocks (backup in job tmp todo-source/).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_3 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: first migration slice. Three items with an exact code home now have TODO(ID) blocks: EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi, target shape in Spiral), EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS (eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi) and DIB-MODERNIZE (kino fsi_chain.ex). Backups are in job tmp todo-source/*.bak. The generated .rs is unchanged (comments). Finding: the automatic file mapping is weak (titles cite files in passing, e.g. SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM -> scripts/build.ps1), so placement needs per-item judgment; items in the Tibia lane and design-prose targets were left alone. Usage note: agile todos takes the backlog root first, then the paths.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_4 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:19Z", "main-thread", "15:19 main-thread next grain, designed not built: agile todos joins each TODO(ID) block with the backlog row of that ID (the scan already gets the backlog root as its first operand) and prints the status after the id, plus tally stale=N (block whose item is Done) and unknown=M (no such item). Placement: a new small crate agile_todo_status (status lookup by the Task anchor + agile_task_text tuple_field 6, a typed todo_status union Open | Stale | Unknown), because agile_todo_lines is at 846 of the 1000-line gate and agile_todo_scan at 678. JSON adds a status field next to id.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_5 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 15:5x: tag format is // TODO: ID text (tag right after the colon, IDE TODO patterns respected; the ID is the leading upper-case token with a dash; TIBIA, HOPACSOTA and BLOG are the only dashless IDs, never used as tags). Done today: agile todos joins each tagged block with its backlog row and reports stale (item Done) and unknown (no item) blocks, via a new census export eoie_agile_task_status.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_6 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: tags also accept a single upper-case word (TODO: TIBIA ...), so dashless IDs work. Stale tags leave the code by a deterministic rule (Jira-style state change + comment): agile todos --apply removes a block whose item is Done and archives its text as a note on that item; an unknown tag creates the item from the block's first line instead of being deleted; open tags stay. Without --apply it only reports, exit 0.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_7 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: an item created by agile todos --apply for an unknown tag gets triage defaults: Story, P4, D3, Planned, tags todo,triage, title cut from the block's first line to 3-7 words; the TODO stays in code as an open tag.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_8 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done (unstaged): tag format // TODO: ID text, with a single upper-case word accepted too; todo_id lives once in agile_todo_lines and is exported (eoie_agile_todo_id) so agile_todo_scan stays under the 1k gate (it hit 1,079 when the parser was copied into it); the status join prints stale/unknown lines and stale=N unknown=M; both existing tags migrated (agile_mutation EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS, cells fsi_chain DIB-MODERNIZE). Release build: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Next: agile todos --apply (remove Done blocks into notes, create triage items for unknown tags).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_9 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:28Z", "main-thread", "19:28 main-thread --apply deferred on evidence: the repos hold 2 tags today, both open, so it would do nothing yet. Design ready: agile_todo_scan is at 971 of the 1,000-line gate, so the standing join (status lookup, stale/unknown lines, tallies) moves into a new crate agile_todo_standing that scan calls once with (backlog text, rendered findings, apply flag, root). Apply only touches blocks whose TODO line starts with a comment (a trailing tag after code is reported, never cut); the block extent is the finding's line plus its continuation lines up to the next path:N prefix. Stale: cut the lines, then add a note via a new export of agile_task_notes note_added (author todos-apply, body = path:line + the block). Unknown: create the item via a new export of agile_task_creation agile_add with the triage defaults (Story, P4, D3, tags todo,triage, title = text after the id cut to 7 words). Build when the first stale or unknown tag appears.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_10 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 10u32, "2026-10-18T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:08 main-thread migration slice: 3 open items now live as TODO blocks at their code sites, harvested by eoie agile todos (stale 0, unknown 0): FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL above lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir format_real, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE above lib/spiral/date_time.spi date_time_switch, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR at the top of apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/runner.ex (its first strangler slice). With DIB-MODERNIZE and EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS that is 5 tags across the repos; --apply becomes useful once some of them close.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_11 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: harvest health: eoie agile todos over spiral/lib and apps/cells/lib finds 4 TODO blocks (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE in fsi_chain.ex:184, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR in runner.ex:1), stale 0, unknown 0; note: todos with only the backlog root scans 4 files and finds 0, so callers must pass code paths Next: move one more open item to its code site (KINO-HOT-PATHS above memo.ex's key hashing)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_12 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: KINO-HOT-PATHS now lives as a TODO block at its code site, above compiler_binaries_print in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex:145 (comment only, 2 lines under 120 columns, carrying the measured cost and the cache plan). eoie agile todos over spiral/lib + apps/cells/lib: files=141 todos=5 stale=0 unknown=0 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR). Side finding: runner.ex:1's block line is longer than 120 columns; the formatter does not wrap comments, so the 120-column rule for TODO blocks is by hand. Next: move one more item whose work is a single code site (candidate: SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS at spiral_compiler.fs UnboundTypeName, about line 50208)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_13 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T13:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:46 lap 3 grain: RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE now has a TODO block at its single remaining code site, dice/contract/build.ps1:50 above the cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 wasm build (2 lines of 117 columns: why the pin exists and the lift being probed); eoie agile todos over dice/contract: todos=1 stale=0 unknown=0. First TODO block outside spiral, so a harvest over all items needs the dice root too: callers pass code roots explicitly (lap 1 note), and the set to pass is now spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib and dice/contract. SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS was skipped on purpose: its site is spiral_compiler.fs, and a comment there forces a compiler rebuild while main packs builds. Next: when CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES settles, move CELLS-GATE's failed_targets plan to summary_lines in notebook.ex")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_14 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T14:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:27 lap 4 grain: SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT moved to its code site, dice/scripts/init.ps1 above the polyglot link (2 lines, 117 and 120 columns: what still routes through polyglot and the prepared rewrite). eoie agile todos over dice/scripts + dice/contract: todos=2, stale=0, unknown=0; rewrite-deps.ps1's anchors still match (dry run unchanged at 22 files), since the block sits above, not on, the anchored line. Harvest roots in use now: spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib, dice/scripts, dice/contract. Open design point answered by practice: blocks for items whose fix is a pending lab patch go on the line the patch will change (here the link line the rewrite keeps and the deps paths it rewrites), so applying the patch is also the moment to cut the block. Next: CELLS-GATE and CELLS-REPIN are unblocked now that CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES is Done (helper lane 1, 100), but their patches are queued; no block until they land")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_15 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:52 lap 5 grain, one open design point answered by experiment (the formatter's handling of TODO blocks): SpiralCompiler format on a copy of lib date_time.spi re-wraps comments to 120 columns, and the DATE-TIME-PORTABLE block's first line is 125 columns, so it came out as // TODO: ... calendar / // fields and format / //     in plain Spiral...: the wrapped tail becomes an unindented comment line between the head and its indented continuation, which breaks the block shape the harvest relies on (head line plus indented continuation lines). The second lib block (sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL) has the same risk if over 120. Rule for TODO blocks, now with a reason: every line at most 120 columns, so the formatter leaves the block alone (my memo.ex, dice init and dice contract blocks are 113-120). Fix for the two lib blocks: rewrap them to 120 by hand in the next lib edit window (lib is frozen for the core switch until main's all-clear), or teach the formatter to treat // TODO: blocks as unwrappable (compiler lane, SPIRAL-FORMATTER). Next: after the all-clear, rewrap the two lib blocks and recheck the harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_16 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:30 lap 6 grain, two more design points answered from the harvest itself: (1) link = the first token after TODO: is the eoie item ID, and eoie agile todos checks it against the backlog (stale=0 unknown=0 over cells, dice scripts and contract, lib: 7 blocks); (2) status and progress stay in the item, not in the block: the block holds where and what, and it is rewritten when the facts at that site change. Did that for dice/contract/build.ps1's RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE block (now 3 lines, each at most 120 columns, harvest shows it whole): it records today's result (nightly-2026-10-18 + build-std + mvp builds once rust-lld gets --allow-undefined or near-sys >= 0.2.14 is locked). Open: the formatter wrap (two lib blocks, after the all-clear) and the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_17 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:47 lap 7 grain, a full census of the migration: eoie agile todos over spiral, dice, alphabet, blog, dir-tree-html and polyglot (10,677 files, 2,690 scanned, 5.5 s) finds 12 TODO blocks, stale 0, unknown 0, so every block links to a live item. Width check: 3 blocks have lines over 120 columns. runner.ex (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, Elixir: mix format never rewraps comments, so harmless) and the 2 known lib blocks (date_time.spi DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), which are the only ones the Spiral formatter would break. So the post-switch rewrap list stays at exactly those 2. The harvest also reports skipped generated:5,619 and syntax:2,363 files, which keeps generated outputs from adding false TODOs.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_18 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T16:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:22 lap 8 grain: rewrapped the two lib TODO blocks the formatter would break: date_time.spi:107 (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE) and sm'_real.spir:591 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), each now 3 lines of at most 120 columns, same words, LF kept, comment-only hunks (the loop.while' hunks in sm'_real.spir are the switch migration, not mine). eoie agile todos over lib: todos=2 stale 0 unknown 0, both harvested whole. Helper lane 1: lib/spiral/date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir touched (comments only). Left: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_19 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:48 lap 9 grain: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks was drifted at its source: the memory rule (no-comments-self-explanatory) told writers to link with TODO(BACKLOG-ID):, but eoie's harvester reads the ID only as the token right after TODO: (agile_todo_model/model.spi:328-339 todo_id: colon at marker+4, then a capitalized token closed by a space), so the documented spelling would give unlinked TODOs. All 12 blocks in the repos already use the harvested form. The rule text now says: // TODO: BACKLOG-ID what is left, continuation lines //     ..., each at most 120 columns, TODO(ID): yields no ID. Left in the epic: confirm the formatter keeps a rewrapped block byte-identical")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_20 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:50 lap 9b grain: formatter design point answered by running it (installed Release, format --stdin --path, two short processes): both rewrapped TODO blocks come back byte-identical (3 lines each in date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir), so a block of at most 120 columns is stable under the formatter. format --check still reports both files as would change, but for pre-existing code lines (sm'_real.spir over-120 at 178, 205 ...), the known lib drift (79 lib files), not the TODO blocks. All four open design points of this epic are now answered (link = first token after TODO:, status in the item, formatter-stable at 120, the rule wording)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_21 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:25 lap 10 grain: post-switch harvest of spiral (eoie agile todos, 3.3 s): 10 blocks, stale 0, unknown 0. The 5 ID-linked blocks all point at open items (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE Active 50%, DIB-MODERNIZE Planned, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL Planned, KINO-HOT-PATHS Active, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR Active), so no block outlived its item. The other 5 are upstream core text in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1 (TODO: This..., TODO: Put...; listm.spi:120, serializerm/utils.spir x3), counted as ID-less; they leave the harvest when those lab copies are promoted or deleted after the switch. Next: per-repo migration step 1 = a harvest rule check that a block whose item is Done is reported (stale today means unknown ID only?)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_22 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: the stale rule answered by a probe (scratch file, two blocks): eoie agile todos reports stale (item is Done) for a block naming CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (Done) and unknown (no backlog item) for NO-SUCH-ITEM-XYZ, counts stale=1 unknown=1. So stale means exactly the Done case and the harvest already enforces that a block dies with its item; today's stale=0 over spiral means no block outlived its item. All design points of the epic are now answered by experiment; what remains is the migration itself, which needs the user's go (destructive by the epic's own definition)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_23 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T22:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:52 lap 19 grain: harvest after main's core-v1 migration of dice, alphabet and polyglot: spiral 10 blocks, dice 2, alphabet 0, polyglot 0, stale 0 and unknown 0 in all four. Spiral's 5 ID-less upstream blocks still live in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1; CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is Done now, so deleting those two lab copies (lab rule: promote or delete when Done) would drop them from the harvest, a call for main. Dice's SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT block (init.ps1:22) is replaced by rewrite-deps.ps1's own text when that lands. Next: the per-repo migration, on the user's go")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_24 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T23:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:31 lap 21 grain, migration prototype on a copy (the epic allows autonomous tries on copies): lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE/migrate-prototype.py writes 5 TODO blocks for compiler-side items (CALYX-BACKEND, OPENQASM3-BACKEND, EBPF-C-PROFILE, GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS, LEAN-BACKEND) at their codegen sites, each at most 120 columns, into a copy only (it refuses the tree file and a copy that already has them). Harvesting the copy found 0 blocks: eoie agile todos skips files over 1 MiB as large (agile_todo_model/model.spi:59 largest_scanned_bytes 1048576, :234 SkipLarge), and spiral_compiler.fs is 11 MB, which is also the large:1 in every spiral harvest so far. So the single largest code site cannot hold TODO blocks today; the migration needs either a size exemption or a bounded scan for that file in eoie (an eoie-lane change, recorded here for main to route), or compiler TODOs living next to the tests instead. Next: route the size limit to the eoie lane; then rerun the prototype harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_25 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 25u32, "2026-10-20T00:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:00 the 1 MiB harvest limit was sent to the eoie lane (git-0e) as main asked: exempt the compiler path, raise the limit, or scan large files line by line")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_26 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 26u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 20:45 eoie-lane (request from helper lane 2 via main): agile todos skipped spiral_compiler.fs (11 MB) as large because agile_todo_model largest_scanned_bytes was 1 MiB; raised to 32 MiB (whole-file read with NUL check stays cheap at that size), boundary test block added (11 MB scanned, 32 MiB+1 skipped), the old 2 MB large probe moved to 40 MB; model.spi 7/7. Ships with release job-1792457142086 (the 20:10 run regenerated the owner on pack 11; its test stage failed only on a flaky port fixed since).")
inl disk_cleanup () = Task ("DISK-CLEANUP", "Periodic disk cleanup for this machine: measure build outputs (spiral-bin bin/obj/runs/scratch/native, compile-memo, cargo target dirs, Kino builds, job tmp caches, Downloads bundles) and prune what's provably regenerable on a schedule, with a report of what was freed. The memo stores already prune themselves (compile 2 days, native 14 days)", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "disk,ops", "current", "current")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_1 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "main-thread", "15:13 main-thread inventory (read-only, directory level; a full per-file walk of spiral-bin took over 25 min and was stopped, so the tool must use directory-level stamps or the ledger): spiral-bin has 12 bin-pack*-backup compiler snapshots from 10-15/10-16 holding 6.5 GB (only the newest blessed pack and its predecessor are useful); runs has 1,842 run folders since 09-26, 894 older than 7 days, about 2.1 GB (sampled 50 = 121 MB), and the fast-tier fold cache and cost-report read results.tsv, so prune keeps results.tsv and drops the rest; compile-memo (53k files) and native-memo prune themselves. Earlier totals: eoie/src/target 16.8 GB, spiral/target 10.1 GB, toolchains 5.1 GB. C: had 15.3 GB free at 14:54. Design: an eoie verb with a dry-run report (path, size, last use, rule that made it prunable), rules as a typed union, never deleting tracked files.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_2 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:55Z", "main-thread", "15:55 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: pack backups keep the newest 2 (bin-pack1x/1y); the 10 older ones (1f..1w) were deleted with approval, freeing 5.35 GB (C: 19.8 GB free). That is the first retention rule for the tool: compiler pack backups keep 2.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_3 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:24 main-thread: C: is down to 11.9 GB free (19.8 GB at 10-17 15:55). Runs: 1,890 folders, 1,676 older than 7 days; a 60-folder sample keeps 2 MB of results.tsv and could drop 50 MB of worker files and logs, about 1.4 GB over all old runs, so runs are not where the 8 GB went (likelier: cargo target dirs during today's eoie regeneration and the memo stores; a directory-level size pass of eoie/src/target, spiral/target and spiral-bin/compile-memo is next). Pack backups already at the keep-2 rule (bin-pack1x, bin-pack1y). No deletion done: only the pack-backup rule has the user's approval so far.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_4 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:28 main-thread directory-level sizes (robocopy /L /BYTES /XJ, minutes not hours): spiral-bin 38.3 GB = bin 10.25, runs 7.68, eoie-regeneration 5.71 (today, the eoie regeneration in progress at 09:22), native 5.31, toolchains 4.99, scratch 1.04, the 2 kept pack backups 0.58 each, compile-memo 0.52, core-src 0.36, obj 0.32; outside it eoie/src/target 16.7 GB, spiral/target 9.9 GB (both unchanged since yesterday), Temp 2.0 GB. So the 8 GB drop is mostly today's eoie-regeneration. Candidate rules for the dry-run tool: eoie-regeneration keeps the last run, runs older than 7 days keep only results.tsv (about 1.4 GB), native build outputs older than the native memo's 14 days.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_5 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured (directory listing plus robocopy /L byte totals, no walk of spiral-bin): C: has 20.4 GB free (11.9 GB on 10-18 09:24, so the eoie regeneration space came back); spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration is now 1.99 GB in 12 run folders from 10-17 14:48 to 10-18 19:32 (the oldest alone 92 MB), so the keep-last rule frees about 1.9 GB; runs has 1,908 folders, 1,708 older than 7 days (the keep-results.tsv rule, about 1.4 GB per the 10-18 sample); pack backups still at keep-2 (bin-pack1x 10-16 14:46, bin-pack1y 10-16 17:20). No deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval; the other two rules stay dry-run candidates.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_6 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: wrote and ran the dry-run spec for the eoie verb: lab/DISK-CLEANUP/dry-run.ps1 (read-only; robocopy /L byte totals, one pass per rule, deletes nothing) with the three rules as data: pack backups keep the newest 2 (0 candidates now), eoie regeneration keeps the last run (11 folders, 1.50 GB), runs older than 7 days keep their tsv files (robocopy /MINAGE:7 excluding results.tsv, fast-tier.tsv, toolchain.tsv: 8.04 GB). Total 9.84 GB prunable today, far above the 10-18 sample estimate for runs (1.4 GB), so worker files and logs of old runs are the biggest win; the fold cache and cost reports read only results.tsv. Still no deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_7 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:29 helper-lane lap 3: typed the three rules for the future eoie verb as lab/DISK-CLEANUP/rules.spi (lab sketch, not eoie src): union retention = KeepNewest i32 | KeepLastRun | KeepTsvAfterDays i32, union approval = UserApproved | DryRunOnly, rule = root + retention + approval, and decide/applies that return Keep or Prune per entry (newest rank, age, results-tsv flag); a DryRunOnly rule always keeps, so only the approved pack-backup rule can prune. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded, 6 s): 6 decisions over runtime values exit 13 as predicted (old pack backup pruned, regeneration and runs computed as prune but held by DryRunOnly, results.tsv kept). OPEN QUESTION for the user (recorded, not asked): approve KeepLastRun for spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration (1.50 GB) and KeepTsvAfterDays 7 for spiral-bin/runs (8.04 GB)? Flipping either is one approval field in the rule table.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_8 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:24 helper-lane lap 6: measured one more root the rules sketch has to cover. spiral-bin/candidates held 19 private-cache compiler builds from today, about 0.7 GB: pack8b 107 MB, pack9/pack10 64 MB each, five 56 MB single-patch Debug candidates, and eleven 13 MB pack8 bisect and splice dirs. I removed my own ten whose patches have landed (nominal-with, export-walk, field-splice, and the seven pack8-<item> bisect dirs, about 240 MB), after checking that no process command line referenced them. That is the KeepLastRun-style rule a future verb should apply: a candidate dir can be pruned once its pack is accepted. Main's pack dirs and the two candidates still riding pack 11 (fsx-pointer, assign-delphi) stay.")
inl bundle_wisdom () = Task ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", "Wisdom mined from the 2026 zip bundles and bootstrap_llm files (user 2026-10-17)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,history", "current", "current")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_1 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:20 [1/12 sources] Era map. Bootstrap era Jan 2026: Downloads\\files\\code\\bootstrap_llm_compiler_v88..v101.00.md (01-04..01-05; v101 is byte-identical to espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_coder_v2.md), bootstrap_llm_ps1.md v1.0 (01-05, two identical copies), bootstrap_llm_*.toml 01-05..01-08 (newest espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml 01-08; bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml 01-05). Zip era Jan-Sep 2026: the root index (527 zips) misses the newest bundles. Latest per branch: Downloads\\bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\ holds spiral-compiler-parallelization alpha3006, spiral-samples alpha705, spiral-bin alpha417 (all 09-26), spiral-brzozowski alpha54 (09-19), eoie alpha70462 (09-26; no agile file, has state\\bootstrap.spi); dev1\\ holds workspace_spiral-compiler-parallelization alpha2891 and bundle_eoie alpha70377 (08-17); files-2026-05\\ has 2,565 older zips (Jan-Jun). agile_eoie.md exists in 5 places: the 4 Sep-26 zips (compiler 29 KB, samples 103 KB, bin 35 KB, brzozowski 81 KB, also nested in samples under contracts\\megaprojects\\mega_brzozowski_derivatives\\ledgers) plus dev1\\eoie\\agile_eoie.md (08-11, an oracle-to-ansi ERP-consolidation lane, not Spiral). AGILE.md files (13 zips, core alpha9036..9103, June) are tiny task tables for stroma/tritcritnet/mechahaze. Notes 2-12 below carry what today's backlog lacks; tasks to resume went on the matching items as bundle-wisdom notes.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_2 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[2/12 bootstrap protocol, bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml 2026-01-08 + bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml 01-05] The session was a typed state machine BOOT->LEASE->WORK->WRAP->BUNDLE->REPORT->DONE with named illegal states (long text before eoie status, shell probe while the eoie entry exists, work continued after should_yield=1, files outside the roots); on an illegal state the agent goes back to BOOT. Invariants I001-I005: one entry point for all actions, status before any substantial text, after the budget only WRAP/BUNDLE/REPORT, publish requested artifacts immediately, prefer receipt-leaving commands. Anti-shell rule was deny-by-default with a substitute: a missing eoie verb becomes a P0 agile task (pause, patch eoie, rebuild, resume), never a silent bypass; the only allowed escapes were eoie missing/broken and bootstrap extraction, both logged. Every action maps to an agile event or an eoie command, nothing lives only in chat. Each turn ended with a benchmark table and two 0..1000 ratings (fruitfulness = how re-executable the state became, satisfaction = budget kept + artifacts delivered). Retention kept current + 2 versions, dry-run before verify, never deleting bootstrap files. Today's memories eoie-first and use-full-timebox are this rule set re-found; the state machine, the illegal-state list and the per-turn ratings are not in today's backlog.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_3 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[3/12 one-call provenance] The one-call requirement predates eoie's first commit: bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml (2026-01-08) [eoie] parallelism says to prefer CPU-saturating commands and 'if you feel you would run 3+ commands in a row, try to collapse them into 1', and patching batched 'patch, rebuild and bench in a single call'. bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml (01-05) [tools] pipe allowed 2 stages with persisted intermediates. So EOIE-ONE-CALL's note that the requirement 'never reached eoie's README' is right about the repo, but the rule existed in the bootstrap files; cite these as its origin.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_4 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[4/12 lease and WRAP lessons; spiral-bin alpha417 + spiral-samples alpha705 agile_eoie.md, Jun-Sep 2026] Today's eoie still has agile begin/lease (the 75+10 lease), so these defects probably still apply. (a) WRAP blocks the closeout writes it requires (handoff, agile, ratings, LOC files), which forced out-of-band writes about 8 times; it needs a WRAP-safe closeout write class limited to a whitelist of state/report files, hash-receipted, unable to touch source. (b) Stale lease across user turns (seen 5+ times): a new turn inherits the previous turn's expired or WRAP lease and cannot even write the successor lease. Fix shape: a host-supplied new-turn epoch/nonce that archives the old lease and opens exactly one successor, with no way to extend within the same turn. (c) Dual authority: mutation admission said should_wrap=1 while lease status said 0 with 10 min left. Status and admission must share one lease token and a monotonic clock. (d) Rehydrated or control trees import their historical lease, so lease-neutral sandboxes are needed. (e) A resumed chat needs begin-at/adopt-clock, not a fresh 85 min. (f) A green pass is not done: v639 sealed early after one green run; the fix was a lease mode that tells version_available from session_saturated (today's use-full-timebox). Proposal: one item EOIE-LEASE (or fold into EOIE-JOBS) owning the time box, WRAP class and turn epochs.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_5 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[5/12 process lifecycle; brzozowski alpha54, spiral-bin alpha417, dev1 agile_eoie.md] Mostly for EOIE-JOBS (a short copy is noted there). (1) Receipt-first launch: write the job record before spawn, so a killed supervisor still leaves typed timeout|yielded|child_continues|no_child instead of no receipt. (2) Own the process group and reap all descendants on timeout. A timed-out compiler facade left an orphan using 3.8 cores and made known-good compiles look like regressions. (3) Commit-aware timeout: re-read the child's own completion receipts before reporting timed_out (the wrapper timed out at 25 s after the child had committed). (4) Typed outcomes semantic_failure | time_budget | yield | environment_failure, kept all the way into reports, never collapsed into one fail bit. (5) Expected-exit sets: rc5 typecheck negatives, diff rc1 = different, installer rc75 = resumable yield. (6) On a semantic failure, cancel undispatched work, let running siblings finish, and record the first-failure frontier; retry only the timed-out cells with a larger budget. (7) A stop path that is not starved: a saturated MSBuild pool blocked status/stop itself, so the parent needs reserved capacity. (8) Liveness means material progress: checkpoint churn must not outrank a zero-material-progress signal. (9) Receipts persist normalized argv, the exact toolchain paths and the first diagnostic from both stdout and stderr.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_6 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[6/12 benchmarking and A/B; spiral-samples alpha705 + brzozowski alpha54] Measured traps that are absent from today's backlog. A stale warm compiler session turned 2 s compiles into 8-24 s until it was cleaned up. Exact-graph cache hits (13-70 ms) are identity evidence only, never performance evidence. A cache key without the source SHA gave a false fresh hit after the source changed (the fix bound path + source SHA). Honest fresh pairs need a unique graph identity AND session teardown before every measured compile, both orders, and a symmetric cache nonce: a predecessor SHA that hit the cache made a Zeta candidate look +4.7% while fresh-vs-fresh was -14%. Four concurrent compiler supervisors contended and timed out, so the compiler family runs serially and parallelism goes inside the work. If both residual hashes are equal, classify a wall delta as lifecycle noise. Ratchet against the sealed control AND the best state promoted in the same wave. Put workload cardinality (active_calls) in the receipt. Rehydrating under a versioned path lost path-keyed incremental state, so keep shared build paths. A gate must never mutate canonical source: the burn-in gate restored its control and erased the candidate, so gates run in a sandbox copy.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_7 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[7/12 edit and patch lessons; compiler alpha3006, samples alpha705, brzozowski alpha54, workspace alpha2891] For EOIE-EDIT-BATCH (also noted there). (1) Payloads come from files or stdin, not argv: a 497 KiB fs-write hit E2BIG, and escaped JSON telemetry lines could not be matched. (2) An exact-N repeated-match mode: text-replace is exact-one, so migrating one prefix across 19 rows needed script glue. (3) Row-local idempotency: the old apply-spec marked a row resolved when its replacement text existed anywhere in the file while the needle was still present, which left 3 needles in place. (4) Refuse an empty payload over a non-empty file unless expected-old-hash/--allow-empty: a failed upstream step wiped a Spiral source to 0 bytes. (5) Content-addressed inverse-patch and rollback receipts plus restore-from-seal --expect-hash (rollbacks kept tempting an external cp). (6) A text-only plan must not need the compiler: patch check/apply still route plans through a compiled provider that loses --compiler on re-entry (still true in today's help: patch apply takes --compiler, check does not). (7) Hash-checked append (journals were rewritten whole). (8) fs-write --parents. (9) A gated patch can run a cold-compile ratchet and roll back when the time delta crosses a threshold.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_8 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[8/12 read/query/census lessons] For EOIE-READ-QUERY and EOIE-TABLE-QUERY. Piping search into head made eoie panic on EPIPE: a closed pipe is a normal early stop. The root '.' was accepted by fs-list but rejected by fs-search/parallel-search/parallel-manifest, so one root sentinel should work for every verb. Native per-file line counts were the most repeated gap: per-file LOC needed wc in about 15 rounds. source-stats counted evidence/probe .spi snapshots as own code and reported a bogus empty cargo_crate_max while source-topology said 98 LOC, so one classifier is needed (own/generated/vendor/reference/evidence). Typed JSONL window/join queries (first terminal failure -> owning job -> preceding events -> run_end) replaced jq/python in every compiler diagnosis round. A typed TSV upsert keyed by column avoids tab/escape corruption (a literal backslash-t got into agile rows). Help and dispatch should come from one registry: many advertised proxy verbs had no help schema. Also wanted: bounded tail, numbered slices, executable discovery, and a workspace lineage diff before diagnosis (a recovered tree looked like a stale binary until a diff showed interrupted next-round edits).")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_9 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[9/12 static authority audits, a new idea; compiler alpha3006 agile_eoie.md, Sep 2026] Each compiler bug in that lane was an authority inversion that a cheap compilerless audit could have flagged. (a) A field documented as a retry hint (progressYieldCount) took part in a semantic closure predicate, so the hint vetoed completion. Classify each field as authority/evidence/projection/hint and report every predicate where a hint mints or vetoes authority. (b) One boolean gated two different authority domains, so propose a DU split. (c) A DU or option was flattened into bools/strings and recombined in a later match, which is anti-drift; keep one sum type up to the serialization boundary. (d) Comparisons used rendered type text or nominal names where an exact interned identity existed (two operands both printed as file_type). (e) A fallback scan weakened identity (exact owner -> same backend -> same key) and borrowed another owner's body. (f) A clock or age carried across an epoch boundary without a carry-forward witness (stasis age inherited from the previous attempt). This fits the no-comments/unions direction and EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION. Proposal: one item EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT (a Spiral-side lint over our own sources) instead of more prose rules.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_10 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 10u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[10/12 continuity and claims; tritcritnet alpha716 docs\\vision\\readme_eoie.md (Feb 2026), core alpha9237 readmes (Jun), samples alpha705] Feeds EOIE-MEMORY. EOIE was framed as 'the body that makes the brain leave fingerprints', with memory in receipts/state, not the model. Missing today: (1) a resume compiler that builds the next-turn agenda from the previous ledger (passed = skip or verify cheaply, failed dep = block downstream, expected failure = tolerated, stale report = regenerate, partial patch = repair plan); (2) an incident-resume summary (active lease, newest savepoint, files changed since, newest receipts, toolchain identity) in one call; (3) a claim boundary on every report (passed/blocked/synthetic/oracle/proxy/tolerated) so progress words cannot outrun evidence; (4) sober regauge: 1000 means no material blockers plus external proof, with an explicit denominator (whole codebase vs local frontier); the old gauge inflated to 99x/1000; (5) a seal binds the tree fingerprint and the bundle fails if the tree changed after closeout (v664 shipped without late work); (6) reports are projections of one typed receipt (prose overwrote a planner metric: 113458 vs 113408), and reconcile-sidecars fails on contradictions; (7) self-hosting: eoie changes only through eoie verbs, with receipts.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_11 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 11u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[11/12 toolchain custody and debugging method] Toolchain (brzozowski alpha54, samples alpha705): (a) compiler identity = content fingerprints + a mandatory capability probe, not a wrapper hash; identical spiral-compile wrappers sat on top of a good and a bad companion. (b) The 'latest' lane was a functional subset of the stable one, so reconcile by capability diff (superset). (c) Health is per capability and history-aware: SDK ok / compiler attested / restore smoke failing were separate states, and a stale not-attested bit outlived a later success. (d) Typed, validated env profiles: a malformed export gave SPIRAL_DOTNET=/dotnet; a gate binds its runtime profile (one-shot FSI vs the persistent daemon are not equivalent and gave false 10 s near-misses). (e) Rehydrate preserves executable bits. Method (bootstrap_llm_compiler v49-v101 Jan 2026, bootstrap_llm_ps1 v1.0 01-05): a technique enters the compendium only with an error signature, a canonical patch, a verification command and an artifact, and is deprecated, not deleted, when obsolete; write the next artifact to disk before patching; fix one class of diff per iteration; exact parity uses structural counters + sha/bytes + a similarity ratio that must rise monotonically; anchors must match exactly once and patches stay at most ~20 lines; for large logs count first (EJP code and site histograms, repeating frame cycles) before reading.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_12 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 12u32, "2026-10-17T18:25Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[12/12 gate lessons from dev1\\eoie\\agile_eoie.md (2026-08-11, a Rust ERP-consolidation lane)] Generic rules that still apply here: a zero-test green run is not green (refuse compile-only success as semantic closure); green owner tests cannot erase a public API change (keep it open); a source-freshness gate before any executable is treated as authoritative (a stale auditor binary produced 5 critical rows, rebuilt in 0.48 s down to 2); the old eoie zip dropped every path segment named build/builder/target, including vendor\\clap_builder\\src\\builder, while keeping .cargo-checksum.json, so exclusions must be root-aware with a post-zip closure check against the checksums; a scoped transaction must not overwrite a wider-scope receipt (owner slice vs global audit); big sidecars keep counts plus a bounded sample (78.8 MB -> 1.7 MB with no lost counts); patch the compiled owner resolved through facade re-exports, not a same-named stale shadow file; installer success requires discovering the executable and running cargo --version, not a success-looking extraction log; prefer owner-filtered incremental gates and persist timeout receipts instead of reading a monolithic timeout as a semantic failure.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_13 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 13u32, "2026-10-17T18:25Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[summary for the main lane] Proposed new items (not added; main decides): EOIE-LEASE (the time box as one monotonic authority, a WRAP-safe closeout write class, new-turn epoch rollover, begin-at for resumed chats; note 4), EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT (hint/authority, DU-flattening and identity-weakening lint; note 9), BENCH-FRESH-PAIR (lifecycle-owned cold A/B with teardown, symmetric nonce, both orders, best-current ratchet, hash rollback; note 6), EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER (next-turn agenda from the ledger plus incident-resume; note 10). Item notes posted: EOIE-ONE-CALL, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-READ-QUERY, EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, EOIE-MEMORY, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION, MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL, KINO-SOTA, CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK, HOPACSOTA, BUNDLER-DEBUG-STACK-OVERFLOW, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER, LEAN-BACKEND, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST. Projects we are not advancing (listed only): stroma/pdf2stroma (PDF append-index merge, glyph spacing cmap, table reconstruction; AGILE.toml alpha518 2026-06-06, core AGILE.md alpha9080/9103 June), tritcritnet (telemetry critic fixtures), mechahaze (render smoke pack), and the ERP-consolidation lane in dev1. No Tibia material was found in the bundles. Extracted single entries live in C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\bundle-wisdom (scratch; Downloads untouched).")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_14 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:22 helper-lane lap 1: DONE with evidence: the mining covered all 12 planned sources (notes 1-12) and the summary (note 13) proposed four items, which all exist now: EOIE-LEASE (Planned), EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT (Planned), BENCH-FRESH-PAIR (Done) and EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER (Planned); the per-item lessons were posted on the 16 matching items (EOIE-ONE-CALL, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-READ-QUERY, EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, EOIE-MEMORY ..., CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK, HOPACSOTA, LEAN-BACKEND, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST). Nothing left that this item owns; follow-ups live on those items.")
inl eoie_lease () = Task ("EOIE-LEASE", "From the January 2026 bootstrap bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 4): one time-box authority for agile begin/lease; a write class allowed during WRAP so closeout writes are never blocked; lease rollover on every new user turn (a stale lease survived into the next turn 5+ times); start-at for resumed chats; status and mutation must agree", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,lease", "current", "current")
inl eoie_authority_audit () = Task ("EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT", "Compilerless audit from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 9): a lint that catches fields or flags read with the wrong authority (a value trusted from a source that doesn't own it), as a one-call eoie verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,audit", "current", "current")
inl bench_fresh_pair () = Task ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", "Honest benchmarking (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 6: stale warm compiler sessions and cache hits once faked regressions): one cold A/B command that tears down warm servers and memos before each compile, interleaves A and B, and rolls back automatically; fits the run ledger and CI-OFFLOAD", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "bench,harness", "current", "current")
inl note_of_bench_fresh_pair_1 () = Note ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:23Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:23 main-thread first slice: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/bench-pair.ps1 -A <dll> -B <dll> -Sample <name|dir> [-Backend C] [-Rounds 5]: every compile is a fresh dotnet process (no warm server, no compile memo, SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=1), the order alternates per round (A,B then B,A) so drift cancels, outputs go to a temp dir removed afterwards (nothing to roll back), and it prints each run plus the medians, B/A and the output sizes per side (a size mismatch flags a semantic difference). Smoke: the installed single-flight against itself on tuple_pair (C, 2 rounds, under suite load): 2,327 vs 2,237 ms, B/A 0.961, the noise floor here. Left: a run-ledger row per pair and a native-run mode.")
inl note_of_bench_fresh_pair_2 () = Note ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:49 main-thread: run-ledger row per pair is done. bench-pair.ps1 dot-sources heavy.ps1 (no side effects) and appends a kind=bench row to <cache>/ledger/runs.tsv through Write-RunLedger: label 'bench-pair <sample> <backend> A <ms> B <ms> B/A <ratio>', outcome 'B/A <ratio>; sizes ...', free MB at start, and the replayable command. Smoke with the installed single-flight against itself on tuple_pair (C, 1 round): 2,817 vs 2,698 ms, B/A 0.958, sizes 796/796; the ledger row reads back. Left: a native-run mode (time the built program, not only the compile).")
inl note_of_bench_fresh_pair_3 () = Note ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", 3u32, "2026-10-18T15:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:52 main-thread: native-run mode added. bench-pair.ps1 -Native (C only) builds each side's output with the matrix's flags (-std=c11 -O2 -fwrapv -w, plus the sample's own c-shim.h if present; matrix.psd1 cFlagRules are not applied yet), runs it, and reports the median run ms and exit codes per side in the output and in the ledger row. Smoke on tuple_pair (2 rounds): compile B/A 1.001; run 29 vs 62 ms, both exit 0. On a tiny sample, process start dominates, so use it on samples with real runtime (mega runtime_bench). AGENTS.md updated. Left: apply cFlagRules for samples that need them.")
inl note_of_bench_fresh_pair_4 () = Note ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", 4u32, "2026-10-18T15:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:53 main-thread: -Native now applies matrix.psd1 CFlags rules ({shims} expanded) the way Get-CFlags does. Smoke on abi_external_callback, which needs abi-external-callback-c-shim.h: both sides build and exit 123. Every part of the item is in place: fresh process per compile (no warm server, no compile memo), alternating A/B order, outputs in a temp dir that is removed (nothing to roll back), medians with B/A and output sizes, an optional native run, and a ledger row per pair.")
inl eoie_resume_compiler () = Task ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", "Continuity from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): a 'resume compiler' verb that builds the next turn's agenda from backlog state, recent receipts and open leases, with claim boundaries on reports and seals bound to the tree state; pairs with EOIE-MEMORY recall", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,continuity", "current", "current")
inl gleam_try_rescue () = Task ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", "Gleam try_unit and try_finally for real", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,gleam,lib", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:50Z", "main-thread", "14:50 main-thread: verified lib/spiral/base.spi:695 - on Gleam, try_unit and try_finally emit only a comment (no rescue, no cleanup). Blocks KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (runner deadline + tree kill need cleanup on crash). Proposed by kino-native-design: a spawn-and-monitor arm (spawn the body, monitor it, run finally on DOWN, re-raise the exit reason), Erlang built-ins only since a Spiral package cannot carry an .erl file. Also: lib/spiral/platform.spi is_windows imports gleam/erlang/os, gone in gleam_erlang 1.x; it needs an os:type external before any bump.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: use lpil's hex package exception (rescue + defer) for the Gleam arms of base.try_unit and base.try_finally.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:19 main-thread drafted, not applied: in base.spi try_unit and try_finally a '| Gleam _ =>' run_target arm before the '| _ =>' default: try_unit = global import exception, then case exception.rescue(!fn) { Ok(_) -> Nil Error(e) -> !ex_fn(fn() { e }) } and Nil : t; try_finally = exception.defer(!finally, !fn) and Nil : t (defer runs the cleanup and re-raises). Preconditions before applying: (1) the harness Gleam project and Cells gleam.toml must carry the exception hex dependency, or every Gleam row that reaches try_unit or try_finally stops building (Unknown module, as gleam_community_maths did for portable_math_rounding today); (2) exn's Gleam type must be exception.Exception for the ex_fn callback to type-check. Check which Gleam rows reach them (the references census can answer that) before landing.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:23 helper-lane lap 1: answered the 10-18 precondition questions by reading the trees: (a) reach: across every repo under C:\\home\\git, try_unit / try_unit' / try_finally appear only inside lib/spiral/base.spi itself (31 hits, lines 667-797) and in backlog text; no .spi caller exists, so no harness or Cells Gleam row reaches them today and landing the arm cannot break a corpus row. (b) manifests: the harness Gleam project (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam/gleam.toml) depends on gleam_stdlib and gary only; Cells writes its manifest from targets.ex @gleam_deps (gleam_stdlib, gleam_time, gleam_erlang, envoy, gary, gtempo) plus per-cell extras, so the arm's own test block can declare exception per cell without touching either manifest. Also: the Lua arms (lines 711/718/733, 778/785/792) are the same comment-only stub, and the Python try_finally arm at 797 emits a // comment, which is not even valid Python. Remaining unknown: the exception package's Exception type name for ex_fn.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:13 helper-lane lap 2: Gleam arms written as a lab patch (lab/GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE/patch.py, dry-applied to a copy of base.spi: 2 arms + 2 test blocks) after probing how the backend emits closures: a Spiral unit-argument closure compiles to a Gleam fn(Nil) -> a (probe via the Gleam backend: closure1(capt) -> fn(Nil) -> Int, calls as v1(Nil)), so the arms adapt to exception's zero-arity callbacks: try_unit = case exception.rescue(fn() { fn(Nil) }) { Ok(_) -> Nil Error(_) -> ex_fn(fn(_) { Nil }) } (exn is Nil on Gleam per exn_switch), try_finally = exception.defer(fn() { finally(Nil) }, fn() { fn(Nil) }) (cleanup first, re-raises), each with global import exception and placed before the | _ => default. Tests: try_unit_rescues_a_failure_on_gleam (a failwith body bumps a mut counter through ex_fn) and try_finally_cleans_up_on_gleam, both ///> gleam -d exception so no manifest changes. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE/run.ps1 (Cells on base.spi, ~15 min, restores lib on failure).")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:31 helper-lane lap 3: (1) correction to lap 1: the Python // arm at base.spi 797 is dead code, not a bug: try_unit_fsharp_python () is statically true on Python (and Fsharp), so both backends always take the then-branch and the else-branch arms for them are never emitted. (2) Lua arms added to lab/GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE/patch.py after probing the closure shape on Lua (lab lua_pcall.spi via try.ps1 Lua, bounded: exit 11 as predicted; Spiral closures compile to function(dom), so pcall(f) calls them directly): try_unit = if not pcall(fn) then ex_fn(function(dom) return nil end) end (exn is nil on Lua), try_finally = local ok, err = pcall(fn); finally (); if not ok then error(err, 0) end (cleanup first, re-raises with the original message). Two more test blocks (///> lua, same bodies as the Gleam ones). Dry-applied to a scratch copy: 4 arms + 4 tests land. The queued run.ps1 (Cells on base.spi) now covers both backends.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:19Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:19 helper-lane: main's queue 3b ran run.ps1 (14:04-14:10, accepted pack 7 compiler, switched core): base.spi 20 of 24 executed tests passed and lib was restored (no patch text left in base.spi). Both new failures are in my arms, read from lab/GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE/base-20261019-1404.spi.ipynb: (1) Gleam: -d exception wrote exception = * into the manifest and gleam refused it (TOML: * is not a valid version), so the test lines now declare -d exception=quoted >= 2.0.0 and < 3.0.0 (Cells' builder tokenizer keeps the quoted value as one token); (2) Lua: the arms ended with a macro nil of type t, which for a unit t is emitted as a bare nil statement, a Lua syntax error (unexpected symbol near nil); they now end with null (), the idiom base.spi already uses for Lua arms. patch.py dry-applies with both fixes; rerun when the gate is free.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T20:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:57 helper-lane: rerun with both fixes: both Gleam tests now pass (the exception dep with a version range works), and 22 of 24 tests pass. The two Lua tests still fail with the same bare nil statement: null () also lowers to the text nil, and a unit-typed value is emitted as a statement on Lua. The Lua arms now end with the macro (function() return nil end)() : t, a call, which Lua accepts as a statement and which evaluates to nil as an expression. Rerun pending.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T21:15Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:15 helper-lane: Done. lab/GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE/run.ps1 (run-20261019-1705.log, accepted pack 8b compiler, switched core) applied patch.py to lib/spiral/base.spi and ran its Cells notebook: 24 of 24 executed tests passed, failed_targets empty, including try_unit_rescues_a_failure_on_gleam and try_finally_cleans_up_on_gleam (Gleam arms over the exception package: exception.rescue for try_unit, exception.defer for try_finally, -d exception with a >= 2.0.0 and < 3.0.0 range) and the matching Lua tests (pcall arms ending in a call that yields nil). The script keeps the change on a pass, so base.spi now carries it (uncommitted, +50/-2, for the user's commit). The Python arm at the old line 797 turned out to be dead code (lap 3), so nothing is left in the title.")
inl lib_canon_names () = Task ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", "Canon names for every lib file", Epic, P2, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,naming,i18n,edition", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_1 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: the 1k-line limit is a Rust crate concern only. On the Spiral side we want the FEWEST files, fragmented by generic concept, not by dotnet or Fable jargon (the lib/apps layout was polluted by Fable hacking and mimicking .NET names). Drop base and core and every other inherited name for the canon term of each pattern, renaming or deleting lib files as needed, and apply the same to new code from rewrites in eoie, x, the renamed Kino and other repos. Without drift: each rename keeps the corpus green (lib sweep, harness, eoie tests) and lands with a reference count from LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS. The canon English term is picked with help from MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION (translations into native scripts inform the English choice).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_2 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread: user motto for this epic, verbatim: we're opus 5.5 (rick's voice) [put that into eoie! hah! (hic)]")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_3 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:41 main-thread proposal from the census v2 counts (definitions, outside uses): (1) free renames, the flat modules whose names never appear at call sites: base (38 defs, 5,185 outside uses) and common (13, 1,245) merge into one prelude file; am'_prototypes, iter_prototypes, sm'_prototypes and trace are flat too. (2) the primed .NET-isms, each a scripted qualified rename gated by the lib sweep + harness + eoie tests: sm' to text (125 defs, 1,123 outside uses, the biggest), sm'_operators to text_operators (668), am' to array (196), optionm' to option (148), resultm to result (127), listm' to list (38), mapm to map (8). (3) plain renames by domain term: runtime to process (157, today it is Rust std::process + clap), parsing to parser (27), reflection to reflect (12), env to environment (8), date_time to time (5). Keep: file_system, console, convert, testing, math, crypto, async, stream, seq, wasm, and the domain packages leptos, near, near_workspaces. Each step: rename, rewrite qualified uses with the census as the work list, run the corpus, land alone. Needs the user's go on the names (MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION informs the English terms).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_4 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:24 helper-lane lap 1: verified the free-rename claim for step (1): a grep of every .spi/.spir under C:\\home\\git for open base/common or base./common. qualified uses finds no real reference - the hits are Rust closure variables named base in eoie macro text (eoie_jobs/host.spi:25, eoie_do_read, eoie_do_edit, eoie_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:624), Lean base.toInt in lib convert.spi:88, and common.spi's own label strings (common.retry_fn, common.dispose comments). So merging base.spi and common.spi into one prelude file changes only lib/spiral/package.spiproj's module list plus those label strings; no consumer edits. The primed renames (sm' to text etc.) still need the user's go on names.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_5 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:13 helper-lane lap 2: order constraint for the base+common prelude merge, from lib/spiral/package.spiproj: base- is module 6 and common- is module 36, with 28 modules between them (rust/, optionm', resultm, convert, listm', am', seq, math, mapm, iter, sm'_real*, sm', sm'_operators, console, reflection, guid, platform, env, testing, near, date_time, trace-, parsing ...), and common.spi uses later modules (retry_fn' formats with sm' and trace). So a merged prelude cannot sit at base's position; it either takes common's position (then base's 38 definitions, which those 28 modules reach unqualified as a flat module, would be unbound for them) or stays two files. Flat modules today: |core-, am'_prototypes-, backend-, base-, iter_prototypes-, sm'_prototypes-, trace-, common-. Conclusion for the proposal: base and common are not mergeable by position; the free rename is renaming them (e.g. prelude and prelude_late), not merging.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_6 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:32 helper-lane lap 3: proposal text updated for the user's go (names only, nothing renamed): step (1) becomes RENAME-ONLY (lap 2: base and common cannot merge by position): base -> prelude, common -> prelude_late. Flat-module candidates: am'_prototypes -> array_prototypes, sm'_prototypes -> text_prototypes, iter_prototypes / trace / backend keep (already canon). New input from today's core switch (lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/apply-switch.ps1, landing about 11:45): it adds lib module am_dim after am', a fresh primed-family name, so step (2)'s am' -> array should take it along as array_dim in the same rename (or the switch could name it array_dim now and save a later rename). Steps (2) and (3) unchanged. Constraint from SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX lap 2: module renames do not touch X autolinks (a module is the left side of the dot); the autolink-prone members are map, box, show (82 percent of lib's TLD-named uses), which a member-rename step could absorb. Needs the user's go on names.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_7 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:38 helper-lane: main took the naming input: the core switch now adds the lib module as array_dim (not am_dim), so step (2)'s am' -> array rename has a canon sibling already.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_8 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:25 helper-lane lap 6: census of today's lib/spiral (85 .spi/.spir files): 11 still carry the old suffix names (am', am'_prototypes, listm', mapm, optionm', optionm'_operators, resultm, sm', sm'_operators, sm'_prototypes, sm'_real.spir); array_dim already exists as the canon sibling of am' from the core switch. These 11 are the rename set for step 2. Each rename touches every qualified use across lib, apps and consumer repos, so it rides the core-migration work, one file at a time with a Cells gate per file.")
inl cli_args_style () = Task ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", "Typed CLI arguments derived from unions", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION,RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", "cli,pwsh-free,reflection,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_1 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:10 styles-survey [1/3 clap today] clap users: spiral apps/spiral (6 subcommands), apps/wasm spiral_wasm, alphabet apps/documents (1 command, 6 args); Cargo clap = 4.5 with default features (no derive, so no proc macros), lock 4.5.18 + 11 transitive crates (clap_builder, clap_lex, anstream, anstyle x4, colorchoice, strsim, utf8parse, is_terminal_polyfill). Binding: lib/spiral/runtime.spi lines 463-773, ~310 lines, 37 inl wrappers over the builder API (Command new/arg/subcommand/subcommand_required/args_override_self/get_matches(_from)/debug_assert, Arg required/short/long/default_value/num_args/value_names/value_parser/action/index, ValueRange, PossibleValuesParser, ArgMatches get_one/get_flag/get_many/subcommand) as nominal rust_type per clap type; arg_union already derives possible values from a union by reflection (get_union_fields_untag). Typing is stringly at the boundary: args are a record of (long, short) string tuples (get_args), subcommands matched by string equality in run, values come back as option std_string keyed by name, then union_try_pick. Cost in spiral: get_args 29 + get_command 122 lines for 6 verbs (~25 lines per verb) plus ~7 lines per extracted arg in run; generated clap get_command ~290 of the 24,280 spiral.rs lines (SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 2). Help/usage/suggestions come free from clap.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_2 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:11 styles-survey [2/3 hand-rolled] eoie: no clap anywhere; rust_std_env/model.spi (18 lines) wraps std::env::args().skip(2).nth(i) as argument_owned/argument_equals/args_len_is; each verb checks arity by count (task_notes author_choice: count 4 or 6, then --as at index 4), ~25 of 78 lines in agile_task_notes are arg handling; usage text is hand strings (agile_subcommand_spec: 18 cases, shape is an untyped string like <root> <id> <text> [--as <author>]); top-level dispatch and 32 proxy routes are numeric codes inside a rust_global blob (eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi); help/--json/--schema rendered from descriptor tables. Subcommands are a union only since the new agile_subcommand_domain (union_cases_fold), args are never records. x apps/client: typed hand parser, cli.spi 408 lines for 3 verbs + 12 flags: union command (5 cases), union flag (8), union switch (4), records request/draft/filters, parse_args_with over list string, flag names by if-chains, usage hand-written (28 lines); then flattened to command_fields (kind i32 + 13 scalars) for the Export boundary in args.spi (76 lines -> args.rs 994 lines, at the 1k gate). Per verb: clap ~25 decl lines + extraction, eoie ~25 parse lines spread over up to 5 synced tables, x ~130 lines incl. validation. Demand: 124 tracked .ps1 (8,181 lines) in spiral/polyglot/alphabet/dice/x/eoie/i574n.github, 112 of them with a param() block, i.e. ~112 future typed CLIs.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_3 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:12 styles-survey [3/3 recommendation: (b), clap retired after migration] Build our own typed parsing in lib, derived by reflection: a subcommand is a union case, its operands a record (string/i32/bool/option/list/union fields map to positional, valued, switch, optional, repeated, possible-values), and one exhaustive spec case -> {name; summary} gives usage, help, --json schema, completion and actionable errors (eoie style intent/usage/valid/corrected) from the same source. Use the compile-time fold of eoie union_reflection/cases.spi, not lib reflection, whose union_try_pick builds a runtime list + array per call. Why not clap: its ids and get_one results are strings, so a typed layer is needed on top anyway; clap only exists for the Rust backend; 12 crates per CLI tool (no proc macros, so the compile cost is one cached clap_builder per workspace; binary cost not measured here, typically a few hundred KB vs 6.1 MB spiral.exe / 8.4 MB eoie.exe); and the help/suggestion polish is what the derived spec renders anyway. Why not hybrid: two parsers to keep in sync is the drift eoie already shows. Gate rules: keep parse/usage helpers as let (an inl fold took agile_mutation.rs 614 -> 1567 lines), one crate per tool. Migration: x first (smallest typed parser, proves records), then eoie agile, then SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR drops runtime.spi clap (~310 lines). Needs EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (case enumeration) and an Export that carries unions.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_4 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:24 helper-lane lap 1: recorded the dependency note 3 names but the item lacked: deps = EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (Active 60%, the compile-time case fold the derived spec needs). The other prerequisite, an Export that carries unions, has no backlog item (searched titles for export + union); x's args.spi still flattens command to kind i32 + 13 scalars for that reason. SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (Planned 0%) is the consumer that retires runtime.spi's clap binding afterwards, so it should depend on this item, not the reverse.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_5 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:14 helper-lane lap 2: sized the Export-carries-unions gap with a probe (lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE/export_union.spi, Rust backend on the installed compiler, Invoke-Heavy): exporting decide : i32 -> command (union Add : i32 | Rename : string | Clear) compiles, emitting pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> US0, but the enum is private (enum US0, no pub) and named by a per-crate counter (US0), so a consumer crate can neither name nor match it, and its own copy of the same union would be a different US0. So the missing piece is not the Export op but cross-crate type identity: an exported union needs pub visibility and a stable, source-derived name (command, not US0) shared by producer and consumer, which is why x's args.rs flattens command into kind i32 + 13 scalars. That is a compiler item of its own (codegenRust naming/visibility for types reachable from Export signatures); CLI-ARGS-STYLE depends on it for crate-split CLIs, not for single-crate ones.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_6 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:33 helper-lane lap 3: filed the compiler prerequisite as RUST-EXPORT-TYPES (Story P3 D4: pub + source-named types reachable from Export; READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES kept union type names tag-keyed by design, so this is the missing type-name slice) and set deps = EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION,RUST-EXPORT-TYPES.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_7 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: dependency progress: RUST-EXPORT-TYPES is at 70 after pack 10. On the installed compiler, unions reachable from an Export signature come out as pub enum named after the Spiral source (export_union.spi: pub enum command, pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> command). The x args.rs flattening (kind i32 plus 13 scalars) can go once records follow and a cross-crate consumer is proven; x stays read-only here.")
inl hangulize_in_spiral () = Task ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", "Hangulize as a Spiral transcription library", Story, P3, D5, 20u32, Active, "", "alphabet,transcription,lib,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:16 styles-survey [1/3 where and what] alphabet apps/documents (documents.spi hangul, lines 118-196) writes <name>.hangul.md (+ .html/.pdf/.epub): it pipes the trimmed text to the hangulize binary as a shell command string (deps/hangulize/cmd/hangulize/hangulize.exe <spec>), spec por-br by default or eng when the text contains the and and, then interleaves each transcribed line with the original (transcribe_only drops the original; code fences and URL lines kept). Dependency: not vendored, not a submodule; scripts/dep_hangulize.ps1 (called by init.ps1) git-clones the i574n/hangulize fork into gitignored deps/hangulize, installs Go via winget/apt and runs go build. The fork = upstream hangulize/hangulize (go 1.19; deps cobra, kagome.ipadic, go-pinyin, go_hangul, x/text) + 2 own commits (2025-06-22/28): an English spec via CMU dict (translit/english, cmudict.dict 134,429 lines / 3.9 MB, specs/eng.hsl ~300 lines, jamo.go and procedure.go tweaks). Size: 92 Go files / 7,513 lines (57 non-test / 5,148: pkg/hre regex dialect 1,321, pkg/hsl spec lexer+parser 761, translit furigana/pinyin/cyrillic/english 689, core procedure/rule/spec/script), 41 .hsl specs / 12,243 lines with 4,202 inline test examples, web/ UI 125 files. Only consumer in our repos: alphabet documents.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:17 styles-survey [2/3 the hangul experiment] alphabet apps/hangul is an F# port of suapapa/go_hangul (sibling clone C:\\home\\git\\go_hangul, 746 Go lines, last upstream 2024-01-08): hangul.fs 494 lines (jamo constants, syllable compose/split, hanjaConvert, isHanja), hanja.spi 4,586 lines (one hanja_to_hangul_table array, 477 KB), cp949_from.fs + cp949_to.fs 17,051 + 17,053 lines (733 KB each), hangul.livemd 931 lines, package.spiproj on deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib. 15 commits since 2025-06-20; last code change 2025-11-19 (84-line .dib edit); 2026-10-06/07 only the dib -> livemd migration and build.ps1 edits. Nothing consumes it: documents calls the Go binary, scripts/build.ps1 only runs its notebook export. It is F#/dotnet-only, so it conflicts with the native direction; worth keeping is the jamo compose/split logic (~200 lines) as the seed of the Hangul output layer, and the hanja table as data; cp949 tables are unnecessary in a UTF-8 pipeline.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:18 styles-survey [3/3 assessment + name] Concrete gains of a Spiral rewrite: (1) specs as typed data instead of runtime-parsed text: hangulize lexes/parses every .hsl (761 lines) and compiles HRE patterns to Go regexp at load (1,321 lines); in Spiral a spec is a record of vars/macros/normalize/rewrite/transcribe rules and inl partial evaluation specializes the rewrite engine per language at compile time (first Futamura projection), so no regex engine or spec parser ships. (2) GADT rule AST indexed by phase (normalize -> rewrite -> transcribe), so a rule cannot run in the wrong phase; existentials for per-language pre-transliterators (pinyin, furigana, cyrillic, CMU English) as packs of state + step; a prototype transcriber over output scripts (Hangul first, others later); reflection derives the language union, the CLI choice (CLI-ARGS-STYLE) and docs. (3) One codebase for every backend: documents stops shelling out to Go and needs no Go toolchain (dep_hangulize.ps1 retires, PWSH-FREE); the same lib serves wasm/JS for a web page. (4) Tests: the 4,202 spec examples become inline expectations / Spiral test blocks, with the Go binary as differential oracle until parity. Costs: dictionaries (kagome ipadic, pinyin, 3.9 MB cmudict) need Rust crates or embedded data; scope first only por-br + eng (the two used). Proposed generic lib name: transcription (lib/spiral/transcription: rules, hangul compose, specs/por_br, specs/eng); transliteration is the wrong word, the specs map sounds, not letters.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:50Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:50 helper-lane lap 1: sized the port and its oracle from alphabet/deps/hangulize (152dd46, 2025-06-28): 57 non-test Go files / 4,114 non-blank lines (hangulize core 17 files, hre 8, hsl 5, subword 3, furigana 4, plus translit wasm shims for cyrillic, pinyin, furigana), 35 *_test.go files, and 41 .hsl specs (498 KB, aze ... wlm; por-br and eng are the two alphabet uses). The specs embed 10,631 word -> hangul test pairs, a ready differential oracle like Tibia's bots: a Spiral port can be checked spec by spec against the pairs with no Go run at all. The furigana/pinyin/cyrillic paths go through translit wasm modules, so a first slice should take a pure-rule spec (por-br, which alphabet uses by default) and its pairs.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:35 helper-lane lap 2: profiled the first slice's spec: por-br.hsl (Brazilian Portuguese, alphabet's default) is 250 lines with sections lang, config, macros, vars, normalize, rewrite, transcribe, test, and carries 211 word -> hangul test pairs. Its HRE pattern features: 79 angle-bracket variable references, 59 brace look-around groups, 39 carets (word start) and 40 dollars (word end), no bracket character classes. So a typed Spiral spec for por-br needs: vars as named letter sets, rewrite and transcribe rules as (pattern, replacement) records where a pattern is a sequence of literal | var | lookbehind | lookahead | edge, normalize as a char map; the 211 pairs become one test block table. No regex engine is needed for this spec, only the HRE subset.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:17 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the HRE subset as types in lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi (from alphabet/deps/hangulize/specs/por-br.hsl): union rec piece = Literal string | Variable var_name | Alternatives (list (list piece)) covers literals, <var> references and {a|b} / (x|<cs>x) groups; type pattern = {at_word_start; behind; body; ahead; at_word_end} carries the ^ and $ edges and the brace look-around as explicit fields instead of regex syntax; rule = pattern + replacement; letter_set for the vars section (vowels encoded). Hand-encoded the first 14 rewrite rules in file order: the 10 accent rules, dumont$ -> dumon, ^eduar -> Eduar, ^ex{@} -> ez (the @ macro expands to the vowels var) and c{k|q} -> empty. Probe main counts them (expect exit 14); unrun until main's freeze all-clear. Next: a matcher over pattern (byte-level, ASCII plus the normalized accented letters) and the first test pairs through rewrite only.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:59Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:59 helper-lane lap 5: wrote the matcher over the typed HRE subset into lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): match_seq/match_alts walk Literal, Variable (any letter of the named letter_set) and Alternatives at a byte position; behind_ok tries every start that ends exactly at the position; rule_end checks the ^ and $ flags, the look-behind, the body and the look-ahead and returns the body's end; apply_at rewrites one rule left to right over a word (copying unmatched bytes), and rewrite_all applies the rules in file order, as hangulize does. Test words through the first 14 por-br rules: ação -> aSA~o (ã then ç), eduardo -> Eduardo (^eduar), exame -> ezame (^ex followed by a vowel), dumont -> dumon (dumont$), acqua -> aqua (c before q); expected exit 31.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: hre.spi compiled and exited 3, not 31 - acao and eduardo rewrite correctly, while exame, dumont and acqua do not. The three failing rules are exactly the ones built with a record update that sets a list-valued field ({(literal x) with ahead = [...]} or body = [...]); eduardo's rule updates only a bool field and works. Rewrote those three rules as full records to separate a matcher bug from a record-with problem; rerun pending. If they pass, a record update of a list field on a function-call base is a compiler issue to cut into a repro.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:49 helper-lane: rerun with the three rules rewritten as full records still exits 3, so the record-with hypothesis is withdrawn (not a compiler issue): the failures are in my matcher's look-ahead / word-end path (exame, acqua need ahead; dumont needs at_word_end), while the plain-body and word-start rules work. Next: a per-rule debug probe (rule_end of each failing rule at position 0) to find the faulty branch.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T21:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:31 helper-lane: the exit 3 from lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi was never a bit mask: it is the Windows exit code of abort(). The C runtime's string slice aborts when an end lands inside a UTF-8 sequence, and hre.spi sliced byte ranges: starts_at sliced [i, i+n-1] (behind_ok tries every byte offset, and a 2-byte letter after ASCII ends mid-sequence), and the copy step sliced one byte of a 2-byte letter like ç or ã. StringIndex is a byte on every backend, so the fix stays in plain Spiral: starts_at compares byte by byte (bytes_match, no slicing), and the copy step takes a whole letter (letter_width over the Portuguese wide letters, default 1). Now exit 31 on C and Rust: all five words (ação, eduardo, exame, dumont, acqua) rewrite as expected. My earlier record-with hypothesis is withdrawn for good. Next grain: load the rule table from the upstream por.hgl text instead of the hand list.")
inl eoie_uses_lib () = Task ("EOIE-USES-LIB", "eoie builds on lib like alphabet", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,lib,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_1 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:44Z", "main-thread", "15:44 main-thread root cause (verified): alphabet reaches lib through packageDir ../../deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-, a copy inside its own tree. eoie regenerates in a hermetic staging copy (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 copies only the eoie tree, rejects links as Unexpected workspace link, skips vendor), so a packageDir into the sibling spiral repo would dangle; plus every eoie package is no-core. That is why eoie re-grew reflection (pipe tables), sm' (rust_std_string), core operators (i32_eq helpers) and option (validation_result). Plan: (1) a pinned lib snapshot staged with eoie (a deps/spiral-lib copy refreshed by a verb, or the staging step copying a hash-pinned lib), (2) a core-only probe crate to measure the 1k-gate cost of core inlining, (3) move eoie's generic pieces into lib (union_cases_fold is now in lib reflection.spi too), (4) switch eoie packages to |core- one owner at a time, removing the copies. User 2026-10-17: make eoie use lib like alphabet does; reuse what you can, move what is generic.")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_2 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: staged pinned snapshot. eoie keeps a pin file with the hash of spiral/lib; regeneration copies that lib next to the staged eoie tree; packages point at it with packageDir and use |core- and spiral-. No committed lib copy in eoie; a verb refreshes the pin.")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_3 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 (later round): the staged lib follows the tree. Every regeneration stages the current spiral/lib (no pin file, no refresh verb).")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_4 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:13 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the 1,685 raw comparison ops in eoie (1,155 LT, 530 EQ, plus NEQ/LTE/Add/Mult/Sub) wait for this item: once packages get |core-, one mechanical pass per owner rewrites them to plain operators; no interim helper layer. Same for the 10 StaticStringConcat sites (6 of them added today in union_reflection and the agile domain).")
inl eoie_experiments () = Task ("EOIE-EXPERIMENTS", "Pre-registered experiment ledger, one call per step: register (question, arms, run count, primary metric, test, decision rule, timestamp) before any data; record runs from a results table; analyze exactly once at the planned count (Welch two-sided with CI, one-sample t against a prediction, critical values from the Student quantile); refuse or flag any read of the primary metric before the planned count (interim-look guard with a disclosure record); post-hoc addenda kept apart from the verdict; a rendered report. Replaces hand-written analysis scripts and markdown pre-registration tables.", Feature, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,experiments,stats,one-call", "current", "current")
inl eoie_machine_windows () = Task ("EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "Exclusive machine windows shared by all sessions on one box, one call each: request a window class (live GUI runs, test-suite runs, compiler installs, heavy builds) with an expected duration, get granted/queued, release; conflicts between classes declared once (no suites during GUI runs, no compiler use during installs); free-memory admission; receipts readable by every client. Replaces chat messages like \"window open/done\" and the separate heavy-gate script.", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,coordination,one-call,sessions", "current", "current")
inl eoie_standards_checks () = Task ("EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "One-call code-standard checks per package, declared in the package (spiproj or a standards file): no comment lines (directives excepted), no floating point in modules marked integer-only, every test block names its target, line length, no CRLF where the repo uses LF; machine-readable findings; usable as a gate.", Feature, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,standards,checks,one-call", "current", "current")
inl eoie_agile_list_fields () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS", "agile list --json lacks fields agents need for grooming without one show per item: notes count, last note time, deps, tags, created time; plus filters by id prefix and tag, and an oldest-first order (by created time). Today grooming \"oldest first\" needs guessing from title dates.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,json", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_list_fields_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 landed: agile list --json rows carry deps, tags, notes (count) and last_note; notes come from one pass over the state (note_index nominal HashMap built once into list_request), not a scan per task")
inl spiral_diagnostic_locations () = Task ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", "Every type error carries file:line:col and the binding it is in. Seen without a location: ETY-ValueRestriction (\"Metavars that are not part of the enclosing function signature are not allowed\", from an unsuffixed literal flowing into conv) and ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith; the agent had to bisect by hand.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,diagnostics,compiler", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T06:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 02:03 two macro escapes that work but give poor errors: a literal @ needs @@ ($'f(\"@god\")' fails with ETY-UnboundVariable 'Unbound type variable: god' instead of hinting @@), and \\n inside a macro becomes a real newline (a Lua string literal then breaks; \\\\n emits \\n). Suggest: on an unbound @name inside a macro, hint 'use @@ for a literal @'; document both escapes next to the macro syntax.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: today's evidence: a macro-text parse error (# splice in a Gleam tuple) did carry file:line:col, but the earlier Swift force-unwrap case surfaced only as ETY-UnboundVariable on a different definition; the @ hint the tibia lane asked for belongs at the UnboundTypeName message (spiral_compiler.fs:50208, Unbound type variable: %s.) when the name came from a macro splice Next: check whether the macro tokenizer marks spliced type names so the message can add: use @@ for a literal @")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:03 lap 2 grain: answered the tokenizer question by reading code: macro_body maps @ to MTypeLit and backtick to MType (spiral_compiler.fs:43396-43398); the parser keeps them as RawMacroTypeLit/RawMacroType(_, RawTVar(r,name)) (44200-44208), and validate_bound_vars.cmacro (49961) sends them through the plain ctype path, so the unbound @god error is the generic UnboundTypeName (it already carries the splice range, so file:line:col is right; only the hint is missing). Fix prepared, not applied to the tree: lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS/patch.py (4 anchored edits, anchor-checked on a copy: 5 lines added): new UnboundNameId case UnboundMacroTypeSplice of splice_char * name, check_macro_ty in validate_bound_vars for a bare RawTVar splice, message: Unbound type variable: god. Macro text reads @god as a type splice; write @@ for a literal @. Error code stays ETY-UnboundVariable (code = TypeError case name), so no pinned code changes; no test fixture pins the old text (grep). Probes literal_at_unescaped.spi / literal_at_escaped.spi (sizeof of @@god = exit 5). HEAVY QUEUE: lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS/run.ps1 (Debug candidate + 2 C probes, ~12 min); can ride the same Debug build as SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE. Next: after the probe, land patch.py in the tree with the next pack; then the ValueRestriction location half of the item")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:48 lap 3 grain: measured the location half with the installed compiler (try.ps1, heavy gate, 3 small compiles): both codes the item names now carry file:line:col. value_restriction_conv.spi (inl scaled x = conv x * 2 called with an unsuffixed literal) fails ETY-ValueRestriction at main.spi:4:14, the literal itself; an annotated hole gives 2:17 at conv; record_with_metavar.spi (inl bump r = {r with a = 1i32}) fails ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith at 1:14. What is still missing is the second half of the goal, the binding it is in: lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS/patch-binding.py (2 anchored edits) adds bundle_binding_names next to bundle_range (names of a let/inl, a rec group, a type, nominal, prototype, instance as prototype + instance, open) and appends In the definition: <names>. to every type error at the one place Infer renders them (errors list map, shared section). Appended at the end, so substring pins (ExpectedErrors Contains, the 208 new ones included) still match. The three lab patches (field splice, @ hint, binding) apply stacked on a copy. run.ps1 now applies patch.py + patch-binding.py and runs 4 probes (expect 4:14 ... In the definition: main. and 1:14 ... In the definition: bump.). The HEAVY QUEUE row stays the same script. Next: after the Debug probe, land both patches with the next pack and rerun the ExpectedErrors replay")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:26 main heavy queue ran the Debug candidate (10:24-10:26), half met: locations are right (value_restriction_conv: main.spi:4:14 ETY-ValueRestriction ... In the definition: main.; record_with_metavar: main.spi:1:14 ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith ... In the definition: bump.; literal_at_escaped compiles and exits 5), but literal_at_unescaped reports main.spi:2:20 ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound type variable: god. In the definition: main. without the expected hint write @@ for a literal @, so the UnboundMacroTypeSplice branch did not fire (patch anchor or condition); fix and rerun before it joins a compiler pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:27 lap 4 grain: impact check for patch-binding.py (suffix In the definition: <names>. on every type error). Consumers of compiler error text across all repos (grep of .ex/.exs/.ps1/.py/.spi/.rs): matrix ExpectedErrors use substring Contains (all 251 pins plus the 13 queued ones end before the message end or at its last period, so a suffix after the period keeps them matching); Cells test fails <text> directives (tibia and lib) are substring matches; Cells' own tests feed fake compiler messages (cells_test.exs:181/197, notebook_report_test.exs:314-728), so they never see the real suffix; no consumer compares a whole error line for equality. One place to watch: Cells' value_result? heuristic parses path:line:col: prefixes, which the suffix does not touch. So the binding suffix is safe to land with the @ hint patch. Still waiting for the queued Debug candidate run")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:40 fix for main's note 5 (hint did not fire): the @god error was not raised by validate_bound_vars but by term inference: Infer's RawMacro arm (spiral_compiler.fs ~51194) sends each RawMacroType/RawMacroTypeLit body through ty_init, whose RawTVar case (~51318-51322) adds the plain UnboundTypeName when v_ty env x is None, so my validate_bound_vars branch never saw this program. patch.py gains a 5th edit at that inference site: a bare RawTVar splice whose name v_ty cannot find reports UnboundMacroTypeSplice ('@' or backtick) and skips ty_init. It anchors on SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE's rewritten infer_parts loop (the same RawMacro arm), so it applies after that patch: the three lab patches stack on a copy (field splice 3 edits, this 5, binding 2) and run.ps1 now applies them in that order. The binding half already passed main's 10:24 run (4:14 ... In the definition: main., 1:14 ... In the definition: bump.). HEAVY QUEUE row unchanged (same run.ps1); expect literal_at_unescaped to report write @@ for a literal @ at 2:20")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:30 lap 6: main's queue-3 rerun of run.ps1 (Debug candidate with the 3 stacked patches, built 70 s) meets every expectation: macro_splice_value_restriction_conv reports main.spi:4:14 (and 5:5) ending In the definition: main.; macro_splice_record_with_metavar reports 1:14 ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith ... In the definition: bump.; macro_splice_literal_at_unescaped now says at 2:20 Macro text reads @god as a type splice; write @@ for a literal @. In the definition: main.; literal_at_escaped compiles and exits 5. So both errors named in this item carry file:line:col and the binding. Left for Done: patch.py rides pack 7 (after SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE, whose rewritten loop it anchors on) and the pack's review shows no residual changes. Progress 85.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:47 lap 7: pack 7 stack re-checked on a scratch copy of today's spiral_compiler.fs in main's order: SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE (3), this item's patch.py (5) and patch-binding.py (2), GLEAM-JS (3), EBPF (now 3), FSX-FROM-SPI, SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE, plus the two lane-2 candidates OPENQASM3 (2) and stack-frames (2). All exit 0, so this item's patches still apply after the field-splice rewrite they anchor on. Main was told.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:22 lap 8 grain: pack 7 is applied in the tree (UnboundMacroTypeSplice and the In the definition text present), so the old run.ps1 would have failed re-applying the patches. run.ps1 now detects the applied tree, skips patch+candidate build, runs the 4 probes against the pack build (Get-SpiralCompilerDll single-flight, -Configuration Debug default) and refuses a DLL older than the tree; the pre-pack path is kept. Parses clean. Done check after main's pack 7 build: run.ps1 under 2 minutes, expectations unchanged")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:50 lap 9 grain: cross-checked run.ps1's expected texts against the pack 7 text now in the tree: the splice report is Unbound type variable: X. Macro text reads @X as a type splice; write @@ for a literal @. (spiral_compiler.fs:50222), raised from both inference arms (51222 @, 51223 backtick) and from validate_bound_vars (49913); the binding suffix In the definition: <names>. is appended to every type error at 51853. Those are exactly the strings the 4 probes expect, so the post-build check is a pure run")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:25 lap 10 grain: run.ps1 readiness checked without running it: the tree has UnboundMacroTypeSplice (pack 7 applied), so it skips the candidate build; the installed Release SpiralCompilerCore.dll (12:43:16) is newer than spiral_compiler.fs (12:37:14), so the staleness guard passes; the run is just 4 try.ps1 C compiles under Invoke-Heavy (about 1-2 min). Next: main runs run.ps1 after the accept and records Done if the 4 expectations print")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_locations_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged); lab run.ps1 on the installed build: literal_at_unescaped reports 2:20 with write @@ for a literal @, value_restriction_conv 4:14 + In the definition: main, record_with_metavar 1:14 + In the definition: bump, literal_at_escaped exits 5.")
inl spiral_record_with_inference () = Task ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", "Record update {r with field = v} on an unannotated parameter of a top-level inl fails with ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith (top-down inference has no row type for it yet); expression bases (calls, local let results) and lambda parameters already work (probes 2026-10-19)", TaskKind, P2, D4, 40u32, Active, "", "spiral,inference,records", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:18Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:18 main-thread analysis (no change): Infer's RawRecordWith (spiral_compiler.fs ~51041-51159) reads the base expression's type through f', which already visit_t's the fresh var, so the TyMetavar case at the final match is a type that is really still unknown at that point of top-down inference, not a missed substitution; the expected type s is usually a fresh metavar as well (a let binding without annotation), so unifying with s does not help either. A fix needs deferral: record the with-expression as pending, finish the enclosing block, then resolve the keys once the base type is known (as the bottom-up segment does), or fall back to the expected type when it is a known record. Workaround stays: bind the base with an annotation first.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:25 helper-lane lap 1: narrowed with three repros on the installed single-flight compiler (lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE, try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy): {(origin ()) with x = 3} compiles and runs (exit 7) both when origin's return is annotated and when it is unannotated, so the title's claim (an expression base always fails) no longer holds for calls of known top-level functions. It still fails for a base whose type is a metavar at that point: inl moved r = {r with x = 3i32} gives ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith at 1:15 and then a Unification failure at the use site. So the gap is record-with over an unannotated (row-polymorphic) parameter, which top-down inference cannot key without deferral (note 1's design). Next: find the original failing site to see whether it was this shape or a local function result.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:14 helper-lane lap 2: two more shapes probed (Invoke-Heavy, try.ps1 C): a local let make (k) = {x; y} then {(make 0) with x = 3} runs (exit 7), and a lambda parameter fun r => {r with x = 3} passed to inl apply f = f {x; y} also runs (exit 7), because the argument's record type reaches the lambda before the with is checked. The only failing shape left is an unannotated parameter of a top-level inl (inl moved r = {r with x = 3}). The original report came with SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS (both errors arrived without a location in eoie work), so its exact shape is not recoverable; retitled the item to the narrowed gap. The workaround stays a parameter annotation (r : point).")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:34 helper-lane lap 3: decision: won't-fix the inference (by design: the top-down segment has no row types, and keying an unannotated parameter's record would need deferred checking), fix the diagnostic instead. Reading spiral_compiler.fs 51113-51167: all three MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith sites already carry range_of_expr (so the error is located, as lap 1 saw at 1:15), but the text (line 50216) says only that record keys need to be fully known. Staged lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/patch.py (one anchor, --check OK): the message now names the cause and the fix (the base of this record update has no known record type yet; an unannotated function parameter cannot be updated; annotate it, e.g. (r : point)). No sample or ExpectedErrors pin uses the old text (0 hits in tmp/ and in the 08:05 baseline results.tsv), so it rides a pack with no residual changes. Close as Done once a pack carries it.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T21:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:38 helper-lane lap 6: verified on the installed compiler (pack 10). lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/generic_param.spi (inl moved r = {r with x = 3i32}) now fails at 1:15 with the new message: the base has no known record type yet, the top-down segment has no row polymorphism, annotate the base, e.g. (r : point). So the user-facing part is in. The inference gap itself is unchanged: it needs a deferred field constraint on the metavar, a row-like constraint next to CNumber/CFloat that RawRecordWith adds and that the parameter's later unification discharges. That is a design-sized change in Infer, so it is not started here.")
inl spiral_macro_field_splice () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", "In $\"...\" macros, !x.field splices x and then emits the literal text .field into the target (the C compiler then fails on a tuple member). Either splice the field access or reject it with a hint to bind the field first.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,macros,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:27Z", "main-thread", "01:27 main-thread, reported by the Tibia lane from Lua-backend work: (1) a splice followed by .. in a macro (!a .. !b, with or without spaces) fails as ETY-MissingBody located at the binding's = instead of the macro, while (!a) .. (!b) works: the same !x. lexing as this item. (2) @ inside a macro string is read as a type-variable splice (Unbound type variable: god from a literal @god), and a \\n escape is unescaped into a real newline, which breaks Lua string literals; workaround: splice Spiral string literals.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T06:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 02:03 related but separate: SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (a splice before the closing ' swallows it). Field access on an opaque foreign value ($'!p.x' over a nominal = $'any') works and emits p.x, which Lua interop relies on; the C tuple case in this item is the one to fix.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:42 main-thread: part (1) of the 01:27 note is fixed: since the SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE lookahead fix the tibia lane reports \\$'!a .. !b' : string compiling to v0 .. v1 and running (corpus check 10-18). Left is the C tuple case. Plan, type-aware so opaque interop keeps working: in Infer's macro handling, when a term splice is followed by text starting with '.' plus an identifier and the spliced value's type is a Spiral record, tuple or union (not a nominal over a macro type such as \\$'any'), report a diagnostic at the splice: field access is not spliced; bind the field first (inl f = x.field) or splice it as !f.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:42 main-thread on part (2) of the 01:27 note: both are macro-text escapes working as designed, with existing spellings. @ starts a type-literal splice, and lib writes a literal @ as \\@ (every Zig arm's \\@panic). A \\n in macro text is processed into a newline like the other escapes, so a target-language string that needs a backslash-n writes \\\\n. Worth a line in the macro docs; no compiler change needed.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T20:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:55 hit again by the Tibia lane (lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/hunter_adjacency_probe.spi): printf(... !h.target ...) with h a 24-field record emitted TupleCreate2(v143, ..., v166).target and gcc failed ('Tuple2' has no member named 'target'). Workaround: bind inl target = h.target first. A compile-time error ('splice a variable, not a field path') would have saved the gcc round trip.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:27 helper-lane lap 1: fix prepared, not applied to the tree: repro lab/SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE/record_field.spi ($'!h.target' : i32 over a record) still emits v0 = TupleCreate0(5l, 2l).target and gcc fails (Tuple0 has no member named target) on the installed compiler. lab patch.py (3 anchors, dry-applied cleanly to a copy: +16/-5) adds TypeError case MacroSplicedFieldAccess with a bind-the-field hint, and in Infer's RawMacro arm (shared section, line ~51194, both cores) walks the parts: a RawMacroTerm followed by RawMacroText starting with . plus a letter or _ whose spliced type is TyRecord or TyPair reports at the splice; nominals (opaque $'any' interop such as Lua p.x) and the .. operator text are untouched. HEAVY QUEUE: C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab\\SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE\\run.ps1 (Debug candidate from a cache copy + 3 probes, ~12 min); landing then needs the usual corpus check and one regression sample.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:15Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:15 helper-lane lap 2: regression sample staged for landing with the fix (memory rule: one minimal sample per fixed compiler bug): lab/SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE/sample/macro_field_splice_rejected/ (main.spi = the record_field repro, package.spiproj with |core-) plus sample/matrix-entry.txt, the ExpectedErrors line Contains A macro splices the variable only. On landing: copy the folder to apps/compiler/tmp/samples, add the line to tests/matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors, then the review shows the new rows. Side find for the main lane: tests/matrix.psd1 lists samples/reactors_negative_port_type twice in ExpectedErrors (lines ~224 and ~226, identical Contains).")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:25 main heavy queue ran the Debug candidate (10:23-10:24): field_splice_record_field fails at compile with main.spi:5:7 ETY-MacroSplicedFieldAccess (A macro splices the variable only, so the field access after it is emitted as text. Bind the field first (inl field = x.field) and splice that.), field_splice_bound_field and field_splice_dotdot_text compile and exit 5 as expected; ready for a compiler pack (apply patch.py to spiral_compiler.fs, review, accept).")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T14:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:36 helper-lane lap 3: corpus-risk read for the pack after main's 10:23 candidate run: lib/spiral has 243 macro sites of the shape !name.field inside single-quoted macros (sm' 66, date_time 27, am' 17, threading 14, listm' 13, async 11, ...), but the new check runs in top-down inference and fires only when visit_t of the spliced term is already TyRecord or TyPair there. Generic helpers splice forall-typed values (TyVar at that point), and the sites read are strings, arrays and Rust handles (.len(), .clone(), .borrow()), so none should trip; a record or tuple spliced before .field was already miscompiled text before the patch. The pack's corpus check (lib Cells gate + apps/spiral + tibia C/WGSL) is what proves it. Landing set unchanged: patch.py + sample/macro_field_splice_rejected + its ExpectedErrors line.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:53 pack 7 assembled (runs after the core switch accept): 7 compiler patches stack cleanly on one copy of spiral_compiler.fs in this order: SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE patch.py (3 edits), SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS patch.py (5) + patch-binding.py (2), GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS patch.py (3), EBPF-C-PROFILE patch.py (2), FSX-FROM-SPI patch.py, SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE patch.py; all 22 changed hunks are in shared sections (no one-sided gpt5parallel pair, so pair-drift passes); plus the SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS accept-prune.py edit to test.ps1. Job script: gaps/pack7-job.ps1 (apply, Release build, -Native review against the switch accept).")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 12:55 pack 7 review clean: single-flight-20261019-124451 (built from the tree with 8 patches: field splice, diagnostic locations x2, gleam-js, ebpf cap flag, fsx export, record-with message, openqasm inf/nan; accept-prune; Zig,C oracle flip; keep-old nominal order in the core), 7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual changes exactly Ebpf 52 + Gleam 25 as predicted, 0 behavior changes beyond the 1,189 oracle-column relabels of the Zig-first flip; stack-frames.py dropped (pair-drift). Accept running.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_field_splice_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged); ETY-MacroSplicedFieldAccess ships (candidate probes 10:24, unchanged residuals in the review).")
inl spiral_unforgeable_witnesses () = Task ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", "Typed-domain gaps: module-private union constructors exist (union private, verified 2026-10-19: no qualified or opened construction outside the module, matching still allowed); still missing a nominal-preserving record update, so {r with field = v} on a nominal keeps the nominal (today: rejected with ExpectedRecord) and a field-type change on a structural record stops being silent", Feature, P2, D5, 80u32, Active, "", "spiral,types,gadt,modeling", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:27 helper-lane lap 1: measured today's constructor visibility (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, witness.spi with union proof = | Proof : i32, try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy): from another module witness.Proof 7 compiles and runs (exit 7), open witness then Proof 7 also works, and a match on Proof outside the module works (exit 8); only an unqualified Proof without open is unbound. So plain-case unions are forgeable. Correction (lap 2): tibia's negatives fail because readiness.spi declares union private ready group, a privacy modifier the language already has; see note 2.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:16 helper-lane lap 2: first half of the title already exists: union private makes constructors module-private. Probes (try.ps1 C, Invoke-Heavy, lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/private_*.spi): private_witness.Proof 7 from another module fails with ETY-ModuleIndexFailed (The module does not have the key: Proof), open private_witness then Proof 7 fails with ETY-UnboundVariable, while private_witness.issue 7 (the module's own constructor function) runs (exit 7) and an outside match on Proof still works (exit 8) - exactly the readiness-witness pattern; tibia/src/readiness.spi uses it (union private ready group). GADT syntax alone gives no privacy (gadt_* probes all compile; my lap-1 note claimed otherwise and is corrected). Second half still open, measured: {h with current = 2.5f64} on a structural type hp = {current : i32; max : i32} compiles silently into a new record type (exit 9), while a nominal hp rejects record-with entirely (ETY-ExpectedRecord), so there is no nominal-preserving update yet.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:38 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the nominal-preserving record update as lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/nominal_with_patch.py (top-down RawRecordWith, spiral_compiler.fs ~51157; anchor --check OK). Rule: when the single base's type is a nominal whose body (after type_apply_split + subst of its vars, the same unwrapping field access uses at 50999) is a record, and there are no withouts, each with field must exist in that record and its value is checked against the field's type (modify: field -> field), and the result type is the nominal itself; a missing field reports RecordIndexFailed at the field, a type change is a unification error at that field. Every other base keeps today's path (structural records still extend or retype silently: that half is a separate decision). The base is now inferred once and shared by both paths. Risk: PartEval's RecordWith may not unwrap a nominal value, which the candidate run shows. Probes: with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps (result passed to a function taking hp) expect exit 9, nominal_type_change expects a compile error, with_type_change (structural) stays exit 9; run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate and runs all four on C and Rust. HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:34 helper-lane lap 4: closed the risk named in lap 3 before the candidate runs: single-flight's partial evaluation of record-with (spiral_compiler.fs 159778-159833) only accepts a DRecord base and raises Expected a record otherwise, so the top-down change alone would have failed at peval. nominal_with_patch.py now also rewrites that tail: a DNominal(DRecord fields, nominal_ty) base with no nested path is updated field-wise and rewrapped as DNominal with the same nominal type; nested paths and other values keep today's error (anchors --check OK). The gpt5parallel side (157420, a different stack-checked implementation) is not patched: its suite no longer gates accepts, so a nominal record-with there stays an error until that core is ported. The queued run.ps1 picks the change up unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:44 main: nominal_with_patch.py taken out of pack 8: the Release build failed with FS0010/FS3118 at the patched Infer code (spiral_compiler.fs ~51180-51191), because a one-line nested match (match visit_t ... with TyRecord fields -> Some fields | _ -> None) swallows the following | None -> None arm; wrap the inner matches in parentheses (or split them into let bindings) and prove it with a Debug candidate build before the next pack. The tree was restored to the accepted pack 7 source (core-src staging 3f21d9014f3e7723).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T20:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:46 helper-lane: the nominal-preserving record update works on a private-cache Debug candidate (build log lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/build-20261019-1558.log; the earlier build failures were an F# local named base, a keyword, now with_base). Probes (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/probes.ps1 on that DLL): with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps (the result passed to a function taking hp) compile and exit 9 on C and Rust; nominal_type_change ({h with current = 2.5f64} on the nominal) is rejected with a unification failure at 5:37, the field's value; the structural with_type_change still compiles (exit 9), as intended for this slice. nominal_with_patch.py rides main's pack 10.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T21:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:32 main: pack 10 INSTALLED 17:32 (nominal-with, Zig Assign as a statement, export-types nominal walk): Release candidate review single-flight-20261019-1723xx vs the pack 9 accept 161549 = 0 differing cells of 7,057 (no sample exercises these paths), DISAGREE 0, shared DLLs hash-equal to the candidate; the baseline does not move, so no separate accept run; the features are proven by helper lane 1's probes on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:33 helper-lane: pack 10 installed (17:32, 0 differing cells vs pack 9). On the installed compiler (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/probes.ps1, now parametrized with -Dll/-Tag): with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps exit 9 on C and Rust; nominal_type_change is rejected (unification failure at 5:37); with_type_change, the structural case, still compiles silently (exit 9). The nominal-preserving record update is in. Not Done: the title's last clause, that a field-type change on a structural record stops being silent, is still open. Next: decide whether to make it an error or a warning (it changes the type of every structural {r with ...} that retypes a field, so it needs a corpus count first).")
inl spiral_literal_defaults () = Task ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", "Unsuffixed integer literals summed into a local leave a numeric metavar that later breaks conv (value restriction). Check whether per-package default-int (pack-1k) closes it; if not, default literals in local bindings or point the error at the literal.", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,inference,literals", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_defaults_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:51Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:51 helper-lane lap 1: answered the item's question with probes on the installed compiler (lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS, try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy): per-package/default ints do NOT close it. inl total = 3 + 4 then conv total fails with ETY-ValueRestriction (Got: _ -> _ -> _ at the +), the same with dyn locals (inl ~a = 3), the same with SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT=i32 set (the env var the --default-int host flag sets), and even inl wide : i64 = conv 7 fails (Got: _ -> i64, error at the literal 2:27). So an unsuffixed literal whose only consumer is conv stays a bare numeric metavar and is never defaulted. The error position already lands on the literal, but the message is the generic value-restriction text. Fix location: Infer's bundle finish (spiral_compiler.fs ~51819-51820) raises ValueRestriction for every annotation with metavars; defaulting metavars whose only constraints are numeric to the default int/float there (Haskell-style defaulting) before that check would close all three shapes.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_defaults_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:35 helper-lane lap 2: found the mechanism in Infer: an unsuffixed literal is RawDefaultLit, typed by unify r s (fresh_subst_var scope (Set.singleton CNumber) KindType) and registered with annotations.Add(x,(r,s)) (spiral_compiler.fs ~50971; the same for the other default-literal path ~51451); at the end of the bundle (~51819-51820) every annotation whose type still has metavars becomes ETY-ValueRestriction. So a literal that only flows into conv keeps its CNumber metavar to the end and is reported, which explains why SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT does not help: the default env is used for the int/uint/float type names (~51858), never to resolve a CNumber metavar. Fix sketch: just before the ValueRestriction pass, walk the annotations and link each still-unbound metavar whose constraint set is exactly CNumber to TyPrim default_env.default_int (CFloat-only ones to default_float); everything else keeps today's error. Needs a Debug candidate and the corpus run, since defaulted literals change residual types where code relied on the error.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_defaults_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:19Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:19 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the defaulting step as lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS/patch.py (one anchor at the ValueRestriction pass, spiral_compiler.fs 51819-51820, --check OK). Checked the names first: MVar is {mutable scope; mutable constraints : Constraint Set; kind} (49465) and an unbound metavar is TyMetavar(m, link) with link.Value = None (49494); infer gets no DefaultEnv, but top_env'.ty (infer's parameter, 50461) holds the base types where int and float are TyPrim default_env.default_int/float (base_types, 51858-51860), so the step reads the defaults from there and therefore follows SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT and per-package defaults. Rule: just before the pass, each annotation whose visit_t is still an unbound metavar with constraints exactly {CNumber} links to int, exactly {CFloat} to float; anything else keeps today's error. run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate from a copy and runs the three lab probes on C (expect exit 8 each). HEAVY QUEUE row added; a residual review comes after, since a default can change residual types where code relied on the error.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_defaults_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T19:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 15:56 helper-lane: Done. The defaulting step (lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS/patch.py) shipped in pack 8b, accepted 15:46 (single-flight-20261019-145950: 7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0; the review attributed no residual change to it, as expected: it only affects programs that used to stop at ETY-ValueRestriction). Evidence on the installed pack 8b compiler (try.ps1 C, bounded): conv_direct, summed_local and summed_dyn - unsuffixed literals that only flow into conv - all compile and exit 8, where before they failed with ETY-ValueRestriction. A metavar whose constraints are exactly CNumber now defaults to the package's int, and exactly CFloat to its float.")
inl gpu_rental_readiness () = Task ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", "Run WGSL lane workloads on rented GPUs at scale: a headless GPU runner outside Windows (Linux, Vulkan/Dawn or wgpu), many-launch batching and multi-GPU dispatch, u64/i64 emulation for integer overflow budgets on WGSL, a storage boundary designed once over host arrays and GPU storage buffers (prototype instances per backend), a throughput-per-dollar benchmark, and a reproducible remote setup script; budget tranches need the user's approval.", Epic, P2, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "gpu,wgsl,infra,backend", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_1 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:41 cost split, from public 2026 prices (rough, check live pages): game rollouts are CPU work (OpenAI Five: ~128k preemptible CPU cores for simulation, 256 GPUs only for inference and optimization; a Dota 2 server is headless Linux, no GPU), so a rollout box is a CPU rental: Hetzner AX42 dedicated ~EUR 68/month after the April 2026 increase (~EUR 0.09/h), CCX33 8 dedicated vCPU ~EUR 138/month after the June 2026 increase. GPU for training: RTX 4090 on vast.ai ~USD 0.15-0.50/h (median ~0.35-0.50), L4 ~0.39/h, A100 80GB 1.19-1.89/h, H100 1.99-3.89/h on RunPod. A small policy net trains on one 4090 or even CPU; H100-class only for large models. Plan: CPU boxes for rollouts plus a short GPU rental for optimization, not GPU machines for the game.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_2 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:28 helper-lane lap 1: readiness audit of the existing runner (spiral-wgsl, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run, wgpu 30 + pollster, nightly-2026-10-18): no Windows-specific code; Instance::new(InstanceDescriptor::new_without_display_handle_from_env) plus default adapter then fallback adapter, so on headless Linux it picks Vulkan (WGPU_BACKEND env can force it) - item 1 (headless runner outside Windows) needs only a Linux build, no code. Batching already exists: SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES runs N lanes in chunks (dispatch_workgroups(chunk/64), per-lane heap slice, per-lane i32 results), SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING(_LOG) records timings, adapter info is read. Missing for rental: multi-GPU (one adapter only; would need enumerate_adapters + a lane split across devices), u64/i64 emulation (WGSL backend still has no i64), and a remote setup script. So the first rental tranche can measure throughput-per-dollar with lanes on one GPU unchanged.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_3 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:17 helper-lane lap 2: drafted the reproducible remote setup as text only (nothing rented or run): lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS/setup.sh for an Ubuntu GPU box - apt build-essential, libvulkan1, mesa-vulkan-drivers, vulkan-tools (an NVIDIA rental ships its own ICD), rustup minimal (the runner's rust-toolchain.toml pins nightly-2026-10-18), shallow clone of i574n/spiral, cargo build --release of tests/native-shims/wgsl-run, vulkaninfo summary, then a lanes sweep (1, 1024, 65536, 1048576) over samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes/main.wgsl with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING, printing wall ms per lane count - the throughput-per-dollar input. Budget tranches still need the user's approval before any rental.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_4 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:40 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the laptop baseline as lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS/baseline.ps1, the Windows twin of setup.sh's sweep: the installed runner (spiral-bin/toolchains/wgsl-run/release/spiral-wgsl.exe, built 07:23 today, so no build step), the same shader (samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes/main.wgsl) and lane counts (1, 1024, 65536, 1048576) with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING, inside Invoke-Heavy, writing host/lanes/ms/exit rows to a baseline-<stamp>.tsv in the lab folder. That tsv is the denominator for the per-dollar table: a rental row only counts if it beats the laptop at the same lane count. GPU use is the main thread's, so HEAVY QUEUE (about 2 min). Rental tranches still need the user's budget approval.")
inl cells_repin () = Task ("CELLS-REPIN", "A failing _assert_eq reports actual and expected as structured data in the run summary, and one call rewrites the literal in the source to the actual value (per test, with a diff to confirm). Today agents scrape the notebook output with regexes and edit long tuple literals by hand after every intended model change.", Feature, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", "cells,testing,one-call", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_repin_1 () = Note ("CELLS-REPIN", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: design anchor: _assert_eq is testing.spi:82 over __expect (=) trace name b a (testing.spi:78-80); structured actual/expected would come from __expect emitting both values as a typed report line the runner parses, then repin rewrites the literal at the test's _assert_eq call site; per-target status already flows to tests.tsv (--metrics), so the report row can join on (file, test, target) Next: read __expect's failure path to see what it prints today per backend")
inl note_of_cells_repin_2 () = Note ("CELLS-REPIN", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:04 lap 2 grain, corrects 0 percent: most of this item already exists. __expect (testing.spi:14-45) prints __assert_eq { actual = A; expected = B } (sm'.show of a record) through console on failure, then assert fails; Spiral.Cells.Accept (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/accept.ex, in HEAD since 59b477ba 10-17) parses exactly that line and rewrites the failing _assert_eq literal in place, picked by how many asserts passed before it; mix spiral.notebook --accept (notebook_cli.ex:81, notebook.ex:376-440) reruns the accepted tests up to 25 rounds and marks rows accepted, or failed with accept_refused; helper lane 1's CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES (uncommitted in accept.ex/notebook.ex now) adds flat tuples and puts accept_refused in the summary. What is left of this item: (1) structured actual and expected on failed report rows (today only message = the first line); a with_mismatch step in Notebook.report/3 mapping Accept.mismatch(verdict message) to actual/expected keys, and the same two columns in --metrics tests.tsv; (2) the confirm diff (print old and new line per accepted site). Not edited now because notebook.ex and accept.ex carry helper lane 1's uncommitted CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES work; dep recorded. Progress 60. Next: after CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES is Done, add with_mismatch + a notebook_report_test case (queue mix test)")
inl note_of_cells_repin_3 () = Note ("CELLS-REPIN", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:49 lap 3 grain: the structured half is prepared as lab/CELLS-REPIN/with_mismatch.py (4 anchored edits to notebook.ex + 1 ExUnit case): Notebook.report/3 pipes each row through with_mismatch(verdict), which uses Accept.mismatch on the full failure message to add actual and expected keys to failed rows (the JSON report then carries them), and summary_lines gains mismatches = test name -> {actual, expected} on every spiral-tests file line, so callers read both values from stdout with no regex of their own. It anchors on CELLS-GATE's failed_targets lines, so it applies after lab/CELLS-GATE/failed_targets.py; both stack cleanly on copies of today's notebook.ex (with helper lane 1's uncommitted accept_refused lines) and parse with Code.string_to_quoted!. The queued lab/CELLS-GATE/failed-targets-run.ps1 now applies both, runs notebook_report_test.exs, and restores both files unless it passes. Left after that: the confirm diff on --accept (old and new literal per site). Next: add the confirm-diff line to Accept.accept results (old site text -> new) as a third patch once helper lane 1 commits accept.ex")
inl note_of_cells_repin_4 () = Note ("CELLS-REPIN", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:51 lap 5: the structured half LANDED (main's 10:14 run of the combined runner: notebook_report_test 30 passed incl. the actual/expected case; summary lines carry mismatches). The last part, the confirm diff, is prepared: lab/CELLS-REPIN/confirm_sites.py (7 edits). Accept gains accept_sites/2, which returns {name, {:ok, before_line, after_line}} for each rewritten _assert_eq (the one line rewrite/5 changed, trimmed); accept/2 keeps its {name, :ok} contract by delegating, so existing callers and tests are untouched. notebook.ex's --accept loop uses accept_sites, keeps {:accepted, before, after} in its outcome, puts accepted_site on accepted rows, and summary lines gain accepted_sites = test -> {before, after}, so an --accept run prints exactly which literal changed. New accept_test case. Checked standalone (file_lock.ex + patched accept.ex loaded with elixir): accept_sites returns {one, {:ok, |> _assert_eq 2i32, |> _assert_eq 1i32}} and the file is rewritten; accept/2 still returns {one, :ok}. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-REPIN/confirm-sites-run.ps1 (accept_test + notebook_report_test, restores unless both pass). After it passes: Done")
inl note_of_cells_repin_5 () = Note ("CELLS-REPIN", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 DONE with evidence: main's queue 11:00 ran lab/CELLS-REPIN/confirm-sites-run.ps1: accept_test + notebook_report_test 38 passed; the patch stayed (accept.ex:65 accept_sites/2, notebook.ex:291-294 accepted_sites in summary lines). Both halves are in the tree: failed rows carry actual/expected and mismatches, --accept prints the before/after line per test. Uncommitted in apps/cells for the user.")
inl cells_gate () = Task ("CELLS-GATE", "One-call gate over a list of files: memo off, per-file and total pass/fail/executed/memo-hit summary, failed test names with actual/expected, daemon retired at the end, deferral while GUI or live runs hold the machine (see EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS); multiple --test names or a pattern in one run.", Feature, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "cells,testing,gate,one-call", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_gate_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:39 main-thread: most of the gate already exists in NotebookCli (repeated --path, --test with several names or comma lists, --no-memo, per-file and total spiral-tests summaries with failed test names). Added mix spiral.notebook --gate: implies --no-memo --no-html and retires the compiler daemons at the end (CompilerClient.shutdown) instead of prestarting the warm pool. Compiles clean; first real use after today's bless (crypto and date_time lib slices). Left: actual/expected per failed test in the summary (overlaps CELLS-REPIN) and deferral while live runs hold the machine (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS).")
inl note_of_cells_gate_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T14:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:33 --gate wrote <file>.spi.ipynb beside every checked source; 11 untracked reports from my 10:08/10:23 runs landed in the blog engine and in lib/spiral, now removed. Fix in tree: Mix.Tasks.Spiral.Notebook.gate_args adds --output-dir <tmp>/spiral-cells-gate-<n> unless the caller passed --output-dir/--output-path, and the task rm_rf's that dir after the run. Test notebook_gate_args_test.exs 2/2 (with notebook_targets 3/3).")
inl note_of_cells_gate_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T14:31Z", "main-thread", "Correction (10:31): note 2 was written at 10:30, not 10:33.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T15:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:57 main-thread: the shape the tibia lane asked for is in place. mix spiral.notebook --metrics <dir> (NotebookCli switch; Notebook.metrics_rows/write_metrics) writes <dir>/tests.tsv with one row per target of every test-mode block: File (the source stem), Test, Target, Status, Ms, Memo. Those are the columns of tibia's target/ci-metrics/tests.tsv, built from the same report as the notebook metadata (spiral_tests), so it works with --gate, whose notebooks go to a deleted scratch dir. Tests: notebook_metrics_test.exs 2/2, with gate_args 4/4 in total (private MIX_BUILD_PATH). apps/cells/README.md documents --gate and --metrics.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_5 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T16:06Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:06 main-thread, planned after today's accept run (it touches tests/native-shims, which should stay fixed between git-0e's review and the accept): name the test in WGSL timing rows (tibia lane request). Every runner row currently names its shader main.wgsl. Plan: Targets.run gets the step's test name (test_cell_names, already computed in Notebook.execute) in opts; tool_env adds SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST=<name> to child processes (per child, not System.put_env, since cells run in parallel); wgsl-run main.rs uses SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST, when set, as the shader column of SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING_LOG; then rebuild the runner. With the per-backend shim key, only the Wgsl rows' native memo is invalidated.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_6 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:41 main-thread: the SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST patch from note 5 is ready in lab/CELLS-GATE/apply_cells_test_name.py (7 anchored edits, each checked to match once; dry run on copies applies). notebook.ex: with_builders puts :test_name on test steps (from test_cell_names), and dispatch_spiral passes it into the run opts. targets.ex: tool_env adds SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST to each child's env (List.keystore, per child, never System.put_env). wgsl-run main.rs: the timing log's shader column is SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST when set, else the shader path. Apply after the accept, then rebuild the wgsl-run runner and run one WGSL test through Cells with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING_LOG to see the name.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_7 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T17:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:52 main-thread: SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST applied after the accept (lab/CELLS-GATE/apply_cells_test_name.py on the tree). Cells compiles without warnings; new targets_test_name_test.exs passes 2/2 (sets the name, overwrites an old value, leaves env alone without a name); the notebook targets/metrics/gate tests still pass 6/6. wgsl-run main.rs now uses SPIRAL_CELLS_TEST, when set, as the timing log's shader column. Left: rebuild the runner (queued behind the Debug compiler build), then one WGSL test through Cells with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING_LOG to see the test name in the row. This invalidates only the Wgsl rows' native memo (per-backend shim key).")
inl note_of_cells_gate_8 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: gap found while probing sm' today: the gate's stdout summary carries failed test names but not the failing target or actual/expected; the per-target status already exists behind --metrics <dir> (tests.tsv: File, Test, Target, Status, Ms, Memo via notebook_cli.ex:88), so lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1 now passes --metrics and prints the not-passed (test, target) rows Next: make --gate print the failed (test, target) pairs on stdout by default, so callers need no metrics dir")
inl note_of_cells_gate_9 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:05 lap 2 grain: the failed (test, target) pairs on stdout are prepared as lab/CELLS-GATE/failed_targets.py (dry run: 3 anchors in notebook.ex + 1 in notebook_report_test.exs each match once): summary_lines gains failed_targets = test name -> targets whose status is failed (target statuses are passed, failed, unsupported, not_run per Spiral.Cells.record_target cells.ex:106-118), on every per-file spiral-tests file JSON line, so the gate's stdout names the failing backend with no --metrics dir; plus an ExUnit case. Not applied: its anchors sit on helper lane 1's uncommitted CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES lines in summary_lines (accept_refused), so it applies after that work settles. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-GATE/failed-targets-run.ps1 (applies the patch, then mix test notebook_report_test.exs in the heavy gate, own MIX_BUILD_PATH, ~3 min). Next: after the run, drop the --metrics parsing from lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1 in favour of failed_targets, and add actual/expected (CELLS-REPIN) to the same line")
inl note_of_cells_gate_10 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:49 lap 3: lab/CELLS-GATE/failed-targets-run.ps1 rewritten to apply failed_targets.py and CELLS-REPIN's with_mismatch.py together, run notebook_report_test.exs in the heavy gate, and restore notebook.ex and the test file unless the run passes (it left patches in place on failure before). Same HEAVY QUEUE row; still waits for CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES (helper lane 1, 90 percent, code-complete, test queued)")
inl note_of_cells_gate_11 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T14:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:16 main heavy queue ran lab/CELLS-GATE/failed-targets-run.ps1: notebook_report_test 30 passed (failing-targets and actual/expected summary cases included), so the patch stays in the tree (10:14).")
inl note_of_cells_gate_12 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:49 lap 5 grain, goal audit after main's 10:14 run landed failed_targets + mismatches (note 11, 30 tests): one call over a list of files (--path repeatable) yes; memo off and compilers retired at the end (spiral.notebook --gate adds --no-memo --no-html and calls CompilerClient.shutdown) yes; per-file and total summary yes; failed test names with actual/expected yes (failed_tests, failed_targets, mismatches); several --test names yes; a pattern: missing, now prepared: lab/CELLS-GATE/test_patterns.py makes a --test containing * a wildcard over each file's test names (one file or several; unknown patterns still error), exact names behave as before (existing case unchanged), plus a new ExUnit case; standalone check on a copy: show* -> show_floats, show; show_* + parse across 2 files -> per-file lists; zz* -> unknown test zz*. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-GATE/test-patterns-run.ps1 (~2 min, restores unless it passes). Left after it: deferral while GUI/live runs hold the machine, which is EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (eoie lane); dep to record. Progress 85")
inl note_of_cells_gate_13 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:51 data for the gate default: the same sm'.spi gate is 537 s with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process and 217 s on the warm daemon, results identical (42/42); a warm default (with process as an opt-in for isolation checks) would cut every lane's gate time by about 60 percent.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_14 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:25 DONE with evidence, the deferral part split off as CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW (dep EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS, which owns window classes and replaces the heavy-gate script; nothing for Cells to do until it exists). Every other part is in the tree, checked in code and runs: one call over a list of files (mix spiral.notebook --gate with several --path, chunked in lab census scripts); memo off and HTML off and a scratch report dir (spiral.notebook.ex gate_args), compilers retired at the end even on failure (CompilerClient.shutdown before the raise); per-file and total summary lines with passed/failed/executed/memo_hits plus failed_targets (main's 10:14 run, 30 passed); actual and expected per failed test as mismatches and --accept before/after (CELLS-REPIN, 38 passed 11:00); several --test names or a * pattern (test-patterns-run 10:57, 31 passed, patch kept: notebook_cli.ex pattern?/1). Open data point from main 10:51 for a separate decision: a warm-daemon default would cut gate time about 60 percent (537 s process vs 217 s warm, same 42/42).")
inl cells_daemon_lifecycle () = Task ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "Compiler daemons started by a run outlive it (about 2 GB each with their --server-tcp compilers) until someone sends retire by hand; retire on run end or after an idle timeout, and list live daemons with their owner run.", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,daemon,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T12:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:41 main-thread audit: most of this exists. compiler_daemon.ex retires itself after SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN idle minutes (default 30, checked every 60 s, only with nothing queued), and CompilerClient.shutdown retires every daemon on the client's ports when mix test ends. mix spiral.notebook deliberately prestarts the pool afterwards (warm reruns), so a notebook run leaves its daemon warm for up to 30 min by design. Observed today: one Cells daemon held 3 server-tcp compilers of 600-780 MB each (2.1 GB) during the 04:49 memory squeeze that reaped a probe chain. Left: (1) list live daemons with port, pid, owner command and idle minutes (a mix spiral.compiler_daemon --list over the pool ports); (2) a lower idle default when the machine is shared, e.g. 10 min, or scale it by free memory at each idle check. Recommendation: (2) memory-aware, retire at the idle check when free memory is under 4 GB and nothing is queued.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:55 main-thread implemented (2) in the tree: the daemon's per-minute idle check also retires it once it has been idle for at least one check interval with nothing queued and free memory is under SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_RETIRE_FREE_MB (default 4096), read through os_mon memsup (started lazily, available_memory else free_memory, any failure means no early retire); the log line names the reason. mix.exs excludes :memsup from xref, as os_mon stays out of extra_applications on purpose. mix compile clean in a private MIX_BUILD_PATH; the Cells suite run is queued after today's bless. Left: (1) listing live daemons.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:59 main-thread implemented (1) in the tree: the daemon protocol answers status (status, OS pid, code stamp, idle minutes, queued compiles, retiring), CompilerClient.live_daemons probes the 16 ports from the base port (an older daemon closes the socket on an unknown line and lists with empty fields), and mix spiral.compiler_daemon --list prints them as a table. Compiles clean (private MIX_BUILD_PATH); --list against the machine now: no compiler daemon is listening (this morning's 3 compilers idle-retired). Left: an ExUnit case that lists a test daemon, with the Cells suite run after today's bless.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:01 main-thread: tests in test/spiral/cells/compiler_daemon_status_test.exs (async false): an idle daemon retires early when free memory is under the threshold (threshold forced high, idle_ms 60 s, check 50 ms), and the status line reports [status, os pid, stamp, 0, 0, false]; with compiler_daemon_idle_test: 4 tests, 0 failures (private MIX_BUILD_PATH, 4.9 s). Found on the way: Mix prunes the code path to declared applications, so os_mon must be in extra_applications (it is now; memsup starts at boot) and the xref exclude is gone. Full Cells suite still to run after today's bless.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: the remaining 5 percent is 'list live daemons with their owner run': mix tasks today are compiler_daemon, edit, html, notebook, render_cells, targets, x_compat; the status line exists ([status, os pid, stamp, ...], compiler_daemon_status_test) but no list-all view Next: add mix spiral.compiler_daemon list (one row per live daemon: port, os pid, owner run, age, memory)")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1: the list view already exists (mix spiral.compiler_daemon --list over CompilerClient.live_daemons, in HEAD 80b16019 10-18: port, pid, stamp, idle_min, queued, retiring). The real gap was the owner run: daemons are spawned detached (Start-Process / setsid), so the OS parent chain is gone and the pid names only the daemon's own BEAM. Applied in the tree (lab/CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE/daemon_owner.py, 9 anchored edits): the spawner passes SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_OWNER = os_pid N argv <spawning command, 160 chars, tabs and quotes blanked> in both the Windows and Unix spawn envs; the daemon appends it as a 7th status field (unrecorded when unset); live_daemons accepts 5 or more fields, so an older daemon lists with an empty owner; --list prints an owner column; compiler_daemon_status_test now expects the 7th field. Parse-checked with Code.string_to_quoted! (4 files); not compiled. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE/owner-run.ps1 (mix test of the status + idle tests, then --list, own MIX_BUILD_PATH, ~3 min). Memory per daemon stays out: the free-memory retire rule covers the 2 GB case. Next: after the run passes, mark Done with that output as evidence")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_7 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:16 main heavy queue ran lab/CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE/owner-run.ps1: 4 passed (compiler_daemon_status_test with the owner field), --list reported no compiler daemon listening at that moment (10:14).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_lifecycle_8 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:48 DONE with evidence: every part of the goal is in the tree and tested. Retire on idle (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN, default 30) and early retire under memory pressure (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_RETIRE_FREE_MB, default 4096) with compiler_daemon_idle_test/compiler_daemon_status_test (10-18); CompilerClient.shutdown retires the pool when mix test ends; list live daemons with their owner run: mix spiral.compiler_daemon --list prints port, pid, stamp, idle_min, queued, retiring and owner (the spawner passes SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_OWNER = os_pid + argv, the 7th status field). Proof: main's heavy queue ran lab/CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE/owner-run.ps1 at 10:14, 4 tests passed including the status test with the owner field (note 7), and the Cells compile gate of 09:51 compiled the edited compiler_client/compiler_daemon/mix task cleanly")
inl cells_probes () = Task ("CELLS-PROBES", "First-class probe runs: compile and run a small main against a package's modules, optionally with text-patch variants of those modules, without copying the package into a scratch folder and without a test block; print the program output. Today experiments use a hand-made scratch project with copied sources and a variant script.", Feature, P1, D4, 60u32, Active, "", "cells,probes,experiments", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_probes_1 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:54 prototype in the tibia repo: lab/tibia-wgsl-shader-size/measure.ps1 copies a package, applies file|old|new text rewrites, turns named test blocks into entry modules (definitions-only copies for test-only files), compiles each to a backend and records bytes/compile/run to a TSV; sweep-let.ps1 drives per-function variants. It found a 35% WGSL reduction in one afternoon, so the shape is worth making first-class.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_2 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 2u32, "2026-10-18T09:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:29 the prototype now lives at tibia lab/cells-probes (moved from tibia-wgsl-shader-size when that item closed): measure.ps1 (package copy + text rewrites + test bodies as entries, compile, record), sweep-let.ps1 (per-function inl->let variants), time-variants.ps1 (cold/warm GPU and lavapipe timings through SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING). Promote into cells or delete it when this item lands.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_3 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: main has a second prototype of the same need: lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1 (gate one lib file against a candidate core package via SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, metrics per target, raw output); with the tibia lane's lab/cells-probes (package copy + text rewrites + timed variants) that is two shapes to unify into one Cells probe verb Next: list the common options of both prototypes (package override, text patch, target, raw output) as the probe verb's arguments")
inl note_of_cells_probes_4 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: option inventory of the two prototypes, mapped to one verb. main's lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1: -File (one lib file), -Lib (source dir), -Candidate (package dir via SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR), -Show/-Detail/-RawOut (output trimming, raw dump), fixed process compiler + memo off + SPIRAL_CC + private MIX_BUILD_PATH, --gate --metrics then not-passed (test, target) rows. tibia lane's lab/cells-probes/measure.ps1 + time-variants.ps1: -Label (run dir under spiral-bin/runs), -Source (copied *.spi), -Tests (file/test names), -Backend Wgsl|C, -Runner + -RunnerLabel (runner override), -RunTimeoutSec, -Rewrite file|old|new (text patch, throws when old is absent), -CompileOnly, -Variants + -Repeat + -Cold (timed variant sweep), -Adapters (GPU adapter env). Common core = 6 arguments: source (dir or file), package override, test selection, target, text patch (file|old|new, must match), raw output; plus timeout and label. Proposed verb: mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi or test name> --package <dir> --patch file|old|new (repeatable, applied in an overlay mount, never in the source tree) --target <t> --raw <file> --timeout <s> --label <run>; variant sweeps stay a caller loop over --patch sets; adapter env stays the caller's (WGSL only). Reuse: the overlay mount already exists (cells-mounts), --test :keep and --metrics exist in notebook_cli.ex. Next: read how cells-mounts builds its overlay to see whether a --patch can apply to the mounted copy only")
inl note_of_cells_probes_5 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:49 lap 3 grain, the overlay question answered by reading apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/mounts.ex: a mount dir (spiral-bin/cells-mounts/<16-hex key>) holds one entry per package, kind :link (a junction to the package) or :shadow (a real dir whose package.spiproj is rewritten to packageDir: .. and every other child is linked back: junction for dirs, and for files mklink, then mklink /H hard link, then a copy). The key hashes entry names, kinds, targets and the shadow's package.spiproj mtime, and a ready marker makes a built mount reusable. Consequences for --patch: (1) never write through a mounted file, because a hard-linked file IS the source (a patch would edit the tree); (2) a patched mount must not share the unpatched key. Design: a third entry kind :patched (package target + list of {file, old, new}) built like :shadow, except each patched file is written as a fresh file (the link for that one name is skipped), the edit throws when old is absent (the tibia prototype's rule), and key/1 adds the sha256 of the edit list, so patched and plain mounts coexist and both are cached. Probe verb args stay as in note 4 (--probe, --package, --patch file|old|new, --target, --raw, --timeout, --label). Next: draft the :patched kind as a patch to mounts.ex (build + key) with an ExUnit case that checks the source file is untouched after a patched run")
inl note_of_cells_probes_6 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:25 lap 4 grain: the --patch overlay exists as a patch and works standalone. lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched_mounts.py (4 anchored edits to mounts.ex + new mounts_patched_test.exs): mount entries gain kind :patched with patches [{file, old, new}]; build shadows the package like :shadow but skips the patched names (shadow/3 with a skip set, the old shadow/2 unchanged), writes each patched file fresh (never through a hard link), replaces the first occurrence, refuses an absent old text, and refuses nested files (top-level package files only in v1); key/1 adds a 16-hex digest of the patch list to the shadow stamp, so plain and patched mounts get different cached dirs. Standalone check (elixir loading file_lock.ex + the patched mounts.ex copy, real cells-mounts root): patched dir differs from the plain shadow dir, the mounted probe.spi reads 2i32 while the source still reads 1i32, the unpatched sibling is linked unchanged, a 9i32 patch returns does not apply. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched-mounts-run.ps1 (applies, runs the 2 new cases + mounts_name_test, restores unless they pass; ~3 min). Next: thread a --patch file|old|new option from notebook_cli.ex into the mount entries of the notebook's packages (the probe verb's first user-visible flag)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_7 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:42 lap 5 grain: --patch is now a user-visible flag end to end in the same lab patch (patched_mounts.py, 13 anchored edits over mounts.ex, cells.ex, memo.ex, notebook_cli.ex + a 4-case ExUnit file; stacked on copies of today's files, all parse). mix spiral.notebook ... --patch <file>|<old>|<new> (repeatable; split on the first two bars, file made absolute) reaches Spiral.Cells via the notebook opts; mount_entries turns a package that owns a patched file into a :patched entry, which keeps the package's own packageDir made absolute when it does not share mounted deps (a plain shadow would have rewritten it to .. and broken its imports), or .. when it does; patches join memo.ex @tool_opts so a patched run never reuses an unpatched result; the mount key carries the patch digest. lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched-mounts-run.ps1 now proves it end to end: the unit cases, then a probe package (answer = 1i32) and a notebook asserting 2i32 that must fail without --patch and pass with --patch pkg/probe.spi|1i32|2i32, while the package source stays 1i32; restores the four Cells files unless all of it passes. Same HEAVY QUEUE row (main runs it after the switch). Next: --probe <main.spi> (a run of a plain program with the package overlay, no test block), the second half of the verb")
inl note_of_cells_probes_8 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 patched-mounts-run 10:56: the 7 unit tests passed; the probe failed for two other reasons: (1) the probe dir has no spiral layout ancestor, so open testing was unbound (fixed by the new SPIRAL_LIB fallback, see CELLS-NEW-PROJECT); (2) --patch file|old|new went through mix.bat and cmd.exe treated the bars as pipes ('1i32' is not recognized). --patch now names a JSON spec file (list of file/old/new, files relative to the spec), which also carries multi-line texts; ExUnit case updated; the runner also checks the unpatched probe fails on the assertion only. Re-queued.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_9 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: --patch is implemented end to end as a lab patch (mounts :patched entries, memo key, JSON spec flag, 4 ExUnit cases, probe notebook); the 10:56 run had the 7 unit tests passing, and the re-queued run decides whether it lands. Progress 60.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_10 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:48 lap 7 grain, design for the last part (run a plain main with no test block), chosen so it reuses everything the --patch work built: mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> [--backend c] [--patch spec.json] makes a transient notebook in the gate's scratch report dir. Its header is ///- --package <the package dir of main.spi> plus one test block, test probe =, whose body is main's body indented (main's return value becomes the test result; a unit main runs for its output). The probe runs through the same notebook path, so mounts, :patched entries, memo keys and target dispatch are shared and nothing is copied by hand. The CLI prints the cell's stdout and the exit value instead of the test summary. NotebookCli needs only a probe: :string switch and a branch in execute_parsed before the missing --path check. Waits for the patched-mounts run (the foundation) to land first.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_11 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:23 lap 8 grain: post-switch check of the queued patched-mounts run: patched_mounts.py dry run matches all 13 anchors on today's apps/cells (mounts.ex 4, cells.ex 5, memo.ex 1, notebook_cli.ex 3; new test file), and the probe package's packages line |core- still matches lib/spiral/package.spiproj after the core v1 switch, so the queued row runs unchanged. Next: that run after main's go, then the --probe branch")
inl note_of_cells_probes_12 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:51 lap 9 grain: refined lap 7's --probe design from the report code: Notebook.run_report's test rows hold index, name, status and per-target status/ms only (notebook.ex:176-186, target_rows 231-233), so the CLI cannot print the program's stdout from the report. The probe therefore writes the transient notebook's .ipynb into the gate scratch dir (output_path, as gate_args does with --output-dir) and prints that probe cell's outputs plus the exit/status. And the transient notebook is simpler than re-indenting main's body: the whole main.spi text followed by ///> <target> and test probe = main (), since a test cell's program is the file up to the block plus the body, so every definition in main.spi is in scope. NotebookCli change stays one probe: :string switch and a branch before the missing --path check (notebook_cli.ex:38)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_13 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:26 lap 10 grain: --probe transient notebook text fixed from lib practice: <main.spi text> + a blank line + one ///> <target> line per --target (lowercase target names as lib uses them: c and zig appear 19 times in lib, e.g. testing.spi x6) + test probe = / indented main (); Cells then runs it like any lib test block (prefix = the whole file, body = main ()), and the probe reads the cell output from the scratch .ipynb. Also checked: patched_mounts.py still dry-runs against current Cells (memo.ex 1 anchor, notebook_cli.ex 3, new mounts_patched_test.exs), mounts.ex unchanged since 10:56. Next: queued patched-mounts-run.ps1, then probe.py stacked on it")
inl note_of_cells_probes_14 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:47 lap 11 grain: the transient-notebook builder for --probe is code now, not a design: lab/CELLS-PROBES/probe_text.exs (ProbeText.notebook(main_text, targets): main.spi text without trailing newlines, a blank line, one ///> <lowercased target> per target, then test probe = / main ()), self-checked with plain elixir (scoop OTP first on PATH): the two-target and no-target cases match their expected text (probe_text ok). probe.py will inject this function into notebook_cli.ex next to the --patch switch. Next: patched-mounts-run.ps1 (queued), then probe.py stacked on it")
inl note_of_cells_probes_15 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:54 lap 19 grain: brought the queued run up to today's patch-script rules. patched_mounts.py now checks every file first and writes only after all anchors matched (before, an --apply that missed an anchor in a later file left the earlier files patched), and refuses when mounts_patched_test.exs already exists; dry run still matches 4/5/1/3 anchors (Cells files unchanged since 10:56). patched-mounts-run.ps1 now keeps the originals as bytes and restores with a byte-for-byte check (it used ReadAllText/WriteAllText, which would drop a BOM), and refuses when the new test exists without a backup; parse clean, restore helper checked on a BOM+CRLF sample. Next: the queued patched-mounts-run.ps1 (touches apps/cells, so main runs it with helper lane 1 told)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_16 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 16u32, "2026-10-19T23:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:43 drive grain, the last part of the item is code now: lab/CELLS-PROBES/probe.py adds mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> [--target <backend>]... to notebook_cli.ex (4 anchored edits, all-or-nothing, refuses a re-apply, works on the plain file or after patched_mounts.py's --patch edits): probe_paths writes a transient notebook under the temp dir (a ///- --package header when main.spi sits in a package, main.spi's text, one ///> line per --target, then test probe = main ()) and adds it to --path, so mounts, --patch overlays, memo and metrics all apply unchanged; missing --path now says missing --path or --probe. --self-check runs the exact inserted functions under plain elixir (no mix, no Cells build): notebook text with and without targets, header only with a package.spiproj, no path without --probe, probed source untouched: probe self-check ok; stacked on a --patch-form copy the result parses (Code.string_to_quoted!). Next: queue it after patched-mounts-run (apps/cells edit, main with helper lane 1 told), then an ExUnit case and one real probe run")
inl cells_explicit_targets () = Task ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", "Untargeted test blocks silently follow the global default backend, which changed (to zig); require a target per test block or a declared package default, and warn on untargeted blocks.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,targets,testing", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:16 main-thread first half in the tree: a test block without ///> lines that falls back to the implicit default (no static prelude, no default_target option, no SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET) now prints 'warning: test <name> has no ///> target and runs on the default backend (zig); name its targets' on stderr (Notebook.with_builders + implicit_default?; default_builders unchanged for its tests). Test notebook_targets_test.exs: 1 test, 0 failures (private MIX_BUILD_PATH). Left: make it an error once the warnings are fixed across lib and the apps (count them with one Cells run over lib first).")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:46 main-thread static count across every .spi with test blocks in spiral lib+apps, blog, x, eoie/src, dice, alphabet and tibia (read-only): 72 files, 44 with a static prelude (a 'test <name> static' block, or a '///- ... --test static' line). Mirroring Notebook.implicit_default? plus the per-block ///> check, only lib/spiral/testing.spi would warn: 4 of its 6 tests (assert_approx_eq_2 and _3, which are print_code; assert_contains and _2). They ran on the implicit zig default, so each now names ///> zig, which keeps behavior identical (gate run pending). That makes 0 untargeted .spi tests in these repos. Before turning the warning into an error, also count .livemd notebooks (apps/cells, lisp, spiral, wasm), which this scan did not cover.")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_3 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T15:55Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 11:55 lib gate count (mix spiral.notebook --gate over 13 lib files, scoop OTP 29 / Elixir 1.20, SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0): 0 stderr lines matching has-no-target in every file: base 0, convert 0, crypto 0, file_system 0, guid 0, math 0, networking 0, sm' 0, sm'_real.spir 0, trace 0, leptos 0, rust/near 0, rust/rust 0 (100 tests discovered, 99 executed)")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_4 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T15:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:59 main-thread: the .livemd half can't be counted with text tools. The 11 notebooks (apps/cells/notebooks/spiral, apps/lisp, apps/spiral, apps/wasm, dice and alphabet copies) keep their Spiral in elixir-fenced Livebook cells (or fsharp ones), so test cells need Cells' own parser. Next: a small mix task or an ExUnit case that runs Notebook.parse plus implicit_default?/warn_untargeted over every .livemd without compiling, then make the warning an error if the count is 0.")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_5 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T16:06Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:06 main-thread, three findings. (1) Bug in my 09:16 half: the warning never fired for .spi test blocks. warn_untargeted matched '^test <name>' on step.cell_source, but parse_spi turns a block into a '///- --test' cell whose name sits in the markdown heading before it, so the regex never matched (that's also why git-0e's 13-file gate showed 0). Fixed: execute passes test_cell_names(doc) (Document.tests, mode test, index -> name) to with_builders, which warns by cell index. (2) New mix spiral.targets <paths> [--check]: Notebook.untargeted_tests/1 applies the same parse, implicit_default? and ///> rules without compiling, and --check fails if any exist. Regression test in notebook_targets_test.exs (bare.spi finds 'one'; a 'test x static' prelude covers 'three'); Cells tests 6/6. Corpus scan of spiral lib+apps, blog/engine, x/apps, dice/lib, alphabet/apps (1,371 .spi/.livemd, 55 s): 0 real untargeted tests after testing.spi's 4 got ///> zig. One false positive: apps/cells/spi/document.spi 'cell'. Its test_cell predicate checks that the whole source contains '///- ' and ' --test', and line 113's own string literals satisfy that, so a definition cell counts as a test. Fix that predicate (match a '///- ' line containing ' --test') before the warning becomes an error. (3) Then the error switch is safe: 0 untargeted across the corpus.")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_6 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:16 main-thread: the document.spi fix is ready in lab/CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS/test_cell_directive.spi. It replaces test_cell (document.spi:112-113) with directive_line_has, which walks text.next_line and requires a line that starts with '///- ' and contains ' --test' or ' --ignore', matching LineDone/LineGot unqualified as document.spi already does at :92. Applying it regenerates the shared src/spiral_cells/domain.gleam through the compiler on the next mix compile in any lane, so apply it when no lane runs Cells. Then rerun mix spiral.targets: the document.spi 'cell' false positive should disappear and the count stay at 0.")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_7 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T16:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:24 main-thread: the Cells gate on lib/spiral/testing.spi with the four new ///> zig lines passed 6/6 with 0 untargeted warnings, and left no .ipynb in lib/spiral (the new --gate scratch report dir works in a real run).")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_8 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 8u32, "2026-10-18T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:30 tibia-lane evidence for turning the warning into an error: the tibia repo (src, apps/arena, lab) has 172 test blocks and 0 without a ///> target, so the error mode would not break tibia. The count left is lib/spiral and the other apps.")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_9 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 9u32, "2026-10-18T19:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:17 main-thread: the warning is now an error. mix spiral.targets over spiral lib+apps, blog, x, eoie/src, dice, alphabet and polyglot (1,937 .spi/.livemd, counted with the Cells parser) found 4 untargeted tests, all in polyglot/apps/scheduler/Tasks.spi (get_actions, get_actions_2, cols, lines). They ran on the implicit zig default, so each got ///> zig (behavior unchanged, recount 0); tibia already had 0 of 172. Under the implicit default, with_builders now marks an untargeted test step, run_step fails it with: test <name> has no ///> target: name its backends (for example ///> zig) or declare a default with a static prelude or SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET. The memo never recalls such a step, other cells still default to zig, and the README says so. notebook_targets_test.exs gained a test: 5 passed with the lib-search tests")
inl note_of_cells_explicit_targets_10 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T12:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:28 helper-lane lap 1: DONE with evidence: the untargeted-test error is committed (65f83164: notebook.ex:675 untargeted_message, README.md:230, notebook_targets_test.exs:33) and the document.spi false-positive fix from the lab is committed too (e0ffc80b: directive_line_has at document.spi:112, test_cell at :119); the 10-18 15:17 recount over spiral lib+apps, blog, x, eoie/src, dice, alphabet, polyglot found 0 untargeted tests after polyglot Tasks.spi got ///> zig, and tibia has 0 of 172. Lab cleanup: the applied draft lab/CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS/test_cell_directive.spi (untracked, stem referenced nowhere) and its folder were removed.")
inl cells_new_project () = Task ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", "Project scaffold so a new app starts smooth: package.spiproj with package defaults, module and test-file layout, target conventions with reasons (C as oracle, WGSL for GPU, Lua for embedded hosts, Rust for host-only tests), standards checks and a gate command wired in, a scratch-probe setup, a backlog epic with a starter plan. Lessons from the first full project on test cells.", Feature, P1, D3, 50u32, Active, "CELLS-GATE,CELLS-REPIN,CELLS-PROBES,CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS,EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "cells,scaffold,onboarding", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_1 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:41 base for the next project repo (e.g. a possible Dota 2 one), from comparing x and tibia: binaries follow x (started 2026-10-15): apps/<app>/<crate>/Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, a Cargo workspace, scripts/gate.ps1 failing any crate whose generated Rust exceeds 1000 lines, --x-compat check, e2e against the built exe. Tests and docs follow tibia: src/ package with targeted test blocks (C oracle, WGSL, Lua, Rust only with a reason), scripts/build.ps1 through cells failing unless every row executed and passed, docs/, harness/, results/, gh-pages CI on Ubuntu + Windows with actions.lock. The scaffold should generate this union, so a new repo starts from it instead of copying either.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_2 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 11:49 tibia-lane: working prototype at tibia lab/cells-new-project/new-project.ps1 -Path <empty dir>. It generates a project from tibia's own files with explicit text rewrites (it fails naming the file when a rewrite target drifts): .gitignore, .gitattributes, .editorconfig, scripts/init|build|publish|workflow.ps1, gh-pages.yml with a single build step, actions.lock, plus src/package.spiproj and src/units.spi (phantom-unit amounts with a C oracle test, a C 'fails Unification failure' type test, a WGSL twin and a Lua host test). Proof: generated into a scratch dir with polyglot/spiral junctions beside it; its own init.ps1 -fast passed (7 s) and build.ps1 went green with 4/4 executed and passed, plus the ci-metrics files and the WGSL row (688 bytes, 111 ms compile, 172 ms run on the local GPU). Shared pieces moved into spiral so generated projects do not copy them: scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (already there) and the new scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1 (wgsl runner build, DXC beside it on Windows, lavapipe in HKLM on Windows CI, mesa-vulkan-drivers on Linux). Left: the deps CELLS-GATE/PROBES/REPIN/EXPLICIT-TARGETS and EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS, plus a home for the generator (cells mix task or eoie verb; it stays in the lab until then), x's Cargo binary layout and the 1000-line gate.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_3 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: deps state: CELLS-GATE 50, CELLS-REPIN 0, CELLS-PROBES 0, CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS 95, EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS (eoie lane); the tibia lane's new-project.ps1 prototype exists (tibia lab/cells-new-project); scaffold should also emit the .cargo/config.toml Linux rustix cfg if the project pulls NEAR deps (lesson of today) Next: after CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS closes, run the prototype into an empty lab dir and gate the generated project")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_4 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:09 lap 2 grain: CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS is Done (helper lane, 08:28), so the scaffold trial is unblocked. Verified the tibia lane's prototype (tibia lab/cells-new-project/new-project.ps1, read only) still applies today: all 4 rewrite anchors match the current tibia scripts/build.ps1, scripts/workflow.ps1 and .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml, and the 6 copied files exist. It emits src/package.spiproj (packages |core-, module units) and units.spi with 4 targeted tests: c oracle, c expected failure (fails Unification failure), wgsl, lua. No Cargo project, so lap 1's .cargo rustix cfg does not apply to this scaffold (only to one that adds Rust/NEAR deps). Still missing against the item: backlog epic and starter plan, standards checks, scratch-probe setup (waits on CELLS-PROBES). HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/scaffold-gate.ps1 (scaffold into spiral-bin/runs/cells-new-project/<stamp>, then the Cells gate on src/units.spi, memo off, ~4 min). Next: after the gate passes, port new-project.ps1 into spiral as the scaffold home (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT), parameterized by a project name instead of tibia's rewrites")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_5 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:50 lap 3 grain, home decided: the scaffold becomes a Cells mix task, mix spiral.new <path> [--name <project>], not a ported pwsh script. Reasons: PWSH-FREE (new tooling should not add pwsh), Cells already owns the gate the scaffold wires in (mix spiral.notebook --gate) and the targets list (mix spiral.targets), and its siblings are mix tasks (compiler_daemon, edit, html, notebook, render_cells, targets, x_compat). Templates live as files under apps/cells/priv/new_project/ (gitignore, gitattributes, editorconfig, src/package.spiproj, src/<name>.spi with the units starter: c oracle test, c expected-failure test, wgsl and lua tests), copied with the project name substituted; the CI workflow and the pwsh build/publish scripts stay out of v1, since they are tibia-specific (GPU legs, arena) and the gate command replaces build.ps1 for a pure Spiral project. Kept from the tibia prototype: refuse a non-empty target dir, print every written file. Next: draft lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex + the priv templates as a lab patch with an ExUnit case (scaffold into a tmp dir, files present, name substituted), queue its mix test with the existing scaffold gate")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_6 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:14 drive slice: mix spiral.new is written, as new files only, in lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/ (mirrors apps/cells): lib/spiral/cells/new_project.ex (Spiral.Cells.NewProject.create(path, name) -> {:ok, files} | {:error, {:invalid_name, n} | {:not_empty, path}}, refusal_text/1; templates copied with PROJECT_NAME substituted in names and text, dotfiles stored undotted), lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex (mix spiral.new <path> [--name], prints each file and the gate command), priv/new_project/ (gitignore, gitattributes, editorconfig taken from tibia's, src/package.spiproj, src/PROJECT_NAME.spi = the units starter: static prelude, c oracle test, c expected-failure test, wgsl and lua tests) and test/spiral/cells/new_project_test.exs (2 cases: files and substitution; non-empty dir and bad name refused, dir untouched). Checked without mix: all 3 .ex/.exs parse, and new_project.ex loaded standalone (elixir, one boot) creates ledger with the 5 files, package.spiproj = packages |core- / modules ledger, first line test ledger static =, refuses the now non-empty dir and the name Ledger. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/spiral-new-run.ps1 copies the files in (refuses to overwrite), runs the 2 tests, scaffolds into spiral-bin/runs/spiral-new/<stamp>/ledger, gates its ledger.spi, and removes the added files unless all three pass (~6 min). Next: after it passes, the backlog-epic and standards-check parts (deps CELLS-GATE, EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS)")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_7 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 both 10:56 runs failed for one reason: the scaffold sits in a cache dir with no spiral/lib/spiral ancestor, so Cells mounted no lib and open testing was ETY-UnboundModule (scaffold-gate only looked passed because it never checked the gate exit). Product fix in the tree: notebook.ex find_spiral_lib falls back to SPIRAL_LIB when no layout lib is found (spiral_lib_from_env/1, layout still wins), new case in notebook_spiral_lib_test.exs, 3 passed in a bounded job 11:05. Both runners set SPIRAL_LIB, scaffold-gate checks the exit code; both re-queued. Edit in apps/cells notebook.ex (helper lane 1 FYI).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_8 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:31 lap 6 grain, the gate command wired in (one part of this item's list): the scaffold now writes scripts/gate.ps1 (template lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1): it finds spiral beside the project or through SPIRAL_ROOT, sets SPIRAL_LIB from it when unset (so a project anywhere gets lib, the 10:56 failure), and runs mix spiral.notebook --gate over every src/*.spi (or the -Path list) from Cells, passing the gate's exit code through. new_project_test expects the new file and checks it names spiral.notebook. spiral-new-run.ps1 now gates the scaffold through the generated script with only SPIRAL_ROOT set (SPIRAL_LIB cleared), so the queued run proves the out-of-tree path end to end. Parse-checked. Left: scratch-probe setup, the backlog epic with a starter plan, standards checks (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_9 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:48 lap 7 grain, the backlog-epic part: mix spiral.new now ends with the two next steps. The gate line is pwsh <path>/scripts/gate.ps1 (the generated script, replacing the raw mix line). The epic line is the exact eoie call for a starter epic, from NewProject.epic_command(name): eoie agile add <backlog root> <NAME with dashes> Epic P2 D5, title '<name>: starter plan (units module, c/wgsl/lua targets, scripts/gate.ps1 green)', tags <name>. It prints the command and does not run it: Cells does not call eoie, and the backlog root is the user's choice (eoie-first: one call, copy-paste ready). The ExUnit case checks ledger_v2 -> LEDGER-V2. All 3 patched files parse (Code.string_to_quoted!). The queued spiral-new-run covers it. Left: the scratch-probe part, after CELLS-PROBES --probe exists.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_10 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:24 lap 8 grain: post-switch check of the scaffold templates (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project): PROJECT_NAME.spi uses nominals, i32 arithmetic and testing only, no array literals or am. calls the core v1 switch rewrote; open testing and _assert_eq still resolve (lib/spiral/testing.spi:82) and the package line |core- matches lib's spiproj. So the queued spiral-new-run stays valid after the switch. Next: that run after main's go")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_11 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:52 lap 9 grain: the scratch-probe part of the scaffold is drafted as lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/pending/scripts/probe.ps1: it resolves spiral and SPIRAL_LIB exactly like the generated gate.ps1 and runs mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> --target <c by default> [--patch <spec.json>] from Cells, passing the exit code through (parses clean). It stays outside patch/priv/new_project for now, because the scaffold copies every file there and the script would call a flag that does not exist until CELLS-PROBES lands --probe; then it moves in, new_project_test's exact file list gains scripts/probe.ps1, and spiral.new prints a probe line next to the gate line")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_12 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:28 lap 10 grain: found and fixed a restore leak in lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/spiral-new-run.ps1: on failure it removed the placed files but kept the directories it created, so the 10:56 run left an empty untracked apps/cells/priv/new_project/src (no file of the patch is in the tree, all 9 files tree=no). The script now records each directory it creates and removes it on failure if empty (deepest first); parse check 0 errors; the two empty leftover directories were removed (no files inside, untracked). Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1 row; probe.ps1 moves into patch/priv once CELLS-PROBES --probe lands")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_13 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: cross-checked the staged ExUnit test against the scaffold payload: new_project_test.exs names all 6 priv/new_project files in their scaffolded form (.editorconfig, .gitattributes, .gitignore from the dotless sources, scripts/gate.ps1, src/package.spiproj, src/ledger.spi from PROJECT_NAME.spi), plus the busy-target and existing-dir cases, so adding probe.ps1 later means one more entry (scripts/probe.ps1) in that list. Next: queued spiral-new-run.ps1 (now leak-free), then probe.ps1 into patch/priv after CELLS-PROBES --probe")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_14 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 12: code reading found that spiral.new on a path that is an existing file crashed in mkdir_p instead of refusing; the lab patch now refuses with {:is_file, path} (own refusal text), new_project_test.exs asserts it, and a standalone elixir check shows the refusal with the file untouched. Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_15 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:54 lap 19 grain: spiral-new-run.ps1 placed the 9 patch files into apps/cells before its try block, so a copy that failed halfway would have left the earlier files behind; the copy now runs inside the try and the existing cleanup removes every placed file and created directory on any failure. Parse clean; none of the 9 files exists in apps/cells yet (the add-only precheck would pass). Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1 (main runs it, helper lane 1 told since it adds files to apps/cells)")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_16 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 16u32, "2026-10-19T23:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:44 lap 22 grain: the scaffold gains scripts/probe.ps1 (lab patch/priv/new_project/scripts/probe.ps1: -Main <file> [-Target c,zig], same Cells and SPIRAL_LIB resolution and heavy-gate wrapping as gate.ps1, runs mix spiral.notebook --gate --probe <main> --target ...), now that CELLS-PROBES' probe.py implements --probe; new_project_test.exs lists it (7 files) and checks it names --probe. Standalone check with plain elixir (the lab new_project.ex against the lab priv) creates the 7 files in order. Order note for main: probe.ps1 needs probe.py applied first; spiral-new-run.ps1 itself only runs gate.ps1, so the two lab patches can land in either order")
inl cells_exhaustive_tests () = Task ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", "Bounded-domain exhaustive and property test blocks: compare two implementations over a declared domain (for example an integer reimplementation against a floating-point oracle over all inputs up to N), report the first counterexamples, run on the oracle backend only.", Feature, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,testing,properties", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:52 helper-lane lap 1: first prototype, library-only (no Cells change): lab/CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS/first_mismatch.spi defines let rec first_mismatch lo hi reference candidate over i64 and checks x / 3 against the multiply-shift trick (x * 43691) >>> 17: no counterexample on 0..65535 and the first one at 131072 on 0..200000 (expected value computed independently in PowerShell). Compiled and run with the installed compiler via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy: C exit 0 and Zig exit 0 (the let rec loop runs 200k iterations on each without stack issues). So the exhaustive part needs no new Cells feature, just a lib helper plus a test block on the oracle backend; what Cells could add is the report (first N counterexamples printed in the cell output instead of a pass/fail exit).")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:36 helper-lane lap 2: moved the helper into lib (uncommitted, additive): lib/spiral/testing.spi gains counterexamples limit lo hi reference candidate : list i64 - a tail-recursive let rec over the domain that collects up to limit mismatching inputs (accumulate then listm.rev, so 200k steps stay iterative on C), plus test counterexamples_find_where_multiply_shift_stops_dividing_by_three on c and zig: no counterexample below 65536 and the first two at [131072; 131075] (both values computed independently in PowerShell). This is the report form the item asked for (first counterexamples, on the oracle backends only) without a Cells feature. HEAVY QUEUE: Cells gate on lib/spiral/testing.spi (~5 min).")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_3 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:52 helper-lane lap 3: ran the testing.spi gate as a bounded eoie heavy job (83 s): 5 passed, 2 failed - assert_approx_eq (an existing test) and the new counterexamples test - both because the tree's lib/spiral/sm'.spi is mid-edit by another lane and does not compile (sm'.spi:246-247 ETY-UnboundVariable obj_to_string_fsharp / obj_to_string_python, reported to main). So no verdict on counterexamples yet; the queue row stays queued until sm'.spi compiles again.")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_4 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:08Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:08 helper-lane lap 3: after main fixed sm'.spi (its gate passed 42/42 at 10:00) I reran the testing.spi gate as a bounded eoie heavy job: it got the slot at once but did not finish inside the 120 s bound this time (the machine is under main's 2.5 h heavy job; the first run took 83 s), so the tree was stopped with no verdict. The queued Cells gate row stays the way to prove counterexamples on c and zig.")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_5 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:19Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:19 helper-lane: main's queue 3b ran the testing.spi gate on the accepted pack 7 compiler: exit 0, 6 of 7 tests passed; the failing one is the new counterexamples_find_where_multiply_shift_stops_dividing_by_three, whose zig target reports not_run with 0 type errors (c's status not reported in the summary). The gate wrote no notebook, so the cause (zig skipped after the c target failed, a zig build failure, or a timeout over the 131k-value domain) is not visible; rerun alone with --output-path into the lab folder when the gate is free.")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_6 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T20:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:57 helper-lane: rerun with a notebook (lab/CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS/testing-20261019-1648.log) found the cause: the counterexample test fails on c, not in the search itself, but at the assertion: _assert_eq on a list value makes the C program format the list through Cells' console module, which has no C arm (gcc: backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_cells_console undeclared); zig was then not run. Changed the second assertion in lib/spiral/testing.spi to a scalar: the two counterexamples are packed as packed * 1000000 + x and compared with 131072131075, so no list formatting is needed on any target (edit in lib testing.spi, my own test). The first assertion already compared a length. Rerun pending.")
inl note_of_cells_exhaustive_tests_7 () = Note ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T21:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:35 helper-lane: Done. A bounded-domain exhaustive test passes in lib: lab/CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS/rerun.ps1 (testing-20261019-1733.log, installed pack 10 compiler) runs the lib/spiral/testing.spi gate with 7 of 7 tests passed and failed_targets empty. The test is counterexamples_find_where_multiply_shift_stops_dividing_by_three: counterexamples compares x / 3 with (x * 43691) >>> 17 over the declared domains, finds none on 0..65535, and finds the first two at 131072 and 131075 on 0..200000. It runs on Zig, the oracle target. The C target was dropped: any _assert_eq on C goes through Cells' console module, which has no C arm yet (backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_cells_console). That is lane 2's CELLS-EVERYWHERE console_c_arm.py, and a ///> c line can be added back when it lands. The second assertion compares a packed scalar (a bool), not a list, so no target needs list formatting. lib/spiral/testing.spi is uncommitted, adding counterexamples and the test, for the user's commit.")
inl tibia_gpu_rental () = Task ("TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL", "Train at GPU scale on rented machines once the model ranks policies like live: needs GPU-RENTAL-READINESS and TIBIA-AGENT; local measurement 0.23 ms per arena clear per lane after ~4 s launch setup; estimate cost per million clears before asking the user for a budget tranche.", Feature, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "GPU-RENTAL-READINESS,TIBIA-AGENT", "tibia,gpu,training", "current", "current")
inl tibia_start_sync () = Task ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", "Live layer: start every competitor's logic at the same moment after login (the policy probe acts ~2.5 s after login, bots at once); needed before the next win-seeking comparison.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,harness,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_start_sync_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:12 analysis from the code, nothing run. competitor.spi ready(): +1500 ms (chasing off, balanced fight), then +1000 ms before the first tick, with started_at at that moment, so our policy acts at login + 2.5 s. observer.spi (vBot, nExBot): client.enable_client_bot runs before login, so the bot acts as soon as the client's bot module loads at login, and the observer's started_at is login + 1 s. Both the head start (about 2.5 s of free bot actions) and the clock origin differ. Options: (a) move our start to login + the time the bot module needs to load, with one shared start-delay constant in probe.spi used by both; (b) enable the client bot from inside ready() at the same delay as our policy, if game_bot can be switched on at runtime (needs a live check, since today it reads the setting at login); (c) at least give both the same clock origin (started_at at the same login offset), so clear times compare even before the head start is removed. (b) is the fair one if OTClient allows it; check with one live vBot run that it starts acting only after enabling.")
inl note_of_tibia_start_sync_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", 2u32, "2026-10-18T16:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:14 option (b) implemented after reading OTClient's mods/game_bot/bot.lua: refresh() reads the bot settings node each time it's called and loads the bot whenever the client is online. At login, online() schedules refresh 20 ms later, which is why bots used to act at once; save() writes only bot storage, never the settings node. Changes (uncommitted, built, not run live): probe.spi start_delay_ms 2500 and settle_ms 1000, shared by both sides; the competitor's 1500 + 1000 delays use them (unchanged timing); client.start_client_bot = enable_client_bot plus modules.game_bot.refresh(); observer.spi keeps the client bot disabled before login (it used to enable it) and starts it at login + start_delay_ms with started_at at that same moment, so vBot/nExBot and our policy start acting and timing at login + 2.5 s. All 9 probes compile and parse; vbot.lua shows the disable at load, scheduleEvent(.., 2500) and modules.game_bot.refresh(). To verify in the next live block: one vBot run whose log shows no bot action (attack, potion) before the start note. Bot baselines before this change had a ~2.5 s head start; compare only against runs after it.")
inl note_of_tibia_start_sync_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", 3u32, "2026-10-18T18:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 14:28 verification ready: observer.spi now stamps its server texts with @t+ ms since login and logs 'client bot starts @t+T'; lab/tibia-start-sync/check.ps1 -Logs <vbot/nexbot logs> reports the bot start and the first bot action (an attack hit or a potion) and fails on any action before the start. Planned for today's 15:30-17:00 live block.")
inl note_of_tibia_start_sync_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", 4u32, "2026-10-18T19:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:41 verified live (results/arena-results-cyclops-startsync-1018.tsv, lab/tibia-start-sync/check.ps1): vBot started at t+3336 ms after login, first bot action at t+12605 ms; nExBot started at t+3169 ms, first action at t+12210 ms; both 'ok' (no action before the start). Both cleared the 3-cyclops arena (vBot 88.7 s, 15 potions; nExBot 82.4 s, 16 potions), so enabling the client bot mid-session with the settings node plus modules.game_bot.refresh() works. Done; bot baselines from now on have no head start.")
inl tibia_tools_to_eoie () = Task ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", "Move tibia-local helper scripts onto the generic eoie verbs once they exist: pr1/pr2 analyses to EOIE-EXPERIMENTS, log and TSV questions to EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, live layer and standards checks to EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS; BENCHMARK pre-registrations become experiment records (with TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE).", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-EXPERIMENTS,EOIE-TABLE-QUERY,EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "tibia,eoie,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:58 dependency EOIE-TABLE-QUERY is Done (table query/diff landed 2026-10-18 15:53), but the installed C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe is from 2026-10-17 16:46 and answers 'unknown verb: table'. First migrations ready once it is installed: per-arm counts and means over results/arena-results-*.tsv (today Import-Csv | Group-Object in run-arena summaries and lab compare scripts), and the zig-oracle compare.ps1 join (table diff --key file). Log-corpus questions (shield-split.ps1, adjacency-summary.ps1, pc3-analysis.ps1) still need the regex-to-columns shape noted on EOIE-TABLE-QUERY 2026-10-17.")
inl tibia_probes_in_spiral () = Task ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite the OTClient probes (harness/*.lua, about 2,600 lines in 21 files) as one Spiral probe library compiled with the Lua backend, as live.spi already does for the policy: typed bindings for the g_game/g_map/creature calls the probes use, one login/hide/log/observe/finish module instead of 18 copies, experiment bodies as Spiral functions. Main gain: the live probe and the simulator share decision and movement code (the 2026-10-15 retreat-step mismatch needed StepAwayLikeTheLiveProbe because the probe step was separate Lua); also dogfoods the Lua backend and macros. Target layout: apps/arena/ (like alphabet apps/documents).", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY", "tibia,harness,lua,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_probes_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:29 first slice done (dogfooding the Lua backend): tibia/apps/arena with client.spi (typed OTClient bindings over opaque native values: spots, tiles, scheduling, login, logging), probe.spi (the lifecycle 18 Lua probes copy: hide/mute, note to /tibia-probe.log, described, finish, started with login and timeout), scan.spi (the arena scan probe, 25 lines vs 99). apps/arena/build.ps1 compiles every probe with a main through spiral's BuildSpiral -Backend Lua into target/arena and parse-checks with lua; scripts/build.ps1 runs it; run-policy-scenario takes a rooted -ProbeBody. Live check: original probe-scan-body.lua vs Spiral scan.lua on the same world, same knight tile, all 26 lines identical; the Lua original is deleted. Lua-backend findings (filed on SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE): splice followed by .. fails as MissingBody at the wrong place, @ in a macro is a type splice, \\n in a macro becomes a real newline. Next probes: walk, steps, path (movement), then policy/diag (share decision code with src/).")
inl note_of_tibia_probes_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-18T06:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 02:54 written, not yet compiled (compiler hold for the tokenizer fix): client.spi extended (creatures, spectators, attack/chase, potions, walking, tile footing incl. ramps, env, clock, Lua cells and tables, session login with text handler), probe.spi (started_as with character, text handler and timeout action; finish_reporting_parity; finish_once), summon.spi (env TIBIA_SUMMON_AT/TIBIA_SUMMON_ORDERS; live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 now uses it through Update-ArenaProbes in live/paths.ps1), competitor.spi (port of probe-policy-body.lua; decisions from src policy/network via packageDir ../.. + src-), vbot.spi (observer). Removed 7 unreferenced Lua probes (dangling-checked across repos). Next: compile, live-compare each against its Lua original, retire the originals; then nexbot, random, path, steps, measure, policy-diag, scan-unknown.")
inl note_of_tibia_probes_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-18T07:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:40 all arena competitors run from Spiral and were verified live against their Lua originals: competitor (rules branch: cleared 3 trolls, 559 ticks/558 strikes/1 hold vs Lua 580/579/1; kiting branch: 3 step-aways, cleared 44.7 s), vbot observer (cleared 36.3 s), summon (GOD /m orders), random control (cleared 50.2 s). Shared modules: scoreboard (tally, summary, end rules), observer (bot watch/observe, used by vbot and nexbot). Lua originals probe-policy-body, probe-vbot-body, probe-summon retired; run-policy-scenario -ProbeBody takes an arena probe name (default competitor) and builds target/arena via Update-ArenaProbes; run-arena uses vbot. Compiler issues found on the way: SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG (worked around), SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT (do...end in client.spi), macro # is an inline-term splice (write ##), @ needs @@.")
inl note_of_tibia_probes_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-18T08:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 04:59 nine Spiral probes now: competitor, random, vbot, nexbot (observer with game-call diagnostics), summon, scan (TIBIA_SCAN_MARKS_UNLOADED=1 replaces scan-unknown), path, measure, steps. TIBIA_ARENA_ADJACENCY=1 in the competitor replaces policy-diag. Layers: lua.spi (generic Lua-side state, the reusable part), client.spi (OTClient only), probe.spi (lifecycle, nearest monster, finish guards), scoreboard/observer/adjacency. Verified live today: competitor rules and kiting, competitor after the module split, adjacency, random, vbot, nexbot, summon, scan (26/26 identical to the reference), path (road steps 452-464 ms, as in PC-2), measure (faithful; it keeps a lost target exactly like the original). Retired Lua: policy, vbot, summon, nexbot, random, path, measure, policy-diag. Left: steps (fixed a nil player position at login, needs one live rerun) and scan-unknown (marks mode needs one live run), both after the compiler lane's heavy jobs. Also fixed: apps/arena/build.ps1 leaked its working directory into scripts/build.ps1, which is why both CI jobs of the 03:09 push failed.")
inl note_of_tibia_probes_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-18T09:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:29 DONE: every live probe is Spiral, no probe Lua is left in live/. The last two were verified live: steps (17 straight steps, median 2022 ms; the nil player position at login is fixed) and scan with TIBIA_SCAN_MARKS_UNLOADED=1 (? outside the client's loaded window). lua.spi carries 5 Lua-targeted test blocks, and scripts/build.ps1 now also runs apps/arena test files (5/5 green through cells). The lab repro moved to lab/spiral-lua-closure-record-arg (it belongs to that item: delete it once the compiler lane's fix is installed and the repro exits 0).")
inl tibia_map_tool_in_spiral () = Task ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite live/otbm-walkability.py (242 lines: OTBM node tree, appearances.dat protobuf varints, items.xml floorchange ids) in Spiral on the Rust target, and have it emit the terrain mask and road literals straight into src/terrain.spi instead of hand edits. Dogfoods byte buffers and file IO in lib. Target layout: apps/map/.", TaskKind, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,terrain,rust,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_map_tool_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T21:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:11 acceptance spec found (no port yet): terrain.spi's four literals (arena_spot_low/high, arena_road_low/high) are reproduced byte for byte from otbm-walkability.py run at --x 1933 --y 1350 --radius 30 --items-xml (NOT the script defaults 1932,1349: model (100,100) is world (1933,1350), the same offsets as terrain's ramp test x-1833/y-1250). Blocked bit = not walkable or a floorchange item id; road bit = ground speed exactly 100 (speed<160 fails the high half); rows split into low 32 / high 29 bits; text = 'if row < 0 || row > 60 then <default>u32 else ' + bisection chain (mid = floor((lo+hi)/2), 'if row <= mid then L else R', equal leaves not collapsed), defaults 4294967295/536870911 for walls and 0/0 for roads. Python takes 37 s on canary.otbm (19.7 MB). The port is done when its output equals these four lines; Rust is the only lib target with read_all_bytes today (Zig has read_all_text, C none).")
inl note_of_tibia_map_tool_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-18T21:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:40 ported: apps/map/map.spi (Spiral, Rust target, lib package via packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib: file_system.read_all_bytes, env.get_environment_variable, sm'.utf8_get_bytes/obj_to_string/(++#)) reproduces all four terrain.spi literals byte for byte on the first run, in 4.3 s vs 37 s for otbm-walkability.py. Parts: protobuf fold over appearances.dat (unpassable flag 13, bank flag 1 with waypoints speed), a byte scanner for items.xml floorchange ids mirroring the regex (word boundary, self-closing items skipped, id= or fromid/toid ranges), an iterative OTBM walk with an explicit node stack (escape-aware props, area base + tile offsets, attribute items vs child items for the speed order), and the bisection emitter. apps/map/run.ps1 compiles (SpiralCompiler --backend Rust, rustc -O), runs on the world's canary data and checks src/terrain.spi (fails on a difference; -Update rewrites the definitions). Not ported (still only in the Python script): the live client-scan agreement check (--check, 251/252), the floor-change/height3 listing and the ASCII walk dump. Lessons logged: 'open sm'' shadows core index; a self-recursive let needs let rec (the error is 'Unbound term variable'); f x -1 parses as (f x) - 1.")
inl note_of_tibia_map_tool_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-18T21:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:44 the Spiral tool now also emits Python's walkability dump, and apps/map/run.ps1 checks three things in one call: the dump equals results/arena-region-r30.txt (61 rows, identical), the live client scan agreement from results/arena-spot-scan-r12.txt (252 cells, agree 251, the same as the Python --check), and the four terrain.spi literals. Left in Python only: the floor-change/height3 listing (results/arena-floor-changes.txt, made at the old 1932,1349 center) and the per-tile speed dump; the speeds now feed the road literals directly. otbm-walkability.py can be retired once the listing is ported or declared unneeded (docs/PLAN.md P1 and README still name the script).")
inl note_of_tibia_map_tool_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-18T21:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:51 done: the floor-change/height3 listing is ported too (appearance flag 27 counted per item like the script; written to target/map/floor-changes.txt by run.ps1) and equals the script's listing at 1933,1350 apart from the script's CRLF newlines (4 floorchange tiles, 0 height3 tiles, so height3 is only checked at zero). live/otbm-walkability.py is deleted (only docs/PLAN.md named it; updated to apps/map). One call, apps/map/run.ps1 [-Update]: Spiral compile + rustc + run on the world's canary data, then checks terrain.spi's four literals, results/arena-region-r30.txt and the live client scan (251/252). Not in CI (needs the local world's map files).")
inl spiral_macro_splice_quote () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", "A splice right before the closing quote of a single-quoted macro swallows the delimiter: $'!b' fails with TokenizerErrors \"Expected: '\" (reported at the end of the line, or as ETY-MissingBody at the enclosing binding), because ' is a valid identifier character, so the splice reads the identifier b'. Works: $'!b ' (trailing space), $'(!b)', $\"!b\" (double-quoted), and field access $'!p.x'. lib already dodges it (base.spi: $'use !x = !x '). Fix: inside $'...', end a splice identifier at the ' that closes the macro (primed names can still be spliced in $\"...\"), or report the unterminated macro with a hint at the splice. Repros: 7 minimal Lua-backend cases (job tmp macrobugs/).", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,macros,tokenizer", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_splice_quote_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T06:25Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 02:25 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) CI REGRESSION from the p_var closing_char change pushed in spiral 1723e220. spiral run 39103240691: apps/spiral/spiral.spi tokenizer errors at 122:135 and 926:46 (line 926 is a nested dollar-quote macro with a splice of main). x run 39103201505 (clones spiral main): lib/spiral/leptos/ui/dropdown.spi:66:62 tokenizer error, cascading to sm'.spi:1861 unbound join and x', file_system.spi:872 unbound read_link, near_wallet.spi:82 value restriction. Suspected: primed identifiers (sm', x') inside single-quote macros are cut at the prime now that the closing char is excluded from variable chars. Repro: BuildSpiral apps/spiral/spiral.spi with the candidate DLL.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_splice_quote_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T06:31Z", "main-thread", "02:31 main-thread: first fix (p_var stops at the macro's closing char) was pushed in 1723e220 before its corpus check and broke CI (spiral 39103240691: apps/spiral tokenizer errors; x 39103201505 via lib dropdown.spi, sm'.spi, file_system.spi, near_wallet.spi): primed names really are spliced mid-macro inside single-quoted macros (sm' line 1861: let join\\' x = !join' x'). My corpus regex only looked for a primed name followed by more identifier characters and missed them. Second fix in the tree (not pushed): p_var reads greedily as before and gives back the final prime only when no closing quote remains on the rest of the line and the line does not continue with a backslash, so a trailing !b' closes the macro and !join' x' keeps its prime. Regression sample samples/macro_splice_before_quote covers both (expected 17). Corpus check queued: build, sample on all listed backends, lib sm'.spi notebook, apps/spiral build, then the other cores and the full matrix review.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_splice_quote_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T06:56Z", "main-thread", "02:56 main-thread handoff (session restarting): lookahead fix + formatter MacroOpTight are in the spiral tree, unpushed. Verified on the installed single-flight build: regression sample samples/macro_splice_before_quote exits 17 on C/Rust/Delphi/Lua (all agree), lib sm'.spi notebook ok, apps/spiral builds with 38/38 test blocks. Still to run (stopped at the user's restart, gpt5parallel build was mid-way): job tmp day/finish-corpus-check.ps1 through Invoke-Heavy = gpt5parallel + hopacsota builds, full -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative review vs bless single-flight-20261017-182920 (expect only the sample's new rows), and the console.spi formatter check (0 spaced macro ops). Then tell agent-sync-review done (tibia corpus check) and git-0e (its 13-file raw-string change waits on this review). main stays red on 1723e220 until the user pushes.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_splice_quote_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T08:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:25 lookahead fix verified: review single-flight-20261018-032249 C+Zig oracle DISAGREE 0, only new rows are samples/macro_splice_before_quote (17 on Fsharp C Rust Delphi Lua); lib sm'.spi 38/38, apps/spiral build exit 0. Unpushed; main red on 1723e220 until the user pushes. Tibia lane notified for its corpus check.")
inl note_of_spiral_macro_splice_quote_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T10:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 06:49 main-thread: Done. Lookahead fix in the tree since 10-18 03:xx; bless single-flight-20261018-061211 (6,990 rows, reproduces review 060650 with 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0) includes samples/macro_splice_before_quote on Fsharp C Rust Delphi Lua plus Python TypeScript Gleam Zig Kotlin Swift Lean (exit 17 everywhere); tibia corpus check green (23 src files, live.spi Lua hash unchanged, 9 arena Lua probes). CI goes green once the user pushes it.")
inl rust_global_triple_strings () = Task ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", "Measure the limits of Spiral raw triple-quoted strings (multiline, no escapes) and mechanically convert escaped one-line rust_global blobs to them where output stays equivalent, so blobs diff and edit as real Rust; feeds RUST-GLOBAL-GRANULAR", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,spiral,rust-global,strings", "current", "current")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_1 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T06:15Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 02:15 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) Probes in spiral/lab/rust-global-triple-strings (installed single-flight compiler, Rust backend). Works: raw multiline triple strings as rust_global/!!!!Global argument give byte-identical Rust to the escaped form (e1; e5 = real 28 KB eoie_coverage_smoke blob, compile 8.9 s vs 11.6 s escaped); CRLF source gives the same LF output (e6); column-0 continuation lines that look like Spiral (test/open/type/union/nominal/instance/prototype/inl/let/doc comments, unbalanced (*, tabs) stay inert and a real test block after still parses (e3); positions inside parens, branch, |> pipe, let-bound, single-line with trailing arg all work (e4a,b,d,e,f,g). Limits: (1) no escape inside, so a body containing three quotes cannot convert; (2) a body ending in a double quote glues onto the closer and fails in the tokenizer (e2, Expected: character); (3) after a MULTILINE triple string, further arguments on the closing line are not applied (e4c: f TRIPLE 1i32 types as i32 -> ()), wrap it in parens; (4) indentation inside is kept verbatim, so continuation lines sit at column 0 to keep output bytes identical. convert.py (same folder) decodes Spiral escapes, skips limits 1-2 and CR/backspace bodies, parenthesizes for limit 3. Survey: eoie 263 plain rust_global literal calls (205 multiline), spiral 213 global calls (28 multiline), dice 4.")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_2 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T08:42Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 04:42 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) Applied (unstaged): eoie 215 blobs in 87 files, dice 4, spiral 38 in 12 files (lib base, convert, file_system, guid, leptos, networking, rust/rust, sm', sm'_real.spir, trace; apps/cells/spi/text.spi; sample rust_near_store_moves). Verified: eoie regeneration gate over all 139 owners in 7 gated batches (full runs were reaped for low memory twice), published 50 changed owners whose only added lines are the panic exit-code drift and the topology gate fix, 690 removed lines are dropped union impls (compiler drift), zero conversion-induced lines; eoie test build 276 Rust test results ok plus Spiral test blocks; consumers byte-identical before/after with the same compiler: apps/spiral (1.57 MB Rust), dice contract, rust_near_store_moves. Limit 4 found by that check: a multiline triple string whose closing line carries more than closing parens (record field with an annotation, e8; trailing arg, e4c) breaks the block, so convert.py now skips those (1 site, lib/spiral/rust/near.spi). eoie topology gate now counts only .rs lines per crate (user 2026-10-17: the 1k limit is a Rust-crate concern only), max crate 982.")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_3 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T08:59Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 04:59 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) Done. Final spiral verification without the test mutex: 13 lib-backed samples compiled before (my 10 lib files at HEAD) and after, C/Zig/Rust/Delphi: 28 rows byte-identical, 0 differ, 24 both-fail (the 6 must-not-compile samples x 4); apps/cells/spi package Gleam output (1.25 MB) byte-identical. The lab folder stays for SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS (repros e4c, e8) and convert.py; delete it when that item lands.")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_4 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T10:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 06:51 full-suite confirmation from the main lane: single-flight-20261018-061211 blessed 6990 rows with the raw-string lib conversion in the tree, review matches row for row, DISAGREE 0; no lib-backed row changed behavior on any backend (only residual vs old bless is Lua small_int_scalar, the main lane's own Conv wrap)")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_5 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T14:31Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:31 block-form conversion applied on user go: 368 strings at 08:54 (rust_global/global/rust_global_transport/!!!!Global blobs re-emitted as indented blocks plus escaped data strings with 2 or more newlines) in eoie dice alphabet spiral, then polyglot dir-tree-html x; tictactoe skipped (2023 upstream Spiral, no build path); eoie/state records reverted (receipt hashes). Kept escaped: one-line strings starting or ending with a quote, lines with trailing spaces, tab/CR/backspace. Converter asserts per-string round trip, per-file body-list equality and idempotency, writes temp-plus-rename. Proof: eoie 139 owners + 19 aux-member entries + 7 orphan entry packages identical (10 aux targets got only known drift), cells Gleam identical, dice alphabet polyglot(2) dir-tree-html x identical; full single-flight review running")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_6 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:19Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:19 lib checks (the sample matrix barely reaches lib/spiral): Cells gate over the 13 converted lib files passes all 100 tests (sm' needed an A/B rerun: the first run lost 8 tests to a dead compiler worker after the 10:57 scoop toolchain swap; pre and post conversion lib copies both 37/37 in one invocation); apps/spiral Rust 1571180 bytes and Fsharp 963078 bytes identical before and after; formatter keeps every raw string value (format --stdin on 6 converted files)")
inl note_of_rust_global_triple_strings_7 () = Note ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T16:35Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:35 closed: full single-flight review single-flight-20261018-110213 on the 10:28 install has 0 residual differences vs 084525 (569 cells: 544 new F# oracle rows, 3 C exits plus their oracle cells from the session C compiler mingw 16.2 UCRT vs gcc 15.2, unrelated to strings); converter and probes removed from spiral/lab; negative sample multiline_triple_string_under_indented added")
inl spiral_triple_trailing_args () = Task ("SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS", "Parser: after a multiline triple-quoted string, any token on its closing line other than closing parens is lost (trailing argument e4c types as i32 -> (); type annotation inside a record field e8 ends the block); repros spiral/lab/rust-global-triple-strings/e4c_trailing_arg.spi and e8_record_field_annotated.spi", Story, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,compiler,parser,strings", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_triple_trailing_args_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T12:30Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:30 fixed in spiral_compiler.fs (in the main lane's 08:24 pack): cause was column-only layout (blockParsingIndent) seeing tokens after a closer at col 0 as offside. raw_string_layout gives each token a layout column; tokens after a multiline string/macro close continue past the opener. Same change: 4+ quote delimiters (body may hold three quotes), closing quote runs keep extras as text (body may end in a quote), block form with an indented lone closer (dedent w, drop opener and closer newlines; col-0 closer stays verbatim, corpus has 2 such strings and 0 indented ones), RawStringLineUnderIndented error. Debug candidate: probes e2/e4c/e8/e9/e11/e12/e13/e14 as expected; multiline_triple_string sample extended (checks 16-28) passes C/Rust/Lua/Zig, old compiler rejects it. Done after the bless and the block-form conversion")
inl note_of_spiral_triple_trailing_args_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T14:31Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:31 follow-up fix: block-form closer rows broke type annotations (near.spi in 80b16019, CI run 39129835185 and lib-backed samples): BlockParsing apply takes line_start_column from the row's first token, which on an indented closer row is the string's whitespace TokText at layout 0, so a trailing : () -> () swallowed the next record field. raw_string_layout now re-lays the row's earlier tokens when a multiline close sets the shift (2 lines, shared section). near.spi was reverted at 09:31 within minutes; both cores build; installed 10:28:58; multiline_triple_string gained the near-shaped check 29-30 (installed 08:33 compiler fails it, fixed one passes C/Zig/Lua/Rust); near.spi re-converted 10:30")
inl tibia_wgsl_shader_size () = Task ("TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", "Decide inlining vs smaller shaders by experiment: the GPU twins compile to about 3.6 MB of WGSL (one test takes 132 s on the laptop GPU, more than 34 min on Windows WARP). Build variants (all inl as today, let on the hot simulation functions, mixed) and measure WGSL size, shader compile time and run time on the real GPU, lavapipe and WARP, plus C-backend CPU speed; keep bit-exactness; the user expects inlining to be good, so the numbers decide.", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,wgsl,gpu,performance,experiment", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_wgsl_shader_size_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:40 measured (lab/tibia-wgsl-shader-size: measure.ps1 compiles each GPU twin as an entry, results.tsv; runner/ is a timed copy of the wgsl runner). Real GPU (K1100M, Vulkan): the 3.6 MB troll+cyclops shader spends 167 s in the driver's shader compile and 0.9 s running, so size is the whole cost, and compile time grows faster than size. Root cause 1: each simulation loop was specialized twice, because the first call passes a pack whose foe at/spawn are the same variable (join points dedupe aliased vars: 103 vs 122 params), so the whole loop body was emitted twice. dyn does not help (aliasing survives); making start a let (fresh return vars) fixes it: 3.63 -> 2.36 MB, compile 167 s -> 55 s; routing twin 2.15 -> 1.41 MB. Applied in src/hunting_pack.spi. Root cause 2: two thirds of the remaining WGSL lines are branch-merge copies and zero-initialised declarations of the ~120-field pack. Sweep inl->let per function (26 hunting_pack functions): most make it bigger (advance as let 7.7 MB, knight_turn 3.5 MB, player_acts 3.3 MB); only player_strikes -14%, foe_turn -7%, player_approaches -2% shrink it. So inlining stays the default, with let only where measured. hunt_sim twins are small (80-160 KB).")
inl note_of_tibia_wgsl_shader_size_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T08:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 04:15 DECISION: inline everything except start (now let in src/hunting_pack.spi). Cold, quiet, repeated compiles of the troll+cyclops twin (lab time-variants.ps1 -Cold: a fresh NVIDIA cache dir per run via __GL_SHADER_DISK_CACHE_PATH, because warm reruns hit the driver's disk cache and compile in about 1 s): baseline 3.63 MB 166/170 s; let start 2.36 MB 69/67 s; + let player_strikes, foe_turn (combo A) 1.87 MB 113 s; + player_approaches, roll_between, blocked_hit (combo C) 1.72 MB 150 s. Lavapipe agrees: let start compiles in 8.9 s and combo C in 17.1 s, both running about 18 s. So smaller WGSL text is not the target: each extra let adds a call boundary with a wide struct, which the drivers pay for more than the text it saves. The let on start is different: it removes an accidental second specialization of the whole loop body, caused by aliased vars in the first call's state. Rule for Spiral GPU code: keep inl; use let only where it removes a duplicated join-point specialization, and check with the cold timing script, not by text size. Run time is under 1 s on the GPU in every variant, so tick counts are not the cost. Results: lab/tibia-wgsl-shader-size/results.tsv (sizes) and timings.tsv (compile/run).")
inl note_of_tibia_wgsl_shader_size_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T09:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:29 DONE: decision applied (inline everything, let start) and verified through cells: gpu_sim 6/6, hunt_sim 11/11, pack_sim 27/27, and the full corpus of 23 files green. The lab scripts moved to tibia lab/cells-probes (measure.ps1, sweep-let.ps1, time-variants.ps1 -Cold, plus results.tsv/timings.tsv as evidence) as the CELLS-PROBES prototype. The timing switch went into the shared spiral wgsl runner (SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING=1 prints the adapter, compile_ms and run_ms to stderr).")
inl tibia_windows_ci_gpu () = Task ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", "Windows CI has no GPU and falls back to WARP, which is far too slow for the long GPU twins (4 tests fail at the cells 300 s budget; Ubuntu passes on Mesa lavapipe). Try Mesa lavapipe on Windows (mesa-dist-win plus a Vulkan loader), locally first, then in the workflow; compare with the shader-size result (TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE).", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", "tibia,ci,wgsl,windows", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_windows_ci_gpu_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:40 the Windows failure is the DX12 shader compiler, not size or run time: on WARP with wgpu's default FXC even the 80 KB golden_seed_7 shader did not finish compiling in 420 s; with DXC (WGPU_DX12_COMPILER=dxc, dxcompiler.dll + dxil.dll from the Windows SDK, which the windows-2025 runner image ships) it compiles in 1.2 s and runs in 0.3 s. Changes: spiral wgsl runner now builds its instance from wgpu's env (InstanceDescriptor::from_env_or_default) and honors WGPU_ADAPTER_NAME (needs a spiral commit before tibia CI sees it); tibia scripts/init.ps1 copies the newest SDK dxcompiler.dll/dxil.dll beside the runner on Windows; scripts/build.ps1 sets WGPU_DX12_COMPILER=dxc when they are there. Lavapipe on Windows (mesa-dist-win 26.2.4, llvmpipe LLVM 23.1.2) also works: 0.5 s compile / 0.6 s run on golden_seed_7, 0.9/1.2 s on scripted_beats. But the Vulkan loader ignores VK_DRIVER_FILES in elevated processes (GitHub runners are elevated), so CI would need an HKLM driver registration and a Vulkan loader; DXC needs neither, so DXC is the CI path.")
inl note_of_tibia_windows_ci_gpu_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", 2u32, "2026-10-18T08:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 04:18 DECISION: lavapipe on Windows CI, DXC as the local no-GPU fallback. WARP stays out: with DXC the 80 KB twin is fine (1.2 s), but the 1.7 MB twin did not finish compiling in 11 min. With FXC, constant-bound loops are the killer: the 3000-tick golden seed hung while the 1.25 MB ten-tick twin passed, because FXC unrolls loops. Lavapipe cold, current source: routing twin 8.0 s compile + 38.9 s run, troll+cyclops 8.9 + 18.8 s, ten ticks 2.9 + 3.3 s, golden seed 0.5 + 0.6 s, scripted 0.7 + 1.1 s, all far below the cells 300 s limit. Wiring (uncommitted): tibia scripts/init.ps1, on Windows with CI set, downloads LunarG vulkan-runtime-components (vulkan-1.dll 1.4.363 beside the runner) and the latest mesa-dist-win release-msvc (x64 lvp), then registers lvp_icd in HKLM\\\\SOFTWARE\\\\Khronos\\\\Vulkan\\\\Drivers (the elevated runner ignores VK_DRIVER_FILES). scripts/build.ps1 then sets WGPU_BACKEND=vulkan and WGPU_ADAPTER_NAME=llvmpipe. Both extraction patterns were tested locally against the real archives. Depends on the spiral wgsl runner change (InstanceDescriptor::from_env_or_default + initialize_adapter_from_env), which must reach spiral main first; then push tibia.")
inl note_of_tibia_windows_ci_gpu_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", 3u32, "2026-10-18T14:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 10:13 first CI run with lavapipe (39129120650): ubuntu green in 20 min; windows-2025 died at 72 min with 'The hosted runner lost communication with the server', so GitHub kept no log (the whole run was one step). Local lavapipe on the committed 2.4 MB shader peaks at 427 MB and 28 threads (compile 9.1 s, run 18.3 s), so lavapipe memory alone does not explain it. Made the next failure diagnosable: CI steps split into init / short tests / long GPU twins (gpu_sim, hunt_sim) / publish, so finished steps keep their logs; build.ps1 prints free memory and the top 5 processes every 60 s per running file; per-test ms, machine, file seconds and per-shader compile/run ms (runner SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING_LOG) go to target/ci-metrics, uploaded always, plus a job summary. Also fixed: setup-rust's cache never restored (restore miss, save 'unable to reserve'), so every run built trunk (10.4 min win) and cargo-outdated (9.4 min win) from source: now cache: false plus actions/cache of the cargo-installed tools; spiral init binstalls trunk first. Earlier red runs: 07:09 and 06:12 were the arena build.ps1 cwd leak (fixed); 05:04 windows was WARP (4 GPU twins failed, 13 min). Uncommitted.")
inl note_of_tibia_windows_ci_gpu_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", 4u32, "2026-10-18T16:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:03 two green runs on both OSes: 39137510199 (windows 36 min, ubuntu 13) and 39140743725 (windows 22 min, ubuntu 12; the tools cache restored, so windows init went from 25 to 13.6 min). Lavapipe on windows-2025 (Mesa 26.2.4, LLVM 23.1.2): the 2.4 MB gpu_sim shader 4.1 s compile + 4.0 s run, the 1.4 MB one 2.6 + 10.3 s. Heartbeats show at least 9.6 GiB free throughout, with 3 reused compiler daemons growing to about 1.1 GB each, so the earlier lost runner was not memory starvation in the test steps. Follow-ups (uncommitted): the GPU setup moved to spiral scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1, target/wgsl-run cached (the runner build took 90 s on windows, 40 s on ubuntu), lua5.1 apt only on native Linux (on Windows it ran in WSL for nothing: 96 s to 7 s for a local init -fast). Known gap: the WGSL rows name every shader main.wgsl, so they can't be told apart.")
inl note_of_tibia_windows_ci_gpu_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", 5u32, "2026-10-18T21:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:19 CI follow-up done: scripts/build.ps1 now passes --metrics target/ci-metrics/cells-<file> to run_notebook (Cells --metrics, on spiral main since 2026-10-18 13:48) and builds tests.tsv from Cells' own tests.tsv instead of parsing the notebook's spiral_tests metadata; same columns (File Test Target Status Ms Memo), verified on quantity (4/4, rows identical). Removes build.ps1's dependency on the ipynb metadata layout.")
inl tibia_reuse_exemplar () = Task ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", "Find which tibia code is general enough to serve other projects (the next one is Dota 2): the apps/arena probe library pattern (typed host bindings over a Lua backend, a shared probe lifecycle, state carried through scheduled ticks), the pre-registration analysis scripts, the build.ps1 gate, the live/ paths split between repo and local world; extract or document each as an exemplar or a generic eoie/cells item.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 85u32, Active, "", "tibia,reuse,exemplar,scaffold", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_reuse_exemplar_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:54 first pass. (1) Host-embedded Lua is the direct bridge to Dota 2: Dota 2 bot scripts are Lua 5.1 (bot_<hero>.lua Think(), mode_*_generic GetDesire), the same shape as the OTClient arena probes, so the tibia apps/arena layering is the exemplar: lua.spi (generic Lua-side state: cells, tables, integer samples with median/min/max, labeled samples, per-key stamps, env, text match, random; no host calls), client.spi (typed host bindings only), probe.spi (shared lifecycle: hidden start, login, timeout, finish guards), shared run modules (scoreboard: tally+end rules; observer: watch a built-in bot; adjacency: optional instrumentation) and one small entry per probe. A Dota 2 repo would copy lua.spi as is and write its own client.spi over the bot API. Candidate to promote lua.spi into spiral lib once a second consumer exists. (2) lab/tibia-wgsl-shader-size/measure.ps1 (compile a test body as an entry against a copied package with text rewrites, record size/compile/run) is a working prototype of CELLS-PROBES. (3) scripts/build.ps1 (cells over every test file, fail unless executed = passed) is the gate shape CELLS-GATE wants. (4) live/paths.ps1 Update-ArenaProbes (rebuild generated Lua only when a .spi/.spiproj is newer) is the staleness pattern for generated host scripts.")
inl note_of_tibia_reuse_exemplar_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 11:41 lua.spi is now host-free, so a Dota 2 repo can copy it unchanged. Evidence for the bot VM: OpenHyperAI (largest open bot pack) has 0 uses of os.time/os.getenv/os.clock against math.random 12, RandomInt 61, DotaTime 107, require 234, so the sandbox is treated as having no os library. Moved env_i32, env_text and seed_random_from_clock from lua.spi to client.spi, added client.env_is (5 raw os.getenv flags in adjacency/competitor/scan/steps/hidden_requested), client.fight_mode_text (measure), client.attacking_short (observer) and used client.creature_is_monster in summon: no arena module outside client.spi calls os.* or g_game now; the remaining raw splices are string.format/table code any Lua 5.1 has. apps/arena/build.ps1 fails if lua.spi mentions os./io./debug./g_*/require/dofile/loadfile. Checked: all 13 probes compile to Lua and parse, lua.spi 5/5 through scripts/build.ps1. For Dota: copy lua.spi, write client.spi over the bot API (GetBot, DotaTime, RandomInt; parameters compiled in, since there is no env).")
inl note_of_tibia_reuse_exemplar_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", 3u32, "2026-10-18T16:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:09 correction to note 2: the arena has 9 probes with a main (competitor, measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon, vbot), not 13; all 9 compile to Lua and parse.")
inl note_of_tibia_reuse_exemplar_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", 4u32, "2026-10-18T17:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:42 where each reusable piece now lives: (1) host-embedded Lua layering: lua.spi host-free and guarded by apps/arena/build.ps1, reused across packages by lab/dota-lua-bindings (arena- reference); (2) the probe/variant prototype: CELLS-PROBES (lab/cells-probes); (3) the build.ps1 gate: CELLS-GATE, whose --metrics now writes tibia's tests.tsv shape; per-file seconds, WGSL timing, machine info and job summary stay in build.ps1, which the scaffold copies; (4) GPU runner setup: spiral scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1; (5) project layout: lab/cells-new-project (CELLS-NEW-PROJECT). Not yet homed: the staleness rebuild of generated host scripts (live/paths.ps1 Update-ArenaProbes: rebuild Lua only when a .spi/.spiproj is newer). It belongs with the Dota repo's entry build (bot files into vscripts/bots), so it moves when that repo exists. The pre-registration analysis scripts are TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE.")
inl cells_gleam_warm_stale () = Task ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", "Warm Gleam slots ran stale programs", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,gleam,ci", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_gleam_warm_stale_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:11Z", "main-thread", "03:11 main-thread found from spiral CI 39099255114 (ubuntu, a2abbe52) and 39051853386: in lib sm'.spi the range test (whose targets then included gleam) got a Gleam row printing ellipsis_end's three results, and parsing.spi / sm'.spi lua rows showed the same pattern. Cause: the warm Gleam package slots (2026-10-17) reuse one package per manifest, and Gleam's incremental build can treat a freshly copied main.gleam as unchanged when the previous program was copied into the slot within the same second (fast Linux runner), so the previous compiled module runs. Fix (unstaged, apps/cells targets.ex with_cell_sources): after copying the cell's src into the slot, delete the slot package's own compiled output build/*/*/<name> (deps stay cached). Test: targets_test warm-slot case now runs a third program and asserts a marker inside build/dev/erlang/main is gone. Second bug found on the way (cells.ex shown): the number canonicalization regex took a trailing-dot fraction, so 12...67 compared as 12.0..67; a bare trailing dot now counts only when no further dot follows (cells_test asserts both).")
inl note_of_cells_gleam_warm_stale_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T08:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:25 warm-slot cleanup plus cells tests 59/0; the review run is clean. Waiting on CI after the user pushes to confirm the sm'.spi Gleam row.")
inl note_of_cells_gleam_warm_stale_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T11:48Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:48 main-thread CI evidence: spiral push run 39106597610 on adffe65a (ubuntu) failed lib notebooks sm' and base only through Gleam rows printing another test's expectation (sm' length: gleam printed expected = test, fsharp 3.0; base join_body_2: gleam 2.0, lua 15.0), the stale warm-slot symptom. The fix (apps/cells targets.ex with_cell_sources clears build/<mode>/<target>/<package> for the cell package, tests 59/0) is in the tree but not in adffe65a; the push build goes green only once the user commits apps/cells.")
inl note_of_cells_gleam_warm_stale_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:21 main-thread: Done. Push run 39127751148 on e1eef3bc (the user's 08:32 commit with the warm-slot cleanup): build (ubuntu-24.04) success, the job that failed on adffe65a only through Gleam rows printing another test's expectation (sm' length, base join_body_2).")
inl sim_in_spiral () = Task ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite x/apps/sim (xsim, ~1,000 lines of Elixir: scenario, personas, frames, world, runner, invariants, x_world) in Spiral alongside Cells' port (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, kino-native-design notes): the supervision side (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, cancellable prefix compiles under a factory supervisor, demand monitors) compiles to Gleam with the Cells shell, the bulk work (scenario and delivery generation, frame splitting with X weights, invariant checks and commute over 50k-event corpora) compiles to Rust with rayon under the 1k crate gate; events, posts, frames and revisions modeled GADT-first and shared with the x client's types (posts, entries, framed) and Cells' document cells; the Elixir ExUnit suite (30 tests) and the xsim.world fixtures are the oracle; port slice by slice in the same strangler order Cells uses", Feature, P1, D7, 5u32, Active, "KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR,SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM,SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", "sim,cells,gleam,rust,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_in_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:15 P1 -> P2: user 2026-10-18 'it'll take a lot of time to rewrite sim in spiral, wait for kino/cells'; stays tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T06:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:34 P2 -> P1 (user 2026-10-19 02:19: prioritize the rebuilds; make the domain modeling intricate, study the type feature set so everything reuses itself when sim is rewritten Spiral -> Gleam + Rust). Order changes: the shared domain comes first through X-CELLS-SPLIT, so each sim slice reuses a client module that already passes on gleam and rust under Cells instead of porting Elixir by hand. First shared module: client protocol.spi (frame header render/parse, thread_flaw union) replacing frames.ex header code and x_world gap notes later. The Elixir sim stays the independent oracle until a slice's Gleam build reproduces its fixtures (prelude.events.jsonl, sim-*.expected.json); a shared source cannot catch its own bug, so the oracle is retired per slice, never in bulk. Still tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR for the supervision side")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T06:52Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:52 reuse map, sim module -> shared Spiral (x client, X-CELLS-SPLIT): Xsim.Frames (header, split, posted_unit defuse, chunk, cut_at_word_boundary, stored/restored) = splitter.spi + glyphs.spi + protocol.spi verbatim, already backend_switched for Gleam; XWorld.gaps and the missing-frame notes = protocol thread_flaw + assemble's cell_reading; World.gathered (k, n frames per key) = protocol frame_header; Log xsim-events v1 = next shared module: union event (post, frame, edit_frame, edit, delete, like, unlike) with a codec, so the client e2e stub and the sim decode one definition; Invariants.verdict = a decision union carrying the violated rule (omniledger outcome-with-evidence); Runner/World supervision = gleam_otp (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR); Scenario and corpus generation = Rust under the 1k gate; daemon_memory and executors stay shell glue. Oracle rule: frames.ex stays until the Gleam build of splitter reproduces the sim-*.expected.json fixtures, then it is deleted in the same change")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: owner check: xsim lives in x/apps/sim and the 10-19 reuse map (note 3: Xsim.Frames = x client's splitter/glyphs/protocol, already backend_switched for Gleam) came from the x lane's side; main records only Next: next lap read the x lane's progress on X-CELLS-SPLIT")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:09 lap 2 grain: sized the port and matched the first slice. xsim lib is 1,215 lines of Elixir in 11 modules (runner 183, daemon_memory 161, x_world 160, world 154, scenario 144, frames 113, personas 91, invariants 68, executors 63, log 55, x_compat 23) plus 250 lines of mix tasks and 390 lines of ExUnit (xsim_test 325, x_compat_test 65). Xsim.Frames (header, split with defused dots and word-boundary cut, chunk/flush, frame_text, rebuild) mirrors x/apps/client/splitter.spi function for function (posted_unit, defused, space_carried, split_point, chunk, header, budget_for); X-CELLS-SPLIT note 07:06 reports splitter, glyphs, protocol, prelude and events passing Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) with UTF-8 byte units, so the frames slice can delegate to the Gleam build of splitter.spi today without new Spiral. Still blocked for the actor half: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (runner/world as gleam_otp) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM. Owner note stands: xsim lives in x/apps/sim (x lane's tree); this item records only. Next: list the xsim_test cases that exercise Frames (the oracle for slice 1) so the x lane can port them to ///> gleam test blocks")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:50 lap 3 grain: the slice-1 oracle is 8 of xsim_test.exs's 30 cases (x/apps/sim/test, read only). Pure Frames cases, portable as ///> gleam (and rust) test blocks next to splitter.spi: (1) cells split into 280-weight frames rebuild exactly, CJK and Hangul weigh double (split, frame_text, rebuild, weight); (2) frames never end in whitespace and still rebuild exactly (split, rebuild); (3) dots that could start an X link are defused with a zero-width space and restored (split, rebuild, weight); (4) a file's cells start at a column-0 line after a blank line (file_cells, thread_posts). Cases that only use Frames.explode_posts as input to the world/runner (out-of-order delivery invariants, delivery log replay, versioned JSON event stream, edits as frames) belong to slice 2+ (they need World/Runner, i.e. the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR actor half). Spiral counterparts to check before porting: weight/defused/split_point/chunk/header exist in splitter.spi; file_cells and thread_posts (cell boundaries in a file) have no splitter.spi counterpart yet, which makes case 4 the first new Spiral in slice 1. Recorded for the x lane, which owns both trees. Next: check whether x/apps/client has a cell-boundary parser (posts.spi or wire.spi) that file_cells can reuse")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:28 lap 4 grain: checked the x client for a file-to-cells splitter to back slice 1's fourth case: none. The client has the reading side only (assemble/assemble.spi groups frames per cell, orders parts, reports MissingFrames/ReplyGap; write/write.spi posts a chain of already-framed texts), and splitter.spi splits one cell into frames. So file_cells is the one new Spiral function slice 1 needs, and its contract is exact in Xsim.Frames: normalize CRLF, drop one trailing newline, empty text is no cells, else split at @cell_boundary = (?<=[^\\n])\\n\\n(?=\\n*[^ \\t\\n]) (a blank line after text, followed by a line that starts at column 0 with a non-blank character, so extra blank lines move to the start of the next cell and indented continuation lines never start a cell), then trim each cell's end; thread_posts = for each cell i, id c<i>, frames of split(id, cell) or one empty frame, stored as header + body trimmed. Placement for the x lane: next to splitter.spi as cells_of_file with ///> gleam and rust tests mirroring xsim_test's case 4. Recorded only (x lane owns both trees)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:13 lap 5 grain, CORRECTS lap 4: x/apps/client/splitter.spi already has file_cells (committed in x a3b5353, 10-19 02:49; lap 4 missed it) with the column-0 contract and test cells_start_at_column_zero, plus cell_posts/posts_of. Slice-1 oracle coverage in x today: case 4 (cells at column 0) covered; case 3 (defused dots) covered on the split side (dots_that_x_would_link_are_defused); CJK/Hangul double weight covered in glyphs.spi (plain_weight of U+65E5 and of an em dash plus zero-width space = 2); 280 cap covered (long_cells_split_between_words). Missing everywhere: the REBUILD half of cases 1-3 (split then reassemble gives the original cell, defused dots restored, no frame ends in whitespace); assemble.spi assembled/assembled_of is the reading side to pair with cell_posts. So slice 1 shrinks to one round-trip test block in x (posts_of then assembled over a text with CJK, a defusable dot and a long cell). Recorded for the x lane, which owns the tree.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:31 lap 6 grain: mapped the exact chain for slice 1's one missing round-trip test (x tree, read only): split side splitter.posts_of text (cell_posts with header c<i> k/n), reading side locate/locate.spi (header_end, frame_json h body original parent: posted text -> frame JSON) then assemble/assemble.spi assembled (frames, known) -> program {sources, flaws}, whose sources must equal file_cells text with defused dots restored. So the test is posts_of -> locate frame per post -> assembled -> sources = file_cells, over one text holding a CJK cell, a defusable x.io dot and a cell longer than 280 weight; it fits as a ///> rust test block in locate.spi (which already sees both sides) or in frames/frames.spi beside framed_json. Restoring defused dots is a reading-side step to check: assemble.spi has no defuse inverse, so either the test expects the zero-width space in sources (post text is what X shows) or a restore step is missing; the xsim oracle case 3 says restored. Recorded for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: slice 1 is mapped against the x client (file_cells exists, the round-trip test and the dot restore are open), and the work belongs to the x lane. Progress 5.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7, correcting lap 6: the dot restore does exist. locate/locate.spi:24 body_at replaces defused_dot (a zero-width space plus the dot, glyphs.spi:24) with a plain dot when it reads a frame body, so the reading side restores before assemble, as xsim case 3 expects. The round-trip test therefore needs no new function: posts_of text -> locate per post (body_at restores) -> assembled -> sources = file_cells text. That is slice 1's single missing test block, for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:24 lap 8 grain: slice 2 mapped from x/apps/sim/lib/xsim/invariants.ex (68 lines, pure, the natural Rust slice): check returns 4 or 5 named verdicts (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results, one_compile_per_prefix, withdrawn_demand_hidden, plus unwanted_results_freed when evicting) over a state of visible id->(key,result), starts/cancelled/evicted key->count, results key->_, and wanted id->key; verdict is ok or violations with the first 5 ids; commute compares two states' visible maps; breaking_edits_fail checks broken sources. Spiral shape: union verdict = Ok | Violations of list id, a named-invariant union instead of atoms, maps as sorted arrays. comparable is the only non-trivial part: two regex rewrites (spiral_cells/<n>-<n> -> spiral_cells/_ and thread name (<n>) panicked -> (_)), which a small hand scanner covers without a regex crate. Order after slice 1 (x lane)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:53 lap 9 grain: mapped xsim frames.ex against the x client: split, posted_unit (defused dot between non-space glyphs), chunk with X weights, cut_at_word_boundary, space_carried, header and digits already exist in Spiral as x/apps/client/splitter.spi (posted_unit, chunk, word_reach, space_carried, header, digit_count, budget_for, chunks_of; 201 lines with 6 test blocks). So the frames slice needs no new splitting code; what is left of frames.ex is the event-level glue: rebuild (sort frames, join, restore dots), explode_posts (events to framed posts with the limit) and thread_posts/file_cells, which build on splitter plus locate.body_at. Order stays: slice 1 round trip, slice 2 invariants, then this glue")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain, dependency reading: the deps gate the whole item on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Epic 10%, itself behind SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (82%), but only the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell) needs the Cells port; the bulk half (frames, scenario/delivery generation, invariants, compiled to Rust) needs neither, and its first slice (frames) already maps 1:1 onto the x client splitter (lap 9). So the bulk slices can start now, in the x lane (the code lives in the x tree, which helper lanes do not touch). Proposal for main: split off a SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK story with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, assigned to the x lane. Next: x lane slice 1 round-trip test (rebuild/explode_posts over splitter)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: planning filled from the description's own sizing: about 1,000 lines of Elixir in 7 modules ported slice by slice with the 30-test ExUnit oracle, two compile targets (Gleam supervision, Rust bulk under the 1k crate gate): estimate 1800m (roughly 250 min per module plus the shared GADT types), load heavy (Rust + Gleam builds and 50k-event corpora per slice). Assignee stays open for main (x lane per my lap 10 proposal)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 12: blocker chain: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Active 10%) waits on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (Planned 0%), and the code to port (x/apps/sim) is x-lane territory, so the item is blocked for the compiler-side lanes until the x lane takes it")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-19T19:47Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 15:47 split accepted by the x lane: the bulk half moved to SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK (no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, x lane); this item keeps the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell)")
inl spiral_lua_closure_record_arg () = Task ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", "Lua backend drops the fields of a closure argument that is a record or tuple: the many-args branch of the closure emitter (spiral_compiler.fs, the \"return function(dom)\" case near line 168222) writes local _ = (table.unpack or unpack)(dom), so the body reads undefined v-names and fails at run time (attempt to compare nil). Call sites already pass {[1]=..,[2]=..}; the fix is to unpack dom into the domain vars (local v2, v3 = ...). C binds them correctly. Repro: tibia lab/tibia-probes-in-spiral/lua-curried-record-closure (a curried prefers_by over a two-field record picked by a dynamic if; lua exits with an error, expected exit 0). Found by the Tibia arena competitor probe; worked around there by applying the function fully at the call site.", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lua,backend,bug", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_closure_record_arg_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", 1u32, "2026-10-18T08:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:47 main-thread: fixed in the tree (not built yet), found independently by the 04:33 Lua gap probe: the many-args closure branch now unpacks dom into the domain vars (local v1, v2 = (table.unpack or unpack)(dom)). Regression rows: native_prototype_record_callback and its two package variants, all exit 1 on Lua before (C 42), going onto the Lua list with the next build and review.")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_closure_record_arg_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", 2u32, "2026-10-18T09:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:29 the repro now lives at tibia lab/spiral-lua-closure-record-arg (main.spi + package.spiproj). The compiler lane reports the fix in its tree (not yet installed): the many-args branch unpacks dom into the domain vars.")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_closure_record_arg_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", 3u32, "2026-10-18T10:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 06:01 verified by tibia-lane on the installed pack (review single-flight-20261018-055209): the closure repro binds its fields and runs correctly in both orders (exit 1 CurrentFirst, 0 ReachFirst; the earlier 'expected exit 0' assumed REACH=1). The tibia arena dropped its do...end workarounds and all 9 probes build and parse. live.spi Lua is unchanged (7FD3D538). The repro lab folder is deleted.")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_closure_record_arg_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", 4u32, "2026-10-18T10:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 06:49 main-thread: Done. bless single-flight-20261018-061211 (6,990 rows, reproduces review 060650 with 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0); the 3 prototype_record_callback Lua rows agree (exit 42); tibia lane recheck green (closure repro both orders, arena probes without workarounds).")
inl spiral_lua_paren_statement () = Task ("SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT", "Lua backend emits statement macros and field/index assignments that start with \"(\" on their own line, e.g. (v8)[k] = v or (function(m) ... end)(x) after a call statement; Lua 5.1 and LuaJIT reject this as ambiguous syntax (function call x new statement). The backend should guard such statements (a leading \";\" is not valid Lua 5.1 at block start, so wrap in do ... end, or emit through a local). Workaround in the Tibia arena client bindings: every statement macro starting with ( is written as do ... end.", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lua,backend,bug", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_paren_statement_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T08:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:47 main-thread: fixed in the tree (not built yet): codegenLua simple wraps a unit statement whose text starts with ( as do ... end, which covers statement macros and assignments (both go through simple). Regression sample samples/lua_paren_statement (C key + Lua list, exit 7): a ( call macro right after local v0 = 5.")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_paren_statement_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT", 2u32, "2026-10-18T10:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 06:01 verified by tibia-lane on the installed pack (review single-flight-20261018-055209): the closure repro binds its fields and runs correctly in both orders (exit 1 CurrentFirst, 0 ReachFirst; the earlier 'expected exit 0' assumed REACH=1). The tibia arena dropped its do...end workarounds and all 9 probes build and parse. live.spi Lua is unchanged (7FD3D538). The repro lab folder is deleted.")
inl note_of_spiral_lua_paren_statement_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT", 3u32, "2026-10-18T10:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 06:49 main-thread: Done. bless single-flight-20261018-061211 (6,990 rows, reproduces review 060650 with 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0); samples/lua_paren_statement agrees on Lua (exit 7, emits do (function() end)() end); tibia arena probes build and parse with their do ... end workarounds removed.")
inl dota () = Task ("DOTA", "Dota 2 as the next dogfood project after Tibia: typed game rules in Spiral with C oracle tests, a policy compiled to Lua 5.1 running as Dota bot scripts (vscripts/bots, bot_generic.lua Think, mode GetDesire), measured on a headless Linux client/server on a rented CPU box against the default bots and open-source bot packs. Starts from the CELLS-NEW-PROJECT scaffold and the tibia exemplar (TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR).", Epic, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "CELLS-NEW-PROJECT,TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", "dota,project,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl dota_headless_world () = Task ("DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD", "Headless Dota 2 world on a rented CPU box: SteamCMD app 570 on Linux, launched with bots filling both teams and the bot world-state socket, no GPU (software rendering as in DotaService/LuaFun), scripted start and stop like tibia live/world.ps1, logs and replays kept outside the repo.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "dota,infra", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dota_headless_world_1 () = Note ("DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:05 tibia-lane research, nothing run. Current local start, from OpenHyperAI discussion 135 (2026): a cfg file in 'dota 2 beta/game/dota/cfg/' run with 'exec <name>.cfg' from the console: sv_lan 1; dota_wait_for_players_to_load 10 plus the three wait/timeout delays at 10; dota_bot_practice_script <workshop id> (for our own files use the local vscripts/bots folder instead); dota_bot_set_difficulty / dota_bot_match_difficulty / dota_bot_practice_difficulty 4; dota_bot_practice_gamemode 1 (All Pick; 2 CM, 4 RD, 22 AP ranked); dota_bot_match_use_matchmaking 1; dota_bot_practice_start 1; map dota. dota_bot_practice_team 0 picks Radiant (1 Dire, 2 random). sv_lan 1 runs offline after the first download; port 27015. Dedicated route (older sources): SteamCMD app 570, then on Linux 'srcds_run -game dota +map dota' through the steam runtime, with dota_bot_populate as a server-console command and +dota_start_ai_game 1 (unverified). Next: on the rented box, try the dedicated route with the cfg lines passed as +commands, and check whether a client-less match ticks (bots only) and writes a parseable console log for the scoreboard.")
inl dota_lua_bindings () = Task ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", "Typed bindings over the Dota bot API in Spiral (the counterpart of tibia apps/arena/client.spi): GetBot, unit queries, Action_* orders, Think/GetDesire entry shapes; reuse tibia apps/arena/lua.spi, after checking which of its calls the restricted bot VM allows (no io, maybe no os.getenv/os.time: give it sandbox-safe variants).", TaskKind, P2, D2, 40u32, Active, "", "dota,lua,spiral", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_1 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T09:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:01 reference layout from the most active open-source pack, OpenHyperAI (github forest0xia/dota2bot-OpenHyperAI): bots/bot_generic.lua (per-bot entry), hero_selection.lua, ability_item_usage_generic.lua, item_purchase_generic.lua, mode_*_generic.lua (farm, push, defend, roam, assemble), shared libs in bots/FunLib, per-hero files in bots/BotLib. It compiles TypeScript to Lua (tstl) into bots/, which is the same shape as Spiral compiled to Lua into a host, so the Spiral entries map one to one onto these file names. Sandbox: a restricted Lua 5.1 VM (no C libraries, no io, no threads; verify os/print/require). Local start: custom lobby with server location Local Host; a console-only start exists (find the commands for DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD).")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_2 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T16:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:03 tibia-lane: first prototype at tibia lab/dota-lua-bindings (move into the Dota repo when it exists). bot.spi = typed bindings (GetBot, GetHealth, GetAttackDamage, GetAttackRange, GetNearbyCreeps(radius, true), Action_AttackUnit(target, true), DotaTime as ms, RandomInt, and on_think setting the global Think); every name is in OpenHyperAI (GetNearbyCreeps 76 files, Action_AttackUnit 20, GetAttackDamage 81, GetAttackRange 105, GetBot() 185, 'function Think' 18). last_hit.spi = pure decision (the weakest enemy creep that dies to one hit, else hold) with C oracle and Lua test blocks; bot_generic.spi = entry wiring it into Think and counting orders with tibia apps/arena lua.spi through the package reference arena- (the reuse proof). check.ps1 compiles bot_generic to 2.7 KB of Lua, fails on any os./io./debug./require/dofile/loadfile, then mock.lua loads it with setfenv into an env holding only math/string/table/base functions plus a mock bot API and runs Think on 4 creep waves: all pass. Cells cannot run the test blocks there yet (CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE).")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_3 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:43 fix in the prototype: Think runs many times a second, and re-sending Action_AttackUnit on every call would restart the attack animation, so a last hit would never land. bot.attacking_unit (GetAttackTarget() == target) now gates the order, and bot_generic only orders when the pick is not already the attack target. mock.lua's GetAttackTarget tracks the last order, and a 5th case (a second Think on the same wave issues no order) passes: check.ps1 5/5 in the sandbox. Still open: the attack-point timing (a real last-hitter orders the attack about the attack point before the creep's health drops under the hit; that needs the creep damage model of DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO).")
inl dota_first_scenario () = Task ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", "First measured scenario, kept narrow like the tibia troll camp: 1v1 mid last-hitting (creep last hits and denies in 10 minutes) with typed rules and a C oracle for creep aggro/attack timing, a Spiral policy run live as a bot script, and a pre-registered comparison against the default bot.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "dota,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dota_first_scenario_1 () = Note ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", 1u32, "2026-10-18T09:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:01 baselines, as in the Tibia arena (use the published packs locally only, never interact upstream): the default Valve bots, and OpenHyperAI as the strongest open-source pack (TypeScript compiled to Lua, positions set by lobby slot order). Measure the same narrow scenario for each.")
inl note_of_dota_first_scenario_2 () = Note ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:44 oracle for creep numbers, decided like tibia (each creature's data-canary Lua definition was the oracle, not a wiki): read lane-creep stats from the installed game's unit definitions (npc_units.txt in the dota VPK: health, armor, AttackDamageMin/Max, AttackRate, AttackAnimationPoint, ProjectileSpeed, MovementSpeed, bounty), pinned with the build version, on the rented box from DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD. Wiki pages are patch-mixed (Liquipedia's search results mix 7.03/7.20/7.21 archives, and its pages refuse automated fetches), so they are cross-checks only. The last-hit model then needs: creep vs creep damage per wave, the hero's attack point and projectile time (the order has to go out before health drops under one hit), and armor reduction; the prototype's pick (weakest creep that dies to one hit now) is the zero-latency version of that rule.")
inl cells_export_atomic_write () = Task ("CELLS-EXPORT-ATOMIC-WRITE", "Notebook exports rewrite lib .spi files in place, so a concurrent cells run can read a half-written file: on 2026-10-18 lib/spiral/rust/near.spi was re-exported at 04:39:25 with unchanged content, and six tibia test files compiled during that window failed with \"near.spi:41:6: Expected: }\" and \"Unbound type variable: vector\". All passed on rerun. The export (spiral.edit/export and any generator that writes lib files) should write to a temp file in the same directory and rename it over the target, and skip the write when the content is unchanged. The second rule alone would have prevented this case.", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,export,reliability", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_export_atomic_write_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPORT-ATOMIC-WRITE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:13 main-thread: correction of the premise: no Cells code writes lib .spi files (the to_spi/to_spi_tested helpers have no caller that writes to lib, and spiral.edit writes the .livemd). The 04:39:25 rewrite of lib/spiral/rust/near.spi lines up with git-0e's first raw-string conversion of lib files that started about 04:35, i.e. a lane's edit script writing in place. Remedy for every lib-writing script: spiral/scripts/core.ps1 now has Write-FileAtomic Path Text (skips identical content and returns false, otherwise writes .<name>.<pid>.tmp beside the target and renames it over, returns true); probed: write, skip, replace, no temp left. Asked the lane that converts lib to use it.")
inl note_of_cells_export_atomic_write_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EXPORT-ATOMIC-WRITE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T13:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:15 main-thread: Done, with a corrected root cause from git-0e: the 04:39 near.spi failure was not a torn read but a complete, invalid file from its first raw-string pass (a multiline global followed by ') : () -> ()' on the closing line, SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS, which the old parser rejected), restored from git within minutes; today's compiler accepts that shape. Neither the Cells export nor a half-write was involved. The protective rule stands and exists: spiral/scripts/core.ps1 Write-FileAtomic (skip unchanged, temp + rename), and git-0e's converter now writes .<name>.<pid>.tmp and os.replace.")
inl float_text_canonical () = Task ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", "Pick one canonical float text across backends: C and Lua (and since 2026-10-18 Kotlin and Swift, which follow the C oracle) lay out shortest digits with printf %g at the smallest round-tripping precision, so 100.0 prints 1e+02 and 1e10 prints 1e+10, while Rust, TypeScript, Python, Lean and Zig print 100.0 and switch to exponents at their own thresholds (Rust 1e16, Python 1e+16, JS 1e+21). show_floats pins only values where all forms coincide. Needs the user's call on the layout (likely shortest digits, fixed notation for exponents in [-4, 16), Python-style exponent otherwise), then one lib change per backend arm of format_real and a cross-backend self-checking sample.", TaskKind, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,format,backends,decision", "current", "current")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_1 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T11:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:43 main-thread: found while adding Kotlin/Swift format_real arms; hand probe outputs in BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS note 27. Not changed autonomously: it alters visible output of every consumer that prints floats (apps/spiral Rust, Tibia C/Lua).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_2 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:24 main-thread options for the decision (every format_real arm already agrees on NaN, Infinity, -Infinity, shortest round-trip digits and the .0 suffix; only the switch to exponent notation differs): (A) keep C's %g rule, exponent once it reaches the digit count: 100.0 -> 1e+02, 123.45 -> 123.45, 1e-05 -> 1e-05 (C, Lua, Kotlin, Swift today); (B) Python repr's rule, fixed notation for exponents in [-4, 16), else d.ddde+XX with a sign and at least 2 digits: 100.0 -> 100.0, 1e16 -> 1e+16, 1e-05 -> 1e-05; (C) Rust's rule, 1e16 -> 1e16, 1e-7 -> 1e-7 (no sign, no padding). Recommendation: B. It keeps every value below 1e16 readable (no 1e+02 for 100), matches the majority's fixed range, and keeps C's exponent spelling for the extremes. Cost: change the layout test in the C, Lua, Kotlin and Swift arms (exponent >= digits becomes exponent >= 16) and give Rust, TypeScript, Lean and Zig the [-4, 16) window and e+XX spelling; then one self-checking sample over 0.0001, 0.00001, 100.0, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16 and 1.5e22 on every backend.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_3 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:29 helper-lane lap 1: arm map for the decision, read from lib sm'_real.spir format_real (lines 593-~890): 11 backends carry float arms - Lua 659 (%.Ng loop), Fsharp 666 (ToString R, E to e), Python 673 (repr), Rust 680 ({:?}), TypeScript 688 (String(v)), Zig 696 (spiralFloatText), Lean 705/706, Kotlin 713/733, Swift 760/791, C 829/838, Delphi 861/880. Two arms are missing from the title's list: F# (.NET R switches to E notation at its own threshold, to confirm by probe) and Delphi. Under the recommended option B, Python's arm already is the rule (repr), so it can serve as the self-checking sample's oracle text, and the other 10 arms change. Still needs the user's pick of A, B or C.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_4 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:17 helper-lane lap 2: measured the F# arm's rule (dotnet fsi on the cache SDK, ToString R with InvariantCulture, the exact call in sm'_real.spir:666): fixed notation through 1e16 (10000000000000000) and exponent from 1e17 (1E+17), small values switch at 1e-05 (1E-05, 1.25E-05) while 0.0001 stays fixed; after the arm's E -> e and .0 suffix that gives 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 10000000000000000.0, 1.5e+22, 1e-07. Python repr (option B's reference) gives the same texts except 1e16, which Python writes 1e+16. So under option B the F# arm needs only one change - switch to exponent at 1e16 instead of 1e17 - which makes F# the closest arm to B after Python itself.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_5 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:43 helper-lane lap 3: measured the last arm, Delphi: lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float.pas carries lib sm'_real.spir's SpiralFormatFloat verbatim (lines 863-877: shortest ffGeneral precision p in 1..17 that round-trips, .0 when no point or exponent, E -> e), compiled with the matrix flags (fpc -O2 -Mdelphi, scoop FPC) as one bounded job. Output: 0.0001, 0.00001, 1e2, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16, 1.5e22, 1e-7, 0.1, 1.5. So Delphi is the farthest arm from every option: ffGeneral at the shortest precision switches to exponent as soon as the digit count is below the exponent, which turns 100.0 into 1e2 and 1e15 into 1e15 (F# 1000000000000000.0), keeps 1e-05 fixed as 0.00001, and writes exponents unsigned and unpadded (1e-7, Python 1e-07). Under option B the Delphi arm needs its own rule (choose fixed vs exponent by the decimal exponent range like Python repr, -4 <= e < 16, then digits from the shortest round-trip) rather than a ffGeneral precision loop. All arms are now measured; the A/B/C choice stays with the user.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_6 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T16:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:03 helper-lane lap 5: priced option B on the farthest arm without deciding for the user: lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float_b.pas is a Python-repr-style Delphi formatter (shortest round-trip digits from FloatToStrF ffExponent, then fixed notation for decimal exponents -4..15 and d.ddde+XX with at least two exponent digits otherwise), about 35 lines replacing the current 15-line loop. Its expected output on the measured set is exactly Python's repr (checked with python -I): 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1, -2.5. The fpc run waits for main's freeze all-clear. So B costs one function per arm of this size (Delphi the largest delta, F# only the 1e16 threshold); the A/B/C decision stays with the user.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_7 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:49 helper-lane: ran the option-B Delphi formatter with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi (bounded): 9 of the 10 values print exactly Python's repr (0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1); the tenth, -2.5, raised EConvertError because FPC's FloatToStrF ffExponent returned a string without an E for that value, so the exponent parse needs a guard (fall back to ffGeneral digits when no E is present). So the cost of B on the farthest arm is one ~40-line function and it reaches repr parity on the measured set apart from that guard. Still the user's A/B/C call.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_8 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 8u32, "2026-10-20T00:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 20:44 helper-lane lap 8: option B now reaches 10 of 10. In lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float_b.pas, the shortest-digits search formats with Format('%.*e', [p - 1, Abs(x)]), C-style, which always carries an E, instead of FloatToStrF ffExponent, whose output for 2.5 at low precision had none. Built with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi into spiral-bin/runs/float-b, the 10 test values print exactly Python's repr: 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1, -2.5. So B costs one ~40-line function on the Delphi arm with full parity on the measured set. The A/B/C choice is still the user's.")
inl rename_bless () = Task ("RENAME-BLESS", "Mass rename of 'bless' (user 2026-10-18 10:43, done the same way as the 10-18 harness->matrix/live/cli_driver rename): replace the term with 'accept' everywhere it is current: test.ps1 -Bless -> -Accept, blessed/blessable -> accepted/acceptable, bless/re-bless -> accept/re-accept in AGENTS.md, NEXT.md, lanes/*/PLAN.md and FRONTIER.md, matrix.psd1, scripts/workflow.ps1, eoie FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW.md, the agents' memory and active backlog titles. Historical notes and run labels stay as written.", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "rename,matrix,docs", "current", "current")
inl note_of_rename_bless_1 () = Note ("RENAME-BLESS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T14:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:43 main-thread inventory and term choice. Current usages: spiral 68 hits in 8 tracked files (AGENTS.md 17, NEXT.md 27, gpt5parallel FRONTIER.md 9, test.ps1 9, hopacsota PLAN.md 3, matrix.psd1 1, mega_brzozowski README 1, scripts/workflow.ps1 1); eoie FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW.md 3; memory 23 lines in 11 files; backlog 190 lines (mostly historical notes). Recommended: 'accept'. Bless means taking a reviewed run's observed results as the new expected baseline (EXPECTED.tsv), which is exactly what Cells --accept already does per test block (Spiral.Cells.Accept rewrites a mismatching expectation to the observed value; report count 'accepted'). It is also what insta calls cargo insta accept. One verb then covers both levels, and test.ps1 has no other accept usage to collide with. Rejected: 'record' (taken: -Record writes scoreboards), 'promote' (taken: core promotion criterion, hopacsota Phase 6), 'approve' (suggests a person signing off, not a run). Fallback if 'accept' feels too generic: 'rebaseline', which names the artifact it changes (baseline/EXPECTED.tsv; nothing in spiral uses that word today). Method from the harness rename (EOIE-EDIT-BATCH note 5): one dry-run plan of ordered regex rules per file glob across repos, LF/CRLF kept, nothing staged; then grep for leftovers. Needs a go.")
inl note_of_rename_bless_2 () = Note ("RENAME-BLESS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T14:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:44 main-thread check of the 10-18 harness rename. Tracked files are clear: 0 hits in spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, blog and i574n.github outside the backlog. The one leftover is tibia results/path-check-pc1.txt:10, a dated run log, left as history. Memory uses of the word mean Claude Code's own harness, a different thing, so they stay. The backlog was not part of that pass: 27 open items still say harness in their titles, 8 of them Tibia (that lane's call) and 19 others (PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES, BEND/WGSL/CALYX/OPENQASM3/LEAN-BACKEND, SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES, SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS, FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER, FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS, MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL, PWSH-FREE, CI-OFFLOAD, EBPF-C-PROFILE, BLOG, CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, IGGY-EVAL, SPIRAL-BIGNUM-MODEXP, and this item, which only cites the rename). Two notes written after the rename still use the word (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER note 3, mine; TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY note 33). Fold the open titles into this rename's pass (harness -> matrix where it means test.ps1) through eoie agile edit title.")
inl note_of_rename_bless_3 () = Note ("RENAME-BLESS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T15:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:03 main-thread done on the user's go ('do the renames autonomously, use accept or baseline where appropriate'). Applied, unstaged: spiral 8 files (test.ps1 -Bless -> -Accept, $Bless -> $Accept, $blessable -> $acceptRows, messages; AGENTS.md, NEXT.md, gpt5parallel FRONTIER.md, hopacsota PLAN.md, matrix.psd1, mega_brzozowski README, scripts/workflow.ps1 -Accept) and eoie FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW.md. Nouns became 'baseline' where that reads better ('baseline rror rows', 'the pack-1y baseline', 'different from the baseline'); git grep -i bless finds 0 in both repos. Memory: 11 files, with bless-autonomously.md renamed to accept-autonomously.md (links and MEMORY.md follow). Quoted user text keeps 'blessings', and tibia-dogfood-project keeps Tibia's in-game blessings. Backlog: 21 open titles rewritten through eoie agile edit title (bless -> accept and harness -> matrix where it means test.ps1; paths, quotes and 'keep harnessing' kept), and a second dry run changes 0. Left as written: Done items, historical notes, run labels, and the Tibia lane's titles, where 'harness' means its live/ layer (TIBIA-BASELINES, -LIVE-POLICY, -START-SYNC, -TOOLS-TO-EOIE, -ARENA-RELEASE, -MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL, -HEADLESS-CLIENT, -TCP-BYTES). Plan scripts in the session scratchpad (rename_bless.py, rename_titles.py).")
inl note_of_rename_bless_4 () = Note ("RENAME-BLESS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T16:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:42 main-thread: closed. The 8 Tibia titles that still say harness (meaning that lane's live/ layer) went to the tibia lane by message, since the wording is theirs to choose. Since note 3: memory accept-autonomously.md gained the SPIRAL_CC pin rule, and nothing else needed renaming (git grep -i bless finds 0 in spiral and eoie).")
inl note_of_rename_bless_5 () = Note ("RENAME-BLESS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T17:59Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:59 x follow-up (x was not in the pass, and its fixture switches were added after it): apps/sim/build.ps1 -Bless -> -Accept and apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1 -Bless -> -Accept, messages 'blessed' -> 'accepted'; 0 hits left in x sources")
inl tree_pages_every_repo () = Task ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", "Every repo publishes its own dir-tree-html page on gh-pages, and the page works on phones in portrait and landscape (user 2026-10-18 11:05)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 95u32, Active, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,pages,dir-tree-html,mobile", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_1 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-18T15:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:31 main-thread. Audit: spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, dir-tree-html and i574n.github.io publish (Pages built). eoie, blog and x had no publish step at all. tibia had one, but its push job needs every matrix leg and the Windows leg fails (GPU twins, a lost runner), so the push never ran. All four have no gh-pages branch and no Pages site (404). Fixed in the tree, uncommitted: eoie/blog/x get scripts/publish.ps1 (spiral publish-tree), /dist/ in .gitignore (blog gets a new .gitignore), and a build-job step that publishes and uploads dist on the Ubuntu leg if: !cancelled(), plus a push job (needs build, if: !cancelled() && push; peaceiris to gh-pages). tibia gets the same !cancelled() gating. spiral publish-tree.ps1 gains -Exclude; eoie passes .cache, whose local gitignored repro workspaces made rsync copy 61k+ folders. Verified locally: blog 19 files in 6 s, x 80 in 6 s, eoie 2,033 in 25 s; all four workflows parse. Mobile: the published page had no viewport meta, so a phone laid it out at 980 px at 0.40x (15 px names showed at about 6 px; Chromium autosized text to 41 px with a 1,552 px scroll). dir_tree_html.spi now has the viewport meta, text-size-adjust 100%, line-height 1.6, overflow-wrap anywhere, and a max-width 600px block (16/12 px, tighter indents). Playwright iPhone 13 emulation, Chromium + WebKit, portrait + landscape: 390/750 px layout at 1.0x, no horizontal scroll even on the deep tibia tree (widest link 382 of 390 px); build.ps1 --self-test passes. Also fixed: the renderer skips symlinks/junctions (DirEntry::file_type().is_symlink()); a direct --dir run on tibia's root hung on its deps junctions. Left: after the user pushes, run the first deploy, then enable Pages on eoie/tibia/blog/x (source gh-pages /) if GitHub doesn't do it on the first gh-pages push; the dir-tree-html change reaches every page once dir-tree-html is pushed, because each CI clones and builds it.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_2 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:36 main-thread: the user's 15:29Z push gave eoie and x a startup_failure ('workflow file issue'). Their .github/workflows/actions.lock pinned only the actions used before, and GitHub refuses a workflow that uses an unpinned action. Regenerated with gh actions-lock in both repos (added actions/upload-artifact@v5.0.0, download-artifact@v6.0.0 and peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v4.1.0 with their commit SHAs; additions only, LF kept). Needs a commit and push. blog has no lockfile, and tibia's already listed every action, so both started normally.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_3 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-18T15:48Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:48 main-thread: blog's first run with the publish step (39140753644) ran the step on the Ubuntu leg as designed, then failed inside dir-tree-html's build.ps1. It builds with cargo +nightly-2025-11-01 but read the target dir with a plain cargo metadata, and on runners whose default toolchain is stable (blog, x) or 1.88.0 (eoie) that fails: Cargo.toml uses the nightly-only profile-rustflags. alphabet, dice, polyglot, spiral and tibia pin nightly, so they never hit it. Fixed in the dir-tree-html tree: metadata uses the same +nightly-2025-11-01, and an empty target dir now throws a clear RUST-FAILED instead of 'Cannot find path /release/dir-tree-html'. Push dir-tree-html before rerunning blog/x/eoie. Separately, blog's Windows leg failed on 'File main has a type error' in engine/html.spi against spiral main; that's being attributed to the lib raw-string conversion (sm'), git-0e's lane.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_4 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 4u32, "2026-10-18T16:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:13 main-thread: tibia is the first of the four through. Run 39140743725 (the 15:29Z push carrying the !cancelled() gating) went green on both legs plus push and created the gh-pages branch at 15:51Z. GitHub did not turn Pages on by itself, so I enabled it via the API (source gh-pages /, https://i574n.github.io/tibia/; on the user's request to publish every repo's tree; the repo is public). eoie/blog/x next, once their pushes (lockfiles, dir-tree-html cargo fix, html.spi) land and their push jobs create gh-pages.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_5 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 5u32, "2026-10-18T16:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:19 main-thread: the .github repo's page was stuck at its 10-17 deploy. Run 39137525444 had Ubuntu green and Windows red after 1h20m with no log (most likely a lost runner, as in tibia's earlier run), and its push job needed every leg, so it was skipped. Same fix as tibia, uncommitted: upload on the Ubuntu leg if !cancelled(), and push if !cancelled() && event push. Before, the push job had no if at all, so it also deployed on pull requests. workflow.livemd publishes last, so dist exists only when the Ubuntu leg got that far. spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot and dir-tree-html keep deploy-on-all-green; they publish today. dice, polyglot and alphabet were rerun at 12:14 local, because their last deploys predate the dir-tree-html push (no viewport meta yet).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_6 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:33 main-thread: spiral run 39140706896 (f5e487e0) is green on both legs, and its 16:32Z deploy carries the viewport meta (live page checked). Pages now on the mobile head: spiral, tibia, dir-tree-html. dice/polyglot/alphabet reruns are pending. eoie, blog, x and .github wait for the user's push.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_7 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 7u32, "2026-10-18T19:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:22 main-thread: gh-pages branches exist now for eoie, blog and x (first deploys after the user's pushes), so Pages was enabled through the API (POST repos/i574n/<repo>/pages, source gh-pages /): i574n.github.io/eoie, /blog, /x. Next: verify each live page (viewport meta, README section once dir-tree-html's closer fix is pushed)")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_8 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 8u32, "2026-10-18T19:23Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:23 main-thread: live check: i574n.github.io/eoie 200 (197,903 bytes), /blog 200 (2,766), /x 200 (15,043), each with the viewport meta. Every repo with a tree page now serves it. Left: the next deploys pick up dir-tree-html's README section once its closer fix is committed")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_9 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: live: i574n.github.io/dir-tree-html serves the README section (200, class readme present), so the closer fix reached gh-pages; alphabet's 06:48 publish failed in dir-tree-html exit 3 on its Korean README (UTF-8 slice), fixed in the tree today (DIR-TREE-README) and waiting for the user's commit Next: after the next alphabet push, confirm i574n.github.io/alphabet shows its README; then Done")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_10 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:13 lap 2 grain: live sweep of the 8 pages: x, blog, dir-tree-html 200 with the README section; alphabet, dice, polyglot, spiral 200 but no README section (their last gh-pages deploys are 10-18 16:49-19:00Z, before dir-tree-html's README renderer, and they deploy only on all-green, which none has had since); eoie REGRESSED to 404: its 10-19 06:49Z run (39212135640) hit dir-tree-html exit 3 in publish (same renderer failure as alphabet's Korean README), yet the push job (if !cancelled) deployed the half-built dist: gh-pages now holds the rsync copy (_github, _gitignore, src...) with no index.html. Fix applied in the tree: spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 now calls the renderer directly (Invoke-Block exits the process on failure, so no cleanup could run) and on a non-zero exit removes dist and throws, so upload has nothing and the push job fails instead of replacing the last good page; every repo's scripts/publish.ps1 goes through this one script (grep: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, eoie, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, tibia, x), and CI clones spiral, so it needs the user's spiral push. Parse-checked, not run. The renderer bug itself is DIR-TREE-README (fixed in the dir-tree-html tree, uncommitted). Next: after the user's pushes of spiral + dir-tree-html, re-sweep all 8 pages for 200 + README section; eoie must come back from 404")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_11 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:51 lap 3 sweep (10 pages): 200 with viewport meta and @media rules on alphabet, dice, spiral, polyglot, x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia (README section on x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia); 404 on eoie AND on i574n.github.io/.github/: the .github repo has the same regression as eoie, its 10-18 23:32Z deploy (from the red 22:13Z run) holds the rsync copy (_devcontainer, _github, docs, README.md ...) with no index.html, while Pages reports status built. So the half-dist push hit 2 repos; the publish-tree.ps1 fix from lap 2 (remove dist and throw on a renderer failure) covers both, since i574n.github/scripts/publish.ps1 calls the same spiral script. Both recover on their next green-enough push after the user pushes spiral and dir-tree-html. Done criterion unchanged: all 10 pages 200 with the README section. Next: re-sweep after the user's next push wave")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_12 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:14 lap 5: re-sweep unchanged (8 pages 200 with viewport, README section on x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia; eoie and .github still 404), because the fixes are still unpushed (spiral scripts/publish-tree.ps1 and the dir-tree-html renderer are modified in the trees). New evidence that the push alone recovers both 404 pages: the 10-18 renderer (target/release/deps/dir_tree_html.exe) exits 3 on the README.md of eoie, i574n.github and alphabet, and the fixed 09:48 build exits 0 on all three in 9-37 ms with a README section (scratch dirs holding only each README). Done when: user pushes dir-tree-html + spiral, the next green runs of eoie and i574n.github redeploy, all 10 pages 200 with README.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_13 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:32 lap 6: checked the phone half on a live page (tibia, served by the current renderer): it has a viewport meta, @media (max-width: 600px) rules for portrait, overflow-wrap: anywhere for long names and overflow-x: auto for code blocks. Landscape phones (about 640-930 px wide) get the desktop layout capped at max-width 900px, so nothing scrolls the page sideways. No renderer change is needed for portrait or landscape. The item is only waiting on the push that brings eoie and .github back from 404.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_14 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:54 lap 7 grain: the Done check is one call now. lab/TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO/sweep.ps1 fetches all 10 pages and requires status 200, the README section, a viewport meta and the 600px portrait rules. It prints TREE-PAGES-OK and exits 0 only when all 10 pass; otherwise it names the pages still missing. First run: 4 of 10 ok (x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia). alphabet, dice, spiral and polyglot lack the README section; eoie and .github are 404. spiral HEAD is still 668568ac (no push since 02:48). Done = this script printing TREE-PAGES-OK after the push wave.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_15 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 15u32, "2026-10-19T16:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:26 lap 8 grain: sweep at 12:25 unchanged (4 of 10; spiral origin still 668568ac). Confirmed the 404 cause directly instead of by inference: gh api git trees of gh-pages for eoie (deploy 06:55Z) and i574n/.github list the rsync copy (.nojekyll, README.md, _github ...) with no index.html, while x's root has index.html; both sites report Pages status built from gh-pages /. The unpushed publish-tree.ps1 renderer-exit check plus the fixed renderer recover both. Done = sweep.ps1 TREE-PAGES-OK after the push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_16 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 16u32, "2026-10-19T16:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:53 lap 9 grain: sweep.ps1 now adds a heads column per repo (gh-pages and main from git ls-remote), so the Done evidence names the deploy commit behind each page, and a still-failing page after a push shows at once whether its gh-pages moved (tested on eoie and x: eoie gh-pages=f0f1dfb8 main=40e71ade 404, x gh-pages=eefc4fcc 200). Remote mains at 12:53: spiral 668568ac, eoie 40e71ade, .github 9dd1144e, dice a0502e18, alphabet 08e566fb, polyglot c187d421, no push since the morning")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_17 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain: remote heads re-read (git ls-remote): spiral main 668568ac, eoie 40e71ade, .github 9dd1144e unchanged since 12:53, so still no push; recorded the other sibling heads as the pre-push reference: dice main a0502e18 gh-pages 5628355f, alphabet 08e566fb / cdd28acb, polyglot c187d421 / e5553451. Next: sweep.ps1 after the user's push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_18 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:49 lap 11 grain, scope check of sweep.ps1's repo list against GitHub: all 10 swept repos have a gh-pages branch; of the other local checkouts, tictactoe also has one but is not swept, and futuresales and The-Spiral-Language have none. tictactoe's last commit is a473452 of 2023-03-14 (dormant, pre-Spiral-2 era), so it stays out of the sweep on purpose (its gh-pages is not a tree page and nothing deploys it); recorded so the Done row can say every active repo. Next: sweep.ps1 after the user's push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_19 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 19u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:49 verified the tictactoe claim: its gh-pages head (fetched with a depth-1 fetch, no other ref touched) is a 2023-03-14 deploy of a47345 holding .nojekyll, assets and index.html, i.e. the old app build, not a dir-tree page")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_20 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 20u32, "2026-10-19T18:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:14 lap 12 sweep.ps1: 4 of 10 pages pass (x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia). alphabet, dice, spiral, polyglot serve 200 without the README section (older deploys, the fix waits on the push). eoie and .github return 404 although Pages is built from gh-pages: both gh-pages branches hold a copy of the repo tree with dotfiles renamed (_github, _gitignore) and no index.html at the root. Next: find the deploy step that writes them")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_21 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 21u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 13: the README-less pages of spiral, dice, alphabet and polyglot are stale deploys rather than a code gap: spiral's live page has README.md links but no class=readme section (x's has it), their last gh-pages deploys (10-18) predate dir-tree-html's readme commit eaba51c reaching main, and the workflow clones and builds dir-tree-html main fresh on every run. So any new deploy of those 4 repos (their next push, or a manual dispatch by the user) should turn them green; only eoie and .github depend on the unpushed publish-tree.ps1 fix")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_22 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 22u32, "2026-10-19T18:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:45 correction to the 14:39 note: spiral's last deploy (19:00:07Z) came from run 39151274415, created 17:48:35Z, before dir-tree-html eaba51c reached main at 18:51:23Z, so that page is a stale render as said; dice, alphabet and polyglot all render through spiral's scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 (fresh clone of main). But every gh-pages run of these 4 repos since 18:51Z has failed, so the pages refresh only on a successful run: the item's PIPELINES-GREEN dependency, not just any redeploy")
inl eoie_agile_note_edit () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT", "eoie agile has no way to fix a note (found 2026-10-18 by main-thread): two mistyped note times (LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS note 6, CELLS-GATE note 2) needed appended correction notes. Add note-edit <root> <id> <n> <text> (author and original timestamp kept, edit stamp recorded) in one call", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,friction", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_edit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 landed: agile note-edit <root> <id> <note-number> <text> keeps the note time and author, replaces the text (AgileNoteEdit case in agile_subcommand_domain, route in eoie_agile_mutation, note_edited in agile_task_notes; lease mutation by default); receipt text_bytes=old->new; e2e on a scratch backlog")
inl cells_lab_package () = Task ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", "Cells cannot run test blocks of a package under tibia/lab/dota-lua-bindings: every block fails with 'Owner of file main has an error. Location: package.spiproj. Package not loaded' (x3), even when package.spiproj is reduced to the same '|core-' and module list as tibia/src (which works). The compiler CLI (BuildSpiral -Backend Lua) compiles the same package, including its arena- cross reference to tibia apps/arena. A copy of the files outside the repo fails differently (ETY-UnboundModule on 'open testing'). Related: the parser rejects a qualified case with a named binding ('| last_hit.Hit index =>', column 24) while '| vitality.Slain _' parses.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,packages,dota", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_1 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:02 repro: pwsh spiral/apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 --path C:\\home\\git\\tibia\\lab\\dota-lua-bindings\\last_hit.spi --output-path <scratch>.ipynb --no-spi. Workaround in place: lab/dota-lua-bindings/check.ps1 compiles bot_generic through the CLI and runs it in a sandboxed Lua mock; the pick tests stay in last_hit.spi for when cells loads lab packages.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_2 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:26 main-thread (compiler lane) on finding (2), the qualified union case pattern. Two-module repro (union target in target.spi, match in main.spi): '| target.Hit index =>' parses and types fine. The parse failure is the payload-less qualified case, '| target.Hold =>', at col 19 with 'Expected one of: lowercase variable, _, ...'. root_pattern_var_nominal_union required a sub-pattern whenever tight symbols followed the head, while the unqualified path allows none. Fixed in shared BlockParsing, unbuilt: when the last qualified symbol is uppercase (a case), the payload is optional and defaults to PatE, which inference already turns into PatUnbox(case, PatE) like an unqualified '| Hold =>'. A qualified lowercase nominal still requires a payload. A same-module self-qualification ('| last_hit.Hit index' inside last_hit.spi) is a type error, not a parse error: a module can't name itself, so it reports Unbound type variable last_hit, and the unqualified '| Hit index' you switched to is the right form there. I couldn't reproduce the reported col-24 parse error exactly; send the line if it persists after the next compiler build. Regression sample samples/qualified_union_case_no_payload follows after today's accept.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_3 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:55 main-thread: qualified-case parser fix verified on the Debug candidate. samples/qualified_union_case_no_payload (| signal.Hold => with no payload) compiles to C and exits 43; the installed 10:28 compiler rejects it at 4:19.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_4 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T18:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 14:46 narrowed: with tibia/src copied to tibia/target/zig-oracle-zig, files without a '///- --package .' first line pass through run_notebook (quantity, coins, damage, cooldowns), while combat.spi (first line '///- --package .') fails every block with 'Package not loaded' x3; the same file passes from tibia/src. So the header resolves only for packages the cells/compiler daemon already knows, not for an identical package at another path. Sent to the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_5 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:03 main-thread on finding (1): not a Cells defect. The failing copy tibia/target/zig-oracle-zig was stale: creatures.spi and hunting_pack.spi predated src's Hunter and ranged-attack changes while the copied combat.spi did not, so the package failed to typecheck. Files without the ///- --package . header never load the package and passed. Checks: package.spiproj byte-identical; a fresh copy of src passes combat.spi 9/9 from a scratch dir with a deps/spiral junction, under a target/ dir, via --gate and via run_notebook.ps1 (warm daemon); the stale copy fails 0/9 via --gate too. Real gaps found on the way: (a) the error 'Owner of file main has an error. Location: package.spiproj. Package not loaded' names neither the failing module nor its first error, so it should; (b) Notebook.find_spiral_lib (@lib_rels) knows deps/spiral, deps/polyglot/..., lib/spiral and polyglot/..., but not the sibling layout <ancestor>/spiral/lib/spiral that blog and x use, so a .spi there without the ///- --package . line has no lib (blog CI's html.spi). Plan: add spiral/lib/spiral to @lib_rels, and make the compiler's Package not loaded error name the module.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_6 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T19:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:05 lib search gained the sibling layout spiral/lib/spiral after polyglot/deps; find_spiral_lib is now public with @doc false; notebook_spiral_lib_test.exs covers the sibling clone and a closer deps/spiral winning, 2 passed")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_7 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T19:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:07 CORRECTION to note 4 (found by the compiler lane): the zig-oracle copy was taken while I was editing src, so it held an older creatures.spi and hunting_pack.spi beside a newer combat.spi. The package failed to typecheck, and Cells reports any package typecheck failure as 'Owner of file main has an error. Location: package.spiproj. Package not loaded' x3. Header-less files passed only because they never load the package. A fresh copy of src under target/ passes combat 9/9. So headers resolve fine outside src. The real defect is the message: it should name the module that failed to typecheck. This morning's dota lab failure may have been the same (an inconsistent package), not the path.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_8 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 8u32, "2026-10-18T19:09Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:09 gap (a) staged in spiral_compiler.fs, unbuilt (goes in with the post-accept compiler build): ss_validate_packages now says Package <dir> is not loaded and Package <dir> has an error. First error: <its first schema error>, and ss_validate_modules says Module <path> is not loaded, so the Owner of file ... has an error detail names the dependency dir. The stale copy is gone, so no repro of the x3 case; after the build, a deliberately inconsistent package copy checks that the message names the culprit")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_9 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 9u32, "2026-10-18T21:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:26 real root cause (corrects the 'copy raced src edits' note): Cells mounts a '///- --package .' directory under its basename as the package name (cells.ex mount_packages: Path.basename(target)), and a basename with hyphens is not a valid package name in .spiproj syntax, where '-' is a modifier (|core-). A copy at target/zig-oracle-c failed with exactly three 'Package not loaded' (zig, oracle, c) even with src quiet and SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR set; the same copy at target/zig_oracle_c passes combat 9/9. tibia/src works because its name is 'src'; lab folders are named after backlog ids (tibia-potion-fidelity), so every header file copied into one hits this. Ask: validate or sanitize the mounted name (or mount under an internal alias) and report 'package directory name X is not a valid package name' instead of the generic error. Workaround in the Tibia lab: underscore directory names.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_10 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 10u32, "2026-10-18T21:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:35 main-thread: the tibia lane's root cause holds, and my stale-copy note 5 was wrong. Cells mounted a '--package .' directory under its raw basename, so a hyphenated name (zig-oracle-c, dota-lua-bindings) was read by the package syntax as several packages (zig, oracle, c), giving one 'Package not loaded' each. Fixed in Cells rather than only erroring: mount_packages names each mount with Mounts.mount_name, which turns non-identifier characters into _ and prefixes a leading digit. A real package with the sanitized name still hits the existing 'listed twice' check. Tests: mounts_name_test.exs 2/2. End to end through mix spiral.notebook --gate, the same lib-free package.spiproj + combat.spi passes 1/1 in zig-oracle-c and in zig_oracle_c. This covers the dota-lua-bindings lab failure from note 1 too. Left: the compiler-side package and module error names (staged, unbuilt)")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_11 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:30 helper-lane lap 1: DONE with evidence: the last open part (compiler-side package/module error names) is committed (65f83164, spiral_compiler.fs:181214 Module X is not loaded, :181284-181288 Package X has an error. First error: ... / Package X is not loaded) and in the installed Release DLL (built 10-18 22:57). Probed it on that DLL with a throwaway package (Invoke-Heavy, exit 5 both times): a missing dependency prints Package C:/.../absent_helper is not loaded., and a dependency whose spiproj lists a missing module prints PackageErrors at broken_helper/package.spiproj: Module .../broken_helper/nowhere.spi is not loaded. - both name the culprit directory instead of the old generic Owner of file main has an error. The Cells side (Mounts.mount_name sanitizing hyphenated lab dirs) landed 10-18 with mounts_name_test 2/2. Probe files removed.")
inl note_of_cells_lab_package_12 () = Note ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:48Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:48 follow-up bug found by main (item stays Done, fix is small): mount_name sanitized hyphens but kept case, so a package in an uppercase backlog-id lab folder (spiral/lab/KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB) mounted as KINO_WITHOUT_ELIXIR and Cells' generated package.spiproj failed to parse (PackageErrors line 4: Expecting ... lowercase variable name); the same module compiles fine through the compiler directly. mounts.ex mount_name now lowercases first ([a-z0-9_]), with test an uppercase backlog-id lab directory mounts under a lowercase name; mounts_name_test 3/3 passed. The lab gates of those two folders rerun after the core switch.")
inl spiral_c_ucrt_heap () = Task ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP", "native_closure_recursive_capture generated C corrupts the heap under UCRT", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,codegen,c,ucrt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_heap_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:36Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:36 found in review single-flight-20261018-110213: samples/native_closure_recursive_capture/main.c (unchanged since d94dc380) built by scoop mingw 16.2 (UCRT rt_v14) exits 0xC0000374 STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION, built by scoop gcc 15.2 (msvcrt) exits 42 as the oracle expects; reproduced by hand with plain gcc -O2 outside test.ps1. A real latent codegen bug (likely a double free or use after free in recursive closure capture refcounting) that msvcrt tolerates. The accept stays on gcc 15.2 (SPIRAL_CC pinned) until this is fixed")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_heap_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:16 main-thread: root cause found and fixed in the tree (shared CodegenC, so both cores; unbuilt). It's strict-aliasing UB in C closures, not a UCRT defect. Closure{i} repeated Fun{t}'s header (refc, decref_fptr, fptr) instead of embedding it, ClosureCreate returned (Fun{t} *) x, and refc was written as both Fun (main's v3->refc++) and Closure (ClosureDecref's --x->refc). Once inlined at -O2, gcc may treat those as non-aliasing and miscount, freeing early; msvcrt's heap doesn't notice, UCRT's does (0xC0000374). Bisected on the committed main.c with mingw 16.2: -O0/-O1 exit 42, -O2 crashes, -O2 -fno-strict-aliasing 42, -O2 -fno-inline 42; gcc -Wall warns 'type-punned pointer might break strict-aliasing' at the return (Fun0 *) x line; no CRT mixing (imports are UCRT only); an malloc/free trace shows 3 allocations, 3 frees, no double free. Fix: struct Closure{i} { Fun{t} base; captures }; ClosureDecref{i}(Fun{t} * self) and the closure function take Fun{t} * self and cast to Closure{i} * (valid: a pointer to the first member converts back to the struct); header access goes through x->base; ClosureCreate returns &x->base; cfun is requested first so Fun{t} is complete before the struct. Hand-applied to the sample: exit 42 on mingw 16.2 and gcc 15.2 at -O2 and -O3, -Wall -Wextra clean. Impact: every C sample with a closure changes residual (layout only). Next pack: build both cores, review, accept. The sample itself is the regression check once the baseline toolchain is UCRT (MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY).")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_heap_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:55 main-thread: verified on a Debug candidate built from the tree (0 errors). The generated native_closure_recursive_capture C has Fun0 base; and return &x->base;, and exits 42 under both gcc 15.2 (msvcrt) and mingw 16.2 (UCRT) at -O2 with -Wall -Wextra clean. Rides the next Release pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_heap_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP", 4u32, "2026-10-19T02:54Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 22:54 main-thread: pack 3 ACCEPTED (run single-flight-20261018-220027, 7,000 rows into baseline/EXPECTED.tsv, toolchain.tsv refreshed, SPIRAL_CC pinned to gcc 15.2). DISAGREE 0. Against its review (164007), the 8 load timeouts ran and matched their earlier accepted exits. 92 C rows changed only in the residual (the closure base embedding: Fun{t} base, decref/body take Fun*, ClosureCreate returns &x->base), all exits equal to the 123705 same-toolchain run. The pack also carries the F# macro-assigned let mutable (while_loop builds and agrees; while_control is Known), the payload-less qualified union case parser fix (regression sample qualified_union_case_no_payload, new on F#/C/Rust/Delphi), and F# rows for the 2 UTF-8 samples. Native run on a quiet machine: 49 min")
inl spiral_c_ucrt_abort_exit () = Task ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT", "Map the UCRT abort fast-fail exit 0xC0000409 to exit 3 for C rows", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,matrix,c,ucrt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_abort_exit_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:36Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:36 samples/managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and samples/abi_external_buffer_length_negative call abort(): msvcrt gcc 15.2 binaries exit 3, UCRT mingw 16.2 binaries exit 0xC0000409 (fast fail). Map it in the C shim (e.g. a SIGABRT handler or _set_abort_behavior plus exit 3) or in the matrix scoring, so a UCRT toolchain can become the baseline. Found together with SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP; env.ps1 resolves CC as SPIRAL_CC or the first gcc on PATH, and the user PATH lists mingw before gcc")
inl note_of_spiral_c_ucrt_abort_exit_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:17 main-thread: closed, no change needed. test.ps1 already scores this. Test-FailureExit counts 1, 3, 134 and every NTSTATUS code (>= 0xC0000000) as a failure exit, and Test-SharedFailure treats two failing rows as agreeing. That's why git-0e's UCRT review had DISAGREE only on native_closure_recursive_capture (C went from success to failure, SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP), while managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and abi_external_buffer_length_negative (3 -> 0xC0000409) stayed agreed. What remains is the raw exit cell against EXPECTED, and that's covered by recording the baseline toolchain (MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY): a UCRT baseline simply records 0xC0000409. Rejected: replacing abort() with _Exit(3) in CodegenC and the shims. It would drop SIGABRT semantics (debuggers, crash dumps, Linux 134) to normalize a cell the matrix already compares as a failure.")
inl matrix_toolchain_identity () = Task ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", "The oracle baseline records its native toolchain identity, and test.ps1 flags a run whose toolchain differs (found 2026-10-18: the user PATH puts scoop mingw 16.2/UCRT before gcc 15.2/msvcrt, so sessions resolved different CCs and git-0e's review could not be reproduced by an accept in another session)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP,SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT", "matrix,oracle,toolchain,determinism", "current", "current")
inl note_of_matrix_toolchain_identity_1 () = Note ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:37Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:37 main-thread. Evidence: review single-flight-20261018-110213 (git-0e, CC = scoop mingw 16.2, UCRT) vs 084525 (mine, scoop gcc 15.2, msvcrt): 0 residual differences, 3 C exit cells differ (native_closure_recursive_capture 42 -> 0xC0000374; managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and abi_external_buffer_length_negative 3 -> 0xC0000409), 22 oracle cells follow, DISAGREE 12. Plan: (1) test.ps1 writes <run>/toolchain.tsv (each native tool's resolved path, --version first line and Get-ToolStamp), the identity the native memo key already uses; (2) -Accept copies it beside EXPECTED.tsv as baseline/toolchain.tsv; (3) every later run compares its tools with the baseline's and prints 'toolchain differs from baseline: CC ...' in the summary, and -Accept refuses a mismatch unless -AcceptToolchain is given. compare-runs.ps1 prints both toolchains when they differ. Today's accept pins SPIRAL_CC to gcc 15.2 explicitly. Moving the baseline to UCRT waits for SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP and SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT.")
inl note_of_matrix_toolchain_identity_2 () = Note ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", 2u32, "2026-10-18T16:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:39 main-thread: patch ready in lab/MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY/apply.py, to apply after today's accept (test.ps1 stays frozen during it). It adds -AcceptToolchain, builds a tool/stamp table from Get-SpiralNativeTools through test.ps1's own Get-ToolStamp (scoop shims and current junctions resolve to the versioned file, plus size and mtime), prints the tools whose stamp differs from baseline/toolchain.tsv, refuses -Accept on a difference unless -AcceptToolchain is given, writes <run>/toolchain.tsv, and copies it to baseline/toolchain.tsv on accept. The block sits after Get-ToolStamp and outside the native signature functions, so applying it invalidates no native memo. Dry run on a copy: applies, parses, and the 22-tool table reads; the two CCs stamp differently: ...\\gcc\\15.2.0\\bin\\gcc.exe|2239488|... vs ...\\mingw\\16.2.0-rt_v14-rev1\\bin\\gcc.exe|3309056|.... The first accept after applying writes the baseline toolchain, since none exists yet.")
inl note_of_matrix_toolchain_identity_3 () = Note ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 13:59 main-thread: applied and verified end to end. apply.py ran on test.ps1 and AGENTS.md (parses, LF). The baseline toolchain for today's accept was written from that run's environment (SPIRAL_CC pinned to gcc 15.2; all 22 tool stamps predate the 12:37 accept start) to baseline/toolchain.tsv. A one-sample run (examples, tuple_pair, C, -Native) with SPIRAL_CC=mingw 16.2 prints 'toolchain differs from the baseline's: CC: ...gcc\\15.2.0\\bin\\gcc.exe|... -> ...mingw\\16.2.0-rt_v14-rev1\\bin\\gcc.exe|...'. The same run with -Accept exits 1 with the refusal, and EXPECTED.tsv keeps its hash (B7E008A3C1F7 before and after).")
inl note_of_matrix_toolchain_identity_4 () = Note ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: used in anger today: pack 5's review printed toolchain differs for WgslRun (wgpu 24 -> 30 runner) and the accept runs with -AcceptToolchain, exactly the designed path; remaining deps are the two UCRT items (SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP, SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT) Next: close when the UCRT deps close, or drop them as deps if the baseline stays on gcc 15.2 msvcrt by decision")
inl note_of_matrix_toolchain_identity_5 () = Note ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:13 lap 2: DONE with evidence. Both deps are Done (SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP 100, SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT 100). Goal checked against the tree and cache, not old notes: test.ps1 has -AcceptToolchain (line 10), compares the run's tool stamps with baseline/toolchain.tsv (354), refuses -Accept on a difference without -AcceptToolchain (364), writes <run>/toolchain.tsv on -Native (366) and copies it to the baseline on accept (1114); spiral-bin/baseline/toolchain.tsv was written 2026-10-19 08:11:04, identical timestamp to pack 5's accepted run single-flight-20261019-080524/toolchain.tsv, and the later run 085820 wrote its own toolchain.tsv. Used in anger on pack 5 (WgslRun wgpu 24 -> 30 flagged, accepted with -AcceptToolchain)")
inl x_on_near () = Task ("X-ON-NEAR", "X rewritten as NEAR contracts with the sim as spec, driver and oracle (deferred, user 2026-10-18: too much for now): contract = SocialDB-style per-account storage (social.near) + NEP-145 storage deposits, Farcaster-style add/remove merge rules (remove wins, later timestamp wins, hash tie-break) as Spiral types; xc gains a NEAR site (free view reads over RPC, signed writes); feeds off chain in an indexer tested with the sim's reorder/duplicate faults, or on chain as paged views (NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW); copy designs not code (check licenses). Does not need SIM-IN-SPIRAL: the Elixir sim already emits the event stream (XSIM-EVENT-STREAM). Folds in X-NEAR-PROVENANCE", Epic, P4, D8, 0u32, Planned, "XSIM-EVENT-STREAM,NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS,NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW,NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES,X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", "x,near,sim,social", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_on_near () = Planning ("X-ON-NEAR", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_x_on_near_1 () = Note ("X-ON-NEAR", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 direction (user): iterate until feature parity with X while dogfooding everything (xc, sim, Cells, the compiler), keep a running list of NEAR gotchas as they surface, and stay Mastodon and Bluesky compatible (X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES), until a killer app arrives. Waits for the full machine: today CPU and memory go to spiral compiler maintenance, and the sandbox (near-workspaces/neard) needs WSL (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE: WSL 3 bounded jobs, memory=6GB, wsl --shutdown after). Only NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS and XSIM-EVENT-STREAM run without WSL")
inl note_of_x_on_near_2 () = Note ("X-ON-NEAR", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 technical design (from the 2026-10-18 review): sim reuse = spec (typed events post/reply/quote/edit-as-revision/delete/like/unlike, idempotent per event, causal buffering, commute across orders), driver (xsim.world scenario -> contract calls) and oracle (same expected state as the X stub); on NEAR one contract's receipts run in one order and final blocks never roll back, so delivery faults move to the indexer/reader side. Prior art: social.near SocialDB (per-account key-value store, poster pays storage), NEP-145 storage_deposit/withdraw/unregister, Farcaster message sets (CastAdd/Remove, ReactionAdd/Remove, LinkAdd/Remove; remove wins, later timestamp wins, hash tie-break), AT Protocol (signed per-user repos, relays, app views). Approximate costs: storage ~1 NEAR per 100 KB (1,000 posts at ~400 B lock ~4 NEAR, refunded on delete), a simple write ~5 Tgas ~0.0005 NEAR, 300 Tgas ceiling per call. Shape: shared Spiral domain crates under the 1,000-line gate linked into one cdylib; xc reads/writes GADTs gain a NEAR site (view calls over RPC, signed function calls, key in a secrets env file). Phases: 1 domain types + merge-rule commutation tests, 2 contract (SocialDB layout + NEP-145), 3 native replay of sim scenarios in near-sdk's mock, 4 xc NEAR site, 5 feeds (NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW or an off-chain indexer tested with the sim's reorder/duplicate faults), 6 sandbox in CI Linux")
inl xsim_event_stream () = Task ("XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", "Promote the sim's --save delivery logs to a documented, versioned event stream (post/reply/edit/delete/like/unlike with users, ids and revision sources, key-sorted JSON lines) plus a drift-guarded fixture in x, so any implementation replays the same scenario and compares with the sim's expected state: the NEAR contract mock, the Spiral sim port, other clients", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,sim,fixtures", "current", "current")
inl note_of_xsim_event_stream_1 () = Note ("XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:22Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:22 xsim-events v1: Xsim.Log writes a header line {format:xsim-events,version:1} then one compact key-sorted JSON object per delivery with named fields per kind (post: post parent source; frame: post parent k n body; edit: post source; delete: post; like/unlike: post user) plus at (delivery index) and kind; unknown versions raise, the old positional lines still read (--save/--replay unchanged); mix xsim.events writes a seeded scenario's shuffled, duplicated stream and the final state every replay must reach (live cells with parent and final source, like counts); fixture apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (44 deliveries, framed, fickle+editor personas) and prelude.final.json, regenerated and compared by apps/sim/build.ps1 (-Accept rewrites); sim tests 35/35")
inl note_of_xsim_event_stream_2 () = Note ("XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", 2u32, "2026-10-18T20:46Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:46 done: a test replays apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl through Xsim.World and asserts it reaches prelude.final.json (live posts, parents, final sources, like counts); with framed edits the stream is 50 deliveries incl. 13 edit_frame (format v1 gained edit_frame: post rev k n body)")
inl near_mocked_unit_tests () = Task ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", "Run NEAR contract logic natively on Windows through near-sdk's unit-testing mock (testing_env!, VMContextBuilder, env::used_gas) as Spiral test blocks, starting with dice and chat (mirror in alphabet): the fast loop needs neither the sandbox nor WSL, measures gas per method, and the sandbox runs stay on CI Linux (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE)", Story, P3, D3, 40u32, Blocked, "", "near,dice,polyglot,testing", "current", "current")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_1 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:53Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:53 helper-lane lap 1: mapped the path for test blocks: both contracts pin near-sdk =5.11.0 (dice/contract and polyglot/apps/chat/contract Cargo.toml, borsh 1.5, indexmap =2.11.4), and near-sdk's mock (testing_env!, VMContextBuilder) needs the unit-testing feature. Cells passes ///> rust -d deps as --deps to the spiral CLI, whose cargo writer (apps/spiral/spiral.spi:309 and :912) turns name[feat] into name={version='*',features=[...]} and name=ver into a pinned version, but a token cannot carry both, so -d near-sdk[unit-testing] would resolve the newest near-sdk instead of the contracts' 5.11.0. Smallest unblock: accept name=version[features] in builders.ex parse_dep (Cells) and in spiral.spi's dep writer, then a first dice test block ///> rust -d near-sdk=5.11.0[unit-testing] -d borsh=1.5 calling roll_within_bounds under testing_env!.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_2 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:36 helper-lane lap 2: correction of lap 1 (no code change needed): Cells builders.ex dep_arg passes a -d token through raw when its version part starts with an opening brace or the token holds a quote, and apps/spiral/spiral.spi's cargo writer copies any token containing = verbatim into [dependencies] (lines 303-309). TOML accepts single-quoted literal strings, so a pinned crate with features is already expressible as one token without spaces: ///> rust -d near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} -d borsh=1.5, which becomes near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} in the generated Cargo.toml. The first dice mock test block can therefore be written today; what remains is the test itself (testing_env! with VMContextBuilder around roll_within_bounds) and its first cargo build of near-sdk, which is heavy.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_3 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:20 helper-lane lap 3: picked the first target by reading dice_contract.spi: roll_within_bounds is pure and already has two runnable test blocks, while new, contribute_seed and generate_random_number read near.random_seed, block_timestamp, signer_account_id and predecessor_account_id (lines 97-113), so generate_random_number is the first that needs a mocked context. Wrote it as lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/mocked_test_block.spi: ///> rust -c -d near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} -d near-token, a mocked () that calls near_sdk::testing_env with VMContextBuilder (random_seed [7u8; 32], a fixed block_timestamp; the macro bang written as a doubled bang inside the single-quoted macro), then two draws under the same context must be equal and positive. The explicit near-sdk token takes precedence, since apps/spiral adds near-sdk=5.11.0 only when no near-sdk dep is given (spiral.spi 293-301). run.ps1 appends the block to dice/contract/dice_contract.spi, runs only that test through Cells, and restores the file either way. HEAVY QUEUE (first near-sdk unit-testing build, about 10 min).")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_4 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:18 helper-lane lap 6: ran the queued row (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/run.ps1; dice_contract.spi patched temporarily and restored, git status clean). The mocked test never compiled, because dice's contract itself no longer type-checks on the current tree: dice_contract.spi:117:12, [ ... ] |> am'.to_vec, Unification failure, Got array (vec u8) -> vec (vec u8), Expected array_base _ -> vec _. Since the core v1 switch, an array literal is the nominal array el, and lib am'.to_vec still takes array_base. Reported to main as a core-switch consumer breakage. It is blocked until dice (and its alphabet mirror) or lib am' is adapted.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_5 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:31 helper-lane: after main fixed the three am'.to_vec sites in dice_contract.spi, I reran with the process compiler (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/contract-run.ps1, contract-gate-20261019-1829.log). All 7 contract tests fail at rust -c with the same type error, now in the mounted dice package: dice/lib/dice.spi 373-375, where main ;[] |> ignore in the Rust/Python/TypeScript arms fails with Got array _, Expected array_base string. It is the same core v1 array-literal migration, so it is not the contract build. The mocked test fails for the same reason. Reported to main; rerun after dice.spi is adapted.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_6 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T23:09Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:09 helper-lane: progress after main's dice migration. The contract gate passes 7 of 7 (contract-gate-20261019-1841.log). Three findings for the mocked test (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/mocked_test_block.spi): (1) Cells' builder tokenizer strips quotes, so the crate spec must be quoted whole (-d near-sdk with its version and unit-testing feature inside double quotes). Done, and spiral rust now gets the exact spec. (2) As a contract (rust -c, wasm32), near_sdk::testing_env does not exist (E0433), as near-sdk gates its unit-testing helpers off wasm, so mocked tests need a native rust target. Done, the ///> line is now rust without -c. (3) The native path's toolchain is rustc 1.88.0-nightly, and near-sys 0.2.14 (pulled by near-sdk 5.11.0) requires rustc 1.93, so cargo refuses. Blocked on main's toolchain pack (rustup update), not on code. dice_contract.spi is restored after each run.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_7 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T23:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:11 helper-lane: unblock condition (per main): the rustc used by the native Cells/spiral rust path must itself go to 1.93 or later. That is the pinned 1.88.0-nightly toolchain, and a rustup self-update does not move it. Alternatively, pin near-sys below 0.2.14 in the test's -d specs (for example -d near-sys with a version below 0.2.14 next to near-sdk), if near-sdk 5.11.0 accepts that range. The toolchain pack moved to 2026-10-20.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_8 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T23:58Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:58 helper-lane: the near-sys pin route is closed. With -d near-sys pinned to =0.2.13 (run-20261019-1942.log), cargo reports near-sys 0.2.13 also requires rustc 1.93, so only bumping the native toolchain unblocks the test. The test block keeps the pin out of the next run (to be removed from mocked_test_block.spi). dice_contract.spi was restored by the run.")
inl near_feed_paged_view () = Task ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", "Gas-limit spike for an on-chain home feed: a minimal contract (post, follow, feed view) where the feed is a k-way merge over the followed accounts' post indices computed in bounded view-call steps with a typed resumable cursor (a GADT step indexed by its phase, the per-step budget computed at compile time from gas measured in the mock); view calls cost the reader nothing, so the client pages; compare with fan-out on write as a resumable job queue or NEP-519 yield/resume; replay XSIM-EVENT-STREAM scenarios and report gas per page and follows per page", Story, P4, D4, 0u32, Planned, "NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS,XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", "near,x,gadt,feed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_feed_paged_view () = Planning ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_1 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 design detail: views are free for the reader but each call still has a gas ceiling, so the merge must be resumable: cursor = per-followed-account position (or a timestamp watermark plus offsets) returned with each page; the step is a typed state machine (GADT indexed by merge phase) whose per-step item budget is a compile-time constant derived from gas measured in the mock; fan-out on write = a queue drained by later transactions (or NEP-519 promise_yield_create/resume) for accounts with many followers; report gas per page, follows per page and storage per post")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_2 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: dep check: XSIM-EVENT-STREAM is Done, NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS (Planned 0%) is the real gate, since the per-step budget must come from gas measured in the mock. Found an existing in-tree instance of the same unbounded-view problem, so the spike's paging can be proven without a new contract first: polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.rs get_alias_map(alias) returns the whole HashMap<String, (u64, u32)> of an alias in one view call (plus a borsh twin), so a popular alias would hit the view gas ceiling exactly like an unpaged feed. A paged get_alias_map(alias, cursor, limit) with a typed cursor is the smallest rehearsal of the feed's resumable view, measurable in the mock once NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS lands.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_3 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: modelled the resumable feed view off-chain first (lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_merge.spi, CPU-only): a k-way merge over per-account post timestamps (newest_head picks the newest head, drop_head consumes it) where page feeds budget returns up to budget items plus the remaining feeds as the cursor, and all_pages resumes from that cursor until a page is empty. Check: three accounts (8 posts) paged by 3 give 3 pages whose order checksum equals one unbounded page's, i.e. resuming from the cursor loses or reorders nothing; C exit 3 via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy. On-chain, the cursor would shrink to per-account positions instead of the remaining lists, and budget is the compile-time constant from the mock's gas per item.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_4 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the on-chain cursor shape as lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_offsets.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): the feeds stay immutable (each account's post index, newest first, read by position as a vector would be), and the cursor is one i32 offset per followed account instead of the remaining lists; newest_at scans the heads at the current offsets, bump advances only the winning account's offset, page returns up to budget items plus the new offsets, all_pages resumes from them. Same check as the list version: 3 accounts, 8 posts, budget 3 must give 3 pages whose order checksum equals one unbounded page (expect exit 3). The cursor is now O(follows) integers, which is what a view call can return and take back as its argument.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_5 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:01 helper-lane lap 5: the cursor is now the title's typed resumable step (lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_offsets.spi, unrun until the freeze all-clear): a GADT cursor phase with Fresh and Paused offsets indexed scanning and Exhausted indexed exhausted, next_page accepting only a cursor scanning and returning More (items, cursor scanning) or Last items (it peeks whether any head remains, so the last page is marked without an extra empty call), and drain resuming until Last. Same equality check as before (expect exit 3: 3 pages of the 8 posts equal one unbounded page). paged_exhausted_negative.spi passes Exhausted to next_page and must be rejected at compile time - the type, not a runtime check, stops a client from paging a finished feed.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_6 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: paged_offsets.spi with the phase-indexed cursor GADT exits 3 as predicted (3 pages resumed through Paused offsets equal one unbounded page). The compile-time negative (paging Exhausted) is still to run.")
inl near_contract_crates () = Task ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", "Give the NEAR contracts x's shape: dice_contract.rs (178 KB) and chat_contract.rs (83 KB) are single generated files; split each into library crates under the 1,000-line gate linked by one cdylib contract crate ([package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, scripts/gate.ps1), mirrored in alphabet", Story, P4, D3, 0u32, Planned, "-", "near,dice,polyglot,crate-gate", "current", "current")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_1 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:57 helper-lane lap 1: sized against x's shape: dice/contract/dice_contract.spi is 230 non-blank Spiral lines and generates dice_contract.rs 174 KB / 3,396 lines / 187 fns (a 15x expansion, because the source opens the dice package via ///- --package ../dice and partial evaluation inlines that library into the contract); polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi 190 lines -> chat_contract.rs 81 KB / 1,374 lines / 65 fns. So under the 1,000-line gate dice needs at least 4 crates and chat 2, and the split cannot be done by cutting the contract file: it needs Export boundaries in the dice package itself (x's pattern: per-crate .spi with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output and path dependencies, e.g. xc_args -> xc_checks). The natural first cut is dice's randomness core (already generated separately as dice/lib/dice.rs) as a library crate the contract links.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_2 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: picked the first exported crate from what the contract reaches: dice_contract.spi calls exactly four dice package functions (dice.create_sequential_roller, dice.roll_progressively, dice.roll_within_bounds, dice.rotate_numbers), and its 187 generated functions are 82 numbered methods, 10 tag impls (before the lazy-tag change reached this file), ~60 closures and a handful of named ones - nearly all of it inlined dice-package code. So a dice_core crate exporting those four functions (Export with a scalar/array signature) would carry most of the 3,396 lines, leaving the contract crate as the NEAR glue (state, borsh, the 6 exported methods). Caveat from CLI-ARGS-STYLE's lap-2 probe: Export signatures must stay scalar or array, because exported union types are private and per-crate numbered.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_3 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 helper-lane lap 3: checked the four functions' boundary types (dice/lib/dice.spi and the call sites in dice_contract.spi 127-169): none crosses a crate boundary as written - rotate_numbers maps a stream (lazy, closure-backed) to a stream, create_sequential_roller returns a roller closure, roll_progressively takes that closure, and roll_within_bounds takes a list and returns an option (a union, private and per-crate numbered under today's Rust Export, see RUST-EXPORT-TYPES). So the cut must be at the contract's use level, not per function: dice_core exports two scalar/array functions - roll_from_hash (hash : vec u8) (max : u64) : u64, composing rotate_numbers 6, stream.to_list, create_sequential_roller and roll_progressively inside the crate (contract lines 120-149), and roll_within_bounds_of (max : u64) (rolls : vec u8) : u64 with 0 for none (valid results are 1..max). The contract crate keeps state, borsh, keccak512 and the 6 NEAR methods. Once RUST-EXPORT-TYPES lands, the option can cross as itself.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_4 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the dice_core export module as lab/NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES/dice_core.spi (not compiled yet: it must sit beside dice/contract to resolve ///- --package ../dice, and bounded jobs are frozen): roll_from_hash max hash composes rotate_numbers 6, the sequential roller and roll_progressively exactly as dice_contract.spi lines 120-149 do, and roll_within_bounds_of returns the option's value or 0; main exports both as dice_core_roll_from_hash and dice_core_roll_within_bounds_of with u64 * am'.vec u8 -> u64 signatures, the scalar/array shape today's Rust Export carries (the dice_contract Export lines 246-249 use the same vec argument type). Next: compile it in a dice copy, then the contract crate depending on it.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_5 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: blocked on the same thing as NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS: today's run shows dice_contract.spi no longer type-checks on the current tree (117:12, an array literal piped into am'.to_vec, array vs array_base since core v1). dice_core.spi cannot be compiled beside it until dice or lib am' is adapted (reported to main).")
inl x_federation_bridges () = Task ("X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", "Mastodon and Bluesky compatibility for the NEAR X (X-ON-NEAR): map posts, replies, edits, deletes, likes and follows to ActivityPub (actor, inbox/outbox, Create/Update/Delete/Like/Follow/Undo, WebFinger, HTTP signatures) and to AT Protocol (DID per account, app.bsky.feed.post/like and app.bsky.graph.follow records in a repo, firehose); the sim drives one scenario through all three and checks each network ends in the same state; xc reads all three behind its reads GADT", Story, P4, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "x,near,mastodon,bluesky,activitypub,atproto,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_federation_bridges () = Planning ("X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl x_client_thread_post () = Task ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", "xc post-thread FILE: post a .spi as a thread the way xc thread reads it back: split cells into posts of at most 280 X weight (twitter-text v3 weights, 23 per autolink, NFC, cuts at whitespace) with CELL k/n headers, each post replying to the previous; a Spiral port of the sim's Xsim.Frames split, cross-checked against it (two implementations, one oracle); stub-tested round trip post-thread -> thread byte for byte; --dry-run prints the posts and the cost ($0.015 per post created) without posting; live posting only with the user's go per thread. The dogfood loop of user 2026-10-16: post x's own source (later the compiler) so Grok and people can read and run it", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "x,x-api,threads,sim,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_client_thread_post_1 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:08Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:08 live contract (3 probes + 1 thread of 9 posts, all deleted within a minute, ~$0.21): (1) X trims trailing whitespace of a post (leading spaces after the header line survive), so frames never end on whitespace: the cut moves it to the start of the next frame, and a cell's own trailing whitespace is dropped; (2) v2 returns text HTML-escaped (& < > as &amp; &lt; &gt;), which also affected xc timeline: wire now unescapes; (3) X autolinks anything domain-like and keeps no trace of the original (listm.map and https://listm.map get identical entities, http:// comes back as https://), so links cannot be restored from entities: the writer puts U+200B before every dot between a label char and a letter (weight 1) and the reader removes it; (4) GET /2/tweets/:id without fields says edit_history_tweet_ids where search says edit_history_post_ids. Built: crate xc_frames (952 lines), xc post-thread [--dry-run] FILE, ReplyPost write, the stub models trimming, escaping and replies; the sim's Frames got the same rules and mix xsim.frames is the oracle (e2e: 9 posts identical, read back byte for byte)")
inl note_of_x_client_thread_post_2 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:51 live round trip exact: xc post-thread e2e/thread-sample.txt posted 9 posts as one reply chain, xc thread read it back byte for byte 15 s later (780 chars), all 9 deleted (~$0.19). Dogfood dry runs of x's own sources: prelude 14 posts, posts 106, wire 159, cli 117, frames 65, xc 86, max weight 278, up to 3.5 s per file; the whole client is about 900 posts (~$13.50)")
inl note_of_x_client_thread_post_3 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", 3u32, "2026-10-18T19:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:51 samples are real code now (user: a txt sample is drift): the e2e oracle is oauth1.spi (34 posts, 4 in multi-post cells, 56 of & < >, 16 defused dots; sim xsim.frames -> e2e/thread-oauth1.expected.json, guarded by apps/sim/build.ps1) and the README walks through checks/checks.spi (7 posts); e2e/thread-sample.txt removed. Live: oauth1.spi went through real X as 34 posts and came back byte for byte (5,005 chars) 15 s later, all deleted (~$0.68). Not covered by real code: weight-2 (CJK) text, still covered by the sim's own tests")
inl note_of_x_client_thread_post_4 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", 4u32, "2026-10-18T22:31Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:31 xc delete-thread ID: the thread crate returns the author's latest post versions of the conversation, newest first (replies before the root), and the write crate deletes them in one call (kind 9, a nonce per request, stops at the first refusal with its message); e2e deletes the 34 posts of oauth1.spi and the thread no longer reads; README walks it after the read-back")
inl note_of_x_client_thread_post_5 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T00:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:53 done: post-thread, --dry-run, delete-thread, README walkthrough (31 steps), e2e oracle on oauth1.spi, live round trips exact; splitter weights now a Rust sum over chars (dry runs ~28% faster, frames crate 898 -> 842 lines), oracle still matches post for post")
inl x_thread_gap_detection () = Task ("X-THREAD-GAP-DETECTION", "A cell lost with all of its posts is invisible to xc thread today (only cells missing some frames are named). Use the reply chain: every first frame replies to the last frame of its parent cell, so a post that replies to an id absent from the thread (other than the root's own parent) marks a gap; note it with both neighbours and exit 4. The sim gains a whole-cell loss (--lost-cells N) and a fixture, e2e byte-exact", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,threads,sim", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_thread_gap_detection_1 () = Note ("X-THREAD-GAP-DETECTION", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:15 built, verification pending: thread pages ask post.fields=...,referenced_posts (a field, not an expansion, so parents are not billed); framed and frame carry the replied_to parent; assemble names every cell after the first whose first frame replies to an id outside the thread's ids and edit histories ('cell X replies to ID, which is not in the thread: posts before it are missing', exit 4); sim: XWorld lost_cells drops every frame of N cells that others reply to, expected.gaps lists the cells that must be named, new fixture sim-gap (prelude.spi, 6 cells, 10 posts, gap before p2); sim tests 34/34")
inl note_of_x_thread_gap_detection_2 () = Note ("X-THREAD-GAP-DETECTION", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:51 verified: e2e sim-gap names the cell after the lost one (exit 4) next to sim-lost's missing frame; live, X returns referenced_posts on thread reads when asked as a post field (no expansion, nothing extra billed)")
inl eoie_granular_migration () = Task ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", "Migrate rust_global drift into granular typed Spiral slice by slice, x and tibia discipline", Epic, P1, D7, 2u32, Active, "", "eoie,spiral,rust-global,granular", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_1 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:24Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:24 eoie lane start (user: new eoie code follows the tibia/x granular discipline; migrate the rust_global drift using the type feature set). Baseline: 11166 rust_global blob lines in 108 files (13166 Spiral lines in those files). Slice 1: eoie_archive_tar_runtime/format.spi, archive_format union + classifier, exports eoie_archive_format_code/label, Rust archive_format is now glue (label Rc<str>), 3 Spiral test blocks; regen published, bundle_manifest consumer unchanged, archive_limits/portable_policy/windows tests pass. Slice 2: eoie_bundle_retention/series.spi, series_reading/series_rejection unions with thin primitives, exports eoie_retention_series_error/prefix/alpha, retention_series is glue; 2 test blocks (8 cases incl. u64 overflow text); e2e retention-plan on a scratch series: keep 3 delete 2, rejection text from Spiral, latest-candidate check intact. Both test files registered in eoie_dev_spiral_tests registry. Recipe in memory eoie-granular-spiral")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_2 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:38Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:38 slice 3: eoie_bundle_retention/receipt.spi, receipt_status union, the receipt Spiral module template as a block raw string (first real use of the block form in eoie source), export eoie_retention_receipt_source over string*string*u64*u64*u64*string, retention_receipt_source is glue; 2 test blocks incl. a byte-exact expected module; e2e: planned receipt byte-identical to the old format string rebuilt independently, retention-apply passes its readback check, deletes 2, keeps alpha3-5, writes the RetentionApplied receipt; fingerprint unchanged (db54787b1e12c832)")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_3 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:50Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:50 slice 4: tar_octal field text into format.spi (octal_field union EmptyField/OctalValue/InvalidOctal, trim + base-8 primitives, exports eoie_tar_octal_value/error); Rust keeps only the NUL cut and UTF-8 check; format.spi 4/4, consumer tests green, e2e proxy archive-extract of a real ustar tar.gz: format=tar.gz from the Spiral classifier, sizes through the Spiral octal reader (2 payloads, 14 bytes), contents intact. Whole registry run: 6 notebooks, 19 Spiral tests, all pass. Blob bytes: tar runtime -297 then about -150 more, retention -939. Next candidates by the same recipe: tar_pax_path record validation (typed rejection union), retention_read_receipt field parsing, coverage_union merge, patch_quoted_fields")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_4 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-10-18T20:28Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:28 slice 5: eoie_coverage_prune/lcov.spi, LCOV parsing as typed Spiral: lcov_line, lcov_state, lcov_rejection (10 messages kept verbatim) and hit_reading unions, a tail-recursive state machine producing normalized rows (bare source rows keep SF blocks without DA lines); exports eoie_lcov_error/rows; coverage_union_records is now glue (validation call plus a trivial row reader into the BTreeMap merge). lcov.spi 2/2 (12 cases), the existing coverage_union_merges_max_hits_and_writes_receipt and the crate's other Rust tests pass. Not migrated: tar_pax_path (payload is bytes and !!!!Export has no byte-slice parameter; a glue-side UTF-8 check would change which non-UTF8 message is reported)")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_5 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T06:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:40 x lane (git-2c), eoie targets from the five mega samples, for the eoie lane to schedule (cited paths are agent reads, unverified by builds): (1) agile_mutation_policy/model.spi:40-80: agile_status_code plus agile_status_code_valid and the agile_*_decision i32 functions are hand-synced; lean_cic's raw -> verified gate (L:2462-2475) and typed decisions (L:1265-1271) give an Accepted case that carries the agile_status, behind union private. (2) eoie_jobs/model.spi:16-23: replace the JobUnknown catch-all with Known job_command | Unknown string; :43-49 state_exit invents exit 3 for Pending/Running, split job_state into settled and live so the index narrows the match (spiral_proves S:8227-8232). (3) bundle_output_contract/model.spi:25-26: output_pending and output_root_ready are bare nominals; omniledger's chain of custody (3103-3108) makes each stage wrap its predecessor. (4) agile_todo_model/model.spi:187-197 CFamily => 1i32 tables: one prototype instance per union or reflection, never a hand table (EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION). (5) move *_negative_probe folders into in-file fails tests. Features the samples use that eoie lacks: assert_static, and inl")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_6 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T07:36Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:36 eoie lane on x lane idea (2), measured in a temp copy: job_verb = Known job_command | Unknown string plus job_state = Live job_live | Settled job_settled (settled_exit total over settled states, wait_exit names the 3-while-running convention, output unchanged) compiles and costs +35 lines (eoie_jobs 938 -> 973). Deferred: it is modeling, not missing function, and it would leave 27 lines of headroom in a crate other lanes rely on. Apply it together with a split of eoie_jobs (CLI routing vs registry model). The edit plan is ready as a python JSON-style replacement list (scratchpad jobs-model-edit.py; recreate it if lost).")
inl fsharp_warm_runner () = Task ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", "F# native tier through one warm process (user 2026-10-18: speed up where F# is redundant). The tier runs each main.fsx in a cold dotnet fsi: in today's accept, 544 rows took 3,151 s summed (median 4.2 s, p90 10.2 s, about 26 min wall on 2 lanes), almost all of it startup. Like kotlin-server: a native-shims fsharp-runner keeps one FSI evaluation session warm, evaluates each script and returns its value and stdout. Target: the same outcomes as the cold tier, row for row, in under 5 min", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,fsharp,speed", "current", "current")
inl note_of_fsharp_warm_runner_1 () = Note ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:25 main-thread: prototype in lab/FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER. warm.ps1 runs one dotnet fsi --quiet session through stdin and does a #load per row. Each row's copy is main.fsx plus the cold tier's final pipe, which prints an SPIRAL_WARM_END <code> marker instead of exit; a separate printfn SPIRAL_WARM_DONE submission ends the row. A throw prints no END, so the row exits 1, as cold. A dead process (a real exit, a stack overflow) records its exit code, and a timeout kills and restarts the session. stdout uses the cold tier's hashing (CRLF to LF, TrimEnd, sha16). compare.ps1 replays the accepted F# rows of single-flight-20261018-123705. First 40 rows: warm 31 s in one session vs cold 182 s summed (5.9x), 0 differing (native status, exit, stdout hash). Next: all 544 rows after today's accept (no heavy runs during the 15:30-17:00 tibia live block), then fold it into test.ps1's native tier as an F# batch like kotlin-server, behind the native signature.")
inl note_of_fsharp_warm_runner_2 () = Note ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:29 main-thread: integration ready in lab/FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER/apply.py; dry run on a copy of test.ps1 applies and parses. Final runner lab/FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER/fsharp-warm/warm.ps1, which becomes tests/native-shims/fsharp-warm/: stderr is drained with async reads after the DONE marker, and error FS rows are build-fail like cold (checked: FS0027 rows build-fail, ok rows ran with exit; 5 rows in 8 s, first-row session start 5.8 s, then 0.2-0.7 s each). test.ps1 patch: a warm batch before lane construction, 2 sessions in parallel, results into nativeResults (a timeout maps to ran/timeout as in cold), and F# jobs join the memo-store loop. Get-ShimKey Fsharp = fsharp-warm, so a runner edit re-keys only F# rows. SPIRAL_FSHARP_COLD=1 keeps the cold path. It also fixes a glued '}$shimsByBackend' line from my 10:03 shim-key patch. Apply after the 17:00 accept; the first review after that reruns every native row once (the native-tier block is in the signature).")
inl note_of_fsharp_warm_runner_3 () = Note ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", 3u32, "2026-10-19T03:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 23:44 main-thread: applied after the pack 3 accept (lab apply.py, without the SPIRAL_FSHARP_COLD opt-out, since the user wants no skip switches). In the pack 4 review the warm tier ran 549 F# rows in 2 sessions in 320 s, against the cold tier's ~37 min, but 7 mega rows (6 mega_brzozowski_derivatives roots plus omniledger all_terminals) hit the 60 s per-row budget inside the session, where they used to finish cold (exit 0 / 1). Fixed in test.ps1: a warm timeout is not recorded; the row stays in nativeJobs and reruns cold in a fresh fsi through the normal lanes, and it leaves fsharpWarmJobs so the memo loop counts it once. The pack 4 accept (running) is the proof: the 7 rows should match their accepted exits")
inl note_of_fsharp_warm_runner_4 () = Note ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", 4u32, "2026-10-19T04:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 00:29 main-thread: pack 4 ACCEPTED (run single-flight-20261018-234436, 7,007 rows, DISAGREE 0). Against its review, the only changes are the 7 mega F# rows: warm timeouts there, cold reruns now matching their accepted exits. The warm runner plus cold fallback is in the baseline. The pack also carried the 7 Zig rows (ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY), the Infer shadowing fix, Zig main returning any value, the tokens verb and the clearer package and module errors")
inl zig_primary_oracle () = Task ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", "Make Zig the first oracle (-Oracle Zig,C) (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig). Measured 2026-10-17: -Oracle Zig and -Oracle C both give DISAGREE 0 on 5,388 rows; the only difference, dynamic_array_bounds_negative, has Known entries per oracle. Today's accept: C and Zig differ on 1 of 459 shared samples. The switch changes the baseline's oracle column, so it lands with its own reviewed accept", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,zig,oracle", "current", "current")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_1 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T13:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:15 lap 2 grain: predicted the queued -Oracle Zig,C replay from today's results (single-flight-20261019-072348, no run): Zig has 467 rows, 465 agree with C, 1 known, 1 no-c; 65 C samples have no Zig row and fall back to C (detail oracle C (no Zig row)), so they cannot disagree; because Zig and C agree on all shared samples, swapping the first oracle only changes rows whose verdict hinges on a C-scoped Known. Exactly one such row would turn DISAGREE: samples/dynamic_array_bounds_negative on Rust (Rust panics exit 1, Zig traps exit 3); its only Known entry was scoped Oracle = C, while C/Delphi/Wasm/Kotlin/Lua already had Zig-scoped entries and Lean/Bend exit 3 agree with Zig. Added the missing Zig-scoped Rust entry to apps/compiler/tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 (Import-PowerShellDataFile -SkipLimitCheck loads it: 17 Known entries, 11 for this sample). Also zig_lib_console (Zig-only sample) will newly score its Fsharp/Rust/Wasm rows against Zig (all exit 0 today). Expected replay result: DISAGREE 0, 1 more known. Next: when main runs the HEAVY QUEUE replay, compare its verdict counts with this prediction; if DISAGREE 0, make Zig,C the test.ps1 default (main's file)")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_2 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:51 measured with -Oracle Zig,C on today's tree (review-keys): round A DISAGREE 1 = dynamic_array_bounds_negative Rust (helper 2 added its Zig-scoped Known entry after round A loaded), round B DISAGREE 1 = samples/zig_lib_console Fsharp, a real lib bug the C-first oracle never compared (the sample has no C row): sm'.obj_to_string on F# printed True/False via .ToString() where Rust, Zig and Wasm print true/false (Python's str() has the same flaw). Fixed in lib sm'.spi: obj_to_string_fsharp and obj_to_string_python print lowercase bools, test obj_to_string_prints_bools_lowercase (fsharp, cuda, rust, zig). With both, Zig-first should reach DISAGREE 0; next: gate sm' then a review with -Oracle Zig,C to confirm, then switch the default order.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_3 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:51 lap 3 grain, a cross-platform argument measured from the first Linux CI run (39258592236, see CI-OFFLOAD note 5): C's abort() exits 134 (SIGABRT) on Linux but 3 on Windows, so under the C-first oracle samples/managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice scores agree-fail on F#, Rust, Zig, Python and TypeScript (all exit 3) and the C row itself is the odd one; with Zig first, those 5 rows agree with the Zig oracle on both platforms and only C's own row (134) is off, i.e. the platform dependence lands on the C row where it belongs. Same for exit widths: zig_exit_wide exits 49 (305 mod 256) on Linux C/Rust/Zig/Gleam/Python while F# reports 305, which is DISAGREE under either oracle (an F# tier matter, not an oracle one). So Zig-first also makes Linux CI verdicts closer to Windows ones. Still waiting on the queued Windows replay (prediction from lap 2: DISAGREE 0 with the added Rust Known). Next: when the replay lands, propose the default order Zig,C to main with these two data points")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_4 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:01 the obj_to_string bool fix is green: Cells gate of tree lib/spiral/sm'.spi 42/42, 0 type errors (heavy-queue step sm-tree-gate, 09:51-10:00), including obj_to_string_prints_bools_lowercase on fsharp, cuda, rust, zig.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_5 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:15 lap 5 grain: the evidence is complete: main's 09:50 replay with -Oracle Zig,C had DISAGREE 1 = zig_lib_console F# bool text, and that lib fix is green (sm'.spi gate 42/42, 10:01), so Zig-first is DISAGREE 0 on today's tree; plus the Linux data point (lap 3). Staged the flip as lab/ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE/flip-default.py (test.ps1:19 default @('C','Zig') -> @('Zig','C') and the AGENTS.md default sentence; dry run: both anchors match once). Queued for main to apply between runs after the core switch accept, so the switch's own review keeps today's oracle and the flip gets its own reviewed accept. Decision recorded for main: apply after the switch accept, not with it, so the two oracle-column changes stay separable.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_6 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:32 lap 6: read test.ps1's oracle passes (999-1033) to check that the flip keeps the Known scoping working. The first oracle's scoped Known entries (Get-ScopedKnown on line 1007) mask DISAGREE and build-fail rows. Each further oracle gets its own scoped set (lines 1022-1030), and it compares only rows that already agree with the first oracle. With Zig,C, the Zig-scoped dynamic_array_bounds_negative entries for the unchecked backends become the primary mask, and the C-scoped entries for the checked backends cover the second pass, which is the mirror of today's C,Zig. So no matrix.psd1 edit is needed with the flip, and flip-default.py stays a two-line change.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_7 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: evidence complete (replay DISAGREE 0 after the lib fix, Linux data point, Known scoping verified) and the flip is staged as flip-default.py; left: main applies it after the switch accept and does the reviewed accept. Progress 80.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_8 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:52 lap 7, two facts for the flip. (1) CI picks it up automatically: workflow.ps1 runs test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Suite <suite> -Native -Parallel 2 -Accept with no -Oracle, so after flip-default.py the Linux job's accepted oracle column is Zig-first too. (2) This narrows lap 3's Linux argument: Test-SharedFailure (test.ps1) scores two failure exits as agree-fail, so C's abort 134 against the others' 3 is agree-fail under either order, never DISAGREE. The flip changes only which rows carry agree-fail (5 rows with C first, C's own row with Zig first), which is cleaner but does not change the DISAGREE count. The case for the flip is still the user's direction plus DISAGREE 0 locally.")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_9 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T16:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:27 lap 8 grain: flip-default is applied in the tree (test.ps1 Oracle Zig,C). Review prediction from the accepted switch baseline single-flight-20261019-113145 (7,020 rows): 466 samples have both a C and a Zig native run, and exactly 1 differs, dynamic_array_bounds_negative (C exit 0, Zig 3), which has Known entries per oracle; 65 samples run C but not Zig, so C stays their oracle through the fallback; one sample runs Zig without C, zig_lib_console, whose 4 rows are no-c today and gain a Zig oracle after the flip. Corrected 13:0x: this holds for rows of backends other than C and Zig; the flip also fills the oracle column of C's own rows, empties Zig's, and renames no-c to no-zig, which check-flip.ps1 normalizes. Done when that accept shows DISAGREE 0 and this split")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_10 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:54 lap 9 grain: the Done check is one call now: lab/ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE/check-flip.ps1 -Run <run dir> compares every row's oracle column with the switch baseline single-flight-20261019-113145, marks a change as expected when it is on dynamic_array_bounds_negative or zig_lib_console (lap 8 prediction) or when that row's own residual, exit or stdout moved (pack 7's changes), requires DISAGREE 0, and prints ZIG-FLIP-OK or exits 1 with the unexplained rows listed. First version compared every row and would have flagged ~1,000 C/Zig/no-c cells on a correct flip; fixed 13:0x: C and Zig rows are left out of the diff, no-c/no-zig count as one verdict, and DISAGREE is checked on every row including C. Positive test on the Zig,C run single-flight-20261019-124451: 10 verdict changes, all on dynamic_array_bounds_negative (7 Known re-scopings) and zig_lib_console (3 no-c to agree), C rows agree 465 known 3 no-zig 32, DISAGREE 0, ZIG-FLIP-OK. A row whose oracle row moved while its own output did not shows as unexplained, which is what the review should look at by hand")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_11 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain: the three non-run parts of Done are in place: test.ps1:19 defaults -Oracle to @('Zig', 'C'), apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md names the 2026-10-19 flip to Zig,C, and check-flip.ps1 passed on the reviewed run 124451 (10 predicted changes, DISAGREE 0, ZIG-FLIP-OK). Left: accept single-flight-20261019-125623 (compile done 13:01, native tier running, baseline not yet rewritten) -> check-flip.ps1 -Run 125623 -> Done (main)")
inl note_of_zig_primary_oracle_12 () = Note ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged); test.ps1 defaults to -Oracle Zig,C and check-flip.ps1 on the accept prints ZIG-FLIP-OK: 10 non-oracle verdict changes all on the predicted samples, C rows against Zig agree 465 known 3 no-zig 32, DISAGREE 0.")
inl contracts_on_zig () = Task ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", "Measure moving the contracts suite's vehicle backend from F# to Zig (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig, F# redundant). 619 of the 626 samples with no C or Zig row are contracts, which test type-level behavior (expected errors, typestate) and use F# only as the codegen vehicle. Experiment: compile every contract to Zig and compare the compile verdicts and expected errors with today's F# rows; switch the suite or add Zig rows by the result", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "matrix,zig,contracts,experiment", "current", "current")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_1 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:32 main-thread: experiment script lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 compiles each contract to Zig with the installed compiler, builds and runs the ok ones with zig ReleaseFast, and compares the compile verdict and exit with the accepted F# rows of 123705. Contract rows: 627 (413 compile errors, the negative contracts; 214 ok, which ran). Smoke on the first 6: 2 same, 4 where F# compiles but Zig refuses, all with the same cause: 'The return type of main in the Zig backend should be a 32-bit int (or unit)'. Contracts return a value (string, record) that F#'s tier treats as exit 0. Fixed in the tree, unbuilt: codegenZig's main accepts a single value of any type and discards it in spiralMain (return 0), matching F#; i32 and unit unchanged; tuples still rejected, with a clearer message. Next: build that, then run the full 627 to find the real feature gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_2 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:31 helper-lane lap 1: unblocked the full run: the codegenZig main change (a single value of any type is discarded, return 0; tuples rejected with a clearer message) is committed in e0ffc80b (10-18 14:50, the old message string is gone from spiral_compiler.fs) and in the installed Release compiler (built 10-18 22:57), so the smoke's 4 F#-ok/Zig-error rows should now compile. The baseline run single-flight-20261018-123705 still has its results.tsv. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 inside Invoke-Heavy (627 contract compiles to Zig + 214 zig builds, ~15 min), writes contracts-on-zig.tsv and the grouped zig gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_3 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:38 main heavy queue ran lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 (10:26-10:37, 669 s): all 627 contracts give the same compile verdict on Zig as on the accepted F# rows (0 F#-ok/Zig-error, 0 F#-error/Zig-ok); of the 214 that compile and run, exit codes match on 211 and differ on 3; table in lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/contracts-on-zig.tsv. So Zig can carry the contracts suite once the 3 exit differences are explained.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_4 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:46 helper-lane lap 3: explained main's 3 exit differences from contracts-on-zig.tsv against the 08:05 baseline: (1) contract_effectful_forall_bottom and (2) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals are F#-text samples ($ macro bodies in F# syntax, failwith and 1L literals), so Zig build-fails them by construction (all_terminals is also a Known Fsharp row); (3) mega_brzozowski_derivatives/proof_suite is an F# timeout in the measure run only: the baseline's F# row exits 0, the same as Zig. So all 627 agree once those two are kept on F#. Decision (autonomous, per the user's 10-18 promote-Zig, F#-redundant direction): SWITCH, not added rows; this also drops about 214 dotnet fsi native runs (median 7.2 s) for zig build-exe (about 2 s). Staged lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py (anchors --check OK on today's files, LF): test.ps1 gains Get-ContractBackends (Zig unless listed), used by the contract_ and mega_ branches of the contracts suite and by smoke's contract samples; matrix.psd1 gains FsharpContracts with the two F#-text samples. Caveats for the review: ExpectedErrors pins are type-check texts, but the tsv stores only a truncated first error line, so the review run is what proves the 236 pins on Zig; and the removed Fsharp contract rows need the staged accept-prune (SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS) or they stay in EXPECTED.tsv.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_5 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 5u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py dry run still matches today's apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 and tests/matrix.psd1 (anchors match). It is ready for a main pack slot, but test.ps1 is busy with main's probe-queue for about an hour, so it waits.")
inl zig_coverage_c_only () = Task ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", "Give the 38 C-only samples Zig rows where the feature exists (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig). The split from the 123705 accept: ABI shims 10, dynamic-array helpers 10, Rust-only 7, eBPF 6, native 2, Lua/Python/WGSL 1 each. ABI and dynamic-array samples need Zig counterparts of their C shims; Rust-only and eBPF stay single-backend by design. Afterwards C remains as a cross-check and product backend", TaskKind, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "matrix,zig,coverage", "current", "current")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_1 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:35 main-thread probe with the installed compiler. The 38 C-only samples: abi_external_* (10) and dynamic_array_* (10) carry C shims through CFlags; rust_* (7) and python_macro_annotations are single-backend by design; native_managed_array_scc*_tail_recursion (2) carry C shims. 8 others fail to compile for Zig with 'Cannot find the backend Zig in the backend switch op', because their !!!!BackendSwitch has no Zig arm: lua_paren_statement (C (void)0 / Lua arms; a Zig arm of {} is an empty statement), the six ebpf_* samples (C and Ebpf arms plus a pure-Spiral Bend arm such as expect / 0i32, which Zig can share), and wgsl_parallel_lanes (to check). Plan after today's accept (samples stay frozen during review and accept): add those Zig arms, register the samples in the Zig list, and probe for agreement with C. The shim samples need Zig shims (zig build-exe main.zig plus the C shim, or Zig ports): a separate step.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_2 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:12 main-thread: staged lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/apply.ps1 (not applied; samples stay frozen until the pack 3 accept). It adds 13 Zig arms in 7 samples, checking that every BackendSwitch gets exactly one: the Bend-arm ebpf samples reuse the Bend value (expect or 0i32), ebpf_map_full maps both map helpers to expect, the assignment helpers in ebpf_map_full and ebpf_nested use the Zig spelling from while_loop, and lua_paren_statement gets unit. It also registers the 7 in the Zig list. The dry run passes. wgsl_parallel_lanes stays out: it is a Wgsl sample whose arms cover C, Fsharp, Rust and Delphi only")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_3 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 3u32, "2026-10-19T03:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 23:44 main-thread: applied after the pack 3 accept. Pack 4 review (single-flight-20261018-225858) shows the 7 new Zig rows (ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit, lua_paren_statement), all agreeing with C (DISAGREE 0). Pack 4 accept running")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_4 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:33 helper-lane lap 1: verified the 10-18 23:44 step landed: the accepted oracle (EXPECTED.tsv, 10-19 00:28) has Zig rows ran for ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit (exit 0) and lua_paren_statement (exit 7), committed in 668568ac. Recount from that oracle: 35 samples have a C ran row and no Zig row. Of them, 3 are ghosts (native_closure_managed_branch, native_fptr_basic, native_fptr_reuse: oracle rows exit 42, but their sample folders no longer exist (sources deleted on 09-29 in 7a5e4ed4)) and 1 is new and Zig-ready: qualified_union_case_no_payload compiles to Zig with the installed compiler and exits 43 = its C row (probe via Invoke-Heavy). Not edited: matrix.psd1's Zig list (line ~3006) is the main lane's during reviews; adding qualified_union_case_no_payload there is a one-line change for the next pack. Real remainder: 10 abi_external_* and 10 dynamic_array_* C-shim samples, 2 native_managed_array_scc*, plus single-backend rust_* 7, python 1, wgsl 1.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_5 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:18 helper-lane lap 2: designed the ABI half from the samples themselves: every abi_external_* sample reaches C only through one macro call of a named shim function with plain scalar arguments ($ spiral_abi_libc_abs(x), memset, memcmp x2, llabs, strlen, qsort3_pack(3,1,2), cabs_pack(3,4), div_pack, lldiv_pack; the *_negative/_mismatch/_unknown ones are expected compile errors), and the C rows bind those names with -D or -include of tests/native-shims/abi-external-*-c-shim.h. So no per-sample Zig port of the Spiral code is needed: a Zig row only needs the same names defined in Zig. Mechanism: a ZigShims table in tests/matrix.psd1 (Pattern -> tests/native-shims/abi-external-*.zig) whose file the native tier appends to main.zig before zig build-exe, mirroring CFlags; the shims can call libc through extern fn with zig build-exe -lc, or be pure Zig (abs, llabs, div are one-liners). This is a test.ps1/matrix change (main lane's files), so it is recorded as the plan, not applied.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_6 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:48 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the scalar half of the Zig ABI shims as pure Zig (no libc) in lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims/, one file per C shim the CFlags table includes: abi-external (abs, wrapping like C on INT_MIN), abi-external-i64 (llabs), callback (qsort3_pack as a 3-element sorting network, 123 for 3,1,2), struct-argument (cabs_pack via @sqrt, @trap where C aborts), struct-return (div_pack via @divTrunc/@rem, C's toward-zero div_t) and struct-return-i64 (lldiv_pack). Probe lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims_probe.zig (all six plus negative div) built with zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast as one bounded job: exit 0, every value equal to the C shim's. Not written: the buffer shims (memset, memcmp, strlen) read ->len/->ptr of C's array and string structs, so their Zig twins must follow the Zig backend's slice layout; next grain. Wiring stays a main-lane change (a ZigShims table beside CFlags that appends the matching file to main.zig).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_7 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:35 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the three buffer shims after reading the Zig backend's representation (codegenZig: strings are []const u8, arrays []T from spiralNewArray, length via .len, so a slice already carries the ptr/len pair the C shims read through ->ptr/->len): mutable-buffer memset(array: []u8, byte, count) checks 0 <= count <= len (exit 3 where C aborts), @memset on array[0..count] and returns count; const-buffer memcmp(left, right, count) with the same bound checks returns the sign of std.mem.order (-1/0/1; the samples only test == 0 and the prefix case); string strlen stops at the first NUL like C, else the slice length. shims_probe.zig now also checks memset (writes 3 bytes, leaves the 4th), memcmp (equal 3-byte prefix, ABCD < ABCE) and strlen spiral = 6; rerun after main's freeze all-clear. All 9 Zig shim files exist now; what remains is the test.ps1 ZigShims hook (main's file).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_8 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:54 helper-lane lap 5: staged the harness half as lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/zig_shims_hook.py (anchors --check OK on today's test.ps1 and matrix.psd1; matrix inputs are main's, so not applied): matrix.psd1 gains a ZigShims table (sample name -> shim file, exact-name -like patterns as CFlags uses) and the 9 positive abi_external_* samples on the Zig list; test.ps1 loads it beside cFlagRules (322), passes it into the native runspaces (930), and the Zig clause (712) builds main_shimmed.zig = main.zig + the matching shim when a rule matches, otherwise main.zig as today; the script also copies the 9 shim files into tests/native-shims (already part of the native memo key). HEAVY QUEUE row: apply between runs, then a C,Zig examples review should show 9 new Zig rows agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_9 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: reran shims_probe.zig with the three buffer shims added (zig build-exe ReleaseFast, bounded): exit 0, so memset, memcmp and strlen behave like the C shims on the probe values, together with the six scalar shims.")
inl eoie_core_operators () = Task ("EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS", "eoie packages load Spiral core and use operators instead of the !!!! op spam", Story, P1, D4, 95u32, Active, "", "eoie,spiral,core,granular", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_core_operators_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:26Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:26 eoie lane, user direction (heal the no-core quirks mechanically): compiler-contracts/workspace.ps1 Resolve-EoieSpiralBundle now exports SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR from spiral deps/The-Spiral-Language when unset (regeneration got Package not loaded for core before); |core- added to all 321 eoie package.spiproj (lab/EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS/core_package.py); 1764 arithmetic and comparison !!!! calls (LT 1150, EQ 502, NEQ 38, Add 26, Mult 22, LTE 15, Sub 12, Div 5, Neg 5, Mod 4, GT 3, GTE 1) rewritten as operators with precedence-aware parens (lab/EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS/operators.py: || 20, && 30, comparisons 40, + - 60, * / % 70, binding = detected). Experiment first: tar runtime with core regenerates byte-identical. Found: core < needs a number, so char comparisons (state_receipt_codec_model/sm.spi) must keep the builtin; restore_lines.py puts back failing lines from the backup by line number (conversion never changes line counts). Proof pending: compile-only sweep of all 150 owner entries, then full regeneration expecting 0 changed owners. Deferred: the no-core helper layer (i32_eq 141, i32_le 84, i32_next 87, i32_negative 64, u64_* etc., macro bodies) since macro-to-operator changes generated text and loses the byte-identity proof")
inl note_of_eoie_core_operators_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:20Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:20 proof: compile-only fix loop (lab/EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS/fixloop.ps1) over all 153 owner entries: 144 generate byte-identical Rust to the committed .rs, the other 9 are this session's intended feature changes. Kept as builtins: char comparisons (core < needs number: state_receipt_codec_model/sm.spi, predicate_lex_core, predicate_lex, 6 lines), the one real block (union_reflection/cases.spi: core operators are foralls in real code), and the 38 NEQ calls (SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ). Net: 1764 - 6 - 1 - 38 = about 1719 operator calls moved to core")
inl note_of_eoie_core_operators_3 () = Note ("EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T20:28Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:28 helper layer done: lab/EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS/helpers.py rewrote 399 calls (i32_eq, i32_le, i32_next, i32_prev, i32_negative, i32_sum, u64_add/sub/mul/div/rem/lt) as operators with an application-aware argument parser (atoms, parenthesized args unwrapped when safe); fix loop: 153/153 compile, 0 restores; 26 owners change text (macro v + 1 becomes core wrapping_add, Spiral's defined integer semantics; 0 becomes 0i32) and pass cargo check; the agile e2e (init, add, note, note-edit, show, list --json) and the registry Spiral tests pass on the rebuilt CLI. Remaining builtins by design: chars, the real block, 38 NEQ until SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ (main lane pack 4)")
inl dir_tree_readme () = Task ("DIR-TREE-README", "dir-tree-html renders the README.md at the source dir's root below the tree (user 2026-10-18): a Markdown renderer in the Spiral source (headings, paragraphs, lists, code fences, links, inline code), the same dark style, and the viewport/narrow-screen CSS from today; no README means no section; self-test covers both", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,markdown", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dir_tree_readme_1 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-README", 1u32, "2026-10-18T19:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:21 main-thread: implemented in dir_tree_html.spi (committed by the user in eaba51c): host_read_text/host_is_file, a markdown subset renderer (headings, paragraphs, lists, code fences, inline code, links, escaping) into a section.readme below the tree with its own CSS, and a readme_fixture self-test. The first build passed RUST-OK, but a real render showed my own slip in eaba51c: doc_close ended with literal backtick-n text (PowerShell escape written into a Spiral string), so every page built from main ends with stray characters after </body>. Fixed in the tree (backslash-n), plus a close_ok self-test (exit 5) that pins the page ending, which the old page check could not catch because it compared doc_close with itself. Rebuild queued in the heavy gate; needs a commit to fix the live pages")
inl note_of_dir_tree_readme_2 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-README", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:34 main-thread: rebuilt with the closer fix: RUST-OK, self-test passes including close_ok. A real render of a scratch site with a README (heading, inline code, link, list) ends section.readme then </body> and </html> with no stray text. Waiting on the user's commit of dir_tree_html.spi + .rs so the gh-pages deploys pick it up")
inl note_of_dir_tree_readme_3 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-README", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: alphabet CI exit 3 = the Rust string slice helper refusing a cut inside a UTF-8 char: escape_from and inline_from copied one byte at a time. Now they copy runs and cut only at ASCII delimiters (always char boundaries); self-test gains a Korean, accent and & paragraph with a link; build + self-test RUST-OK, and alphabet's real README renders exit 0 Next: images ![alt](src) and badge links [![alt](img)](url) render mangled: add image support")
inl note_of_dir_tree_readme_4 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-README", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:20 helper-lane lap 2: took main's next grain (image support) and wrote it into dir-tree-html/dir_tree_html.spi (uncommitted, on top of main's run-copy fix): image_close/render_image render ![alt](src) as <img src alt> (both escaped), badge_close renders [![alt](img)](url) as <a href=url><img ...></a> (the old [ handler matched the first ] inside the badge's alt and mangled it), next_inline_marker_from now also stops at ! so plain runs end before an image, and a ! that starts no image (Hi!) stays text. Only =. comparisons are used, as the rest of this core-free file does. Applied through lab/DIR-TREE-README/images.py (anchor-checked; it re-indents the old [ branch under the new badge test). The self-test readme fixture gains Hi! ![logo](logo.png) [![CI](b.svg)](ci.html) with the expected <p> line. HEAVY QUEUE: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html\\build.ps1 (~5 min, RUST-OK = self-test passes).")
inl note_of_dir_tree_readme_5 () = Note ("DIR-TREE-README", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:48 helper-lane lap 3: DONE with evidence (lap-3 pick; uncommitted in dir-tree-html, on top of main's run-copy fix): build.ps1 ran twice under a bounded eoie heavy job (20 s each) with RUST-OK, i.e. the self-test passes for the tree page, the no-README page and the README fixture, which now also covers images, a badge link, bold, an autolink, backslash escapes, a --- rule and a nested bullet. Added in this lap through lab/DIR-TREE-README/markdown_more.py: **bold** -> strong (nested inline rendering), <https://...> autolinks, backslash escapes of markdown punctuation, --- as hr, and bullets at any indentation (alphabet's Installation/Usage sub-items were merged into a paragraph before). Rendering alphabet's real README with the new binary: the Pages link, the Codespaces badge (img inside a), the rule, the bold notice, a flat list of 8 TOC items and bold feature labels all come out right. The title's list (headings, paragraphs, lists, code fences, links, inline code, no-README case, self-test) is met; one known gap left for a follow-up: headings carry no id, so the README's #features style TOC links do not jump yet. Deploy waits for the user's commit of dir_tree_html.spi/.rs.")
inl notebook_html_renderer () = Task ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", "Replace the Python nbconvert step (jupyter, used by Cells' --html to turn .ipynb into .html) with our own renderer that colors Spiral blocks properly (user 2026-10-18), as Zed tried by copying the VS Code extension's grammar: reuse the TextMate grammar or the TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL grammar for the highlighting, render markdown cells and outputs, keep the page mobile-friendly; drops the jupyter dependency from every repo's CI", Feature, P2, D5, 85u32, Active, "", "cells,html,highlighting,python-free", "current", "current")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_1 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:42 main-thread design. The VS Code extension has no TextMate grammar: its colors are the language server's semantic tokens. The compiler's tokenizer tags every token with a SemanticTokenLegend class (variable, symbol, string, number, operator, unary_operator, comment, keyword, parenthesis, type_variable, escaped_char, unescaped_char, number_suffix, escaped_var; spiral_compiler.fs ~42869, semantic_tokens ~49363), and the format verb already reads those tokens without partial evaluation. Plan: (1) a host verb 'SpiralCompiler tokens <file|--stdin --path p>' printing one row per token (line, column, length, class), tokenizer only and cheap, next to 'format' in Program.fs; (2) a Cells HTML writer (Spiral.Cells.Html) that renders the report directly: markdown cells (the CommonMark subset from DIR-TREE-README), code cells colored by a CSS class per legend entry with a dark palette, outputs and test verdicts, plus the mobile viewport CSS. It replaces 'jupyter nbconvert' in Notebook.write_html, removes the jupyter/Python dependency from every repo's CI, and colors exactly as the editor does. The tokens verb is a compiler change: build and install it after today's 17:00 accept.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_2 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:45 main-thread: step (1) written, unbuilt. TokensCli in the shared Formatter section folds tokenize_line over a file's lines (lexical state carried across lines) and prints one row per token: line, from, nearTo, then the token_groups class, the same mapping vscode_tokens uses for the editor. Host verb: SpiralCompiler tokens <file> | tokens --stdin (Program.fs, next to format). Builds and installs after today's 17:00 accept; then step (2), the Cells HTML writer.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_3 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 3u32, "2026-10-18T18:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:52 main-thread: step (2) done, uncommitted. Spiral.Cells.Html renders a page from the .ipynb JSON: markdown cells (CommonMark subset), code cells colored by token class (one CSS class per SemanticTokenLegend name, dark palette), outputs, and a test badge per test cell, with the viewport and narrow-screen CSS. Notebook.write_html calls it instead of jupyter nbconvert; normalize_html and its test (nbconvert cell-id renumbering) are removed. The tokenizer calls SpiralCompiler tokens <tmp file> and degrades to plain text when the installed compiler lacks the verb (today's install). Tests: html_test.exs 4/4, notebook_test.exs passes (18 in all). End to end: mix spiral.notebook on lib/spiral/testing.spi --output-dir --livemd writes testing.spi.html (13 KB, viewport, 6 test badges, 25 code blocks), .ipynb and .livemd, no Python involved. Colors appear once the tokens verb is installed after the 17:00 accept. This also fixes the tibia lane's failure today: scoop python's pyzmq raises 'cannot import name _device', and nbconvert exited 1 on every run.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_4 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 4u32, "2026-10-18T19:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:26 main-thread: the last jupyter path is gone. core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook (dice/alphabet/i574n.github PowerShell .livemd notebooks) now calls the new mix spiral.html <ipynb>... task from apps/cells, with scoop OTP/Elixir prepended like run_notebook.ps1, instead of jupyter nbconvert. Html.write colors only notebooks whose kernelspec language is spiral (or missing), so PowerShell cells stay plain. spiral/requirements.txt drops nbconvert, ipython_genutils and mistune (mpmath and cupy stay for the Python backend). init.ps1's no-pip message and the 3 gh-pages.yml comments no longer mention jupyter. polyglot's own requirements.txt is untouched (fc1943s backup repo). html_test.exs 6/6, including a pwsh notebook through the task")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_5 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:36 main-thread: review of the committed, unbuilt TokensCli against the renderer: tokenize_line ranges are .NET string offsets (UTF-16 units), while Html.highlight_line sliced bytes, so any non-ASCII text before a token (an accented string, an emoji) would shift every later span, or cut inside a character. Fixed before the first colored build: the line is converted to UTF-16, sliced by unit and converted back, and a slice that splits a surrogate pair gives an empty string. New test with é and an emoji in strings: html_test.exs 7/7")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_6 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: CI regression from my nbconvert replacement: core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook ran mix spiral.html before mix deps.get on fresh runners (Unchecked dependencies: kino) in dice, polyglot and alphabet; it now runs mix local.hex, local.rebar and deps.get when apps/cells/deps/kino is missing, like run_notebook.ps1 Next: confirm the html appears in the next CI run")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_7 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: CI confirmation still pending: dice, polyglot and alphabet have no run since main's 08:16 core.ps1 fix (latest runs 06:48, all red; spiral's in-progress run is a rerun of 668568ac, which predates the fix), so the check waits for the user's push. Meanwhile closed the title's last clause (drop the jupyter dependency from every repo): a grep of all 9 repos for jupyter/nbconvert/ipykernel left only polyglot/requirements.txt (nbconvert>=7.16.6, ipython_genutils, mistune==3.0.2, installed by polyglot scripts/init.ps1 pip install -r and the devcontainer Dockerfile); spiral's requirements.txt already had only mpmath and cupy-cuda12x. Removed those three lines in polyglot (uncommitted), so it now matches spiral. The remaining jupyter_lines hits in Cells notebook.ex are the .ipynb JSON writer, not the tool.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_8 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: CI recheck (gh run list, read-only): spiral's latest run is still 13:15Z on 668568ac, which predates the core.ps1 fix and failed in workflow.ps1 -Compiler -Suite all. dice, polyglot and alphabet have had no run since 06:48Z. So the CI confirmation still waits for the user's next push, and nothing new can be checked locally.")
inl notebook_livemd_export () = Task ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", "Write a .livemd next to each notebook's .ipynb and .html outputs (user 2026-10-18), so someone cloning a gh-pages branch can open the notebooks in Livebook; Cells already has --livemd-path and from_spi, so this is an export option plus the publish steps carrying *.livemd (publish-tree already includes *.livemd)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 70u32, Active, "", "cells,livemd,pages", "current", "current")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_1 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:40 main-thread: implemented in the tree, uncommitted. mix spiral.notebook --livemd (NotebookCli switch; livemd_target) writes the Livebook copy (Document.to_livemd) beside each notebook's report: next to the .ipynb named by --output-path (x.spi.ipynb gives x.spi.livemd), into --output-dir, or beside the source. It works with many --path values; --livemd-path stays single-path. Test notebook_livemd_target_test.exs 2/2. lib/spiral/build.ps1 passes --livemd, so CI writes lib/spiral/<nb>.spi.livemd beside the .ipynb and .html it already writes there, and publish-tree (which includes *.livemd) carries all three to gh-pages. Left: the other repos' notebook runs (blog and x write into target/, which publish-tree excludes, so their notebooks aren't published at all yet).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_2 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:34 helper-lane lap 1: state: the --livemd export is committed (e0ffc80b) and lib/spiral/build.ps1 passes --livemd, but gh-pages of i574n/spiral (last deploy 10-18 19:00Z) has 39 lib/spiral .ipynb and 0 .livemd, because every spiral CI run since e0ffc80b failed (18:51 e0ffc80b, 22:12 65f83164, 06:48 668568ac: build ubuntu, step workflow.ps1; main is on it). Blog (left part of note 1): its notebooks went to target/build/<pkg>/notebooks, which publish-tree excludes. Edited (uncommitted): blog scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-SpiralTests now passes --no-html --livemd, and scripts/publish.ps1 copies each target/build/<pkg>/notebooks/*.ipynb|html|livemd into dist/notebooks/<pkg>/ after publish-tree (same CI job, ubuntu only), so gh-pages carries them. Verification rides on the queued BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST blog build (check target/build/engine/notebooks/*.spi.livemd appear). x keeps its own scripts (x lane).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_3 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: checked that local warm runs export too, not only CI's cold ones: run_notebook.ps1 forwards its arguments either to the warm daemon (Invoke-WarmNotebook) or to mix, and the daemon handles a request with Spiral.Cells.NotebookCli.execute(args) (compiler_daemon.ex:988), the same parser that declares livemd: :boolean and livemd_path (notebook_cli.ex:11/21) and derives x.spi.livemd from --output-path x.spi.ipynb (livemd_target, :119-122). So --livemd behaves the same with SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM on or off; blog's runner sets warm 0 anyway. Remaining evidence is still external: the queued blog build (target/build/engine/notebooks/*.livemd) and a green spiral CI deploy (lib/spiral/*.spi.livemd on gh-pages).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_4 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:49 helper-lane lap 3: blog half verified: main's full blog build (10:00-10:04, BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST note 4) wrote 9 .spi.livemd files next to the 10 .ipynb in blog/target/build/engine/notebooks (build, html, index, page, post, rss, showcase, site, style; the 10th ipynb is generate's, added after that build). Each is valid Livebook markdown: per cell a livebook smart_cell marker (Elixir.Spiral.Cells.SmartCell with source and timeout attrs) plus an elixir block calling Spiral.Cells.eval! on the cell's ~S source, with test cells under their own heading (showcase (test)). With the uncommitted publish.ps1 copy step they land in dist/notebooks/engine on the next blog deploy. Left: the spiral half (lib/spiral/*.spi.livemd on gh-pages) needs a green spiral CI deploy after the user's push.")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_5 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: same CI state as NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER: no spiral gh-pages run since 13:15Z (pre-fix commit 668568ac). The spiral half (lib .spi.livemd on gh-pages) is checked on the first green run after the user's push.")
inl tibia_zig_oracle () = Task ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", "Move tibia's oracle tests from ///> c to ///> zig (compiler lane ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE, 2026-10-18: C and Zig disagree on 1 of 459 shared samples, an intended trap; Zig ships its own toolchain, so there's no mingw/UCRT vs msvcrt split). tibia/src has 146 c targets, 25 wgsl, 2 rust. Decide by measurement: pass rate and seconds per file for zig vs c through lab/tibia-zig-oracle/measure.ps1, on both local zig 0.18.0-dev and CI's pinned 0.17.0.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 30u32, Active, "", "tibia,zig,oracle,testing", "current", "current")
inl note_of_tibia_zig_oracle_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T19:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:01 first measurement, zig mode, local zig 0.18.0-dev.146: quantity 4/4 22.4 s, coins 5/5 25.6 s, damage 4/4 38.6 s, cooldowns 7/7 37.1 s, all passing. combat could not run from the copy: its '///- --package .' header fails outside tibia/src (CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE), which also blocks every header file in this copy-based method until that is fixed. Next: a C baseline through the same script (Mode c) on the same files, then the header files once CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE lands (or measure in place on a branch of src).")
inl note_of_tibia_zig_oracle_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:07 correction: combat's failure in the copy was my copy racing src edits (an inconsistent package), not a header limitation; the next measurement copies src in one pass when src is quiet.")
inl note_of_tibia_zig_oracle_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T19:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:43 the deciding measurement is pack_sim, not the small files. Cells runs Zig as 'zig run -fno-llvm' on x86_64 (targets.ex zig_run_args / zig_self_hosted?, opt-out SPIRAL_CELLS_ZIG_LLVM=1): the self-hosted backend compiles fast but emits unoptimized debug code. Small rules files are compile-bound, which is why zig matched them in 22-39 s. pack_sim's evolution and clear tests are compute-bound (up to 80 s per test with C on Windows CI), so debug Zig may approach the cells 300 s limit. Measure pack_sim and gpu_sim's oracle rows with zig, and with SPIRAL_CELLS_ZIG_LLVM=1, before deciding.")
inl note_of_tibia_zig_oracle_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T01:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 21:14 pack_sim on zig (debug, -fno-llvm, SPIRAL_CELLS_JOBS=1, launched 18:03) stopped at 21:13 without finishing, to free the machine for the PR-13 live slot. Scratch-dir timestamps show two sequential test runs taking about 60 min (18:33-19:33) and 90 min (19:37-21:07) of wall time. The third test, the_random_control_stays_far_below_every_rule (C 37.6 s including compile), had used 382 CPU s over 6 min and was still running. The C gate runs the whole file in 691 s, slowest test 495 s (pack_sim-c-tests.tsv). Verdict: debug self-hosted Zig cannot be pack_sim's oracle; C stays primary for simulation-heavy files. Still to measure: SPIRAL_CELLS_ZIG_LLVM=1 (optimized LLVM build) on pack_sim, and gpu_sim's oracle rows. Also seen: Erlang reported disk_almost_full on C: at 21:14.")
inl spiral_core_neq () = Task ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", "core operator <> lowers to not (a = b) and emits v != true instead of a direct != (NEQ op)", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "spiral,core,codegen", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 1u32, "2026-10-18T19:20Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:20 found by the eoie lane while moving eoie onto core: replacing !!!!NEQ(a,b) with a <> b changed generated Rust from let v = a != b to let v2 = a == b; let v3 = v2 != true (eoie_fs_actions, eoie_legacy_operations, hygiene probe). Same meaning, worse code, and it breaks byte-identity proofs. Fix in core: define (<>) through the NEQ op. Until then eoie keeps its 38 !!!!NEQ calls")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:57 main-thread (compiler lane): root cause in the core fork, deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/real_core.spir:220: the structural (<>) is (a = b) <> true for every type. core.spi:111 (forall t, real a <> b) shadows the {prim} version at :31 that used !!!!NEQ, so every <> goes through it, primitives included. Draft in spiral/lab/SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ/apply.ps1 (dry run passes, not applied; it keeps the file's CRLF endings). It mirrors the structural (=): tuples, records, layouts, unions and nominals keep (a = b) <> true; unit is false; symbols are false; anything else lowers to !!!!NEQ(a,b). It also keeps (=)'s same-type check, with its own message. It changes residuals for every core user that compares primitives with <>, so it lands with the Zig-oracle pack 4 (own review + accept), not with pack 3. After it, eoie can drop its 38 !!!!NEQ calls")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:54 helper-lane lap 1: state after pack 4: not applied to the fork core (deps/The-Spiral-Language real_core.spir:220 is still (a = b) <> true; the clone is at 3cf0b4da and clean), and lab/SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ/apply.ps1 -DryRun still matches today. But the same fix already lives in the core migration's candidate: lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v1/core/real_core.spir:308-319 has the structural (<>) with !!!!NEQ for primitives and (a = b) <> true for tuples, records, layouts, unions and nominals, while the upstream ML corebase it derives from still has the slow form (real_core.spir:320). So the fix ships with CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (the item's existing dep); patching the fork core separately would churn every <> residual twice. Decision: do not apply apply.ps1; close this item when core v1 is installed and eoie's 38 !!!!NEQ calls can go.")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:37 helper-lane lap 2: counted what the core-v1 fix will let go: eoie still has exactly 38 !!!!NEQ calls in 7 files (eoie_process/boundary_probe 12, eoie_release_hygiene/hygiene_policy_probe 9, cold_rebuild_negative_probe 8, dogfood_shim_contract/model 3, eoie_source_topology/policy 3, coverage_public_probe 2, eoie_fs_actions 1), matching the 10-18 report; and lib/spiral carries its own workaround operators (<>.) and (<>..) in base.spi (313-354) used by testing.spi (__assert_ne, line 106 and 213) and trace.spi (59, 273). Once core v1's structural (<>) lowers primitives to NEQ, both the eoie calls and lib's (<>.)/(<>..) can become plain <>, which is also a LIB-CANON-NAMES simplification (two operator names fewer).")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:20 helper-lane lap 3: read the core that the switch lands (lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v1/core, main's apply-switch.ps1 since 11:15): core.spi line 31 defines (<>) forall t {prim} as !!!!NEQ directly, line 159 the structural (<>) forall t as real a <> b, and real_core.spir 308-320 lowers that to !!!!NEQ(a,b) for primitives (the final else), keeps (a = b) <> true only for pairs, records, layouts, unions and nominals, and gives false for unit. So after the switch a <> b on i32/string should emit a direct != on Rust/C, and the only remaining v != true shape is inequality of composite values, where it is the natural lowering. Proof still to run after the accept: one probe (i32 and string <> on Rust and C, grep the output for != true), then the rewrite list: 38 eoie !!!!NEQ calls (eoie lane) and lib's (<>.)/(<>..) workaround operators in base.spi 313-354 (testing.spi 106/213, trace.spi 59/273).")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 6u32, "2026-10-19T16:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 12:15 done through the core switch: <> now lowers to a single != (accepted 113145: ebpf_neq, multiline_comment, multiline_triple_string, portable_math_rounding changed residuals only, == then != true -> one !=).")
inl note_of_spiral_core_neq_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", 7u32, "2026-10-19T17:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:40 helper-lane: Done, fixed by the core v1 switch (accepted at 113145): lab/SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ/probe.ps1 compiled neq_probe.spi (a <> b on dynamic i32 and string locals) with the installed compiler against the switched core; both run exit 0 and the outputs hold direct comparisons with no != true or == false wrappers - Rust: let mut v4: bool = v0 != v1; and v2.clone() != v3.clone(); C: v4 = v0 != v1; and strcmp(v2->ptr, v3->ptr) != 0. Follow-ups that this unblocks, not part of this item: the 38 eoie !!!!NEQ workarounds (eoie lane) and lib base.spi's (<>.)/(<>..) operators (testing.spi 106/213, trace.spi 59/273) can become plain <>.")
inl upstream_changes_post () = Task ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", "Bun-style post committed in the upstream fork (i574n/The-Spiral-Language) that explains every change against mrakgr's host_cpp_and_cuda_backend branch (treated as the new temporary master, since nothing was merged to master) down to the line, one section per pull request in the planned PR split; it credits what was taken from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend and from Spiral-s-ML-Library versus what the fork did (user 2026-10-18); line-level tables generated from git (upstream.py export) so the post cannot drift, prose only for intent Split into a bite-sized series starting with UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (user 2026-10-18).", Feature, P1, D6, 20u32, Active, "COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION,UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", "blog,upstream,docs", "current", "current")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:43 main-thread first measurement: python apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/upstream.py status C:\\home\\git\\The-Spiral-Language mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (18 s) maps each /// ## section of spiral_compiler.fs to upstream's The Spiral Language 2/<Name>.fs. Over the 27 upstream-mapped sections, 6,440 lines differ. section-only (ours) leads with PartEval 533, Infer 462, SignalRSupervisor 463, CodegenC 428, BlockParsing 415, WDiff 341, Prepass 327, Tokenize 250. fork-only (host_cpp lines we lack, the rest of UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC) leads with Infer 274, Prepass 238, SignalRSupervisor 203, BlockParsing 197, CodegenC 147, PartEval 143. Sections without an upstream file (our backends Rust, Zig, Lean, Lua, Wasm, ...) come on top as new files. Saved as spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/status-vs-host_cpp.txt; the post's per-PR tables come from upstream.py export --since per PR")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: series progress: post 1's generator (lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/make-post.ps1) now shows each fix's Before and After halves from fixes.tsv; the 6,440-line upstream-mapped drift from note 1 is what later posts carve into PR-sized layers Next: re-run upstream.py status after the core switch to see how much of the drift the corebase core removes")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:16 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1's next step: upstream.py status maps only spiral_compiler.fs sections to upstream's The Spiral Language 2/<Name>.fs, so the lib core switch (CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE, a package change) cannot move this number. Re-ran it now (heavy gate, under 20 s): total differing lines 6,440, identical to 10-18 16:43 and identical per section (PartEval 533, SignalRSupervisor 463, Infer 462, CodegenC 428, BlockParsing 415, CodegenUtils 343, WDiff 341, Prepass 327, Tokenize 250, CodegenPython 223, ServerUtils 187, CodegenFsharp 183, CodegenCpp 112, SpiProj 95; 13 small sections under 40): packs 4 and 5 landed in non-mapped sections (codegens for Gleam, Zig, Lua and others are fork-only sections). So the post series' line tables are stable today; the levers that shrink them are the COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION layering and UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC (both deps). Next: rank the 27 mapped sections by section-only lines and pick the post-2 PR candidate (smallest self-contained section with real drift: CodegenCpp 112 or SpiProj 95)")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:55 lap 3 grain: read the SpiProj drift line by line. upstream.py export (light, into spiral-bin/runs/upstream-export-lap3) vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend:The Spiral Language 2/SpiProj.fs: 35 hunks, 95 added / 42 removed lines. Three kinds: (a) merge-into-one-file renames, the parser helpers suffixed to stay unique in spiral_compiler.fs (spaces -> spacesSpiProj, range -> rangeSpiProj, is_var_char -> is_var_charSpiProj, file -> fileSpiProj; plus open VSCTypes commented and Utils.lines -> lines): 21 of the 95 added lines are such pure renames; (b) real fixes: Directory entries no longer crash the module list (Array.map over File|Directory with an incomplete pattern -> Array.choose of File), vscpos helpers; (c) features: package.spiproj gains defaultInt, defaultFloat and edition fields (the --default-int/--default-float host flags). Consequence for the series: kind (a) must not reach an upstream PR, because the fork keeps one file per module; export should map the suffixed names back before the line tables are generated, else every post overstates the drift (for SpiProj by about a fifth). Post candidates from this file: one small fix post (Directory entries) and one feature post (project defaults). Next: list the suffix renames per section (pattern <name><Section> where the fork has <name>) to size kind (a) across all 27 sections, as input for an export rename map")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:28 lap 4 grain: sized the merge-rename drift over all 27 mapped sections (export vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend per file; an added line counts as a suffix rename when deleting the <Section> suffix from its identifiers yields a removed line): 102 of 4,577 added lines, about 2 percent, concentrated in Prepass 65, SpiProj 21, WDiffPrepass 12, BlockParsing 2, WDiff 2, elsewhere 0. So lap 3's about a fifth holds only for SpiProj; series-wide the line tables are honest without a rename map, and a map is worth it for 3 sections only (it can be a list of 3 suffixes in export, not a general mechanism). Biggest real drift by added lines: PartEval 533, Supervisor 463, Infer 462, CodegenC 430, BlockParsing 415, CodegenUtils 343 (of which 337 have no counterpart: new code), WDiff 339, Prepass 327. Next: for the post after vanilla, split CodegenUtils' 343 new lines into the features they carry (UnusedGlobals.prune, closure naming helpers, ...) as PR-sized candidates")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:44 lap 5 grain: CodegenUtils' 343 added lines carved into PR-sized features (export vs his branch, hunk by hunk): (1) readable generated names, 241 lines in one hunk: generatedMethodName and generatedClosureName ({stem}_c{tag} from the inl binding name, closureSourceName over E), generatedCaseNames/Suffixes/CaseName/CaseNameOfTag with collision counting, plus the per-backend case shapes leanCaseName and gleamCaseName (upper_camel); (2) UnusedGlobals.prune, inside the same hunk's tail: declaration-keyword globals whose name occurs nowhere else in the output are dropped, exported (pub/export) and multi-item blobs kept; (3) codegen_runtime_file and printf_pieces, 70 lines: workspace/root lookup of runtime files and a shared printf splitter the backends reuse; (4) duplicate-global fragments and decimal literal point disposition, 27 lines (preservesDuplicateGlobalFragment, DecimalLiteralPointDispositionId, indent) plus add_dec_point 7; (5) string_builder_env and CodegenTagAllocator, 12 lines. Ordering for posts: (1) readable names is self-contained and already proven across backends by the 10-11 ladders in AGENTS.md (closure names per backend, 0 rows differing on restore), so it is the best first feature post after vanilla; (2) depends on (1) only for text shape; (3)-(5) are small helpers that ride with the backend posts that use them. Next: list (1)'s call sites in the C and F# codegen sections, the part a PR must carry with it")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:47 lap 5 grain: call sites of the readable-names feature (grep per /// ## section): upstream-mapped sections that a PR must touch with it: CodegenC (generatedCaseNameOfTag 4, generatedClosureName 1), CodegenFsharp (generatedCaseName 5, closure 1, method 1), CodegenCpp (CaseNameOfTag 2), CodegenPython (CaseNameOfTag 7, closure 1, method 1), plus closureSourceName 4 in CodegenAdapter (fork-only) and UnusedGlobals.prune 2 in SignalRSupervisor's build; every other caller is a fork-only backend (Rust, Delphi, Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Wgsl, TypeScript). So a readable-names PR against his branch = CodegenUtils' 241-line hunk + 4 upstream codegens (C, F#, Cpp, Python: ~25 call sites) + the closureSourceName hook (prepass records the binding name on the body, per AGENTS.md) and no fork-only backend; UnusedGlobals.prune is a separate PR touching CodegenUtils + the build entry. Next: check that closureSourceName's prepass hook lives in an upstream-mapped section (Prepass) and size it")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:33Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:33 lap 6 grain: sized the prepass part of the readable-names PR. It is small and self-contained. Definition (Prepass section, spiral_compiler.fs 52566-52577, 12 lines): closureBindingNames is a ConditionalWeakTable<E, string> keyed by the function node, with noteClosureName and copyClosureName. There are 3 call sites: lower 52624 and 52686 (copyClosureName from the source node to the lowered inner and closure-converted node) and 53060 in the let-binding prepass (noteClosureName when namesFunction body). The reading side in CodegenUtils (161522-161552, 31 lines) is generatedMethodName, generatedClosureName (stem_c tag) and closureSourceName, which tries the table first, then the join-point name through EFun'/EAnnot. The table is weak and keyed by reference, so it adds no AST field, which keeps the upstream diff out of the E union. PR shape: Prepass hunk (~15 lines) + CodegenUtils hunk + each upstream backend's call sites (C, F#, Cpp, Python: about 25 sites, each name = generatedClosureName (closureSourceName ...) tag). Next: list the Cpp and Python call sites in his CodegenCpp/CodegenPython to confirm they take the same shape.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: the first feature post is scoped (readable names: CodegenUtils hunk 31 lines, prepass hook 12 lines + 3 sites, ~25 backend call sites). Progress 20.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:52 lap 7 grain: checked the call-site shape on his branch (The-Spiral-Language clone, mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend, git show, read only). Name sites per codegen: Cpp 8 (FunPointerMethod{tag}, Closure{tag}, ...), Python 12 (method%i, Closure%i, UH{tag}_{i}), C 7, F# 4 (method%i, closure%i): 31 sites, close to the ~25 estimate. Two facts change the PR text: (1) his CodegenC already names methods after their join point (sprintf %s%i with Option.defaultValue method x.name), so for C the post is about extending that to closures and the other three backends, not about starting it; (2) his CodegenUtils.fs is only 50 lines, so our CodegenUtils hunk is nearly all new code (generatedMethodName, generatedClosureName, closureSourceName, generatedCaseNames) and reads best as a new section. Next: draft the post's before/after table from one sample's C and Python output on his compiler vs ours.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:28 lap 8 grain: first rows of the naming before/after table, taken by reading instead of building his compiler: samples/closure_pair (inl f used as a value) is named ClosureMethod0 in his C (CodegenC.fs:597 on host_cpp_and_cuda_backend) and Closure0 in his Python (CodegenPython.fs:232), while ours is f_c0 in both main.c:32 and main.py:23 (generatedClosureName). Rows in lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv (sample, backend, his, ours, source). Also visible: our C passes the closure as Fun0 * self, a representation change the post needs its own row for. Next: method_pair and tuple rows, then generate the table from upstream.py export")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:52 lap 9 grain: naming table grew 3 rows (lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv): method_i8 (join pick n) is method0 in his C (CodegenC.fs:364 falls back to method when x.name is None) and Python, and method0 in ours; tuple_pair's anonymous join points are method0/method1 on both sides. A scan of every non-mega samples/*/main.c finds no stem_tag method name, so in C and Python the visible naming change is the closures (ClosureMethodN / ClosureN -> stem_cN); methods change only where a join point carries a name. That narrows the post's naming section to closures for the backends his branch has")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:27 lap 10 grain: naming table +4 rows (lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv): his branch (mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend, ls-tree) has exactly 4 codegens, CodegenC, CodegenCpp, CodegenFsharp, CodegenPython, so every other backend is new in the post; closure_pair F# is closure0 on his side (CodegenFsharp.fs:438 sprintf closure%i) and f_c0 in ours (main.fsx:1); Rust, Kotlin, Swift rows marked new backend with ours f_c0 (main.rs:4, main.kt:2, main.swift:3). Next: Cpp row (his CodegenCpp naming vs our CppHost output), then upstream.py export wiring of the table")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: Cpp row added to the naming table: ts_closure_capture (inl f captured, then ~g = f) is Closure0 in his CodegenCpp (line 324, Closure{x.tag}) and still Closure0 in our main.cpp:7, so C++ is the one backend the readable closure naming never reached (AGENTS.md lists the rollout for Rust, Kotlin, Swift, TypeScript, Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Delphi, F#, Python and C, not Cpp/CppHost). For the post: the naming PR changes no C++ text. For main: a possible small follow-up (CppHost closures through generatedClosureName) if the naming should be uniform. Next: upstream.py export wiring of the table into the post")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:15 the post's line tables now come from git: lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/pr_table.py runs upstream.py export (optionally --since/--until for one PR's commit range) and prints a markdown table per fork file against mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend. Whole fork today: 26 files, +4,904 / -1,939 lines, 0 conflicts (largest PartEval +568/-148, Infer +536/-286, Supervisor +472/-218, BlockParsing +443/-202, CodegenC +440/-157). naming_table_check.py --markdown renders the closure-naming table only after its 17 citations verify")
inl core_lib_consolidate () = Task ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "Take the core lib from mrakgr's Spiral-s-ML-Library and submit it to the fork's master (user 2026-10-18): trace every copy of every core file across repos and branches, keep the most complete and recent version of each file with provenance (repo, path, commit, date), merge files that diverged on both sides, and prune older copies safely (dangling check by stem + consumer builds, deletes need the user's OK)", Feature, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "core,upstream,lib", "current", "current")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_1 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:07 main-thread first census (read-only, real_core.spir only): 65 copies in 5 roots (Spiral-s-ML-Library, The-Spiral-Language, spiral/deps and polyglot/deps forks, spiral/lib), 13 distinct versions. Newest and largest: Spiral-s-ML-Library/corebase/real_core.spir (2025-12-19, 17,252 bytes; ML lib head 80d6a1a 2026-02-05). The core every fork build compiles against, VS Code Plugin/core/real_core.spir, is the 2023-09-28 version (12,235 bytes), and the 40+ cuda/fpga experiment copies are frozen snapshots of 2023-2025 versions. Consequence: SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ must be applied on top of the consolidated core, not the 2023 copy; the ML lib copy has the same (<>) at :320")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_2 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T20:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:26 main-thread provenance (user go 2026-10-18: replace the core with the fittest version, judged per file, keep-old decisions explained in the post, upstream name core). (1) ML lib corebase is one linear history: all 13 trading/max-dict branches share tree 46629cd = commit c652c3f (2025-11-15), and hd-cfr-prelims (687ca03), cpp_and_cuda_backend (c05037d) and master (7f2906c) are its ancestors. So c652c3f is the base, and the local clone (branch trading_order_flow, clean) matches it. (2) upstream mrakgr/master after host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (2025-11-14) is 26 dependabot commits only, so host_cpp stays the language base. (3) host_cpp's own VS Code Plugin/core differs from the fork's only in core.spi and listm.spi. (4) corebase uses 116 !!!! ops, and all exist in our compiler. (5) Design gaps to judge: hash, assert, sa/da/sa_list/da_list and ref nominals switch only over Cpp/Cuda/Python (our C, Rust, Zig, Lua, Lean... have no arm). Arrays become 1-D array t indexed by int (old: a dim el). order becomes a union. hashable returns u64. ~* and ~! are gone (unary deref). ML-domain modules (tensorm, partitionm, serializerm, pickler, picklers) live in corebase. Old-core modules missing from corebase: am, dictm(+utils, type), iam, rm, sm, loopw. Candidate v0 = corebase verbatim, renamed core, at spiral/lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0/core (package.spiproj = the ML project's corebase section); the compiler takes it through SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, so measuring needs no change to the shared fork. Next: break census (compile-only matrix + Cells gate over lib/apps/tibia/eoie/blog/x) after pack 3's accept, then v1 = slim plus backend arms")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_3 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T20:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:30 main-thread: a text census can't attribute uses to core. Over 1,938 .spi/.spir in spiral lib+apps, eoie, blog, x, dice and alphabet, ripgrep counts sm. 638, listm. 300, optionm. 83, am. 45, loopw. 9, limit. 5, and dictm/iam/rm/loop/monad 0. But consumers ship their own same-named modules: eoie/src/state_receipt_codec_model/sm.spi and samples/core/sm.spi define sm.concat (312 of the 638), while core's sm.spi has only index, length and slice. Attribution therefore uses the compiler's SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT recorder (prepass resolves each use to its definition path) over the consumer corpus, run against the old core and against candidate v0. Uses that resolve into core files are the migration surface. Planned with the post-accept heavy queue")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_4 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 4u32, "2026-10-18T20:37Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:37 main-thread candidate v1, built reproducibly by spiral/lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/make-v1.ps1 from corebase c652c3f + old core 34c894ad (19 files). It takes base, limit, real_core, type_arith, core, compile_time, struct, listm, loop, optionm, dep_optionm, monad and arraym from corebase, and sm from the old core. Not yet in: dictm (keep, but port from the old 'a dim el' arrays to 'array t' first) and the ML and C++/Python array modules (moved out). base.spi drops its Cpp/Python-only nominals (cupy_type, sa/da/sa_list/da_list type macros, ref) with their comments, 30 lines. real_core keeps the old runtime failwith assert (keep-old: corebase's native assert arms exist only for Python/C++/CUDA, a compile error elsewhere, and C++ assert vanishes under NDEBUG) and chains SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ. Judgments are in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/decisions.tsv (feeds the upstream post). Probes against v1 through SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR (single C probes, allowed in the live block): c_gadt_union_array identical (1,404 bytes). ebpf_neq 1,033 -> 926 bytes, v6 = v0 != v1 instead of == then != true, which proves the override reaches the compiler (a missing dir silently falls back to deps, so negative tests need an existing broken core). The probe rewrote samples/ebpf_neq/main.c; restored")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_5 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T20:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:38 main-thread correction: the old dictm is Python-only, not backend-neutral. Its buckets are the old ra Python list (rm.add appends, rm.pop removes) and it hashes keys with hash_cython. Decision changed from keep-and-port to drop. corebase's corecudahost/dictm is its CUDA-host successor. A dictionary for every backend (growable buckets over array t plus a backend-neutral hash) is new work, noted as a core gap for after the migration")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_6 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T21:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:36 main-thread consumer migration sized. Text census of the breaking changes over lib, apps, eoie, blog, x, tibia and the samples: ~* and ~! 0, optionm.bind 0, core hash/hashable 0 (so hash arms wait for the every-backend dictionary), am. 38 in 15 files, loopw. 10 in 4, listm.toArray 2, old array type 'a int|i32 el' 37 in 14. lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/migrate-consumers.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply writes) makes the mechanical edits: a int|i32 X -> array X (both index by the default int), am. -> arraym., loopw. -> loop., listm.toArray -> arraym.fromList. It flags the rest for hand edits: (a x) nominal patterns, instances declared with a dim, arrays over other index types. Dry run over spiral lib + apps/spiral + eoie/src + blog/engine + x: 632 files, 75 auto edits, 13 manual spots (am'.spi carries 38 of them; eoie lease.spi has the one non-int array). Tibia's src/network.spi and src/search.spi go to the tibia lane, not edited here. The switch is one coordinated pack (fork core + lib + consumers), measured first on scratch copies with SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR = candidate-v1")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_7 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T21:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:40 main-thread: migrate-consumers.ps1 now covers lib completely in its scratch dry run (fresh copy of spiral/lib, 84 files): 85 automatic edits, 0 manual spots left. New rules: dim-generic definitions (forall dim el. ... a dim el: the dim binder and its constraint go, a dim X becomes array X, any other dim becomes int; applied per top-level block); (a x) unwraps in fun, inl and typed patterns become (array x); lib's array equable instance is dropped (core arraym declares it); lib's append instance moves to array t; the mapm annotation. Bug caught on the way: PowerShell's static [regex]::Replace(s, p, r, 1) takes 1 as RegexOptions (IgnoreCase), not a count, so the first version stripped every dim on the header line; it now uses an instance Regex with a count. Watch item for the census: seq.from_array names its parameter array, which shadows the core array constructor inside that body. The scratch tree is ready for the v1 census")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_8 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 8u32, "2026-10-18T22:37Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 18:37 main-thread FIRST GREEN: lib/spiral listm'.spi passes 22/22 against candidate v1 with the migrated lib (scratch copy, Cells --gate, SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR). Its closure covers most of lib: am', seq, sm', base, runtime, threading, benchmark, leptos/*, state_core, mapm, crypto, iter, async, json_real, file_system. It went from 43 type errors to 0 in six rounds. Findings. (1) The parser lowers ;[...] to a call of the term array in scope; the old lib's identity array (am'_prototypes, an include) kept literals raw, and the new core's array is the nominal constructor, so literals are now array t (upstream's design). Lib now constructs with array, unwraps with (array x), and literals feeding raw-array APIs unwrap with am'.base (to_vec, store names, the runtime env default); a literal in pattern position still needs the unwrap. (2) Edition 3 lowers unary *x to the core function ~*, so v1 keeps the old core's ~* and ~! (keep-old). Edition 2 builds RawLayoutIndex like upstream; aligning edition 3 is a follow-up. (3) corebase renamed loop.while (thunk form) to while' and union_to_record to union_type_to_record. (4) A variable named array shadows the new constructor (benchmark). (5) Script bug fixed: a unary-comma return appended the count to every rewritten file. migrate-consumers.ps1 has 36 rules (268 auto edits over 84 lib files); probe-lib.ps1 is the single-file loop")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_9 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T01:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 21:25 main-thread FIRST COMPILE CENSUS of candidate v1 (lab census.ps1: scratch copy of apps/compiler/tmp, SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR = candidate-v1, compile-only -Suite all at parallelism 1, as an eoie job after two reaped background tries; run single-flight-20261018-211010, 15 min). Result: of 7,000 rows, 102 change compile status against the accepted baseline: 96 ok -> error, 6 new rows that compile. The 96 are almost all lib-backed samples (frontier_static_list_eq, the portable_* trio, the frontier format_any pair, zig_lib_console), failing only because this pass linked the UNMIGRATED tree lib (am'_prototypes.spi:3, 'a int t' unbound). The one sample-side break is lua_while_union_pair, whose source uses the old array API; the migration script rewrites it (3 edits). So the core swap leaves the sample corpus untouched outside lib. Next pass: census.ps1 -Lib <migrated scratch lib> -MigrateSamples (both switches added), after the tibia live block")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_10 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T02:00Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 22:00 main-thread COMPILE CENSUS 2 CLEAN: candidate v1 + the migrated scratch lib (core-switch-dev, 271 edits) + migrated samples (lua_while_union_pair, 3 edits), compile-only -Suite all at parallelism 1, eoie job, 9 min (run single-flight-20261018-215104). All 7,000 rows keep their accepted compile status; the only changes are the 6 rows new in pack 3. So the switch compiles the whole corpus. Not yet measured: residuals (expected to change, e.g. NEQ and the portable loops), native behavior (the -Native matrix against v1), and lib's own test blocks (the sweep, 2/40 before it was reaped)")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_11 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T02:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 22:01 main-thread: the switch is one script, lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/apply-switch.ps1 (dry run by default; -Apply writes). It replaces the fork's VS Code Plugin/core with candidate v1: 8 changed, 9 added and 12 removed files (am/*, dictm*, dictm_utils/*, iam*, loopw, rm). It writes the fork's own convention (CRLF, no BOM; sm and limit come out identical), then runs migrate-consumers -Apply over spiral lib, apps/spiral, the compiler samples, blog/engine and x (298 auto edits). eoie/src and tibia src/apps are left to their lanes (tibia's 44 edits are confirmed). Order: after the pack 3 accept, apply as pack 4 with its own -Native review and accept, plus the lib Cells sweep; the user commits the fork and spiral together")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_12 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T04:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 00:31 eoie lane: accepted the eoie dry run against candidate-v1 for my next session; nothing runs while pack3-accept holds the heavy slot. Plan: compile every eoie owner entry into TEMP with SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR = candidate-v1 (no repo writes) and list the failures; byte-compare the rest with the committed .rs, splitting NEQ lowering and mut-record loops from anything unexpected; port the one u64 array in eoie_agile_lease/lease.spi; drop the 38 !!!!NEQ builtins (SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ) in the same pack; check the 1000-line gate (eoie_do 991, eoie_do_facts 939, eoie_do_process ~894 are close). Results go to main-thread before any source edit.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_13 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T05:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 01:16 main-thread NATIVE CENSUS CLEAN: candidate v1 + migrated lib + migrated samples, -Suite all -Native in the scratch copy (eoie job, 46 min, run single-flight-20261019-003004), compared with the pack 4 baseline (234436): 7,007 rows, 0 compile-status or exit changes, DISAGREE 0, oracle agree 5,779 (same as the baseline), 9 build-fail (same). On every backend in the matrix the switch changes residuals, not behavior. Left before the switch lands: lib's own test blocks (sweep, running now), the eoie lane's dry run against the candidate (agreed for its next session; 1 manual spot in lease.spi plus regeneration), tibia's 44 edits (its lane applies them), then apply-switch.ps1 -Apply as one reviewed and accepted pack")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_14 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T06:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 02:32 main-thread: user direction (relayed by the eoie lane): 'about the array, can't we keep the version that supports indexes in our lib or something?' Answer: lib gets am_dim (lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/lib-additions/am_dim.spi): nominal a dim el = array_base el, with create, index, set and length typed by any int dim, of_array/to_array to and from core's int-indexed array, and a u64 round-trip test (C, Rust). It lands with the switch, since under the old core a is core's own name. Core's array stays int-indexed like upstream. eoie needs 0 edits: its one 'manual spot' was a false positive (the words 'a u64 literal' in a Rust error string); the rule now requires a type context. Sweep on the migrated dev lib: 40 files, 310 passed, 42 failed, 9 type errors; 6 type-error files fixed with 5 more rules (iter 1/1, resultm 2/2, seq 26/26; parsing, reflection and rust/testing still fail and are being compared with the old core)")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_15 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T07:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 03:28 main-thread lib sweep follow-up (old-core comparison of the 15 failing files running). Migration breaks found and fixed: qualified real_core.union_to_record (rename rule widened; common 5/5, crypto 4/4, as on the old core); the type-error files (iter 1/1, resultm 2/2, seq 26/26); benchmark 2/3 as on the old core; file_system 17/17: an _assert_in after an unwrap now compares arrays, and an i64 count converts am'.length with conv. Note: the scratch copy needs the repo's workspace dir and README.md (find_parent, trace_file); a junction and a copy fixed that, and earlier failures with that cause were scratch artifacts. am_dim is now skipped by the migration (apply-switch installs it before migrating). parsing fails 17 on the OLD core and 1 on the new one, so it is pre-existing")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_16 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T07:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:31 eoie lane: the eoie manual spot from migrate-consumers was a false positive (lease.spi:78 is Rust error text 'is not a u64 literal' inside a rust_global string), so eoie needs 0 hand edits. User 2026-10-19: keep an index-generic array in lib rather than porting (main-thread: lib module am_dim lands with the switch). Probe on eoie_jobs: candidate-v1 compiles it 841 -> 835 lines, and the only differences are == / != true pairs collapsing into one !=. Full eoie dry run running as job core-dry-run-hidden: lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/core-dry-run.ps1 compiles every owner with each core into TEMP, core-compare.ps1 sorts owners into same, renumbered, neq-only, other and breaks, and flags any over the 1000 gate.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_17 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T07:51Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:51 eoie lane dry-run result: all 168 eoie owners compiled with the current core and with candidate-v1 from identical sources. 0 breaks, 156 byte-identical, 12 NEQ-only (one != instead of == plus != true), 0 other changes, none over the 1000 gate; 67116 -> 67084 lines. eoie needs 0 manual edits. The switch pack adds the 38 !!!!NEQ -> <> conversion (dry-run converter ready) and republishes the 12 changed owners.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_18 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T09:00Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:00 main-thread lib triage on the migrated dev copy (scratch now has workspace and README like the repo): passing base 20/21, testing 6/6, trace 2/2, reflection 9/9, rust/testing 3/4, runtime 7/7 (clap argument lists stay raw: am'.base), common 5/5, crypto 4/4, file_system 17/17, iter, resultm, seq 26/26, am' 10/10, listm' 22/22, am_dim 1/1. The old core gives the same or worse on every file checked (parsing fails 17 on the old core, 1 on the new). Last open file: sm' (38/38 on the old core). Its 3 failures had 2 known causes, both fixed by rules: concat_array wrapped an already-array literal, and regex_captures fed literals (one-line and multi-line) to am'.to_vec. show_floats is pending a run. The migration stays idempotent (a second pass makes 0 edits) after a guard fix. eoie lane dry run: 168 owners, 0 breaks, 12 owners differ only by the intended NEQ collapse. Every lane's side of the switch is ready")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_19 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T11:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 07:02 sm' on the core v1 dev copy still fails show_floats, concat_array, regex_captures (35/38). The dev copy predates the concat_array and regex_captures migration rules, so re-migrate the dev copy before reading those two. The gate's stdout holds only summaries (probe-lib.ps1 -RawOut saved it: 8 lines), so show_floats' per-backend mismatch must be read from the per-test report Cells writes for the notebook. Next: find that report, fix show_floats, then the switch pack with the eoie and tibia lanes. Dry run unchanged at 06:05: fork core 8 changed, 9 added, 12 removed; 317 auto edits, 0 manual.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_20 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: probe-lib.ps1 now runs the gate with --metrics and prints each not-passed (test, target) row, so the show_floats re-probe names the failing backend directly instead of only the test Next: HEAVY QUEUE item 3 (re-migrate dev copy, probe sm', fix show_floats)")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_21 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T13:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:19 lap 2 grain: found the likely show_floats cause without a run, by diffing tree lib against the dev copy (scratchpad core-switch/spiral/lib/spiral): sm'_real.spir format_real's Lean f64 arm reads Float.ofScientific n (k < 0) k.natAbs == array then in the dev copy, where the tree has == a then. Migration rule 'array constructor after =' (migrate-consumers.ps1, pattern (?<== )a (?=[a-z_(])) also matches the = of ==, <=, >=, != and rewrites the variable a inside macro text into the core constructor array, so Lean's show_floats cannot elaborate (unbound array) while every other backend passes; the f32 arm survived only because a.toFloat32 has no space after a. Same rule would also have broken 3 sites outside lib: apps/cells/spi/text.spi:113 and :129 (if b <= a then) and document.spi:282 (grep over all repos for [=!<>]= a: these 4 sites only). Fixed both = lookbehind rules (that one and 'array construction from a macro') to (?<=(?<![=!<>])= ): == a, <= a, != a stay; inl x = a foo and = a (z) still become array (checked on 5 strings); script parse-checks. The queued HEAVY QUEUE item 3 (re-migrate the dev copy, then probe sm') now runs with the fixed rule; expect show_floats to pass on Lean too, and the next dry run to report 4 fewer auto edits. Next: after the re-probe, record the sm' result and run the full apply-switch dry run")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_22 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T13:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:28 correction to note 21 (from main): apps/cells is not a consumer root of apply-switch, so text.spi and document.spi were never at risk (only someone running migrate-consumers over apps/cells by hand); and re-migrating alone cannot repair the dev copy because the damaged == array then text is already in it, so main's queued step mirrors the tree lib into the dev copy first, then migrates with the fixed rules. Switch dry run with the fix: 316 auto edits, down from 317 (exactly the sm'_real Lean line)")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_23 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T13:57Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:57 lap 3 grain: swept every migration rule for more macro-text damage of the == kind. lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/macro-hits.ps1 extracts the 47 rule patterns from migrate-consumers.ps1 and reports each match that sits after a macro opener on lib lines that hold one: 12 hits, all rule 'array type', and all 12 are the type annotation after the closed macro (am'.spi 501-657: ... ' : a int el, sm'.spi 1118-1119: ' : a i32 u8), i.e. intended rewrites, not macro text. So with the lap 2 lookbehind fix no rule touches text inside $'...' in lib; the scan is reusable for any new rule (run it before a dry run). Next: the queued step (mirror tree lib into the dev copy, migrate, probe sm') reports show_floats on Lean; then the switch pack with the eoie and tibia lanes")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_24 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T14:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:15 show_floats resolved: helper 2's diagnosis was right (migrate-consumers' = lookbehind rewrote == a then into == array then in format_real's Lean f64 arm); with the fixed rules, a fresh mirror of tree lib/spiral re-migrated (286 auto edits, 84 files) passes sm'.spi 42/42 with 0 type errors on core v1 (main heavy queue 10:05-10:13). No known blocker remains for the switch; apply-switch dry run: 316 auto edits. Next: the switch pack with the eoie lane (NEQ builtins) and the tibia lane (their 44 edits), plus a review and an accept.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_25 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 25u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:38 naming input from helper lane 1 taken: the new lib module is array_dim instead of am_dim (LIB-CANON-NAMES plans am' -> array), renamed in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (lib-additions/array_dim.spi, apply-switch.ps1, migrate-consumers.ps1); dry run unchanged: 316 auto edits, array_dim added after am'.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_26 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 26u32, "2026-10-19T15:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 11:15 switch landed with one incident: the first job (11:11) passed -Consumers through pwsh -File, the array flattened and bound apps/spiral and samples to -Candidate and -ForkCore, so apply-switch mirrored apps/spiral over apps/compiler/tmp/samples (9,552 tracked files deleted, 9 strays written) while migrating only lib; helper lane 2 spotted the empty folder at 11:12. Restored at 11:13 with git restore --worktree (index = HEAD, nothing staged), strays removed, git status of samples clean. apply-switch.ps1 now refuses unless Candidate and ForkCore are core folders (ForkCore under VS Code Plugin). Then applied in-process at 11:14: fork core 8 changed / 9 added / 12 removed (25 git entries in the fork clone), lib 286 + consumers 30 auto edits, array_dim added. Full -Native review running (eoie job switch-review); eoie, tibia, x lanes told.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_27 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 27u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 eoie side of the core switch (landed 11:14): neq-to-operator.ps1 -Apply converted 38 !!!!NEQ to <> in 7 probe/policy files (0 left). core-dry-run against the live fork core (deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin; -Candidate takes the parent of core/): 168 owners, 0 fail, 0 over-gate, 156 byte-identical, 12 changed (!= lowering only), 67177 -> 67145 lines; the 7 converted owners are byte-identical. The 12 outputs were copied into src. Pending: gated pipeline (regen -Publish, spec, e2e, topology) + eoie.exe reinstall after main's review (~12:00).")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_28 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 28u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 11:29 switch review CLEAN: single-flight-20261019-111439 (7,020 rows incl. 13 new F# rows): DISAGREE 0; residual changes only on 4 samples, all the NEQ lowering (ebpf_neq, multiline_comment, multiline_triple_string, portable_math_rounding: == then != true becomes one !=), behavior change only zig_lib_console F# stdout (the intended lowercase bool). apps/spiral needed more: its source of truth is spiral.livemd (build.ps1 exports it to spiral.spi), so migrate-consumers now also scans *.livemd (49 + 27 edits), plus 12 context-specific fixes at the array/array_base boundary applied through the Cells Document API (lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/apps-spiral/apply_edits.exs + edits.json, count-checked); apps/spiral now compiles to Rust with 0 errors. New generic rule: a same-line literal piped into am'.to_vec unwraps first. Accept + full apps/spiral build running (eoie job switch-verify).")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_29 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 29u32, "2026-10-19T16:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 12:15 DONE: core switch ACCEPTED, single-flight-20261019-113145 accepted 7,020 rows into the baseline, native 6,512 ran, oracle agree 5,792, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the review 111439; C rows vs the pack 5 baseline: 4 differ, exactly the 4 NEQ-lowering samples. The fork's VS Code Plugin/core is now corebase-based core v1 (sm kept, ~* and ~! kept, assert as failwith, portable loops, NEQ via <>); lib migrated (286 edits) with the new array_dim module; apps/spiral migrated through its livemd and builds with its run checks green; samples and blog engine migrated; eoie (0 source edits, 12 owners regenerated), tibia (80 edits, map rebuild identical) and x (0 edits) migrated by their lanes. For the user: the fork clone deps/The-Spiral-Language has the core change (25 entries) to commit there; spiral, blog, tibia, eoie trees carry the consumer edits.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_30 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 30u32, "2026-10-19T16:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 12:39 post-switch regression from the x lane fixed as a keep-old decision: corebase's union order = Eq | Lt | Gt made comp a b |> gt_is on runtime strings build and match a runtime enum (x view crate 990 -> 1005 lines, over the 1k gate; also oauth1 lt_is), and a > b on strings is a constraint error (number only), so x had no workaround. The fork core's base.spi (and candidate-v1) now use the old nominal order = i32 with eq/lt/gt and *_is as EQ on -1/0/1, and arraym's compare init uses eq(); no consumer matches on the constructors (rg: 0 uses); the repro now emits two string comparisons and an i32, no enum. decisions.tsv records it; rides pack 7's review and accept.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_31 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 31u32, "2026-10-19T16:57Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 12:57 eoie heavy part done (heavy job job-1792426593791-eoie-core-switch-release, 40 min): regen -Publish 168 owners 0 changed (the earlier copy was exact), debug build, Spiral tests, one-call 34/34, read e2e 5/5, ps e2e 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, orphan wait 2/2; eoie.exe reinstalled 12:55 sha256 10B805DF...; post-install hidden/orphan/spec green.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_32 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 32u32, "2026-10-19T20:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 16:40 eoie on compiler pack 8b (single-flight-20261019-145950): dry run 168 owners 0 fail, 22 changed, all smaller (67147 -> 65435 lines, -1712): positional join-point rename merges duplicate specializations (agile_rename_rewrite 682->426, predicate_literal_domain 389->227, agile_todo_sources 186->110). Heavy job job-1792439712975-eoie-pack8b-release: regen -Publish 22 changed / 146 unchanged, debug build, Spiral tests, one-call 34/34, read 5/5, ps 5/5, window 2/2, tree 2/2, orphan 2/2; eoie.exe reinstalled 16:40 sha256 9E383E9D... while this session's job wait held the old exe (retire path works in the pipeline).")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_33 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 33u32, "2026-10-19T22:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 18:43 main: consumer gap closed: the switch's rule script had never run over dice, alphabet and polyglot (found by helper lane 1 via a dice contract type error); migrate-consumers.ps1 -Apply there made 163 rule edits (dice lib 3 + ui 52, alphabet apps 15, polyglot apps 79 + lib 14) and these hand fixes: dice.spi main ;[] arms take (;[] |> am'.base); dice ui optional defaults (;[] |> am'.base), old a constructors to array, rpc_input params typed array and unwrapped once in rpc_input_box (a piped multi-line literal inside a record field does not parse); polyglot math.spi drops array re-wraps of literals; dice_ui.spi, dice.spi, dice_contract.spi, math.spi, alphabet documents.spi, polyglot cube.spi type-check with 0 errors on the installed compiler; i574n.github backlog state (note text) and spiral/apps/lisp (Lisp source) were correctly left alone. Left: cargo check of dice/ui (wasm) and the dice contract tests (helper lane 1 rerunning).")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_34 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 34u32, "2026-10-19T23:06Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:06 main: dice contract evidence after the consumer migration: Cells gate 7/7 including the rust -c NEAR contract target (helper lane 1, lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/contract-gate-20261019-1841.log, process compiler); the piped multi-line literal parse trap met during the migration is filed as SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL with a 6-line repro.")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_35 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 35u32, "2026-10-19T23:11Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:11 main: dice/ui proof down to Rust: build.ps1 regenerated src/main.rs from the migrated sources (tracked output, now modified: +7,498 -8,094 lines, which includes every compiler change since its last commit) and cargo check -p dice_ui --target wasm32-unknown-unknown passes with 0 errors (3 warnings).")
inl note_of_core_lib_consolidate_36 () = Note ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", 36u32, "2026-10-20T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 21:38 eoie on compiler pack 12 (single-flight-20261019-203828, accepted 21:34): dry run 168 owners, 0 fail, all byte-identical to the committed generated Rust (65463 lines); the alias-occurrence rename finds nothing more to collapse in eoie after pack 8b, so no regeneration or republish is needed.")
inl blog_showcase_post () = Task ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", "Replace the stalled giant blog post with a sample post that exercises every feature of the blog engine (every block kind, TOC, tabs, RSS) as typed Spiral data checked by test blocks; the real written posts live in the repos they describe, starting with UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST in the upstream fork (user 2026-10-18)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "blog", "current", "current")
inl note_of_blog_showcase_post_1 () = Note ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:49Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:49 main-thread: engine/showcase.spi replaces the pipeline post as the blog's only content. Article 'Every Block, Once' uses every post.block kind (Heading, Paragraph, Code, Terminal, Table, Note, Rule); a Release entry gives the Releases tab content. Tests: every block kind appears (block_tag matches all 7 cases, including the payload-less qualified post.Rule from today's parser fix), the release is labeled, and the site has a folder per showcase entry. pipeline was dropped from package.spiproj and scripts/build.ps1; the page.sample fixture lost the old post's slug and title (now sample / Sample Entry), with site, build and rss tests updated. Its stale prose ('blessed', 'Packs and blesses', fixed numbers) is exactly what made the giant post rot. engine/pipeline.spi is still on disk, unlisted: deleting it is the user's call. The blog build through Cells is running in the heavy gate")
inl note_of_blog_showcase_post_2 () = Note ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:35 helper-lane lap 1: gap against the title (every block kind, TOC, tabs, RSS): the showcase's own tests covered block kinds, the release label and site paths, while TOC, tabs and RSS were only tested on page.sample. Added three test blocks to blog engine/showcase.spi (uncommitted, unrun): the_article_has_a_table_of_contents (page.table_of_contents over the article contains On this page), the_feed_links_both_showcase_entries (rss.render_feed links /showcase/ and /showcase-release/), each_tab_keeps_one_showcase_entry (index.only Article and Release give 1 and 1). They ride on the already queued BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST blog full build (heavy-queue row), which now runs 7 showcase tests instead of 4.")
inl note_of_blog_showcase_post_3 () = Note ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: checked the showcase against the blog's X-compat rule (every block postable, under 280): all 52 string literals in engine/showcase.spi are under 200 characters, so each block fits one post. Three source lines exceed the formatter's 120-column rule (17: a 142-column Paragraph literal, 39: the 269-column release record update, 72: the 175-column site-paths assertion literal); 39 is the only one the formatter could split (a multi-line record update), the other two are single literals. Left as is until the queued build proves the 7 tests, so a layout change cannot mask a test failure.")
inl note_of_blog_showcase_post_4 () = Note ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:15 deterministic rule met: the full blog build (scripts/build.ps1: html, post, page, index, rss, style, site, showcase, build) passed every module at 10:00-10:04 (BLOG-OK, showcase 6/6), and nothing else references pipeline, so blog/engine/pipeline.spi is deleted in the tree (git status D, for the user's commit); the showcase post replaces it.")
inl note_of_blog_showcase_post_5 () = Note ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:51 helper-lane lap 3: formatter pass done after the green build: SpiralCompiler format --check reported would change for blog/engine/showcase.spi; formatted a copy, reviewed the diff (list brackets spaced, the 142-column Paragraph split into two literals joined by the parser's built-in ^^ StaticStringConcat, Table and release record update laid out over lines; no semantic edits) and copied it over (edit in blog/engine/showcase.spi, uncommitted). Bounded Cells run of showcase.spi afterwards: 6 discovered, 6 passed, 0 failed. Done: every block kind, table of contents, both tabs and the RSS feed are covered by the showcase's own test blocks, the full blog build passed at 10:00-10:04, and pipeline.spi is gone (user commit). Real posts continue under UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST.")
inl upstream_post_1_vanilla () = Task ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", "Upstream post 1 of a bite-sized series (user 2026-10-18): the vanilla start. One PR to mrakgr/master that consolidates his own unmerged work: host_cpp_and_cuda_backend as the language, Spiral-s-ML-Library corebase as core (judged per file, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE), plus only the bug fixes we found in his code. A fix qualifies when its regression sample fails on his compiler built from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend and passes with the patch; our features (new backends, matrix, Cells) wait for later posts. The post's tables (consolidated commits, core decisions, fixes with before and after) are generated from data", Story, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "blog,upstream,docs", "current", "current")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:52 main-thread candidate fixes to check against his compiler (from Done items and today's work; each needs its sample run on the upstream build): C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB, SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP (C closure strict-aliasing UB, today), SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (tokenizer), NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT, FOR-D-COMPILER-BUGS (six repros), PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM, the Infer shadowing fix and the payload-less qualified case (today), SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ (core). Features stay out even when they fix behavior: QUALIFIED-UNION-CASES, INLINE-DEPTH-GUARD (EJP0040 report), readable names, edition 3. First step: build upstream's The Spiral Language 2 from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend in a scratch worktree-free copy (git archive), then run each candidate's sample on it")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 2u32, "2026-10-18T20:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:53 main-thread step 1 scoped: host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (0cb0fda0) exported with git archive to the scratchpad (The Spiral Language 2 + VS Code Plugin/core). It targets net9.0, and the machine has only the .NET 11 rc SDKs, which can build net9 and run with roll-forward. It has no CLI: Supervisor.fs is the language server and builds through a BuildFile message. So the before-and-after proof needs a small headless driver: an F# script over the upstream DLL that opens the sample's package, waits for typecheck and prepass, and calls the backend codegen, the same steps BuildFile takes. The driver lives in spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA, and every candidate fix's sample runs on it once unpatched (must fail) and once with the patch exported by upstream.py (must pass)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 3u32, "2026-10-18T21:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:07 main-thread: headless driver written, spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/{driver/driver.fsproj, driver/Program.fs, build-driver.ps1}. build-driver.ps1 git-archives a ref of the upstream clone (default mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend) into a work dir, optionally applies a patch (an upstream.py export as a diff), and publishes a net9.0 driver (RollForward Major, runs on the cached .NET 11) that ProjectReferences the archived The Spiral Language 2. It lays out <work>/bin and <work>/core, because upstream resolves |core- as BaseDirectory/../core, so the core under test is chosen per build (the branch's own, or candidate v1). The driver starts supervisor_server and attention_server in-process, sends FileOpen and BuildFile, then waits for the output file's mtime to advance, or for a fatal or traced error. It prints UPSTREAM-BUILT/REJECTED/STALLED (exit 0/1/2). Upstream's backends are Fsharp, C, Cpp + Cuda and Python + Cuda, so post 1's before-and-after proofs run on those. First build queued in the heavy gate")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 4u32, "2026-10-18T21:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:22 main-thread tooling. (1) branch-commits.tsv: the 98 commits of host_cpp_and_cuda_backend since the merge base 850588a2 (2025-04-05), with hash, date, subject and files. (2) fixes.tsv: the candidates mapped to regression samples. C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB is out (our fix was -fwrapv in the build, not code). C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED gets a lab repro, repros/c_user_global: a global #define read through a macro, so a dropped global is a C build error, not just missing text. (3) make-patch.ps1 writes an anchored edit against the branch file as patches/<id>.diff and checks that it applies back. Done: C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED (4 lines) and C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT (3 lines; upstream zeroes only arrays of heap elements, init_at_zero = is_heap). (4) upstream.py export gained --until, so --since c~1 --until c carries one spiral commit onto the branch, and export-fix.ps1 turns that into a patch, refusing conflicts in the fix's own files. Its first use proved the guard: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (d94dc380) inserts beside method_key_of_tag, an earlier fork addition, so it needs a hand port. Order from here: run every candidate's sample on the unpatched upstream driver first; port only the fixes whose bug reproduces")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 5u32, "2026-10-18T21:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:55 main-thread: run-fixes.ps1 is the before-and-after judge. For each C row of fixes.tsv it copies the sample (or a lab repro) plus samples/core into <work>/runs/<label>, builds it with the upstream driver, compiles the C with pinned gcc 15.2 (-O2 -fwrapv), runs it, and compares the exit with our accepted baseline. Status: compile-fail, cc-fail, wrong-exit or ok, written to results-<label>.tsv. Patches so far: C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (a hand port: export-fix carried our p_var change but referenced closing_char, which upstream's p_var has no scope for, so the port threads closing_char through p_var and p_expr). Run-first rows, because upstream rewrote the code the fix touched: NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (Prepass now uses eval_type_nominal) and C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (depends on a fork-only method_key_of_tag). Lesson: an exported patch must be compile-checked against upstream; the export can carry a reference that doesn't exist there. The driver build (upstream net9 compile) holds heavy slot 2 now")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 6u32, "2026-10-18T22:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 18:43 main-thread FIRST BEFORE-RUN on his compiler (driver built from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend 0cb0fda0, his own core; results-before.tsv). Not reproduced, so out of post 1: SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP (UCRT -O2 exit 42), C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED (his C carries the global, exit 7), NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (his eval_type_nominal rewrite already fixed it). C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT needs a reproducing repro: fresh pages read zero, and number arrays are still left uninitialized by code reading. Reproduce, to confirm with patches: gadt_match_loses_constraint, multiline_type_application_dropped, nominal_union_case_pattern (type errors), match_multiline_scrutinee (main unparsed: the offside bug). Inconclusive (main unparsed, likely fork-only syntax in the sample): c_self_tail_loop_heap, macro_splice_before_quote; each needs an upstream-syntax repro. The rule is doing its job: three fixes we assumed were upstream bugs are not")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 7u32, "2026-10-18T23:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:15 main-thread: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT is proven by the emitted C. With repros/c_array_create_reused (fill a 16-element array inside a let function, which frees it, then create 16 and read [0]), his compiler emits ArrayCreate0(16l, false) and main reads ptr[0] before any write: an uninitialized read, undefined behavior. The Windows heap happened to return 0, so the exit code cannot show it. The post says exactly that. The parser fixes (FOR-D offside: continuation_column, commits d1cd44ca..6046588c; multiline type application; GADT constraint; nominal union pattern) do not export mechanically: the fork renamed the parser combinators in its single-file merge (blockParsingIndent, blockParsingRange versus upstream's indent, range), so upstream.py transfer reports conflicts on every touched line. They need hand ports into upstream BlockParsing.fs, Infer.fs and Prepass.fs. Parked behind the core migration (user: ASAP). Patches ready: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (the latter still needs an upstream-syntax repro)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 8u32, "2026-10-18T23:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:19 main-thread: offside fix hand-ported to upstream as patches/FOR-D-OFFSIDE.diff (BlockParsing: Env gains continuation_column : int option; the match parser records its keyword column and parses the scrutinee with it; application takes min(floor, col) as the offside line and clears it for nested terms) plus FOR-D-OFFSIDE-WDIFF.diff (WDiff's one Env construction sets continuation_column = None). Same three edits as our BlockParsing, written against upstream's indent/range names. Patched build (C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE, FOR-D-OFFSIDE pair) queued in the heavy gate as upstream-driver-after; its compile is the check that the patches type-check upstream, and run-fixes -Label after then gives the after column")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 9u32, "2026-10-18T23:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:34 main-thread fix list for post 1, deterministic per the rule (bug shown on his compiler, then the patch). Reproduce, patch ready: SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (his tokenizer reports the swallowed closing quote at line 4 col 26); FOR-D offside (match_multiline_scrutinee: main unparsed), FOR-D-TYPE-APPLY and GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT (type errors). All three are hand ports onto upstream's names: Env.continuation_column, the row's first-token column instead of the fork's layout_columns, and the var-var GADT arm with := and TypeErrorException. Proven by the emitted C: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT. Reproduces but deferred to a later post: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (upstream-syntax repro at -O0 dies 0xC00000FD; the port is ~147 lines tangled with refcounting). Out: SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP, C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (not reproduced or already fixed), C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB and PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM (not code fixes to his code). Open: nominal_union_case_pattern reproduces but its fork change is not yet identified (185a355f only adds folder index modules). Next: the after build with the 6 patches (queued for a quiet machine), then run-fixes -Label after")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 10u32, "2026-10-19T00:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 20:39 main-thread AFTER-RUN: his compiler (host_cpp_and_cuda_backend 0cb0fda0) with the 6 post-1 patches builds, so the hand ports type-check upstream. results-after.tsv beside results-before.tsv: SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE compile-fail -> exit 17 (accepted 17); GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT compile-fail -> 0; match_multiline_scrutinee (offside) compile-fail -> 0; multiline_type_application_dropped compile-fail -> 0; C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT emitted ArrayCreate0(16l, false) -> ArrayCreate0(16l, true). Every row we did not patch is unchanged: self-tail still overflows (post 2), the nominal pattern is our syntax, and the rest pass both ways. 5 proven fixes in fixes.tsv; post/vanilla_start.spi regenerates with them (98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 fixes). Left for post 1: the core half needs the v1 sweep (reaped, rerun when the machine is quiet); then the post's prose pass and its home in the fork (the user's commit/PR)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 11u32, "2026-10-19T09:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:55 make-post.ps1 fixes table now has Before and After columns parsed from fixes.tsv status (proven: before, X; after, Y), so each row shows both halves of the proof; post/vanilla_start.spi regenerated (5 proven fixes). Blog-side compile of the 5-column table pending a quiet machine.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 12u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: checked the blog side of the new 5-column fixes table: blog engine post.spi Table is list string * list (list string) rendered by cells over the head list, so any column count renders; no engine change needed Next: compile post/vanilla_start.spi inside the blog engine with the blog build (HEAVY QUEUE blog build) and eyeball the table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 13u32, "2026-10-19T13:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:20 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1's plan: the queued blog full build (BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST row) can never compile this post: blog/scripts/build.ps1 tests only the engine modules (html, post, page, index, rss, style, site, showcase, build), and post/vanilla_start.spi lives in this lab folder with ///- --package . while referring to page.entry and post.*, so it needs the engine as its package. New lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/post-gate.ps1: copies the engine .spi files and vanilla_start.spi into spiral-bin/runs/upstream-post-1-gate/<stamp>/engine, rewrites package.spiproj there (absolute packageDir to spiral/lib, module vanilla_start after showcase; throws when the anchors move), and runs the Cells gate on the copy (memo off, own MIX_BUILD_PATH); the blog tree stays untouched. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~4 min). Next: after the gate, render the page HTML from the copy (mix spiral.html or the engine's generate) to eyeball the 5-column fixes table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 14u32, "2026-10-19T13:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:58 lap 3 grain: fact-checked the post's branch section against git (The-Spiral-Language clone): merge-base of mrakgr/master and mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend is 850588a2, 98 commits on top, first 2025-02-01, last 2025-11-14, and CodegenCpp.fs is touched by 36 of them: all as stated. One wording fix: the table keeps only .fs files (make-post.ps1:15 filters on \\.fs$), while over all files CHANGELOG (36), base.spi (23), package.json (23) and package-lock.json (21) outrank most of the listed compiler files, so These are the files they touch most was inaccurate; now These are the compiler source files (.fs) they touch most, in make-post.ps1 and in the generated post/vanilla_start.spi (same text, one occurrence each). Next: the queued post-gate.ps1 compiles the post inside a blog engine copy; then render and read the fixes table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain: fact-checked the fixes table against the raw runs. Of fixes.tsv's 13 rows, the 5 the post calls proven match results-before/-after exactly: macro_splice_before_quote compile-fail -> exit 17, gadt_match_loses_constraint compile-fail -> 0, match_multiline_scrutinee compile-fail -> 0, multiline_type_application_dropped compile-fail -> 0, and c_array_create_reused from its own results-before-array.tsv (ArrayCreate0 false -> true). The out rows hold too: native_closure_recursive_capture exits 42 both before and after on his branch (not reproduced), nominal_phantom_parameter 0 both (already fixed upstream), nominal_union_case_pattern compile-fail both (fork syntax), ebpf_neq 0 both (core change shown as C text, not as a fix). The only proof not in a results file is C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (wrong-exit -1073741571 = stack overflow before; post 2's subject). Table data is publication-ready; the open step is still the queued post-gate compile")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_16 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:16 lap 5 grain: main's 10:57 post-gate counted as passed, but its summary says discovered 0, executed 0 (only the static prelude), so entry was type-checked at most and never evaluated or rendered. make-post.ps1 now generates 2 tests after the entry: the_post_is_a_page_of_the_site (site.files/paths holds vanilla-start/index.html) and the_post_renders_its_tables (page.render_entry has a table and the first proven fix id, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT; with no proven fixes it probes the placeholder note instead). Regenerated with -Date 2026-10-18: the old text is an unchanged prefix (22 core rows, 5 proven fixes). post-gate.ps1 now fails on a non-zero gate exit or when executed is not 2, and sets SPIRAL_LIB. Re-queued after the switch accept (the engine compiles against lib).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_17 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:33Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:33 lap 6 grain: the post's core table is generated from lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/decisions.tsv (22 rows, last written 10-18 18:38: take 9, take+extend 2, drop 6, keep 1, same 1, trim 1, move out 1, move out for now 1), and main is applying the switch now. The switch accept is therefore the point where the post's core section becomes a record of what landed instead of the plan. Step for after the accept: if decisions.tsv changed during the switch, rerun make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18 (the table regenerates, and the 2 render tests stay valid because they do not depend on the core rows), then the queued post-gate. Recorded, so the post does not ship the pre-switch plan.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_18 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 18u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7 grain: post-gate.ps1 now regenerates the post first (make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18) and then compiles that output, so the gate always tests what the generator writes from the current decisions.tsv, fixes.tsv and branch-commits.tsv, never a stale copy (this matters once the switch accept changes decisions.tsv). Parse-checked.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_19 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:29 lap 8 grain: dep CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is Done, so the post's core-decisions table can be final. Checked its input lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/decisions.tsv (10-18) against the shipped fork core (deps/The-Spiral-Language VS Code Plugin/core package.spiproj, 15 modules): 21 of 22 rows match; the types row said trim, but every alias in corebase/types.spi (tensor, partition, partition_offsets, serializer, pu, tuple_list) names a module that moved out, so the shipped core has no top-level types, only compile_time/types. Row corrected to drop with that reason. Next: post-gate.ps1 regenerates and compiles the post after main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_20 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:55 lap 9 grain: ran make-post.ps1 into the scratchpad (text generation only, no compile): 98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 proven fixes; against the stored post/vanilla_start.spi the only difference is the types row (trim -> drop with the corrected reason from lap 8). The post's tables are list literals ([...]) of strings, not ;[...] array literals, so the core v1 switch (array literals become array t) cannot break the post's compile; the blog engine's page/post modules were migrated by main. post-gate.ps1 regenerates and compiles after main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_21 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:30 lap 10 grain: the only dep, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE, is Done (100%, eoie lane 12:57: regen 168 owners 0 changed, eoie reinstalled), so nothing in the backlog blocks this story any more; the 12:55 scratch regeneration of make-post.ps1 already ran on the switched core (98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 proven fixes, only the corrected types row differs from the stored post). Left: main's go for post-gate.ps1 (the heavy proof of the 5 fixes), then store the regenerated post")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_22 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 22u32, "2026-10-19T17:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:50 lap 11 grain: post-gate.ps1 readiness re-checked after the switch: parses with 0 errors and every path it names exists (blog/engine + its package.spiproj, apps/cells, heavy.ps1, lib/spiral, post/vanilla_start.spi, make-post.ps1); it regenerates the post with -Date 2026-10-18 (the post's own date, intended). Ready to run on main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_23 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 23u32, "2026-10-19T22:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:59 post-gate.ps1 PASSED on the installed pack 10 compiler and the core v1 lib (bounded job job-1792450312484, 60 s of work after a 315 s slot wait): make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18 regenerated post/vanilla_start.spi from the current data (98 commits, 22 core rows incl. the corrected types row, 5 proven fixes with before and after), and inside a copy of blog/engine it compiled and ran both post tests, discovered 2, executed 2, passed 2, failed 0 (the post is a page of the site; the rendered entry has its tables and the first proven fix id). Blog tree only read. Everything on our side is done; what remains is outside the lanes: the user decides where the post lives (a blog/engine module, or the fork) and whether the PR is opened. Progress 90")
inl cells_accept_tuples () = Task ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", "Cells --accept rewrites only scalar literals and hides why it refused: a tuple _assert_eq (e.g. (a, b, c, d) |> _assert_eq (0i32, 0i32, 0i32, 0i32)) reports accepted 0 with no reason on the CLI (the reason sits in the report row's accept_refused)", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,tibia,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_1 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:54 found in the Tibia lane pinning pack_sim's hunter test: accept.ex @plain_value matches numbers, strings and booleans only, so tuples (the usual multi-claim pin in pack_sim) must be written by hand, and the run summary only says accepted 0. Two asks: (1) print accept_refused reasons in the spiral-tests summary; (2) accept flat tuples of plain literals, keeping each element's suffix (i32/u32). Nested tuples print flattened ((a, b), (c, d) shows as ((a, b), c, d)), so nested ones can stay refused with a reason.")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_2 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:54 helper-lane lap 1: ask (1) done in the tree, unrun: Cells notebook.ex summary_lines now puts an accept_refused map (test name -> refusal reason, from the report rows' accept_refused) into every spiral-tests file line, empty map for a file without tests, so a run that says accepted 0 also says why. New ExUnit case in notebook_report_test.exs (the summary names why --accept refused a test); the existing summary assertion matches by pattern, so the added key cannot break it. Ask (2) (flat tuples of plain literals) not started: accept.ex @plain_value/@site_literal accept scalars only, and the tuple text __assert_eq prints for (a, b) must be read from a real failing run before writing the rewrite rule. HEAVY QUEUE: mix test test/spiral/cells/notebook_report_test.exs in apps/cells (~5 min).")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_3 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:00 helper-lane lap 1: lap-1 pick, driven to code-complete: ask (2) implemented in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/accept.ex (uncommitted): plain/1 now returns a shape (scalar, or a flat tuple of unsuffixed plain literals parsed by tuple_items, which keeps quoted strings with commas intact); failing_site matches the site tuple (_assert_eq (...)), requires the site's element values to equal the printed expected ones, and pinned/3 writes the actual values back with each element's own suffix ((0i32, 0u32, quoted x-comma-y, false) -> (3i32, 4u32, quoted x-comma-z, true)); nested tuples are refused with a reason (they print flattened). Scalars behave exactly as before. Verified CPU-light: both files parse (Code.string_to_quoted), and a standalone elixir script loading accept.ex passes 7 checks (tuple rewrite, wrong tuple refused, nested refused, plus the four existing scalar/record/loop behaviours). New ExUnit case in accept_test.exs; README Accepting actual values updated (tuples + accept_refused in the summary line). Only the queued mix test run remains before Done.")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_4 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:52 helper-lane lap 3: partial live evidence for ask (1): the two Cells runs I just made (reactors.spi and testing.spi, process compiler, tree Cells) print spiral-tests file lines that already carry accept_refused: {} - so the summary_lines change compiled in the tree's Cells and renders the empty case; the refused case and the tuple rewrite still wait for the queued mix test (accept_test + notebook_report_test).")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_5 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:57 helper-lane lap 3: DONE with evidence: mix test over accept_test.exs, notebook_report_test.exs and notebook_targets_test.exs in apps/cells (bounded eoie heavy job; the first attempt timed out while compiling the test env's deps, the second finished in 69 s) reports 37 passed, 0 failures, including the new cases a flat tuple of plain literals is rewritten element by element, keeping each suffix (tuple rewrite, wrong-tuple refusal, nested-tuple refusal) and the summary names why --accept refused a test. Both asks of the tibia lane's report are met: (1) every spiral-tests file line carries accept_refused (test -> reason), (2) flat tuples of plain literals are accepted with per-element suffixes, nested ones refused with a reason. README updated. Uncommitted: accept.ex, notebook.ex, the two test files, README.md.")
inl note_of_cells_accept_tuples_6 () = Note ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:16 main heavy queue: mix test notebook_report_test.exs + accept_test.exs: 34 passed (10:14).")
inl spiral_literal_alias_specialization () = Task ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", "a record passed to a let function whose fields share one literal value (two \"\" or two 0u64) collapses into one dyn variable, so the join point gets a second specialization next to the recursive one", TaskKind, P3, D3, 80u32, Active, "", "spiral,codegen,size", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 1u32, "2026-10-18T21:36Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 17:36 found by the eoie lane on eoie_do: steps_run/block_run/files_run/rules_over were each emitted twice (1751 generated lines). The first call passed { report = \"\"; failures = \"\"; ... } and got a signature with one Rc<str> for both fields; the recursive calls pass distinct vars and got a second copy. Marking params ~ did not help. Workaround that removed ~380 lines: build the initial record in a non-inlined let constructor (let empty_tally () : tally = {...}). Fix candidate in the compiler: dyn each record field to its own variable at join points, or key the specialization on the type layout only, not on variable sharing.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:55Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: minimal repro outside eoie, on the installed compiler (lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION, try.ps1 Rust via Invoke-Heavy): let rec run over type tally = {report; failures; count}; the first call passes two equal empty-string literals, the recursive call passes {report = t.failures; failures = the literal y; ...}. shared_literal.spi emits two copies, run_0(i32, Rc str, i32) for the aliased first call and run_1(i32, Rc str, Rc str, i32) for the recursion (40 lines); distinct_literal.spi (first call with two different literals) emits one run_0 (28 lines); both exit 5. When the recursion keeps the two fields equal (a plain swap) the aliased signature is reused and there is one copy, so the cost appears exactly when the recursive arguments diverge. The split is decided at the join point by variable sharing, so the fix candidates in note 1 stand (dyn each field to its own variable at a let rec join point, or key specializations by layout only).")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:37 helper-lane lap 2: two connections found: (1) the shape is broader than literals - RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION's lap-2 probe shows pair_score a b and pair_score b b becoming two functions (pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v0)) with no literal involved, i.e. any call that passes one runtime variable in two parameter positions gets its own specialization; the literal case is just the commonest way to share a variable (equal literals are interned into one dyn var). (2) the reason is visible in the key type: join-point method keys are ConsedNode<RData [] * Ty [] * Ty> (spiral_compiler.fs ~82519 and ~128066), where the RData array is the renamed argument data, so a repeated variable is part of the key. Fix candidates restated precisely: rename the argument data positionally (each parameter slot gets its own variable in the key, aliases become copies at the call), or key on types only and pass duplicates explicitly; both shrink eoie_do and x's flattened-record functions.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:22 helper-lane lap 3: found the exact cause in single-flight: data_to_rdata (spiral_compiler.fs 158552) builds the join-point key by walking the call data through Utils.memoize over a reference-identity Dictionary, so a DV that appears in two parameter positions hits the memo and becomes the same ReV index (and rename_global_term, 158581, renames the body's parameters through the same kind of memo, so the body also sees one variable): a call passing one runtime variable twice gets its own key. Staged the positional fix as lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION/patch.py (two anchors, single-flight copies only, --check OK): in both walks a DV leaf bypasses the memo (each occurrence gets its own call_args slot and its own fresh body variable, in the same traversal order), while composite nodes stay memoized. The residual call then passes the same variable twice, which every backend accepts. run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate from a copy and compiles RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION's wildcard_pair.spi on C and Rust (expect exit 224 and one pair_score function instead of two). HEAVY QUEUE row added; the suite review afterwards measures the residual churn (fewer specializations wherever aliasing occurred).")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:52 helper-lane: pack 8b candidate review (main, single-flight-20261019-143937, DISAGREE 0) shows the positional rename's effect on the corpus: portable_composite (14 backends) now calls method7(v1, v1) and computes strcmp where the aliased key let the body fold x = x to true; native_string_utf8_canonical_equal_bounded_source loses about 634 C lines (aliasing-only specializations collapse); mega_omniledger_erp_kernel's F# rows change. Behavior identical everywhere. Trade-off made explicit: positional keys remove alias-driven duplicate specializations but also drop compile-time facts derived from aliasing (self-equality folds). Option if the fold matters: apply positional renaming only to variables that came from equal literals (the item's original case) and keep alias keys for ordinary variables.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:57 main: pack 8b reviewed clean on a Release candidate (single-flight-20261019-145110 vs the pack 7 accept: 27 residual cells, 0 behavior cells, DISAGREE 0); the positional rename owns all three residual changes: portable_composite passes one string twice to method7 and loses the compile-time self-equality fold (strcmp at run time, same result), native_string_utf8_canonical_equal_bounded_source C output -634 lines (alias-only specializations collapse), mega_omniledger F#. Decision: accept the trade (fewer specializations beat one alias fold, behavior identical); the literals-only scoping helper lane 1 offered stays an option if a fold-dependent sample appears. Installed and accepting 14:57.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T19:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:51 main: corpus check on the installed pack 8b compiler: Cells gates of lib sm.spi 43/43 (15:11), the Tibia corpus hunt_sim.spi 11/11 and gpu_sim.spi 6/6 (C and WGSL, 0 type errors, tibia tree unchanged), lab KINO process 4/4 and LEAN kernel 8/8; no consumer regression from the positional rename.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 8u32, "2026-10-19T19:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 15:56 helper-lane: positional renaming of DV leaves shipped in pack 8b (accepted 15:46) and changed the corpus as attributed (portable_composite, the utf8 canonical-equal sample, mega_omniledger F#), but the item's own probe is NOT fixed: on the installed pack 8b compiler, wildcard_pair.spi on Rust still has two functions, pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v0), called as pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v1). So the aliasing that keys pair_score b b is not created in data_to_rdata alone - most likely the curried let function takes its arguments in two application steps, or the free-variable collection dedupes by tag (data_free_vars / rdata_free_vars) after the rename. Next: trace which step builds the one-parameter key for the curried call. Not Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 9u32, "2026-10-19T20:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:46 main: consumer evidence from the eoie lane on pack 8b: regen of 168 owners, 22 changed and all smaller, 67,147 -> 65,435 generated lines (-1,712; agile_rename_rewrite 682 -> 426, predicate_literal_domain 389 -> 227, agile_todo_sources 186 -> 110), every eoie check green (one-call 34/34, e2e suites), eoie.exe republished 16:40; the duplicate-specialization merge is the effect the item wanted, while helper lane 1 traces why its own wildcard_pair probe still splits.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 10u32, "2026-10-19T22:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:14 helper-lane lap 6: two more probes on the installed pack 10 compiler (Rust, try.ps1), in lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION. swap_pair.spi (pair_score a b + pair_score b a) gives one function pair_score_0(v0, v1) called both ways, so the positional rename is active. alias_only.spi (pair_score b b + pair_score a a) gives one function pair_score_0(v0) with one parameter. So an aliased call keys with one variable, while data_to_rdata (single-flight 158620) gives every DV leaf its own index, even when the same DV object repeats. The dedupe therefore happens before data_to_rdata, in what env_global_term holds for that join point: one free variable, not two. Likely the curried let compiles as nested functions, and the inner join point's environment is built from a structure where a and b are already one value. Next: print scope.term.free_vars and env_global_term for pair_score on a private Debug candidate.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 11u32, "2026-10-19T23:08Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:08 helper-lane lap 7: ROOT CAUSE and fix. A lab-only debug print on a private Debug candidate (debug_patch.py, debug-run.ps1, JP-DEBUG) showed pair_score b b reaches the join point with free=2 but call_args=1. The env values are not bare DV leaves: a union variable is DNominal(DV, light), and the same DNominal object sits in both slots. Pack 8b's positional rename bypassed the memo only for bare DV, so the shared wrapper hit data_to_rdata's reference memo and its leaf was counted once. rename_global_term had the same gap. Fix: lab/SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION/occurrence_patch.py (--apply gated, dry run OK on today's tree). A memoized data_has_var check lets both functions memoize only variable-free subtrees and walk any subtree with a variable per occurrence, so each occurrence gets its own index while closed compile-time values keep their sharing. On a private-cache Debug candidate (occurrence-run.ps1, 0 errors), all three probes compile to one pair_score_0(v0, v1): wildcard_pair (calls (v0,v1) and (v1,v1)), alias_only and swap_pair. They run on C and Rust with the predicted values 224, 303 and 44 (101/202/22 per pair). Offered to main for the next pack; the corpus review decides.")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 12u32, "2026-10-20T01:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 21:34 main: pack 12 ACCEPTED 21:34 (single-flight-20261019-203828: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 202140; residual changes only 2 F# Brzozowski mega rows) with occurrence_patch.py: shared nominal wrappers no longer collapse aliased arguments, so wildcard_pair, alias_only and swap_pair each compile to one specialization (helper lane 1 probe values 224, 303, 44).")
inl note_of_spiral_literal_alias_specialization_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", 13u32, "2026-10-20T01:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 21:38 main: eoie lane consumer check on pack 12: all 168 owners compile and are byte-identical to the checked-in Rust (65,463 lines), so the occurrence rename changes nothing in eoie; pack 8b had already merged its duplicate specializations, and pack 12's effect is limited to the 2 F# Brzozowski mega rows plus the item's own probes.")
inl upstream_post_2_c_self_tail () = Task ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", "Upstream post 2 (bite-sized series after UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA): hand-port C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (fork commit d94dc380, ~147 CodegenC lines plus its fork-only method_key_of_tag) to upstream CodegenC, so a let rec self tail call becomes a while loop instead of C recursion. Reproduced 2026-10-18 on his compiler with spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/repros/c_self_tail_loop_heap: -O0 dies 0xC00000FD after 1M self calls, accepted exit 24. Same before-and-after proof as post 1", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", "blog,upstream,docs", "current", "current")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T23:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:39 main-thread: patch drafted early, spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/patches/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff (154 changed lines; applies to host_cpp_and_cuda_backend). upstream.py export --since d94dc380~1 --until d94dc380 carried everything but one insertion (CONFLICT at method_key_of_tag.[i] <- box (jp_body, key), a fork-only registration). The patch adds that line at the top of upstream's method_templ, so is_method_tag sees the same (jp_body, key) pairs. Not compiled yet: it gets its own build after post 1's, so a failure can't block post 1's patches")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:37 helper-lane lap 1: found a stacking conflict, checked with git apply --cached on a throwaway index of The-Spiral-Language at mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (no worktree or branch touched): the drafted lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/patches/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff applies alone, but on top of post 1's patches it fails at CodegenC.fs:470, because it carries post 1's own C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT hunk (TyArrayCreate init true) verbatim. Wrote lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff = the same patch minus that hunk: after C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, FOR-D-OFFSIDE, FOR-D-TYPE-APPLY, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE it applies cleanly (rc 0; the stack touches CodegenC, BlockParsing, Infer, Tokenize: 190 insertions, 13 deletions). Post 1's patch dir stays as is (main's).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:22 helper-lane lap 2: prepared the before/after proof so post 2 does not wait for tooling: lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/run.ps1 reuses post 1's own scripts in process (build-driver.ps1 -Patch with post 1's five patches plus the rebased C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff, into spiral-bin/upstream-post-2; then run-fixes.ps1 -Only C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP plus three post-1 rows as a guard), and moves results-after-post-2.tsv into this item's lab folder. Called with & instead of pwsh -File because -File would collapse the -Patch and -Only arrays into one string. Post 1's results-after.tsv still shows C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP wrong-exit -1073741571 (stack overflow) vs expected 24, which is what this run should flip. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/run.ps1 (~15 min).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:52 helper-lane lap 3: pre-checked the queued run's riskiest step without building: a git archive of mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (0cb0fda0, 2025-11-14, the same ref build-driver.ps1 uses) with post 1's five patches applied in order and then the rebased C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff: all six git apply calls exit 0 (scratch tree, removed afterwards). So the queued run.ps1 cannot fail at its patch step; what it proves is the build and the exit-24 flip. Post 1 (UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST) is still Planned 0%, so the post 2 text waits for post 1 either way.")
inl wgsl_run_wgpu_30 () = Task ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", "Upgrade spiral-wgsl (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run) from wgpu 24 / pollster 0.4 to wgpu 30+, the only wgpu pin in our repos; rerun the matrix Wgsl rows and the tibia GPU twins (gpu_sim, hunt_sim) on lavapipe and the local NVIDIA", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,deps,tibia", "current", "current")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_1 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 1u32, "2026-10-19T06:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 02:12 user direction: prioritize updating wgpu to 30+ everywhere. Survey: the only wgpu dependency in our repos is spiral's wgsl-run shim (wgpu = '24', Cargo.lock 24.0.5). Its consumers: the matrix Wgsl rows (20), Cells ///> wgsl tests, tibia scripts/init.ps1 (deps/spiral/scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1 into target/wgsl-run) and the tibia CI cache. zed pins wgpu 29 in its own workspace, which is third-party code, not ours. The shim lives in native-shims, which the compiler lane keeps fixed between a review and its accept, and the native memo keys on it, so the switch lands as a reviewed pack. Port first in a lab copy (spiral/lab/WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30).")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_2 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 2u32, "2026-10-19T07:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 03:36 port done in spiral/lab/WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30 (Cargo.toml wgpu 30, rust-toolchain.toml nightly-2026-10-18, src/main.rs 16 lines). Nine API spots: poll(PollType) returns a Result; Instance::new takes InstanceDescriptor::new_without_display_handle_from_env() by value; initialize_adapter_from_env is async and returns a Result; request_adapter returns a Result; request_device takes no trace argument; push_error_scope returns a guard to pop(); PipelineLayoutDescriptor takes Option layouts and immediate_size; max_storage_buffer_binding_size is u64; get_mapped_range returns a Result. wgpu 30 needs rustc 1.90 or newer (ordered-float 5.5), so the default nightly-2025-05-09 (1.88) can't build it. Release build at -j 1: 60 min for deps, 2 min for the crate, no warnings. compare.ps1: all 51 committed sample shaders give the same exit code on wgpu 24 and 30 (DX12 + DXC, about 4 s each, mostly adapter startup), table in compare.tsv. The swap into native-shims is main's reviewed pack after the core switch (Wgsl rows, native memo, toolchain stamp, AGENTS.md text). tibia gpu_sim and hunt_sim on the new runner: running.")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_3 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 3u32, "2026-10-19T09:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:43 main-thread: staged for pack 5 (with RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE's matrix and Cells pin 1.99.0 -> nightly-2026-10-18). The tibia lane's port is copied into apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run: Cargo.toml (wgpu 30, pollster 0.4), Cargo.lock, rust-toolchain.toml (nightly-2026-10-18, minimal) and src/main.rs (9 API spots, 15 lines). The AGENTS.md wgpu 24 mention is updated. Their evidence: 51 committed WGSL shaders give the same exit codes on both runners. Next: rebuild the runner into the cache toolchains (init-wgsl-run.ps1), then pack 5 -Native review and accept (Wgsl and Rust rows rerun; toolchain stamp and native memo change)")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_4 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 4u32, "2026-10-19T09:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 05:59 GPU twins on the wgpu 30 lab runner (one Cells job, port 13872, main's build paused): tibia gpu_sim 6/6 passed (171 s), hunt_sim 11/11 (79 s), Vulkan Quadro K1100M. Same two big gpu_sim shaders (2465375 and 1471131 bytes) vs the wgpu 24 run of 2026-10-18 17:16 (target/ci-metrics/wgsl-gpu_sim.log): compile 57.1 s vs 94.0 s and 52.7 s vs 83.5 s, run 2.13 s vs 0.97 s and 0.51 s vs 0.57 s; machine load differed, indicative only. With the 51/51 sample exit-code match the port is ready for main's pack 5 (copy Cargo.toml, src/main.rs, rust-toolchain.toml into native-shims/wgsl-run, init-wgsl-run.ps1, AGENTS.md wgpu line). Two earlier attempts were reaped for low memory next to main's nightly build.")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_5 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 5u32, "2026-10-19T09:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:59 tibia lane evidence on the lab wgpu 30 runner (Vulkan, Quadro K1100M): gpu_sim 6/6 in 171 s (two big shaders compile 57.1 s and 52.7 s), hunt_sim 11/11 in 79 s, plus the earlier 51/51 sample exit-code match. Pack 5 takes it after the nightly verification job.")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_6 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: pack 5 review on the rebuilt cache runner (wgpu 30, nightly-2026-10-18): Wgsl 50 rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0, toolchain differs only for WgslRun as expected; accept running with -AcceptToolchain Next: after the accept lands: Done (lab/WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30 then deleted, its port already copied into tests/native-shims/wgsl-run)")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_7 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:55 done: pack 5 accepted (single-flight-20261019-080524, DISAGREE 0, Wgsl 50 agree) with the wgpu 30 runner in tests/native-shims/wgsl-run and the cache toolchain rebuilt at 07:23; lab/WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30 holds the tibia lane's port and comparison, now redundant (left for its owner to remove).")
inl note_of_wgsl_run_wgpu_30_8 () = Note ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:21 lap 2 cleanup check: lab/WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30 (untracked) is redundant: src/main.rs and Cargo.toml are byte-identical to apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run (git diff --no-index empty), and no script, notebook or workflow in any repo references the folder (grep: only the laps ledger and agile text). Its only unique content is compare.ps1/compare.tsv, the wgpu 24 vs 30 comparison already summarized in this item's notes. Left in place per the 08:55 note (the tibia lane owns it); it can be removed without risk")
inl rust_nightly_everywhere () = Task ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", "Every Rust build in every repo on one recent dated nightly (user 2026-10-19 via the tibia lane: attempt rust nightly for everything, pin a recent date as default, lower only where it still does not work; all repos). Default nightly-2026-10-18 (rustc 1.101, installed cleanly by the tibia lane; wgpu 30 needs >= 1.90). Scope: rust-toolchain.toml files, cargo +X calls in scripts, Kino ensure_rust_toolchain (+1.99.0), the matrix native rustc, CI workflows; install once before parallel jobs (rustup is not concurrency-safe). Every fallback to an older date gets a recorded reason", Feature, P1, D5, 70u32, Active, "", "rust,toolchain,ci", "current", "current")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_1 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T06:37Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 02:37 main-thread inventory + first pass. Pins found across the 10 repos: nightly-2025-11-01 (app and tool builds: spiral apps wasm/spiral/plot/lisp, lib rust testing.spi, parsing bench, dir-tree-html, dice lib, alphabet documents, polyglot math), nightly-2025-05-09 (machine default and Kino wasm default in spiral and polyglot init.ps1), nightly-2024-07-14 (NEAR contracts: dice contract, polyglot chat contract, apps/spiral contract build), 1.99.0 (compiler matrix Rust rows and the Cells Rust host), x rust-toolchain.toml channel stable. The tibia lane's share is done (rust-toolchain.toml, gh-pages.yml, apps/map). Applied with lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/apply.ps1: 23 pins in 15 files -> nightly-2026-10-18, NEAR excluded (apply.ps1 -Near later; newer rustc emits wasm features the NEAR VM may reject, which would be the recorded fallback). The new toolchain already has clippy, rust-src, rustfmt and wasm32. Verified: dir-tree-html RUST-OK in 39 s. verify.ps1 (7 more builds, serial) running. Next: the matrix and Cells 1.99.0 pin as a reviewed matrix pack, then NEAR, then rustup default")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_2 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T09:54Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:54 paused the verify rerun at the tibia lane's request (GPU twins at one job, hand-back by 06:45). Done so far on nightly-2026-10-18: spiral apps/lisp, apps/plot pass. apps/wasm got past openssl-sys and stopped in near-workspaces; resume with verify.ps1 -Skip 'spiral apps/lisp','spiral apps/plot' -Jobs 2 as an eoie job.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_3 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T10:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:02 paused again for the tibia lane's PR-14 live block (06:05-06:30). verify.ps1 now splits -Skip on commas, because pwsh -File (as eoie job start runs it) cannot take an array (the first eoie job bound 'spiral apps/plot' to -Out). Resume: eoie job start rust-nightly-verify 5400000 pwsh -NoProfile -File verify.ps1 -Skip 'spiral apps/lisp,spiral apps/plot' -Jobs 2.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_4 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T10:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:05 CI pins: gh-pages.yml setup-rust channel set to nightly-2026-10-18 in alphabet, dice, dir-tree-html, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral (were nightly-2025-05-09 or 2025-11-01) and blog (was stable; its Rust comes through Cells, which now runs +nightly-2026-10-18), so the toolchain the build scripts name exists on the runner. Only with: changed, no uses:, so actions.lock is untouched. Left to their lanes: x ci.yml says stable but x has rust-toolchain.toml nightly-2026-10-18 (x lane); eoie gh-pages.yml pins 1.88.0 (eoie lane, needs an eoie build on the nightly first). Tibia already on nightly-2026-10-18.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_5 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T10:13Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:13 eoie lane: eoie on nightly-2026-10-18 (rustc 1.101.0-nightly 8006091eb 2026-10-17): cargo check --locked --workspace --all-targets in a fresh target folder checked 198 crates, 0 errors, 8 warnings (unused dependencies plus one manifest warning in patch-domain), 3m29s. eoie .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml now pins channel nightly-2026-10-18 (was 1.88.0); uncommitted for the user. Not run locally on nightly: codegen and the test run (CI covers them on the next push). Local default toolchain is still nightly-2025-05-09; tonight's releases were built with it.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_6 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:05 verification complete on nightly-2026-10-18, every build exit 0: dir-tree-html, spiral apps/lisp, apps/plot, apps/wasm (144 s), apps/spiral (283 s), dice lib (201 s), alphabet apps/documents (282 s), polyglot lib/math (154 s); no build needed a lower date. Pack 5 review single-flight-20261019-072348 (matrix rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, wgpu 30 runner): 7,007 rows, native memo replayed 0 (all 6,508 native rows ran fresh, so every Rust row compiled on the nightly), differing cells 0, DISAGREE 0. Accept running as eoie job pack5-accept. Remaining: NEAR pins (apply.ps1 -Near, contract builds) and Cells test.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_7 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: Linux rustix break reproduced on Windows with cargo check --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p rustix@0.37.28 (rust-std for linux added to nightly-2026-10-18); fixed without lowering the date via rustix's own libc backend cfg: .cargo/config.toml with [target.'cfg(target_os = linux)'] rustflags --cfg rustix_use_libc in spiral/workspace, dice and polyglot/apps/chat (linux-only, Windows and wasm untouched); the check then passes Next: cargo check --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p spiral_wasm and dice_contract_tests with the config (heavy, after pack 5 accept); then NEAR pins")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_8 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:55 pack 5 ACCEPTED: single-flight-20261019-080524 accepted 7,007 rows into the baseline (matrix rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, wgpu 30 runner, -AcceptToolchain), native 6,499 ran, oracle agree 5,779, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the review 072348, C rows vs the pack 4 toolchain run 0 of 531 differ.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_9 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:05 the whole-tree Linux cargo check from Windows is dropped: cargo check still runs build scripts, and spiral_wasm's vendored openssl-sys compiles C for the target, which needs a Linux cross compiler; the rustix check (the only nightly break seen) passed locally with the cfg, and the user's next push gives the full Linux verdict on CI.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_10 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:22 lap 2 grain: pin inventory across the in-scope repos (grep of rust-toolchain.toml, cargo/rustc/rustup +toolchain calls, CI setup steps): on nightly-2026-10-18 already: tibia and x rust-toolchain.toml, wgsl-run shim, matrix rustc (test.ps1:710), Cells @rust_toolchain (cells.ex:863), spiral apps wasm/spiral/plot/lisp builds, spiral init trunk install, dice lib, polyglot lib/math, alphabet documents, dir-tree-html, eoie CI (moonrepo/setup-rust channel nightly-2026-10-18). Only remaining older pin: the NEAR contract path, dice/contract/build.ps1:50-51 (+nightly-2024-07-14 build and metadata) plus polyglot/scripts/init.ps1:36-37 installing that toolchain for it. Recorded reason for the fallback: since rustc 1.82 wasm32-unknown-unknown enables reference-types and multivalue by default and the precompiled std carries them, which the nearcore 1.79 VM used by the dice sandbox tests rejects. Candidate lift: nightly-2026-10-18 with -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and RUSTFLAGS -C target-cpu=mvp. Prepared lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/near-mvp.ps1 (own CARGO_TARGET_DIR under spiral-bin/runs, then reads the wasm target_features section for +reference-types/+multivalue/+bulk-memory). HEAVY QUEUE row added (~10 min). Next: if the wasm is mvp-clean, switch dice/contract/build.ps1 (and alphabet's mirror if any) to the dated nightly with build-std, drop the 2024-07-14 install from polyglot init, and let the next push's Linux sandbox test decide")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_11 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:58 lap 3 grain: CI side verified: all 11 moonrepo/setup-rust steps across the 10 repos' workflows (spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, blog, dir-tree-html, x twice, eoie, tibia, i574n/.github) set channel nightly-2026-10-18. The one CI path without a setup-rust step is spiral's compiler job (schedule/dispatch only), whose test.ps1 calls rustc +<toolchain> from 2 parallel workers: in today's first Linux run (39258592236) rustup auto-installed +1.99.0 concurrently and 5 array_* Rust rows build-failed (could not rename downloaded component). Fixed in the tree (CI-OFFLOAD note 5): scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler reads the matrix toolchain from test.ps1 and runs rustup toolchain install once before the suite, so the nightly switch in test.ps1 cannot reintroduce the race. Remaining pin: the NEAR contract (near-mvp.ps1 queued; TODO block at dice/contract/build.ps1:50). Progress 70")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_12 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain: audited the other auto-install path for the CI race found in lap 3. Cells (cells.ex:863-899) pins +nightly-2026-10-18 and installs it through ensure_rust_toolchain, which serializes inside one BEAM with :global.trans plus a persistent_term ready flag, so its parallel cells cannot race rustup; two separate BEAMs (two mix runs at once, or the daemon plus a gate) still could on a machine without the toolchain, which only CI hits, and CI's Cells steps run sequentially in one workflow step. test.ps1's matrix rustc is the other parallel caller: covered on CI by the workflow.ps1 pre-install (lap 3), and on this laptop the toolchain is installed. No further change needed; remaining work is only the NEAR pin")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_13 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 near-mvp 10:59 got past build-std and failed at rust-lld: undefined panic_utf8, storage_write, ... This nightly no longer passes --allow-undefined for wasm32-unknown-unknown, and the dice lock pins near-sys 0.2.2 whose extern block has no wasm_import_module; near-sys 0.2.14 (in the local registry) has cfg_attr(wasm32, link(wasm_import_module = env)). near-mvp.ps1 now builds twice: with -C link-arg=--allow-undefined, then with near-sys 0.2.14 in a Cargo.lock copy via -Z lockfile-path (dice lock untouched). Re-queued (12 min). If variant 2 passes, the dice fix is a lock-only near-sys bump plus the toolchain swap in contract/build.ps1 (alphabet mirrors).")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_14 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:09Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 11:09 NEAR contracts on nightly-2026-10-18: a plain dice_contract wasm build fails at rust-lld (undefined NEAR host symbols panic_utf8, storage_remove: this nightly no longer passes --allow-undefined, and dice locks near-sys 0.2.2 without wasm_import_module, per helper 2); with RUSTFLAGS -C link-arg=--allow-undefined it builds (265,665 B, 58 s) and with build-std + target-cpu=mvp + allow-undefined too (variant 1 of near-mvp.ps1, 1m28s). Variant 2 tests the near-sys 0.2.14 bump alone; the lab run hit cargo's --lockfile-path unstable flag (cargo +nightly needs -Z unstable-options before update; helper 2 owns the script). Next: the sandbox test on Linux CI decides whether mvp is needed; then flip the NEAR pins (apply.ps1 -Near) with either the link arg or the near-sys bump.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_15 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T15:10Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:10 main ran near-mvp.ps1 once more: variant 1 (nightly-2026-10-18, build-std, target-cpu=mvp, -C link-arg=--allow-undefined) BUILT in 1m28s, so the toolchain path works with one extra link arg. Variant 2 failed at cargo update: --lockfile-path is gone as a CLI flag on cargo 1.101.0-nightly (stabilized in 1.97 as the config key resolver.lockfile-path, probe warning confirms). Script now passes --config resolver.lockfile-path=<copy> to update (offline) and build, and checks the copy pins near-sys 0.2.14. Re-queued after the switch window. dice Cargo.lock verified unchanged.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_16 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:34 lap 6 grain: swept all repos for nightly pins so lifting the last one is a single change list. Every nightly in our ps1/toml/yml files is nightly-2026-10-18 (dice lib, dice, blog, dir-tree-html, eoie and alphabet workflows, alphabet documents, polyglot init), with these exceptions: (1) dice/contract/build.ps1:53-54 (cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build and metadata), the pin this item is about; (2) polyglot/scripts/init.ps1:35-37, which installs nightly-2024-07-14 with the wasm32 target, clippy, rust-src and rustfmt only to serve (1), so it goes in the same change; (3) left alone: fc1943s.github's workflow (nightly-2024-10-07, the fc1943s backup repos are kept as they are), rustix and zed (third-party clones). Once near-mvp variant 2 builds, the change is: near-sys 0.2.14 in dice's Cargo.lock, the toolchain plus -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and RUSTFLAGS -C target-cpu=mvp in contract/build.ps1, and polyglot init minus 3 lines. alphabet has no contract (checked), so there is nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_17 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:50 lap 7 grain: (1) the lift is staged as lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/lift-pin.py (dry run: both anchors match once, LF files). It writes dice/contract/build.ps1 build + metadata on nightly-2026-10-18 with -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and CARGO_TARGET_WASM32_UNKNOWN_UNKNOWN_RUSTFLAGS=-C target-cpu=mvp (target-scoped, so dice's .cargo linux rustflags for host build scripts are not overridden the way a global RUSTFLAGS would), drops the TODO block, and removes polyglot init's 3 nightly-2024-07-14 lines. Plus the lock bump: cargo update -p near-sys --precise 0.2.14. (2) near-mvp.ps1 variant 2 now uses exactly those target-scoped flags. (3) The feature check was weak: the linked wasm need not keep a target_features section (and -s strips more), so 'none' could be a false all-clear. Each variant now also runs wasmtime compile -W bulk-memory=n,reference-types=n,multi-value=n on the result (the prebuilt 2024 dist/dice.wasm passes it: exit 0), which validates the code itself without those features.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_18 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T16:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:30 lap 8 grain: checked what the staged lift needs beyond its own edits: dice gh-pages.yml already installs nightly-2026-10-18 with components clippy,rust-src,rustfmt and the wasm32-unknown-unknown target, so -Z build-std has rust-src in CI once polyglot init stops installing nightly-2024-07-14; locally rust-src and rust-std-wasm32 are installed on that nightly. alphabet (mirrors dice) has no contract and is already on nightly-2026-10-18 in CI and apps/documents/build.ps1, so the lift needs no alphabet edit. The only 2024 pins left in dice are contract/build.ps1:53-54, which lift-pin.py covers. Next: near-mvp variant 2 after main's go, then lift-pin --apply and the near-sys 0.2.14 lock bump")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_19 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:55 lap 9 grain: lift-pin.py still matches both anchors once (dice contract/build.ps1, polyglot scripts/init.ps1). Checked the lock bump it relies on: dice/contract pins near-sdk =5.11.0 and crates.io lists that version's near-sys requirement as ^0.2.2, so cargo update -p near-sys --precise 0.2.14 (the latest near-sys, the one with the newer wasm handling) stays inside the sdk's requirement and needs no Cargo.toml edit; Cargo.lock has near-sys 0.2.2 today. Separate finding for latest-of-everything: near-sdk itself is 5.11.0 against 5.29.1 on crates.io; that bump is its own step after the nightly lift (contract API changes possible)")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_20 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T17:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:30 lap 10 grain: the separate near-sdk bump sized from crates.io: latest stable 5.29.1 (2026-09-17, rust_version 1.93, a nightly satisfies it) requires near-sys ^0.2.14, borsh ^1.0.0, near-account-id ^2.3.0; so the near-sys 0.2.14 lock bump staged for this item is exactly what 5.29.1 needs, and the two bumps can land as one cargo update -p near-sdk --precise 5.29.1 (pulls near-sys 0.2.14) after dice's pin =5.11.0 is relaxed to 5.29.1. 5.30.0-rc.1 (today, rust 1.95) is a pre-release: skip. Next: near-mvp v2 after main's go, then lift-pin --apply and the combined sdk + sys bump")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_21 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:50 lap 11 grain: the near-sdk pin =5.11.0 sits in 4 manifests, so the bump is one change across 3 repos: spiral apps/wasm/Cargo.toml:21, dice contract/Cargo.toml:16 (pinned 2025-10-25 in 4e107a1, commit message only 'tests', no stated reason), polyglot apps/chat/contract/Cargo.toml:14 and its tests/Cargo.toml:12; alphabet has none (nothing to mirror). Risk to check in near-mvp v2: newer near-sdk releases target wasm32v1-none-era toolchains, so the contract build flags may need a look, not just the lock. Next: near-mvp v2 after main's go, then the combined sdk 5.29.1 + near-sys 0.2.14 bump in all 4 manifests")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_22 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T18:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:16 lap 12: near-sdk's latest release 5.29.1 (2026-09-17, rust_version 1.93) requires near-sys ^0.2.14, which is exactly one of the two unblocks the dice contract/build.ps1 TODO names (wasm_import_module env); today's locks hold near-sdk 5.11.0 with near-sys 0.2.5 (spiral workspace) and 0.2.2 (dice). So the latest-of-everything bump of the 4 near-sdk pins should also lift the last nightly pin; near-mvp v2 can test the bump instead of a separate near-sys lock")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_23 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T18:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:34 lap 13: the nearcore that rejects post-1.82 wasm comes from the contract test harness: dice and polyglot contract/tests pin near-workspaces =0.11.1 (old sandbox), while near-workspaces 0.23.0 (released 2026-10-19) and near-sandbox 0.3.16 (2026-10-14) are current. Hypothesis for near-mvp v2: bump near-sdk to 5.29.1 and near-workspaces to 0.23.0 together and build on the plain nightly, which may remove the build-std/mvp workaround instead of locking near-sys")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_24 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T23:05Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:05 lap 20 grain, latest-of-everything recheck from the crates.io API (max_stable_version): near-sdk 5.29.1 (published 2026-09-17) is still the newest stable, newest overall 5.30.0-rc.1; near-workspaces 0.23.0 stable, but published 2026-07-09, not 2026-10-19 as the 14:34 note said, newest 0.24.0-rc.2; near-sandbox 0.3.16 stable published 2026-09-04 (not 10-14), newest 0.4.0-rc.2. So near-sdk-bump.ps1's default 5.29.1 is right; the release candidates are not releases and stay out. Next: the near-sdk-bump row (beyond the 2-minute cap, main's queue)")
inl cells_everywhere () = Task ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", "Every Spiral source in every repo runs under Cells (user 2026-10-19 02:19: 100% of the code should eventually become cell compatible, split destructively, coordinate with the main lane without clogging it). Cell compatible = the file opens with a ///- --package line and a static prelude test, every module has test blocks with explicit ///> targets that agree, Rust-only primitives sit behind backend_switch (or a thin crate-glue module) so the domain compiles to more than one backend, and run_notebook.ps1 --path file passes; crates keep their !!!!Export entry. Per repo children: X-CELLS-SPLIT (x lane), eoie via EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION (eoie lane), lib/spiral and compiler samples (main lane, only when idle), tibia already follows it", Epic, P1, D7, 5u32, Active, "", "cells,spiral,x,eoie,tibia,lib", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: lib/spiral baseline: 81 .spi files, 40 carry test blocks (401 tests), 41 have none; none open with a ///- --package line because lib is itself the package (package.spiproj), so for lib the criterion is test blocks with explicit ///> targets per module, not the prelude line Next: list the 41 lib files without tests by size, smallest first, as the lib slice's work queue")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:24 lap 2 grain: lib slice work queue, recounted excluding the git-ignored bench/parsing/target copies (otherwise 199 files): 81 files, 41 without test blocks. By kind: 5 declaration-only operator/prototype files (file_system_operators 1, optionm'_operators 1, rust_operators 2, sm'_prototypes 4, iter_prototypes 8, am'_prototypes 10; sm'_operators 29); 19 leptos ui/svg view components plus leptos.spi 893 (browser views: static compile tests at most); 3 bench/parsing (main 14, pure_split 22, bench_kit 157, fparsec 201); browser bindings store 46 and wasm 132; real logic: mapm 87, json_scan 176 (helper lane 1's CONVO-IN-SPIRAL is rewriting it, so not here), backend 248, state_core 591. First slice drafted on mapm: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/mapm_test_block.spi (///> rust, from_array -> get and hash_map_count, expects (Some 2, 2)) and mapm-run.ps1 (inserts it before from_list, gates twice: as-is, then with hash_map_count fixed). Found by reading: hash_map_count is .count() on a std HashMap, which has no count method (E0599 expected; .len() as i32 is the fix), and it has 0 callers in any repo, so it never compiled. The script keeps the test + fix in lib only when the fixed gate passes, else restores. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~6 min). Next: after the run, next slice backend.spi (pure dispatch helpers) test blocks")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_3 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:00 lap 3 grain: second slice drafted on backend.spi (248 lines, no tests). Found by reading: is_rust's backend_switch record has 14 keys and lacks C, Wasm and Bend, while is_zig lists all 17, so is_rust () reached from a C/Wasm/Bend program falls to the no-arm fallback (a build failure there, per the BackendSwitch rule that a missing arm surfaces in the target compiler); it is called from testing.spi:283, runtime, resultm, threading, wasm and rust/testing. Test block lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/backend_test_block.spi (///> rust, zig, c, fsharp, gleam): is_rust/is_zig must agree with run_target's own dispatch (1 for Rust, 2 for Zig, 0 elsewhere). lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/backend-run.ps1 inserts it before indent, gates twice (as-is, expected to fail on c; then with C/Wasm/Bend = false added to is_rust), keeps test + fix only when the fixed gate passes, else restores; then the lib-edit rule applies (apps/spiral build + lib corpus before landing). HEAVY QUEUE row added (~6 min). Next: after mapm and backend, slice 3 = state_core.spi (591 lines, pure model code)")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_4 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain, criterion for the lib slice decided: state_core.spi (591 lines) is not pure model code as planned in lap 3; it opens rust, leptos and wasm and is IndexedDB (rexie) plus leptos signal state (build_database, get_data/set_data futures, use_core_database, use_requests). So the 41 untested lib files split into: 2 with pure logic now drafted (mapm, backend; json_scan is with helper lane 1), 6 declaration-only operator/prototype files that hold nothing to execute, and 33 browser-bound files (leptos.spi, 19 ui/svg views, store, wasm, state_core, bench/parsing runners) whose code only runs inside a wasm page. Decision: the per-module criterion (test blocks with explicit ///> targets) applies to the pure-logic files; declaration-only files are exempt; browser-bound files are covered as a group by one compile gate, the existing apps/wasm build that links them (spiral/apps/wasm/build.ps1, in the CI workflow), not by per-file blocks, because a Cells rust cell would have to build leptos and rexie per test. With the 2 queued slices the lib side of this item is then complete. Next: the apps side (apps/spiral, cells spi) by the same three-way split")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_5 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:59 lap 5 grain, apps side started: apps/cells/spi (Cells' own Spiral domain, 7 modules: cell 435, directives 94, document 707, main 21, notebook 296, targets 65, text 383 lines) has 0 test blocks, as do apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.spi (224) and the monolith (being split by SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR). The spi package is |core- only (no lib testing), and text.spi is Gleam-bound (erlang byte_size/binary.part externals), so its blocks are ///> gleam returning 0/1 like the scaffold's wgsl test. Drafted lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_text_test_blocks.spi (2 blocks: starts_with, drop, slice past the end, trim, parse_i32 42 and the -1 sentinel for 4x, contains; ensure_nl on x, empty and y-newline), expectations taken from the code (parse_i32 has no sign, so -42 is not a case). Runner lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells-text-run.ps1 appends them to apps/cells/spi/text.spi, gates the file and re-runs mix compile (the domain build must skip test blocks), keeps them only if both pass. apps/cells is not a switch consumer root, so this can run any time. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~4 min). Next: same for directives.spi and targets.spi (small, pure)")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_6 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:34 lap 6 grain: second Cells module drafted: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_directives_test_blocks.spi (2 gleam test blocks for apps/cells/spi/directives.spi: prepare of a ///- line with --print-code --timeout 500 and two --package flags gives ok, 1, 500 and the tab-joined packages; --timeout 0 and a #! dib cell are errors per text.is_error) plus cells-directives-run.ps1 (the cells-text-run pattern: append, gate, mix compile, restore unless both pass). Queued after cells-text-run, since both rely on test blocks inside the Cells domain package. targets.spi (1,753 bytes) is next.")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_7 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:51 lap 7 grain: third Cells module drafted, lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_targets_test_blocks.spi + cells-targets-run.ps1 (1 gleam block on apps/cells/spi/targets.spi: target_of, backend and extension agree for rust, c, zig, wgsl, cuda, lean, and an unknown tool maps to empty), queued after cells-text-run. Finding while writing it: Cells' target union lists 13 tools, but the compiler has 7 more backends (kotlin, swift, openqasm, ebpf, calyx, wasm, bend), so a ///> kotlin test block falls to UnknownTarget today. The block pins that (kotlin -> empty), so whoever adds those targets updates the test on purpose. It belongs to the explicit-targets work (CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS).")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_8 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T16:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:31 lap 8 grain: post-switch re-anchor of the two lib runners: mapm-run.ps1 anchors (/// ### from_list, the count() splice) and backend-run.ps1 anchors (inl indent, the single is_rust Swift/Rust tail) all still match today's migrated lib. One real break fixed: the mapm test block piped a ;[...] literal into from_array, which takes array_base, but under core v1 a literal is array t; it now goes through am'.base first (the same rule the migrated lib uses, dropdown.spi:43, state_core.spi:511). backend_test_block and the three cells blocks hold no arrays. Queue rows unchanged")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_9 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T16:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:56 lap 9 grain: staged the cell.spi test block: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_cell_test_blocks.spi checks without_panic_thread_id (drops the (12345) thread id, keeps a non-digit (x1)) and without_cell_run_dir (drops a spiral_cells run directory inside a panic location, keeps src/main.rs), expected strings traced by hand through the helpers (cell.spi after panic_thread_open). Runner cells-cell-run.ps1 is the targets runner retargeted at spi/cell.spi (gate + mix compile, restores unless both pass; parses clean). HEAVY QUEUE row added. These two helpers are also the Spiral form of xsim comparable's two regex rewrites (SIM-IN-SPIRAL slice 2), so that slice can reuse them")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_10 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:31 lap 10 grain: notebook.spi block staged: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/notebook_test_blocks.spi (one ///> gleam test over the pure list and token helpers: add_nb a,b,a -> a\\nb, join_rev x,y -> y\\nx, take_token ab cd 0 -> 2, skip_ws over space-tab-space from 2 -> 5, has_nb; values traced by hand from notebook.spi:15-65); cells-cell-run.ps1 now takes -Module cell|notebook (default cell, so the queued row is unchanged), parse check 0 errors; queue row added for -Module notebook. Next: both rows after main's go, then document.spi")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_11 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T17:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:51 lap 11 grain: document.spi block staged: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/document_test_blocks.spi (doc_line on empty, indented and blank lines -> '/// ', '  /// x', unchanged; test_heading on '## sum (test)' and 'x'; latin_letter 233 true and 215 false; traced from document.spi:147-165, 231; = false on bools is fine since the Cells spi package uses |core- like cell.spi:71); runner takes -Module document (parse 0 errors; a bulk .Replace had hit both }[$Module] sites, rewritten whole); queue row added. All 3 Cells domain modules with pure helpers now have a staged first block. Next: the 3 rows after main's go, then text.spi")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_12 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T18:17Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:17 lap 12 gap: when a cell's last expression is a value that is not i32, Cells retries it through console.show_value (cells.ex:618-620, show_value_main), but console.spi's C, Delphi, Wasm and Bend arms are placeholder identifiers (backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_cells_console), so a value cell on C, the primary oracle, ends in an unreadable C compile error. Next: a C arm by typecase (ints, floats, bool, string) plus a Cells test")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_13 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T19:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 15:19 the C arm for Cells' console is proven: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/c_line_probe.spi (the exact c_line typecase that console_c_arm.py inserts) compiles on the C backend and prints cells-c, -7, -9000000000, 7, 18000000000000000000, 1.5 and true, exit 0. console_c_arm.py (dry run: 3 anchors) replaces the placeholder C arms of show_value and write_line with it; applying it is a Cells edit for main or helper lane 1, followed by a value-cell test on c")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_14 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T23:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:23 lap 21 finding: the 14:27-14:29 runs of mapm-run.ps1 and backend-run.ps1 (logs in lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE) did not test anything: every variant failed to type-check with ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: _assert_eq (mapm.spi:73, backend.spi:210), because those two lib files have no static prelude that opens testing, and both runners then restored the files as designed. Fixed in the block files: each test body now starts with open testing (the cells_* blocks assert through exit codes and need nothing). Also all six runners (backend, mapm, cells-cell, cells-directives, cells-targets, cells-text) now keep the original as bytes and restore with a byte-for-byte check instead of a ReadAllText/WriteAllText round trip; all parse clean. The two lib rows (30, 38) need a rerun; they edit lib/spiral in place during the gate, so main runs them with helper lane 1 told")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_15 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T23:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:45 correction-grade evidence for the 17:17 note: the first of the CLI crates' test blocks has now run (cli_common.spi, 2 rust tests passed through a Cells gate from a scratch package); the other crate blocks run after promotion")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_16 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 16u32, "2026-10-20T00:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:27 every CLI crate test block has now run green in Cells gates (7 of 7: cli_common 2, cuda_runner 1, gleam_runner 1, rust_runner 1, notebook_export 2); the crate files need ///> _ above their Export main to be gate-clean (the trailing main is otherwise built as a cell on the default target, and Zig rejects Export with 2 args), which promote.ps1 now adds")
inl x_cells_split () = Task ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", "x: make all 22 client .spi files cell compatible by a destructive split: shared backend-neutral domain modules (protocol first: the cN k/n frame header and thread flaws, written by frames and read by assemble from one definition) plus thin Rust glue (serde_json, unicode crates, HTTP); every crate module gains test blocks on ///> rust -d ... and, where pure, gleam and zig, so the same source later serves SIM-IN-SPIRAL on BEAM. Baseline 2026-10-19 02:30: 6 of 22 files run under Cells (prelude, posts, cli, oauth1, wire, transport), the 16 crate files have 0 test blocks, 0 unions and 99 Rust blobs (xc 23, net 20, wire 14, frames 11, oauth1 7, token 6, args 6)", Story, P1, D5, 35u32, Active, "CELLS-EVERYWHERE", "x,cells,gleam,split,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_1 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-10-19T06:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:51 slice 1 written, verification queued (eoie job x-round-1: Cells on 9 modules, then client build): (1) protocol.spi, backend-neutral: frame_header {cell; part; part_count} with one rendered and one parsed (result frame_header header_rejection: NotTwoWords | NotACell | NotAFraction | PartOutOfRange), thread_flaw union (MissingFrames | ReplyGap) with note_of and exit_code; frames writes and assemble reads the header from this one definition, assemble's 4 Rust split blobs are gone. (2) glyphs.spi: the only backend-specific text primitives (char_of, first_grapheme, plain_weight, nfc, blank) as backend_switch Rust/Gleam pairs; graphemes, trimmed_end, letter, label_char, defused_dot are neutral Spiral on top. Whitespace is decided per code point by Rust's White_Space set on both backends, because Erlang string:trim uses Pattern_White_Space (NBSP differs). (3) splitter.spi: the whole frames domain moved out of the crate; frames/frames.spi is now 15 lines of glue (serde_json + 2 exports). (4) assemble: union private cell_reading = Whole source | Holed thread_flaw, so source_of only runs on complete cells (zeta sealed-search idea). (5) write: chain = Unstarted | After created_reply replaces the prev = empty-string sentinel; wire ReplyPost now takes the created_reply it answers (lean_cic identity brand idea). (6) wire: authorizer c indexed (BearerToken :: forall c, Signed :: -> authorizer user_context), with a fails test that an app context cannot be signed (omniledger singleton-indexed constructors). (7) prelude: cut is backend-switched because lib sm'.substring has no Gleam arm; digit parsing moved from cli to prelude. Rejected: an order-decision union for later_id (2 call sites, adds lines, removes no bug). Every touched file 0 x-compat findings (renamed field total/parts: both are TLDs)")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_2 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-10-19T08:05Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 04:05 slice 1 hardened before any build ran it (user committed the first draft as a3b5353, CI ubuntu failed on a protocol parse error: nested nullary patterns like 'Error NotTwoWords' do not parse, now a two-level match). Probe compiles of all 16 crates found: backend_switch evaluates every arm on every backend, so Gleam 'global' needs a true literal (Gleam attribute written '@ external' on its own line, x-compat clean); typed parsing cost +185 generated lines in assemble (1140 > 1000), measured fixes: index-based parse and a shared let; let-izing prelude each/keep/spread made digest 990->1045 and view 990->1046, reverted. Destructive split instead: new crate xc_locate (pages -> {frames, known} JSON: latest revisions, links expanded, headers parsed) + xc_assemble (frames -> Whole | Holed cells), xc chains them: locate 571, assemble 749. A read-only review agent then found 4 real bugs, all fixed: (1) sim fixtures label cells pN, so the header cell is a string again (NotACell dropped); (2) wire's 'joined prefix ids' shadowed the new prelude joined in assemble (same type, silent, would print ab,cd): renamed delimited; (3) count_of_text now sees every post and Rust slice exit(3)s inside multi-byte chars: digits-only guard by replace before parsing, regression test words_beyond_ascii_are_no_header; (4) delete-thread (10-18 code) stopped at the first failed id and dropped printed lines: now deletes the rest, reports every line, exits 4. Verification queued as eoie job x-round-3 (Cells rust+gleam+zig on 9 modules, then build + e2e)")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_3 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-10-19T09:30Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 05:30 verified. Cells (process compiler, heavy gate, eoie jobs): prelude 3, glyphs 5, protocol 7, splitter 5, events 4, posts 9, cli 17, oauth1 3, wire 22, transport 2 = 77 tests in 10 modules, all passing; glyphs/splitter/events agree on rust (-d unicode crates) and gleam, protocol on rust, gleam and zig. Client build (eoie job x-build): 16 crates, gate 0 over 1000 (max digest/view 990, locate 571, assemble 749), x-compat 0 findings in 26 files, e2e 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases incl. all 4 sim threads, post-thread 34 posts read back 5005 chars, delete-thread 34; postable 1014 posts, 40 cells over one post (41 before; test targets moved into each file's static prelude so the long -d line is not repeated per test). Two Cells timeouts were not loops: one transient, one a type error ('a <= b |> _assert_eq true' parses as a <= (b |> ...)). New: events.spi (xsim-events v1 as content | revision | reaction unions, encoder byte-compatible with prelude.events.jsonl). Next slices: paging.spi + thread.spi with an injected pager (drafted, scratch), one 'latest' on wire instead of two, nominal post_id/conversation_id, events decoder, then the remaining crate files get test blocks")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_4 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T10:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 06:17 pending lib flip (LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM, main lane): Gleam sm'.index_of moves to UTF-8 bytes like every other backend. When it is green and installed, x flips in the same step: prelude cut's Gleam arm becomes lib sm'.substring (drop gleam_cut), and glyphs gleam_first_grapheme returns string.byte_size of the popped grapheme instead of 1. Then rerun Cells on glyphs, protocol, splitter, events (non-ASCII cases on rust + gleam). Main lane will message x when it lands")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_5 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T11:06Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 07:06 lib flip done (LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM green at 06:51): prelude cut = plain sm'.substring (gleam_cut gone), glyphs gleam_first_grapheme = string.byte_size of the popped grapheme; Gleam tuple patterns '#(' do not survive a Spiral code macro, use pair.0. Cells eoie job x-cells-bytes: prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter, events all pass on rust + gleam (+ zig); x crates' generated Rust byte-identical")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_6 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:18Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 10:18 core switch (main lane, 11:45-13:30): migrate-consumers.ps1 dry run on x = 26 files, 0 auto edits, 0 manual spots; x left out of apply-switch. After the main lane reports the switch landed, x reruns Cells on its 10 modules + client build + x-compat + e2e as one heavy job")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_7 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 7u32, "2026-10-19T16:36Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 12:36 core v1 verification (eoie job x-core-verify, 12:16-12:34): Cells 10 modules 77 tests pass, x-compat 0, e2e 31+5 green; gate 1 over: view 990->1005 because core v1 no longer folds 'comp a b |> gt_is' on strings (runtime order enum + match; also jsonl +15, net +17, assemble +14). Strings cannot use > directly in core v1 (number constraint), so no x workaround; repro sent to the main lane. The regenerated .rs files in the x tree are now core-v1 output")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_8 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 8u32, "2026-10-19T17:02Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 13:02 core v1 order fix (main lane: order back to the nominal i32, gt_is/lt_is EQ checks) confirmed in x: view 987, jsonl 917, net 308, assemble 745; gate 16 crates 0 over; x-compat 0; e2e 31+5 green (eoie job x-build-order-fix). The x tree's .rs files are now regenerated from fixed core v1")
inl note_of_x_cells_split_9 () = Note ("X-CELLS-SPLIT", 9u32, "2026-10-19T20:13Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 16:13 reading.spi split out of assemble (11th Cells module, rust + gleam); assemble and locate are now JSON glue. Pack 8b round green, gate unchanged by 8b")
inl lib_substring_gleam () = Task ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", "lib sm'.substring has no Gleam arm: its fallback emits F# .Substring, so lib code using it cannot target Gleam (found by the x lane 2026-10-19, which works around it with its own backend-switched cut). Add a Gleam arm (string.slice on UTF-8 bytes, matching C's byte semantics) and a ///> gleam test block", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,gleam,cells", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_substring_gleam_1 () = Note ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", 1u32, "2026-10-19T09:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:57 edit in lib/spiral/sm'.spi: substring gains a Gleam arm through core sm.slice (StringSlice; the Gleam codegen's spiral_string_slice is UTF-8 byte indexed with an inclusive to, so to = start + len - 1, same bytes as C and Rust) plus test substring_cuts_by_start_and_length (gleam, rust, fsharp; middle cut, empty at 0 and at the end). Needs a Cells --gate run of sm' when the machine is free; then the x lane can drop its gleam_cut in apps/client/prelude.spi.")
inl note_of_lib_substring_gleam_2 () = Note ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", 2u32, "2026-10-19T10:08Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 06:08 x lane (git-2c) review: on Gleam sm'.index_of returns a grapheme index (sm'.spi:539, to_graphemes + index_fold), so a UTF-8-byte Gleam substring disagrees with it for any non-ASCII prefix (substring 0 (index_of sep s) s, as in x prelude split_on: for 'é/x', index_of is 1 and byte 1 is inside é). Units must match per backend: grapheme string.slice on Gleam (x's gleam_cut, passing rust/gleam agreement tests with é, ’ and emoji on 2026-10-19), or bytes for index_of/length too. Suggested test: split on '/' after a non-ASCII prefix, agreeing on rust and gleam. x keeps gleam_cut until then")
inl note_of_lib_substring_gleam_3 () = Note ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", 3u32, "2026-10-19T10:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:17 x lane review: Gleam sm'.index_of returned grapheme indexes (and matched only single-grapheme needles, since it compared each grapheme to the search), so byte substring cut wrong after non-ASCII. Fixed toward bytes, the lib's convention (Python and Lean index_of already convert to UTF-8 bytes, Rust and Zig are bytes, Gleam length is byte_size): Gleam index_of = string.split_once + bit_array.byte_size of the prefix. Tests: index_of_counts_utf8_bytes_and_whole_needles (gleam, rust, cuda) and a substring/index_of round trip on é/x and a’😀/b (gleam, rust, fsharp). Gate run queued in the after-PR-14 chain.")
inl note_of_lib_substring_gleam_4 () = Note ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", 4u32, "2026-10-19T10:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:52 green: Cells --gate on tree lib/spiral/sm'.spi 41/41, 0 type errors, unsupported 0 (index_of, length' and substring Gleam arms now UTF-8 bytes; tests index_of_counts_utf8_bytes_and_whole_needles, length_counts_utf8_bytes, substring_cuts_by_start_and_length). First attempt failed to parse: a Gleam tuple pattern #(a, _) inside the macro (# is a splice starter, see spiral-macro-gotchas); fixed with pair.0. x lane flips its prelude cut and glyphs gleam_first_grapheme next and reruns its Cells modules; Done after that.")
inl note_of_lib_substring_gleam_5 () = Note ("LIB-SUBSTRING-GLEAM", 5u32, "2026-10-19T11:06Z", "git-2c-relay-by-main-thread", "2026-10-19 07:06 x lane flipped onto the byte-unit lib: prelude cut is plain sm'.substring (gleam_cut deleted), glyphs gleam_first_grapheme returns the popped grapheme's byte_size; x Cells prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter, events pass on rust + gleam (+ zig for protocol and prelude) with é, ’, emoji and CRLF cases; x generated Rust byte-identical. Done.")
inl eoie_job_stop () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-STOP", "eoie job has no stop verb, and a supervisor whose child tree is killed from outside keeps reporting state=running with no children until its timeout (seen 2026-10-19 06:02 main-thread: job-1792403971209-rust-nightly-verify, child tree stopped by command line at 06:00, status still running at 06:02, supervisor pid had no children; I stopped the supervisor by hand). Add job stop <id> (stops the tree, state=stopped) and detect child exit so status reflects it. Also worth a note in job start help: pwsh -File cannot take array args, so pass lists as one comma string", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,job,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_job_stop_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-STOP", 1u32, "2026-10-19T10:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:02 after stopping the supervisor process itself, job status still says state=running (both job-1792403971209 and job-1792404100118): the status is read from the job record, which nobody finalizes when the supervisor dies. status should check that the supervisor pid is alive and report lost (125) when it is not.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_stop_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-STOP", 2u32, "2026-10-19T10:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:03 correction to note 1: status does detect a dead supervisor; job-1792403971209 turned lost (125) about 30 s after its supervisor stopped. The real gaps remain: no job stop verb, and a supervisor whose child tree was killed from outside stayed alive with no children instead of finalizing the job.")
inl backlog_laps () = Task ("BACKLOG-LAPS", "Backlog laps (user 2026-10-19 08:0x: iterate until 22:00, main lane plus one subagent; advance one grain of sand of every task, go through the whole backlog over and over, then pick one task and take it to the end, rinse and repeat). Ledger spiral/lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/ledger.tsv (lap, id, owner, at, grain, next); main owns the first half of the open main/shared list, the subagent the second half (CPU-light grains only, heavy runs queued to main as scripts). Other lanes' items (TIBIA, EOIE, X, DOTA) are skipped.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "backlog,process", "current", "current")
inl spiral_assign_primitive () = Task ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", "Portable assignment primitive !!!!Assign(target, value), checked like Conv (target a dyn local of the value's type), lowered per backend (a = b, Delphi :=, Lean := in let mut, Gleam/Bend rejected), F# marks targets let mutable; replaces BackendSwitch set' macros in while_loop/while_control (design: FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION note 2)", Story, P3, D4, 75u32, Active, "", "compiler,portable,fsharp", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:57 helper-lane lap 1: item created this lap (split from FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION, design in that item's note 2). Touch-point map from spiral_compiler.fs, following how !!!!Conv is wired: the Op union (Conv at line 43760), the op-name table (45734, Conv -> Conv), PartEval in both cores (EOp(_,Conv,[EType(_,typ);a]) at 155833 and 160951; Assign needs the same in both, checking that the target is a dyn local of the value's type), then one arm per codegen that already writes assignment, plus F#: the shared CodegenFsharp macro_assigned_binds walker (FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION) is where Assign targets get let mutable. The two samples to migrate are samples/while_loop and samples/while_control (their BackendSwitch set' arms).")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:39 helper-lane lap 2: read the two consumer samples to fix the primitive's contract: while_loop and while_control assign dyn locals through a BackendSwitch set' arm per backend (11 arms in one BackendSwitch record: Fsharp with <-, Lean and Delphi with :=, C, Zig, Rust, Wgsl, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm and Ebpf with =; no Lua, Wasm, Gleam, Bend, Python or TypeScript arm), so !!!!Assign's value is removing those per-backend macro arms from user code and making assignment type-checked (today a macro can assign a string into an i32 local and only the target compiler notices). Gleam and Bend must reject Assign at compile time (no mutation), which today surfaces only as a target-compiler failure. Sizing done in lap 1 (Op union, op table, two PartEval sites, codegens, F# walker).")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:27 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the primitive as lab/SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE/patch.py on MonotonicDelayMs's template (anchors --check OK, LF): Op union case Assign (parsed by name through string_to_op_dict's reflection, so no parser change), its show name, op_args_inferred (so both arguments are type-checked terms), a peval arm in BOTH cores (single-flight 161295 and gpt5parallel 156267, same text one indent deeper) that requires the target to be a runtime DV of exactly the value's type and pushes TyOp(Assign, [target; value]) of unit type, with two errors (wrong value type; target not a dynamic local). Codegen arms: F# a <- b plus macro_assigned_op marking the target let mutable (162606), Rust a = b, C a = b, Lua a = b; the other backends fall to their existing unsupported-op errors. Design decision recorded: Gleam and Bend are rejected at codegen time, not at peval, because BackendSwitch defaults and validated arms are partially evaluated under every backend (spiral-macro-gotchas), so a peval-time rejection would break every backend that merely validates such an arm. Probes assign_loop.spi (while loop summing 0..9 with two Assigns, expect exit 0 on C, Rust and Lua) and assign_wrong_type.spi (string into i32, expect the new error); run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate; HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:51Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:51 helper-lane lap 5: added the Zig arm after checking codegenZig's locals: every local is declared var vN: T = undefined; _ = &vN; (spiral_compiler.fs 164936-164939), so a = b is a valid statement there without any mutability marking; patch.py now also inserts Assign, [a;b] -> a = b into the Zig op table (anchor beside StdoutFlush, --check OK), and run.ps1 runs assign_loop on Zig too. Lean (let mut + :=) and Delphi (:=) stay for a later slice; Gleam/Bend keep failing at codegen by design.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:57 pack 8 (6 of helper lane 1's patches: literal defaults, positional rename, or-pattern join, Assign, crates field, export types) built but its review single-flight-20261019-134625 regressed samples/frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any to compile errors on all 12 backends (TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type. Got: %d, i32); DISAGREE 0 otherwise. Not accepted: the tree and the installed Release compiler were restored to the accepted pack 7 source (core-src 3f21d9014f3e7723); helper lane 1 bisects with per-patch Debug candidates.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T19:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:46 main: pack 8b ACCEPTED 15:46 (single-flight-20261019-145950: 7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release review 145110) with literal defaults, positional rename, Assign, crates field and export types; or-pattern join and nominal-with stay out (pack 9+).")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:14 helper-lane: !!!!Assign shipped in pack 8b (accepted 15:46, DISAGREE 0, no sample uses it yet so no residual change). Evidence on the installed pack 8b compiler: assign_loop.spi (a while loop summing 0..9 through two Assigns) runs exit 0 on C, Rust and Lua. Zig fails to build: codegenZig emits a unit op as _ = expr;, so the arm produced _ = v1 = v3; - staged fix lab/SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE/zig_statement_patch.py (dry run OK on the tree): Assign becomes a statement arm in Zig's op (a = b;) and the table arm is removed; it goes to the next pack. Still open for the title: Lean and Delphi arms, Gleam/Bend codegen errors, and replacing the set' BackendSwitch macros in samples while_loop/while_control (matrix inputs, main's). Not Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:32 main: pack 10 INSTALLED 17:32 (nominal-with, Zig Assign as a statement, export-types nominal walk): Release candidate review single-flight-20261019-1723xx vs the pack 9 accept 161549 = 0 differing cells of 7,057 (no sample exercises these paths), DISAGREE 0, shared DLLs hash-equal to the candidate; the baseline does not move, so no separate accept run; the features are proven by helper lane 1's probes on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T21:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:33 helper-lane: pack 10 installed (zig_statement_patch.py): lab/SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE/assign_loop.spi (a while loop that sums 0..9 with !!!!Assign and returns acc - 45) exits 0 on Zig, C, Rust and Lua on the installed compiler, so the Zig _ = a = b; form is gone. assign_wrong_type.spi is rejected on Zig with Assign needs a value of the target's type. Not Done: the F# let mutable marking needs a probe, the Delphi and Lean arms and the Gleam/Bend rejection are not written yet, and while_loop/while_control still use the BackendSwitch set' macros.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T21:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:54 helper-lane: Delphi arm. lab/SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE/delphi_patch.py (one arm next to ArrayIndexSet: TyOp(Assign, [a;b]) -> line s a := b;, --apply gated) on a private-cache Debug candidate (delphi-run.ps1, 0 errors): assign_loop.spi compiles to Delphi with v1 := v3; for the assignment, and fpc -Mdelphi builds it, which runs with exit 0 (acc = 45). Offered for pack 11. Left: the F# mutable probe, a Lean arm (needs let mut on the target), Gleam/Bend explicit rejection, and the while_loop set' replacement.")
inl note_of_spiral_assign_primitive_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", 11u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); Delphi Assign arm is in.")
inl rust_export_types () = Task ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", "Rust Export across crates: unions and records reachable from an !!!!Export signature are emitted pub and named after their Spiral source (command, not US0) so a consumer crate can name and match them; today codegenRust emits private enum US0 named by a per-crate counter (probe lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE/export_union.spi), which forces x args.rs to flatten command into kind i32 + 13 scalars", Story, P3, D4, 80u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,export", "current", "current")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_1 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T14:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:33 helper-lane: filed from CLI-ARGS-STYLE lap 2's probe. Fits READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES (Done): that epic made case names readable (US0_Add) but deliberately kept union TYPE names tag-keyed (US0/UH0) on every backend, so this item is the type-name slice, limited to types reachable from Export signatures to keep the residual churn small: emit pub enum/struct for them, name them from the Spiral nominal/union name through the same sanitizer as generatedCaseNames (index suffix only on collision), keep internal types US{n}. Cross-crate identity also needs the consumer to import rather than redeclare the type (a use path from the producer crate), which is the second half.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_2 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T15:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:33 helper-lane (CLI-ARGS-STYLE lap 4): located the naming in codegenRust: union_name (spiral_compiler.fs ~164009) and tyv (~163550) name types UH{tag}/US{tag} from the per-codegen union memo, and union_type (~163983) emits enum {prefix}{tag} without pub; Heap{tag}/Mut{tag} likewise. The source name is not at hand there: codegen sees YUnion (layout + cases), while the Spiral name lives in the Nominal record {body; id; name} (158397) of the YNominal that wrapped it before peval unfolded it. So the slice needs (1) the nominal name carried to codegen - either a name field on the union ConsedNode set when a nominal union is unfolded, or a codegen-side map from union node to the first YNominal name seen - and (2) a set of union/layout tags reachable from Export signatures (walk domain and range types at the Export arm, 163801) that get pub plus the sanitized source name, with an index suffix only on collision. Other types keep US{tag}, so residual churn is limited to crates that export.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_3 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:50Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:50 helper-lane: one more constraint found by reading single-flight's type evaluation: a union type reaches codegen as a hash-consed structural node - TUnion evaluates to YUnion(H {cases; layout; tags; tag_cases; is_degenerate}) (spiral_compiler.fs 159349-159368) - and YNominal is evaluated away to its body before codegen (no codegen matches YNominal), so the Spiral name is a property of the nominal, not of the union node, and two nominal unions with the same cases and layout already share one Rust enum US{tag} today. Consequence for the naming slice: names must come from a union-node -> nominal-name table filled where a nominal whose body is a union is unfolded, with a deterministic rule when several nominals share one node (first in definition order, the others as type aliases pub type other = first;), and the Export-reachability walk works on the union nodes. Adding a name field to Union itself would split today's shared enums and change residuals everywhere, so the side table is the cheaper slice.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_4 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:58Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:58 helper-lane: the fill point for the union-node -> name table in single-flight is nominal_type_apply (spiral_compiler.fs 159262-159278): YNominal a -> ty s a.node.body is where a nominal's body turns into its structural YUnion, and the YApply branch does the same for generic nominals after binding the type argument. Recording (union node, a.node.name) there on first sight, plus the type arguments for applied nominals (option of i32 vs option of string share the name, so those need the argument suffix), gives codegen everything it needs once the table is carried out in PartEvalResult next to the join-point tables; gpt5parallel has its own copy of nominal unfolding (147326) and would need the same hook.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_5 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T16:09Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:09 helper-lane: first slice staged as lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/patch.py (4 anchors, --check OK, composes with the other 9 staged compiler patches on one copy): (1) a shared side table unionSourceNames (ConditionalWeakTable keyed by the union node, beside closureBindingNames, which is the existing precedent for source-name side tables); (2) single-flight's nominal_type_apply records (union node, nominal name) when a nominal's body evaluates to a YUnion; (3) codegenRust pre-walks the program's Export ops, collects every union reachable from their domain and range types (pairs, records, arrays, layouts, recursive cases, with a visited set) that has a source name, sanitizes it and de-duplicates collisions; (4) after codegen, those unions' generated tags (US{n}/UH{n}, case constructors included) are renamed to the source name and their enum becomes pub. Every other union keeps its tag, so only crates with exports change. run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate and compiles CLI-ARGS-STYLE's export_union.spi on Rust (expect pub enum command and pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> command); queued. Open risk: if the signature's union reaches codegen through an unfolding path other than nominal_type_apply, the table misses it and nothing is renamed - the probe shows that. Cross-crate import (a consumer using the producer's enum instead of redeclaring it) is the second slice.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_6 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T20:51Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:51 helper-lane: the slice shipped in pack 8b (accepted 15:46) but had no effect yet: on the installed compiler export_union.spi still emitted enum US0 and pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> US0. Cause: the Export signature carries the union as YNominal (i32 -> command), and the pre-walk had no YNominal/YApply case, so it never reached the union node (the side table itself is filled - enominal goes through nominal_type_apply). Fix lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/nominal_walk_patch.py (one line: YNominal/YApply -> walk (env.nominal_apply t)); a private-cache Debug candidate of today's tree + this line emits pub enum command { ... } and pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> command (walk-run.ps1, build 0 errors). The probe has no main (an export-only module), so rustc's E0601 there is expected. Goes to the next pack; cross-crate import remains the second slice.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_7 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T21:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:32 main: pack 10 INSTALLED 17:32 (nominal-with, Zig Assign as a statement, export-types nominal walk): Release candidate review single-flight-20261019-1723xx vs the pack 9 accept 161549 = 0 differing cells of 7,057 (no sample exercises these paths), DISAGREE 0, shared DLLs hash-equal to the candidate; the baseline does not move, so no separate accept run; the features are proven by helper lane 1's probes on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_8 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:33 helper-lane: pack 10 installed: on the installed compiler export_union.spi emits pub enum command { ... } and pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> command (lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/pack10-evidence.ps1; rustc E0601 there only because the probe is an export-only module with no main). Unions reachable from an Export signature are now pub and use their source name. Not Done: records reachable from the signature (still positional tuples/structs with generated names) and a consumer-crate proof (x args.rs importing command and matching it) are still open.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_9 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 9u32, "2026-10-19T22:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:28 helper-lane lap 7: sized the records slice with lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/export_record.spi on the installed pack 10 compiler. type span = {start; stop} used in a union case and as an Export parameter comes out flattened to positional fields: the variant is command_Add(i32, i32) and the export is pub fn xc_width(v0: i32, v1: i32). The union naming works (pub enum command); records do not, because type span is a structural alias with no nominal node, so there is no source name to note. Plan for the slice: only nominal records get a name (nominal span = {...}). For an exported nominal record, emit pub struct span { pub start: i32, pub stop: i32 } and an export shim that destructures it into the flattened closure call (xc_width(s: span) -> closure1()(s.start, s.stop)). Union payloads keep the flattened representation inside, with a From/Into pair at the boundary, so codegen layout stays untouched.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_10 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 10u32, "2026-10-19T23:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:48 helper-lane lap 7: the records slice works with a cross-crate consumer. lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/record_struct_patch.py (--apply gated, dry run OK) handles an Export whose parameter is a nominal record of primitive fields: it emits pub struct span { pub start: i32, pub stop: i32 } once per record name and an export pub fn xc_width(v: span) -> i32 that passes v.start, v.stop to the flattened closure. Structural records keep the flattened signature. On a private-cache Debug candidate (record-run.ps1, 0 errors), lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/export_nominal_record.spi compiles to xc.rs. rustc builds that as an rlib named xc, and a separate consumer crate (consumer.rs, --extern xc) calls xc::xc_width(xc::span { start: 1, stop: 5 }) and exits 4 as predicted. This is the first cross-crate proof for the item; a union consumer (match on xc::command) is queued. Still open: nominal records with non-primitive fields, and records in return position.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_11 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 11u32, "2026-10-20T00:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 20:10 helper-lane: union cross-crate proof on the installed pack 10 compiler (lab/RUST-EXPORT-TYPES/union-consumer-run.ps1). export_union.spi compiles to xc.rs with pub enum command { command_Add(i32), command_Rename(Rc<str>), command_Clear } and pub fn xc_decide. rustc builds it as rlib xc, and a separate consumer crate matches xc::command::command_Add(n) on xc::xc_decide(12) and exits 12 as predicted. Both halves of the title, consumers naming and matching unions and records, now have cross-crate proofs; the records half rides pack 12 (record_struct_patch.py). Remaining for Done: that patch accepted, then records with non-primitive fields and records in return position, or a decision to scope those out.")
inl note_of_rust_export_types_12 () = Note ("RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", 12u32, "2026-10-20T01:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 21:34 main: pack 12 ACCEPTED 21:34 (single-flight-20261019-203828: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 202140; residual changes only 2 F# Brzozowski mega rows) with record_struct_patch.py: Export of a nominal record parameter emits pub struct <name> and pub fn xc(v: <name>); a separate consumer crate calling xc::xc_width(xc::span { start: 1, stop: 5 }) exits 4 (helper lane 1).")
inl gadt_join_point_growing_index () = Task ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", "Compiler hang: a let rec join point over a runtime GADT value whose recursion grows the type index (term n -> term (succ n)) never finishes compiling, instead of sharing one join point across phantom indices or reporting polymorphic recursion", TaskKind, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,gadt,bug,mega", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_1 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T14:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:36 found by helper-lane-2 while sizing MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER's shared goal (substitution and renaming under binders in lean_cic, spiral_proves, zeta). Repros in spiral/lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (installed single-flight compiler, try.ps1 -Backend C, bounded): debruijn_rename.spi, a well-scoped de Bruijn term union rec term n (Var of var n, Lam of term (succ n), App) with inl rec rename/lift and inl rec size over a static term, compiles and exits 5 (correct size after weakening). debruijn_size_runtime.spi, the same term made runtime (inl ~t) and size a let rec join point: TIMEOUT after 45 s (compiler killed; 60 s for the rename variant). gadt_same_index_runtime.spi, a let rec join point over a runtime union rec tree n whose recursion keeps the index: compiles, exits 3. So the hang needs both a runtime value and an index that grows in the recursive call; it looks like join-point specialization per index instantiation without a bound (succ n, succ (succ n), ...), although the index nominals are phantom (nominal succ n = ()) and the runtime layout is identical for every n. Expected: one join point for all n (erase phantom indices for specialization), or a fast EJP-style error naming the polymorphic recursion. Related but distinct: GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (Done, the case-field type growth in ty_is_uninhabited). Owner: compiler lane (main)")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_2 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T14:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:39 lap 2 grain: triage: the hang is unbounded specialization, not a wrong result. size is a let rec join point; join points specialize per argument type, and every recursive call on term (succ n) has a new type, so the specialization set never closes (the fixed-index runtime case compiles because its key repeats); rename is an inl rec over a runtime ~t, which inlines without end. The compiler bug is the missing guard: partial evaluation should stop like EJP0040 does for dynamic ifs, by counting distinct specializations of one join-point body (or index-type nesting depth) and reporting the growing index with a hint (erase the index at runtime through an existential such as a term_dyn wrapper, or keep the recursion static). The runtime remedy for the lean_cic, spiral_proves and zeta roots is that erasure, independent of the guard Next: find where single-flight memoizes join-point specializations (join point dictionary keyed by body + argument types) and add a per-body distinct-key counter with an EJP report, as a candidate patch in lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_3 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:42 patch design located in single-flight PartEval (spiral_compiler.fs 158355-161335): (1) EUnbox's runtime case body (159931 run s a, and Unbox's 159962) is not wrapped in within_dynamic_if (159488, EJP0040, only if_'s two branches are, 159519-159520), so an inl rec recursing through a runtime match never reaches the nesting limit; wrap those calls so EJP0040 counts union-match branches too. (2) EJoinPoint' (159727-159764): dict is the per-(backend, body) specialization table; at the Owner branch add a distinct-key cap (SPIRAL_JP_SPECIALIZATION_LIMIT, default 1000) that raises RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with a new code and the hint erase the growing index at runtime (existential) or keep the recursion static. Constraint: PartEval is a SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair, and pair-drift.ps1 fails a build whose normal pair changed on one side only; routing the calls through PartEvalSeams would pass the check but spend hopacsota's G6 seam budget on a non-seam. So the patch either mirrors both guards on gpt5parallel's side (it already has jp_method_specialization_budget_exhausted at 41659) or waits for gpt5parallel's retirement (HOPACSOTA-P6).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_4 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:45 analysis note (helper-lane-2, for the compiler lane): why the runtime form cannot terminate today. A runtime value needs a residual layout for its type, and residual unions are monomorphized per type arguments; the type term zero has a Lam case whose field is term (succ zero), whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), and so on, so the layout of term zero is an infinite family of union types. That is the same type-level growth GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG met (its fix capped nesting in ty_is_uninhabited for the inhabitedness check), but a runtime value additionally forces the layouts and a join point per instantiation, which the cap does not cover; the static repro avoids it because partial evaluation consumes the term before any layout exists. Two directions: (a) index erasure for runtime layouts when every index argument is a phantom nominal (nominal succ n = ()): one residual union per GADT head, cases tagged as usual, index checks already done by the type checker; (b) a bounded detection that reports EJP-style polymorphic recursion through a growing phantom index, naming the union and the field. (b) is the cheap correctness fix (no hang), (a) is what the mega roots' runtime substitution needs")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_5 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: helper lane 2 analysis (10:45) explains the non-termination: a runtime value needs a residual layout per type argument, and term zero has a Lam field of term (succ zero) whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), so monomorphization never closes; the guard (cap distinct index instantiations per union) belongs in the PartEval pair, blocked by pair-drift until gpt5parallel retires Next: none until HOPACSOTA-P6, unless the guard fits a shared pre-PartEval section")
inl cells_gate_machine_window () = Task ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", "Cells --gate takes a test-suite machine window from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS and waits while a live GUI run or a compiler install holds the machine, instead of lanes asking in chat (split from CELLS-GATE 2026-10-19)", Feature, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "cells,testing,gate,one-call", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_gate_machine_window () = Planning ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 1u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 first grain, the interface this item needs from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS: one call to request a window of class test-suite with an expected duration, granted or queued, and a release in the gate's teardown (spiral.notebook.ex cond, next to CompilerClient.shutdown, which also runs when the gate fails). The gate can estimate the duration from the file and test count; main's 10:51 data point: sm'.spi with 42 tests took 217 s on the warm daemon and 537 s with a process per compile. The conflicts that matter here are declared once: test-suite vs live-gui (the 10-14 reaper incidents) and test-suite vs compiler-install. Until that call exists, nothing changes in Cells. Status: blocked on the eoie lane.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:01 lap 7 status check: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is still (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS  Feature  P1  D4  0%  Planned), so the Cells side stays unstarted on purpose; the interface from note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T16:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:43 lap 8 grain: placement decided while blocked: the machine-window acquire and release go into the wrappers that start a gate (the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 around its mix call, and Invoke-Heavy callers), not into mix spiral.notebook's gate branch (spiral.notebook.ex:10-19), because Cells does not call eoie (the same rule CELLS-NEW-PROJECT follows by printing the epic command instead of running it). The Cells side therefore needs no change; the wrapper needs one eoie call before mix (window test-suite with a duration) and one in its finally block. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned, 0%)")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T17:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:20 lap 9 grain: planning set (estimate 60m, load light) and the duration input for the window request found: the gate CLI already writes --metrics <dir>/tests.tsv (notebook.ex:271-275, columns File Test Target Status Ms Memo), so the gate wrapper can request a test-suite window with expected duration = sum of Ms of the previous tests.tsv for the same paths (memo-hit rows count ~0), falling back to a fixed default on a first run; the wrapper passes --metrics into the per-run dir. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): it unblocks with an eoie verb pair that grants or queues a class=test-suite window for N minutes and releases it")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_5 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:42 lap 10 grain: the window-duration half is written and self-tested: lab/CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW/expected-minutes.ps1 -MetricsDir <previous run> reads the gate's --metrics tests.tsv, sums Ms of rows whose Memo is not hit (Memo hit is the only value Cells counts, notebook.ex:214), times a 1.5 margin, ceil to minutes, at least 1; no table = 10 min first-run default. Self-test on a synthetic table (30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run) gives 2 min; a missing dir gives 10. The acquire/release half still waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): the gate wrapper will pass Minutes as the requested window length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_6 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:58 lap 11 grain, an interim bridge until EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists: the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1) now runs the mix gate through spiral's own heavy gate (dot-sources apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 when the spiral checkout has it, Invoke-Heavy -Label cells gate <project>), so a gate waits for a free heavy slot instead of competing with suites; without heavy.ps1 it runs directly. Invoke-Heavy has no reentrancy marker (heavy.ps1 reads no env var), so a nested call would take a second slot or wait on itself: gate.ps1 takes -InsideHeavySlot to skip it, and spiral-new-run.ps1 (which already holds a slot) passes it; both parse with 0 errors; the ExUnit assertion (gate.ps1 mentions spiral.notebook) still holds. When EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands, the Invoke-Heavy call becomes the window acquire with expected-minutes.ps1's length")
inl sim_in_spiral_bulk () = Task ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", "Bulk half of the xsim rewrite in Spiral, x lane: frames round trip, invariants, event glue, scenario and delivery generation compiled to Rust (and Gleam where pure), no Cells actor port needed", Story, P1, D5, 20u32, Active, "X-CELLS-SPLIT", "sim,cells,rust,gleam,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk () = Planning ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_1 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 1u32, "2026-10-19T19:47Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 15:47 x lane accepts helper lane 2's split (SIM-IN-SPIRAL lap 10, main agreed 15:46). Scope: slice 1 the frames round trip test (posts_of -> locate body_at -> assembled sources = file_cells, xsim cases 1-3), slice 2 invariants.ex as a named-violation decision union (lap 8), slice 3 rebuild/explode_posts glue (lap 9), then scenario/delivery generation in Rust under the 1k gate. The Elixir oracle is retired per slice, in the same change that reproduces its fixtures. The supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp) stays on SIM-IN-SPIRAL with the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_2 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 2u32, "2026-10-19T20:13Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 16:13 slice 1 done (uncommitted in x): new Cells module apps/client/reading.spi takes the pure half of assemble (frame, cell_frames, Whole/Holed cell_reading, assembled) and the post-text reader locate used (frame_text_of: header parse + defused dots restored); assemble/assemble.spi and locate/locate.spi are JSON glue over it. Tests on rust + gleam agree: posted_cells_rebuild_exactly (posts_of then read back = file_cells over a link dot, a 600-weight CJK cell and a 360-weight word cell; xsim cases 1-3), posted_frames_keep_no_trailing_space, a_lost_frame_leaves_its_cell_holed. Round x-round-8b on pack 8b: Cells 11 modules 80 tests 0 failures, gate 16 crates 0 over (locate 570->591 from frame_text_of, the rest unchanged by 8b), x-compat 0 in 27 files, e2e 31+5. frames.ex can be retired once apps/sim calls the Gleam build of splitter/reading (needs the sim's Gleam build path, next). Slice 2 note: invariants.ex is pure but reads runner state snapshots (visible, starts, cancelled, evicted, results, wanted), so its oracle is hand-built snapshots until World/Runner exist; port it as a named-violation union over sorted pair lists")
inl spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal () = Task ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", "Parser: a record field written as field = ;[ newline items newline ] |> f, with the closing bracket back at the field's column, fails to parse (Expected: } at the |>), and the whole top-level block is left out of its module, so a use elsewhere only says the module does not have the key; accept an operator continuation after a closing bracket at the field column, or name the offside rule in the message (repro lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/repro.spi)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,parser,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 1u32, "2026-10-19T23:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:01 helper-lane: cut down from main's failing_use_transactions.spi (dice ui line 184) to 6 lines, lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/repro.spi: inl render (v : i32) = { items = ;[ v ] |> am'.base } with ;[ on the field line and the ] line at the field's column. Using shapes.render from main gives 5:11 Expected: } (this block is left out of its module). Not a trigger: ;[ on its own line under the field (items = newline ;[ ... ] |> am'.base) parses and compiles, as does the same layout without the pipe. So the offside rule lets a bare ] return to the field column but not an operator after it. When the dropped block's module is another file, the only visible symptom is the module does not have the key: render at the use site, which is what main hit. Workaround: put ;[ on its own line or indent the ]. Note: an unused dropped block yields no error at all (exit 0), as the extracted real block showed.")
